/* Angus's family: the data behind the tree, the profiles and the timeline.
   To add someone, add one line: id: {n:'Name', d:'dates', b:birthYear, s:side, p:['fatherId','motherId'], r:['au'], t:'notes'}
   They appear in the tree as soon as one of their children is in it, and in the profiles of their parents and children.
   sides: b Broesder (Rory's father) · f Frankish (Rory's mother) · p Parkinson, Smith and Webb · h Hazenberg (Claire's father) · y Hymus and Brazier (Claire's mother) · r Rory and Claire · a Angus
   regions: nl Netherlands · uk England · au Australia · png New Guinea · de Germany · dk Denmark
   u:true  = the link to these parents is likely but not yet proven (drawn dashed)
   q:'...' = parents still to be found (shown as a question in the tree) */
window.ROOT = 'angus';
window.PEOPLE = {
angus:{n:'Angus Broesder',d:'',s:'a',p:['rory','claire'],r:['au'],t:'Son of Rory and Claire, and big brother of Elke. Through his parents he descends from Dutch bakers and brick makers, Lincolnshire farm workers, Western Australian goldfields families, Victorian timber millers and a New Guinea government clerk.'},
elke:{n:'Elke Broesder',d:'',s:'a',p:['rory','claire'],r:['au'],t:'Daughter of Rory and Claire, born in November 2026, and little sister of Angus. She shares every line in this tree with him: the Dutch bakers and brick makers, the Lincolnshire farm workers, the Western Australian goldfields families, the Victorian timber millers and the New Guinea government clerk.'},
rory:{"n":"Rory Broesder","d":"Cairns","s":"r","p":["greg","merrilee"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Cairns and grew up in Mareeba on the Atherton Tableland, where his grandparents Lawrie and Phyllis Frankish farmed. His sister is Alena. He married Claire Hazenberg, and they have two children, Angus and Elke."},
claire:{n:'Claire Broesder',sn:'Claire Broesder',d:'',s:'r',p:['trevor','sue'],r:['au'],t:'Daughter of Trevor and Sue Hazenberg; her siblings are Alexander and Georgina. She married Rory Broesder; their children are Angus and Elke.'},
greg:{"n":"Gregory Broesder","sn":"Greg Broesder","d":"Adelaide","s":"b","p":["jerry","carla"],"r":["au"],"t":"One of five children of Jerry and Carla Broesder, raised in Adelaide's southern suburbs with Diana, Monique, Robert and Yolanda. His father had come from Dordrecht on the Fairsea in 1952 and his mother flew out from the Netherlands in 1958; they became Australian citizens together on 18 March 1968, when the family home was at Mitchell Park. His mother's brother, Johannes Degenhart, had settled in Adelaide too, and their mother came out from the Netherlands to stay for a year in 1962–63.\n\nHe met Merrilee Frankish in the mining town of Mount Isa; they moved to Cairns, where Rory was born, and then to Mareeba. Their children are Rory and Alena.\n\nIn February 2024 the Mareeba paper's golf notes listed him as the winner of the Mareeba Golf Club's Sunday 18-hole Stableford, with 37 points, and of the nearest-the-pin prize on the 13th hole.","src":[["Golf club drives forward in 2024, The Express (Mareeba), 13 Feb 2024","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/golf-club-drives-forward-in-2024-2024-02-13"]]},
merrilee:{"n":"Merrilee Frankish","d":"b. 1961 · Yarra Junction","b":1961,"s":"f","p":["lawrie","phyllis"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Yarra Junction, Victoria, in 1961, the eldest child of Lawrie and Phyllis, when her parents had their first small dairy farm, Marston Park, at Gladysdale. Her younger siblings Anita, Anthony and Cathrine were born at Kerang on the Murray River plains between 1963 and 1965. She grew up on the family dairy at Murrabit, near Kerang, where her parents milked up to about 100 cows, and was about twelve when they moved in January 1974 to a grazing property at Tara on Queensland's Western Downs. She met Greg Broesder in Mount Isa; they moved to Cairns, where Rory was born, and then to Mareeba. Their children are Rory and Alena.\n\nShe became a doctor, known in Mareeba as Dr Merrilee Frankish. The Mareeba paper reported in 2020 that she grew up at Tara and, after placements around Queensland, settled in Mareeba in 1990 as a trainee at the Mareeba Medical Clinic. She later ran her own practice, and then worked at Mareeba Hospital as a Senior Medical Officer in obstetrics and newborn care, teaching the students and junior doctors who came through. She made several trips to Papua New Guinea with Australian Doctors International. At the end of 2016 she completed a Graduate Diploma of Health Professional Education at James Cook University, where she had earlier taken a Master of Public Health and Tropical Medicine.\n\nIn December 2020, nominated by junior doctors from Cairns, she was one of three doctors highly commended as Clinical Educator of the Year for Australia and New Zealand. In 2019 she was a director of Forever Wild, the group that put on a community music evening at the Mareeba wetlands reserve. In January 2022 Mareeba Shire Council appointed her to its Regional Arts Development Fund advisory committee for three years, in January 2023 she was among the shire's Australia Day award nominees, and in 2024 she joined the Mareeba Multicultural Festival committee.","src":[["Mareeba doctor awarded Clinical Educator of the Year, The Express (Mareeba), 24 Dec 2020","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/mareeba-doctor-awarded-clinical-educator-of-the-year-2020-12-24"],["Student profile, James Cook University","https://www.jcu.edu.au/publications/student-profiles/merrilee-frankish"],["Tropical Wetlands to host live music, The Express, 13 May 2019","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/tropical-wetlands-to-host-live-music-2019-05-13"],["New members appointed to RADF, The Express, 30 Jan 2022","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/new-members-appointed-to-radf-2022-01-30"],["Australia Day awards to recognise worthy locals, The Express, 24 Jan 2023","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/australia-day-awards-to-recognise-worthy-locals-2023-01-24"],["New committee ready to showcase diversity, The Express, 6 Jul 2024","https://www.theexpressnewspaper.com.au/new-committee-ready-to-showcase-diversity-2024-07-06"]]},
trevor:{"n":"Trevor Hazenberg","d":"b. 1964 · Bundaberg","s":"h","p":["bill","meg"],"r":["au"],"t":"Trevor Willem, born at the Lady Chelmsford Hospital, Bundaberg, in May 1964, son of Bill Hazenberg and Meg Hodgetts and “brother for Joanne and Gail”. His second name is his father's Dutch first name. Bill had come from Friesland as a boy of 14 in 1950 and was by then a driver on Fairymead Plantation; in the August after Trevor was born he drove the Bundaberg-built Scott Harvestall cane harvester in trials near Mackay. On his mother's side he is a grandson of Everard Hodgetts and Amy Haken of Bundaberg. He married Sue Hymus, and their children are Claire, Alexander and Georgina.","src":[["Births, Bundaberg News-Mail, 1964","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/282940702"]]},
sue:{"n":"Sue Hazenberg","d":"","s":"y","p":["doug","beverly"],"r":["au"],"t":"Daughter of Doug Hymus and Beverly Brazier, and granddaughter of Bruce and Aileen Brazier. Her father was born at Cooroy in 1927, the son of a Bank of New South Wales officer, Francis Hymus, and Saddie Napier, and trained with the RAAF in 1945. Her mother was born in 1933 in New Guinea, where Bruce Brazier worked for the Territory administration. Her parents' engagement was announced in the Courier-Mail on 1 November 1951, and by early 1956 they were living in Cairns. She married Trevor Hazenberg, and they are the parents of Claire, Alexander and Georgina."},

jerry:{"n":"Tjaard Jacob Broesder","sn":"Jerry Broesder","d":"1932–2022 · Dordrecht","b":1932,"x":2022,"s":"b","p":["jakobt","grietje"],"r":["nl","au"],"img":"img/fairsea.jpg","ch":"crossing","t":"Born in Dordrecht on 2 June 1932, the youngest of three and the only son of Jakob Tjaart Broesder and Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn. He was registered as Tjaard Jacob, the names of his grandfather, the baker and café keeper. He was seven when Germany invaded the Netherlands in May 1940 and twelve in the Hunger Winter of 1944-45, when food and fuel ran out in the occupied west of the country.\n\nAged 20 he emigrated to Australia on the Fairsea, reaching Sydney on 10 October 1952. The Fairsea was a migrant ship of the Sitmar Line that carried assisted settlers from Europe to Australia in those years. Known in Australia as Jerry, he married Carla Degenhart and they raised five children in Adelaide. He and Carla became Australian citizens together on 18 March 1968, when the family lived at Mitchell Park in Adelaide's southern suburbs.\n\nHe died in January 2022, aged 89.","src":[["Alien registration file, NAA D4881","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7503487"],["Dordrecht family card","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:AD7A4373-C760-4C8D-8AD1-9DA06DF32109"],["Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, 22 Aug 1968 (Jerry)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article241058115"],["Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, 22 Aug 1968 (Carla)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article241058090"]]},
carla:{"n":"Carolina Antonia Degenhart","sn":"Carla Degenhart","d":"1935–2023","b":1935,"x":2023,"s":"b","p":["hendricus","mariavdb"],"r":["nl","au"],"ch":"crossing","t":"Born 15 April 1935. Her father was lost at sea when she was six. She came to Australia alone under the Netherlands–Australia Migration Agreement, flying rather than sailing, and landed in Sydney on 4 November 1958. She married Jerry Broesder. Her funeral Mass was held at Holy Spirit Catholic Church, Seacombe Gardens, in 2023.\n\nShe and Jerry were naturalised as Australian citizens on the same day, 18 March 1968, when they lived in Minkie Avenue, Mitchell Park.\n\nHer parents had married in Amsterdam on 4 September 1929. The record describes her father as a ship's engineer in the merchant navy (machinist ter koopvaardij) and her mother as a housekeeper.","src":[["Migration file, NAA A2478","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=1227504"],["Funeral notice","https://siebert.com.au/notices/carolina_broesder/"],["Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, 22 Aug 1968 (Jerry)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article241058115"],["Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, 22 Aug 1968 (Carla)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article241058090"],["Parents' marriage, Amsterdam, 4 Sep 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7807FA7A-94F4-4BD9-A382-FC69FC2C504A"]]},
lawrie:{"n":"Lawrence Wetherall Frankish","sn":"Lawrie Frankish","d":"b. 1937 · Perth","b":1937,"s":"f","p":["walter","mona"],"r":["au"],"ch":"frankish","img":"img/lawrie-cadets-1951.jpg","t":"Born in Perth on 24 January 1937, the son of Walter Frankish; his middle name, Wetherall, was the maiden name of his father's mother. From Perth Boys' School he was one of seven Western Australian boys chosen in January 1951 to enter the Royal Australian Naval College at Flinders Naval Depot, Victoria, as a cadet midshipman, the first step of a four-year course for permanent officers. Home on his first term leave in May 1951, aged 14, he and three other cadets called on the Governor, Sir James Mitchell, who jokingly told them they should have become dairy farmers.\n\nHe married Phyllis Parkinson at Gladysdale, Victoria, on 6 September 1958, and they started out there on a small dairy farm they called Marston Park. In 1962 they sold it and moved to a much bigger dairy at Murrabit, out towards Gonn Crossing north of Kerang, milking up to about 100 cows, which was a big herd then. In January 1974 they bought a 3,000-acre grazing property at Tara on the Western Downs, which they called Marston, and they also owned Tara Independent Tyre Service. Their farm at Mareeba was Marston Farm.\n\nThe name Marston comes from his grandmother's side. The family's own account is of a farm on Marston Moor in Yorkshire, and the records bear it out: Lawrie's grandmother, Emily Wetherall, was born in 1863 at Marston, beside Marston Moor, where her father Robert farmed.\n\nHe was the third of Walter and Mona's four children, after Ramona (1932) and Ted (1935) and before young Walter. He and Phyllis had four children: Merrilee, born at Yarra Junction in 1961, and Anita, Anthony and Cathrine, born at Kerang between 1963 and 1965.","src":[["W.A. cadets for Navy, West Australian, 10 Jan 1951","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/48145636"],["Cadet midshipmen call on Governor, West Australian, 24 May 1951","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/48198147"]]},
phyllis:{"n":"Phyllis Alma Parkinson","sn":"Phyllis Parkinson","d":"b. 1936 · Yarra Junction","b":1936,"s":"p","p":["ben","alma"],"r":["au"],"img":"img/parkinson-sisters-1956.jpg","ch":"yarra","t":"Born at Yarra Junction on 24 February 1936, eldest of the three daughters of Ben Parkinson and Alma Smith; her second name is her mother's. Joan was born the next year and Pam in 1946. She grew up at Gladysdale, in the timber country of the Upper Yarra, where her father and his brother Don worked together in the mills and the two Parkinson families lived side by side; Don's wife Elsie was her mother's sister.\n\nShe married Lawrie Frankish at Gladysdale on 6 September 1958, and they began on a small dairy farm there that they called Marston Park. With Lawrie she farmed at Gladysdale, Murrabit, Tara and Mareeba: the bigger dairy at Murrabit, north of Kerang, from 1962; the 3,000-acre grazing property at Tara on the Western Downs from January 1974; and Marston Farm at Mareeba. Their children are Merrilee, born at Yarra Junction in 1961, and Anita, Anthony and Cathrine, born at Kerang between 1963 and 1965.\n\nThe crescent-moon brooch made by her great-uncle, the Prahran jeweller Charles Elliott Webb, has come down to her, and it is through her that the Frankish family's own account of its past is told: that the family were Huguenots from near Flanders, and that they once farmed on Marston Moor."},
bill:{"n":"Willem Hazenberg","sn":"Willem (Bill) Hazenberg","d":"b. 1936 · Irnsum, Friesland","b":1936,"s":"h","p":["taeke","aukje"],"r":["nl","au"],"img":"img/jvo1950.jpg","ch":"hazenberg","t":"Born at Irnsum in Friesland on 25 January 1936, the only son of Taeke Hazenberg and Aukje Elzinga; his three sisters, Aaltje, Ietje and Janna, were younger. He was 14 when the family sailed on the Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, which reached Fremantle on 4 October 1950 and landed its Sydney passengers on 12 October (his sister Aaltje's file gives the 13th). Known in Australia as Bill, he made his life in Queensland. The family spent a year at Monto before moving to Bundaberg, and in April 1955, aged 19 and a driver at Fairymead, he was the youngest of nine Bundaberg new Australians awarded Rotary agricultural scholarships for a twelve-day course at Gatton College. His engagement to Nancy Joan (Meg) Hodgetts was announced at the end of 1957, and they married at the Barolin Street Methodist Church, Bundaberg, on 7 February 1959. Their children are Joanne, Gail and Trevor. Working as a driver on Fairymead Plantation, he became an Australian citizen at the Gooburrum Shire Council chambers on 9 June 1961. In August 1964 he drove the Bundaberg-built Scott Harvestall cane harvester in trials near Mackay, where it cut a patch of tangled, lodged cane that onlookers had thought impossible.","src":[["9 Leave For Course At Gatton, Bundaberg News-Mail, 4 Apr 1955","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283695423"],["Engagement notice, 1957","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283916690"],["Dutchman Naturalised, Bundaberg News-Mail, 10 Jun 1961","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284488547"],["B'berg Harvester Trials, News-Mail, 15 Aug 1964","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283106640"],["Population register, Rauwerderhem","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:70ba4515-13b5-1d1b-45ff-9ffaf4cd1124"],["Passenger list, Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, 1950","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=30133074"],["Naturalisation file, NAA J25 1960/9014","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=1674717"],["1342 Migrants Arrive, Daily News (Perth), 4 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/84479917"],["Dutch migrants reach Fremantle, West Australian, 5 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47890457"],["Large Party Of Dutch Migrants, Daily Mirror (Sydney), 12 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/274630383"],["National Archives file of his sister, Aaltje Brouwer (NAA SP908/1)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7227348"]]},
meg:{"n":"Nancy Joan Hodgetts","sn":"Meg Hodgetts","d":"1937–2021","b":1937,"x":2021,"s":"h","p":["everard","amyh"],"r":["au"],"ch":"hazenberg","t":"Known as Meg. Born on 26 September 1937, one of the Hodgetts family of the Bundaberg district. Her parents were Everard Hodgetts and Amy Louisa Haken, who married in 1934, and her brothers and sisters were Margaret, Peter, Wendy, Tony, Barry, Laurie and Aussie. Barry was Barry Everard Hodgetts (died at Ingham in 2007, aged 72), Laurie was Kevin Lawrence (died near Mackay in 1998, aged 56), and Aussie was Lewis Gordon (died at Turkey Beach in 2016, aged 70); Barry's and Aussie's middle names are those of their father and of their mother's father.\n\nOn 31 December 1957 the Bundaberg News-Mail announced the engagement of Nancy Joan, second daughter of Mr and Mrs E. Hodgetts of Perry Street, to Willem, only son of Mr and Mrs T. Hazenberg of Springfield Road. She married Bill Hazenberg at the Barolin Street Methodist Church, Bundaberg, at 4 p.m. on Saturday 7 February 1959, and they had Joanne, Gail and Trevor. She later married Kevin Wills and became stepmother to Diane, Cristine, Roslyn and Brett.\n\nThe family remember her running a corner grocery store in Gin Gin, where she lived at Mulgrave Street. She died on 10 March 2021 at Millbank Village, Bundaberg, and her funeral was held at the Gin Gin Community Church.","src":[["Engagement notice, Bundaberg News-Mail, 31 Dec 1957","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283916690"],["Marriage notice, Bundaberg News-Mail, 7 Feb 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284346822"],["Funeral notice, Browns Funerals Bundaberg","https://brownsfunerals.com.au/wills-nancy-joan-meg/"]]},
doug:{"n":"Edward Douglas Hymus","sn":"Doug Hymus","d":"1927–2012 · Cooroy","b":1927,"x":2012,"s":"y","p":["francish","saddie"],"r":["au"],"ch":"guinea","t":"Known as Doug. Born at Cooroy, in the Noosa hinterland of Queensland, on 5 January 1927, the younger son of Francis Edward Hymus and Saddie Dorothy Napier. His father had come to Cooroy four months earlier as teller at the Bank of New South Wales, and his mother was secretary of the town's Country Women's Association.\n\nA bank officer's family moved every few years, and Doug grew up in a string of Queensland country towns: Proserpine in 1935, Allora on the Darling Downs in 1936, Millaa Millaa on the Atherton Tableland in 1940 and 1941, and Bowen by 1945. His elder brother Neville went into the Army in 1942.\n\nHe joined the RAAF in Brisbane on 23 February 1945, aged 18, giving his home as Bowen, and trained at the Signals School before his discharge in September 1945; his next of kin was Francis Hymus. In November 1951 the Courier-Mail announced his engagement to Beverly: “Edward Douglas, younger son of Mr and Mrs F. E. Hymus, Charters Towers”. They married, and he died in Brisbane on 12 October 2012, aged 85.","src":[["Engagement, Courier-Mail, 1 Nov 1951","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/50221142"],["WW2 Nominal Roll, DVA","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["RAAF service file, NAA A13859 171392","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=32242894"],["Ryerson Index of death notices","https://www.ryersonindex.org/"],["Parents at the Bank of New South Wales, Allora, Toowoomba Chronicle, 11 Jun 1936","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285366493"]]},
beverly:{"n":"Beverley Joan Brazier","sn":"Beverly Brazier","d":"b. 1933 · New Guinea","b":1933,"s":"y","p":["bruce","aileen"],"r":["png","au"],"img":"img/montoro1934.jpg","ch":"guinea","t":"Known in the family as Beverly. She was born in 1933, while her father worked for the Territory of New Guinea administration. At one, she sailed from Rabaul to Brisbane on the Montoro with her parents and her sister Margaret. In June 1940, when the family's home was at Kavieng, the two girls came to Brisbane with their mother. She was eight when European women and children were evacuated from New Guinea in December 1941; by 1943 the family's home was at Rose Bay in Sydney. On 2 January 1951, aged 17, she landed at Brisbane from New Guinea on the Bulolo. On 1 November 1951 the Courier-Mail announced her engagement to Doug Hymus, “younger son of Mr and Mrs F. E. Hymus, Charters Towers”; by early 1956 they were married and living in Cairns with their first two children. Their daughter Sue married Trevor Hazenberg, and their daughter Claire is Angus’s mother.\n\nShe was the second of Bruce and Aileen's three children: Margaret was born in 1932 and Bruce in 1941. Margaret's passenger card from the Bulolo, of the same day in January 1951, is kept with hers in the National Archives in Brisbane.","src":[["Engagement, Courier-Mail, 1 Nov 1951","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/50221142"],["The Social Round, Telegraph, 20 Jun 1940","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/188198567"],["Port Moresby Has City Comforts, Telegraph, 11 Feb 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/293831648"],["Passenger list, Montoro, 1934 (NAA A907)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7829294"],["Passenger card, Bulolo, 1951 (NAA BP26/1)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=9106590"],["Passenger card of Margaret Elaine Brazier, Bulolo, 1951 (NAA BP26/1)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=9106592"]]},

jakobt:{"n":"Jakob Tjaart Broesder","d":"1902–1975 · Nieuwolda","b":1902,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl","au"],"img":"img/dordrecht.jpg","ch":"dordrecht","t":"Born in Nieuwolda on 1 June 1902, the fourth of the nine children of the baker Tjaard Broesder and Antje Dijk. His brothers and sisters were Epke (1897), Roelfien Antje (1899), Antje (1900), Augustinus (1904), two girls named Elena (1905, who lived four months, and 1906), Emkelenus Hendrik (1909) and Harm (1914).\n\nAs a young man he is listed in the Utrecht population register on the card of the Oosterheerd family, together with his brother Augustinus. That household was family: Elena Broesder, born at Nieuwolda in 1866, was his father's elder sister and the widow of the Loppersum butcher Harm Apotheker, who died in 1922. Her daughter Epke Apotheker married Albert Oosterheerd, a life-insurance inspector, at Loppersum on 12 October 1922, and their children were born in Utrecht from 1923. Elena died at Oudenrijn, near Utrecht, on 26 March 1952, aged 85. A second Utrecht card lists him among a group of young men of his own age.\n\nHe married Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn of Schelluinen; the marriage record itself has not been found. The Maurik register of 1910-1939 lists him with Grietje and their daughter Antje, the Zwijndrecht registers of 1921-1935 have entries for both of them, and the Dordrecht family card lists the whole family of five, the youngest being Tjaard Jacob, born at Dordrecht in 1932.\n\nHe ended his life in Australia, where his son Tjaard Jacob, known as Jerry, had arrived on the Fairsea in 1952. Jacob Tjaart Broesder died at Nowra, New South Wales, on 5 May 1975, aged 72. The New South Wales death register names his parents as Tjaart Jacob and Antje, which settles that he is the man born at Nieuwolda, and the death notice in the Sydney Morning Herald called him Jake and gave his last home as Myola, near Callala Beach on Jervis Bay. Nowra is the main town of the Shoalhaven district on the south coast of New South Wales. No record found shows when he and Grietje left the Netherlands.","src":[["Birth record","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:da916386-3756-a489-febf-578e2d3a3e51"],["Population register, Utrecht","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:373975D4-3D73-69A2-E063-8F04000A801D"],["Parents' marriage, Zuidbroek, 16 May 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c7bca28f-7f9c-d092-4384-d78f8533ef12"],["Utrecht population register (second page)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:37392FB5-B3CC-67EE-E063-8F04000ABAF5"],["Maurik population register 1910-1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rar:AFE4A852-699A-4FC2-9BA1-0C6BCE08618C"],["Zwijndrecht population register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:4bab9802-3ebe-d0c8-0ff6-1aeecf88f09e"],["Marriage of Epke Apotheker and Albert Oosterheerd, Loppersum 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b3d665b4-1755-3704-d0f6-5359e241ce5f"],["Marriage of Elena Broesder and Harm Apotheker, Appingedam 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:50c71284-258c-df40-e9c2-c454a51393d0"],["Death of Harm Apotheker, Loppersum 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:faa25544-bd9d-0497-e223-21ad4e71f2f4"],["Death of Elena Broesder, Oudenrijn 1952","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:DCD107A9-DD35-4506-9758-A5D70FB6AA2E"],["Ryerson Index: death notice of Jacob Tjaart (Jake) Broesder, Sydney Morning Herald, 7 May 1975","https://www.ryersonindex.org/search.php"],["NSW death index: Broesder, Jacob Tjaart, reg. 22425/1975, father Tjaart Jacob, mother Antje","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"]],"x":1975},
grietje:{"n":"Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn","d":"b. 1908 · Schelluinen near Gorinchem","b":1908,"s":"b","p":["leendertb","gijsbertje"],"r":["nl"],"ch":"dordrecht","t":"Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn was born at Schelluinen, a village just north of Gorinchem, on 10 August 1908, the youngest of the eight children of the innkeeper Leendert de Bruijn and Gijsbertje de Kreij. Her father registered the birth himself. One brother, Willem, had died as a baby in 1895, years before she was born; her eldest sister Lijntje married in 1909, when Grietje was one.\n\nOn 14 March 1929, aged 20, she married Jacob Tjaart Broesder at Schelluinen. He was 26, a driver (chauffeur) living at Gorinchem, born at Nieuwolda in Groningen at the far north-east of the country; his parents, the grocer Tjaard Broesder and Antje Dijk, came from Zuidlaren in Drenthe for the wedding. Her father, then 60, was described as a café-keeper (cafehouder), and both her parents gave their consent. Her brother Jan Willem, a road-paver (straatmaker), was one of the witnesses. Their first daughter, Antje, was born at Maurik in the Betuwe in 1929; Gijsbertje followed at Zwijndrecht in 1930, named after Grietje's mother, and Tjaard Jacob at Dordrecht in 1932. The Dordrecht population register files her under her husband's name: her card reads \"Zie Broesder, Jacob Tjaart\" (see Broesder).\n\nHer mother died at Schelluinen in 1932 and her father there in 1939. Of her brothers and sisters, Leendert died in Rotterdam in 1951, Cornelia Metje at Gouda in 1965 and Jan Willem at Schelluinen in 1966.\n\nHer husband Jakob Tjaart died at Nowra, New South Wales, on 5 May 1975. No immigration file in her name or his has been found in the National Archives of Australia, which hold one for their son Tjaard Jacob, who arrived in 1952, and no death of a Grietje Broesder is in the New South Wales index up to 1996. A death notice for a Grietje Broesder, who died on 5 August 2002, was published in the Adelaide Advertiser. Adelaide was her son's home, but the notice as indexed gives no age, and no record yet shows that it was hers.","src":[["Birth record, 1908","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:37033f81-c60a-aaf9-060e-7366cce49b8e"],["Death of Cornelia Metje de Bruijn, Gouda, 1965","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:0456b2fe-1c9e-fe77-237c-215d9b7a992d"],["Death of Leendert de Bruijn, Rotterdam, 1951","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:93219a49-9687-b41b-00fe-6db4cd5f3ce2"],["Dordrecht population register card","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:7DDC961C-67B7-4F58-800A-D8A2128AB8CD"],["Marriage, Schelluinen, 14 March 1929 (register scan read)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21095534"],["Birth register, Schelluinen 1908","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6891711"],["Ryerson Index: death notice of Grietje Broesder, Adelaide Advertiser, 8 August 2002","https://www.ryersonindex.org/search.php"],["NSW death index: Broesder, Jacob Tjaart, reg. 22425/1975, father Tjaart Jacob, mother Antje","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"]]},
hendricus:{"n":"Hendricus J. J. Degenhart","d":"1903–1941 · Amsterdam","b":1903,"x":1941,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"ch":"crossing","t":"Ship's engineer in the Dutch merchant navy. He served on the steamer SS Groenlo, which was sunk by German motor torpedo boats off the Norfolk coast on 24 November 1941. He was 37.\n\nHe was born in Amsterdam on 13 December 1903. When he married Maria van den Berg in Amsterdam on 4 September 1929 he was 25 and a merchant-navy engineer (machinist ter koopvaardij); his father is called a diamond cleaver in the record. The Dutch merchant-navy personnel cards list him as married and lost with the Groenlo. His death was not entered in the civil register until 27 February 1946, more than four years later. That record gives his age as 37, the place as the Atlantic Ocean, and the ship as the \"s.s. Groelo\". The file kept by the Netherlands War Graves Foundation gives the place as the North Sea, on board the s.s. Groenlo.\n\nHe was the third of the six known children of Johannes Degenhart, an Amsterdam diamond polisher, and Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann. His brothers George (a teacher) and Johannes (a window dresser) were born in 1899 and 1900; Elizabeth, Carolina and Henricus followed between about 1906 and 1916. His three given names, Hendricus Johannes Jacobus, were those of his grandfather, a cabinet maker who had come to Amsterdam from Kampen in the 1860s, and of an uncle who was a schoolteacher in the city.","src":[["Marriage record, 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7807FA7A-94F4-4BD9-A382-FC69FC2C504A"],["Canadian Virtual War Memorial","https://www.veterans.gc.ca/eng/remembrance/memorials/canadian-virtual-war-memorial/detail/2641104"],["Death entry, Amsterdam register, 24 Nov 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2AFED5D4-9258-470E-BB9A-1EA129BE5D73"],["Merchant navy personnel card (Koopvaardijpersoneel)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nim:C80EDBA6-155D-4F30-85F8-1B8014CE646C"],["Amsterdam militia register (born 13 Dec 1903)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9853345b-66d2-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["War Graves Foundation personal file, Nationaal Archief","https://www.openarchieven.nl/ghn:603fce8e-a136-11e8-828a-00505693001d"],["Marriage of his grandparents, Zwolle, 28 May 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D6569E41-EAC6-46B3-B9B1-C217F80EE620"],["Marriage of his uncle Hendrikus J. J. Degenhart, teacher, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:BA863A53-B41B-4B23-83EF-1DC771493D06"]]},
mariavdb:{"n":"Maria Catharina Louiza van den Berg","sn":"Maria van den Berg","d":"b. 1901 · Meppel","b":1901,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Meppel, Drenthe, on 5 June 1901. A housekeeper when she married in Amsterdam in 1929, she was left to raise two children in occupied Amsterdam. She visited Adelaide for a year in 1962–63.\n\nShe was the sixth of nine children of a shoemaker whose work took the family from Tilburg to Waalwijk, Besoijen, Meppel, Zaandam and Amsterdam. Her brothers Bernardus, Franciscus and Josephus were shoemakers too, and Petrus was a house painter; her sisters were Maria Geertruida, Maria Elisabeth, Johanna and Wilhelmina. Her father, a shoemaker aged 30, registered her birth at Meppel the next day. As a child she is listed with her parents and brothers and sisters in the Rotterdam population register, and in 1913 in the register of The Hague. An Amsterdam register of the annexed municipalities also lists her, with the address Sloten. She was 28 at her wedding in 1929. Her husband's death at sea in 1941 was only registered in February 1946, and she is named in that record as his wife.\n\nThe Amsterdam marriage record of 4 September 1929 describes her as a housekeeper (huishoudster) of 28. Her husband Hendricus Degenhart was 25 and a ship's engineer in the merchant navy (machinist ter koopvaardij), the son of an Amsterdam diamond cleaver. Her younger sister Wilhelmina had married in Amsterdam twelve weeks earlier, on 13 June. The household is also entered in the population registers of Zaandam and of Waalwijk; the Waalwijk register writes her third name as Josephina, where her birth record has Louiza.","src":[["Birth of sister Johanna, Meppel, 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:6392eb59-30d5-4095-8fbd-c38ee10a1dac"],["Birth, Meppel 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:8d4b5451-679c-4e75-a185-891322e97330"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["The Hague population register 1913","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:84AF0AC3-716E-4C99-BE74-FB1F8EA8F657"],["Amsterdam register of annexed municipalities (Sloten)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9853346a-d11d-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Husband's death record","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2AFED5D4-9258-470E-BB9A-1EA129BE5D73"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 4 Sep 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7807FA7A-94F4-4BD9-A382-FC69FC2C504A"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Waalwijk population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:815a5693-cbeb-1b2d-5c2e-b8f4d0760628"],["Marriage of sister Wilhelmina, Amsterdam, 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:CFA15BCE-DE55-4433-A495-093485FCB315"]]},
walter:{"n":"Walter Tom Wetherall Frankish","sn":"Walter Frankish","d":"1904–1964 · Northam","b":1904,"x":1964,"s":"f","p":["thomas2","emily"],"r":["au"],"ch":"frankish","t":"Born at Northam, WA, in 1904, the youngest of Thomas and Emily's four sons and the only one born in Australia. In March 1908, aged four, he ran across Newcastle Street in Perth, dodged one tram and was caught by the front guard of another; he spent days in Perth Hospital, and the accident made the papers. He was still in his teens when his father died, and for years he and his mother put an anniversary notice in the paper together.\n\nHe married Mona Fisk at St George's Cathedral, Perth, at the end of 1931. Archdeacon Riley officiated, assisted by Dean Moore; Mona wore ivory embossed satin with a Brussels lace veil and the rope of pearls Walter had given her, and after the wedding breakfast they left for Tawarri, their beach home. They lived first at Scarborough Beach, where Walter, a civil servant, was secretary of the Scarborough Beach Progress Association; Ramona was born there in December 1932. By 1935 they had moved to Gallipoli Street, Victoria Park, where they lived through the war years; Edward (Ted) was born in January 1935 and Lawrie in January 1937, and a fourth child, Walter, followed.\n\nWalter had a fine bass-baritone voice, and Perth knew it. He sang on the radio with the Perth City Band in 1937, was the bass soloist in Stainer's \"The Crucifixion\" at St George's Cathedral on Good Friday 1938 and again in 1940, and in the \"Messiah\" at Christmas 1940. In 1939 he won a heat of the 6PR radio talent quest singing \"The Smugglers' Song\", and with Nell Shortland-Jones won another heat with the duet \"Sweethearts\". In the war years he sang small roles in the ABC opera seasons under William G. James, including Marullo in \"Rigoletto\" in 1944, and he appeared in musical comedy and plays after the war. In 1941, with the children at Rivervale School, he was a vice-president of its Parents and Citizens' Association.\n\nIn 1952 or 1953 he and Mona moved to Melbourne with Ted, young Walter and Ramona; Lawrie was already in Victoria with the Navy. Walter died at Hawthorn East in 1964.","src":[["Frankish-Fisk wedding, Sunday Times, 24 Jan 1932","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/58656304"],["Scarborough hotel hearing (Walter Frankish, civil servant), Daily News, 27 Aug 1934","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/85472568"],["Singer wins S.T.C. session, Daily News, 20 Apr 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/79237602"],["The Crucifixion, St George's Cathedral, West Australian, 6 Apr 1938","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/41674525"],["Rigoletto cast, West Australian, 25 Oct 1944","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/44983227"],["Tram accident, Daily News, 21 Mar 1908","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/77262158"],["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["WA birth index: Walter Tom Wetherall Frankish, Northam, 1904, reg. 4207, parents Thomas Frankish and Emily Wetherall; WA marriage index, Perth, 1931","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"]]},
mona:{"n":"Mona Elsie Fisk","d":"1907–1996 · Kalgoorlie","b":1907,"x":1996,"s":"f","p":["edwardf","matilda"],"r":["au"],"ch":"frankish","t":"A goldfields girl, born at Kalgoorlie in 1907, the daughter of Edward Charles (Charlie) Fisk and Matilda Anthony. She was about eleven when her parents' divorce was reported in the Perth papers in 1918.\n\nShe married Walter Frankish at St George's Cathedral, Perth, at the end of 1931, in ivory embossed satin with a Brussels lace veil; with her brother away, Mr G. Sherington walked her up the aisle. Their children were Ramona (born at their home at Scarborough Beach in December 1932), Edward (Ted, born in January 1935 at Narembeen), Lawrence (Lawrie, January 1937) and Walter. Her mother Matilda was killed in an accident in August 1935.\n\nMona was a doer. In the late 1930s she was honorary secretary of the Victoria Park Free Kindergarten committee, raising money for a hall, and she ran a parents' discussion group at the YWCA. When the war stopped the kindergarten building, she took a Kindergarten Union training course and from September 1942 ran a play centre for about twenty neighbourhood children in her own home at Gallipoli Street; the Western Mail photographed it in July 1943, and the Lord Mayor, Dr Meagher, praised her for it. After the war she was secretary of the West Australian Children's Book Council; it was in her name that the council was incorporated in 1948, with Sir Hal Colebatch among its trustees, to promote children's books and set up free children's libraries.\n\nWhen her father died in Perth in 1954, his death notice named her as Mona, Mrs W. Frankish, of Victoria; she and Walter had moved to Melbourne in 1952 or 1953. She died in 1996 aged 88.\n\nHer only brother, Edward Anthony (Ted) Fisk, was born at Kalgoorlie in 1909. He named her as his next of kin when he enlisted in 1940, and finished the war a captain in the 2/2nd Machine Gun Battalion.","img":"img/home-kindergarten-1943.jpg","src":[["A Home Kindergarten, Western Mail, 1 Jul 1943","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/38545836"],["Play centre, Western Mail, 1 Jul 1943","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/38545865"],["Play Centre's Helpful Service, Daily News, 11 Jun 1943","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/78452703"],["Children's Book Council incorporation notice, Sunday Times, 6 Jun 1948","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/59481707"],["Frankish-Fisk wedding, Sunday Times, 24 Jan 1932","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/58656304"],["Birth of a daughter, West Australian, 8 Dec 1932","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/32576581"],["WWII nominal roll, E. A. Fisk","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=144525&c=WW2"]]},
ben:{"n":"William Benjamin Parkinson","sn":"Ben Parkinson","d":"1908–1983 · Thornton near Alexandra","b":1908,"x":1983,"s":"p","p":["wgp","martha"],"r":["au"],"img":"img/parkinson-wedding-1935.jpg","ch":"yarra","t":"Born at Thornton near Alexandra on 17 July 1908, the fourth of five children of William George Parkinson and Martha Wilson; his sisters Olive, Doris and Ivy were older, and his brother Don was born in 1910. His birth was registered at Alexandra as William Benjamin. He was six when his father enlisted in July 1915, four days before his seventh birthday, and eight when his mother moved the children to Hoddles Creek, in the timber country of the Upper Yarra, in May 1917. The family remember that in later years he and Don would barely acknowledge their father.\n\nHe married Alma Beatrice Smith at Hoddles Creek on 6 April 1935. Alma's sister Elsie had married his brother Don the year before, and the two families lived side by side at Gladysdale. He and Don worked together in timber mills all their working lives. Ben and Alma had three daughters, born at Yarra Junction in 1936, 1937 and 1946.\n\nHis three sisters, Olive, Doris and Ivy, were all living when their mother died in November 1949; her funeral left Lilydale for the Lilydale Cemetery. He died at Yarra Junction in March 1983, aged 74.\n\nCricket was part of life at Gladysdale. The Healesville Guardian reported D. Parkinson bowling accurately for Gladysdale in November 1949, and B. Parkinson making 53 for Gladysdale against Powelltown in October 1953; these were probably Don and Ben.","src":[["Mother's death notice, The Age, 7 Nov 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article189479189"],["Victorian BDM index: birth 15971/1908 (Alexandra); marriage 4513/1935; death 17888/1983 (Yarra Junction, aged 74, born Thornton)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Healesville Guardian, 5 November 1949, 'Cricket: Gladysdale win' (Trove)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article60679646"],["Healesville Guardian, 16 October 1953, 'Gladysdale v. Powelltown' (Trove)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article65382603"]]},
alma:{"n":"Alma Beatrice Smith","sn":"Alma Smith","d":"1907–1990 · Armadale, Prahran","b":1907,"x":1990,"s":"p","p":["ern","mabel"],"r":["au"],"img":"img/parkinson-wedding-1935.jpg","ch":"yarra","t":"Alma Beatrice Smith was born on 10 September 1907 at Armadale, in the old City of Prahran, Melbourne, the fourth of the five children of Ern Smith, a painter and paperhanger, and his wife Mabel Ann Webb. Her eldest sister, Mabel Ruth, had died at five months in 1902, before Alma was born; her other siblings were Ernest Joseph (born 1903), Elsie Alice (1905) and Doris Kate, born in 1910, who died in 1913 aged three.\n\nAround 1911 the family moved out to Hoddles Creek in the Upper Yarra, where her grandparents Joseph Henry and Eliza Smith had taken up land, and she grew up there. In 1935 she married William Benjamin (Ben) Parkinson. Her sister Elsie had married Ben's brother Don the year before, and the two Parkinson families lived side by side at Gladysdale, a small farming settlement in the hills south of Yarra Junction.\n\nHer mother died in 1958 and her father in 1964. Ben died in 1983, and Alma died at Yarra Junction on 27 May 1990, aged 82. Her death registration gives her birthplace simply as Melbourne.","src":[["Birth index, 1907 (reg. 23511/1907), Armadale","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
bruce:{"n":"Bruce Wilton Brazier","d":"1900–1971 · Toowoomba","b":1900,"x":1971,"s":"y","p":["george","ellen"],"r":["au","png"],"img":"img/brazier-bulolo-1948.jpg","ch":"guinea","t":"Born at Toowoomba on 14 February 1900. From Brisbane Central school he won a State Scholarship in 1914, and spent two years at Brisbane Grammar School. He enlisted at the very end of the First World War, and in September 1927 was appointed a clerk in the New Guinea service of the Commonwealth. He married Aileen Warbrick at Scots Church, Clayfield, on 27 November 1929.\n\nIn 1934 he was a civil servant based at Rabaul. In November 1935 he was transferred from Rabaul to Kieta on Bougainville, in January 1936 promoted within the District Services Department, and in May 1938 sent from Rabaul to Kavieng. Over the years the family lived at Rabaul, Kokopo, Kavieng and Wau; in 1940 their home was Kavieng, New Ireland.\n\nHe enlisted again in Sydney on 22 November 1943, giving his home as Balfour Road, Rose Bay, and served in New Guinea with ANGAU, the Australian New Guinea Administrative Unit, as a Warrant Officer, for 517 days overseas. Pacific Islands Monthly knew him as Bill Brazier: in January 1945 it reported \"Bill Brazier (Rabaul and Wau)\" serving near Finschhafen. Discharged in April 1946, he went back to the Treasury Department at Port Moresby.\n\nIn August 1948 he sailed north again on the Bulolo after leave, photographed for Pacific Islands Monthly; in June 1949 he flew to Daru on Treasury business on one of the new Qantas Catalina services. The family came to Brisbane on the Bulolo in January 1951. After a spell in Greenslopes Military Hospital in July 1953 he was appointed a Paying Officer for the Territory that October, and a Receiver of Public Moneys in June 1957. He died in Brisbane in 1971.\n\nThe Queensland death index gives the date of his death as 18 May 1971.","src":[["State Scholarships, Telegraph, 30 Jan 1914","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/174265386"],["Brazier–Warbrick wedding, Queensland Figaro, 7 Dec 1929","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/84907722"],["N.G. Public Service, PIM, 20 Nov 1935","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-328451533"],["N.G. Public Service, PIM, 24 Jan 1936","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-328582224"],["N.G. Public Service, PIM, 25 May 1938","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-328959018"],["TNG men on a TNG job, PIM, 19 Jan 1945","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-329358830"],["More Bulolo passengers, PIM, 1 Sep 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-330001004"],["More air services for Papua-NG, PIM, 1 Jun 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-330167663"],["Norfolk Island Notes, PIM, 1 Aug 1953","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-334099769"],["Appointment of Paying Officer, TPNG Gazette, 15 Oct 1953","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/249430699"],["Receivers of Public Moneys, TPNG Gazette, 4 Jul 1957","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/249422311"],["New Guinea Federal Appointments, The Week, 30 Sep 1927","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/187598588"],["The Social Round, Telegraph, 20 Jun 1940","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/188198567"],["WWII service record, NAA B884 N465736","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=6163296"],["WWI enlistment, NAA B2455","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=3115142"],["New Guinea staff file, NAA A452","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=3483245"],["Birth, Qld 1900/C/3499","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/327e45dda48e141ff29d00df5fbc321c76f47a9ee718ac67cba17b0d2f0ab105"],["Death, Qld 1971/B/23435","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/591cf54900362534f2930e61d6375263acd3f08ae209f22b3277e0b12eb925f3"]]},
aileen:{"n":"Aileen Lucy Warbrick","d":"1903–1992 · Brisbane","b":1903,"x":1992,"s":"y","p":["frank","adele"],"r":["au","png"],"ch":"guinea","t":"Born in Brisbane on 25 May 1903. She married Bruce Brazier at Scots Church, Clayfield, on 27 November 1929, in a white and silver frock with a veil of Honiton lace lent by a cousin; the reception was at Ryecroft, the Warbrick home in Bayview Terrace, and the couple honeymooned at Tweed Heads. Her Brisbane friends gave her farewell teas when she left on 19 May 1930 to live in New Guinea; over the years her homes were at Rabaul, Kokopo, Kavieng and Wau.\n\nIn March 1932, after eighteen months at Kokopo, she and Bruce came down to Brisbane on a long visit and stayed with her mother at Clayfield, and the Telegraph interviewed her about housekeeping in the tropics. They sailed home on the Tanda in July 1932.\n\nShe was evacuated during the war: in April 1942 Pacific Islands Monthly listed her among the evacuees as Mrs A. Brazier and three children, care of her mother in Doncaster Street, Hendra. Later in the war she kept the family home at Rose Bay, and she spent six years in Sydney before going back.\n\nShe sailed home from New Guinea on the Bulolo, reaching Brisbane on 2 January 1951, but was soon back: in February 1956, visiting her mother at Hendra with her elder daughter Margaret, she told the Telegraph that Port Moresby, with its frock shops and beauty salon, was now a nice place to live. Her son Bruce, 14, was boarding at Churchie, and her married daughter, Mrs E. D. Hymus, lived in Cairns with her husband and two children.\n\nShe died in 1992, late of Holland Park, Brisbane, aged 89, and was buried at Nundah Cemetery on 31 July 1992, in the grave of her mother Adele and beside her father Frank.\n\nShe was the only child of Frank Warbrick, a Brisbane accountant and fire insurance adjuster, and Adele White. Her father died suddenly at Toowoomba on 14 May 1916, eleven days before her thirteenth birthday. He had been brought to Queensland as an orphaned infant in 1874; her middle name, Lucy, was also the name of his elder sister.","src":[["The Telegraph (Brisbane), 4 April 1932: Life in Mandated Territory","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article184040160"],["The Telegraph (Brisbane), 26 March 1932: Social notes","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article180051740"],["Brazier–Warbrick wedding, Queensland Figaro, 7 Dec 1929","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/84907722"],["Evacuees from NG and Papua, PIM, 17 Apr 1942","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-329097613"],["Bon Voyage Tea, Telegraph, 19 May 1930","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/184441285"],["Port Moresby Has City Comforts, Telegraph, 11 Feb 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/293831648"],["Passenger arrival, Bulolo 1951, NAA BP26/1","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/"],["Brisbane City Council memorial search","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31754732"],["Birth, Qld 1903/B/6848","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/392194af469cc1abdb3d739e32c556e4a92469170ce509511540dea4f76896c9"],["Personalities (death of Frank Warbrick), The Queenslander, 20 May 1916","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23605591"]]},

tjaardj:{"n":"Tjaard Jakob Broesder","d":"1871–1948","b":1871,"x":1948,"s":"b","p":["jakob","epke"],"r":["nl"],"img":"img/groningen.jpg","ch":"groningen","t":"A baker in Nieuwolda for nearly 20 years, father of at least nine. By 1922 he ran Café \"Dennenbosch\" in Zuidlaren, known locally as Café Broesder. He was still keeping it when he died there in 1948.\n\nHe was born at Nieuwolda on 13 October 1871, the son of a farm labourer. He was 24 and already a baker when he married Antje Dijk at Zuidbroek on 16 May 1896. Birth records at Nieuwolda from 1897 to 1914 give his trade as baker. The children were Epke (1897), Roelfien Antje (1899), Antje (1900), Jakob Tjaart (1902), Augustinus (1904), Elena (1905), a second Elena (1906), Emkelenus Hendrik (1909) and Harm (1914). The first Elena died on 14 July 1905, four months old, and the next daughter was given her name. At his daughters' weddings in 1928 and 1929 he is a café keeper (caféhouder) in Zuidlaren, and at his son's wedding in 1935 he is called a hotel keeper (hotelhouder). He died at Zuidlaren on 25 June 1948, aged 76, still recorded as a café keeper.\n\nHe had an elder sister, Elena, born at Nieuwolda on 3 November 1866. She married the pork butcher Harm Apotheker at Appingedam in 1894 and lived at Loppersum, where she was recorded as a shopkeeper (winkelierster) after his death in 1922. She died at Oudenrijn, near Utrecht, on 26 March 1952, aged 85. On his wife's side, Antje's sister Emkelina Dijk married the Nieuwolda shoemaker Hindrik Harhuis in 1898. The son born to Tjaard and Antje in 1909 was given the names Emkelenus Hendrik, the male form of his aunt's name and that of her husband.","src":[["Marriage record, 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c7bca28f-7f9c-d092-4384-d78f8533ef12"],["Birth of Tjaard Broesder, Nieuwolda 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7b8f4741-fc89-7c7e-59ed-cb1a0fc650c0"],["Death of Elena Broesder, 4 months, Nieuwolda 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a1186e0f-754b-3ffe-e1d5-4ad9a5fea60c"],["Birth of Harm Broesder, Nieuwolda 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0a128e00-2a59-dfba-c887-213e063b73f0"],["Marriage of Elena Broesder, Zuidlaren 1929 (father café keeper)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:84d8d23d-cc4d-4579-84a6-78f8b5a0e7cf"],["Marriage of Emkelenus Broesder, Zuidlaren 1935 (father hotel keeper)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:4aff0cbd-ea70-72bf-6e4c-d31e33df0ebf"],["Death of Tjaard Broesder, Zuidlaren 1948","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:97c98bb0-eff6-4c20-9230-cc40ff672bbc"],["Birth of Elena Broesder, Nieuwolda 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e439936-805a-76fc-f613-c93841f78e61"],["Death of Elena Broesder, Oudenrijn 1952","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:DCD107A9-DD35-4506-9758-A5D70FB6AA2E"],["Marriage of Emkelina Dijk and Hindrik Harhuis, Nieuwolda 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb587ed6-e60e-58e0-fcb2-a146a764f81c"],["Marriage of Frans Apotheker, Termunten 1923 (mother a shopkeeper)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f954389a-d2f1-69e0-a5b9-a7b5f363d5d1"]]},
antje:{"n":"Antje Dijk","d":"1865–1941 · Hoogkerk","b":1865,"x":1941,"s":"b","p":["augustinus","antjeh"],"r":["nl"],"ch":"groningen","t":"Born on 10 March 1865 at Hoogkerk, west of the city of Groningen, where her father, the blacksmith Augustinus Dijk, had his forge at Vierverlaten on the Hoendiep. She was the fifth of six children of Augustinus and Antje Hundt. Her sister Grietje died there at 19 when Antje was fourteen. Her brother Aaldrik became a smith like their father.\n\nBy August 1893 her parents had moved to Zuidbroek, where her sister Roelfie kept a shop. Her father died there in August 1893 and her mother in October 1894. Antje was 31 when she married the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder, six years her junior, at Zuidbroek on 16 May 1896. Her sister Emkelina married the Nieuwolda shoemaker Hindrik Harhuis two years later.\n\nShe had nine children at Nieuwolda between 1897 and 1914, the last when she was 48, and lost a daughter, Elena, at four months in 1905. The family later moved to Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where Tjaard kept a café. She died there on 13 April 1941, aged 76.","src":[["Marriage Broesder-Dijk, Zuidbroek 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c7bca28f-7f9c-d092-4384-d78f8533ef12"],["Death of Antje Dijk, Zuidlaren 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:07f62992-504a-b41e-1cda-5fc7b9a5ac13"],["Birth record, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4513be63-dde3-f935-c1ff-c7f0b6996fb4"],["Death of her father, Zuidbroek 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7380e0f-6492-ecdf-27c7-2532dc352414"],["Death of her mother, Zuidbroek 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fa21a877-d2ee-3a98-861d-fe25ec28c697"],["Marriage of Emkelina Dijk, Nieuwolda 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb587ed6-e60e-58e0-fcb2-a146a764f81c"],["Marriage of Roelfie Dijk, Zuidbroek 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3164b2cb-6286-c203-b7ae-76e01056737f"]]},
johannesd:{"n":"Johannes Degenhart","d":"b. 1875 · Amsterdam","b":1875,"s":"b","p":["hjjd","mariaklara"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 17 April 1875, the youngest known child of Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus Degenhart, a cabinet maker from Kampen, and Maria Klara Vreenegoor. A sister, Maria Elisabeth, and two brothers, Hendrikus (later a schoolteacher) and Petrus (a furniture maker), had been born in the city before him.\n\nHe became a diamond polisher (diamantslijper), the trade Amsterdam was famous for. On 27 July 1898, aged 23, he married Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann, 26, the Amsterdam-born daughter of a German seaman. He was still polishing diamonds in his fifties: the records of 1925 to 1928 call him a diamond polisher and the record of 1929 a diamond cleaver (diamantklover), the worker who splits a rough stone before it is cut.\n\nThe marriage records of his children name six of them: George Jacobus Hendricus, born in 1899, a teacher (married 1925); Johannes Maria Hendricus, born 25 September 1900, a window dresser (1927); Hendricus Johannes Jacobus, born 13 December 1903, the ship's engineer (1929); Elizabeth Bernardina Jacoba, born about 1906, who married a shop assistant (1928); Carolina Hermina Maria, born 26 December 1914, who married a ship's carpenter (1940); and Henricus Maria, born 11 April 1916, a printer (1941).\n\nTheir son Hendricus died on 24 November 1941 aboard the SS Groenlo, during the Second World War. The marriage records of 1940 and 1941 give Johannes no occupation. His own death is not in the Amsterdam indexes that are open to search.\n\nThe Amsterdam militia registers and the family card that the city kept for his household from 1893 show two more sons who did not marry in Amsterdam: Antonius Maria Herman, born on 10 March 1902, who later appears in the population register of Tilburg, and Bernardus Jacobus Laurentius, born on 10 February 1907. With them the couple had eight known children. The family card also gives Elizabeth Bernardina Jacoba's birth as 13 November 1905.","src":[["Marriage record, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:79F960AD-15A8-46A1-8169-5CC4C0FB825C"],["Open Archives: Degenhart records","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Degenhart&number_show=100"],["Marriage of George Degenhart, 1925","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7ACA777E-FD70-4329-9D9D-8104B98016BB"],["Marriage of Hendricus Degenhart, 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7807FA7A-94F4-4BD9-A382-FC69FC2C504A"],["Marriage of Henricus Maria Degenhart, 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:357A5825-F58F-489C-846F-79D92A40A711"],["Amsterdam militia register (born 17 April 1875)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:98533456-d066-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Amsterdam population register 1874-1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:cd09cb52-83c2-4ed9-9d77-70828c4253a1"],["Amsterdam family card","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:ae7fca82-3700-cbd2-51ba-4defc2d15ee5"],["Marriage of Johannes M. H. Degenhart, 1927","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:F35DE39E-72C4-427C-8A56-C0F7451A10BB"],["Marriage of Elizabeth Degenhart, 1928","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:172CF9C1-3335-448E-906A-EFC80F82FE0F"],["Marriage of Carolina Degenhart, 1940","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:AA7F8373-E088-4610-AD93-89D0615AD6BE"],["Militia register, Antonius Maria Herman Degenhart (b. 1902)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9853345a-06ea-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Tilburg population register, Antonius Maria Herman Degenhart","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:611a2049-f4c5-d03b-8bf1-699af088698f"],["Militia register, Bernardus Jacobus Laurentius Degenhart (b. 1907)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:98533458-bdf2-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Family card (gezinskaart) of J. Degenhart, b. 17-04-1875, Stadsarchief Amsterdam archive 5422 inv. 303","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Degenhart%20Gezinskaarten%22%7D"]]},
catharinab:{"n":"Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann","d":"b. 1871 · Amsterdam","b":1871,"s":"b","p":["georg","hendrina"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 28 September 1871, the daughter of Georg Bruckmann, a German seaman from Kassel, and Hendrina Holst, whose father came from Nordborg in Denmark. She had an older brother, George, born on 14 November 1863, and a younger sister, Carolina Maria, born about 1873.\n\nHer mother's family lived among sailors. Her grandmother had kept a bar and a seamen's lodging house, and her mother's four sisters had married seamen from Sweden, Denmark, Finland and Prussia.\n\nShe married the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart on 27 July 1898, aged 26. They had six known children between 1899 and 1916: George, Johannes, Hendricus, Elizabeth, Carolina and Henricus. Hendricus, a ship's engineer, was lost at sea in November 1941. Her sister Carolina married Herman Peters of Venlo in December 1898 and died a widow in Maastricht in 1955.\n\nShe shared her name with her father's sister. That Catharina Elisabeth Brückmann was born at Oberkaufungen near Kassel, married an Amsterdam confectioner in 1873, and died at Blaricum in 1920.","src":[["Marriage record, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:79F960AD-15A8-46A1-8169-5CC4C0FB825C"],["Open Archives: Bruckmann records","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Bruckmann"],["Death of Catharina Elisabeth Brückmann, Blaricum 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2FE10B7E-7DA4-4785-B041-8B55B317BD16"],["Amsterdam population register 1874-1893 (born 28 September 1871)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:86a1265a-c63d-40c8-88ee-e2cd7eb1d248"],["Brother George Bruckmann, born 14 November 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:d5f24ec0-16f2-4895-a19d-96ccab82d2ef"],["Marriage of sister Carolina, 14 December 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A9F5E46D-9217-45A5-ADA0-03B4B812F3E5"],["Death of sister Carolina, Maastricht, 1955","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhl:48c41c4f-a731-ba30-8837-82850924c8fd"]]},
josephus:{"n":"Josephus Johannes van den Berg","d":"1871–1927 · Tilburg","b":1871,"s":"b","p":["bernardus","gertruda"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg on 19 May 1871 and a shoemaker all his life. He married Maria de Kock at Tilburg on 8 April 1891, when both were 19. The family moved with the shoe trade: children were born at Tilburg (1892), Waalwijk (1893), Besoijen (1894, 1895), Meppel in Drenthe (1899) and Zaandam (1905), and by 1908 they were in Amsterdam, where his sons were shoemakers too.\n\nThe Rotterdam population register also lists the family, which adds Rotterdam to their moves. It lists him and his wife with eight of their children: Bernardus Josephus (born Tilburg, 28 January 1892), Franciscus (Waalwijk, 5 July 1893), Maria Geertruida (Besoijen, 14 October 1894), Maria Elisabeth (Besoijen, 19 December 1895), Johanna Maria Catharina (Meppel, 30 September 1899), Maria Catharina Louiza (Meppel, 5 June 1901), Wilhelmina Josephina (Meppel, 27 August 1902) and Petrus Anthonius (Zaandam, 3 October 1905). A last son, Josephus Johannes, was born in Amsterdam about 1913. Three of his sons were shoemakers like him, and a daughter married a shoemaker. He was 28 when he registered a daughter's birth at Meppel in 1899 and 30 at Maria's in 1901.\n\nHe was the thirteenth and last child of Bernardus Hubertus van den Berg and Maria Gertruda Hamers; his mother was 42 when he was born. Both his parents died at Tilburg in 1894, his mother in August and his father in October. The civil records follow him year by year. At Waalwijk in July 1893 he was a shoemaker (schoenmaker) of 22, and two Waalwijk shoemakers witnessed his son's birth. At Besoijen from 1894 to 1896 his wife's brothers-in-law Lourens van der Wiel and Floris van Eekert, both shoemakers, witnessed his daughters' births, and he in turn helped to register the deaths of three of their infant children. He was still a shoemaker at Meppel in 1899, 1901 and 1902, and the Zaandam population register lists the whole family after the move to North Holland.\n\nHis children's Amsterdam marriage records call him a shoemaker in 1914, 1923 and 1926. In 1929, 1938 and 1940 they give no occupation for him. His daughter Wilhelmina married in June 1929 and Maria in September 1929.\n\nThe Amsterdam person card of his wife records that their marriage ended with his death at Amsterdam on 10 September 1927, aged 56; his own card carries only a cross-reference to hers. The same card shows that the family had been registered in Amsterdam since 2 April 1908.","src":[["Marriage record, 1891","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:08da0c40-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Open Archives: van den Berg & de Kock records","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Maria+Catharina+Louisa+de+Kock"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth of Johanna Maria Catharina, Meppel 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:6392eb59-30d5-4095-8fbd-c38ee10a1dac"],["Birth of Maria Catharina Louiza, Meppel 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:8d4b5451-679c-4e75-a185-891322e97330"],["Marriage of son Franciscus, Amsterdam 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:0E2C8EC9-F187-4D84-A6E4-FAADC648B8E6"],["Marriage of son Josephus Johannes, Amsterdam 1940","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:8DF67C6A-3923-49A2-BA9D-F06AE1CD8C02"],["Birth record, Tilburg, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:57d894f6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Franciscus, Waalwijk, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:a3b485b4-e14b-ab5d-c4fc-cd128ed3aff3"],["Birth of daughter Maria Geertruida, Besoijen, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:9c565094-4811-e56b-952a-39667a25dca6"],["Death of nephew J. P. van der Wiel, Besoijen, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:8f31a7d8-9807-3f5a-dc71-d35cf11e6235"],["Death of niece M. W. van der Wiel, Besoijen, 1895","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:9b401fcd-80a7-dc6c-8673-a893310fb10d"],["Death of nephew J. J. van Eekert, Besoijen, 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:b7e77974-239b-dca8-c40c-3a55f9f20d5c"],["Birth of daughter Wilhelmina, Meppel, 1902","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:5ff80400-c884-401e-b1a7-1f151d7c05e9"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Waalwijk population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:815a5693-cbeb-1b2d-5c2e-b8f4d0760628"],["Amsterdam person card of his wife (Archiefkaarten, inv. 438)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/438"],["Amsterdam reference card for him (Archiefkaarten, inv. 57)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/57"],["Stadsarchief Amsterdam index search","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Josephus%20Johannes%20van%20den%20Berg%22%7D"]],"x":1927},
mariadk:{"n":"Maria Catharina Louisa de Kock","d":"1871–1940 · Besoijen","b":1871,"s":"b","p":["franciscus","johannavb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Besoijen, a shoemaking village in North Brabant, on 18 September 1871. Her father, who registered the birth, was then a toll collector (tolgaarder); he later kept an inn. She was the fifth of seven children of Franciscus de Kock and Johanna Maria van den Brekel, and the second born after the family left Tilburg. Her mother died in January 1884, when she was 12, and her father in December 1889, when she was 18.\n\nSixteen months later, on 8 April 1891, she married the shoemaker Josephus van den Berg at Tilburg; both were 19. She followed his work across the country, from Brabant to Meppel in Drenthe, then Zaandam and Amsterdam, raising nine children along the way. At Besoijen in the mid-1890s her sisters Antonetta and Elisabeth, both married to shoemakers, were living in the village too.\n\nThe records name nine children, born between 1892 and about 1913 in Tilburg, Waalwijk, Besoijen, Meppel, Zaandam and Amsterdam. The Rotterdam population register lists her there too, with her husband and eight of the children, and so do the registers of Waalwijk and Zaandam. Eight of her children married in Amsterdam between 1914 and 1940, and each marriage record names her as the mother.\n\nHer Amsterdam person card, kept by the city from 1939, records her birth at Besoijen on 18 September 1871, her occupation as none (zonder), and her death at Amsterdam on 15 September 1940, three days before her 69th birthday. She had been widowed on 10 September 1927. The card lists all nine children with the dates they left her household, most of them on marriage; Maria Geertruida left for Sloten, then a separate municipality on Amsterdam's western edge, on 21 June 1915, and Maria Elisabeth followed her there on 20 August 1917. The family had lived in Amsterdam since 2 April 1908. Her last child to leave home was Josephus, who married two months before her death.","src":[["Marriage record, 1891","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:08da0c40-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Open Archives: de Kock records","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Maria+Catharina+Louisa+de+Kock"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Population register of her parents' household, Besoijen","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:6a7fab1f-c9df-067f-1205-82cbfe147f75"],["Mother's death, Besoijen, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:7d9cdb80-c357-5a14-ad02-c362dacf8126"],["Father's death, Besoijen, 1889","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:0a08ff6f-95dd-2fef-027b-da74cd52f3e7"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Waalwijk population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:815a5693-cbeb-1b2d-5c2e-b8f4d0760628"],["Amsterdam person card (Archiefkaarten, inv. 438)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/438"],["Stadsarchief Amsterdam index search","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22de%20Kock%20Maria%20Catharina%22%7D"],["Birth record, Besoijen, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:0d2b5f37-cb86-8369-43c1-f07fdc237b81"]],"x":1940},
thomas2:{"n":"Thomas Frankish","d":"1863–1921 · Hull","b":1863,"x":1921,"s":"f","p":["virgilr","jane"],"r":["uk","au"],"img":"img/kalgoorlie-hannan-street-1901.jpg","ch":"frankish","t":"Born in Hull, the son of Virgil Robert Frankish and Jane Batler. He married Emily Wetherall in Sculcoates, Hull, in the last months of 1889, and their sons Jesse (1890), Robert (1892) and Richard (1895) were all born there.\n\nThey went out to Western Australia in the 1890s; the family tell that the WA Government Railways were bringing skilled steam engine drivers out from England. In April 1897 \"Mrs Frankish and three children\" landed at Albany from the mail steamer Orizaba. By 1901 Thomas was living at Northam, the railhead for the goldfields. In July 1902, when the railway employees of Northam celebrated winning the eight-hour day, he answered the toast, saying that what the men wanted was not 48 hours crammed into a few days but a true eight-hour day. Emily sat on the ladies' committee of the Northam Railway Institute. Their youngest son, Walter, was born at Northam in 1904.\n\nHe spent a quarter of a century with the State Railway Department, much of it in Perth, where the family lived at West Leederville and then 42 Farnley Street, Mount Lawley. Early in 1921 he was transferred to Kalgoorlie as sub-foreman at the locomotive sheds, and on his way to work one morning at the end of May he suffered a severe stroke. Emily came up from Perth to be with him; he lay unconscious for two weeks and died in the Kalgoorlie Government Hospital on 12 June 1921, aged 58. Probate of his estate, £270 10s 7d, went to Emily. For years afterwards she and Walter placed a notice in the paper on the anniversary: \"Until the day breaks.\"\n\nHis birth was registered in Hull in the spring of 1863. In the 1871 census he was eight, a schoolboy in Porter Street, the fifth of nine children and the elder son of Virgil Frankish, a brass finisher; his mother Jane worked as a pastry cook. He lost both parents in 1889, his mother early in the year and his father at its end, the same winter that he married Emily.","src":[["Stroke at the Kalgoorlie loco sheds, Kalgoorlie Miner, 8 Jun 1921","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/93237795"],["Death notice, Kalgoorlie Miner, 13 Jun 1921","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/93237957"],["Shipping intelligence (Orizaba), Albany Advertiser, 13 Apr 1897","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/69897828"],["Eight hours day celebrations in Northam, 1902","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/24749314"],["Railway Institute, Northam, 1902","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/211862712"],["Probates and Administrations, West Australian, 18 Jul 1921","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/27969254"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["WA Registry online index","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["Birth index, Hull, Jun quarter 1863, vol. 9d p. 201 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1871 census, Porter Street, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5fdcc38ff4040bf5bc2e3ec3"],["Death notices, Robert Walter Frankish, West Australian, 23 Feb 1923","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23888277"]]},
emily:{"n":"Emily Wetherall","d":"1863–1939 · Marston, Yorkshire","b":1863,"x":1939,"s":"f","p":["robertw","annie"],"r":["uk","au"],"ch":"frankish","t":"Born late in 1863 at Marston, near Tadcaster in Yorkshire, the farm country beside Marston Moor, where the great battle of 1644 was fought. Her father Robert Wetherall was a farmer there, and in the 1871 census the household at Marston held Robert, 37, and Anne, 32, with their children William Richard, Catherine, Stephen, Emily, Alfred and Jesse, and an old great-uncle, John Coates, a retired farmer. Her younger brother Walter, born about 1878, followed her to Western Australia.\n\nShe married Thomas Frankish in Hull in 1889 and gave every one of her sons the middle name Wetherall; her eldest was Jesse, the same name as one of her own little brothers. In April 1897 she landed at Albany with the three boys to join Thomas. In 1921 she went up to Kalgoorlie to nurse him after his stroke, and was widowed that June. She lived on at 42 Farnley Street, Mount Lawley, and died in Perth on 31 January 1939, aged 75. Her funeral notice recorded that she was born at Marston, Yorkshire, and had lived in Western Australia for 42 years. Dean Moore took the service at Karrakatta, with her four sons and her brother Walter among the mourners; Mona's father, E. C. (Charlie) Fisk, was among those present.\n\nHer brother Walter Wetherall enlisted in the AIF at Blackboy Hill, No. 4616, giving his birthplace as Hull and naming his sister, Emily Frankish, as next of kin.\n\nHer parents had married in the Tadcaster district early in 1857. In 1861 Marston Hall was farmed by John Coates, the uncle who lived with them ten years later. The family left Marston in the 1870s: the births of a Richard and a Walter Wetherall were registered in the York district in 1874 and 1877 (the birth index says York for Walter, his enlistment papers Hull), and by the mid-1880s they were in Hull, where her eldest brother William Richard married in 1884 and her brother Jesse, whose name she gave her own first son, died in 1886 aged 16. A Robert Wetherall of her father's age died in Hull at the end of 1897, a few months after she had landed in Western Australia.","src":[["1871 census, Marston (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a94476bf4040b568941898a/emily-wetherall-1871-yorkshire-west-riding-wetherby-1864-"],["Birth index, Bramham district, Dec quarter 1863 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["The Late Mrs. Emily Frankish, West Australian, 4 Feb 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/46298049"],["Death notice, West Australian, 1 Feb 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/46296828"],["NAA B2455, Wetherall Walter, No. 4616","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=8383014"],["Marriage index, Robert Wetherall and Anne Walton, Tadcaster, Mar quarter 1857, vol. 9c p. 621 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Marston Hall: John Coates (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906e859e9379091b16e54cd"],["WA death index: Emily Frankish, North Perth, 1939, aged 75, parents Robert Wetherall and Annie Walton; Walter Wetherall, Nedlands, 1957, aged 79, parents Robert Wetherall and Annie Walton","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["1881 census, Hull: Robert Weatherall, 48, born Healaugh, with ten children (Findmypast free transcript)","https://findmypast.ie/1881-census/robert-weatherall-0022361276"]]},
edwardf:{"n":"Edward Charles Fisk","sn":"Grandpa Fisk","d":"c. 1879–1954","b":1879,"x":1954,"s":"f","p":["johnwfisk","rebeccafisk"],"r":["au"],"t":"Known to the family as Grandpa Fisk, and as Charlie to his own people. He came from a big Adelaide family: the son of John William Fisk, a plumber and painter, and his first wife Elizabeth Margaret Roscorla, who died when he was about five, and a brother of William Fisk, the Glenelg chemist who became Mayor of Glenelg and a member of the South Australian Parliament. Like many South Australians he went west in the gold years. He married Matilda Anthony at St John's Anglican Church, Kalgoorlie, in November 1906; Mona and Edward were born there in 1907 and 1909. About 1910 they moved to Perth, where he worked at Foy's and then as a traveller for Beale's, the piano firm. In August 1918 Matilda divorced him in the Perth Divorce Court, and the case ran in the Daily News, the Kalgoorlie Miner and, under a headline of its own, in Truth. The family still remember how public it was. He had already been through the Bankruptcy Court once, in 1917, as a land agent, and offered to pay off his debts at £10 a month.\n\nThrough the 1920s and 1930s the newspapers follow an E. C. Fisk in the mining fields: pegging gold leases at Meekatharra and bringing a specimen from the new find to Geraldton in 1922, suing a partner in the Warden's Court in 1923, and in 1932–33 as managing director of the London Mining Options and Investment Company, taking options over leases at Comet Vale and Mount Jackson.\n\nIn January 1925 Edward Charles Fisk, commission agent, was bankrupt for the second time, which ties these mining ventures to him by name. He blamed the Oroya East gold mine: he had taken an option over it for £4,000, paying the owners £1, then floated a company to take it over. His assets were a 41 lb gold specimen he valued at £177, 2,000 Oroya East shares then quoted at 4½d, and book debts. He owed £364 borrowed from eight people, bills for motor hire and petrol, two hotelkeepers, and £42 to the High School for one of his boys' schooling. Since the divorce he had paid Matilda £2 a week under a consent order; he had married again, and was earning £4 a week. His second wife was Marjorie Catherine Moyle, whom he had married in Perth in 1919; her daughter Jean became his stepdaughter.\n\nHe was known as Charlie, and his death was registered as Edward Charlie Fisk, son of John William Fisk and Rebecca Roscorla, which runs his mother's maiden name together with his grandmother's first name. He died at Royal Perth Hospital on 7 June 1954, aged 75, living in Canning Highway, Attadale, and late of Como. His death notice named him the loving father of Mona (Mrs W. Frankish, Victoria) and Ted (Queensland), and he was buried in the Presbyterian section at Karrakatta on 10 June. Marjorie lived until 1985.\n\nThe family remember him dealing with the Mines Department, forever advertising for a partner with £100 for a new venture, and seeing the future in mineral sands before anyone else, with a fortune waiting if he could only have got started.","src":[["Death notice of his brother Jack, West Australian, 6 Jun 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/46397020"],["Death notice, The West Australian, 9 Jun 1954","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/52947871"],["Funeral notice, The West Australian, 10 Jun 1954","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/52948077"],["Oroya East Flotation, Kalgoorlie Miner, 16 Jan 1925","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/93836856"],["Twice in Eight Years, Daily Mail (Brisbane), 15 Jan 1925","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/218336558"],["Bankruptcy Court, The West Australian, 5 Jul 1917","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/27304080"],["Fisk v. Fisk, Kalgoorlie Miner, 16 Aug 1918","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/92831023"],["Fisk v. Fisk, Truth (Perth), 24 Aug 1918","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/207376959"],["A straying husband, Daily News, 15 Aug 1918","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/81363621"],["Mt Jackson area, options secured, 1933","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22E.%20C.%20Fisk%22%20London%20Mining%20Options"],["WA Registry online index","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"]]},
johnwfisk:{"n":"John William Fisk","d":"1847–1932 · Saxmundham, Suffolk","s":"f","p":["williamfisk","rebeccasr"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Grandpa Fisk's father, and the only son of William Fisk, an Adelaide plumber. He married Elizabeth Margaret Roscorla, second daughter of the late Harry Munro Roscorla of Gilles Street, at St Paul's Church, Adelaide, on 23 July 1870. Their first son, William, was born in Flinders Street on 17 July 1871, followed by Nellie (born 1873), Jack (about 1876), Charlie (about 1879) and others. In the 1870s he was a plumber in Carrington Street, building and selling cottages around the east end of the city and Parkside; from about 1878 he was a painter in Pulteney Street, where his father's firm, W. Fisk & Son, painters, plumbers and gas and water fitters, had its premises. In 1884 his business failed and he assigned his estate to his creditors, who were paid nine shillings in the pound.\n\nThat same year, on 7 February 1884, Elizabeth died at St Ann's Place, Parkside, aged 38, leaving him with young children. On 8 April 1885 he married Eva Jane Bates, eldest daughter of the late James W. Bates, at her mother's house at Currency Creek. Eva Jane had a second family with him, among them Elsie, Eva, Fred (killed in the Great War), Tom, Gertie, Olive, Harold and James Watkins (Jim), the youngest; so several of Charlie's brothers and sisters were half-brothers and half-sisters. In April 1890 Elizabeth's brother, Harry Munro Roscorla the younger, died at John William's house in Pulteney Street.\n\nThe family later lived in Third Avenue, St Peters, where Eva Jane died on 20 March 1908, aged 52; she was buried at Payneham. He was still living there in retirement in 1922, and by January 1928 he had moved to Kenthurst, outside Sydney, where some of the younger children had settled. The death notice of his son Jack in 1939 names Jack's brothers and sisters as William, Nellie, Bessie, Charley (Perth), Bert, Elsie, Eva, Fred, Tom, Jim, Harold and Gertie. William, the eldest, was the Glenelg chemist known as \"the Village Apothecary\", Mayor of Glenelg from 1931 to 1938 and member for Glenelg in the House of Assembly until his death in 1940; Jack served 48 years in the Postal Department and was postmaster at Clare; Nellie married George H. Carr, a Queensland Land Commissioner, and died at Wynnum in 1928.\n\nThe Western Australian registration of Charlie's death in 1954 names his mother as Rebecca, with the maiden name Roscorla. Rebecca was in fact John William's mother; Elizabeth Margaret Roscorla was Charlie's.\n\nHis obituary said he came out to South Australia from Framlingham, Suffolk, with his parents in 1854. As a young man he was a well-known athlete, and he kept his strength all his life. He was a keen rifle shot: on his 72nd birthday he surprised the East Torrens Rifle Club by scoring 103 out of a possible 105.\n\nHe spent his last ten years in New South Wales, visiting his children in the other states from time to time. At Christmas 1922 he gave the children of Annangrove a Christmas tree, which they enjoyed so much that the parents' association made it a yearly event. He died on 28 January 1932 at the home of his daughter, Mrs S. B. Harvey, on Rouse Hill Road, Kenthurst; the Sydney death notice gave that date and his age as 85, and the Adelaide obituaries gave the date as 29 January. They said he left 49 grandchildren and thirteen great-grandchildren; among his surviving sons they named William, Mayor of Glenelg, John, postmaster at Pinnaroo, and Charlie, in Perth.\n\nThe State Library of South Australia holds ambrotype portraits of him, his parents and his sister, taken about 1863, and its catalogue gives his birth as 28 June 1847 at Saxmundham, Suffolk. The English birth index agrees: John William Fisk was registered in the Plomesgate district, which took in both Saxmundham and Framlingham, in the September quarter of 1847. That makes him 84, not 85, when he died, and seven when the family landed at Port Adelaide from the barque Historia in July 1854. The same catalogue note says he finished his plumbing apprenticeship in 1865, went back to England in the Orient, and returned in the Caesar Godeffroy in September 1867. It counts seven children born to Elizabeth between 1871 and 1882 and seven to Eva Jane between 1886 and 1896.","src":[["Fisk–Roscorla marriage, South Australian Register, 8 Aug 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/196733150"],["Birth of a son (William), 17 Jul 1871","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/197658215"],["Assigned estate of John William Fisk, painter, 1884","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/160097156"],["Death of Elizabeth Margaret Fisk, Evening Journal, 8 Feb 1884","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/197790136"],["Fisk–Bates marriage, Evening Journal, 15 Apr 1885","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/198393840"],["Death of Harry Munro Roscorla at J. W. Fisk's, Advertiser, 29 Apr 1890","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/73202384"],["Death of Eva Jane Fisk, Register, 21 Mar 1908","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/56463028"],["Mr J. W. Fisk living in retirement, Register, 18 Mar 1922","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/63580250"],["Death of Nellie Carr, Register, 10 Jan 1928","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/55060921"],["Death notice of Jack Fisk, West Australian, 6 Jun 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/46397020"],["Mr J. Fisk, Chronicle, 8 Jun 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/92430544"],["Death of Mr. W. Fisk, M.P., Advertiser, 19 Dec 1940","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/74460868"],["Death of Jim Fisk, Chronicle, 1 Aug 1963","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/298465681"],["Death notice, Sydney Morning Herald, 29 Jan 1932","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article16837376"],["Obituary, Advertiser, 8 Feb 1932","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article46839009"],["Obituary, Glenelg Guardian, 10 Feb 1932","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article215209079"],["Annangrove Christmas tree, Windsor and Richmond Gazette, 30 Nov 1923","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article85869274"],["William and Rebecca Fisk and their children, portraits c. 1863, State Library of South Australia B 29370","https://collections.slsa.sa.gov.au/resource/B+29370"],["GRO birth index, John William Fisk, Plomesgate, September quarter 1847, vol. 12 p. 349 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Arrival of the Historia, South Australian Register, 28 Jul 1854","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article49202323"],["Notes on William Thurlow, Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/notes/not0197.html"]],"b":1847,"x":1932},
rebeccafisk:{"n":"Elizabeth Margaret Roscorla","d":"c. 1846–1884 · Adelaide","x":1884,"s":"f","p":["harryroscorla","sarahroscorla"],"r":["au"],"t":"Grandpa Fisk's mother. She was the second daughter of Harry Munro Roscorla and Sarah Ann, who had come to South Australia in about 1838, and grew up in Gilles Street, Adelaide, where her father died of consumption when she was about thirteen. She married John William Fisk, the only son of William Fisk, plumber, at St Paul's Church, Adelaide, on 23 July 1870. Their children included William (born 1871), Nellie (1873), Jack (about 1876) and Charlie (about 1879).\n\nShe died at St Ann's Place, Parkside, on 7 February 1884, aged 38, when Charlie was about five. Her son Jack's obituary in 1939 remembered him as a grandson of the old pioneer Harry Munro Roscorla. Seventy years after her death, Charlie's death registration in Perth gave his mother's maiden name, Roscorla, correctly, but her first name as Rebecca, which was his grandmother's.\n\nThe State Library of South Australia's note on the Fisk family portraits counts seven children born to her and John William between 1871 and 1882. Her brothers and sisters included Sarah Ann, who married Thomas Tidswell in 1865, Harry Munro the younger, Charles Perry, a law clerk, and Bertram.","src":[["Fisk–Roscorla marriage, South Australian Register, 8 Aug 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/196733150"],["Death notice, Evening Journal, 8 Feb 1884","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/197790136"],["Mr J. Fisk, Chronicle, 8 Jun 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/92430544"],["WA Registry online index","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["William and Rebecca Fisk and their children, State Library of South Australia B 29370","https://collections.slsa.sa.gov.au/resource/B+29370"]],"b":1846},
williamfisk:{"n":"William Fisk","d":"c. 1822–1898 · Framlingham, Suffolk","s":"f","p":["fkwilliamfisksr","fkfannybridges"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Grandpa Fisk's grandfather. He came from Framlingham, a market town in east Suffolk, and married Rebecca Thurlow there by licence on 5 February 1845. The register entry, as copied out by a Thurlow family researcher, calls him a glazier of that parish and names his father as William Fisk, plumber. A descendant's notes on the same site give his birth as 3 April 1822 and his mother as Fanny Bridges; no register seen here confirms that, so his parents are shown as unproven. Plumbing and glazing went together then: the plumber worked in lead, on roofs, gutters and pipes, and leaded and set window glass as well.\n\nHannah Maria was born at Framlingham on 31 December 1845 and John William at Saxmundham, Rebecca's home town, on 28 June 1847. On 22 April 1854 the four of them sailed from Plymouth in the barque Historia, 443 tons. She reached Port Adelaide on Thursday 27 July with 195 passengers, who praised Captain Mowat's kindness and seamanship and blamed the agents in England for provisioning the ship badly. The State Library of South Australia's note on the family gives the arrival as 31 July.\n\nIn Adelaide he worked as a plumber from the 1850s. A Mr Fisk had a shop in Freeman Street by 1854, and William Fisk's name is on many civic requisitions from then on. His firm, W. Fisk & Son, painters, plumbers and gas and water fitters, was in Pulteney Street. The State Library also calls him a grazier, and holds ambrotype portraits of all four of the family taken about 1863. John William was his only son. His only daughter, Hannah Maria, married Philip Trelore Hill at St Paul's, Adelaide, on 21 December 1869, had six children and died on 16 October 1890, aged 44.\n\nRebecca's brother William Thurlow died at the Pulteney Street house on 30 May 1874, aged 52, and Rebecca died there on 26 March 1880, aged 63. William lived another eighteen years. He died at his home in Alfred Street, Parkside, on 14 February 1898, aged 76, and was buried at West Terrace Cemetery the next day.","src":[["Hill–Fisk marriage, Adelaide Observer, 1 Jan 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/158940341"],["Fisk–Roscorla marriage, 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/196733150"],["Death of William Thurlow, Register, 1 Jun 1874","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/39816933"],["W. Fisk & Son, Pulteney Street, Register, 1881","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47104792"],["Obituary of J. W. Fisk, Advertiser, 8 Feb 1932","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article46839009"],["William and Rebecca Fisk and their children, portraits c. 1863, State Library of South Australia B 29370","https://collections.slsa.sa.gov.au/resource/B+29370"],["Fisk and Thurlow marriage entry, Framlingham, 5 Feb 1845, transcribed on the Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0001/f0000107.html#I04907"],["GRO marriage index, William Fisk and Rebecca Thurlow, Plomesgate, March quarter 1845, vol. 12 p. 515 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Arrival of the Historia, South Australian Register, 28 Jul 1854","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article49202323"],["Historia passenger list (Mr and Mrs Fisk and two children), SA shipping lists, late 1854","https://localwiki.org/adelaide-hills/Overseas_Arrivals_to_South_Australia_-_late_1854"],["Death notice, Evening Journal, 15 Feb 1898","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article199926079"],["Funeral notice, Evening Journal, 15 Feb 1898","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article199926058"]],"b":1822,"x":1898,"u":true},
rebeccasr:{"n":"Rebecca Thurlow","d":"c. 1815–1880 · Saxmundham, Suffolk","x":1880,"s":"f","p":["fkjohnthurlow","fkrebeccawells"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Saxmundham, Suffolk, and baptised there in 1815, the elder daughter of John Thurlow, stonemason and builder, and Rebecca Wells. Her brother Thomas became a sculptor, whose busts and monuments are in the churches at Aldeburgh, Yoxford, Kelsale and Leiston, and her brother Henry a builder.\n\nShe married William Fisk, a glazier, by licence at Framlingham, her mother's home town, on 5 February 1845. The entry names her father as John Thurlow, stone mason, and the marriage is in the civil index for the Plomesgate district that quarter. Hannah Maria was born at Framlingham on 31 December 1845 and John William at Saxmundham on 28 June 1847. Her father died in 1850. In April 1854 she sailed from Plymouth with William and the two children in the barque Historia, leaving her widowed mother at Saxmundham, and landed at Port Adelaide that July.\n\nThe family lived in Pulteney Street, Adelaide, where William had his plumbing business. A portrait of her taken about 1863 is in the State Library of South Australia. Her brother William Thurlow, a bricklayer and plasterer, also came out to the colony; a descendant's note on the Thurlow family site says he lived with her family and went back to England in 1863 with his nephew John William; the State Library of South Australia dates John William's voyage to England after his apprenticeship ended in 1865. He died at her house on 30 May 1874, aged 52. She died at home on 26 March 1880. The death notice gave her age as 63; a baptism in 1815 makes her 64 or 65.\n\nHer name lived on in the family: when her grandson Charlie Fisk died in Perth in 1954, his mother was registered, wrongly, as Rebecca.","src":[["Death notice, Adelaide Observer, 27 Mar 1880","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/160130659"],["Death notice, South Australian Register, 27 Mar 1880","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/43103013"],["Rebecca Fisk, nee Thurlow, portrait c. 1863, State Library of South Australia B 29370","https://collections.slsa.sa.gov.au/resource/B+29370"],["Family of John Thurlow and Rebecca Wells of Saxmundham, with register, census and gravestone transcripts (Thurlow family site)","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0000/f0000061.html"],["Fisk and Thurlow marriage entry, Framlingham, 5 Feb 1845, transcribed on the Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0001/f0000107.html#I04907"],["GRO marriage index, Rebecca Thurlow, Plomesgate, March quarter 1845, vol. 12 p. 515 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of William Thurlow at W. Fisk's, Register, 1 Jun 1874","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article39816933"],["Thomas Thurlow, sculptor (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Thurlow_(sculptor)"],["Arrival of the Historia, South Australian Register, 28 Jul 1854","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article49202323"],["Notes on William Thurlow, Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/notes/not0197.html"]],"b":1815},
harryroscorla:{"n":"Harry Munro Roscorla","d":"1814–1859 · Lewes, Sussex","x":1859,"b":1814,"s":"f","p":["henryroscorla","annroscorla"],"r":["au"],"t":"Grandpa Fisk's grandfather on his mother's side. He was baptised at St Thomas in the Cliffe, Lewes, Sussex, on 18 September 1814, the son of Henry Roscorla, schoolmaster, and his wife Ann. Roscorla is a Cornish name. His father died when he was seven. By 1838 he was a carpenter living at 11 Church Street, Brighton, and on 3 June that year he married Sarah Ann Hindmarsh, a seamstress of 45 Suffolk Place, at St Nicholas, Brighton. Within months they had sailed for the new colony of South Australia, where his grandson Jack's obituary dated his arrival to 1839.\n\nIn 1842 he held a licence for a store at Tusmore, just east of Adelaide, and he was on the roll as a freeholder in Gilles Street, in the city's south-east corner, where the family settled; in 1850 the ratepayers' complaints describe his three-roomed house there, \"neatly done up\". In 1855 his wife gave evidence at the inquest of a neighbour who had burned to death after borrowing a light from her. He died at home in Gilles Street on 3 September 1859, aged 45, after twelve months of consumption. His widow wrote to the City Council that year asking for her rates to be let off, explaining that her husband was dead and illness had struck her numerous family.\n\nTheir children included Sarah Ann (who married Thomas Tidswell in 1865), Elizabeth Margaret (Charlie Fisk's mother), Harry Munro the younger (who died in 1890 aged 46, at John William Fisk's house), Charles P. Roscorla, who stayed in Gilles Street, and Bertram, named after his Hindmarsh grandfather.\n\nThe Lewes registers do not say where his father came from. The one Henry of the surname found in Cornish baptism transcripts for the right years was the son of John Rescorla and Mary Mureau, a name also written Muro and Murow. If he was the schoolmaster, Harry's middle name, Munro, echoes his grandmother's maiden name; that link is unproven. The ship that brought Harry and Sarah Ann out has not been identified: their name is not in the compiled passenger lists for arrivals in 1838 and the first half of 1839.\n\nSarah Ann, the eldest daughter, married Thomas Tidswell on 26 January 1865, when both were living in Gilles Street. Charles Perry Roscorla was a law clerk in Adelaide and went through the Insolvency Court in 1876; his eldest daughter, Winifred Mary, died in Gilles Street on 13 September 1874, aged six.","src":[["Licences, Southern Australian, 10 Jun 1842","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/71621499"],["Inquest, South Australian Register, 26 Feb 1855","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/207022214"],["Death notice, Adelaide Observer, 10 Sep 1859","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22Harry%20Munro%20Roscorla%22%20Lewes"],["Arrears of rates, South Australian Advertiser, 20 Dec 1859","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22Sarah%20Ann%20Roscorla%22%20rates"],["Mr J. Fisk, Chronicle, 8 Jun 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/92430544"],["Electoral roll, Gilles Street, Southern Australian, 4 Oct 1842","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/71622161"],["Rates in Gilles Street, South Australian, 26 Apr 1850","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/71626144"],["Baptism, St Thomas in the Cliffe, Lewes, 18 Sep 1814 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f847572f493fdbdbc46f866/harry-munro-roscorla-baptism-sussex-lewes-1814-09-18"],["Marriage, St Nicholas, Brighton, 3 Jun 1838 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ead1b45f493fd4207379c12/sarah-ann-hindmarsh-harry-munro-roscorla-marriage-sussex-brighton-1838-06-03"],["Death notice, Adelaide Observer, 10 Sep 1859","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article158129668"],["Tidswell and Roscorla marriage, Adelaide Express, 30 Jan 1865","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article207599112"],["Charles Perry Roscorla, law clerk, adjudication, Register, 24 Apr 1876","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article43003891"],["Death of Winifred Mary Roscorla, Advertiser, 15 Sep 1874","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article28734101"],["SA shipping lists, arrivals of 1838","https://localwiki.org/adelaide-hills/Overseas_Arrivals_to_South_Australia_-_1838"],["Marriage of Charles Augustus Perry and Mary Ann Roscorla, Rochester, 1 Feb 1836 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e388c7df493fd72f279bf5b/charles-augustus-perry-mary-ann-roscorla-marriage-kent-rochester-1836-02-01"],["Death of Mary Ann Perry, South Australian Register, 7 Aug 1876","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article43012425"],["Resident Magistrate’s Court, O’Leary v. Duell, Adelaide Observer, 1 Aug 1846","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article158922940"]]},
sarahroscorla:{"n":"Sarah Ann Hindmarsh","d":"c. 1815–1877","x":1877,"b":1815,"s":"f","p":["bertramh",null],"r":["au"],"t":"Charlie Fisk's grandmother on his mother's side. She was the daughter of Bertram Hindmarsh, an accountant, and was working as a seamstress, living at 45 Suffolk Place, Brighton, when she married Harry Munro Roscorla, a carpenter of 11 Church Street, on 3 June 1838. The wedding was at St Nicholas, the old parish church of Brighton. Both were of full age and both signed the register; John Newnham and Ann Cordery were the witnesses. Her age at death puts her birth about 1815, but no baptism has turned up in the transcribed registers of Sussex, London, Surrey or Kent.\n\nThey sailed for South Australia soon after the wedding, when the colony was barely two years old, and settled in Gilles Street, Adelaide. Their children included Sarah Ann, Elizabeth Margaret, Harry Munro, Charles Perry and Bertram, who was named after her father. In 1855 she gave evidence at the inquest on a neighbour who had burned to death after borrowing a light from her.\n\nHarry died of consumption in September 1859, leaving her a widow with a large family, and that year she wrote to the City Council asking to be let off her rates because of his death and illness among the children. Sarah Ann married Thomas Tidswell in 1865 and Elizabeth Margaret married John William Fisk in 1870. She died at her home in Regent Street, Adelaide, on 1 December 1877, aged 62, \"a colonist of 39 years\", after a long and painful illness.","src":[["Death notice, Evening Journal, 1 Dec 1877","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/197706561"],["Arrears of rates, 1859","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22Sarah%20Ann%20Roscorla%22%20rates"],["Marriage, St Nicholas, Brighton, 3 Jun 1838 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ead1b45f493fd4207379c12/sarah-ann-hindmarsh-harry-munro-roscorla-marriage-sussex-brighton-1838-06-03"],["Death notice, South Australian Register, 3 Dec 1877","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article40474844"],["Tidswell and Roscorla marriage, Adelaide Express, 30 Jan 1865","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article207599112"]]},
henryroscorla:{"n":"Henry Roscorla","d":"c. 1771–1821 · Lewes, Sussex","b":1771,"x":1821,"s":"f","p":["fkjohnrescorla","fkmarymureau"],"r":["uk"],"t":"A schoolmaster in the Cliffe, the part of Lewes, Sussex, that lies across the River Ouse from the old town. He married Charlotte Hoey by licence at St Thomas in the Cliffe on 29 July 1809; B. Ginn and Edward Egles were the witnesses, and all four signed. Their baby, Charlotte Mary, was baptised on 29 October 1811 and buried a fortnight later, and Charlotte herself was buried on 30 January 1813, aged 22.\n\nWith his second wife, Ann, he had Harry Munro (baptised 18 September 1814), Mary Ann (17 July 1816), Barrington (23 October 1818) and Elizabeth (16 July 1820); each entry calls him a schoolmaster of Cliffe. The register also has an infant Barrington Roscorla buried on 1 September 1818, seven weeks before that baptism, so there may have been two boys of the name. A Francis Roscorla of Cliffe, aged 54, was buried on 30 December 1818; the entry does not say how he was related. Henry was buried at St Thomas in the Cliffe on 11 October 1821, aged 50, and on 4 July 1822 an Ann Roscorla of the parish married Edward Carleton of Brighton. That was his widow: she was living with Edward Carleton in Brighton in 1851. Their daughter Mary Ann married Charles Augustus Perry at Rochester, Kent, in 1836, and went out to South Australia, where she died at Kensington, Adelaide, in 1876. Nearly forty years later his son's death notice in Adelaide still named him: \"son of the late Henry Roscorla, of Lewes, Sussex\".\n\nRoscorla is a Cornish name. The only Henry of the surname found in Cornish baptism transcripts for those years was baptised at St Breock, beside Wadebridge, on 29 April 1772, the son of John Rescorla and Mary, whose maiden name was Mureau, also written Muro. They are shown here as his parents, but the link is unproven. It rests on the rare name, on the dates and on the echo of Muro in his son's middle name, Munro. The dates do not fit exactly: his age at burial points to a birth in 1770 or 1771, while the St Breock baptism was in April 1772.","src":[["Death of Harry Munro Roscorla, Adelaide Observer, 10 Sep 1859","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22Harry%20Munro%20Roscorla%22%20Lewes"],["Marriage to Charlotte Hoey, Lewes, 29 Jul 1809 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5fb18a83f493fdb1be3cdcda/charlotte-hoey-henry-roscorla-marriage-sussex-lewes-1809-07-29"],["Burial of Charlotte Roscorla, 30 Jan 1813 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43c9dcf493fdea97dc9851/charlotte-roscorla-burial-sussex-lewes-1813-01-30"],["Baptism of Harry Munro Roscorla, 18 Sep 1814 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f847572f493fdbdbc46f866/harry-munro-roscorla-baptism-sussex-lewes-1814-09-18"],["Burial of Barrington Roscorla, infant, 1 Sep 1818 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43c9f2f493fdea97dc993b/barrington-roscorla-burial-sussex-lewes-1818-09-01"],["Baptism of Barrington Roscorla, 23 Oct 1818 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f84759bf493fdbdbc46fb5c/barrington-roscorla-baptism-sussex-lewes-1818-10-23"],["Burial of Francis Roscorla, aged 54, 30 Dec 1818 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43c9f3f493fdea97dc9956/francis-roscorla-burial-sussex-lewes-1818-12-30"],["Burial of Henry Roscorla, aged 50, 11 Oct 1821 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43ca05f493fdea97dc99ee/henry-roscorla-burial-sussex-lewes-1821-10-11"],["Baptism of Henry Rescorla, St Breock, 29 Apr 1772 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/baptisms/"],["Death of Harry Munro Roscorla, Adelaide Observer, 10 Sep 1859","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article158129668"],["Marriage of Charles Augustus Perry and Mary Ann Roscorla, Rochester, 1 Feb 1836 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e388c7df493fd72f279bf5b/charles-augustus-perry-mary-ann-roscorla-marriage-kent-rochester-1836-02-01"],["Death of Mary Ann Perry, South Australian Register, 7 Aug 1876","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article43012425"]],"u":true},
annroscorla:{"n":"Ann Roscorla","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Second wife of Henry Roscorla, schoolmaster of the Cliffe, Lewes, and mother of Harry Munro Roscorla. Her maiden name is not in the baptism entries of her children, and no entry for the marriage was found in the transcribed registers; it took place between the burial of Henry's first wife in January 1813 and the baptism of Harry Munro on 18 September 1814. Mary Ann followed in 1816, Barrington in 1818 and Elizabeth in 1820. The 1851 census gives her birthplace as Bletchingley, in Surrey, and her age as 53, which would make her only about sixteen at her first marriage; census ages for older women were often understated, so she may have been born a few years earlier.\n\nHenry was buried on 11 October 1821, when Harry was seven. On 4 July 1822 she married Edward Carleton of Brighthelmston, as Brighton was then written, by licence at St Thomas in the Cliffe; Frederick Collins and Eliza Piper were the witnesses, and all four signed. The Carletons stayed in the Cliffe at first. Their daughters Harriett (about 1824) and Isabella (about 1833) were born in Lewes, and two Carleton infants, Charles and Octavius, were buried at the Cliffe in May and July 1833.\n\nOn 1 February 1836 her daughter Mary Ann Roscorla married Charles Augustus Perry at St Nicholas, Rochester, Kent, and \"Anne Carelton\" signed as a witness. By 1838 Harry was a carpenter in Brighton, and in 1851 Ann was living in Eastern Road, Brighton, with Edward, then a builder's clerk of 54, born in London, their two daughters and several lodgers. An Edward Carleton married Mary Ann Hemsley at St Nicholas, Brighton, on 30 March 1857; if he was her husband, Ann had died by then.","src":[["Marriage of Ann Roscorla and Edward Carleton, Lewes, 4 Jul 1822 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f7df33ff493fd1468b74d72/ann-roscorla-edward-carleton-marriage-sussex-lewes-1822-07-04"],["Baptism of Mary Ann Roscorla, 17 Jul 1816 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f84757df493fdbdbc46f9bd/mary-ann-roscorla-baptism-sussex-lewes-1816-07-17"],["Baptism of Elizabeth Roscorla, 16 Jul 1820 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f8475a8f493fdbdbc46fd16/elizabeth-roscorla-baptism-sussex-lewes-1820-07-16"],["Burial of Henry Roscorla, 11 Oct 1821 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43ca05f493fdea97dc99ee/henry-roscorla-burial-sussex-lewes-1821-10-11"],["1851 census, Eastern Road, Brighton, Edward and Anne Carleton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/59a39b8ff4040be6b149639c"],["Marriage of Charles Augustus Perry and Mary Ann Roscorla, Rochester, 1 Feb 1836, witness Anne Carelton (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e388c7df493fd72f279bf5b/charles-augustus-perry-mary-ann-roscorla-marriage-kent-rochester-1836-02-01"],["Burial of Charles Carleton, infant, Cliffe, 24 May 1833 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43ca5af493fdea97dc9dbd"],["Burial of Octavius Carleton, infant, Cliffe, 20 Jul 1833 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f43ca5bf493fdea97dc9dc8"],["Marriage of Edward Carleton and Mary Ann Hemsley, Brighton, 30 Mar 1857 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6075140ff493fd2d524ec39d"]],"d":"b. c. 1798 · Bletchingley, Surrey","b":1798},
bertramh:{"n":"Bertram Hindmarsh","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"An accountant, named as the father of Sarah Ann Hindmarsh when she married Harry Munro Roscorla at St Nicholas, Brighton, on 3 June 1838. In the 1830s the word meant a man who kept the books of merchants and tradesmen. The entry does not say whether he was still living or where he worked, and Sarah Ann's baptism, about 1815, has not been found in the transcribed registers of Sussex, London, Surrey or Kent. Her son Bertram Roscorla, born in Adelaide, carried his name.\n\nBertram, or Bartram, is a north-country first name, and Hindmarsh is a Northumberland and Durham surname. A Bartram Hindmarsh, son of Matthew Hindmarsh, yeoman, was baptised at St Andrew's, Newcastle upon Tyne, on 5 August 1781. He is the right age to be Sarah Ann's father, but nothing found so far connects him with Brighton, and no record ties this family to Captain John Hindmarsh, the first Governor of South Australia.","src":[["Marriage of his daughter, St Nicholas, Brighton, 3 Jun 1838 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ead1b45f493fd4207379c12/sarah-ann-hindmarsh-harry-munro-roscorla-marriage-sussex-brighton-1838-06-03"],["Baptism of Bartram Hindmarsh, Newcastle upon Tyne, 5 Aug 1781 (FreeREG), a possible match only","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5818cdf4e93790ec8b32b378/bartram-hindmarsh-baptism-northumberland-newcastle-upon-tyne-1781-08-05"]]},
matilda:{"n":"Matilda Anthony","d":"1873–1935 · Sebastopol","b":1873,"x":1935,"s":"f","p":["herbert","mara"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Sebastopol, near Ballarat, Victoria, in 1873, the daughter of Herbert Anthony, a gold miner, and Mara James. She grew up at Stawell and lost her mother at 20 and her father at 24; her father's will made her and her brother Albert wait for their inheritance until a tombstone was raised over his grave. She married Edward Charles Fisk at Kalgoorlie in 1906; they divorced in 1918. On the night of 24 August 1935, at about 11.20, she was knocked down by a car in Hay Street near Harvest Terrace, West Perth, and she died of a fractured skull the next day, aged 62. At the inquest the coroner heard that she had been blind in her left eye, and found the evidence insufficient to say why the driver had not seen her.\n\nShe was the younger of two children: her brother Albert was born at Sebastopol in 1869. Her mother died at Stawell in 1893, aged 53, and her father on 26 December 1897.","src":[["Woman killed in Hay Street, West Australian, 26 Aug 1935","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/32904659"],["Inquest, West Australian, 24 Sep 1935","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/32916316"],["Will case, Age, 20 Jul 1901","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190029801"],["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["WA Registry online index","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["Will and probate of Herbert Anthony, miner, Stawell, died 26 Dec 1897 (PROV, VPRS 7591/P2, 68/107)","https://prov.vic.gov.au/archive/6622B417-F536-11E9-AE98-4D14E6289853"]]},
wgp:{"n":"William George Parkinson","sn":"Grandpa Parky","d":"1877–1960 · Greendale","b":1877,"x":1960,"s":"p","p":["georgewp","emilybrowell"],"r":["au","uk"],"img":"img/parkinson-aif-1915.jpg","t":"Known to the family as Grandpa Parky. Born at Greendale, Victoria, in 1877, the son of George William Parkinson and Emily Browell; his brothers and sisters, all registered at Greendale, were Claria (1875), Emelia Alice (1879), Valentine Christopher (1881), Frederick Arthur (1883) and Walter Edmund (1885). He married Martha Wilson in Victoria in 1899, and their children were all registered at Alexandra: Olive Amelia Martha (1900), Doris Maggie Rose (1902), Ivy Myrtle (1905), William Benjamin, known as Ben (1908, born at Thornton), and Donald Frederick George, known as Don (1910). On 13 July 1915 he enlisted in Melbourne, a married labourer giving his age as 36 (by his birth registration he was 37 or 38), and sailed on 18 October 1915 with the 10th Reinforcements for the 14th Battalion.\n\nBy August 1916 he was in the training camps on Salisbury Plain, in and out of hospital, with his eyesight among the complaints. On 26 July 1917 he walked out of camp at Codford, and a court of inquiry declared him an illegal absentee. He stayed away for two years, until 29 July 1919. A district court martial at Sutton Veny on 11 September 1919 found him guilty; his sentence of two years' detention was commuted to the loss of thirty days' pay, and he came home on the Devon and was discharged in December 1919.\n\nIn May 1917 Martha had moved with the boys to Hoddles Creek. The family remember that his desertion left her and the children without income; that he turned up again in the 1930s; and that when they visited Grandma, Ben and Don would barely acknowledge him. They also remember that he fought in France and was evacuated sick; the service file as it survives records his time in England. He died at Highett, Melbourne, in 1960, aged 83.\n\nHis death registration gives his birthplace as Blackwood, the goldfield a few miles from Greendale where his father's family had been since the late 1850s; the birth itself was registered at Greendale. His sister Clara married Maurice Fox in 1897 and his sister Amelia Alice married Henry Stephenson, and both raised large families at Footscray, where their parents spent their last years. His brother Walter died at Footscray in 1915, aged 29, and his brother Frederick lived there until 1972. In November 1909 the Alexandra Shire Council paid a W. Parkinson six shillings for day labour, which fits the labourer of his army papers. When his father died in May 1920 the death notice listed him as 'Will (Hoddle's Creek)', placing him there five months after his discharge.","src":[["Victorian BDM: birth 9311/1877; death 6967/1960","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["AIF service record, NAA B2455, PARKINSON WILLIAM GEORGE","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=8008374"],["Court martial, NAA A471, 11332","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=6859849"],["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Death notice of George William Parkinson, The Age, 22 May 1920","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article203714912"],["Alexandra Shire Council accounts, Alexandra and Yea Standard, 5 November 1909","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article57484959"]]},
georgewp:{"n":"George William Parkinson","d":"1838–1920 · Bristol","b":1838,"x":1920,"s":"p","p":["johnpark","maryanncole"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born in Bristol in the first weeks of 1838. The birth of a George William Parkinson was registered there between January and March, a few months after John Parkinson and Mary Ann Cole married in the same city in the summer of 1837. Those are the parents named in his Victorian death registration, and his age at death, 82 in May 1920, fits. Bristol was then the chief port of the west of England.\n\nThe family were in Victoria by 1854, when his brother James Henry was registered at Prahran; George was about sixteen. By 1859 they were on the Mount Blackwood goldfield, where his brother Edmond Albert (1859) and sister Alice Mary (1861) were registered. Gold had been found at Blackwood, in the ranges north of Ballan, at the end of 1854, and thousands of diggers rushed there the following year. George stayed in that district. He married Emily Browell in Victoria in 1874, and their six children were registered at Greendale, the farming settlement on the Ballan side of Blackwood: Clara (registered as Claria, 1875), William George (1877, Grandpa Parky), Amelia Alice (registered as Emelia Alice, 1879), Valentine Christopher (1881), Frederick Arthur (1883) and Walter Edmund (1885).\n\nGeorge and Emily spent their last years at Footscray, where their married daughters lived. Emily died there in 1914 and their youngest son Walter in 1915, aged 29. George died on 16 May 1920 at the Footscray home of his daughter Mrs Stephenson, aged 82, and was buried privately at Footscray two days later. The death notice called him 'late of Greendale and Blackwood' and listed his children: Clara (Mrs Fox, of Seddon), Will (Hoddle's Creek), Mrs Stephenson, Chris (Western Australia), Fred (Footscray) and the late Walter.","src":[["Victorian BDM: marriage 3645/1874; death 5861/1920","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["FreeBMD: marriage of John Parkinson and Mary Ann Cole, Bristol, Jul-Sep 1837 (vol. 11, p. 180); birth of George William Parkinson, Bristol, Jan-Mar 1838 (vol. 11, p. 171)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death notice of George William Parkinson, The Age, 22 May 1920","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article203714912"]]},
emilybrowell:{"n":"Emily Browell","d":"c. 1844–1914 · Shropshire","b":1844,"x":1914,"s":"p","p":["williambrowell","emilybmother"],"r":["au"],"t":"The daughter of William Browell and his wife Emily, as her death registration records. She was born in Shropshire in about 1844. In April 1861, aged 17, she was living in Cave Street in the parish of St Paul, Bristol, as the granddaughter of Jasper Browell, a 70-year-old annuitant (a person living on a fixed yearly income) born in Northumberland, his wife Mary Ann, and two unmarried aunts, Rosa Eliza and Ellen. Her age matches her death at 70 in 1914 exactly, and Bristol was also the birthplace of her future husband, so this census entry is very probably her, although no single document joins the two. No birth of an Emily Browell was registered in England and Wales in the 1840s, so her baptism is still to be found.\n\nShe came to Victoria on her own: 'Emily Browell, 22' is on the list of assisted immigrants who reached Melbourne on the Atalanta from London in July 1867, the only passenger of that surname. The passenger list describes her as English and a servant, and the immigration register heads the page 'Miss Rye's Selection': she was one of a party of single women chosen in England under the scheme run by Maria Rye, who organised the emigration of women to the colonies in the 1860s. The register shows she was engaged by an employer in Melbourne on 12 July 1867, within days of landing, at wages entered as £18.\n\nShe married George William Parkinson in 1874, when she was about thirty and he thirty-six, and raised their six children at Greendale, near Ballan: Clara (1875), William George (1877), Amelia Alice (1879), Valentine Christopher (1881), Frederick Arthur (1883) and Walter Edmund (1885). The registrars spelt her name Emily, Emley and Emely by turns.\n\nBy the 1900s her daughters Clara Fox and Amelia Stephenson were bringing up large families of their own at Footscray, and Emily and George spent their last years there. She died at Footscray in 1914, aged 70.","src":[["Victorian BDM: marriage 3645/1874; death 13972/1914","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Atalanta, July 1867: Emily Browell, 22 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 14A, p. 150)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3ABROWELL"],["FreeBMD: no birth of an Emily Browell 1838-1851","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Bristol St Paul, Cave Street: household of Jasper Browell, with granddaughter Emily Browell, 17, born Shropshire (FreeCEN, RG9/1718)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/690e8c61b18f7c9de27d4e29/emily-browell-1861-gloucestershire-bristol-st-pauls-1844-"],["Register of assisted immigrants, 'Miss Rye's Selection', ship Atalanta, July 1867: Browell, Emily, 22 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 14A, p. 150, image)","https://images.prov.vic.gov.au/loris/3001%2F6343%2F36%2Fimages%2F74%2Ffiles%2F00014-P0000-000020_074.jp2/full/1400,/0/default.jpg"],["Passenger list, Atalanta, master John G. Ballingall, July 1867: Browell, Emily, 22, servant, English (PROV VPRS 947, Jul-Dec 1867, p. 5, image)","https://images.prov.vic.gov.au/loris/2191%2F7903%2F05%2Fimages%2F117%2Ffiles%2F00947-P0000-000018-0030-116.jp2/full/1400,/0/default.jpg"]]},
johnpark:{"n":"John Parkinson","s":"p","p":[],"t":"Father of George William Parkinson, named in George's death registration in 1920. He married Mary Ann Cole in Bristol in the summer of 1837, in the first three months of civil registration in England, and their son George William was born there early in 1838.\n\nBy 1854 John and Mary Ann were in Victoria, where a son, James Henry, was registered at Prahran that year. Five years later they were on the Mount Blackwood goldfield: Edmond Albert was registered at Mount Blackwood in 1859 and Alice Mary at Blackwood in 1861. Blackwood was an alluvial field, worked along the Lerderderg River and the gullies running into it.\n\nNo passenger list has been matched to the family, who are not in the index of assisted immigrants, and no death entry for John has been identified in the Victorian index. His son George was still described as 'late of Greendale and Blackwood' when he died in 1920.\n\nLater searches added little in England. The marriage page in the index for Bristol, July to September 1837, holds four couples, among them John Parkinson and Mary Ann Cole, confirming the match. No household of John and Mary Ann Parkinson, or a George William born about 1838, appears in the transcribed 1841, 1851 and 1861 censuses of Gloucestershire on FreeCEN, though the Bristol returns for 1841 and 1851 are only partly transcribed there, and no Bristol baptism or marriage entry for the family is in the parish transcripts online. The family's crossing to Victoria before 1854 has not been found.","d":"Bristol, England","src":[["FreeBMD: marriage of John Parkinson and Mary Ann Cole, Bristol, Jul-Sep 1837 (vol. 11, p. 180); birth of George William Parkinson, Bristol, Jan-Mar 1838 (vol. 11, p. 171)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Victorian BDM index: births 2159/1854, 1862/1859, 7220/1861 (father John Parkinson, mother Mary Ann Cole); death of George William Parkinson 5861/1920","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["FreeCEN: Parkinson in Gloucestershire censuses","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_queries/new"]],"r":["uk","au"]},
maryanncole:{"n":"Mary Ann Cole","s":"p","p":[],"t":"Mother of George William Parkinson, named with her maiden name in his death registration in 1920. She married John Parkinson in Bristol between July and September 1837, and her first child, George William, was registered there early in 1838.\n\nFour children are known, born over 23 years. After George came James Henry, registered at Prahran in 1854, Edmond Albert, registered at Mount Blackwood in 1859, and Alice Mary, registered at Blackwood in 1861; each Victorian entry gives her maiden name as Cole. The sixteen years between George and James Henry cover the family's move from England to Victoria, and any children born in that interval have not been traced.\n\nHer youngest children were born on a goldfield only a few years old, in the ranges north of Ballan. No death entry for Mary Ann has been identified in the Victorian index.","d":"Bristol, England","src":[["FreeBMD: marriage of John Parkinson and Mary Ann Cole, Bristol, Jul-Sep 1837 (vol. 11, p. 180); birth of George William Parkinson, Bristol, Jan-Mar 1838 (vol. 11, p. 171)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Victorian BDM index: births 2159/1854, 1862/1859, 7220/1861 (mother Mary Ann Cole)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]],"r":["uk","au"]},
williambrowell:{"n":"William Browell","s":"p","p":["pmjasperbrowell",null],"t":"Father of Emily Browell, named in her death registration in 1914. He does not appear in Victorian records. His daughter came out alone in 1867, aged 22, and no William Browell of his generation is in the Victorian death index, which points to his having stayed in Britain. Emily was born about 1844, so he married in the early 1840s or before.\n\nIn the English civil registration indexes of the 1840s Browell is an uncommon surname, found mostly on Tyneside around Gateshead, Tynemouth and Sunderland, with a few entries in London and the West Country. Four William Browells married in England between 1839 and 1846, at Tewkesbury, Clifton, Gateshead and St Pancras. Which of these families, if any, was Emily's has not been established.\n\nThe 1861 census of Bristol points to his family. An Emily Browell, 17, born in Shropshire, was then living in Cave Street, St Paul's, as the granddaughter of Jasper Browell, a Northumberland-born annuitant of 70. Because she carried the Browell surname, her father was most probably one of Jasper's sons, and this tree links William to Jasper on that basis; the link is marked as uncertain. Jasper also had a daughter named Emily, unmarried and aged 25 in the 1841 census, so it is possible that the Browell blood came through Emily's mother instead.\n\nTwo of the four William Browell marriages of 1839 to 1846 can now be set aside. The 1839 marriage at Tewkesbury was to Caroline Barnes or Dorothy Cull (the two brides on that index page), and William Robert Browell, who married at Clifton in 1841, was a clergyman, rector of Beaumont in Essex in 1861, whose wife was the Swedish-born Emma Matilda. Neither bride was named Emily.","src":[["Victorian BDM index: death of Emily Parkinson 13972/1914 (father William Browell, mother Emily)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["FreeBMD: Browell births and marriages 1837-1848","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Bristol St Paul: Jasper Browell household with granddaughter Emily (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/690e8c61b18f7c9de27d4e29/emily-browell-1861-gloucestershire-bristol-st-pauls-1844-"],["1841 census, Horfield, Gloucestershire: Jasper Browell household (FreeCEN, HO107/346)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/68a13eadb18f7c42c506ecb9/jasper-browell-1841-gloucestershire-horfield-1791-"],["1861 census, Beaumont cum Moze, Essex: William Robert Browell, rector (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/66312ea5f493fd6e27cf5b04/william-robert-browell-1861-essex-beaumont-cum-moze-1807-"]],"u":true},
emilybmother:{"n":"Emily Browell","sn":"Emily (Mrs William Browell)","s":"p","p":[],"t":"Mother of Emily Browell. Her daughter's death registration at Footscray in 1914 gives her first name, Emily, but records her maiden name as unknown: whoever gave the details knew her only by her Christian name. A Victorian death certificate asks for the names of both parents and the mother's maiden surname, and for people born overseas those details depended on what the family in the colony could remember.\n\nShe gave her own name to her daughter, and it was carried on again by a great-granddaughter, Alice Emily Stephenson, registered at Carlton in 1895. Like her husband William she is not found in Victorian records; her daughter travelled out alone in 1867.\n\nThe 1841 census of Horfield, just north of Bristol, lists an unmarried Emily Browell, aged 25, in the household of Jasper Browell, the man recorded twenty years later as the grandfather of the emigrant Emily. Whether that Emily was the emigrant's aunt or her mother has not been established, and no marriage of a William Browell to an Emily has been found.","src":[["Victorian BDM index: death of Emily Parkinson 13972/1914 (mother Emily, maiden name unknown); birth of Alice Emily Stephenson 1820/1895","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["1841 census, Horfield, Gloucestershire: Jasper Browell household (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/68a13eadb18f7c42c506ecb9/jasper-browell-1841-gloucestershire-horfield-1791-"]]},
martha:{"n":"Martha Wilson","d":"1869–1949 · Diamond Creek","b":1869,"x":1949,"s":"p","p":["josephwil","sarahw"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Diamond Creek, north-east of Melbourne, in 1869, the second of the eight children of Joseph Wilson and Sarah Whatmough, and named after her grandmother Martha Burgess. Her birth entry gives her mother's maiden name as Hill, which was really her grandmother Whatmough's. She grew up in the family cottage on what is now Glen Park Road, among the orchards and small gold workings of the Diamond Creek valley, and was seventeen when her father died in 1887.\n\nShe was thirty when she married William George Parkinson, eight years her junior, in 1899. Their children were Olive (1900), Doris (1902), Ivy (1905), Ben (1908) and Don (1910), all registered at Alexandra; the family lived at Thornton, on the Goulburn River. In May 1917, with William George away at the war, she moved with the children to Hoddles Creek. Her mother's obituary in 1934 counts her among the children living far from Diamond Creek, and the probate index describes her as a married woman of Hoddles Creek.\n\nShe was the \"Grandma Parky\" of the family memoirs, who lived in a wattle-and-daub house with newspaper on the walls. She died at the Yarra Junction Bush Nursing Hospital in November 1949, aged 81, and was buried at Lilydale. Her death entry names her father as \"James Emmeth\" and her mother as \"Sarah Watmaugh\": James Emmett was in fact her younger brother, who had died at Diamond Creek earlier the same year.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["A Pioneer Passes: Mrs Sarah Wilson, Advertiser (Hurstbridge), 3 August 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article56745828"],["Probate index: Martha Parkinson, Hoddles Creek, married woman (PROV VPRS 28/P4, 590/674)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A28%20AND%20family_name%3AParkinson%20AND%20given_name%3AMartha"]]},
ern:{"n":"John Ernest Mark Smith","sn":"Ern Smith","d":"1875–1964 · Prahran","b":1875,"x":1964,"s":"p","p":["jhsmith","eliza"],"r":["au"],"ch":"yarra","t":"John Ernest Mark Smith, always called Ern, was born at Prahran, Melbourne, in 1875, the second of the nine children of Joseph Henry Smith, a brickmaker, and Eliza Ruth Warren. He worked all his life as a painter and paperhanger.\n\nOn 3 September 1901 he married Mabel Ann Webb, who had come from Adelaide with her family in 1887, at the Baptist Church, St Kilda. Her family did not approve of the match. They had five children, all born at Prahran or neighbouring Armadale: Mabel Ruth (1902), who died at five months; Ernest Joseph (1903); Elsie Alice (1905); Alma Beatrice (1907); and Doris Kate (1910), who died in 1913 aged three.\n\nAround 1911 Ern moved the family to Hoddles Creek in the Upper Yarra, where his parents had taken up land, and kept working in Melbourne during the week, coming home by train on Friday nights. The railway from Lilydale had been extended up the valley through Yarra Junction to Warburton in 1901, which made this possible. His daughters Elsie and Alma both married Parkinson brothers and settled at Gladysdale.\n\nMabel died in 1958. Ern died at Yarra Junction on 29 November 1964, aged 89.","src":[["Birth index, 1875 (reg. 25066/1875), Prahran","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
mabel:{"n":"Mabel Ann Webb","d":"1882–1958 · Adelaide","b":1882,"x":1958,"s":"p","p":["elliott","marthah"],"r":["au"],"img":"img/mabel-ann-webb-18.jpg","ch":"yarra","t":"Mabel Ann Webb was born in Adelaide on 22 June 1882, the daughter of Elliott Agar Webb and Martha Hicks. Her father had been the court bailiff at Laura and by that August was managing the Singer sewing machine company's business at Port Adelaide; her brother Charles was born about 1878, and her sisters Lucy and Gladys followed in about 1886 and 1889. The family came to Melbourne in 1887 and settled in Richmond, where her father became an insurance agent.\n\nOn 3 September 1901 she married Ern Smith, a painter and paperhanger from Prahran, at the Baptist Church, St Kilda. Her family disapproved of the marriage, and she never spoke to most of them again. When her father died in March 1928, his death notice named his children Charles, Lucy and Gladys, but not Mabel. Her brother Charles, a jeweller, made her a crescent-moon brooch, which is still in the family.\n\nMabel and Ern had five children: Mabel Ruth, who died in 1902 aged five months, Ernest Joseph (1903), Elsie Alice (1905), Alma Beatrice (1907) and Doris Kate, born in 1910, who died in 1913 aged three. Around 1911 the family moved to Hoddles Creek in the Upper Yarra.\n\nShe died on 11 February 1958, aged 75. The family tree places her death at Warburton, while the death index records it at Gladysdale, where her daughters lived, and gives her age as 76.","src":[["The Argus, 24 Mar 1928: death notice of Elliott Agar Webb","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article3920437"],["Marriage index, 1901 (reg. 5597/1901)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
george:{"n":"George Herbert Brazier","d":"1860–1913 · Wilton, Wiltshire","b":1860,"x":1913,"s":"y","p":["jamescb","caroline"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Wilton, near Salisbury in Wiltshire, in the summer of 1860, a younger son of James Coles Brazier, a master plumber, glazier and painter, and his wife Caroline Taylor. The 1861 census found him, eight months old, in his parents' house in North Street, Wilton, and in 1871, when the family lived in West Street, he was a ten-year-old schoolboy. His son Bruce Wilton carries the name of the town.\n\nHe and his brother Mark Taylor Brazier, two years younger, came out together as assisted immigrants on the Scottish Admiral, which reached Queensland on 19 August 1883; the passenger list gives their ages as 22 and 21. He settled at Toowoomba on the Darling Downs. In 1892 he was one of the ratepayers nominating a candidate for the municipal council, and for a time he was a partner in Lightbody and Brazier, merchants, of Ruthven Street. In Brisbane on 28 March 1893 he married Ellen Baldwin, who had come from Suffolk as a girl, and they had Alan Baldwin (1895), Felix Howard (1897) and Bruce Wilton (1900).\n\nHe bought a cottage and land in Toowoomba in 1895, and in 1899 he and his neighbours in Wilcox Street offered to plant ornamental trees along the street if the council would supply the tree guards. In 1903 he was secretary of the newly floated Toowoomba Industrial Co-operative Society, a co-operative store for groceries, drapery and general goods. For his last nine or ten years he worked for the A.M.P. Society, the life insurance company. By 1908 the family had moved to Warwick, where he was the society's resident representative and drew a £200 loan from the Warwick Bowkett building society, and in July 1909 he was signing up policies as far west as St George. Felix won a scholarship to Toowoomba Grammar from Warwick West school in 1910, graduated in civil engineering from the University of Queensland, and was engineer to the Townsville City Council for more than thirty years.\n\nGeorge died suddenly in Brisbane on 16 January 1913, aged 52, while there on A.M.P. business, and was buried in Lutwyche Cemetery on 17 January. The Toowoomba Chronicle wrote that he was very popular on the Downs, especially around Toowoomba and Warwick.","src":[["The Week, 28 January 1910: scholarship lists (Felix Howard Brazier)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article187333210"],["Townsville Daily Bulletin: Felix Brazier, City Engineer","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article62991176"],["Brisbane City Council memorial search","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31748627"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Darling Downs Gazette, 23 Jan 1892","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article170728244"],["Toowoomba Chronicle, 4 Jul 1899","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article217713487"],["Warwick Examiner and Times, 31 Oct 1908","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article82309279"],["Balonne Beacon, 7 Jul 1909","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article215389554"],["Toowoomba Chronicle, 7 Sep 1895","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article218304372"],["Toowoomba Chronicle, 31 Jan 1903","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article284332634"],["1861 census, North Street, Wilton: George H, son, 8 months (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/676337e6b18f7c0fd1f1ea79"],["1871 census, Wilton: George, son, 10, scholar (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906aa2be9379091b11b03b7"],["FreeBMD: birth of George Herbert Brazier, Wilton district, Sep quarter 1860, vol 5a p 180","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Passenger list, Scottish Admiral, 19 Aug 1883: Geo H Brazier 22, Mark T Brazier 21 (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR39458"],["Marriage, Qld 1893/B/16142","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e9af512284eaa381e73511f48242829d325b7e8cd2429cce14e732510ec80f1a"],["Death, Qld 1913/B/17051 (parents James Coles Brazier and Caroline Taylor)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/5b05ac2f98feb625884e64430ac1ecce819c2f0cb4cdecaeaabf10579fbe60d9"],["Obituary, Toowoomba Chronicle, 17 Jan 1913","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285533788"],["Personal, Darling Downs Gazette, 17 Jan 1913","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article182648045"]]},
ellen:{"n":"Ellen Baldwin","d":"1864–1957 · Earl Stonham, Suffolk","b":1864,"x":1957,"s":"y","p":["howard","annahaill"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born late in 1864 at Earl Stonham, a farming village in the Bosmere district of Suffolk, the eldest child of Howard Baldwin and Anna Haill, who had married there that summer. In 1871, aged six, she was living on her father's forty-acre farm at Old Newton, near Stowmarket, with her younger brother and sister, Howard and Bessie, a baby brother, Frank, and her grandfather William Haill, who was visiting.\n\nShe was 17 when the whole family, her parents and seven children, sailed for Queensland on the Mairi Bhan, arriving on 28 November 1882. Her father set up as a poultry and egg merchant in Fortitude Valley, Brisbane. On 28 March 1893 she married George Herbert Brazier, a Wiltshire man living at Toowoomba, and they had three sons: Alan Baldwin (1895), Felix Howard (1897) and Bruce Wilton (1900). The family lived at Toowoomba and, from about 1908, at Warwick.\n\nShe was widowed in January 1913, when George died suddenly in Brisbane and the boys were 17, 15 and 12. When Alan married in Brisbane in 1924 she made the wedding cake. She died on 22 November 1957, aged 93, having outlived her husband by nearly forty-five years.","src":[["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["FreeBMD: birth of Ellen Baldwin, Bosmere district, Dec quarter 1864, vol 4a p 528","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1871 census, Old Newton: Ellen, 6, born Earl Stonham (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a942f0df4040b56892575f8"],["Passenger list, Mairi Bhan, 28 Nov 1882: Ellen Baldwin, 17 (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR38800"],["Marriage, Qld 1893/B/16142","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e9af512284eaa381e73511f48242829d325b7e8cd2429cce14e732510ec80f1a"],["Death, Qld 1957/C/5762","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f2f82d5c16387503fbbc74c28745c4d7fc32bb9a356d44fb95b13389afd86376"]]},
frank:{"n":"Frank Warbrick","d":"1872–1916 · Oldham","b":1872,"x":1916,"s":"y","p":["henry","marthamoore"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Oldham, Lancashire, early in 1872, the youngest child of William Henry Warbrick, a mechanic, and Martha Moore, a cotton weaver. His father died at Oldham in the same three months, aged 30.\n\nIn 1874 his widowed mother took her three children, Lucy, 8, Ernest, 4, and Frank, still listed as an infant, aboard the immigrant ship Zoroaster for Queensland. She died at sea on 22 August 1874, and the three children landed at Brisbane as orphans on 25 September. Lucy never married and died in 1921; Ernest married in 1897 and died in 1911.\n\nFrank became an accountant and a well-known fire insurance adjuster, sent to assess the damage after fires around Queensland, such as one at Pittsworth in 1912 and a Bundaberg drapery in 1914. He married Adele Janet White in Brisbane on 22 March 1899, and their only child, Aileen Lucy, was born on 25 May 1903.\n\nIn May 1916 he travelled to Toowoomba by the Wednesday mail train to adjust a claim, was taken ill that afternoon with an old internal complaint and went into the Wilga Private Hospital. Adele was sent for and reached him the day before he died, early on Sunday 14 May 1916. He was 44. His body went back to Brisbane by the mail train that afternoon, and he was buried at Nundah Cemetery on 15 May. Aileen was twelve, eleven days short of her thirteenth birthday.","src":[["Brisbane City Council memorial search","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/search"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Toowoomba Chronicle, 15 May 1916","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285024427"],["Bundaberg Mail, 13 Oct 1914","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article215706720"],["Pittsworth fire, Toowoomba Chronicle, 11 Jan 1912","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285229435"],["FreeBMD: birth of Frank Warbrick, Oldham district, Mar quarter 1872, vol 8d p 603","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Passenger list, Zoroaster, 25 Sep 1874: Martha Warbrick 31, Lucy 8, Ernest 4, Frank infant (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR39711"],["The Zoroaster Immigrants, The Telegraph (Brisbane), 25 Sep 1874","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article169513685"],["Marriage, Qld 1899/B/19843","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7d1eca89b490b704a10724bb1b09db92c4a1949f96fe7abe885c0b5df22e4006"],["Death, Qld 1916/C/2828 (parents Henry Warbrick and Martha Moore)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/4c3fd4d9f8474cd5cb2cb457e3d40c29926a6d30735f262272e26a9a2e97448d"],["Personalities, The Queenslander, 20 May 1916","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23605591"],["Death of his brother Ernest, Qld 1911/C/2037","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/c4f1202691ca3b3d16f1bb9c0e037202906f9fe713d762e7a8e2e864379916bc"],["Death of his sister Lucy, Qld 1921/B/34109","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/5cf4b88766a8f6fae33341383f5314770a831a995a23a4c0134eb50f7290e6d8"]]},
adele:{"n":"Adele Janet White","d":"1873–1967 · Queensland","b":1873,"x":1967,"s":"y","p":["victor","marys"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Queensland on 8 August 1873, the eldest child of Victor White and Mary Stewart McGregor, who had married the previous September. A brother, Ernest Henry Victor, was born at the end of 1875 and a sister, Ethel, in 1877; Stuart, Eugenie, Muriel and a baby sister, Ilma, who died in 1889, came later.\n\nOn 22 March 1899, in Brisbane, she married Frank Warbrick, an Oldham-born accountant and fire insurance adjuster. Their only child, Aileen Lucy, was born on 25 May 1903. Frank died suddenly at Toowoomba on 14 May 1916, the day after she had been called to his bedside; she was 42 and Aileen was twelve.\n\nShe did not remarry. By 1929 her home was Ryecroft, in Bayview Terrace, where Aileen's wedding reception was held, and after Aileen went to live in New Guinea Adele's house was the family's base in Brisbane: Aileen and Bruce stayed with her at Clayfield in 1932, and in 1942 Aileen and her three children came to her at Hendra as evacuees. Her mother died in Brisbane in 1927.\n\nAdele died in Brisbane on 7 March 1967, aged 93. In 1990 her ashes were placed at Nundah Cemetery, in the grave beside Frank's, and two years later her daughter Aileen was buried with her.","src":[["Brisbane City Council memorial search","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31660591"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Birth, Qld 1873/B/16292","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/aa2b3b8f8ab9ec392162321ae83bcf7505d9b98c26f4e2e8741250784c19ca9d"],["Marriage, Qld 1899/B/19843","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7d1eca89b490b704a10724bb1b09db92c4a1949f96fe7abe885c0b5df22e4006"],["Death, Qld 1967/B/87665","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7e3bd459f4a52560f8ee76c4ae78b287188c0fdfa8e70e3d1dfae05c857a0483"],["Personalities (death of Frank Warbrick), The Queenslander, 20 May 1916","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23605591"]]},

jakob:{"n":"Jakob Broesder","d":"1843–1930 · Wagenborgen","b":1843,"s":"b","p":["tjaardh","anje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wagenborgen on 8 July 1843 (registered as \"Broeisder\"). A farmhand, he married Epke Hulzebos at Noordbroek on 24 May 1866.\n\nHe was 28 and a labourer (arbeider) when his son Tjaard was born at Nieuwolda in October 1871. His wife Epke is called a labourer (arbeidster) in the same record.\n\nTwo of his sisters married near home: Anna, who married the Nieuwolda baker Ekko Nap in 1870, and Jantje, who married Pieter Brons in 1878. In old age he and Epke lived at Zuidlaren in Drenthe. He died there on 25 October 1930, aged 87, ten days after his sister Jantje died at Wagenborgen; Epke followed him three months later.\n\nHis first child, Elena, was born at Nieuwolda on 3 November 1866, five months after the wedding. She married the butcher Harm Apotheker in 1894 and lived until 1952. He himself was one of at least five children: Jan (1834), Jacob, Aaltje (1846), who married Wilko Viswat and died at Nieuw-Scheemda in 1906, Anna (1849) and Jantje (1854). His father died at Wagenborgen in 1874 and his mother in 1885. The records call him a farmhand in 1866, a labourer in 1871 and a day labourer (daglooner) in 1894.","src":[["Birth record, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:09e17000-3eab-cb6b-627e-d6a417415266"],["Marriage, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:39e4137b-4a92-a7fe-fe79-741828ffd1bb"],["Birth of Tjaard Broesder, Nieuwolda 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7b8f4741-fc89-7c7e-59ed-cb1a0fc650c0"],["Death record, 1930","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:98d7e3b1-3a4b-40ec-ac0b-293ef17022b3"],["Marriage of Anna Broesder, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3d106ed9-956c-cd9c-f508-004597a62641"],["Death of Jantje Broesder, 1930","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4437c7e2-9216-bd56-44ac-0d0dfae8bd17"],["Birth of Elena Broesder, Nieuwolda 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e439936-805a-76fc-f613-c93841f78e61"],["Marriage of Elena Broesder, Appingedam 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:50c71284-258c-df40-e9c2-c454a51393d0"],["Death of Aaltje Broesder, Nieuw-Scheemda 1906","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:9bffcb11-39c3-a979-8f41-41bc4c848418"]],"x":1930},
epke:{"n":"Epke Hulzebos","d":"1843–1931 · Noordbroek","b":1843,"x":1931,"s":"b","p":["harmh","helena"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Noordbroek on 20 October 1843, the daughter of the tailor Harm Hulzebosch and Helena Plat, and named for her grandmother Epke Derks Hekman. Epke is a woman's name in this family.\n\nShe was a maid (dienstmeid) of 22 when she married the farmhand Jakob Broesder at Noordbroek on 24 May 1866. They lived at Nieuwolda, where their daughter Elena was born on 3 November that year and their son Tjaard on 13 October 1871. She worked as a labourer (arbeidster) alongside her husband. Elena married the butcher Harm Apotheker at Appingedam in 1894 and Tjaard became the village baker. One of Tjaard's sons was given her name in 1897 and one of Elena's daughters in 1901.\n\nShe was married for 64 years. In old age the couple lived at Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where Tjaard kept a café. Jakob died there in October 1930 and she in January 1931, aged 87.","src":[["Birth record, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8dec7ddb-f99f-977a-00c9-5e1252f424b3"],["Death record, 1931","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:c9dfcad6-8aaa-8b53-c32e-13140639caa7"],["Marriage, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:39e4137b-4a92-a7fe-fe79-741828ffd1bb"],["Birth of Elena Broesder, Nieuwolda 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e439936-805a-76fc-f613-c93841f78e61"],["Birth of Tjaard Broesder, Nieuwolda 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7b8f4741-fc89-7c7e-59ed-cb1a0fc650c0"],["Marriage of Elena Broesder, Appingedam 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:50c71284-258c-df40-e9c2-c454a51393d0"],["Birth of Epke Apotheker, Loppersum 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f69a448-91fb-09e0-bcea-7883e9ab090c"]]},
augustinus:{"n":"Augustinus Dijk","d":"1828–1893 · Kalkwijk","b":1828,"s":"b","p":["gerrit","roelfien"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kalkwijk, Hoogezand, on 30 January 1828, the youngest child of the ship's carpenter's man Gerrit Willems Dijk and Roelfien Feibes Huisman, who was 43. His brothers went into the shipyards; he became a blacksmith (grofsmid), a trade the yards and the canal traffic kept busy with ironwork.\n\nHe married Antje Hundt, a carpenter's daughter, at Sappemeer on 23 May 1856, and worked there as a smith until the early 1860s, when he moved his forge to Vierverlaten in Hoogkerk, at a lock on the Hoendiep west of Groningen. He was still an ironsmith there in 1893. His children were Roelfie, Aaldrik, Grietje, Antje and Gerritdina. Aaldrik took over the trade and was still a smith at 66; Roelfie kept a shop; Antje married the baker Tjaard Broesder.\n\nA sixth child, Emkelina, was born at Sappemeer about 1863, which dates the move to Hoogkerk to the two years before Antje's birth there in March 1865. In old age he and his wife left the forge and moved to Zuidbroek, where their daughter Roelfie kept a shop. He died there on 17 August 1893; the record gives his age as 66. His widow followed fourteen months later.","src":[["Birth record, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666265fe-59df-2e86-ee7a-893d98843fd5"],["Marriage, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:993752ec-a708-4f99-cccd-94015ce39769"],["Birth of Aaldrik Dijk, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29df9458-96f2-ed63-0e39-868d57daa340"],["Marriage of Gerrittina Dijk, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6b6f756b-a662-379f-ab3b-df50bd895133"],["Marriage of Roelfie Dijk, 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3164b2cb-6286-c203-b7ae-76e01056737f"],["Death record, Zuidbroek 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7380e0f-6492-ecdf-27c7-2532dc352414"],["Marriage of Emkelina Dijk, Nieuwolda 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb587ed6-e60e-58e0-fcb2-a146a764f81c"]],"x":1893},
antjeh:{"n":"Antje Hundt","d":"1824–1894 · Sappemeer","b":1824,"s":"b","p":["aaldrik","emke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Sappemeer on 9 September 1824, the eldest child of the carpenter Aaldrik Derks Hundt and Emke Alberts Gernaat. She was a housemaid when she married the blacksmith Augustinus Dijk at Sappemeer on 23 May 1856, aged 31.\n\nTheir first children were born at Sappemeer: Roelfie (about 1857), Aaldrik (15 December 1858) and Grietje (about 1860). In the early 1860s the family moved to Hoogkerk, on the other side of the city of Groningen, where Antje was born on 10 March 1865 and Gerritdina about 1871. They lived at Vierverlaten, a hamlet at a lock on the Hoendiep canal, and there Grietje died in October 1879, aged 19. Gerritdina married a skipper at Hoogkerk in March 1893.\n\nThere was a sixth child, Emkelina, born at Sappemeer about 1863 and given the name of Antje's mother, Emke Gernaat. Augustinus died at Zuidbroek on 17 August 1893, and Antje died there on 26 October 1894, aged 70. Three of her daughters married after her death: Antje to the baker Tjaard Broesder in May 1896, Roelfie that October and Emkelina in 1898.","src":[["Birth of Aaldrik Dijk, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29df9458-96f2-ed63-0e39-868d57daa340"],["Birth of Antje Dijk, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4513be63-dde3-f935-c1ff-c7f0b6996fb4"],["Death of Grietje Dijk, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5179a22b-9d00-f4f0-9b96-3e6aeb164f8a"],["Marriage of Gerrittina Dijk, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6b6f756b-a662-379f-ab3b-df50bd895133"],["Death record, Zuidbroek 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fa21a877-d2ee-3a98-861d-fe25ec28c697"],["Death of Augustinus Dijk, Zuidbroek 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7380e0f-6492-ecdf-27c7-2532dc352414"],["Marriage of Emkelina Dijk, Nieuwolda 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb587ed6-e60e-58e0-fcb2-a146a764f81c"]],"x":1894},
hjjd:{"n":"Hendrikus J. J. Degenhart","d":"b. 1836 · Kampen","b":1836,"s":"b","p":["johannesd0","mariador"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kampen on 3 April 1836, the son of the tailor Johannes Degenhart and his second wife, Maria Elisabeth Dorr. His mother died when he was ten, and his father married a third time in 1853.\n\nHe did not become a tailor like his father, uncle and half-brother but a cabinet maker (kastemaker; the later records say meubelmaker, furniture maker). On 28 May 1863, aged 27, he married Maria Klara Vreenegoor at Zwolle. She was a tailor's daughter from Kampen and her mother was a Degenhard too, very probably the daughter of his father's sister, which made the couple cousins.\n\nThey settled in Amsterdam at once. Maria Elisabeth was born there about 1864, Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus on 14 February 1869, Petrus Paulus Hendricus Johannes about 1871 and Johannes on 17 April 1875. The Amsterdam population register of 1874 to 1893 lists the family, and he was still working as a furniture maker when his children married in 1888, 1893, 1894 and 1898. Maria, a servant, married a furniture maker from Arnhem; Hendrikus became a teacher, Petrus a furniture maker and Johannes a diamond polisher.","src":[["Birth record, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:644359DB-9BE7-41D2-A8AF-8A22233729B6"],["Marriage, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D6569E41-EAC6-46B3-B9B1-C217F80EE620"],["Amsterdam population register 1874-1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:88523f33-d650-42d8-b2d6-62ba50ebbc36"],["Marriage of daughter Maria Elisabeth, 1888","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:09598947-BB52-456C-8593-3DFBC1A2B73C"],["Marriage of son Petrus, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:29FD929A-915E-45C2-BF01-BA0B21106C46"],["Marriage of son Hendrikus, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:BA863A53-B41B-4B23-83EF-1DC771493D06"],["Marriage of son Johannes, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:79F960AD-15A8-46A1-8169-5CC4C0FB825C"]]},
mariaklara:{"n":"Maria Klara Vreenegoor","d":"b. 1835 · Kampen","b":1835,"s":"b","p":["dirk","elizabethd"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kampen on 28 December 1835, the third of the nine known children of the tailor Dirk (Theodorus) Vreenegoor and Elizabeth Degenhard. An elder brother died in 1839, when she was three.\n\nShe left for Amsterdam as a young woman: the city's population register of 1853 to 1863 already has her name. On 28 May 1863, aged 27, she married Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus Degenhart, a cabinet maker from Kampen, at Zwolle. Her mother was a Degenhard, so she married into her mother's family; the bridegroom's father and her grandmother were very probably brother and sister.\n\nThe couple lived in Amsterdam, where her children were born: Maria Elisabeth (about 1864), a servant who married a furniture maker from Arnhem in 1888; Hendrikus (1869), a teacher; Petrus (about 1871), a furniture maker; and Johannes (1875), a diamond polisher. She is named in the marriage records of all four between 1888 and 1898, and again in 1907 when Petrus married a second time at The Hague.\n\nOf her brothers, Johannes Albertus and Nicolaas went to sea as young men, Bartholomeus was a tailor at Groningen and Dirk a merchant at Kampen.","src":[["Marriage, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D6569E41-EAC6-46B3-B9B1-C217F80EE620"],["Birth record, 28 December 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:035B1854-F6AB-40CB-BC96-64141E0A08F7"],["Amsterdam population register 1853-1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:7ebc76cf-28ee-4fd6-bd79-86429293efe6"],["Amsterdam population register 1874-1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:15628a82-7198-4bc8-9c4c-e5684f0c7b2d"],["Marriage of daughter Maria Elisabeth, 1888","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:09598947-BB52-456C-8593-3DFBC1A2B73C"],["Second marriage of son Petrus, The Hague, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:FA705856-1BD0-4F6F-93EC-BA78607B16CA"],["Marriage of brother Bartholomeus, Groningen, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6173a043-41e3-ffff-16ec-345d6b48a20e"]]},
georg:{"n":"Georg Bruckmann","d":"b. c. 1834 · Kassel","b":1834,"s":"b","p":["jakobb","marie"],"r":["de","nl"],"t":"A seaman from Kassel in the Electorate of Hesse, born about 1834, the son of Jakob Bruckmann, a cloth weaver, and Marie Elisabeth Dietrich. Amsterdam kept a register of foreigners, and it has him on 10 April 1860 and again on 11 October 1861, each time as George or Georg Brückmann from 'Cassel, Keur Hessen'.\n\nOn 18 December 1862, aged 28, he married Hendrina Holst, 22, in Amsterdam, bringing German ancestry into Carla's family. She was the daughter of a Danish ship's mate, and her mother had kept a lodging house for seamen. Their son George was born on 14 November 1863, Catharina Elisabeth on 28 September 1871 and Carolina Maria about 1873. The two daughters both married in Amsterdam in 1898; those records give him no occupation.\n\nHe was not the only one of his family to come to Holland. A brother, Heinrich, a smith, married in Amsterdam on 3 May 1865, aged 27. A sister, Catharina Elisabeth Brückmann, born at Oberkaufungen near Kassel about 1852, was registered as a foreigner in Amsterdam from 1868, married there in 1873, and died at Blaricum on 21 September 1920 as the wife of Johannes Stephanus Kieft. All three records name the same parents, Jacob Brückmann and Maria Elisabeth Dietrich.","src":[["Marriage, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C7DB11CC-8B28-403C-96C5-9CC631323CE9"],["Death of Catharina Elisabeth Brückmann, Blaricum 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2FE10B7E-7DA4-4785-B041-8B55B317BD16"],["Amsterdam foreigners register, 10 April 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:787ad7a5-b5d4-4336-981e-004072401e22"],["Amsterdam foreigners register, 11 October 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:3c1447e9-8a11-49ca-8c2f-b4ae2bd465bf"],["Son George, born 14 November 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:d5f24ec0-16f2-4895-a19d-96ccab82d2ef"],["Marriage of brother Heinrich Brückmann, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:EFEB6D0D-C1A6-438B-A5EF-4B014CD77AC4"],["Marriage of sister Katharina Elisabeth, 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:FFB69EB2-40F2-4263-9B66-F6BB955E762A"],["Marriage of daughter Carolina, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A9F5E46D-9217-45A5-ADA0-03B4B812F3E5"]]},
hendrina:{"n":"Hendrina Holst","d":"b. 1840 · Amsterdam","s":"b","p":["christen","hendrinav"],"r":["nl"],"b":1840,"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 12 June 1840, the fourth of the five daughters of Christen Holst, a Danish ship's mate from Nordborg on the island of Als, and Hendrina Verhoeckx. One index gives that date; the population registers say 12 June 1844, but her age at marriage fits 1840.\n\nHer father died when she was small, and in January 1846 her mother married another Danish seaman, Ole Christiansen. Her mother kept a bar and, by 1849, a lodging house for seamen. Her sisters married sailors from Sweden (1849), Denmark (1856), Finland (1858) and Prussia (1866).\n\nAt 22, on 18 December 1862, she married Georg Bruckmann, a German seaman from Kassel, so her children had Danish, German and Dutch grandparents. George was born on 14 November 1863, Catharina Elisabeth on 28 September 1871 and Carolina Maria about 1873. The Amsterdam population register of 1874 to 1893 lists her with the children, and she is named in the marriage records of both daughters in 1898.","src":[["Marriage, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C7DB11CC-8B28-403C-96C5-9CC631323CE9"],["Amsterdam register index (born 12 June 1840)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:51061295-8fa5-4ab6-b6f6-36300046669d"],["Amsterdam population register 1851-1853 (born 12 June 1844)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:5b0c2c81-d5c0-4fbe-9092-7df56e5a61e9"],["Amsterdam population register 1874-1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:2348f869-17ff-48b3-8efd-dc05731d30b9"],["Her mother's second marriage, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A02321F5-BFBC-4567-B7B6-81334AFE864D"],["Marriage of sister Antonia Maria, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:0FC486E2-6807-4402-A423-72D045F7B45A"],["Marriage of daughter Catharina Elisabeth, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:79F960AD-15A8-46A1-8169-5CC4C0FB825C"]]},
bernardus:{"n":"Bernardus Hubertus van den Berg","d":"1826–1894 · Tilburg","b":1826,"x":1894,"s":"b","p":["bernardus0","johannac"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg on 13 January 1826, the youngest of seven known children of Bernardus van den Berg and Johanna Cornelia de Vries. He was given the names of a brother who had died aged two on Christmas Day 1824. His father died in 1847, when he was 21. His elder brother Antonius was a shoemaker, the trade his own youngest son later followed.\n\nOn 25 April 1850, aged 24, he married Maria Gertruda Hamers, who was 21. They had thirteen children at Tilburg in twenty years: Gerardus Hubertus (1851), Bernardus Franciscus Maria (1852), Maria Philomena Theresia (1854), Petrus Ludovicus (1856), Antonius Petrus (1858), Maria Cornelia (1859), Joanna Henrica (1861), Johannes Henricus (1863), Josephina Catharina (1865), Petrus Antonius (1866), Anna Catharina (1868), Henricus Josephus (1870) and Josephus Johannes (1871).\n\nThree of the children died young: Josephina Catharina at seven months in October 1865, Johannes Henricus at three in October 1866 and Henricus Josephus at two months in May 1870. Two sons married in 1873 and three daughters between 1882 and 1887; Joanna Henrica was widowed and married again in 1889. His youngest, Josephus, married in 1891. In January 1894 his son Petrus Antonius died at 27, and on 15 August 1894 his wife died. Bernardus himself died at Tilburg seven weeks later, on 5 October 1894, aged 68. Seven weeks later his daughter Anna Catharina married a man from Mechelen in Belgium.","src":[["Marriage, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a04092c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c78deeae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth record, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3af2e3be-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of brother Bernardus Hubertus, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9b244c82-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Gerardus Hubertus, 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:06291bf8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Bernardus F. M., 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1e74902a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Johannes Henricus, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:455487b3-3cc5-0e52-6468-5fdf5eeaa64b"],["Death of son Petrus Antonius, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:5f33377f-9477-2f30-f79c-4dd67bd6f2b3"],["Marriage of daughter Anna Catharina, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0482dd70-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Josephus Johannes, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:57d894f6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of his wife Maria Gertruda Hamers, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c778e27a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Josephina Catharina, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:aef7d8c8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Henricus Josephus, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:d5376654-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
gertruda:{"n":"Maria Gertruda Hamers","d":"1829–1894 · Tilburg","b":1829,"s":"b","p":["gerardus","mariavg"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg on 2 February 1829, the youngest of six known children of Gerardus Hamers and Maria van Gestel. Her mother was 43 when she was born, and her grandmother Geertrui Reijnen, whose name she carried, had died less than three weeks earlier. Her father died in December 1849.\n\nFour months after that, on 25 April 1850, she married Bernardus Hubertus van den Berg at Tilburg; she was 21 and he was 24. Her mother died in December 1851, the year her first child was born.\n\nShe had a large family in Tilburg: thirteen children between 1851 and 1871, from Gerardus Hubertus to Josephus Johannes, born when she was 42. Three died young: Josephina Catharina at seven months in October 1865, Johannes Henricus at three in October 1866 and Henricus Josephus at two months in May 1870. Her son Josephus became a shoemaker. Six of her children married at Tilburg between 1873 and 1891.\n\nHer son Petrus Antonius died in January 1894 at 27. Maria Gertruda died at Tilburg on 15 August 1894, aged 65; her death record names her parents, Gerardus Hamers and Maria van Gestel, and her husband, who died seven weeks after her, on 5 October. Of her children, Joanna Henrica died at Tilburg in 1903, aged 41, and Petrus Ludovicus at Haarlem in 1909.","src":[["Marriage record, 1850 (gives her birth date)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a04092c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Husband's death record, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c78deeae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Joanna Henrica, 1903","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b50644f0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Petrus Ludovicus, Haarlem, 1909","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C225BE6C-A7D6-4056-8404-4BDC907FEA43"],["Birth of son Bernardus F. M., 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:497ce556-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, Tilburg, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c778e27a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Josephina Catharina, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:aef7d8c8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Johannes Hendrikus, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:af9d776a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Henricus Josephus, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:d5376654-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"x":1894},
franciscus:{"n":"Franciscus de Kock","d":"1835–1889 · Tilburg","b":1835,"s":"b","p":["peter","antoinetta"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg on 29 May 1835, the son of Peter de Kock and Antoinetta Peijnenburg, and one of at least seven children. He married Johanna Maria van den Brekel of Goirle at Tilburg on 5 January 1860; he was 24 and she was 21. Both his parents died within thirteen months of the wedding.\n\nTheir first three children were born at Tilburg: Antonetta (1860), Petrus (1863) and Adrianus (1865). Around 1866 the family moved to Besoijen, where Elisabeth (1868), Maria Catharina Louisa (1871), Franciscus (1874) and Johanna Maria (1878) were born. The Besoijen population register calls him a toll collector (tolgaarder), a man who took the fees from traffic on a toll road. In 1874 he witnessed a birth registration at Besoijen as a farmer (landbouwer), and from 1878 the records call him an innkeeper (herbergier).\n\nHis wife died on 22 January 1884. In September 1885 his daughter Antonetta married a Besoijen shoemaker, Lourens van de Wiel. Two farmers from Tilburg were among the witnesses, Adriaan de Kock, 61, and Petrus de Kock, 23. Their ages match his brother Adriaan and his son Petrus, though the record does not state the relationship. Franciscus died at Besoijen on 20 December 1889, aged 54; his son-in-law, a shoemaker, registered the death. His sons Petrus and Adrianus married at Tilburg in 1885 and 1887; Petrus died in 1931 and Adrianus at Tilburg in 1939.","src":[["Birth record, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3e411ba8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Open Archives: Franciscus de Kock","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Franciscus+de+Kock"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d69ce98-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Besoijen population register (toll collector)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:6a7fab1f-c9df-067f-1205-82cbfe147f75"],["Witness as farmer, Besoijen, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:9beeea5f-b4af-14d1-9b39-81a8e50187a3"],["Birth of daughter Johanna Maria, Besoijen, 1878","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:a8b77721-da2f-afcf-d988-d101e3179d0b"],["Marriage of daughter Antonetta, Besoijen, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:ce1d9966-e3e9-3e49-4644-89acb45456be"],["Death record, Besoijen, 1889","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:0a08ff6f-95dd-2fef-027b-da74cd52f3e7"],["Death of son Petrus, 1931","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:e2875a44-385f-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Adrianus, 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ced39b54-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Maria Catharina Louisa, Besoijen, 1871 (father a toll collector)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:0d2b5f37-cb86-8369-43c1-f07fdc237b81"]],"x":1889},
johannavb:{"n":"Johanna Maria van den Brekel","d":"1838–1884 · Goirle","b":1838,"s":"b","p":["petervb","aldegonda"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Goirle, just south of Tilburg, on 25 March 1838, the daughter of Peter van den Brekel and Aldegonda de Rooij; her marriage record gives her birth date and names both parents. Her parents had lost a stillborn child in 1830 and a three-week-old son in 1831; her brother Cornelis (1832) and sister Elisabeth (1835) grew up with her. The family moved to Tilburg while she was young, and Elisabeth married there in 1857.\n\nShe married Franciscus de Kock at Tilburg on 5 January 1860, aged 21. Three children were born at Tilburg: Antonetta (1860), Petrus (1863) and Adrianus (1865). Around 1866 they moved to Besoijen, where her husband appears as a toll collector, a farmer and an innkeeper, and where Elisabeth (1868), Maria Catharina Louisa (1871), Franciscus (1874) and Johanna Maria (1878) were born.\n\nShe died at Besoijen on 22 January 1884, aged 45, leaving children between five and 23. Her husband died there in 1889, and her mother outlived her by two years.","src":[["Death record, Besoijen, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:7d9cdb80-c357-5a14-ad02-c362dacf8126"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d69ce98-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Besoijen population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:6a7fab1f-c9df-067f-1205-82cbfe147f75"],["Birth of daughter Antonetta, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4e0f572a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Petrus, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:5033b12c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Adrianus, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:51d7a290-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"x":1884},
virgilr:{"n":"Virgil Robert Frankish","d":"1832–1889 · Barrow upon Humber","b":1832,"x":1889,"s":"f","p":["thomas1","catherine"],"r":["uk"],"img":"img/hibaldstow.jpg","ch":"frankish","t":"Baptised on 16 September 1832 at Holy Trinity, Barrow upon Humber, the eighth child and only son of Thomas Frankish, a servant, and Catherine Atkinson. Barrow is the village where John Harrison, the clockmaker who solved the longitude problem, had built his first clocks a century earlier. His father died when he was barely a year old, by which time the family had crossed the Humber to Hull, and he grew up there with his widowed mother and his sisters.\n\nHe grew up to work in Hull as a brass finisher, the craftsman who filed, turned and polished rough brass castings into finished taps, valves and fittings for ships, engines and houses. He married Jane Batler there in 1854. In 1861, a journeyman of 28, he was living at Maria's Place in the Myton district with Jane and four small daughters; in 1871 they were in Porter Street with eight children at home, and Jane was working as a pastry cook.\n\nThey had nine children, all born in Hull: Sarah Hannah (1855), Catherine (1857), Jane (1859), Alice Mary (1861), Thomas (1863), Emma (1865), Josephine (1867), Mildred (1869) and a last son, entered in the 1871 census as Richard W., five months old; the birth index has a Robert Walter Frankish registered in Hull at the end of 1870. The Hull marriage index shows daughters of these names marrying between 1882 and 1890.\n\nJane died early in 1889, aged 55. Virgil died in Sculcoates, Hull, late in 1889, aged 57, a few weeks after his son Thomas's wedding.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Baptism, Barrow upon Humber, 16 Sep 1832 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edac3eaf493fd08378e2b54"],["Marriage index, Hull, Jun quarter 1854, vol. 9d p. 292 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Maria's Place, Myton, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5903087fe9379091b15279c2"],["1871 census, Porter Street, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5fdcc38ff4040bf5bc2e3ec3"]]},
jane:{"n":"Jane Batler","d":"c. 1834–1889 · Hull","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born in Hull about 1834, by the ages she gave in the census. She married Virgil Robert Frankish, a young brass finisher, in Hull in the spring of 1854; the marriage is indexed in the June quarter of that year.\n\nTheir first child, Sarah Hannah, was born within the year, and eight more followed over the next sixteen years: Catherine, Jane, Alice Mary, Thomas, Emma, Josephine, Mildred and a baby son at the end of 1870. In 1861 the family lived at Maria's Place, Myton, in the old town parish of Holy Trinity. By 1871 they were in Porter Street, and Jane, aged 37 with eight children at home, the eldest sixteen and the youngest five months, was working as a pastry cook. Her mother-in-law Catherine lived elsewhere in Hull with Virgil's sister Eliza until her death in 1873.\n\nJane died in Hull in the first months of 1889, aged 55. Her husband died before the year was out. That same winter their son Thomas married Emily Wetherall, and in the 1890s he took his family to Western Australia.\n\nHer parents have not been identified. The surname was an old one in Hull: Batler Street in Sculcoates is named in deeds from 1812, and a seaman named Peter Batler sailed in Hull ships in the late 1830s, but no record yet found names Jane's father or mother.","b":1834,"x":1889,"src":[["Marriage index, Hull, Jun quarter 1854, vol. 9d p. 292 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Maria's Place, Myton, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5903087fe9379091b15279c2"],["1871 census, Porter Street, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5fdcc38ff4040bf5bc2e3ec3"],["Lease, land on the east side of Batler Street, Sculcoates, 8 Jan 1812 (Hull History Centre C DBR/323)","https://catalogue.hullhistorycentre.org.uk/catalogue/C-DBR-323"],["Trinity House muster roll, Laura, 1 Jun 1839, crew including Peter Batler (Hull History Centre C DSTR/66/94)","https://catalogue.hullhistorycentre.org.uk/catalogue/C-DSTR-66-94"]]},
robertw:{"n":"Robert Wetherall","d":"c. 1834–1897 · Healaugh, Yorkshire","b":1834,"s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1834 at Healaugh, a village three miles north of Tadcaster that was once the site of an Augustinian priory. He married Anne Walton, from the hamlet of Angram, in the Tadcaster district early in 1857, and they farmed at Marston, beside Marston Moor. In the 1871 census he was a farmer of 37 with six children at home, Emily among them: William Richard, 11, Catherine, 9, Stephen, 8, Emily, 7, Alfred, 5, and Jesse, 1. Living with them was his uncle John Coates, 71, a retired farmer born at Kirk Hammerton, who ten years earlier had been the unmarried farmer of Marston Hall.\n\nHis own parents have not been identified. Two Wetherall farming households had children born at Healaugh in the 1830s and 1840s: that of Ann Wetherall, a widow born at Sheriff Hutton, who by 1861 farmed 200 acres at Angram with her son Richard, born at Healaugh about 1835; and that of Stephen Wetherall, born at Bilton, who farmed at Hutton Wandesley. Robert named sons William Richard and Stephen, and the records found do not show which household was his.\n\nThe family left the farm in the 1870s and moved to Huntington, just north-east of York, where Richard (about 1874), Benjamin Harry (about 1876) and Walter (about 1878) were born. By the 1881 census Robert, 48, was in Hull with ten of his children, from William Richard, 21, down to Arthur, a baby born in Hull; he was recorded as married, but Anne was not in the house that night. His son William Richard married in Hull in 1884, and young Jesse died there in 1886. A Robert Wetherall died in the Sculcoates district of Hull at the end of 1897, aged 65; the name, place and age fit him. His son Walter, who settled in Western Australia, was registered there as the son of Robert Wetherall and Annie Walton.","src":[["1871 census, Marston (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a94476bf4040b568941898a/emily-wetherall-1871-yorkshire-west-riding-wetherby-1864-"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Marriage index, Tadcaster, Mar quarter 1857, vol. 9c p. 621 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Marston Hall: John Coates (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906e859e9379091b16e54cd"],["1861 census, Angram: Ann Wetherall, widow, farmer (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906bba6e9379091b12e6a78"],["1861 census, Hutton Wandesley: Stephen Wetherall, farmer (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6886c33cb18f7c2a2a8a4929"],["1881 census, Hull: Robert Weatherall, 48, born Healaugh, with ten children (Findmypast free transcript)","https://findmypast.ie/1881-census/robert-weatherall-0022361276"]],"x":1897},
annie:{"n":"Anne Walton","sn":"Anne (Annie) Walton","d":"b. c. 1839 · Angram, Yorkshire","b":1839,"s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1839 at Angram, a hamlet of a few farms in the parish of Long Marston, on the level farmland west of York. The births of two girls named Ann Walton were registered in the York district, one in the spring of 1838 and one in the spring of 1839, and either may be her.\n\nShe married Robert Wetherall in 1857, at about eighteen, in the Tadcaster district, and was a farmer's wife at Marston, where her children, Emily among them, were born. In the 1871 census she was 32, with six children aged from eleven years to one: William Richard, Catherine, Stephen, Emily, Alfred and Jesse. Her husband's uncle, the retired farmer John Coates, shared the house. The birth indexes show more Wetherall children in the district after that census: a Robert Walter, born late in 1871, who lived less than a year, and an Ellen in 1873, then Richard, Benjamin Harry and Walter, born at Huntington, near York, between about 1874 and 1878, and Arthur, born in Hull about 1880.\n\nThe family moved to Hull, and there she lost her son Jesse in 1886, aged 16. Her daughter Emily married in Hull in 1889 and went out to Western Australia, and her youngest son Walter followed; he was registered there as the son of Robert Wetherall and Annie Walton.\n\nIn the 1881 census her husband and ten of the children were living in Hull, but she was not at home on census night. No record of her death has been found in Hull or York, or in Western Australia.","src":[["1871 census, Marston (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a94476bf4040b568941898a/emily-wetherall-1871-yorkshire-west-riding-wetherby-1864-"],["Marriage index, Tadcaster, Mar quarter 1857, vol. 9c p. 621 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1881 census, Hull: Robert Weatherall, 48, born Healaugh, with ten children (Findmypast free transcript)","https://findmypast.ie/1881-census/robert-weatherall-0022361276"],["WA death index searched for Wetherall (no Ann or Annie)","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"]]},
jamesw:{"n":"James Emmett Wilson","d":"1883–1949 · St Helena","s":"p","p":["josephwil","sarahw"],"r":["au"],"t":"A younger brother of Martha Wilson (Grandma Parky), and the seventh of the eight children of Joseph Wilson and Sarah Whatmough. His birth was registered at Greensborough in 1883 under the names Joseph Emmett, but he was James all his life: his mother's obituary lists him as James, between Ben and Charles, and his death entry calls him James Emmet Wilson, born at St Helena, the district between Greensborough and Diamond Creek. The middle name came from his mother's family; her brother was Robert Emmett Whatmough. He was three or four when his father died.\n\nHe stayed at Diamond Creek. In 1934 he and his brother Charles were the two of the family still living in the district, and the probate index describes him as a farmer of Diamond Creek. He died there in 1949, aged 65, the same year as his sister Martha and his brother Joseph. When Martha's death was registered later that year his name, written \"James Emmeth\", was entered by mistake as her father's.","b":1883,"x":1949,"src":[["Victorian BDM index: birth 9808/1883 (registered as Joseph Emmett Wilson, parents Joseph Wilson and Sarah Whatmough); death 16833/1949 (James Emmet Wilson, Diamond Creek, aged 65, born St Helena); death of Joseph Wilson 19260/1949 (Seymour, aged 78, born Diamond Creek)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["A Pioneer Passes: Mrs Sarah Wilson, Advertiser (Hurstbridge), 3 August 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article56745828"],["Probate index: James E. Wilson, Diamond Creek, farmer (PROV VPRS 28/P3, 418/436)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A28%20AND%20family_name%3AWilson%20AND%20given_name%3A%22James%20E%22"]]},
josephh:{"n":"Joseph Hodgetts","d":"1870–1948 · Aston, Birmingham","x":1948,"s":"h","p":["josephh0","sarahharris"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Aston, Birmingham, the son of Joseph Hodgetts and Sarah Ann Harris, who had married there at the end of 1869; the birth of a Joseph Hodgetts was registered at Aston in the first three months of 1870. He was a moulder by trade: a moulder makes the sand moulds into which molten metal is poured to cast machine parts.\n\nHis obituary says he came to Queensland about sixty years before his death, which points to about 1888. The 1891 census of England, though, lists a Joseph Hodgetts, 21, single, an iron moulder born in Birmingham, lodging with a Dudley-born iron turner at Kimberworth, Rotherham. Name, age, birthplace and trade all fit, so he may not have sailed until after April 1891. He is not in the Queensland index of assisted immigrants, which suggests he paid his own fare.\n\nHe married Rosetta Maud Bastock, who was from Aston like him, on 17 October 1900, the same month her ship arrived. They farmed in the Rosedale district, then ran the strawberry gardens at Newtown, Bundaberg, a popular place for parties and outings, and raised thirteen children. He worked at the Bundaberg Foundry, which made machinery for the district's sugar mills, for 26 years, kept the strawberry gardens for 20 years, and sang in the Methodist church choir for 26.\n\nHe died on 27 December 1948 at his home in Water Street, South Bundaberg (Walkervale), leaving his wife, six daughters, seven sons, 40 grandchildren and five great-grandchildren. He is buried in the Bundaberg General Cemetery.","src":[["Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Feb 1958","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284352215"],["Queensland marriage and death indexes","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Bundaberg cemetery records","https://cemeterysearch.bundaberg.qld.gov.au/?q=Hodgetts"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"],["Marriage, Qld 1900/C/179","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/bfb819f5682b852d708ffd03520c71cf4ddfc0b8ceebe3eb746738ae9a74ca9a"],["Death, Qld 1948/C/4214","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f76ebfa9b15e022072f04edff27ddf928891f579781d741b0667472fac8f2471"],["FreeBMD: birth of Joseph Hodgetts, Aston, Mar qtr 1870 (6d 318)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1891 census, Kimberworth, Rotherham (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/590719cae9379091b1ab570b/joseph-hodgetts-1891-yorkshire-west-riding-rotherham-1870-"],["Queensland assisted immigration index 1848–1912","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset?q=assisted+immigration+1848+to+1912"]],"b":1870},
jhsmith:{"n":"Joseph Henry Smith","d":"1848–1933 · England","b":1848,"x":1933,"s":"p","p":["joseph","isabella"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Joseph Henry Smith was born in England in 1848, the eldest known child of Joseph Smith, a labourer born in Suffolk, and Isabella Jones. In January 1857, aged eight, he arrived in Melbourne with his parents, his sister Elizabeth (5) and brother Walter (2) as assisted immigrants on the Appleton. More brothers and a sister were born in Victoria: Kate Sarah at Kew in 1858, Frederick John in 1860 and Charles James at Inglewood, on the goldfields, in 1863.\n\nThe family settled in Prahran and Windsor, and Joseph Henry became a brickmaker there. In 1872 he married Eliza Ruth Warren, who had come out from Devonport as a child on the Parsee in 1854. Their nine children were all born at Prahran or Armadale: Harry William (1874), John Ernest Mark, known as Ern (1875), Isabella Eliza, known as Bell (1877), Elizabeth Kate (1879), who died in 1880 aged six months, George Joseph (1881), the twins Nellie Beatrice and Frederick Charles (1884), Kate Elizabeth (1886) and Alice May (1888).\n\nHe later took up land at Hoddles Creek in the Upper Yarra and farmed there. A Victorian land selection file for a Joseph Henry Smith, allotment 67A in the parish of Woori Yallock, about 100 acres, runs from 1888 to 1924; Hoddles Creek is in that district, but the file has not yet been shown to be his. His son Fred died at Yarra Junction in 1916 aged 32, and Eliza died at Hoddles Creek in 1927.\n\nJoseph Henry died on 10 January 1933, aged 84, at the home of one of his daughters in Glen Iris, Melbourne. His death notice called him \"late of Hoddle's Creek\" and named his surviving children Harry, Ern, Bell (Mrs Johnston, spelt Johnson in her mother's notice of 1927), George, Nell, Kate and Alice (Mrs Smith).","src":[["Assisted immigrants, Appleton, January 1857 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 11 p. 304)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Smith%20AND%20Appleton%20AND%20series_id:14"],["Marriage index, 1872 (reg. 913/1872)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Death notice, The Age, 11 Jan 1933","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article205511085"],["Land selection file, Joseph Henry Smith, Woori Yallock allotment 67A (PROV VPRS 5357, 14877/47.49) – identity unconfirmed","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=40&q=%22Joseph%20Henry%20Smith%22"]]},
eliza:{"n":"Eliza Ruth Warren","d":"1849–1927 · Devonport","b":1849,"x":1927,"s":"p","p":["josephw","maryfox"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born in Plymouth and arrived in Melbourne aged four on the Parsee in 1854, with her parents Joseph Warren, a Devonport mason and butcher, and Mary Fox. She married Joseph Henry Smith, a Prahran brickmaker, and they raised their family at Prahran before taking up land at Hoddle's Creek in the Upper Yarra. She died there on 5 February 1927, aged 78. Her death notice named her children Harry, Ern, Bell (Mrs Johnson), Nell (Mrs le Poidevin), Kate (Mrs Worlley), George, Alice (Mrs Smith) and Fred, who had died before her.\n\nThe Lilydale Express called her a very old resident of the Upper Yarra district, well known to the people there. She was buried on the Monday, and at its meeting that day the Upper Yarra Shire Council voted to send the family a letter of condolence.\n\nThe 1851 census, taken at Mill Street, Stoke Damerel, shows her aged two and born at Devonport, which differs from the Plymouth birthplace above; the two towns lie side by side on Plymouth Sound. On the Parsee passenger list in 1854 she was entered as Elizabeth, aged four.\n\nShe and Joseph Henry Smith married in 1872 and had nine children, all born at Prahran or Armadale between 1874 and 1888: Harry William, John Ernest Mark (Ern), Isabella Eliza (Bell), Elizabeth Kate, who died in 1880 aged six months, George Joseph, the twins Nellie Beatrice and Frederick Charles (1884), Kate Elizabeth and Alice May. Fred died at Yarra Junction in 1916 aged 32. Her death was registered at Yarra Junction, and the index names her parents as Joseph Warren and Mary Fox.","src":[["Death notice, Age, 7 Feb 1927","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article204205840"],["Lilydale Express, 11 Feb 1927","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article267616571"],["Census 1851, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64de7fa3f493fd3e50c8abd4"],["Assisted immigrants, Parsee, June 1854 (PROV VPRS 14)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Warren%20AND%20Parsee%20AND%20series_id:14"],["Marriage index, 1872 (reg. 913/1872)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
elliott:{"n":"Elliott Agar Webb","d":"1849–1928","b":1849,"x":1928,"s":"p","p":["drwebb","annagar"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Vestry House, St Martin in the Fields, London, on 7 January 1849, a month after his father reached Adelaide. He followed him out in the 1860s. He married Martha Hicks at Laura, South Australia, in 1878, moved to Melbourne in 1887, and became an insurance agent in Richmond, where he died on 18 March 1928.\n\nHe had been married before: his first wife, Elizabeth Ann, died of typhoid fever at Black Springs on 31 March 1875, aged 20. At Laura he served as the local court bailiff. On 30 May 1881, by then described as \"our late bailiff\", he was arrested there for debt; the Register reported that it took a bailiff and a trooper together to drag him out of Dr Kaufmann's office, and he was taken to Redruth the next day. By August 1882 he was manager for the Singer Machine Company at Port Adelaide, where he prosecuted a collector for keeping customers' payments. On 7 January 1928 he and Martha marked their golden wedding with a notice in the Age, giving their address as 59 Cutter Street, Burnley, and he died there ten weeks later. His death notice named three children, Charles (Queenscliff), Lucy (Mrs Pozzi, Numurkah) and Gladys (Mrs Grant, Pakenham); Mabel was not named. He was buried at Burwood Cemetery on 19 March 1928.\n\nHis children with Martha were Charles Elliott, born in Adelaide in about 1878; Mabel Ann, born in Adelaide in 1882; Lucy May, born at Goodwood, South Australia, in about 1886, who married Nilo Ezio Pozzi in 1913 and died at Numurkah in 1952; and Gladys, born at Richmond in 1889, who married Charles James Grant in 1910.\n\nHis Victorian death registration gives his age as 79 and his parents as Charles Holten (Houlton) Webb and Ann \"Elliot\". The family tree has his mother as Ann Agar, which is also his own middle name, so the records disagree on her surname.\n\nHis birth was registered as Elliott Agar Webb in the St Martin in the Fields district in the March quarter of 1849. An elder brother, Charles Joseph Webb, had been registered in the same district in 1842.","src":[["Express and Telegraph, 6 Apr 1875: death of Elizabeth Ann Webb","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article208189365"],["South Australian Register, 2 Jun 1881: arrest at Laura","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article43163478"],["Port Adelaide News, 29 Aug 1882: Singer Machine Company case","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article195869054"],["The Age, 7 Jan 1928: golden wedding notice","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article204177477"],["The Argus, 24 Mar 1928: death notice","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article3920437"],["Death index, 1928 (reg. 3533/1928), Richmond","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Births index, March quarter 1849, St Martin 1/133; June quarter 1842, St Martin 1/131","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
marthah:{"n":"Martha Hicks","d":"1851–1943 · near Peterborough","b":1851,"x":1943,"s":"p","p":["charlesh","anns"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Martha Hicks was born late in 1851 near Peterborough, the eldest daughter of Charles Hicks, a farm labourer, and Ann Spencer, who had married at Chesterton, Huntingdonshire, the year before. Her birth was registered in the Peterborough district in the last quarter of 1851. Her sister Sarah followed in 1852 and her brother William Spencer Hicks was baptised at Alwalton in May 1854.\n\nThe family sailed from Southampton on the Telegraph in October 1854 and arrived at Port Adelaide on 22 January 1855, when Martha was three; the passenger list enters her simply as a child. They lived near Prospect and at Walkerville, then moved to the farming districts of the north, to Wasleys and Caltowie.\n\nOn 7 January 1878 she married Elliott Agar Webb at St John's, Laura, a new town of the mid-north where Elliott served as the local court bailiff. Their children were Charles Elliott (about 1878), Mabel Ann (1882), Lucy May (about 1886) and Gladys (1889). In 1887 the family moved to Melbourne, where Elliott became an insurance agent in Richmond.\n\nOn 7 January 1928 she and Elliott marked their golden wedding at their home in Cutter Street, Burnley; he died there on 18 March 1928. Martha died at Bacchus Marsh in 1943, aged 91.","src":[["The Age, 7 Jan 1928: golden wedding notice","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article204177477"],["The Argus, 24 Mar 1928: death notice of Elliott Agar Webb","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article3920437"],["Birth index, Dec quarter 1851, Peterborough 15/377","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Passengers in History: Telegraph, 1854–55","https://passengers.history.sa.gov.au/node/942321"],["Telegraph passenger list (Hicks entries)","https://passengers.history.sa.gov.au/voyage-passengers-all/942321/28/10/1854%20-%2023/01/1855?page=6"],["Death index, 1943 (reg. 18814/1943), Bacchus Marsh","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
jamescb:{"n":"James Coles Brazier","d":"c. 1817–1884 · Wilton, Wiltshire","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Wilton, Wiltshire, about 1817. Wilton, three miles west of Salisbury, was a small market town known for its carpet factory and for Wilton House, the seat of the Earls of Pembroke. He married Caroline Taylor in the Melksham district of Wiltshire in the summer of 1840, and their first two daughters were born at Trowbridge, her home town. By early 1846 the family was in Wilton, in North Street, where the censuses of 1851 and 1861 found them; in 1871 they were in West Street.\n\nHe worked with lead, glass and paint. The 1851 census calls him a plumber and glazier, aged 33, with a house servant; in 1861 he was a painter employing one man and two boys; in 1871 he was a master plumber, with his sons Frederick and John beside him as plumber and apprentice plumber.\n\nHe and Caroline had at least ten children: Jane Elizabeth (1841), Sarah Maria (1843), Frederick James (1846), Henry William (1848), Alfred John (1851), Matilda Caroline (1853), Alma Ann (1855), a first Mark Taylor whose birth and death were both registered in the summer of 1857 (he is placed in this family on the strength of his name, as other Brazier families lived in Wilton), George Herbert (1860) and a second Mark Taylor (1862). The two youngest, George and Mark, emigrated to Queensland together in 1883. James died in the Wilton district early in 1884, aged 67.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["1851 census, North Street, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/69835873b18f7c29a8d755d9"],["1861 census, North Street, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/676337e6b18f7c0fd1f1ea79"],["1871 census, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906aa2be9379091b11b03b7"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Melksham district, Sep quarter 1840, vol 8 p 501; death of James Coles Brazier, Wilton district, Mar quarter 1884, aged 67, vol 5a p 143","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of his son George, Qld 1913/B/17051, naming both parents","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/5b05ac2f98feb625884e64430ac1ecce819c2f0cb4cdecaeaabf10579fbe60d9"]],"b":1817,"x":1884},
caroline:{"n":"Caroline Taylor","d":"c. 1821–1910 · Trowbridge, Wiltshire","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Trowbridge, the Wiltshire woollen-cloth town, about 1821. She married James Coles Brazier in the summer of 1840 in the Melksham registration district, which took in Trowbridge. They began married life there, where Jane Elizabeth (1841) and Sarah Maria (1843) were born, and by 1846 had moved to North Street in Wilton, James's birthplace, where he worked as a plumber, glazier and painter.\n\nThe censuses of 1851 and 1861 show her in North Street with a growing household and a young house servant, and that of 1871 in West Street. She had at least ten children over twenty-two years. One boy, Mark Taylor, who carried her own maiden surname, died as a baby in 1857, and the name was given again to her last child in 1862. Her son George Herbert was born in 1860, when she was about forty.\n\nIn 1883 her two youngest sons, George and Mark, sailed for Queensland. James died early in 1884. Caroline outlived him by twenty-six years and died in the Wilton district early in 1910, aged 89.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["1851 census, North Street, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/69835873b18f7c29a8d755d9"],["1861 census, North Street, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/676337e6b18f7c0fd1f1ea79"],["1871 census, Wilton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5906aa2be9379091b11b03b7"],["FreeBMD: death of Caroline Brazier, Wilton district, Mar quarter 1910, aged 89, vol 5a p 123","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]],"b":1821,"x":1910},
henry:{"n":"William Henry Warbrick","d":"1841–1872 · Oldham","s":"y","p":["jameswarbrick",null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Known as Henry. His birth was registered in the Ashton and Oldham district in the last quarter of 1841, and he was the son of James Warbrick, a mechanic. In Oldham a mechanic was a man who built or maintained machinery, most often for the cotton mills; the town was on its way to becoming the largest cotton-spinning centre in the world, and its engineering works built the machines for the mills.\n\nHenry followed his father's trade. On 12 March 1865, a mechanic of Coldhurst aged 23, he married Martha Moore, a 22-year-old weaver of the same part of Oldham, at Holy Trinity Church, after banns. The witnesses were William Bradbury, Sarah Hannah Busill and John Ogden. His elder brother John, also a mechanic, had been baptised at the Moravian chapel in Dukinfield, just across the Tame from Ashton, in March 1840, as the son of James and Margaret Warbrick; John married in 1860 and died in 1871, aged 31. No baptism has been found for Henry himself.\n\nHenry and Martha's children Lucy (1866), Ernest (1870) and Frank (1872) were born at Oldham. He did not live to emigrate: his death was registered at Oldham in the first quarter of 1872, the same quarter as Frank's birth. He was 30. Two years later Martha took the three children to Queensland, and died on the voyage.","b":1841,"src":[["Marriage, Holy Trinity, Oldham, 12 Mar 1865 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683f73bb865574024b6cfa73"],["Marriage, Holy Trinity, Oldham, 12 Mar 1865, with witnesses (Lancashire Online Parish Clerks)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"],["FreeBMD: birth of William Henry Warbrick, Ashton and Oldham district, Dec quarter 1841, vol 20 p 50; death, Oldham district, Mar quarter 1872, aged 30, vol 8d p 462","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]],"x":1872},
jameswarbrick:{"n":"James Warbrick","d":"Oldham","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"James Warbrick was a machine maker, the trade later called a mechanic. On 29 March 1840 he and his wife Margaret had their son John baptised at the Moravian chapel in Dukinfield, Cheshire, where they then lived; John had been born on 31 December 1839. Dukinfield lies just across the River Tame from Ashton-under-Lyne, and had a Moravian congregation from the eighteenth century. Other Warbricks, machine makers, mechanics and iron moulders, were living there in the 1830s and 1840s.\n\nHis second known son, William Henry, was born late in 1841, his birth registered in the Ashton district, which took in Dukinfield. James is named, as a mechanic, as the father of both sons when they married: John, 21, of St James Street, Oldham, to Mary Shaw, a weaver, at Prestwich on Christmas Day 1860, and William Henry, 23, to Martha Moore at Holy Trinity, Oldham, on 12 March 1865. No baptism has been found for William Henry, so his mother's name is not confirmed; Margaret is the only wife of James found in the records searched.\n\nBoth sons died young. John was buried at Waterhead, on the edge of Oldham, in May 1871, aged 31, and his three children all died in infancy. William Henry died early in 1872, aged 30. Through him James was the grandfather of Frank Warbrick, who was taken to Queensland as an infant in 1874. No marriage or burial for James himself has been found in the registers transcribed online.","src":[["Marriage of William Henry Warbrick, Holy Trinity, Oldham, 12 Mar 1865 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683f73bb865574024b6cfa73"],["Marriage of John Warbrick, St Mary the Virgin, Prestwich, 25 Dec 1860; burials at Holy Trinity, Waterhead (Lancashire Online Parish Clerks)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"]]},
marthamoore:{"n":"Martha Moore","d":"c. 1843–1874 · Oldham","x":1874,"s":"y","p":["thomasmoore",null],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"A cotton weaver of Coldhurst, Oldham, born about 1843, the daughter of Thomas Moore, a joiner. She married Henry Warbrick, a mechanic, at Holy Trinity Church, Oldham, on 12 March 1865, aged 22. The wedding fell at the end of the Lancashire Cotton Famine, the four years when the American Civil War cut off the supply of raw cotton and put many mill hands out of work.\n\nTheir children Lucy (1866), Ernest (1870) and Frank (1872) were born at Oldham. Henry died there early in 1872, aged 30, about the time Frank was born, leaving her a widow at 29 with three children under seven.\n\nIn 1874 she took an assisted passage to Queensland on the immigrant ship Zoroaster with Lucy, 8, Ernest, 4, and the infant Frank. She died on board on 22 August 1874, aged 31, a month before the ship arrived; her death is recorded in Queensland's marine register, the register of deaths at sea. The Brisbane Telegraph, reporting the ship's arrival on 25 September, listed her among the deaths on the voyage, \"leaving three children\".","src":[["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Marriage, Holy Trinity, Oldham, 12 Mar 1865 (FreeREG record)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683f73bb865574024b6cfa73"],["Passenger list, Zoroaster, 25 Sep 1874: Martha Warbrick, 31, and three children (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR39711"],["Death at sea, Qld 1874/M/1243","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/809d808b5c4e8c83e5624ce46f47cb38a4b667ba6bebeb3eae8c0b5926db7080"],["The Zoroaster Immigrants, The Telegraph (Brisbane), 25 Sep 1874","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article169513685"],["FreeBMD: death of William Henry Warbrick, Oldham district, Mar quarter 1872, aged 30","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]],"b":1843},

tjaardh:{"n":"Tjaard Hindriks Broesder","d":"1808–1874 · Finsterwolde","b":1808,"x":1874,"s":"b","p":["hindrik","eisse"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 14 December 1808 and baptised at Finsterwolde on Christmas Day, the eldest son of the labourer Hindrik Harms Broesder and Eisse Tjaards Grein, and named for his mother's father. His brothers Harm, Pieter and Derk and his sister Wupke were born at Midwolda.\n\nHe was a farmhand (boerenknecht) of 25 when he married Anje Jans Lewe, a farm maid of 21, at Nieuwolda on 21 December 1833. They settled in her village, Wagenborgen, and he worked there as a labourer for forty years. In the registers of those years the name is written Broeisder as often as Broesder.\n\nHe died at Wagenborgen on 6 May 1874, aged 65. The record names his parents and his wife, who outlived him by eleven years. The civil registers show five children born at Wagenborgen: Jan (7 December 1834), Jacob (8 July 1843), Aaltje (16 October 1846), Anna (30 August 1849) and Jantje (23 September 1854). Aaltje married the labourer Wilko Viswat and died at Nieuw-Scheemda in 1906, and Anna married a Nieuwolda baker and lived to 88.","src":[["Baptism, 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c69797d8-f242-a420-2c49-bda0126a7272"],["Marriage, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:12786296-077d-0984-0b70-9944f2ab3bd8"],["Birth of Jacob, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:09e17000-3eab-cb6b-627e-d6a417415266"],["Birth of Jantje, 1854","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:42a6188d-ebfa-30a3-035c-1003490eae23"],["Death record, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:def9012f-1540-57ce-3779-a1da9b8ab43d"],["Birth of Jan, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fa14ab38-1e25-b8be-e919-7b9d59e9064a"],["Birth of Aaltje, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b0e436de-d0ba-37bb-3e42-f6be6b5725a7"],["Birth of Anna, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ba43383f-4b34-c126-7a77-6e558f4a3664"],["Death of Aaltje Broesder, 1906","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:9bffcb11-39c3-a979-8f41-41bc4c848418"]]},
anje:{"n":"Anje Jans Lewe","d":"1812–1885 · Wagenborgen","b":1812,"s":"b","p":["janlewe","aaltjeok"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wagenborgen on 10 January 1812, the daughter of the labourer Jan Jacobs Lewe and Aaltje Okkes Eekstra. Her father died when she was fourteen, leaving her mother with four children, and she went out to work as a farm maid (boerenmeid).\n\nShe was 21 when she married Tjaard Hindriks Broesder, a farmhand from Midwolda, at Nieuwolda on 21 December 1833. They lived at Wagenborgen, where their children were born. She worked as a labourer herself: she is called arbeidster when Jacob married in 1866 and again when Anna married in 1870. Her younger sister Gepke married at Muntendam in 1836, and her brother Jacob died at twenty in 1845.\n\nTjaard died in May 1874 and she lived on at Wagenborgen as his widow. She died there on 18 April 1885, aged 73. The registers show five children: Jan (1834), Jacob (1843), Aaltje (1846), Anna (1849) and Jantje (1854). Besides Gepke she had a sister Derktje, born in 1816, who married at Delfzijl in 1840.","src":[["Birth record, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f49b6b35-2c7a-c7d7-0d03-17fb1e65713a"],["Marriage, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:12786296-077d-0984-0b70-9944f2ab3bd8"],["Marriage of Anna Broesder, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3d106ed9-956c-cd9c-f508-004597a62641"],["Marriage of Gepke Jans Leeuw, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e432d37-cbe8-40ad-0c5e-8f3c84a6eddf"],["Death record, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0f52598d-49f9-d023-fbdb-da61749aa2f8"],["Death of Tjaart Broesder, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:def9012f-1540-57ce-3779-a1da9b8ab43d"],["Marriage of Derktje Jans Leeuw, Delfzijl 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d7267c62-bfbb-e953-9007-7367ee435792"]],"x":1885},
gerrit:{"n":"Gerrit Willems Dijk","d":"c. 1789–1858 · Kielwindeweer","b":1789,"x":1858,"s":"b","p":[null,"regina"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kielwindeweer, in the peat colonies of Hoogezand, about 1789; only his mother, Regina Hendriks, is named in his death record. He worked as a shipwright's labourer (scheepsbouwersknecht) in the Hoogezand and Sappemeer yards, where wooden ships were built along the canal, and died at Sappemeer on 2 February 1858, aged 68.\n\nHe and Roelfien had Grietje (1812), Feibe (1814), Hindrik (1818), Willem (1824) and Augustinus (1828). The first two were born at Windeweer and the last two at Kalkwijk; Hindrik was born at Anloo in Drenthe, so the family spent a few years away. He is called a ship's carpenter's man (scheepstimmerknecht) in most records and a plain labourer in 1824. Two sons took up the trade: Feibe became a shipbuilder and Hindrik a ship's carpenter.\n\nA Regina Hendriks of De Kiel had three children baptised at Zuidlaren in Drenthe, a few kilometres to the west: Grietje (born at De Kiel in 1791), Hendrik (1794) and Trijntje (1799). No father is named for any of them. The name, place and years fit his mother, but no record states that she is the same woman. Grietje and Hendrik used the second name Ottes or Ottens, where Gerrit was a Willems, so the children did not share one father. Grietje died at Windeweer in 1814, aged 23. His own baptism has not been found in the Groningen or Drenthe indexes.","src":[["Death record, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2a0a2dbf-8a37-352c-5912-c0116548ccde"],["Birth of Feibe, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3ef06b97-20d6-5519-d5f0-b1a30033bb23"],["Birth of Augustinus, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666265fe-59df-2e86-ee7a-893d98843fd5"],["Marriage of Hindrik Dijk, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3b43197b-9e81-2712-3f34-193715a011f2"],["Baptism of Grietje, Zuidlaren 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:6c681a8c-94aa-4e62-bd15-f911f47f9c3f"],["Baptism of Hendrik Ottens, Zuidlaren 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:5a6804a5-5cb1-1944-1d66-e12025745970"],["Death of Grietje Ottes de Graaf, Windeweer 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:278be077-13a7-2240-c341-af1adbc4384e"]]},
roelfien:{"n":"Roelfien Feibes Huisman","d":"c. 1784–1847 · Windeweer","b":1784,"x":1847,"s":"b","p":["feibe","sjoukje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Windeweer, a peat-colony village south of Hoogezand, about 1784, the daughter of Feibe Alberts Huisman and Sjoukje Hindriks. The clerks wrote her name as Roelfje as often as Roelfien.\n\nShe married Gerrit Willems Dijk, a ship's carpenter's man, and had five children over sixteen years: Grietje (1812), Feibe (1814), named for her father, Hindrik (1818), Willem (1824) and Augustinus (1828). She was 43 when Augustinus, the youngest, was born at Kalkwijk. The family lived along the canals where wooden ships were built, at Windeweer, for a time at Anloo in Drenthe, and then at Kalkwijk. She died at Kalkwijk on 16 April 1847, aged 63.\n\nShe had an elder sister, Stijntje (about 1774-1853), and a younger brother, Hindrik (about 1788), a house carpenter at Windeweer. Her mother died there on 25 January 1815, aged 72.","src":[["Death record, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dda1b8a6-044e-61b3-eeb2-31f5fcce49f2"],["Birth of Augustinus, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666265fe-59df-2e86-ee7a-893d98843fd5"],["Birth of Grietje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19534c7b-82a3-eb15-14eb-16e91cf973ee"],["Death of her mother, Windeweer 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1ecc8329-a315-dc3b-05fc-23d58dd6c615"],["Death of Stijntje Fiebes Huisman, 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b944901a-5451-9e02-03e6-becabca4b849"]]},
aaldrik:{"n":"Aaldrik Derks Hundt","d":"c. 1797–1874 · Sappemeer","b":1797,"x":1874,"s":"b","p":["derkp","bouwina"],"r":["nl"],"t":"A carpenter of Sappemeer, like his father Derk Pieters Hundt before him and his brother Jannes beside him; he was named for his mother's father, the innkeeper Aaldrik Andries Hes. He was 25 when he married Emke Alberts Gernaat, a maid from the peat colonies, at Sappemeer on 13 April 1822.\n\nHe worked as a house carpenter (huistimmerman) in the village for half a century. The children were Antje (1824), Derk (about 1826), Grietje (1830), Aaltje (1832), Albertje (1836) and Klaassie (about 1844). His daughters married a carpenter's man, a blacksmith and a Groningen manservant; his son Derk became a travelling grocery seller. Emke died in 1871 and he died at Sappemeer on 11 February 1874, aged 76.\n\nHe was one of at least seven children of Derk Pieters Hund and Bouwina Hes: Grietje (1794), Aaldrik, Pieter (1801), Albertje (about 1805), Jannes (1808), Klaassien, who died at eleven in 1822, and Remmert (1816). Pieter, Jannes and Remmert were carpenters too. Their father lived to 82 and died at Sappemeer in 1843.","src":[["Marriage, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb53da9b-f8e5-4ece-90dd-5a538665ff00"],["Death record, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6da896e8-dfd1-df9c-19c0-d3b088485e2f"],["Marriage of Albertje Hund, 1859","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:53013166-4023-fc0f-3f77-3a8c7a0e3f25"],["Death of Derk Hund, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6faf0bcf-15ab-1f8b-7fa3-a04f14f9fa12"],["Death of his father, Sappemeer 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:64740e5b-0bfe-ae68-0e2f-fbb6518f852f"],["Death of Klaassien Derks Hund, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a4b717bf-e174-88fa-229a-5df52a32672e"]]},
emke:{"n":"Emke Alberts Gernaat","d":"1798–1871 · Kielwindeweer","b":1798,"x":1871,"s":"b","p":["albert","antjej"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Windeweer on 4 November 1798, the youngest of the nine children of the labourer Albert Lues Gernaat and Antje Jacobs. Her parents lived at Windeweer and Kielwindeweer, in the peat colonies south of Hoogezand. Emke is here a woman's name; the baptism register writes it Hemke. She went into service as a maid, and was 23 when she married the carpenter Aaldrik Derks Hundt at Sappemeer on 13 April 1822. Her father had died four years earlier.\n\nThey lived at Sappemeer and had a large family: Antje (1824), Derk (1826), Grietje (1830), Aaltje (1832), an Albertje born in 1835 and another born in 1836, and Klaassie (about 1844). The records call her a carpenter's wife (timmermansche). She died at Sappemeer on 28 May 1871, in her seventy-third year, three years before her husband. A granddaughter born about 1863 was named Emkelina, and a great-grandson of 1909 Emkelenus.","src":[["Death record, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ac2f9a2b-f86c-1098-e31d-50b206030265"],["Marriage, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb53da9b-f8e5-4ece-90dd-5a538665ff00"],["Birth of Albertje, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e372992-99b2-a2c8-b674-c2713e16e724"],["Baptism, Windeweer 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0a47dc6c-4019-d576-dd08-95e73429400f"],["Birth of Derk, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fb2fc10f-52a5-760c-b1c8-a621a84b2f4f"]]},
johannesd0:{"n":"Johannes Degenhart","d":"c. 1791–1860 · Kampen","b":1791,"x":1860,"s":"b","p":["hendricusd","elisabethvdb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"A tailor (kleermaker) of Kampen, the old Hanseatic town near the mouth of the IJssel. He was the son of Hendricus Degenhart, a labourer, and Elisabeth van den Berg. His later marriage records say he was baptised at Kampen on 8 February 1791; the first, of 1815, gives 8 February 1790.\n\nOn 29 September 1815 he married Catharina Zoet, a bricklayer's daughter. They had seven children. One boy died in 1827 aged four, and the summer of 1832 took four more of the family: Catharina died on 27 June, aged 39, and Jacobus (7), Hendrikus (3) and Maria Catharina (1) on 22 August, 29 August and 5 September. Three children grew up: Henricus (1816-1892), Elisabeth Maria (1819-1907) and Johannes Josef Antoon (about 1827-1880), who was a tailor as well.\n\nOn 15 November 1832 he married Maria Elisabeth Dorr, a servant. They had Kornelis (1833), Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus (1836), Gerrit Jan (1839), Pieternella Maria (1841) and Jacobus Wilhelmus, who lived two weeks in 1844. Widowed again in 1846, he married Mechelina Koenen, a widow born at Tiel, on 10 November 1853.\n\nHe owned a house with a garden on the Vloeddijk, shared with the children of his first marriage. In 1840 the court let him borrow 275 guilders on it to pay for improvements, in 1847 the family borrowed another 200, and in April 1856 the house was sold. He died at Kampen on 11 November 1860, aged 69.","src":[["Marriage, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6402D82-E48E-4CD2-ABD8-1F94ED92D2B3"],["Death record, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6A4A349-6DBF-40D1-B819-88AEF55F5F8E"],["First marriage, 29 September 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:FE3C50C6-80DB-479F-B7C0-112E5E541F98"],["Death of Katharina Zoet, 27 June 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:649E0B3A-377C-4B21-B1F7-BC396CEEF714"],["Death of son Jacobus, 22 August 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:23D2354F-56EC-40FD-92DB-9584A47C4A96"],["Death of son Hendrikus, 29 August 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:680DD4B0-7D53-45DA-AA25-15F37216C05C"],["Death of daughter Maria Catharina, 5 September 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:8AE8D6D4-B456-4B80-84A8-61CDBE7CC35D"],["Third marriage, 10 November 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:39E3AE57-DC07-4C8D-9610-876A3CFD7D84"],["Birth of son Hendrikus J. J., 3 April 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:644359DB-9BE7-41D2-A8AF-8A22233729B6"],["Death of daughter Elisabeth Maria, Deventer, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:A59C68E4-8AB6-4A84-92B5-78DE7EF02FE8"],["Kampen notarial deeds 1840, 1847 and 1856 (house on the Vloeddijk), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=Degenhar*"]]},
mariador:{"n":"Maria Elisabeth Dorr","sn":"Maria Dorr","d":"c. 1798–1846 · Kampen","b":1798,"x":1846,"s":"b","p":["joannesdorr","margaritha"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The daughter of Joannes Dorr and Margaritha Tellemans. Her marriage record says she was baptised at Kampen on 1 October 1799; her death record makes her a little older. A Jan Dörr, probably her father, lived on the Vloeddijk in Kampen in 1795.\n\nShe was working as a servant (dienstbode) when, on 15 November 1832, she married Johannes Degenhart, a tailor of about 41 who had buried his first wife and three of his children that summer. She took on his three surviving children, aged sixteen, twelve and about five, and had five of her own: Kornelis (born 4 November 1833), Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus (3 April 1836), Gerrit Jan (21 February 1839), Pieternella Maria (14 June 1841) and Jacobus Wilhelmus, who was born on 20 August 1844 and died two weeks later. The family owned a house with a garden on the Vloeddijk.\n\nShe died at Kampen on 26 April 1846, aged 48 according to the record. Her son Hendrikus, who became a cabinet maker and moved to Amsterdam, was ten.","src":[["Marriage, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6402D82-E48E-4CD2-ABD8-1F94ED92D2B3"],["Death record, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:FC98AE56-7C56-4037-B40B-3E1D89B3C982"],["Birth of son Kornelis, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:ED09D22F-3763-499A-8AC9-FD7E46550FCF"],["Birth of son Hendrikus J. J., 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:644359DB-9BE7-41D2-A8AF-8A22233729B6"],["Birth of son Gerrit Jan, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:308E03B8-D8ED-425C-A032-61BA04C5D09D"],["Birth of daughter Pieternella Maria, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:485F3954-7484-4436-9575-7F38FF24B8AA"],["Death of son Jacobus Wilhelmus, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:6667D710-9232-40C3-A7E3-D42F4B3E0377"]]},
dirk:{"n":"Dirk (Theodorus) Vreenegoor","sn":"Dirk Vreenegoor","d":"1810–1876 · Kampen","b":1810,"s":"b","p":["nicolaasv","mariaalberts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"A tailor of Kampen, baptised there on 28 October 1810, the son of Nicolaas Vreenegoor, the town's gravedigger, and Maria Alberts. His father died when he was eleven. The records call him Dirk and Theodorus by turns; they are the Dutch and Latin forms of one name.\n\nOn 22 September 1831, not yet 21, he married Elizabeth Degenhard, who was 19. They had nine children: Nicolaas Johannes (1832, died 1839 aged seven), Catharina Maria (1834), Maria Klara (1835), Johannes Albertus (1838), a second Nicolaas Johannes (1840), Bartholomeus (1842), Dirk (1845), Leonardus (1848, died 1875 aged 26) and Hendrikus Gerhardus (1850, died 1856 aged five).\n\nJohannes Albertus and Nicolaas were both seamen (varensgezel) when they married in the 1860s; Nicolaas was later an engineman (machinist) and died at Zutphen in 1889. Bartholomeus became a tailor at Groningen. Their son Dirk, a merchant, died at Kampen in 1901. Maria Klara married her kinsman Hendrikus Degenhart in 1863 and moved to Amsterdam.\n\nHe was still a tailor when he died at Kampen on 25 April 1876, aged 65. His death record, under the name Theodorus, gives his parents as Nicolaes Vrenegoor and Maria Alberts and his wife as Elizabeth Degenhard, which settles the question of whether Dirk and Theodorus were the same man.","src":[["Marriage, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:29173F07-E25F-48AC-9BF2-BD6CCFB9EADB"],["Death of his son Dirk, 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:826842C9-3F85-4342-8EC2-13F56993B6E0"],["Death record, 25 April 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:6E22607D-FC40-4494-BF49-3C32682FA796"],["Birth of son Hendrikus Gerhardus, 1850 (father named Theodorus)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:CF032B2D-D730-4734-ABFA-0E460DB7180D"],["Birth of daughter Maria Klara, 1835 (father named Dirk)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:035B1854-F6AB-40CB-BC96-64141E0A08F7"],["Death of son Nicolaas Johannes, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5911905A-00F8-4A41-B20E-84A1DB091696"],["Death of son Leonardus, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:67D687BF-C51B-4CF2-A0EA-0131083FD707"],["Death of son Nicolaas Johannes, Zutphen, 1889","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:D5BF6B4E-41B8-4668-A4F8-0C97AD82ED7D"],["Marriage of son Johannes Albertus, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:32CC1CE4-B726-4AF6-8E4F-058F15B9935F"]],"x":1876},
elizabethd:{"n":"Elizabeth Degenhard","d":"1812–1893 · Kampen","b":1812,"x":1893,"s":"b","p":["annacwd"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kampen on 24 May 1812. The birth was reported by the midwife, J. E. Peffer-Smeding. The child was registered as Elizabeth Bakker Degenhard, daughter of the unmarried Anna Catarina Willemina Degenhard, and no father was named. A month later Elisabeth van den Berg, the wife of Hendricus Degenhart and very probably her grandmother, died in the town.\n\nOn 22 September 1831, aged 19, she married the tailor Theodorus (Dirk) Vreenegoor, who was 20. They had nine children between 1832 and 1850. Four died before her: the first Nicolaas in 1839 aged seven, Hendrikus Gerhardus in 1856 aged five, Leonardus in 1875 aged 26 and the second Nicolaas at Zutphen in 1889.\n\nIn 1863 her daughter Maria Klara married Hendrikus Degenhart, a son of the tailor Johannes Degenhart, who was probably Elizabeth's uncle. Her husband died on 25 April 1876. She died at Kampen on 28 January 1893, aged 80; the record still names her mother as Anna Catharina Willemina Degenhard.","src":[["Marriage, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:29173F07-E25F-48AC-9BF2-BD6CCFB9EADB"],["Death record, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:7D3AC879-B69E-4705-B849-2AFC553CB199"],["Birth record, 24 May 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:48509D0D-DB31-4E27-88C6-024248409040"],["Death of her husband, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:6E22607D-FC40-4494-BF49-3C32682FA796"],["Death of son Hendrikus Gerhardus, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:3D041C98-925B-4D00-834D-C13E1DA8CADA"],["Death of son Leonardus, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:67D687BF-C51B-4CF2-A0EA-0131083FD707"]]},
jakobb:{"n":"Jakob Bruckmann","d":"cloth weaver, Kassel","s":"b","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"A cloth weaver (lakenwever) of Kassel, the capital of the Electorate of Hesse in central Germany. He is known from the Dutch marriage records of three of his children, who all moved to Amsterdam: Georg, a seaman, born about 1834 (married 1862); Heinrich, a smith, born about 1838 (married 1865); and Katharina Elisabeth, born about 1852 (married 1873). His trade is given in the record of 1862.\n\nThe two sons named Kassel as their birthplace. The daughter, eighteen years younger than Georg, named Oberkaufungen, a village about ten kilometres east of the city that grew up around a medieval convent.\n\nThe Electorate of Hesse was annexed by Prussia in 1866. That is why the Amsterdam clerks who registered his daughter as a foreigner from 1868 wrote 'Prussia' after the name of her village.\n\nNo German record of him has been found online. The church books of Kassel and Oberkaufungen are kept by the Protestant church archive in Kassel and can be read on the subscription site Archion.","src":[["Marriage of son Georg, Amsterdam, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C7DB11CC-8B28-403C-96C5-9CC631323CE9"],["Marriage of son Heinrich, Amsterdam, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:EFEB6D0D-C1A6-438B-A5EF-4B014CD77AC4"],["Marriage of daughter Katharina Elisabeth, Amsterdam, 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:FFB69EB2-40F2-4263-9B66-F6BB955E762A"],["Daughter in the Amsterdam foreigners register, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9f6e4379-2137-45e2-ac6e-6f1e98ff8aad"]]},
marie:{"n":"Marie Elisabeth Dietrich","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"Wife of the Kassel cloth weaver Jakob Bruckmann. Her full name, Marie (or Maria) Elisabeth Dietrich, is given in the Amsterdam marriage records of her sons Georg (1862) and Heinrich (1865) and of her daughter Katharina Elisabeth (1873). The daughter's death record of 1920 calls her Maria Dietrich.\n\nHer known children were born over at least eighteen years, from about 1834 to about 1852, the youngest at Oberkaufungen outside Kassel. All three left Hesse for Amsterdam: Georg, a seaman, was there by 1860, Heinrich, a smith, by 1865, and Katharina Elisabeth by May 1868, when she was about sixteen.\n\nHer second name was passed on to her youngest daughter and then to a granddaughter, Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann, born in Amsterdam in 1871.","src":[["Marriage of son Georg, Amsterdam, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C7DB11CC-8B28-403C-96C5-9CC631323CE9"],["Marriage of son Heinrich, Amsterdam, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:EFEB6D0D-C1A6-438B-A5EF-4B014CD77AC4"],["Marriage of daughter Katharina Elisabeth, Amsterdam, 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:FFB69EB2-40F2-4263-9B66-F6BB955E762A"],["Death of daughter, Blaricum, 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2FE10B7E-7DA4-4785-B041-8B55B317BD16"]]},
christen:{"n":"Christen Holst","d":"b. c. 1793 · Nordborg, Denmark","b":1793,"s":"b","p":["laurensius","metthe"],"r":["dk","nl"],"t":"A ship's mate in the merchant service (stuurman ter koopvaardij), born about 1793 at Nordborg on the island of Als. Als then belonged to the Duchy of Schleswig under the Danish crown; it passed to Prussia in 1864 and returned to Denmark in 1920. The Amsterdam clerk wrote his parents' names as Laurensius Holst and Metthe Christensdochter.\n\nOn 9 May 1832, aged 39, he married Hendrina Verhoeckx in Amsterdam. She was 32, a servant, born at Nijkerk. They had five daughters: Antonia Maria (born about 1833), Bernardina Laurina (about 1835), Geertruida Sophia (about 1836), Hendrina (1840) and Anna Catharina (about 1843).\n\nHe had died by 21 January 1846, when Hendrina, now keeping a bar, married another Danish seaman, Ole Christiansen. No record of his death has been found in Amsterdam.\n\nAll five daughters married seamen who had come to Amsterdam from abroad: a Swede in 1849, a Dane in 1856, a Finn in 1858, the German Georg Bruckmann in 1862 and a Prussian in 1866.\n\nThe Amsterdam population registers of 1851-1863 give the exact birth dates of the children: Antonia Maria on 27 February 1833, Bernardina Laurina on 12 September 1834, Geertruida Sophia on 11 December 1835 and Anna Catharina on 4 May 1843 (later registers say 1845, but her ages at her two marriages fit 1843). They also show a son who is missing from the marriage records: Hendrikus Laurentius Holst, born in Amsterdam on 12 December 1836 and entered on the same register page as his sisters Geertruida Sophia and Anna Catharina. His second name repeats that of his grandfather Laurensius. What became of him later has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2A57B623-F124-49BC-BD2F-274F5B4FE795"],["Marriage of daughter Antonia Maria, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:0FC486E2-6807-4402-A423-72D045F7B45A"],["Marriage of daughter Geertruida Sophia, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:F9980E8E-8340-4775-99E9-84E487DB0E7B"],["Marriage of daughter Bernardina Laurina, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:D48CDE4C-9F2A-4154-BFF5-825EA11C744A"],["Marriage of daughter Hendrina, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:C7DB11CC-8B28-403C-96C5-9CC631323CE9"],["Marriage of daughter Anna Catharina, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:1612875E-10D8-4105-A7C5-20AE4345717A"],["Second marriage of his widow, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A02321F5-BFBC-4567-B7B6-81334AFE864D"],["Population register 1851-1853, Hendrikus Laurentius Holst (b. 1836)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:112c42f9-f6a4-4e2f-8ff4-814d78ac6114"],["Population register 1853-1863, Hendrikus Laurentius Holst","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:53fa300e-e9a2-4245-870d-3de1aabf1c84"],["Population register 1851-1853, Antonia Maria Holst (b. 1833)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:a7fd6552-7a6c-431e-b97f-977381ec67f6"],["Population register 1851-1853, Dina Laurina Holst (b. 1834)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:31b5bef2-7750-4c81-a19f-074f01b22105"],["Population register 1851-1853, Geertruida Sophia Holst (b. 1835)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9de097be-0815-4714-a219-55b4e35de6bb"],["Population register 1851-1853, Anna Catharina Holst (b. 1843)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:41def11d-73ed-4d01-92d1-e8a0c94cb801"]]},
hendrinav:{"n":"Hendrina Verhoeckx","d":"b. 1800 · Nijkerk","b":1800,"s":"b","p":["henricusv","joanna"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised a Catholic at Nijkerk in Gelderland on 25 February 1800, the daughter of Henricus Verhoeck (the name is also written Verhoeks and Verhoeckx) and Joanna Antonia Gosseling. Her godparents were Jacobus Verhoeck and Catharina ten Brink. Within two years the family had moved to Amsterdam, where her brothers Bernardus (1802) and Jacobus (1804) and her sister Bernardina (1806) were baptised.\n\nShe was a servant (dienstbode) of 32 when she married the Danish ship's mate Christen Holst on 9 May 1832. They had five daughters between about 1833 and 1843.\n\nWidowed, she was a barkeeper (tapster) of 45 when, on 21 January 1846, she married Ole Christiansen, a seaman of 29 from Gilleleje, a fishing village on the north coast of Zealand in Denmark. By 1849 she was keeping a seamen's lodging house (slaapsteehoudster).\n\nHer daughters all married sailors from abroad: Antonia Maria, at sixteen, a Swede in 1849; Geertruida Sophia a Dane in 1856; Bernardina Laurina a Finn in 1858; Hendrina the German Georg Bruckmann in 1862; and Anna Catharina a Prussian in 1866.\n\nBesides her five daughters she had a son, Hendrikus Laurentius Holst, born in Amsterdam on 12 December 1836, who is listed with his sisters in the Amsterdam population registers of 1851-1853 and 1853-1863.","src":[["Baptism, 25 February 1800 (Gelders Archief)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:980C7F68-15C2-4B57-B444-79FC5C045B11"],["First marriage, 9 May 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2A57B623-F124-49BC-BD2F-274F5B4FE795"],["Second marriage, 21 January 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A02321F5-BFBC-4567-B7B6-81334AFE864D"],["Marriage of daughter Antonia Maria, 1849 (mother a lodging-house keeper)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:0FC486E2-6807-4402-A423-72D045F7B45A"],["Marriage of daughter Geertruida Sophia, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:F9980E8E-8340-4775-99E9-84E487DB0E7B"],["Marriage of daughter Bernardina Laurina, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:D48CDE4C-9F2A-4154-BFF5-825EA11C744A"],["Marriage of daughter Anna Catharina, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:1612875E-10D8-4105-A7C5-20AE4345717A"],["Population register 1851-1853, Hendrikus Laurentius Holst","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:112c42f9-f6a4-4e2f-8ff4-814d78ac6114"]]},
peter:{"n":"Peter de Kock","d":"1793–1860 · Tilburg","b":1793,"s":"b","p":["adriaan","johannas"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Petrus at Tilburg on 31 July 1793, the youngest of five known children of Adriaan de Kock and Johanna Span. His brother Wilhelmus married in 1817.\n\nPeter married Antonetta Peijnenborg at Tilburg on 31 January 1822; he was 28 and she was 23. Their known children were Adriaan (1823), Maria Anna (1830), Johanna (1831), a second Maria Anna (1834), Franciscus (1835), Johannes Cornelis (1836) and a third Maria Anna (1837). His sister Maria died in 1827, aged 41.\n\nHis eldest son Adriaan, who appears as a farmer (landbouwer) at Tilburg in 1885, did not marry until 1872, when he was 48. The youngest Maria Anna married Lambertus van Blerk in 1865 and married again in 1893, aged 55, to a Belgian-born man.\n\nPeter saw his son Franciscus marry in January 1860 and died at Tilburg on 30 September that year, aged 67. Antonetta died four months later.","src":[["Marriage, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1115d114-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2eb940c8-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:abba49a2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Adriaan, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3aa5a112-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Maria Anna, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3dbf31d8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Johanna, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3c159304-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Maria Anna, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3fb48790-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Johannes Cornelis, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:406fcd2a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Maria Anna, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:422834ea-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Adriaan, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:128e8a18-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Maria Anna, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1cebae46-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Second marriage of daughter Maria Anna, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1cf2ed50-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"x":1860},
antoinetta:{"n":"Antonetta Peijnenborg","d":"1798–1861 · Tilburg","b":1798,"s":"b","p":["janfp","mariavh"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Antonia at Tilburg on 1 August 1798, a daughter of Jan Francis Peijnenborg and Maria van den Hout. She had an elder sister Joanna (1787) and two younger sisters, Lucia (1800) and Maria Elizabeth (1802); an elder sister Antonia, baptised in 1788, had evidently died young, and the new baby was given her name. Her mother was buried at Tilburg in June 1809, when Antonia was ten. Her first name is written Antonia, Antonetta, Antoinetta and Antonette, and the surname Peijnenborg, Peijnenburg or Pijnenburg.\n\nShe married Peter de Kock at Tilburg on 31 January 1822, aged 23; he was 28. They had at least seven children between 1823 and 1837: Adriaan, Johanna, Franciscus, Johannes Cornelis and three daughters in turn named Maria Anna. Her sister Lucia married Petrus Jongbloets in 1827, and Maria Elizabeth married Peter de Brouwer in 1842, aged 39.\n\nHer son Franciscus married Johanna Maria van den Brekel in January 1860. Her husband died on 30 September 1860, and Antonetta followed him on 30 January 1861. The death record gives her age as 61. Lucia lived until 1876 and Maria Elizabeth until 1879.","x":1861,"src":[["Baptism, Tilburg, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31f450b6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a69240e2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1115d114-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of sister Lucia, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:086fd474-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of sister Maria Elizabeth, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0871e8a4-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Lucia, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b60220ba-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Maria Elizabeth, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b83049e8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of sister Joanna, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fe8b7b2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of her mother, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:00fc5ad0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
petervb:{"n":"Peter van den Brekel","d":"b. 1807 · Goirle","b":1807,"s":"b","p":["petervb0","elisabethk2"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Goirle on 15 November 1807, the son of Peter van den Brekel and Elisabeth de Kort. Goirle is a village just south of Tilburg, close to the Belgian border, where linen weaving was the main cottage industry. He lost an elder brother, Francis Michiel, in 1826 and his mother in 1831; his brother Jan Baptist died in 1837 and his sister Maria Catharina in 1838, both young.\n\nOn 2 May 1829, aged 21, he married Aldegonda de Rooij at Goirle; she was 25. Their first child was stillborn on 17 January 1830, and a son Adriaan died on 25 March 1831 at three weeks. Three children then lived: Cornelis (12 July 1832), Elisabeth (15 April 1835) and Johanna Maria (25 March 1838).\n\nThe family later moved to Tilburg. The Tilburg registers record a son Adriaan born to the couple on 7 May 1851, who lived three days; Aldegonda was then 47. Elisabeth married at Tilburg in 1857 and Johanna Maria in 1860. His father died at Loon op Zand in 1855. Peter is named as Aldegonda's husband in her death record of 1886. His son Cornelis died at Tilburg in 1905.","src":[["Marriage, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:6e9e8fdd-9304-d246-54cf-82b06d3b7ce3"],["Marriage, Goirle, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fd807352-3860-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Stillborn child, Goirle, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:107868c2-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Adriaan, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:105fd348-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Cornelis, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2e502306-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Elisabeth, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2e854c84-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Adriaan, Tilburg, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4738af3c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Elisabeth, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d69790c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Cornelis, 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:08ce4dec-c30d-5639-d479-bcfc44ed7614"]]},
aldegonda:{"n":"Aldegonda de Rooij","d":"1804–1886 · Goirle","b":1804,"s":"b","p":["antonie","catharinavg"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Aldegundis at Goirle on 13 February 1804, the second daughter of Antonie de Rooij and Catharina van Gorp. She carried the name of her mother's mother, Aldegonda Spaepen. Her sisters were Cornelia (1800) and Johanna Cornelia (1808). A brother Adrianus was born in 1813, and a baby sister, Adriana Elisabeth, died in 1821 at ten months.\n\nOn 2 May 1829, aged 25, she married Peter van den Brekel at Goirle; he was 21. Her first child was stillborn in January 1830 and her second, Adriaan, lived three weeks in 1831. Cornelis (1832), Elisabeth (1835) and Johanna Maria (1838) survived. The Tilburg registers also record a son Adriaan born in May 1851, who lived three days.\n\nHer mother died at Goirle in January 1848 and both her sisters in 1852. By then she was living in Tilburg, where her daughters married in 1857 and 1860. Johanna Maria died at Besoijen in 1884. Aldegonda died at Tilburg on 1 April 1886, aged 82; her death record calls her the wife of Peter van den Brekel.","x":1886,"src":[["Baptism, Goirle, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:41e7f2ca-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, Tilburg, 1886","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:be195fc0-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Goirle, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fd807352-3860-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Cornelia, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:10bee324-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Johanna Cornelia, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:10bf9544-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Adriaan, Tilburg, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:0414811d-ece0-3303-9d66-4999fb6592d5"]]},
thomas1:{"n":"Thomas Frankish","d":"1793–1833 · Elsham","b":1793,"x":1833,"s":"f","p":["virgil1","ann"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Baptised at Elsham on 1 September 1793, the third son of Virgil Frankish and Ann Hickson. As a young man he moved a few miles north to Barrow upon Humber, a large village on the south bank of the Humber facing Hull, and he was \"of Barrow\" when he married Catherine Atkinson at Scawby, her parish, on 22 October 1816.\n\nAt Barrow he worked as a servant, the word used for him in every baptism entry; at that time it usually meant a man hired by the year on a farm or in a household. He and Catherine had eight children baptised at Holy Trinity, Barrow: Martha Ann (4 August 1817), Catharine Atkinson (11 September 1818), Mary (9 September 1819), Marian (14 January 1822), Sarah Elisabeth (22 November 1824), Ann (9 April 1827), Eliza (9 August 1829) and, after seven daughters, a son, Virgil Robert (16 September 1832).\n\nWithin a year of that last baptism the family had crossed the river to Hull, and there Thomas died. He was buried at Elsham on 20 October 1833, aged 40, giving his home as Hull. His father had been buried in the same churchyard four years earlier, and his mother followed in June 1834.","src":[["Baptism, Elsham, 1 Sep 1793 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684162f7865574f305c7aa10"],["Marriage, Scawby, 22 Oct 1816 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f412c3bf493fd97df5ba765"],["Baptism of daughter Martha Ann, Barrow upon Humber, 1817 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6840b38e865574f3058271fd"],["Baptism of daughter Catharine Atkinson, Barrow, 1818 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6840b390865574f305827370"],["Baptism of daughter Eliza, Barrow, 1829 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6840b3a5865574f305827f5a"],["Baptism of son Virgil Robert, Barrow, 16 Sep 1832 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edac3eaf493fd08378e2b54"],["Burial, Elsham, 20 Oct 1833 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416330865574f305c7b993"]]},
catherine:{"n":"Catherine Atkinson","d":"c. 1793–1873 · Bleasby","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1793 at Bleasby, by her own account in two censuses. There is a hamlet of that name in the parish of Legsby, near Market Rasen in Lincolnshire, and a village of the same name in Nottinghamshire, and the census does not say which. By 1816 she was of Scawby, near Brigg, and she married Thomas Frankish of Barrow there on 22 October 1816. The witnesses were Sarah Shimells and Mary Sison.\n\nShe had eight children at Barrow upon Humber between 1817 and 1832, seven daughters and then a son, Virgil Robert; her second daughter was baptised Catharine Atkinson Frankish, carrying her maiden name. By 1833 the family was in Hull, and that October Thomas died, aged 40. She was left a widow with a baby son and a houseful of daughters. In 1841 two of the girls, Sarah, 15, and Ann, 14, were back in Barrow as servants in other people's houses.\n\nCatherine spent the rest of her life in Hull. In 1861, aged 68 and described as a retired housekeeper, she was living in Vauxhall Street with her youngest daughter Eliza, Eliza's husband William Gleadow, a railway clerk, and their two children. She was still with them in 1871, in Clarendon Street, aged 78. She died in Hull early in 1873, aged 80, after almost forty years as a widow.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Marriage, Scawby, 22 Oct 1816 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f412c3bf493fd97df5ba765"],["Baptism of daughter Catharine Atkinson Frankish, Barrow, 1818 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6840b390865574f305827370"],["Burial of Thomas Frankish, Elsham, 20 Oct 1833 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416330865574f305c7b993"],["1841 census, Barrow upon Humber: Sarah Frankish, servant (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/60b091f4f493fd25a0c3a8c8"],["1841 census, Barrow upon Humber: Ann Frankish, servant (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/60b0920af493fd25a0c3b2ce"],["1861 census, Vauxhall Street, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5903089ce9379091b152f5a0"],["1871 census, Clarendon Street, Hull (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/59030af3e9379091b15c5404"],["Death index, Hull, Mar quarter 1873, vol. 9d p. 150, aged 80 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]],"b":1793,"x":1873},
joseph:{"n":"Joseph Smith","d":"c. 1816–1878 · Suffolk","b":1816,"x":1878,"s":"p","p":["smjosephsmith","smanndeaves"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Joseph Smith was born in Suffolk in about 1815 or 1816. His death registration in Victoria names his parents as Joseph Smith and Ann Deaves, and his death notice says he was \"formerly of Suffolk, England\". The only marriage of a Joseph Smith and an Ann Deaves found in Suffolk was at Stoke by Nayland in 1811, so that couple are taken to be his parents, although his own baptism has not been found.\n\nHe married Isabel (Isabella) Jones in the Cambridge registration district in the last quarter of 1845. Their son Joseph Henry was born in 1848, Elizabeth in about 1851 and Walter Emanuel in about 1854. Before emigrating, Joseph was a labourer at Fenny Stratford, Buckinghamshire, a small market town on Watling Street, the old Roman road from London to the north-west.\n\nThe family sailed as assisted immigrants on the Appleton, reaching Melbourne on 25 January 1857 after 102 days at sea; Joseph was listed as 40 and Isabella as 35. Assisted passages to Victoria were paid for out of the colony's land sales, to bring out workers and their families. Three more children were born in Victoria: Kate Sarah at Kew in 1858, Frederick John in 1860 and Charles James at Inglewood in 1863. Gold had been found at Inglewood in 1859, and the town grew quickly as a goldfield.\n\nThe family settled in Prahran and Windsor. Joseph died at Albert Street, Windsor, on 16 January 1878, aged 62. The notice in the Illustrated Australian News called him the brother-in-law of Mr R. E. Jones (printed \"Janes\"), auctioneer, of Melbourne.","u":true,"src":[["Death index, 1878 (reg. 2923/1878): parents Joseph Smith and Ann Deaves, born Suffolk","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Death notice, Illustrated Australian News, 20 Feb 1878","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article60093718"],["Marriage index, Dec quarter 1845, Cambridge 14/7 (Joseph Smith and Isabel Jones)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Assisted immigrants, Appleton, January 1857 (PROV VPRS 14)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Smith%20AND%20Appleton%20AND%20series_id:14"],["Marriage of Joseph Smith and Anne Deaves, Stoke by Nayland, 1811","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/685720ee865574b5db3f4c22"]]},
isabella:{"n":"Isabella Jones","d":"c. 1821–1896","b":1821,"x":1896,"s":"p","p":["johnjones","sarahwragg"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Isabella (often written Isabel) Jones was born in about 1821, the daughter of John Jones and Sarah Wragg, as her death registration records. She married Joseph Smith, a labourer born in Suffolk, in the Cambridge registration district at the end of 1845. Her parents are most probably the John Jones and Sarah Wragg who married at Arrington, Cambridgeshire, in 1812. That rests on the names and on her marriage in the Cambridge district, as her own baptism has not been found, so the link to the Arrington couple is marked as uncertain.\n\nHer son Joseph Henry was born in 1848, Elizabeth in about 1851 and Walter Emanuel in about 1854. Before emigrating the family lived at Fenny Stratford in Buckinghamshire, and they sailed as assisted immigrants on the Appleton, reaching Melbourne in January 1857, when Isabella was 35. In Victoria she had three more children: Kate Sarah at Kew in 1858, Frederick John in 1860 and Charles James at Inglewood, on the goldfields, in 1863.\n\nThe family settled at Prahran and Windsor. Joseph died in January 1878 and Isabel lived on as a widow for eighteen years. She died at her home, Ivanhoe Cottage, Clarke Street, Prahran, on 1 August 1896, aged 75. Her death notice named six children: J. H., W. E., F. J. and C. J. Smith, Mrs R. Vines of Prahran and Mrs J. F. Moore of Geelong.","src":[["Death index, 1896 (reg. 11092/1896): parents John Jones and Sarah Wragg","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Death notice, The Age, 4 Aug 1896","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190611224"],["Death notice, The Age, 2 Aug 1896","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190616099"],["Death notice, The Leader, 8 Aug 1896","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article198016222"],["Marriage index, Dec quarter 1845, Cambridge 14/7","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Assisted immigrants, Appleton, January 1857 (PROV VPRS 14)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Smith%20AND%20Appleton%20AND%20series_id:14"]],"u":true},
josephw:{"n":"Joseph Warren","d":"c. 1810–1892 · Devonport","b":1810,"x":1892,"s":"p","p":["williamwarren","smmarthawilson"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Joseph Warren was born at Devonport, Devon, in about 1810, the son of William Warren and Martha Wilson, as his death registration records. Devonport grew up around the Royal Navy dockyard beside Plymouth and was called Plymouth Dock until 1824; its parish was Stoke Damerel.\n\nHe married Mary Fox at Devonport in 1839. In 1841 he was a mason, living in Barrack Street with Mary and their daughter Amelia in the same house as Mary's parents, Peter Fox, a pork butcher, and Jane. By 1851 Joseph had himself become a butcher and the family lived at Mill Street, Stoke Damerel, with a young servant. Their children were Amelia Jane (born about 1838), Alfred (about 1843), John J. (about 1845), Mark A. (about 1847) and Eliza Ruth (1849), all born at Devonport.\n\nThe family sailed as assisted immigrants on the Parsee, reaching Melbourne on 9 June 1854; Joseph was listed as 43 and Mary as 44, and Amelia travelled with the single women. Mary died in Victoria in July 1862, and their son Mark died in 1870 aged 22. In 1872 Eliza married Joseph Henry Smith, a Prahran brickmaker.\n\nJoseph died in 1892, aged 82, in the Melbourne Benevolent Asylum at Hotham (North Melbourne), which had cared for old and destitute people since 1851. A Joseph Warren married Isabella Cameron in Victoria in 1863, but it has not been shown that this was him.","src":[["Death index, 1892 (reg. 10577/1892): parents William Warren and Martha Wilson","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Census 1841, Barrack Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a1464cdf4040b9d6e5aebb1"],["Census 1851, Mill Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64de7fa3f493fd3e50c8abd4"],["Assisted immigrants, Parsee, June 1854 (PROV VPRS 14)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Warren%20AND%20Parsee%20AND%20series_id:14"]]},
maryfox:{"n":"Mary Fox","d":"c. 1809–1862 · Pennycross, Devon","b":1809,"x":1862,"s":"p","p":["peterfox","janebone"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Mary Fox was born in about 1809 at Pennycross, Devon, the daughter of Peter Fox, a pork butcher, and Jane Bone. Her death registration in Victoria names both her parents.\n\nShe married Joseph Warren, a Devonport mason, in 1839. In 1841 they were living in Barrack Street, Devonport, with their little daughter Amelia, sharing a house with Mary's parents and her younger brother and sister, Peter and Amelia. By 1851 the family had its own home in Mill Street, Stoke Damerel, where Joseph worked as a butcher and they kept a servant girl. Her children were Amelia Jane, Alfred, John, Mark and Eliza Ruth, the youngest, born in 1849.\n\nIn 1854 the family sailed as assisted immigrants on the Parsee and reached Melbourne on 9 June. Mary was listed as 44. She died in Victoria in July 1862, aged 53, eight years after arriving.","src":[["Death index, 1862 (reg. 7370/1862): parents Peter Fox and Jane Bone","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Census 1841, Barrack Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a1464cdf4040b9d6e5aebb1"],["Census 1851, Mill Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64de7fa3f493fd3e50c8abd4"],["Assisted immigrants, Parsee, June 1854 (PROV VPRS 14)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&rows=60&q=Warren%20AND%20Parsee%20AND%20series_id:14"]]},
drwebb:{"n":"Dr Charles Houlton Webb","d":"1818–1870","b":1818,"x":1870,"s":"p","p":["revwebb","susan"],"r":["uk","au"],"ch":"yarra","t":"A London surgeon who sailed on the Hooghly in 1848, leaving his wife Ann behind. He is said to have married again in South Australia within six months, though no record of that marriage has been found. He became the first doctor of Clare, ran the Clare Inn and was mayor when he died in 1870. About 160 vehicles followed his funeral.\n\nHe was gazetted a Justice of the Peace in October 1857. In May 1858 he carried on a sharp public quarrel in the Register with E. B. Gleeson over the District Council's accounts, writing that he had been nearly ten years in the colony and had always paid his debts in full. Gleeson became Clare's first mayor, and on his death the townspeople elected Dr Webb to succeed him. He was medical officer to the Oddfellows and the Foresters, who walked at his funeral in their regalia on 11 June 1870, while members of the Corporation carried the coffin.\n\nHe died early on Thursday 9 June 1870 at his house, Windsor Lodge, Clare, in his 52nd year. He had been unwell for some time, but nothing seemed serious until the Monday before, so the news surprised the town; the paper put it down to a general decay of the whole system. Besides his private practice he had been Government Medical Officer for the district, doctor to the Catholic Benefit Society since it began, and several times chairman of the District Council. He had been elected mayor only the previous November. He was buried in the Church of England cemetery at Clare.\n\nThe barque Hooghly, 460 tons, Captain Henry, left London in mid-August 1848, called at Plymouth and reached Port Adelaide on 5 December 1848 with about 130 passengers. The South Australian listed him among the cabin passengers as \"— Webb, surgeon\"; the newspaper does not say that he was the ship's surgeon. The official South Australian passenger index describes him as \"Chas Webb, surgeon, publican\". An Elizabeth Meyler and an Edward Meyler were also on board; online family trees say Elizabeth Meyler became his second wife, but no record of that marriage has yet been found.\n\nClare was laid out in the 1840s by Edward Burton Gleeson, who named it after his home county in Ireland, and it grew into the main town of a farming and wine district north of Adelaide.\n\nThe national marriage index confirms his first marriage: Charles Houlton Webb married Ann Agar in the June quarter of 1839 in the St George, Hanover Square district. A son, Charles Joseph Webb, was registered in the St Martin in the Fields district in the June quarter of 1842, and Elliott Agar Webb in the March quarter of 1849.","src":[["Adelaide Observer, 17 Oct 1857: Government Gazette appointments","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article158116574"],["South Australian Register, 27 May 1858: Reports from Clare (letter)","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article49774592"],["South Australian Chronicle, 18 Jun 1870: Clare, June 11","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article92285516"],["Northern Argus, 10 Jun 1870: death of Dr Webb","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article92777857"],["The South Australian, 8 Dec 1848: Shipping Intelligence","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article71613125"],["Passengers in History: Hooghly, 1848","https://passengersinhistory.history.sa.gov.au/node/946838"],["Marriage index, June quarter 1839, St George Hanover Square 1/31; births index, St Martin 1842 (1/131) and 1849 (1/133)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
annagar:{"n":"Ann Agar","d":"b. c. 1815 · London","b":1815,"s":"p","p":["peteragar","anndouglass"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Ann Agar was born in about 1815 in the parish of St Martin in the Fields, London, the daughter of Peter Agar and Ann Douglass. St Martin's was a crowded parish around Charing Cross, between the Strand and Covent Garden.\n\nOn 16 May 1839 she married Charles Houlton Webb, a young surgeon and the son of a Windsor minor canon, at St George's, Hanover Square. Her youngest son, Elliott Agar Webb, was born at Vestry House, St Martin in the Fields, on 7 January 1849. By then her husband had already sailed for South Australia on the Hooghly, reaching Port Adelaide in December 1848, and by the family's account he married again there within six months, though no record of that marriage has been found. Ann stayed in London with Elliott. The family tree has her in the 1861 census, but that entry has not been found in the free indexes.\n\nElliott later followed his father to South Australia. When he died in Melbourne in 1928, his death registration gave his mother's name as Ann Elliot rather than Ann Agar, so the records disagree on her surname. Her death has not been found.\n\nThe marriage index records the wedding in the June quarter of 1839 in the St George, Hanover Square district, with the groom entered in full as Charles Houlton Webb. Two of her sons appear in the birth index for the St Martin in the Fields district: Charles Joseph Webb in the June quarter of 1842 and Elliott Agar Webb in the March quarter of 1849. Online family trees name further children, Samuel Houlton and Louisa Ann, but their births were not found in the St Martin, Strand or St George Hanover Square indexes for 1839 to 1848.\n\nBoth the marriage index and her son's own birth registration as Elliott Agar support the surname Agar. The \"Elliot\" in his 1928 death entry matches his own first name, which suggests a mix-up by whoever gave the details, though that cannot be proved. The St Martin's Vestry Hall, in St Martin's Place, was built in about 1830 as part of the Charing Cross improvements.","src":[["Death index of her son Elliot Agar Webb, 1928 (reg. 3533/1928)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Marriage index, June quarter 1839, St George Hanover Square 1/31; births index, St Martin 1842 (1/131) and 1849 (1/133)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Survey of London, vol. 20: The Vestry Hall, Vicarage and Church Schools","https://prod.british-history.ac.uk/node/59411"]]},
charlesh:{"n":"Charles Hicks","d":"1829–1910 · Alwalton, Huntingdonshire","b":1829,"x":1910,"s":"p","p":["williamhicks","smelizabethhelmes"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Huntingdon. A farm labourer, he married Ann Spencer at Chesterton, Huntingdonshire, on 12 November 1850, and sailed for South Australia on the Telegraph, arriving in 1855. He farmed at Caltowie and later at Clarendon, where he died on 29 September 1910.\n\nHis church's obituary fills in the years between. He lived first near Prospect, then at Walkerville, and at Caltowie he farmed on what it called a somewhat extensive scale, until bad seasons sent him to Clarendon, where he lived for his last 23 years. About 1897 a severe accident cost him a leg. He was a steady member of the Clarendon Methodist church, and died at the home of his son-in-law, George White, aged 81, leaving two sons and two daughters.\n\nHe was baptised at Alwalton, Huntingdonshire, on 20 September 1829, the son of William Hicks, a labourer, and Elizabeth; the family tree's \"born at Huntingdon\" means the county rather than the town. In 1841 he was living in the High Street at Alwalton with his parents, his brother George and baby sister Hannah. Alwalton lies on the River Nene just west of Peterborough, next to Chesterton, where he married.\n\nHis children born in England were Martha (late 1851), Sarah (late 1852) and William Spencer Hicks, baptised at Alwalton on 7 May 1854, when Charles was a labourer living in the village. In June 1854 he witnessed his brother George's marriage to Charlotte Rayner at Alwalton, only months before the family left for South Australia.\n\nThe Telegraph, Captain J. Irvine, left Southampton on 27 October 1854 with more than 400 emigrants and reached Port Adelaide on 22 January 1855. The passenger list names Charles Hicks, farmer, his wife Ann and the children Martha and William; their daughter Sarah, born in 1852, is not on it. A George Hicks, agricultural labourer, and a William Hicks were on the same ship, but it has not been shown whether they were Charles's brother and father.","src":[["Australian Christian Commonwealth, 9 Dec 1910: Our Sainted Dead, Mr Charles Hicks","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article214049151"],["Baptism, Alwalton, 1829","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825dc865574838e90fd66"],["Passengers in History: Telegraph, 1854–55","https://passengers.history.sa.gov.au/node/942321"],["Telegraph passenger list (Hicks entries)","https://passengers.history.sa.gov.au/voyage-passengers-all/942321/28/10/1854%20-%2023/01/1855?page=6"],["Census 1841, Alwalton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64f0b932f493fd6cd8db826e"],["Marriage index, Dec quarter 1850, Peterborough 15/693","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Baptism of son William Spencer Hicks, Alwalton, 1854","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825e9865574838e910522"],["Marriage of George Hicks and Charlotte Rayner, Alwalton, 1854","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68382665865574838e9133fc"]]},
anns:{"n":"Ann Spencer","d":"c. 1827–1890","b":1827,"x":1890,"s":"p","p":["georgespencer",null],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Ann Spencer was born in about 1827, the daughter of George Spencer. On 12 November 1850 she married Charles Hicks, a farm labourer from Alwalton, at Chesterton in Huntingdonshire, a small parish on the River Nene a few miles west of Peterborough.\n\nTheir daughters Martha and Sarah were born in 1851 and 1852, and their son William Spencer Hicks, who carried her surname, was baptised at Alwalton on 7 May 1854. The family sailed from Southampton on the emigrant ship Telegraph, Captain J. Irvine, on 27 October 1854 and reached Port Adelaide on 22 January 1855; the list names Charles, a farmer, Ann, and the children Martha and William. Sarah, born in 1852, is not on it. They lived first near Prospect and then at Walkerville, on the edge of Adelaide, before moving north to farm at Wasleys and at Caltowie, where Charles farmed on a fairly large scale until bad seasons drove them south.\n\nAbout 1887 they settled at Clarendon, in the hills south of Adelaide. Ann died there on 10 September 1890, aged 63, after a long illness. Her death notice described her as the wife of Charles Hicks of Clarendon, late of Caltowie and Wasleys.\n\nAn Ann Spencer, daughter of George Spencer, a carpenter, and his wife Rhoda, was baptised at Bythorn, Huntingdonshire, on 17 June 1827. Her father's name and her age fit, but nothing yet proves she is the Ann who married Charles Hicks.","src":[["The Advertiser, 17 Sep 1890: death notice","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article26761629"],["Marriage index, Dec quarter 1850, Peterborough 15/693","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Telegraph passenger list (Hicks entries)","https://passengers.history.sa.gov.au/voyage-passengers-all/942321/28/10/1854%20-%2023/01/1855?page=6"],["Baptism of son William Spencer Hicks, Alwalton, 1854","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825e9865574838e910522"],["Australian Christian Commonwealth, 9 Dec 1910: obituary of Charles Hicks","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article214049151"],["Baptism of Ann Spencer, Bythorn, 17 Jun 1827","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6708ff09b18f7c7954fe262e"]]},

hindrik:{"n":"Hindrik Harms Broesder","d":"1782–1863","b":1782,"x":1863,"s":"b","p":["harm","anna"],"r":["nl"],"ch":"groningen","t":"Baptised at Midwolda on 21 April 1782, the first known to carry the name Broesder; fixed surnames were made compulsory in 1811, under Napoleon. A labourer, he married Eisse (Aijsse) Tjaards at Finsterwolde on 22 March 1807, and died at Midwolda on 14 December 1863, aged 81.\n\nHe was born on 13 April 1782 and had one older brother, Derk. The records call him a day labourer (dagloner) in 1815 and a labourer ever after. His children were Tjaard (1808), Harm (about 1811), Pieter (1812), Wupke (1815) and Derk (about 1817). The clerks spelled the new surname as they heard it: Broesder, Broeisder, and for his son Derk, Broezer.\n\nHis father died and his mother married again; the name of her second husband is not certain. When she died at Midwolda in 1827 the record said she left one son of her first marriage, so by then his brother Derk was dead and Hindrik was the only one left of that family.","src":[["Baptism, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2c192c04-d624-e42b-8bab-b9fc77794106"],["Marriage, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48e1fbf9-f27a-6d21-c3f1-ebfc60d7625d"],["Death record, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d13597c5-02a0-abe4-5214-c429f0d0fac1"],["Birth of Wupke, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3ecdc844-e7c9-3a60-af9d-4659b493c2e8"],["Marriage of Derk Hindriks Broezer, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:79ca7752-5917-c56a-8454-cb71b8ca3818"],["Death of Anna Hindriks, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ff0d016d-74ec-9bcc-f61c-458cea54bd9b"]]},
eisse:{"n":"Eisse Tjaards Grein","d":"1784–1880 · Finsterwolde","b":1784,"x":1880,"s":"b","p":["tjaartp","wupke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Finsterwolde on 17 April 1784 and baptised there on 25 April, the daughter of Tjaart Pieters and Wupke Roelfs. She married Hindrik Harms Broesder at Finsterwolde on 22 March 1807. She outlived him by sixteen years and died at Meerland, Midwolda, on 4 May 1880, aged 96.\n\nShe and Hindrik had Tjaard (1808), Harm (about 1811), Pieter (8 November 1812), Wupke (13 March 1815), named for her mother, and Derk (about 1817). All five grew up, married and stayed in the Oldambt, the sons as farmhands and labourers. She is called a labourer herself (arbeidster) in 1844. Four of her children lived past eighty, and Wupke reached 89.\n\nHer parents' families are now known. She was named for her father's mother, Eise Thijes of Oostwold, and her younger sister Grietje (1789) carried the name of her mother's mother, Grietje Eilderts of Finsterwolde.","src":[["Baptism, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483118de-9d0a-1a95-84e3-0411ad06a678"],["Marriage, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48e1fbf9-f27a-6d21-c3f1-ebfc60d7625d"],["Death record, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:684ee992-43b3-5778-b6fa-ede0895412e3"],["Birth of Wupke, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3ecdc844-e7c9-3a60-af9d-4659b493c2e8"],["Marriage of Pieter, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:769e7582-ff57-81d7-d87b-0328382e8f8c"],["Baptism of her father Tjaart, Oostwold 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83c221f4-283f-ad04-9bc3-123c78f80bc7"],["Baptism of her mother Wupke, Finsterwolde 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aa5dd05f-e70c-79e8-ca5d-2db06cc5c5cb"]]},
virgil1:{"n":"Virgil Frankish","d":"1757–1829 · Hibaldstow","b":1757,"x":1829,"s":"f","p":["virgil0","mary0"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Baptised at Hibaldstow on 16 October 1757, son of Virgil and Mary; the clerk entered both father and son as \"Virgin\". He was their third son, after John and Robert, who had been baptised at Frodingham.\n\nBy his late twenties he was living at Elsham, a village at the foot of the Wolds about four miles north-east of Brigg. He married Ann Hickson at Elsham on 25 June 1787, both of them described as of that parish. The witnesses were Edward and Jane Jackling; Edward Jackling had also witnessed the wedding of a John Frankish and Elizabeth Stamp at Elsham in 1780. Virgil and Ann had three sons baptised there: Robert on 10 June 1788, John on 20 March 1791 and Thomas on 1 September 1793. His father was buried at Hibaldstow five weeks after Robert's baptism.\n\nRobert stayed in Elsham as a labourer and named his own first son Virgil in 1825. Thomas, the youngest, went north to Barrow upon Humber and then to Hull. Virgil was buried at Elsham on 16 April 1829, aged 69 by the register, though his baptism would make him 71. Ann outlived him by five years.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Baptism, Hibaldstow, 16 Oct 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841fd7a865574f305081cf9"],["Marriage, Elsham, 25 Jun 1787 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416364865574f305c7c6b1"],["Baptism of son Robert, Elsham, 10 Jun 1788 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684162f5865574f305c7a948"],["Baptism of son John, Elsham, 20 Mar 1791 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684162f6865574f305c7a9bc"],["Baptism of son Thomas, Elsham, 1 Sep 1793 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684162f7865574f305c7aa10"],["Marriage of John Frankish and Elizabeth Stamp, Elsham, 1780 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416363865574f305c7c666"],["Baptism of grandson Virgil, Elsham, 1825 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684162ab865574f305c78858"],["Burial, Elsham, 16 Apr 1829 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841632e865574f305c7b93e"]]},
ann:{"n":"Ann Hickson","d":"c. 1756–1834","b":1756,"x":1834,"s":"f","p":["frjohnhickson","frmaryhickson"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Ann Hickson was of Elsham when she married Virgil Frankish there on 25 June 1787. They had three sons baptised at Elsham: Robert (1788), John (1791) and Thomas (1793). She outlived Virgil by five years and was buried at Elsham on 16 June 1834, aged 78, eight months after her youngest son Thomas had been brought back from Hull to be buried in the same churchyard.\n\nHer age at burial puts her birth about 1755 or 1756. In the registers transcribed for Lincolnshire, the only Ann Hickson baptised in the 1750s was at Brocklesby, about eight miles east of Elsham, on 10 September 1754, the daughter of John and Mary Hickson, and they are shown here as her parents. The link is unproven: it rests on the name, the date and the short distance, and that baptism would make her 79, not 78, when she died.\n\nSome later records fit the Brocklesby family having moved towards Elsham. A Mary Hixon of Elsham married Robert Dee there in 1780, and an Elizabeth and a Maria Hickson married at Wrawby, the next parish, in 1781 and 1783; Elizabeth and Mary were the names of Ann's older sisters at Brocklesby. A John Hickson and a Mary Hickson were buried at Wrawby in 1776 and 1778. None of these records names a parent, so the link stays unproven.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Marriage, Elsham, 25 Jun 1787 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416364865574f305c7c6b1"],["Burial, Elsham, 16 Jun 1834 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416330865574f305c7b9a8"],["Baptism of Ann Hickson, Brocklesby, 10 Sep 1754 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59c0f493fd6830701aa6"],["Marriage of Robert Dee and Mary Hixon, both of Elsham, 28 Nov 1780 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416363865574f305c7c672"],["Marriage of William Buley and Elizabeth Hickson, Wrawby, 22 May 1781 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edeb8d0f493fd400f3696e6"],["Marriage of William Wilkinson and Maria Hickson, Wrawby, 11 May 1783 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edeb8d2f493fd400f369787"],["Burial of John Hickson, Wrawby, 5 Apr 1776 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ede915ff493fd400f35ad6c"],["Burial of Mary Hickson, Wrawby, 21 Oct 1778 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ede9168f493fd400f35af64"]],"u":true},
revwebb:{"n":"Rev. Richard Webb","d":"c. 1770–1829 · Gloucester","b":1770,"x":1829,"s":"p","p":["richards","maryb"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Richard Webb was born in Gloucester in about 1770, the son of Richard Webb, a staymaker and freeman of the city, and Mary Baylis. In 1782 he became a chorister at Magdalen College, Oxford. He matriculated there on 15 May 1787, aged 16, entered as the son of a commoner (\"pleb.\"), and stayed on as a lay clerk of the college from 1790 to 1799. He took his BA in 1791 and his MA in 1796, and was also chaplain of New College.\n\nHe became a Minor Canon of St Paul's Cathedral and of Westminster Abbey, and a Priest in Ordinary to the King. Minor canons were the clergy who sang the daily services in a cathedral or royal chapel. He was vicar of Kensworth, then in Hertfordshire, from 1802, and in 1808 he published a collection of madrigals. In 1809 he was elected a Minor Canon of St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle, and from 1812 he was also Conduct (chaplain) of Eton College. From 1814 to 1826 he held the London living of St Mary Magdalen with St Gregory.\n\nHe married Susan Houlton at St George's, Hanover Square, on 6 July 1814, by licence. The printed register describes him as a widower of the parish of St John the Evangelist, Westminster, and Susan as a spinster of St George's parish, so he had been married before; his first wife has not been identified. Their sons included George Houlton Webb and Charles Houlton Webb, born in 1818, who became a surgeon and later the first doctor and mayor of Clare in South Australia.\n\nRichard died at Windsor on 13 April 1829, aged about 58, and is buried in the Dean's Cloister of St George's Chapel.\n\nThe Clergy of the Church of England database records his ordination as deacon on 20 December 1795 and as priest on 22 May 1796, both by Edward Smallwell, Bishop of Oxford. It also lists him as vicar of Kensworth from 12 May 1802, as domestic chaplain to Peniston Lamb, 1st Viscount Melbourne, from 8 July 1814, two days after his second wedding, and as vicar of Wyrardisbury (Wraysbury), Buckinghamshire, from 1 July 1824. It dates the end of his posts to 25 June 1829, which is when his livings were recorded as vacant, not his death.\n\nE. H. Fellowes, in his history of the minor canons of St George's Chapel, records that he was elected a minor canon of Windsor on 25 April 1809, succeeding John Hand, and that he held the living of Langley cum Wraysbury from 2 January 1824 until his death; the clergy database gives 1 July 1824 instead, so the two sources differ on the date. He died on 13 April 1829 and was buried at St George's Chapel on 20 April. Fellowes also notes the tombstone of his daughter Harriet, buried on 30 January 1812, in the south-west corner of the Cloisters. Harriet died two years before Richard married Susan Houlton, so she was a child of his first marriage, whose bride has still not been identified.\n\nThe Gentleman's Magazine obituary of 1829 adds that he had been chaplain of both New College and Magdalen College, that the Dean and Chapter of St Paul's presented him to Kensworth in 1802 and to St Mary Magdalen with St Gregory in London in 1814, and that he had resigned the London living two or three years before he died.","src":[["Alumni Oxonienses 1715–1886, vol. 4 (Wikisource transcription)","https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Alumni_Oxoniensis_(1715-1886)_volume_4.djvu/318"],["Marriage register of St George, Hanover Square, vol. III (Harleian Society), 6 July 1814","https://archive.org/stream/registerbookofma22stge/registerbookofma22stge_djvu.txt"],["Clergy of the Church of England database, person 38429","https://theclergydatabase.org.uk/jsp/persons/DisplayPerson.jsp?PersonID=38429"],["E. H. Fellowes, The Vicars or Minor Canons of St George's Chapel, Windsor (St George's Chapel monograph 5), p. 103","https://www.stgeorges-windsor.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Monograph-Vol-5.pdf"],["Gentleman's Magazine, vol. 99 part 1 (1829), obituary, p. 474","https://archive.org/details/gentlemansmagaz236unkngoog"]]},
susan:{"n":"Susan Houlton","d":"m. 1814 London","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Susan Houlton married the Reverend Richard Webb, a Minor Canon of St George's Chapel, Windsor, and Conduct of Eton College, at St George's, Hanover Square, London, on 6 July 1814. The marriage was by licence; the register calls her a spinster of that parish and Richard a widower of St John the Evangelist, Westminster. St George's was the parish church of Mayfair and one of the most popular places in London to marry.\n\nHer surname became a family middle name: her sons were George Houlton Webb and Charles Houlton Webb, born in 1818, who trained as a surgeon and became the first doctor and later mayor of Clare in South Australia. Her husband also held the London living of St Mary Magdalen with St Gregory from 1814 to 1826, as well as his posts at Windsor.\n\nRichard died at Windsor on 13 April 1829, leaving Susan a widow; Charles was then ten. Her own birth, family and death have not been found; no Houlton entries of the right years appear in the free parish transcripts for London, Middlesex, Berkshire and neighbouring counties.","src":[["Alumni Oxonienses 1715–1886, vol. 4 (entry for her husband Richard Webb)","https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Alumni_Oxoniensis_(1715-1886)_volume_4.djvu/318"],["Marriage register of St George, Hanover Square, vol. III (Harleian Society), 6 July 1814","https://archive.org/stream/registerbookofma22stge/registerbookofma22stge_djvu.txt"]]},

harm:{"n":"Harm Derks","d":"m. 1780 Midwolda","s":"b","p":[],"q":"He came \"from Bonda\", most likely Bunde across the border in East Frisia, where his baptism would be","r":["nl","de"],"t":"A labourer, recorded in his banns as \"van Bonda\", a name that matches Bunde, the East Frisian village just across the border, though no record confirms which place was meant. The banns were read at Oostwold on 23 April 1780 and he married Anna Hindriks at Midwolda on 21 May 1780. Their sons Derk (1781) and Hindrik (1782) were born at Midwolda.\n\nMidwolda and Oostwold lie in the Oldambt, the heavy clay country of east Groningen, where a few big grain farmers owned the land and most men worked for them by the day. Harm was one of those labourers, and when his son Hindrik died in 1863 the record still described him as one (arbeider), and Anna as a labourer too. Derk, the elder boy, was born on 10 March 1781 and Hindrik on 13 April 1782.\n\nNo burial entry for him has been found. He died before his wife: her death record of 1827 says she left one son from her first marriage, so she had married again. That son was Hindrik, so Derk had died by then. Other men called Harm Derks lived at Midwolda in the same years; one died there in 1814 aged 84, with a wife named Antje, and they are not to be confused with him. His parents are not named in any record found. The church registers of Bunde are not in the Dutch indexes.","src":[["Banns, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1f2870cb-6677-f35a-ced4-50fdcc25cbdf"],["Marriage, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2123db4a-e69d-445e-2c01-3becf692fbbd"],["Baptism of Hindrik, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2c192c04-d624-e42b-8bab-b9fc77794106"],["Baptism of Derk, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3fd01991-6d8a-5b74-81be-d8058b96663e"],["Death of Anna Hindriks, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ff0d016d-74ec-9bcc-f61c-458cea54bd9b"],["Death of another Harm Derks, Midwolda 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:96ee0880-c7d3-54b3-2e26-8a3074a3dbbe"]]},
anna:{"n":"Anna Hindriks","d":"1755–1827","b":1755,"x":1827,"s":"b","p":["hindrikslik","lupke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Nieuw-Scheemda on 27 July 1755, the first child of Hindrik Jans Slik and Lupke Ides, who had married there that January. Her death record gives her birthday as 1 July 1755. Her father died while she was small, and in November 1761 her mother married Hindrik Harms of Woldendorp. By 1764 the family had moved to Midwolda. She had a sister, Stijntje (1758), who became a farmer's wife at Westerlee, and a half-brother Harm (1762) and half-sister Ida (1764).\n\nIn 1780, described as 'of Nieuw-Scheemda', she married Harm Derks, a labourer 'of Bonda'. The banns were read at Oostwold on 23 April and they married at Midwolda on 21 May. Their sons Derk and Hindrik were born on 10 March 1781 and 13 April 1782. Hindrik was the first of the family to be called Broesder.\n\nHarm Derks died, and she married again: her death record says she left one son from her first marriage. The record also names a Hindrik Harms Oltman without stating how he was related to her, and no record of her second marriage has been found. She worked as a labourer (arbeidster). She died at Midwolda on 28 April 1827, aged 71. The clerk wrote down her stepfather, Hindrik Harms, as her father and called her mother Lupke Hindriks. The baptism register, her Nieuw-Scheemda origin and the birth date together show that she was the daughter of Hindrik Slik.","src":[["Baptism, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:43d4f406-8cd7-0911-f7d0-d0864ae31dc9"],["Marriage, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2123db4a-e69d-445e-2c01-3becf692fbbd"],["Death record, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ff0d016d-74ec-9bcc-f61c-458cea54bd9b"],["Banns, Oostwold 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1f2870cb-6677-f35a-ced4-50fdcc25cbdf"],["Baptism of Derk, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3fd01991-6d8a-5b74-81be-d8058b96663e"],["Baptism of Hindrik, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2c192c04-d624-e42b-8bab-b9fc77794106"],["Death of Hindrik Harms Broesder, 1863 (mother a labourer)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d13597c5-02a0-abe4-5214-c429f0d0fac1"]]},
virgil0:{"n":"Virgil Frankish","d":"m. 1752 Frodingham, d. 1788","x":1788,"s":"f","p":["virgil1705","susannah"],"u":true,"r":["uk"],"t":"He married Mary Ellis at Frodingham on 30 March 1752, after banns; the register spells them Virgil Frankiss and Mary Elliss. Their son John was baptised there on 16 September 1753 and their son Robert was baptised there in 1755, on 2 July, the family's home given each time as Frodingham. By 16 October 1757 they had moved a few miles south-east to Hibaldstow, near Brigg, where their third son Virgil was baptised; the clerk wrote both father and son down as \"Virgin\". He was buried at Hibaldstow on 16 July 1788.\n\nFrodingham was then a small farming village east of the River Trent. Ironstone began to be dug from its fields around 1860, and it is now part of the steel town of Scunthorpe.\n\nHis name and age match the Virgil baptised at Caistor in 1724, the youngest son of Virgil Frankish and Susannah Dales, and that is the link shown here; no single record joins the two, so it is marked as unproven. One thing supports it. The Caistor Virgil had a brother John, baptised in 1719, and a John Frankish with a wife Mary was already having children baptised at Hibaldstow in 1746 and 1749, before Virgil arrived there.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Marriage, Frodingham, 30 Mar 1752 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68417ce2865574f305d2bbcd"],["Baptism of son John, Frodingham, 16 Sep 1753 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68417c38865574f305d28ac8"],["Baptism of son Robert, Frodingham, 2 Jul 1755 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68417c0c865574f305d27ae2"],["Baptism of son Virgil, Hibaldstow, 16 Oct 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841fd7a865574f305081cf9"],["Burial, Hibaldstow, 16 Jul 1788 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841fe7f865574f305089229"],["Baptism of Virgil, son of Virgil, Caistor, 2 Mar 1724 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52fa6f493fda395f41d8b"],["Baptism of John, son of John and Mary Frankish, Hibaldstow, 1746 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841fd75865574f3050819e9"]]},
mary0:{"n":"Mary Ellis","d":"m. 1752 Frodingham","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Married Virgil Frankish at Frodingham on 30 March 1752, after banns; the register spells her name Mary Elliss. Their first two sons, John (1753) and Robert (1755), were baptised at Frodingham, and the third, Virgil, at Hibaldstow on 16 October 1757, by which time the family had moved the few miles to that village just south of Brigg. Her husband was buried there in 1788. Her son Virgil settled at Elsham, on the edge of the Wolds, and carried the line on.\n\nHer baptism has not been identified. Two girls named Mary Ellis were baptised in the right years at Flixborough, a few miles north of Frodingham: one on 16 September 1722, daughter of Robert and Jane Ellis, and one on 17 December 1727, baptised with her sister Dorothy as the daughters of Francis and Elizabeth Ellis. Nothing found so far shows which of them, if either, was the bride of 1752.","src":[["Marriage, Frodingham, 30 Mar 1752 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68417ce2865574f305d2bbcd"],["Baptism of son Virgil, Hibaldstow, 16 Oct 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6841fd7a865574f305081cf9"],["Baptism of Mary Ellis, Flixborough, 1722 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/61979b88f493fd1809dcc104"],["Baptism of Dorothy and Mary Ellis, Flixborough, 1727 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/61979b8af493fd1809dcc1b0"]]},
richards:{"n":"Richard Webb","d":"c. 1740–by 1794 · Gloucester","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Richard Webb was a staymaker and a freeman of the City of Gloucester. Stays were the stiffened bodices, laced and boned with whalebone, that women wore under their dresses, and in the eighteenth century staymaking was a skilled trade carried on mostly by men. Being a freeman gave him the right to trade in the city and to vote in its elections.\n\nHe married Mary Baylis, and their son Richard was born in about 1770. Their son became a chorister at Magdalen College, Oxford, in 1782, matriculated there in 1787, where the college register called him the son of Richard Webb of the city of Gloucester, and went on to be a Minor Canon of St George's Chapel, Windsor.\n\nRichard senior had died by 1794. His baptism, marriage and burial have not been found, because the Gloucester city parish registers are not in the free transcripts searched.\n\nHis marriage licence survives in the records of the Diocese of Gloucester. On 13 February 1768 Richard Webb, of the parish of Holy Trinity in the City of Gloucester, staymaker, a bachelor aged 28, swore that he intended to marry Mary Baylis, a spinster aged 23, of the precincts of the College in the City of Gloucester. The \"College\" was the precinct of Gloucester Cathedral. He signed the bond himself, and his bondsman was Ralph Adey, a gentleman of Holy Trinity parish; the two were bound in £500. The wedding was to take place at St Mary de Lode, the church beside the cathedral close.\n\nThis puts his birth in about 1740. Between 1771 and 1774 a Richard Webb, staymaker of the City of Gloucester, also stood as witness to at least four other marriage bonds in the diocese.","src":[["Alumni Oxonienses 1715–1886, vol. 4 (entry for his son Richard Webb)","https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Alumni_Oxoniensis_(1715-1886)_volume_4.djvu/318"],["Marriage licence bond, Webb and Baylis, 13 Feb 1768 (GDR/Q1/1768/53)","https://catalogue.gloucestershire.gov.uk/records/GDR/17/1/8511"],["Marriage licence allegation, Webb and Baylis, 13 Feb 1768 (GDR/Q3/64)","https://catalogue.gloucestershire.gov.uk/records/GDR/17/3/64/9"],["Marriage bond witnessed by Richard Webb, staymaker, 1773 (GDR/Q1/1773/118)","https://catalogue.gloucestershire.gov.uk/records/GDR/17/1/10552"]],"b":1740},
maryb:{"n":"Mary Baylis","d":"c. 1745 · Gloucester","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mary Baylis was the wife of Richard Webb, a staymaker and freeman of the City of Gloucester. Their son Richard was born in Gloucester in about 1770.\n\nYoung Richard was taken as a chorister at Magdalen College, Oxford, in 1782, at about eleven, and went on to a career in the Church and in music: he became a Minor Canon of St Paul's, Westminster Abbey and St George's Chapel, Windsor, a Priest in Ordinary to the King and Conduct of Eton College. Her husband had died by 1794.\n\nHer own baptism, her marriage and her death have not been found, as the Gloucester city parish registers are not among the free transcripts searched.\n\nHer marriage licence of 13 February 1768 describes her as Mary Baylis, spinster, aged 23, living within the precincts of the College, the close of Gloucester Cathedral, so she was born in about 1745. Her bridegroom was Richard Webb, a staymaker of Holy Trinity parish, aged 28, and the licence allowed them to marry at St Mary de Lode, the parish church next to the cathedral.","src":[["Alumni Oxonienses 1715–1886, vol. 4 (entry for her son Richard Webb)","https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Alumni_Oxoniensis_(1715-1886)_volume_4.djvu/318"],["Marriage licence allegation, Webb and Baylis, 13 Feb 1768 (GDR/Q3/64)","https://catalogue.gloucestershire.gov.uk/records/GDR/17/3/64/9"],["Marriage licence bond, Webb and Baylis, 13 Feb 1768 (GDR/Q1/1768/53)","https://catalogue.gloucestershire.gov.uk/records/GDR/17/1/8511"]],"b":1745},
virgil1705:{"n":"Virgil Frankish","d":"b. 1683, m. 1705 Caistor","b":1683,"s":"f","p":["virgil1668","tabitha"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Baptised at Caistor on 22 July 1683, son of Virgil and Tabitha, the youngest of his father's twelve recorded children by two wives. He was sixteen when a Virgil Frankish, most likely his father, was buried there in November 1699.\n\nHe married Susannah Dales at Caistor on 8 May 1705; she had been baptised in the same church in 1682. Their children Thomas, Edward, William, John and Virgil (1724) were all baptised there: Thomas under 24 February 1705 (probably early 1706 by modern reckoning, since the year then began on 25 March), a first Virgil who was baptised and buried in 1708, Edward on 9 February 1710, William on 29 April 1716, John on 9 August 1719 and the second Virgil on 2 March 1724.\n\nSusan, wife of Virgil Frankish, was buried at Caistor on 13 August 1728. The register transcript also dates the marriage of a Virgil Frankish to Sarah Green to 26 March 1728, and a son Robert was baptised to Virgil and Sarah in January 1729. The two 1728 dates do not fit together, so either one entry is mis-dated or there was a second Virgil in the town. A Virgil Frankish was buried at Caistor on 2 September 1737.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Baptism, Caistor, 22 Jul 1683 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1ef493fda395f40e0b"],["Marriage to Susannah Dales, Caistor, 8 May 1705 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c5f865574f305a9b2da"],["Baptism of son Thomas, Caistor (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f65f493fda395f41652"],["Baptism of son John, Caistor, 9 Aug 1719 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f9cf493fda395f41c40"],["Baptism of son Virgil, Caistor, 2 Mar 1724 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52fa6f493fda395f41d8b"],["Burial of Susan, wife of Virgil, Caistor, 13 Aug 1728 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b80865574f305a96677"],["Marriage of Virgil Frankish and Sarah Green, Caistor, 26 Mar 1728 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c67865574f305a9b566"],["Burial of Virgil Frankish, Caistor, 2 Sep 1737 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b87865574f305a9687d"]]},
susannah:{"n":"Susannah Dales","d":"1682–1728 · Caistor","s":"f","p":["fredwarddales","frruthdales"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Baptised at Caistor on 29 October 1682, the daughter of Edward and Ruth Dales. She was the youngest of their children in the register: a brother John had been baptised in 1673, and a brother Thomas and sisters Ruth and Elizabeth were buried as small children in 1676 and 1681, the year before she was born. An Edward Dales married Anne Skepper at Caistor in July 1683 and a Ruth Dales married John Hudson there in 1693; the registers do not say how they were related to her.\n\nShe married Virgil Frankish at Caistor on 8 May 1705, aged 22. They had six children baptised there: Thomas, a first Virgil who died as a baby in 1708, Edward, William, John and a second Virgil in 1724, through whom the line runs. The clerks wrote her name as Susanna, Susan and Susannah by turns.\n\nSusan, wife of Virgil Frankish, was buried at Caistor on 13 August 1728. An entry in the same register dated 26 March 1728 has a Virgil Frankish marrying Sarah Green, which cannot be right if both dates are correct and both entries are about her husband.","b":1682,"x":1728,"src":[["Baptism, Caistor, 29 Oct 1682 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1cf493fda395f40d9a"],["Marriage, Caistor, 8 May 1705 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c5f865574f305a9b2da"],["Burial of Susan, wife of Virgil Frankish, Caistor, 13 Aug 1728 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b80865574f305a96677"],["Marriage of Virgil Frankish and Sarah Green, Caistor, 26 Mar 1728 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c67865574f305a9b566"],["Marriage of Edward Dales and Anne Skepper, Caistor, 1683 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c5a865574f305a9b125"]]},
virgil1668:{"n":"Virgil Frankish","d":"m. 1657 and 1668, d. 1699","x":1699,"s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"img":"img/caistor.jpg","ch":"frankish","t":"The earliest Frankish in the line. He first appears by his full name on 23 November 1657, when Virgil Frankish of Marshchapel, a widower, married Mary Pinder, spinster, at Caistor; the marriage was entered again the next day in the register of neighbouring Rothwell. Marshchapel is a village on the coastal marsh between Grimsby and Louth, about fifteen miles east of Caistor. No baptism has been found for him, so his year of birth is not known.\n\nHe had been married before. On 7 February 1650 the Marshchapel register records the marriage of a groom written down only as \"Virgill\" to Ellen Swabye, whose family had lived in the parish for two generations. The clerk left out the groom's surname, so the link is not certain, but Virgil was a very rare name and this is probably Virgil Frankish's first marriage. The transcribed Marshchapel registers have no entries from the mid-1650s to 1670, so Ellen's burial has not been found. The name itself was not new there: a Virgill Howson was baptised at Marshchapel in 1598 and buried the next year.\n\nHe and Mary Pinder had six children baptised or buried at Caistor between 1658 and 1668: Thomas, Virgil, Eliza, two Williams and Elizabeth, three of whom were buried as small children. Mary was buried on 12 February 1668, five weeks after Elizabeth's baptism, and after her death he married Tabitha Patchet there on 3 May 1668. They had six more children: Elizabeth (1669–1673), Thomas (1672), Susanna (1675–1677), Anne (1678), Tabitha, buried twelve days after her baptism in February 1681, and Virgil, baptised on 22 July 1683, through whom the line runs. Caistor is a small market town on the edge of the Lincolnshire Wolds, on the site of a Roman walled town; in 1681 a fire destroyed much of it. A Virgil Frankish was buried at Caistor on 6 November 1699.\n\nFamily tradition, as Phyllis tells it, holds that the Frankishes were Huguenots, Protestants from near Flanders who left Catholic France for England more than 300 years ago. The records show the surname in north Lincolnshire well before Virgil's time. A Janett Frankish was baptised at Swallow in 1561. The Lincoln probate calendars list wills or administrations of Frankishes at Nettleton (1559, 1561 and 1596), Utterby near Louth (1580), Cabourne (1590 and 1591), Stallingborough (1591) and Conisholme, the parish next to Marshchapel, where Mary Frankishe, alias North, a widow, died in 1591; later ones follow at Nettleton in 1610, 1631 and 1647. Hugh Frankishe, a yeoman of Nettleton, bought John Ewerbie's houses and land at Stallingborough for £40 in January 1591; Frankishes appear in the Great Limber registers between 1591 and 1594, and an Isabel Frankish married at Caistor in 1602. No Virgil Frankish appears in the Lincoln probate calendars up to 1659.","src":[["FreeREG parish registers","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"],["Marriage to Mary Pinder, Caistor, 23 Nov 1657 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c52865574f305a9ae93"],["Same marriage in the Rothwell register, 24 Nov 1657 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6842e253865574f30564b1a6"],["Burial of his wife Mary, Caistor, 12 Feb 1668 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b47865574f305a956b9"],["Marriage to Tabitha Patchet, Caistor, 3 May 1668 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c56865574f305a9afba"],["Baptism of son Virgil, Caistor, 22 Jul 1683 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1ef493fda395f40e0b"],["Burial of Virgil Frankish, Caistor, 6 Nov 1699 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b68865574f305a96033"],["Marriage of Isabel Frankish, Caistor, 1602 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c43865574f305a9a962"],["Baptism of Janett Frankish, Swallow, 1561 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5f4e6fdaf493fdc53e085a0e"],["Marriage of Virgill [surname not given] and Ellen Swabye, Marshchapel, 7 Feb 1649/50 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6803782db18f7c79668b4160"],["FreeREG search: Virgill Howson, baptised 24 Jun 1598 and buried 5 Apr 1599, Marshchapel","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["Calendars of Lincoln Wills, vol. I, 1320–1600 (Index Library, 1902), index under Frankish","https://archive.org/details/calendarsoflinco28linc"],["Calendars of Lincoln Wills, vol. II, 1601–1652 (Index Library), index under Frankish","https://archive.org/details/calendarsoflinco4142linc"],["Calendars of Administrations in the Consistory Court of Lincoln, 1540–1659, under Frankishe","https://archive.org/details/calendarsofadmin00churrich"],["Deed: John Ewerbie of Stallingborough to Hugh Frankishe of Nettleton, yeoman, 24 Jan 1591 (Lincolnshire Archives TYR 7/6)","https://discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk/details/r/95128f68-3763-4b8a-9ec9-149d34a246e8"]]},
tabitha:{"n":"Tabitha Patchet","d":"m. 1668 Caistor","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Tabitha Patchet married Virgil Frankish at Caistor on 3 May 1668. He was a widower twice over: his wife Mary had been buried there less than three months before, leaving young children from that marriage in the house.\n\nOver the next fifteen years Tabitha had six children baptised at Caistor: Elizabeth (6 March 1669), Thomas (21 April 1672), Susanna (7 November 1675), Anne (1 September 1678), Tabitha (9 February 1681) and Virgil (22 July 1683). She buried three of them young: Elizabeth in April 1673, aged four, Susanna in July 1677, not yet two, and her namesake Tabitha twelve days after the baptism. Her youngest, Virgil, married at Caistor in 1705 and carried the line on.\n\nHer own baptism has not been found. The surname was rare around Caistor. The registers there and at neighbouring Rothwell show only a William Patchett, who married Isabel Walsh at Rothwell on 23 June 1642, and an Isabel Patchett, buried at Caistor on 19 February 1661. They may be her family, but no record says so.","src":[["Marriage, Caistor, 3 May 1668 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c56865574f305a9afba"],["Baptism of daughter Elizabeth, Caistor, 6 Mar 1669 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52ef2f493fda395f408fa"],["Baptism of daughter Tabitha, Caistor, 9 Feb 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1af493fda395f40d34"],["Burial of daughter Tabitha, Caistor, 21 Feb 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b58865574f305a95bfa"],["Baptism of son Virgil, Caistor, 22 Jul 1683 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1ef493fda395f40e0b"],["Marriage of William Patchett and Isabel Walsh, Rothwell, 1642 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6842e252865574f30564b17d"],["Burial of Isabel Patchett, Caistor, 1661 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b40865574f305a9545c"]]},
lupke:{"n":"Lupke Ides","d":"Nieuw-Scheemda","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Nieuw-Scheemda, in east Groningen. She married twice, and both times a marriage contract was drawn up first with Jan Ides, who shared her second name, standing on her side. Her first husband was Hindrik Jans Slik, whom she married on 26 January 1755; they had two daughters, Anna (1755) and Stijntje (1758), before he died.\n\nOn 29 November 1761 she married Hindrik Harms of Woldendorp. Their son Harm was baptised at Nieuw-Scheemda in December 1762 and a child named Ida, a name that matches Lupke's own second name, Ides, at Midwolda in October 1764, so the family had moved the few kilometres to Midwolda by then. Hindrik Harms was her daughters' stepfather, and when Anna died in 1827 it was he who was written down as her father.","src":[["First marriage, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:126b07d6-a9cd-45b1-e4a5-5336b22c252e"],["Second marriage, 1761","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1e54ee7c-96a7-ef71-4258-16dc9d0ade20"],["Marriage contract, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29518a51-a98a-95fe-da04-72fa121878db"],["Marriage contract, 1761","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5c63da0d-6b85-c5bd-7e92-926a5efc66e4"],["Baptism of Harm, 1762","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7e436d6a-ef68-c68a-fddc-e7c7564e8410"],["Baptism of Ida, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4c000589-d60c-8bce-00ae-6e5f8c03b4d0"]]},

taeke:{"n":"Taeke Hazenberg","sn":"Taeke (Teake) Hazenberg","d":"1910–1987 · Grouw","b":1910,"x":1987,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl","au"],"ch":"hazenberg","t":"Born at Grouw, Friesland, on 5 November 1910, the third of the six children of Willem Hendriks Hazenberg and Yttje van der Meer; Rinske (1908) and Hendrik (1909) were older, and Aaltje (1916), Tjeerd (1919) and Sipkje (1922) were born after the family moved to Warga, where his father was a dairyman and cattle farmer. He carried the name of his mother's father, Taeke Tjeerds van der Meer of Wartena.\n\nHe married Aukje Elzinga at Idaarderadeel on 13 May 1933. His wedding day was a double one: his elder brother Hendrik married Anna Barbara van Schagen there on the same day. Taeke was 22 and Aukje 23. A cattleman with a dairy farm near Leeuwarden, he and Aukje had four children: Willem (1936), Aaltje (1937) and two younger daughters. His mother died in Leeuwarden on 19 October 1945, aged 61.\n\nIn 1950 he brought his family to Australia on the Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, a Dutch liner then carrying migrants. It reached Fremantle on 4 October 1950 and landed its Sydney passengers on 12 October. The family spent a year at Monto, in the Burnett district, and then lived at Fairymead, the sugar plantation north of Bundaberg, before settling just outside the town in Woongarra Shire. In June 1958 a T. Hazenberg advertised a Guernsey heifer close to calving and a cow due the next month, \"both suit milk supplier\". Taeke, Aukje and their daughter Ietje were naturalised together at the Rowers Hall, Bundaberg, on 26 January 1959. On 11 June 1960 their daughter Eva married Colin Herbert Linderberg at the Church of Christ in Barolin Street, Bundaberg.\n\nHe was not the only one of his family to leave for Australia. His youngest sister Sipkje and her husband Gabe Hornstra were living at Drouin in Victoria by December 1950, and when their father died at Dokkum on 7 December 1958, aged 81, the death notice listed both households under \"Australië\". Taeke died in 1987, a resident of Bundaberg, and was buried in the lawn section of the Bundaberg General Cemetery on 17 July 1987. Aukje placed a notice in Frisian in the Leeuwarder Courant of 3 October 1987. It thanked people for their sympathy on the death of her husband, \"ús heit en pake\" (our father and grandfather), and said that so much remembrance from the homeland, \"út it Heitelân\", had done the family a great deal of good. In the notice his name is spelled the Frisian way, Teake.","src":[["9 Leave For Course At Gatton, Bundaberg News-Mail, 4 Apr 1955","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283695423"],["Marriages, Bundaberg News-Mail, 11 Jun 1960","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284153577"],["Woongarra Has Six For Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 24 Jan 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284343415"],["Birth record","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b5a93e4-09ce-11e5-a98f-2333d9fcd84e"],["Marriage record, 1933","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e13678c2-09d0-11e5-b1c8-f7f0cecb7bd8"],["Bundaberg cemetery records","https://cemeterysearch.bundaberg.qld.gov.au/?q=Hazenberg"],["Marriage of brother Hendrik, 13 May 1933","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e136784a-09d0-11e5-b1c7-f7908df37256"],["Death of Yttje van der Meer, 19 Oct 1945","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cac223c-131e-885c-a71e-802552d0f419"],["Death of Willem Hazenberg, 7 Dec 1958","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a54752a-0dae-11e5-beb4-ffa66d0cbc72"],["Thank-you notice from Aukje Hazenberg-Elzinga, Leeuwarder Courant, 3 Oct 1987","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19871003-10027"],["Birth notice Hornstra-Hazenberg, Drouin (Vic.), Leeuwarder Courant, 12 Dec 1950","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19501212-6033"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"],["Population register, Grouw (born 5 Nov 1910)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:17414495-576a-3867-86dc-f831d25e4bff"],["Woongarra Has Six For Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 24 Jan 1959","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article284343415"],["20 Take Oath In Simple Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 27 Jan 1959","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article284339531"],["Advertising (T. Hazenberg, cattle for sale), Bundaberg News-Mail, 10 Jun 1958","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283818361"],["Passenger list, Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, 1950","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=30133074"],["1342 Migrants Arrive, Daily News (Perth), 4 Oct 1950","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article84479917"],["Large Party Of Dutch Migrants, Daily Mirror (Sydney), 12 Oct 1950","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article274630383"],["Breaking-Up Picnic At Fairymead, Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Dec 1953","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283576948"]]},
aukje:{"n":"Aukje Elzinga","d":"1909–1994 · Akkrum","b":1909,"x":1994,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl","au"],"t":"Born at Akkrum, Utingeradeel, on 18 November 1909. She married Taeke Hazenberg in 1933, and in 1950 sailed with him and their four children to Australia. She and her daughter Ietje were naturalised with Taeke at Bundaberg in January 1959. In later life she lived at Fennell Bay, on Lake Macquarie in New South Wales. She died in 1994 and was buried with Taeke at Bundaberg on 26 July 1994.\n\nShe was the second of seven known children. Her elder brother Johannes was born at Gevelsberg in Germany about 1906; after her came Jacob (1912), Theodora (1916), Wiemer (1919), Ybeltje (1922) and Jantje (about 1923). Her father Jan was by turns a labourer at Irnsum, a milk boatman and a cattle farmer at Terband; he died at Assen on 24 August 1952, aged 76. Her mother died in 1967, also after Aukje had left for Australia. After Taeke died she placed a thank-you notice in Frisian in the Leeuwarder Courant of 3 October 1987, signed \"Aukje Hazenberg-Elzinga, Bundaberg, Australië, Queensland\".","src":[["Looking Forward To Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 24 Jan 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284343417"],["Birth record, 1909","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b9af182-09ce-11e5-b86c-ab680b12052a"],["Bundaberg cemetery records","https://cemeterysearch.bundaberg.qld.gov.au/?q=Hazenberg"],["Death of Jan Elzinga, Assen, 24 Aug 1952","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6b9d4ad7-b880-161c-c68b-f9e695fc34a6"],["Parents' marriage, Rauwerderhem, 25 May 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9a9ce2a9-0b80-5c58-fd8b-edf9aa0c710f"],["Marriage of Hendrik Hazenberg, same day","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e136784a-09d0-11e5-b1c7-f7908df37256"],["Thank-you notice from Aukje Hazenberg-Elzinga, Leeuwarder Courant, 3 Oct 1987","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19871003-10027"]]},
willemh:{"n":"Willem Hendriks Hazenberg","d":"1877–1958","b":1877,"x":1958,"s":"h","p":["hendrikw","aaltjei"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 9 March 1877 in Tietjerksteradeel, Friesland, and raised at Bergum. He married Yttje van der Meer at Idaarderadeel on 18 May 1907 and they lived around Grouw. He died at Dokkum on 7 December 1958, aged 81. His grandson Willem, born in 1936, was named after him.\n\nHis marriage and death records give his birthplace as Bergum. In September 1911 he and his father-in-law Taeke van der Meer, both then of Wartena, were buyers at a sale of property at Warga. He was a dairyman (koemelker) at Warga in deeds of 1918 and 1919, when he bought again at a public sale, and a cattle farmer (veehouder) there when his children were born in 1916, 1919 and 1922. He and Yttje had six children: Rinske (1908), Hendrik (1909), Taeke (1910), Aaltje (1916), Tjeerd (1919) and Sipkje (1922). Yttje died in 1945. He was living in Dokkum when he died. The family's notice in the Leeuwarder Courant says he died in the Dutch Reformed rest home at Dokkum, a widower since 19 October 1945, aged almost 82. It lists his children by where they lived: Warga, Rotstergaast, Dokkum and Hengelo, and two households in Australia, \"T. Hazenberg, A. Hazenberg-Elzinga\" and \"S. Hornstra-Hazenberg, G. Hornstra\". It also mentions grandchildren and one great-grandchild. The funeral service was held in the Dutch Reformed church at Irnsum on Wednesday 10 December 1958.\n\nBy September 1943, when his youngest daughter Sipkje married, he was a farm labourer (landarbeider) living at Irnsum, aged 66, and it was at Irnsum that his grandchildren Willem (1936) and Aaltje (1937) were born. His wife Yttje was still living at Irnsum when she died in Leeuwarden in 1945.","src":[["Birth record, 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:396727cf-dd0e-0c16-d591-c93201f4da63"],["Marriage record, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d4cb962d-4281-2265-1a0f-3c78a200cf03"],["Death record, 1958","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a54752a-0dae-11e5-beb4-ffa66d0cbc72"],["Notarial deed, sale at Warga, 2 Sep 1911","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e7276a39-1269-4e4e-b79c-73df38e0e85f"],["Notarial deed, division of an estate, Warga 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fbfe975b-d2f8-4910-a25d-28a5bf0a20ab"],["Notarial deed, sale at Warga, 6 Dec 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0990fdd4-986a-49d1-be0c-fe3417d068e7"],["Birth of Sipkje Hazenberg, Warga 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5e2f98d1-be2b-4aa1-94d4-4baf48d4d217"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"],["Marriage of Sipkje Hazenberg, 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3988c2d6-8565-8264-7452-1d334dd1042b"]]},
yttje:{"n":"Yttje van der Meer","d":"1884–1945 · Warga","b":1884,"s":"h","p":["taeketvdm","rinske"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Warga, Idaarderadeel, on 9 February 1884, the daughter of Taeke Tjeerds van der Meer, a labourer, and Rinske Douwes IJsselstein. Her parents' first child, a boy named Tjeerd, had died at three weeks old in 1880, and a sister, Dirkje, died at Warga in 1893 aged one. Her youngest sister Antje was born in September 1898, and her mother died eight weeks later, when Yttje was 14. Her father never remarried and lived at Wartena until 1939.\n\nOn 18 May 1907, aged 23, she married Willem Hendriks Hazenberg of Bergum at Idaarderadeel. They lived first at Grouw, where Rinske (28 June 1908, named after Yttje's mother), Hendrik (1909) and Taeke (5 November 1910, named after her father) were born, and then at Warga, where Willem was a dairyman and cattle farmer and Aaltje (1916), Tjeerd (1919) and Sipkje (1922) were born. In 1911 her husband and her father bought property at Warga at the same sale.\n\nHer eldest children married in the 1930s, Hendrik and Taeke on the same day in May 1933. By 1943, when Sipkje married, Yttje and Willem were living at Irnsum. She died in Leeuwarden on 19 October 1945, aged 61, still living at Irnsum. Willem died at Dokkum in 1958.","src":[["Birth record, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:73c2a4c2-cd9e-2279-bb29-3ebcfe87d30b"],["Death of Yttje van der Meer, Leeuwarden 1945","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cac223c-131e-885c-a71e-802552d0f419"],["Marriage Hazenberg-van der Meer, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d4cb962d-4281-2265-1a0f-3c78a200cf03"],["Marriage of Sipkje Hazenberg, 1943 (parents at Irnsum)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3988c2d6-8565-8264-7452-1d334dd1042b"],["Death of Rinske IJsselstein, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb34f746-a7dd-bec3-0507-d55a8b53602f"],["Birth of Rinske Hazenberg, 1908","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b5a1bee-09ce-11e5-a87c-131354e96ba8"],["Birth of Taeke Hazenberg, 1910","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b5a93e4-09ce-11e5-a98f-2333d9fcd84e"],["Notarial deed, sale at Warga, 2 Sep 1911","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e7276a39-1269-4e4e-b79c-73df38e0e85f"],["Death of Willem Hendriks Hazenberg, 1958","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a54752a-0dae-11e5-beb4-ffa66d0cbc72"]],"x":1945},
hendrikw:{"n":"Hendrik Willems Hazenberg","d":"1842–1923 · Augustinusga","b":1842,"x":1923,"s":"h","p":["willemw","annagrab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Augustinusga in Achtkarspelen on 2 July 1842, the second son of the merchant Willem Wiebes Hazenberg and his German-born wife Anna Grabhorn. His father died at Bergum in January 1849, when Hendrik was six, and his mother stayed in Bergum for the rest of her life. He had an elder brother Wiebe, a younger sister Aafke, and three older half-siblings named Blok from his mother's first marriage.\n\nOn 20 May 1876, aged 33, he married Aaltje Johannes Iedema of Eestrum, who was 24. They lived at Bergum, where he bought property at a public sale in February 1879 and where all six children were born: Willem (1877), Sipkje (1879), Johannes (1882), Jan Lodewijk (1884), Wiebe (1887) and Andreas (1891). Notaries wrote his name as Hein. In 1894 he was named, as Hein Hazenberg of Bergum, in the division of an estate among his wife's Fransbergen and Iedema relatives; her unmarried uncle Sjuk Fransbergen had died that January.\n\nSipkje married in January 1903 at Baarderadeel, the municipality that includes Mantgum, south-west of Leeuwarden, and Hendrik spent his last years at Mantgum. In 1920, as Hein Hazenberg of Mantgum, he was a seller at two auctions held by the notary at Jorwerd, the second together with Sipke Iedema of Leeuwarden and Berend Iedema of Drachten, the names of his wife's brothers. A will made in April 1921 calls him a market gardener (tuinier) at Mantgum. All six children married, between 1903 and 1918. He died at Mantgum on 25 August 1923, aged 81. Aaltje died at Bolsward on 7 November 1932.","src":[["Birth record, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:066ef9cb-139f-6f66-fded-c9c43cbb1ee3"],["Marriage record, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c165f81d-a996-deb0-3254-e72c3e993d5a"],["Death record, 1923","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:847ef19d-6bd9-bab6-87bb-4d58f85ed7a1"],["Population register, born 2 July 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2219f379-9005-84f0-8615-457b08b36fb4"],["Notarial deed, purchase at auction, 1 Feb 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1da9284d-6d17-4027-83bb-c3c702ac2dac"],["Birth of Sipkje, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7f616f1b-031a-2c57-2b44-112c0e589fe3"],["Birth of Johannes, 1882","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0977a5ea-19e6-8ca3-ed28-4bd55b39f886"],["Birth of Wiebe, 1887","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:953ad128-09f2-7a91-6b68-a12bd2b66add"],["Birth of Andreas, 1891","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6ecf76d2-9d9d-f124-861b-7f0168a6a684"],["Notarial deed, estate division, 19 Nov 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:87ec742b-e9ff-42d0-80ed-8271db137b99"],["Marriage of Sipkje, Baarderadeel 1903","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51f8612f-59b4-e3a2-b1de-529048546586"],["Marriage of Johannes, 1910","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7b53aba6-781f-9219-f522-e798eb4bf526"],["Marriage of Jan Lodewijk, 1912","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b4573d1d-2af9-230b-d7ea-ea214b77c6f9"],["Marriage of Wiebe, 1913","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b1db12e3-13c5-06f7-4981-a0bfc11441e5"],["Marriage of Andreas, 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:657dddbc-972d-5c80-e083-7edc7be4acb9"],["Notarial deed, auction at Mantgum, 10 Jun 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a2137f19-2ec8-416b-83df-9dc1b1c62bcb"],["Notarial deed, auction at Mantgum, 22 Jul 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:be52413f-1e50-4104-b13e-8623d830f6ef"],["Will, 22 Apr 1921","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1c6d8acc-5b04-4faa-a229-32a206fbec50"],["Death of Aaltje Idema, 1932","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b7de6d41-759f-bfbc-1524-8bd06dd20e1b"]]},
aaltjei:{"n":"Aaltje Johannes Iedema","d":"1851–1932 · Eestrum","b":1851,"s":"h","p":["johannesidema","sipkjen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eestrum, Tietjerksteradeel, on 23 November 1851, the second child of Johannes Wybes Iedema and Sipkjen Sipkes Fransbergen. Her father ended his working life as an errand-runner; her mother's father kept the inn at Eestrum. Her mother died in October 1868, when Aaltje was 16, and her father married again in 1871.\n\nOn 20 May 1876, aged 24, she married Hendrik Willems Hazenberg, who was 33 and came from a Bergum merchant's family. They lived at Bergum, where she had six children in fourteen years: Willem (1877), Sipkje (1879), Johannes (1882), Jan Lodewijk (1884), Wiebe (1887) and Andreas (1891). All six grew up and married. Her father died at Eestrum in January 1880.\n\nThe family later moved to Mantgum in Baarderadeel, a village where her uncle Liebbe Fransbergen had been ferry skipper and where her brother Sipke was living by 1894. Hendrik died there in 1923. Aaltje outlived him by nine years and died at Bolsward on 7 November 1932, aged 80; the death was also entered in the Baarderadeel register.","x":1932,"src":[["Birth record, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36509de1-ff88-8d94-d57a-76b9fe9df867"],["Marriage record, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c165f81d-a996-deb0-3254-e72c3e993d5a"],["Death record, Bolsward 1932","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b7de6d41-759f-bfbc-1524-8bd06dd20e1b"],["Notarial deed, estate inventory, 12 Mar 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:81431529-7d38-4f84-84a6-781f7f50bebb"]]},
janelz:{"n":"Jan Elzinga","d":"1876–1952 · Grouw","b":1876,"x":1952,"s":"h","p":["johannesje","theodora"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Grouw on 27 July 1876, the second child and eldest son of Johannes Jans Elzinga, a labourer, and Theodora Joukes Stok. His parents moved several times while he was small: sisters were born at Sneek in 1878 and in Utingeradeel in 1880, and from 1882 the family was at Irnsum.\n\nOn 25 May 1905, aged 28, he married Aaltje Postma, 22, from Hemrik, at Rauwerderhem. Their first child, Johannes, was born about 1906 at Gevelsberg, a town in the Ruhr area of Westphalia in Germany. By November 1909 they were back in Friesland, at Akkrum, where Aukje was born. Jacob (1912) and Theodora (1916) were born at Irnsum, where Jan was a labourer (arbeider).\n\nBetween 1916 and 1919 the family moved to Terband, just north of Heerenveen. In July 1918 a Jan Johannes Elzinga of Terband, almost certainly he, took out a mortgage before a Heerenveen notary. At Wiemer's birth in 1919 Jan was a cattle farmer (veehouder); at Ybeltje's in 1922 he was a milk boatman (melkvaarder), a man who collected the churns from the farms by boat for the dairy. Jantje, the youngest, followed about 1923.\n\nIn 1943 he and Aaltje were living at Haskerdijken, and by 1946 they were back at Terband. He died at Assen on 24 August 1952, aged 76, still described as a cattle farmer of Terband.","src":[["Marriage record, 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9a9ce2a9-0b80-5c58-fd8b-edf9aa0c710f"],["Death record, 1952","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6b9d4ad7-b880-161c-c68b-f9e695fc34a6"],["Birth of Jan Elzinga, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:448f8e35-f466-ebf9-cb65-259a5371707a"],["Birth of sister Jantje, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b0291fe2-9459-48fa-ee67-0a35b2fed118"],["Birth of daughter Theodora, Irnsum, 1916","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:32c495fd-8407-4415-917b-f77cc06fcf5a"],["Birth of son Wiemer, Terband, 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25a143e3-ec50-996a-b834-bf62ca0c6e90"],["Birth of daughter Ybeltje, Terband, 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ebc5fc9-98ea-b660-8a1c-30d78c525d56"],["Mortgage, Heerenveen notary, 11 July 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b15461b-95ad-4877-8df1-71b8ef0c5e9f"],["Marriage of daughter Ybeltje, Haskerland, 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2e9f115e-dab5-2ef0-b3dc-b0ba9dccdf40"],["Marriage of daughter Jantje, Heerenveen, 1946","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9db3270f-5ffd-9c24-acfd-00daca162d5f"]]},
aaltjep:{"n":"Aaltje Postma","d":"1882–1967 · Hemrik","b":1882,"x":1967,"s":"h","p":["jacobp","aukjenw"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Hemrik in Opsterland on 8 July 1882, the third of the nine children of Jacob Postma, a labourer, and Aukjen Jilts Wijnstra. She had two elder brothers, Jan and Jilt, and younger sisters Aukje, Ybeltje and Neeltje and brothers Feitze and Roel.\n\nOn 25 May 1905, aged 22, she married Jan Elzinga at Rauwerderhem, the district of Irnsum. Their eldest child, Johannes, was born in Germany about 1906; six more followed in Friesland: Aukje (1909, at Akkrum), Jacob (1912) and Theodora (1916) at Irnsum, and Wiemer (1919), Ybeltje (1922) and Jantje (about 1923) at Terband near Heerenveen, where the family kept cattle. Two of the girls carried the names of her mother and of her sister Ybeltje.\n\nHer father died in 1917 and her mother in 1921. In 1943 she and Jan were living at Haskerdijken, and by 1946 at Terband again. Jan died in 1952. Aaltje died at Heerenveen on 16 November 1967, aged 85.","src":[["Death record, 1967","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1964461-d763-7faa-53b2-bd77c6a7de69"],["Marriage Elzinga-Postma, 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9a9ce2a9-0b80-5c58-fd8b-edf9aa0c710f"],["Birth record, 1882","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:dcca0cb5-7554-1bbd-e854-787c82e28471"],["Marriage of daughter Ybeltje, Haskerland, 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2e9f115e-dab5-2ef0-b3dc-b0ba9dccdf40"]]},
aaltjeh:{"n":"Aaltje Hazenberg","d":"1937–2014 · Irnsum","b":1937,"s":"h","p":["taeke","aukje"],"r":["nl","au"],"t":"Born at Irnsum in Friesland on 27 May 1937, the second child and eldest daughter of Taeke Hazenberg and Aukje Elzinga, sixteen months after her brother Willem. She carried the name of her mother's mother, Aaltje Postma.\n\nShe was 13 when the family sailed for Australia on the Johan van Oldenbarnevelt, a Dutch liner chartered to carry migrants: 1,342 of them on that voyage, 520 of them children under twelve. The ship reached Fremantle on 4 October 1950 and Melbourne on 10 October, and on 12 October landed 833 people at Sydney, the largest party of Dutch migrants yet to arrive there. A National Archives file in her name gives her own arrival at Sydney as 13 October 1950. The family spent a year at Monto and then moved to the Bundaberg district.\n\nIn Australia she was Alice. On 29 December 1956, aged 19, she married the youngest son of Mr and Mrs Brouwer, of Holland, at the Church of Christ in Barolin Street, Bundaberg, where her sister Eva was married in 1960. In later life she lived at Marmong Point on Lake Macquarie in New South Wales, close to Fennell Bay, where her mother spent her last years. She died on 16 March 2014, aged 76.","x":2014,"src":[["Population register, Irnsum (born 27 May 1937)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5eab20cf-2e13-d9a2-c938-c6236839449f"],["Marriage notice Brouwer–Hazenberg, Bundaberg News-Mail, 29 Dec 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283722537"],["National Archives file, Aaltje Brouwer also known as Hazenberg (NAA SP908/1)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7227348"],["Ryerson Index: funeral notice, Newcastle Herald, 19 Mar 2014 (died 16 Mar 2014, aged 76)","https://ryersonindex.org/search.php"],["1342 Migrants Arrive, Daily News (Perth), 4 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/84479917"],["Dutch migrants reach Fremantle, West Australian, 5 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47890457"],["Large Party Of Dutch Migrants, Daily Mirror (Sydney), 12 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/274630383"]]},
tetje:{"n":"Ietje (Eva) Hazenberg","d":"b. c. 1942","s":"h","p":["taeke","aukje"],"r":["nl","au"],"t":"The second daughter of Taeke Hazenberg and Aukje Elzinga, born in Friesland about 1942. The Bundaberg papers of 1959 print her name as Ietje, another spelling of the name of her father's mother, Yttje van der Meer; in Australia she was Eva. She was about eight when the family arrived on the Johan van Oldenbarnevelt in October 1950.\n\nBy 1953 the family was at Fairymead, the sugar plantation outside Bundaberg, and at the Fairymead State School breaking-up picnic that December two Hazenberg girls were among the place-getters: a J. Hazenberg, third in the girls' 11–12 years race and in the senior skipping race, and a Y. Hazenberg, second in the girls' 9–10 years race. The paper gives only initials; by their ages the elder was Ietje and the younger Janna.\n\nHer engagement to Colin, eldest son of Mr and Mrs H. Linderberg of North Bundaberg, was announced on 27 December 1958. A month later, on 26 January 1959, as Miss Ietje Hazenberg, “single, domestic duties”, she was naturalised at Bundaberg with her parents. She married Colin Herbert Linderberg at the Church of Christ in Barolin Street, Bundaberg, on 11 June 1960.","src":[["Breaking-Up Picnic At Fairymead, Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Dec 1953","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283576948"],["Engagement Linderberg–Hazenberg, Bundaberg News-Mail, 27 Dec 1958","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284345821"],["Holland, Italy Will Supply New Citizens, Bundaberg News-Mail, 13 Jan 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284342378"],["Looking Forward To Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 24 Jan 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284343417"],["20 Take Oath In Simple Ceremony, Bundaberg News-Mail, 27 Jan 1959","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284339531"],["Marriage notice, Bundaberg News-Mail, 11 Jun 1960","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284153577"]]},
janna:{"n":"Janna Hazenberg","d":"b. c. 1944","s":"h","p":["taeke","aukje"],"r":["nl","au"],"t":"The youngest of Taeke Hazenberg and Aukje Elzinga's four children, born in Friesland about 1944 and about six when the family reached Australia on the Johan van Oldenbarnevelt in October 1950. After a year at Monto the family moved to the Bundaberg district. At the Fairymead State School breaking-up picnic in December 1953 a Y. Hazenberg was second in the girls' 9–10 years race; the paper gives only the initial, which fits her age and the Dutch sound of Janna, where the J is said as a Y. In October 1966 the Ayr Advocate, in North Queensland, reported that Janna Hazenberg had just taken a permanent place as the No. 4 player in the Peanuts team in the town's ladies' squash fixtures, and had won her match three games to two. The report says nothing of her family, but the name is a rare one in Queensland.","src":[["Breaking-Up Picnic At Fairymead, Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Dec 1953","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283576948"],["Ladies Squash Fixtures, The Advocate (Ayr), 12 Oct 1966","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/291158538"]]},
joanne:{"n":"Joanne Powell","d":"b. 1960","s":"h","p":["bill","meg"],"r":["au"],"t":"Joanne Nancy, born at the Lady Chelmsford Hospital, Bundaberg, in January 1960, the eldest of Bill and Meg's children; her second name is her mother's first. Her parents had married at the Barolin Street Methodist Church, Bundaberg, eleven months earlier. Her father, who had come from Friesland as a boy in 1950, was a driver on Fairymead Plantation and became an Australian citizen in June 1961, when she was a year old. Her sister Gail was born in August 1961 and her brother Trevor in May 1964. Her married name is Powell.","src":[["Births, Bundaberg News-Mail, 11 Jan 1960","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284482254"]]},
gail:{"n":"Gail Stallan","d":"b. 1961","s":"h","p":["bill","meg"],"r":["au"],"t":"Gail Amy, born at the Lady Chelmsford Hospital, Bundaberg, in August 1961, the second child of Bill Hazenberg and Meg Hodgetts and a sister for Joanne. Her second name is that of her mother's mother, Amy Louisa Haken. She was born two months after her father, a driver on Fairymead Plantation, was naturalised at the Gooburrum Shire Council chambers. Her brother Trevor was born in May 1964. Both sets of grandparents lived in Bundaberg: the Hazenbergs, who had come from Friesland in 1950, and the Hodgetts family. Her married name is Stallan.","src":[["Births, Bundaberg News-Mail, 7 Aug 1961","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/282963793"]]},

peteragar:{"n":"Peter Agar","d":"","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Peter Agar was the father of Ann Agar, who was born in about 1815 in the parish of St Martin in the Fields, London, and married the surgeon Charles Houlton Webb at St George's, Hanover Square, on 16 May 1839. His wife was Ann Douglass. The family's name was carried on by Ann's son Elliott Agar Webb, born in 1849 at Vestry House in St Martin in the Fields, who emigrated to South Australia and died in Melbourne in 1928.\n\nNo Peter Agar, and no marriage of an Agar to a Douglass, appears among the London and Middlesex parish entries in the free transcripts searched, so his birth, trade and death are not yet known.","src":[["Death index of his grandson Elliot Agar Webb, 1928 (reg. 3533/1928)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
anndouglass:{"n":"Ann Douglass","d":"","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Ann Douglass was the wife of Peter Agar and the mother of Ann Agar, who was born in about 1815 in the parish of St Martin in the Fields, London. Her daughter married Charles Houlton Webb, a surgeon, at St George's, Hanover Square, in 1839, and stayed in London when he left for South Australia in 1848. Her grandson Elliott Agar Webb was born in St Martin in the Fields in 1849 and later emigrated, dying in Melbourne in 1928.\n\nHer own baptism, marriage and death have not been found; the free London parish transcripts searched contain no marriage of an Agar to a Douglass.","src":[["Death index of her grandson Elliot Agar Webb, 1928 (reg. 3533/1928)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
williamhicks:{"n":"William Hicks","d":"b. c. 1806","s":"p","p":["sncharleshicks","snmarywright"],"r":["uk"],"t":"William Hicks was born in about 1806, outside Huntingdonshire according to the 1841 census. On 12 October 1828, as William Hicks of Yaxley, he married Elizabeth Helmes of Water Newton in the church of St Remigius at Water Newton, Huntingdonshire. Neither of them could write, and both signed with a mark, as did the witnesses Charles and Lucy Hicks.\n\nThe couple settled at Alwalton, a village on the River Nene west of Peterborough, where William worked as a farm labourer. Their children were baptised there: Charles (20 September 1829), George (15 September 1833), Mary (20 September 1835), Hannah (23 June 1839), Emma (8 December 1840) and William (19 December 1846). In 1841 the family lived in the High Street at Alwalton.\n\nHe was still a labourer when his son George married at Alwalton in June 1854. His eldest son, Charles, emigrated to South Australia with his family in 1855. William's own baptism and death have not been found; Yaxley baptisms before 1813 are not in the free transcripts.\n\nHis likely parents are Charles Hicks and Mary Wright of Warmington, Northamptonshire, a village beside the River Nene a few miles upstream from Alwalton. A William, son of Charles and Mary Hicks, was baptised at Warmington on 14 October 1804, which fits his age and the 1841 census note that he was born outside Huntingdonshire. A Charles Hicks witnessed William's wedding in 1828, and William named his eldest son Charles. This link rests on these matches and is not proven by a record naming William's parents.","b":1806,"src":[["Marriage, Water Newton, 1828","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68384a2b865574838ea08125"],["Census 1841, Alwalton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64f0b932f493fd6cd8db826e"],["Baptism of Charles, 1829","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825dc865574838e90fd66"],["Baptism of George, 1833","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825de865574838e90feb7"],["Baptism of Mary, 1835","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825df865574838e90ff88"],["Baptism of Hannah, 1839","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825e2865574838e9100db"],["Baptism of Emma, 1840","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825e2865574838e910148"],["Baptism of William, 1846","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825e5865574838e9102ba"],["Marriage of son George, 1854","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68382665865574838e9133fc"],["Baptism, Warmington, 14 Oct 1804","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec41865574b95e4bc664"]],"u":true},
georgespencer:{"n":"George Spencer","d":"","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"George Spencer was the father of Ann Spencer, who was born in about 1827 and married Charles Hicks, a farm labourer, at Chesterton, Huntingdonshire, on 12 November 1850. Ann and Charles lived at Alwalton, the next parish, where their son William Spencer Hicks was baptised in 1854, and in 1855 they emigrated to South Australia. Ann died at Clarendon, South Australia, in 1890.\n\nGeorge's own home and work are not yet known. No baptism of an Ann Spencer of the right years has been found in the parishes around Chesterton and Peterborough in the free transcripts searched.\n\nOne possible family has been found. George Spencer, a carpenter of Bythorn in western Huntingdonshire, and his wife Rhoda (written \"Rhode\") had children baptised at Bythorn: Diana Maria (1823), George (1825), Ann (17 June 1827) and John (1830). That Ann is the only daughter of a George Spencer found baptised in the Huntingdonshire transcripts in the years 1824 to 1830, and her baptism fits Ann's age of 63 when she died in 1890. Bythorn lies in the west of the county, well away from Chesterton, however, and no record yet ties her to Charles Hicks's wife, so this remains a lead only.","src":[["Marriage index of his daughter Ann, Dec quarter 1850, Peterborough 15/693","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Baptism of Ann Spencer, Bythorn, 17 Jun 1827","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6708ff09b18f7c7954fe262e"],["Baptism of Diana Maria Spencer, Bythorn, 1823","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6708ff06b18f7c7954fe2595"],["Baptism of George Spencer, Bythorn, 1825","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6708ff08b18f7c7954fe25e8"],["Baptism of John Spencer, Bythorn, 1830","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6708ff0ab18f7c7954fe2670"]]},
williamwarren:{"n":"William Warren","d":"Devonport","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"William Warren was the father of Joseph Warren, a mason and later a butcher of Devonport, who was born there in about 1810 and emigrated to Victoria in 1854. Joseph's death registration in Melbourne in 1892 names his parents as William Warren and Martha Wilson. That entry is the only record of William found so far.\n\nNo household of a William and Martha Warren appears in the 1841 or 1851 census transcripts for Devon. A William Warren, aged 50, a navy pensioner, was living in John Street, Stoke Damerel, in 1841 with a wife named Sarah; he has not been shown to be related. Many men in Devonport at this time worked in the naval dockyard or served in the navy.","src":[["Death index of his son Joseph Warren, 1892 (reg. 10577/1892)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
peterfox:{"n":"Peter Fox","d":"c. 1775–1850 · Devon","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Peter Fox was a pork butcher in Devonport, Devon. He was born in Devon in about 1775, and he and his wife Jane Bone had at least three children: Mary, born at Pennycross in about 1809, Amelia, born there in about 1818, and Peter Joseph, born at Devonport in about 1827.\n\nAt the 1841 census Peter, aged 65, was living in Barrack Street, Devonport, with Jane, Amelia and young Peter, and in the same house were his daughter Mary, her husband Joseph Warren, a mason, and their daughter Amelia. His son Peter Joseph followed him into the trade and was a pork butcher in Stoke Damerel in 1851.\n\nPeter died early in 1850; his death was registered in the Stoke Damerel district in the first quarter of that year. By 1851 Jane was a widow living on the income of houses she owned. His marriage and his own parents have not yet been found.\n\nRobson's directory of Devonport for 1839 lists him twice: as Peter Fox, shopkeeper, 31 Barrack Street, and as P. Fox, pork butcher, of Barrack Street and in the Market. No baptism or marriage for Peter Fox or Jane Bone has been found; the free transcripts do not yet cover the Stoke Damerel, Plymouth or Pennycross registers.","b":1775,"x":1850,"src":[["Census 1841, Barrack Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a1464cdf4040b9d6e5aebb1"],["Death index, March quarter 1850, Stoke Damerel 9/347","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Census 1851, Peter Joseph Fox, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64de7fa9f493fd3e50c8ad90"],["Death index of his daughter Mary Warren, Victoria, 1862 (reg. 7370/1862)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Robson's Directory, Devonport, 1839 (transcription)","https://www.devonportonline.co.uk/historic_devonport/transcriptions/1839-robsons-directory.aspx"]]},
janebone:{"n":"Jane Bone","d":"b. c. 1788 · Pennycross, Devon","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Jane Bone was born at Pennycross, just north of Plymouth, in about 1788. Pennycross was the old name for the parish of Weston Peverell, then a rural parish just north of Plymouth and Devonport. She married Peter Fox, a pork butcher, and their children included Mary (about 1809), Amelia (about 1818) and Peter Joseph (about 1827).\n\nIn 1841 the family lived in Barrack Street, Devonport, together with Mary, her husband Joseph Warren and their baby. Peter died early in 1850. At the 1851 census Jane, a widow of 63, was living in Tavistock Street, Stoke Damerel, with her unmarried daughter Amelia, and was described as a \"proprietor of houses\", living on rents.\n\nHer daughter Mary emigrated to Victoria with her family in 1854 and died there in 1862; Mary's death registration names her mother as Jane Bone. Jane's own death has not yet been found.","b":1788,"src":[["Census 1841, Barrack Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a1464cdf4040b9d6e5aebb1"],["Census 1851, Tavistock Street, Stoke Damerel","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6328d407f493fd24d321f538"],["Death index of her daughter Mary Warren, Victoria, 1862 (reg. 7370/1862)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
johnjones:{"n":"John Jones","d":"m. 1812 · Arrington, Cambridgeshire","s":"p","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"John Jones was the father of Isabella Jones, who married Joseph Smith in 1845 and died in Melbourne in 1896; her death registration names her parents as John Jones and Sarah Wragg.\n\nHe is most probably the John Jones of Arrington, Cambridgeshire, who married Sarah Wragg of Arrington in the parish church of St Nicholas there on 28 July 1812, by licence. The witnesses were James Wragg, Sarah's brother, and Richard Moul. This identification rests on the names: it is the only Jones and Wragg marriage found in the English parish transcripts of these years, and Isabella married in the Cambridge registration district, but her own baptism has not been found, so it is not proven.\n\nArrington is a small village on Ermine Street, the old Roman road north from London, beside the Wimpole estate, about ten miles south-west of Cambridge. Nothing more has been found about John's birth, work or death.","src":[["Marriage of John Jones and Sarah Wragg, Arrington, 1812","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68298aaa86557451b4feb666"],["Death index of his daughter Isabel Smith, 1896 (reg. 11092/1896)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
sarahwragg:{"n":"Sarah Wragg","d":"b. 1789 · Arrington, Cambridgeshire","s":"p","p":["smjohnwragg","smmaryjanson"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Sarah Wragg was the mother of Isabella Jones, who married Joseph Smith in 1845 and died in Melbourne in 1896; Isabella's death registration names her mother as Sarah Wragg.\n\nShe is most probably the Sarah Wragg of Arrington, Cambridgeshire, who married John Jones of Arrington there on 28 July 1812, by licence. That Sarah was born at Arrington on 31 July 1789 and baptised on 1 October 1789, the daughter of John Wragg and Mary Janson. Her brother James, born in 1787, witnessed her wedding and was farming at Arrington by 1817, and her sister Elizabeth married William Fordham of Great Eversden at Arrington in March 1812, with Sarah as a witness. Sarah's mother died in 1801 and her father married again the next year.\n\nThe link to Isabella rests on the names and on Isabella's marriage in the Cambridge registration district; Isabella's baptism has not been found, so the Arrington family is the likeliest match rather than a proven one.","b":1789,"u":true,"src":[["Baptism, Arrington, 1789","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd0865574c41a1bd49b"],["Marriage of John Jones and Sarah Wragg, Arrington, 1812","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68298aaa86557451b4feb666"],["Marriage of William Fordham and Elizabeth Wragg, Arrington, 1812","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68298aaa86557451b4feb658"],["Death index of her daughter Isabel Smith, 1896 (reg. 11092/1896)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
don:{"n":"Donald Frederick George Parkinson","sn":"Don Parkinson","d":"1910–1979 · Thornton","b":1910,"x":1979,"s":"p","p":["wgp","martha"],"r":["au"],"t":"Ben's younger brother, the fifth and last child of William George Parkinson and Martha Wilson, born at Thornton on 2 May 1910 and registered at Alexandra. He was five when his father enlisted and seven when his mother took the children to Hoddles Creek in May 1917.\n\nHe married Elsie Alice Smith in 1934; her sister Alma married Ben the following year, and the two families lived side by side at Gladysdale. The brothers worked together in the timber mills of the Upper Yarra all their working lives. Mountain ash from the forests behind Powelltown, Gladysdale and Yarra Junction was sawn at bush mills and sent out by tramway and rail, and milling was the main work of the district.\n\nHe was named with his sisters and brother in his mother's death notice in November 1949. He died at Yarra Junction on 8 August 1979, aged 69.","src":[["Victorian BDM index: birth 7765/1910 (Alexandra); marriage 949/1934 (Elsie Alice Smith); death 18037/1979 (Yarra Junction, aged 69, born Thornton)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},

willemw:{"n":"Willem Wiebes Hazenberg","d":"1799–1849 · Leek","b":1799,"x":1849,"s":"h","p":["wiebe","aafke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Midwolde on 3 February 1799 and born at Leek, in the Westerkwartier, the western corner of Groningen that borders Friesland. He was the eldest child of Wiebe Willems and Aafke Gales. His father was buried at Grootegast in March 1809, when Willem was ten, and his mother married a shoemaker the next year and died before 1816.\n\nBy January 1834 he was living at Drachten in Friesland, where his sister Lutske had married in 1829, and was lending money on bond. He bought property at Drachten in 1837 and 1838. On 25 May 1839, at Smallingerland, he married Anna Grabhorn, a German-born widow from Leeuwarden with three children. The couple moved at once to Augustinusga, where Wiebe (October 1839), Hendrik (1842) and Aafke (about 1845) were born. Deeds of 1839 to 1841 show him selling property and borrowing, and in January 1843 he was a defendant before the district court.\n\nThe family then moved to Bergum, where he died on 6 January 1849. The record calls him a merchant (koopman) aged 51; by his baptism he was 49. His baptism and marriage records name his mother as Aafke Arends, his death record as Aafke Gales, which is the name in all her other records. Another Willem Wiebes Hazenberg, born at Grootegast in 1798 and married at Leek in 1823, was a different man.","src":[["Marriage record, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:899fdae3-ed0b-cfd5-ce74-8f0724fe948c"],["Death record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ecba6ec4-62c5-b3eb-e59e-0cd932211e19"],["Baptism, Midwolde, 3 Feb 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fec59394-1853-ec7f-709a-24077f7d2d6c"],["Notarial deed, bond, 16 Jan 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e43de05-fd0f-42e0-bb83-21bc4f7a7a8e"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Drachten, 25 Sep 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2b77a0dc-f76b-41fc-b253-5d61c16b309b"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Drachten, 5 Nov 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2df2ddd8-d4b4-43f2-8534-029fd0392b72"],["Notarial deed, sale, 28 Dec 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2b881527-f05a-4427-a2ca-df47ed851660"],["Notarial deed, bond, 28 Dec 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:23d371f1-abf3-4ebe-91ff-817c921eebb7"],["Notarial deed, sale, 29 Dec 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:013fd1ec-94f9-48f1-aeef-ecfbb02e5c46"],["Notarial deed, sale, 27 Apr 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:09681bf6-a7a7-4017-9927-25fcc3e4cd64"],["District court roll, 14 Jan 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5b6c0cca-3129-4070-98b6-9c409f29d847"],["Birth of Hendrik, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:066ef9cb-139f-6f66-fded-c9c43cbb1ee3"],["Marriage of son Wiebe, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:65249a27-b3bc-96bc-247b-e7a1955f9f8e"],["Marriage of daughter Aafke, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:648723f1-6cbc-2173-3fa0-fd4dcc43398d"],["Marriage of the other Willem Wiebes Hazenberg, Leek 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:71634321-5277-2164-4f1a-95044d247540"]]},
annagrab:{"n":"Anna Catharina Dorothea Christiana Grabhorn","sn":"Anna Grabhorn","d":"1805–1887 · Oldenburg","b":1805,"x":1887,"s":"h","p":["johanngrab","eleonora"],"r":["de","nl"],"t":"Born on 15 November 1805 in Oldenburg, then a duchy in north-west Germany, the daughter of Johann Diedrich Grabhorn and Eleonora Sophia Schröder. She was a Lutheran and came to Friesland as a young woman. On 10 October 1828, aged 22, she married at Leeuwarden the merchant Jacob Blok, who was 46. They had Cornelis (1830) and Johan Diederich (1831). Jacob died on 1 July 1836, and five days later Anna gave birth to their daughter Jacoba Cornelia.\n\nIn November 1837 the widow moved to Drachten. There, on 25 May 1839, aged 33, she married the trader Willem Wiebes Hazenberg, and the next day she was entered in the church register at Augustinusga as a member of the Evangelical Lutheran congregation of Leeuwarden. Her Hazenberg children were Wiebe (born 25 October 1839), Hendrik (1842) and Aafke (about 1845). The family moved to Bergum, where Willem died in January 1849.\n\nWidowed a second time at 43, she stayed at Bergum. On 18 October 1850 she had a daughter, Leonora Sophia Hermandina Grabhorn, registered under her own surname and named after her mother. Wiebe, Hendrik, Aafke, Jacoba Cornelia and Leonora are all known to have married; Wiebe died at Buitenpost in 1920 and Aafke at Leeuwarden in 1918. Anna died at Bergum on 8 August 1887, aged 81. Dutch clerks spelled her name Grabhorn and Graphorn.","src":[["Death record, 1887","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:294c5fae-a2d7-5bf6-9a9b-2813b5811988"],["Population register, born 15 Nov 1805 at Oldenburg","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3faa8de-c11c-924b-eb4e-ffa5de0394e4"],["First marriage, Leeuwarden, 10 Oct 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c68edecf-4da1-6895-963e-190a6aa157f0"],["Birth of Cornelis Blok, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f54be5e-8b81-d60f-8500-ea6c923a35d5"],["Birth of Johan Diederich Blok, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5080f935-fe6c-7a7a-6bbf-99e859bd722a"],["Death of Jacob Blok, 1 Jul 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69b80536-d76c-4023-b128-5d404fbc63e2"],["Birth of Jacoba Cornelia Blok, 6 Jul 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:37c7e277-5898-b4af-11e0-3fdf7c70abd8"],["Church members, Smallingerland, arrived 19 Nov 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:435f951a-9f41-4cbe-9aeb-647bc2be6eca"],["Second marriage, Smallingerland, 25 May 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:899fdae3-ed0b-cfd5-ce74-8f0724fe948c"],["Church members, Achtkarspelen, 26 May 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:34f8efe3-7612-4cf2-9e01-aaf87d090374"],["Population register, son Wiebe born 25 Oct 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:23e50a80-7a2f-3892-71a0-a09cb7d8806c"],["Birth of Leonora Sophia Hermandina Grabhorn, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:080dcf68-b358-e7d7-7462-83ce413e12cb"],["Marriage of Leonora Grabhorn, 1881","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4d979276-a7d4-db5d-c70c-0d3f6f0a93c8"],["Marriage of Jacoba Cornelia Blok, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4d7907ee-d192-6f25-82b1-105f54db7244"],["Death of son Wiebe Hazenberg, Buitenpost 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:352966a1-2ca7-810e-5a0c-ef8078bdd011"],["Death of daughter Aafke Hazenberg, Leeuwarden 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ff4d9fc9-dab4-24fd-02ca-d78dede31cba"],["Power of attorney, Bergum, 27 Jun 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e81da3d8-1cc4-4ac0-bea0-543576d260e8"]]},
wiebe:{"n":"Wiebe Willems Hazenberg","d":"c. 1776–1809","b":1776,"x":1809,"s":"h","p":["willemharms","lootske"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Grootegast in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. He was the Wibe baptised at Grootegast on 10 March 1776, son of Willem Harms and Lootske Wibes. No record of his adult life names his parents, but the fit is close: he was \"of Grootegast\" when he married, he was \"about 33\" when he was buried in March 1809, and he named a daughter Lutske. A search of all indexed baptisms in the province of Groningen from 1773 to 1779 found no other boy called Wibe, Wijbe or Wiebe whose father was named Willem.\n\nHis banns with Aafke Gales were read at Grootegast on 1 April 1798 and they married at Midwolde on 22 April. They lived first at Leek, where Willem (baptised 3 February 1799), IJnje (1801) and Gale (1803) were born, then back at Grootegast, where Lutske was born on 22 April 1806.\n\nHe was buried at Grootegast on 3 March 1809, \"married, leaving four children\". A fifth, Wiebe Postema, was born on 10 May and baptised on 21 May with the note that the father had died. The children all used the surname Hazenberg, which Lootske's father Wijbe Wiggels already carried. IJnje died at Grootegast in 1874, Lutske married three times at Drachten, and the youngest, Wiebe, became a cooper (kuiper) and died at Nuis in 1855. A second Wiebe Willems Hazenberg, a farmer at Grootegast married to Doetje Jetses, was a different man, baptised in 1768, the son of Willem Wijbes. The shared names suggest that the two were related, but the link has not been traced.","src":[["Marriage, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:760fb3a7-2562-90d8-8937-d9a6139d9fc4"],["Baptism of Willem, 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fec59394-1853-ec7f-709a-24077f7d2d6c"],["Burial, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2f59e3ae-f8da-4ba3-3e77-d737658985e6"],["Baptism of Wiebe, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0c592e93-17f4-9be6-f36e-553a9e0d8180"],["Baptism of Wibe, Grootegast, 10 Mar 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a4769de2-798f-7765-7b3e-ca96075a306f"],["Banns, Grootegast, 1 Apr 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6667b096-6a58-55ca-cde5-754da351d0e6"],["Baptism of IJnje, 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:548d8da5-51d1-828d-711d-02591d9a98df"],["Baptism of Gale, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6bef17d0-21af-7876-cef4-e2d3f63509ea"],["Baptism of Lutske, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:be6cbdb3-8557-0438-778e-6dac63255d93"],["Death of daughter IJnje, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8aa8d336-7ad0-753d-98ba-02b79550d845"],["Marriage of son Wiebe Postema Hazenberg, cooper, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:08152408-d1e3-4aa7-c5a2-cc1667bc0552"],["Death of son Wiebe Postema Hazenberg, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d1ec46e7-1092-5543-8df2-1105cbb03cc0"],["Marriage of daughter Lutske, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:406487e3-5b67-819d-46e2-dc04c92d8cb6"],["Baptism of the other Wijbe, son of Willem Wijbes, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ee81db4-dee1-848e-3ace-7f12e139e546"]]},
aafke:{"n":"Aafke Gales","d":"1770–c. 1815","b":1770,"s":"h","p":["galeh","eenje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Niekerk on 14 January 1770, the eldest child of Gale Harms and Eenje Jans. An Aafke Gales of Niekerk married Sipke Hindriks of Leek at Midwolde on 19 October 1794 and had a son Hindrik baptised at Leek in June 1796. Aafke was living at Leek when her banns with Wiebe Willems were read in 1798, so this may have been her first marriage, but the registers do not call her a widow.\n\nShe married Wiebe Willems at Midwolde on 22 April 1798 and named two of their children, IJnje and Gale, after her parents. Wiebe was buried in March 1809, leaving her with four children and a fifth, Wiebe, born that May. On 12 September 1810 she signed a marriage contract with the shoemaker Bralt Pieters Hovinga, a man about sixteen years younger, and they married at Grootegast on 14 October. They had a son Pieter, later a day labourer, and a child Ake who died on 1 November 1813 aged 42 weeks.\n\nShe died between 1813 and 9 May 1816, when Bralt married again as her widower; no burial entry has been found. His own death record of 1859 calls her Aafke Gales Staal, the surname her father and her brother Harm took, which confirms her place in that Niekerk family. Her son Willem's baptism and marriage records call her \"Aafke Arends\"; his death record gives Aafke Gales.","src":[["Baptism, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f7203cee-b324-2ff1-d950-d1b84a778af5"],["Marriage, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:760fb3a7-2562-90d8-8937-d9a6139d9fc4"],["Second marriage, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1a425e69-af16-68ef-57a8-04d93680ed87"],["Marriage of Aafke Gales and Sipke Hindriks, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ab972e17-1b62-9d48-1cea-f3221daf5cf7"],["Baptism of Hindrik, Leek 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5e221de0-e3a7-64b7-787e-5a40617d41de"],["Banns, Grootegast, 1 Apr 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6667b096-6a58-55ca-cde5-754da351d0e6"],["Marriage contract, 12 Sep 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ab10a0a5-4395-af53-09de-0a2132c3f7a6"],["Death of child Ake Hovinga, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ccd4bd66-1811-caff-e306-04307ae577b8"],["Remarriage of Bralt Hovinga, widower of Aafke Gales, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b2c594e2-b1d0-b6b0-9a3c-febb39dbc2d5"],["Marriage of son Pieter Bralts Hovinga, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cca22369-54cc-d956-190e-1ee58d493ea9"],["Death of Bralt Hovinga, widower of Aafke Gales Staal, 1859","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:525d129f-64fd-ea40-50cd-d0cc0cc16ddc"]]},
johanngrab:{"n":"Johann Diedrich Grabhorn","d":"Oldenburg","s":"h","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"Of Oldenburg in north-west Germany. He is known only from the Frisian records of his daughter Anna Catharina Dorothea Christiana, who was born at Oldenburg on 15 November 1805. Her two marriage records, at Leeuwarden in 1828 and at Smallingerland in 1839, name her parents as Johann Diedrich (or Diederich) Grabhorn and Eleonora Schröder, and her death record of 1887 repeats the names.\n\nAnna named her second son, born at Leeuwarden in 1831, Johan Diederich after him. The Oldenburg church books that would show his own baptism, marriage and trade are not freely available online, and no German record of him has been checked.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Anna, Leeuwarden 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c68edecf-4da1-6895-963e-190a6aa157f0"],["Marriage of daughter Anna, Smallingerland 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:899fdae3-ed0b-cfd5-ce74-8f0724fe948c"],["Death of daughter Anna, Bergum 1887","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:294c5fae-a2d7-5bf6-9a9b-2813b5811988"],["Birth of grandson Johan Diederich Blok, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5080f935-fe6c-7a7a-6bbf-99e859bd722a"]]},
eleonora:{"n":"Eleonora Sophia Schröder","d":"Oldenburg","s":"h","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"Of Oldenburg in north-west Germany, the wife of Johann Diedrich Grabhorn and mother of Anna Catharina Dorothea Christiana Grabhorn, who was born there on 15 November 1805. The Frisian clerks wrote her name three ways: Eleonora Schroder at Anna's first marriage in 1828, Eleonora Schröder at the second in 1839, and Leonora Sophia Schröder in Anna's death record of 1887.\n\nHer name lived on in Friesland. In October 1850 Anna called her youngest daughter Leonora Sophia Hermandina; that granddaughter married Sytze Blom at Tietjerksteradeel in 1881. No German record of Eleonora herself has been found in the sources that can be searched freely.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Anna, Leeuwarden 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c68edecf-4da1-6895-963e-190a6aa157f0"],["Marriage of daughter Anna, Smallingerland 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:899fdae3-ed0b-cfd5-ce74-8f0724fe948c"],["Death of daughter Anna, Bergum 1887","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:294c5fae-a2d7-5bf6-9a9b-2813b5811988"],["Birth of granddaughter Leonora Sophia Hermandina Grabhorn, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:080dcf68-b358-e7d7-7462-83ce413e12cb"]]},
johannesidema:{"n":"Johannes Wybes Iedema","d":"1822–1880 · Oostermeer","b":1822,"x":1880,"s":"h","p":["wybe","grietjebvdm"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 6 December 1822 at Oostermeer, the first child of the labourer Wybe Johannes Idema and Grietje Berends van der Meer, who had married that March. On 27 April 1850, aged 27, he married Sipkjen Sipkes Fransbergen, 25, the daughter of the innkeeper at Eestrum, and settled in that village.\n\nThey had six children at Eestrum: Grietje (18 August 1850), Aaltje (23 November 1851), Wiebe (1853), Sipke (1855), Berend (1857) and Sjuk (1862). In January 1867 he sold a small piece of arable land and heath at Harkema Opeinde, where his parents had ended their days, for 200 guilders. Sipkjen died on 13 October 1868. In December 1870 he bought a lot at Eestrum at the sale of the Wiersma family, his late wife's relatives, and in 1871 he borrowed money from a Leeuwarden lender.\n\nOn 28 June 1871, aged 48, he married Pietje Gerks van der Veer of Eestrum, who was 40; their daughter Doetje was born in 1874. He died at Eestrum on 4 January 1880, aged 57. The death record gives his work as boodschaploper, a man who ran errands and carried messages and parcels between villages for a small fee. His sons Sipke and Sjuk later moved to Mantgum and Wirdum.","src":[["Marriage record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1f6afe9a-95b4-0124-2267-109d4a02476c"],["Birth record, 6 Dec 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b974ee6-32d2-68c2-de7a-78ee29c5439e"],["Birth of Grietje, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56db282c-a742-4719-b868-dc936a5748f8"],["Birth of Aaltje, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36509de1-ff88-8d94-d57a-76b9fe9df867"],["Birth of Wiebe, 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1140df0d-6724-dadf-0f9f-2c1518341716"],["Birth of Sipke, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:26faac5c-7ea7-66d1-7631-8a7c60bb1bcd"],["Birth of Berend, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50a75f86-6782-5e17-a561-c23f024d06e9"],["Birth of Sjuk, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5a70bacb-d964-0c48-feeb-4e28555e2a13"],["Notarial deed, sale at Harkema Opeinde, 5 Jan 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:03fbfe2a-2659-4dcc-af97-9cfa4faa556c"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Eestrum, 15 Dec 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3db59626-9714-45e5-ac76-140f43c902ca"],["Notarial deed, bond, 21 Nov 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:48c60e6e-6e27-4bda-9fd4-f0f0fe5f7b88"],["Second marriage, 28 Jun 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2c458a83-9609-fa35-9882-b9f28b5a1665"],["Birth of Doetje, 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2e56627a-aad3-6037-0a6d-f6210aa2258d"],["Death record, Eestrum, 4 Jan 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f3ffd5bf-8757-2c2c-6105-fe803c24ebbf"],["Death duties file, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4928b7a2-c3fd-4f76-9b60-e3b61ff1c825"]]},
sipkjen:{"n":"Sipkjen Sipkes Fransbergen","d":"1824–1868 · Eestrum","b":1824,"s":"h","p":["sipke","aaltjelw"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eestrum on 15 November 1824, a daughter of Sipke Sjuks Fransbergen and Aaltje Liebbes Wiersma. Her father kept the village inn and owned a house and land there. She had brothers Sjuk and Liebbe and sisters Gepke and Lutske.\n\nOn 27 April 1850, aged 25, she married Johannes Wybes Iedema, a labourer's son from Oostermeer. Their first child, Grietje, was born on 18 August that year, followed by Aaltje (1851), Wiebe (1853), Sipke (1855), Berend (1857) and Sjuk (1862), all at Eestrum. Sipke and Sjuk carried the names of her father and his father.\n\nShe died at Eestrum on 13 October 1868, aged 43, when her youngest child, Sjuk, was six. Both her parents outlived her; her mother died in 1871 and her father in 1874. Her husband married again in 1871.","x":1868,"src":[["Population register, born 15 Nov 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:23b495d0-dff3-d0dd-4cf2-44afa58f497a"],["Marriage record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1f6afe9a-95b4-0124-2267-109d4a02476c"],["Birth of Grietje, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56db282c-a742-4719-b868-dc936a5748f8"],["Birth of Aaltje, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36509de1-ff88-8d94-d57a-76b9fe9df867"],["Birth of Sjuk, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5a70bacb-d964-0c48-feeb-4e28555e2a13"],["Death duties file, Eestrum, 13 Oct 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4a249784-90f0-4928-b18f-4954ed84bca9"]]},
wybe:{"n":"Wybe Johannes Idema","d":"c. 1794–1863 · Oostermeer","b":1794,"s":"h","p":["johannesci","saakje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Oostermeer about 1793 or 1794, a son of the labourer Johannes Cornelis Idema and Saakje Wybes Bander. When his father registered the family surname in 1811, Wybe was listed as 18 and living away at Bergum.\n\nOn 28 March 1822, aged 28, he married Grietje Berends van der Meer, 25, also born at Oostermeer. Their first child, Johannes, was born on 6 December 1822; a second son, Berend, was born about 1826, married at Dantumadeel in 1854 and died at Eestrum in 1860, aged 34. He is called a labourer (arbeider) in 1860 and again at his death.\n\nIn later years the couple lived at Harkema Opeinde in Achtkarspelen, a poor settlement of labourers on the heath north-east of Oostermeer. Grietje died there on 20 August 1854. In June 1860 Wybe borrowed 300 guilders from a man at Oostermeer. He died at Harkema Opeinde on 5 March 1863, a widower aged 69.","src":[["Marriage record, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c866d746-6702-0a02-08b8-8bb93e2c5888"],["Surname register, Oostermeer 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ac8efe1d-d133-4e98-acee-a9ec4679dc95"],["Birth of son Johannes, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b974ee6-32d2-68c2-de7a-78ee29c5439e"],["Marriage of son Berend, 1854","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b08dd0fd-789d-dff3-18bd-17c90e5a9b7f"],["Death of son Berend, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e8df4ea5-b1d4-d9d4-befd-cce65f9bfcd3"],["Notarial deed, bond, 5 Jun 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0fa370ef-fae7-4bd2-b382-297fa764a749"],["Death record, Harkema Opeinde, 5 Mar 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e7acda2a-93c9-fe6b-e871-121ff3659caf"]],"x":1863},
grietjebvdm:{"n":"Grietje Berends van der Meer","d":"1796–1854 · Oostermeer","b":1796,"s":"h","p":["berendw","grietjet"],"r":["nl"],"x":1854,"t":"Born at Oostermeer on 26 November 1796 and baptised there on 29 January 1797, the daughter of Berend (Berent, Beern) Wybes and Grietje Tobias. An elder sister, also Grietje, born in 1793, had died young. The family lived at Witveen, a hamlet of Oostermeer.\n\nHer father died at Witveen on 6 February 1808, when she was eleven, and her little sister Geeske two weeks later. Her mother married again that autumn and moved to Rottevalle in February 1809. The children took the surname van der Meer. Her sister Stijntje married in 1815, her brother Rienk in 1821 and her sister Sjoerdtje in 1822.\n\nOn 28 March 1822, aged 25, she married Wybe Johannes Idema, a labourer of Oostermeer. Their first son, Johannes, was born that December and another son, Berend, about 1826. The couple later lived at Harkema-Opeinde in Achtkarspelen, where she died on 20 August 1854, aged 57.","src":[["Marriage, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c866d746-6702-0a02-08b8-8bb93e2c5888"],["Death record, 1854","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cf1ced38-4f90-e997-901f-7fb1505efd9b"],["Baptism, Oostermeer, 29 Jan 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9f6a0328-572a-44a3-a962-756e1faa3df3"],["Baptism of elder sister Grietje, 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ab9eb389-e443-4d51-a81e-46c6afabd48e"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Beern Wybes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea247357-27a3-4762-b261-be208496cf58"],["Marriage of sister Stijntje, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:707e8692-4e43-4853-e751-e7c5dbb0e87f"],["Marriage of brother Rienk, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fffbf705-1014-5c5a-3168-9ca4b179f913"],["Marriage of sister Sjoerdtje, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:858dba33-b381-ea2d-092f-29aeaeb5faac"]]},
johannesci:{"n":"Johannes Cornelis Idema","d":"1763–1849 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hzcornelishayes","hzwytskewytses"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Oostermeer, the village in Tietjerksteradeel now called Eastermar, on 11 March 1763. He was the son of the labourer Cornelis Hayes; his mother is Wytske Wytses in the reconstruction of the village population made from the church books, and Wytske Pieters in his own death record. He was a labourer (arbeider) all his life, called a day labourer (journalier) in the French civil register, and in 1796 he was receiving poor relief.\n\nHe married Saakje Wybes Bander, who came from Noorderdrachten. In 1811, when every Frisian family had to register a fixed surname, he chose Ydema and listed nine children: Wytske (22), Trijntje (20, then at Opende in Groningen), Wiebe (18, at Bergum), Ybeltje (17, at Garijp), Lysbert (15), Cornelis (13), Wieger (11), Grietje (born 27 February 1804) and Antje (3 August 1805). A daughter Aafke, born about 1798, appears in later records. Clerks afterwards wrote the name Idema and Iedema. Saakje was baptised as an adult in December 1813, and the two youngest girls on 31 July 1814.\n\nSix of the children married in Tietjerksteradeel between 1817 and 1828. Antje died in 1830 aged 25 and Wyger in 1847 aged 47. Saakje died on 29 September 1839. Johannes died at Oostermeer on 13 January 1849, a widower aged 85, still described as a labourer.","b":1763,"x":1849,"src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Johannes Cornelis Ydema","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cba1652e-6b20-4894-87ee-70c717d38835"],["Death record, Oostermeer, 13 Jan 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b1ff9639-0fd5-b2b5-fefd-17fe83094df7"],["Surname register, Oostermeer 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ac8efe1d-d133-4e98-acee-a9ec4679dc95"],["Church members, Tietjerksteradeel, Dec 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:943ac552-831e-4f54-bcb1-462768031d11"],["Marriage of daughter Trijntje, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8b1a3b91-017c-6334-7fa5-bb8efba2e0e9"],["Marriage of son Wyger, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3f5b4630-83c4-a11b-11d9-1b402bfed5ed"],["Marriage of daughter Wytske, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f491909e-b012-3688-65a0-7cd70bb3eb08"],["Marriage of son Cornelis, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:539708bf-2b69-199a-596f-459f3fe5a06f"],["Marriage of daughter Grietje, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1121ccb5-0787-fb70-3da9-f303d0b2616f"],["Death of daughter Antje, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2c78b9ef-ea03-9a43-125a-87638cd0c16b"],["Death of son Wyger, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b0408c70-735a-4d2f-c194-47333a61051d"],["Death of daughter Aafke, 1876","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:52c12874-b9f1-07c4-120b-cc9b71add846"]]},
saakje:{"n":"Saakje Wybes Bander","d":"1762–1839 · Noorderdrachten","s":"h","p":["hzwybemichiels","hztrijntjegurbes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Noorderdrachten on 17 August 1762, the daughter of Wybe Michiels and Trijntje Gurbes, who had married at Drachten on 1 June 1760. She had brothers Michiel, Gurbe, Alle and Anne and a sister Jetske. The family surname is written Bander in the civil records and Brander in one later compilation.\n\nShe married Johannes Cornelis, a labourer of Oostermeer who took the surname Ydema (Idema) in 1811, and had at least ten children between about 1789 and 1805, among them Wybe, born about 1793. In October 1807 she was one of the heirs of her brother Michiel, who died childless. She was baptised as an adult, on profession of faith, at Oostermeer in December 1813, when she was 51.\n\nShe saw six of her children married and lost her daughter Antje in 1830. She died at Oostermeer on 29 September 1839, aged 77. Her husband outlived her by nine years.","b":1762,"x":1839,"src":[["Death record, Oostermeer, 29 Sep 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c21f5797-f445-5b4d-99ff-580ca39379ad"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Saakje Wybes Brander","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ef290bac-c86f-4228-8033-018d9bd5406e"],["Marriage of parents, Drachten, 1 Jun 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d4c1ad7-f046-49ed-a883-2ef7ea1a6901"],["Death duties file of brother Michiel Wybes, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:67694f8e-acae-4a24-8e7d-597050f2d852"],["Church members, Tietjerksteradeel, Dec 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:943ac552-831e-4f54-bcb1-462768031d11"],["Surname register, Oostermeer 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ac8efe1d-d133-4e98-acee-a9ec4679dc95"]]},
berendw:{"n":"Berend Wybes","d":"1763–1808 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hzwybeannes","hzgrietjebeerns"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 20 March 1763, the son of Wybe Annes and Grietje Beerns, according to the reconstruction of the old Tietjerksteradeel population published by the Frisian archives. His name is written Berend, Berent and Beern.\n\nOn 8 May 1791 he married Grietje Tobias at Augustinusga, where both were then living. The tax register of Oostermeer shows him arriving from Opeinde in 1792 as head of a household, and still there in 1805. A daughter was born in 1794 \"in het Kattegat\" at Oostermeer, and by 1798 the family lived at Witveen, a hamlet of the village. He and his wife made their profession of faith together on 10 August 1798.\n\nTheir children were Grietje (1793, died young), Stijntje (1794), a second Grietje (26 November 1796), Sjoerdtje (1798), Rienk (1801) and Geeske (1804). He died at Witveen on 6 February 1808, aged 45, leaving five children; Geeske died two weeks after him. He never used a surname. His children called themselves van der Meer.","b":1763,"x":1808,"src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Beern Wybes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea247357-27a3-4762-b261-be208496cf58"],["Marriage, Augustinusga, 8 May 1791","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d027e9e2-d144-4ff3-a604-92159f468acb"],["Tax register (speciekohier), Oostermeer no. 156","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d2f35f7c-e4cc-4c8f-8cce-98ba06e29872"],["Baptism of Styntje, Rottevalle 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:04a617b1-2959-42ce-8a8e-005ef683fae5"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9f6a0328-572a-44a3-a962-756e1faa3df3"],["Baptism of Sjoerdtje, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b6811058-668b-4032-9b66-52db35249ea4"],["Baptism of Rienk, 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:41eb7a95-7f51-4285-9d78-3a26312050c4"],["Baptism of Geeske, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:927b137e-965b-4b52-a4e3-1c6a7c48052f"],["Church members, profession of faith, 10 Aug 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f3fad548-3a85-46f8-9237-e5734b373621"]]},
grietjet:{"n":"Grietje Tobias","d":"c. 1769–1842 · Kuikhorne","s":"h","p":["hztobiaspieters","hzstijntjerienks"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The daughter of Tobias Pieters and Stijntje Rienks, who married at Oostermeer in 1761 and moved to Kuikhorne in Achtkarspelen. Her parents had three daughters called Grietje: one baptised at Oostermeer on 14 March 1762, one at Twijzel in 1767, and one baptised at Twijzel on 26 May 1771, then about a year and a half old. Re-using a name in this way usually means the earlier child had died. The reconstruction of the Tietjerksteradeel population identifies her with the first, but her death record gives her age as 72 in May 1842, which fits only the youngest, born about late 1769.\n\nShe married Berend Wybes at Augustinusga on 8 May 1791 and lived with him at Oostermeer, from 1798 at the hamlet of Witveen, where both made their profession of faith on 10 August that year. They had six children between 1793 and 1804. Berend died on 6 February 1808 and their youngest, Geeske, on 21 February.\n\nOn 6 November 1808 she married Ybele Pieters of Rottevalle and moved there in February 1809 with a church attestation. In later records she is called Grietje Tobias van der Meer. She left Smallingerland for Ureterp in April 1841 and died there on 21 May 1842, a widow and a labourer (arbeidster), the record of her death naming her father, Tobias Pieters. She left no real estate. The death-duties file lists her children: Stijntje, wife of the skipper Rinze Klazes van der Schuit at Rottevalle; Grietje, wife of the labourer Wiebe Johannes Iedema at Drogeham; Sjoerdtje (Sjutje), wife of Libbe Jakobs at Twijzel; and Rienk Berends van der Meer, a shopkeeper.","x":1842,"src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Grietje Tobias van der Meer","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:54215b3d-7d73-4ae3-8759-a8776e4b6ff6"],["Marriage, Augustinusga, 8 May 1791","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d027e9e2-d144-4ff3-a604-92159f468acb"],["Second marriage, Rottevalle, 6 Nov 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3fd38fda-aa87-446b-ac32-0bb0a24938da"],["Church members, left for Rottevalle, 13 Feb 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0ab27b3f-14df-463a-89b8-a98259ae77d9"],["Church members, left for Ureterp, 20 Apr 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36282aae-839d-4a08-9f76-221be8a3ff24"],["Baptism of a sister Grietje, Twijzel 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8048cc73-4da2-4fc3-8d44-13bfea2ff508"],["Baptism of a sister Grietje, Twijzel 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c94b4932-6868-4333-bb13-cdd0c74c3a12"],["Death record, Ureterp 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:deadbffc-7fdc-9213-0303-c008905b6dab"],["Death duties file, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d4604e76-ad26-4860-ae2f-183955a69e8d"]],"b":1769},
sipke:{"n":"Sipke Sjuks Fransbergen","d":"1793–1874 · Kooten","b":1793,"x":1874,"s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Kooten in Achtkarspelen on 30 November 1793, according to the population register. By 1817 he was married to Aaltje Liebbes Wiersma and living in her village, Eestrum, a few kilometres away across the municipal border in Tietjerksteradeel.\n\nHe was the innkeeper (kastelein) of Eestrum. The land survey of 1832 lists him under that trade as an owner of property, and so does a bond of 1847. In December 1837 he bought a house, yard and a piece of arable land at Eestrum for 1,400 guilders, and in 1866 he was able to lend 1,000 guilders to a man at Opeinde.\n\nHis children were Sjuk (born 9 December 1817, died unmarried in 1894), Sipkje (1824), Liebbe (1827), Gepke (1829) and Lutske. Sipkje married Johannes Iedema in 1850 and died in 1868. Liebbe became a ferry skipper (veerschipper), running a regular boat service from Mantgum, and died there on 10 September 1871. Aaltje died two weeks later, on 24 September. Sipke died in Tietjerksteradeel on 28 November 1874, a widower aged 81. The index entries of his death and Aaltje's do not name their parents.\n\nNotaries at Bergum wrote his name Sipke Sjuks Fransberg. In August 1821 he bought partly dug-out peat land at Eestrum from his wife's brothers and sister for 150 guilders; in 1823 he borrowed 1,400 guilders from his brother-in-law Pieter Gerks van der Veer and sold him a piece of land; and in December 1827 he bought the house numbered 38 at Eestrum for 426 guilders. A first son named Liebbe, born in January 1820, died young. Where he was baptised and who his parents were has not been found: the name Fransbergen does not appear in the 1811 surname registers, and no baptism of a Sipke son of Sjuk in 1793 has turned up in the indexes for Kooten or Twijzel.","src":[["Population register, born 30 Nov 1793 at Kooten","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2899bc51-e2be-86a7-cf43-03360dd35fad"],["Birth of son Sjuk, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ada2a52-850d-9b56-565e-18b4b08795c8"],["Birth of daughter Gepke, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b928d03-3c76-8389-82be-557af7aeff38"],["Land registry 1832, Oostermeer section G","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:234fecb0-ef3b-4771-8c7b-e160c7c80b89"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Eestrum, 9 Dec 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1e2ab106-30a7-4ef1-826f-b762dc5db3f9"],["Notarial deed, bond, 11 Jun 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1b2e8f2c-9e86-4552-9efc-26c2cb79fc66"],["Notarial deed, loan, 19 Jul 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:585ba8d1-505e-4256-9098-28c786697346"],["Death of son Liebbe, Mantgum 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:697f659a-e609-1ba0-7352-d6c5a6083bb2"],["Death of son Sjuk, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:313c8bf5-94ae-6491-42a5-1d9ff741ec37"],["Death record, 28 Nov 1874","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f6647755-42ed-9530-aa38-80e71b15fd2f"],["Notarial deed, purchase of land, 29 Aug 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d162b8d-a00f-4710-8b77-57226bed2869"],["Notarial deed, bond, 5 Mar 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:03833af0-0588-4be6-9af4-728c8eaac87f"],["Notarial deed, purchase of house no. 38, 5 Dec 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:799f8e27-c8ab-48d8-8aed-f9a216d266ee"],["Birth of Liebbe, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e947c4d-f62f-435d-0867-a05bcc1066a4"]]},
aaltjelw:{"n":"Aaltje Liebbes Wiersma","d":"1794–1871 · Eestrum","x":1871,"s":"h","p":["hzliebbeklazes","hzlutskesjoukes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eestrum on 2 January 1794 and baptised there on 26 January, the daughter of Liebbe Klazes, a farmer, and Lutske Sjoukes. In 1811 her father registered the surname Wiersma. She was named after her mother's mother, Aaltje Klases. Her brothers Klaas (1787) and Klaas (1797) died young; Sjouke (1789), Gepke (1792) and Ebe (1795) grew up with her. In May 1818 she and her brothers and sister, Sjouke, Gepke and Ebe Liebbes Wiersma, all of Eestrum, jointly owed 1,500 guilders on a bond, and in April 1826 the four of them, with their cousins, the children of their father's brother Ouwe Klazes Wiersma, sold the house and garden numbered 45 at Eestrum with pasture land.\n\nBy December 1817 she was married to Sipke Sjuks Fransbergen, who became the village innkeeper. Their children were Sjuk (1817), Liebbe (born January 1820, who died young), Sipkje (1824), a second Liebbe (1827), Gepke (1829) and Lutske; Liebbe and Lutske carried her parents' names. In 1820 she, her sister and two brothers bought half a house and land at Eestrum for 2,000 guilders, and in 1821 Sipke bought from her brothers and sister a piece of partly dug-out peat land. Her daughter Sipkje died in 1868.\n\nHer son Liebbe, a ferry skipper at Mantgum, died on 10 September 1871. Aaltje died in Tietjerksteradeel two weeks later, on 24 September 1871, aged 77, after more than fifty years of marriage. Sipke outlived her by three years.","b":1794,"src":[["Population register, born 1794 at Eestrum","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:709c2522-2edc-b333-4ac1-dbd5e3e2d99c"],["Notarial deed, bond, 4 May 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6f932f48-3369-4b05-9631-6c78585ece07"],["Notarial deed, sale at Eestrum, 18 Apr 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:27d0eba8-659f-4706-86b4-8eb2f0cfb43e"],["Notarial deed, 31 Mar 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:589c1d5e-5c07-48d2-9c2e-5324923fb0b9"],["Birth of son Sjuk, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ada2a52-850d-9b56-565e-18b4b08795c8"],["Death of son Liebbe, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:697f659a-e609-1ba0-7352-d6c5a6083bb2"],["Death record, 24 Sep 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c35794db-f07c-af43-a5cc-9f39c028a00d"],["Baptism, Eestrum 26 Jan 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:157c185d-816c-4465-addc-663fdcd53f1c"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Liebbe Klazes Wiersma","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea568fdd-0ed7-4b79-897c-1013f690089d"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Eestrum, 17 Apr 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:764ddc1e-e0d3-42ce-a570-8dfebf39cd9c"],["Notarial deed, purchase of land, 29 Aug 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d162b8d-a00f-4710-8b77-57226bed2869"],["Birth of Liebbe, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e947c4d-f62f-435d-0867-a05bcc1066a4"],["Death of brother Ebe Liebbes Wiersma, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e2b3c27-aa26-42ab-1002-cecce9b2ebd8"]]},
taeketvdm:{"n":"Taeke Tjeerds van der Meer","d":"1852–1939 · Surhuizum","b":1852,"s":"h","p":["tjeerd","ytjemt"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Surhuizum in Achtkarspelen on 28 May 1852, the eldest son of the labourer Tjeerd Taekes van der Meer and Ytje Martens Tillema; later population registers give 12 June 1852. He was named after his grandfather, Taeke Minderts van der Meer.\n\nHe moved west to Idaarderadeel, and on 19 May 1880, aged 27, he married Rinske Douwes IJsselstein of Wartena there. He was a labourer (arbeider) at Warga that December, when their first son, Tjeerd, born on 1 December, died at three weeks old. Their daughter Yttje was born at Warga on 9 February 1884. A daughter Dirkje died at Warga in October 1893, aged one. The last child, Antje, was born in September 1898, and a married woman named Rinske IJsselstein, aged 41, died at Idaarderadeel on 31 October 1898; his own death record in 1939 calls him the widower of Rinske IJsselstein.\n\nHe lived at Wartena. On 2 September 1911 he bought a farmhouse and yard (\"een boerenhuis c.a.\") at Warga for 1,925 guilders at a sale before a Leeuwarden notary, in a deed that also names his son-in-law Willem Hazenberg as a buyer. A deed of 1918 calls him a workman (werkman) at Wartena. He died at Wartena on 29 April 1939, aged 86.","src":[["Marriage record, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08e7a90a-fddd-8261-ef8c-bb26a9901927"],["Notarial deed, sale at Warga, 2 Sep 1911","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e7276a39-1269-4e4e-b79c-73df38e0e85f"],["Notarial deed, Warga 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fbfe975b-d2f8-4910-a25d-28a5bf0a20ab"],["Death of son Tjeerd, Warga 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58f88c49-5ee2-a1d9-8445-0a18a2db7cb6"],["Birth of daughter Antje, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:afe33860-baf9-8536-6ff2-7cce1486e0af"],["Birth record, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5e9e5324-7761-5e3a-3057-023e3f53556b"],["Population register, Surhuizum (born 12 Jun 1852)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aae98127-c30e-d411-688d-7757fdcd2f62"],["Birth of son Tjeerd, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1edb1629-ed53-3dde-b9ca-561774048094"],["Birth of daughter Yttje, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:73c2a4c2-cd9e-2279-bb29-3ebcfe87d30b"],["Death of daughter Dirkje, Warga 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6d44e06d-f07d-3bf1-07f0-9ab8c16d09b3"],["Death of Rinske IJsselstein, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb34f746-a7dd-bec3-0507-d55a8b53602f"],["Death record, Wartena 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:23aa1054-ba6c-ad38-39bf-4ebe38a36f64"]],"x":1939},
rinske:{"n":"Rinske Douwes IJsselstein","d":"1857–1898 · Wartena","b":1857,"s":"h","p":["douwe","froukje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wartena on 30 September 1857, the daughter of the labourer Douwe Taekes IJsselstein and Froukje Ulbes Visser; the birth was registered at Idaarderadeel. She was one of at least five children: her sisters Neeltje and Grietje and her brother Ulbe all married in Idaarderadeel, and a younger sister Geertje lived until 1952.\n\nShe was 22 when she married Taeke Tjeerds van der Meer, a labourer from Surhuizum, on 19 May 1880. Their first child, Tjeerd, died at Warga on 24 December 1880, three weeks old; both parents are called labourers in the record. Yttje was born at Warga on 9 February 1884. A daughter Dirkje died at Warga in October 1893, aged one. The last child, Antje, was born on 3 September 1898, when the couple lived at Warga.\n\nA married woman named Rinske IJsselstein, aged 41, died at Idaarderadeel on 31 October 1898, eight weeks after that birth. The index entry names no husband or parents, but the age fits her exactly, and when Taeke died in 1939 his death record called him the widower of Rinske IJsselstein. He never remarried.","src":[["Birth of Rinske IJsselstein, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5de11e39-162a-fa49-cf11-81f6c641e3c2"],["Marriage van der Meer-IJsselstein, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08e7a90a-fddd-8261-ef8c-bb26a9901927"],["Death of son Tjeerd, Warga 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58f88c49-5ee2-a1d9-8445-0a18a2db7cb6"],["Birth of daughter Antje, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:afe33860-baf9-8536-6ff2-7cce1486e0af"],["Death of Rinske IJsselstein, aged 41, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb34f746-a7dd-bec3-0507-d55a8b53602f"],["Death of daughter Dirkje, Warga 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6d44e06d-f07d-3bf1-07f0-9ab8c16d09b3"],["Death of Taeke van der Meer, widower of Rinske IJsselstein, 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:23aa1054-ba6c-ad38-39bf-4ebe38a36f64"],["Marriage of sister Neeltje, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1f01b433-1c90-82e4-9cd9-a49b380a79ce"]],"x":1898},
tjeerd:{"n":"Tjeerd Taekes van der Meer","d":"1822–1907 · Surhuisterveen","b":1822,"s":"h","p":["taekem","auktjen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 11 June 1822 in Achtkarspelen, at Surhuisterveen, the youngest child of the labourer Taeke Minderts van der Meer and Aukjen Tjeerds de Vries. He carried the name of his mother's father, Tjeerd Hinnes. His mother died in 1847.\n\nOn 18 May 1850, aged 27, he married Ytje Martens Tillema of Surhuizum at Achtkarspelen. Surhuizum is the next village west of Surhuisterveen, and the couple spent their lives there. He was a labourer (arbeider) all his working life. Their son Taeke, named after Tjeerd's father, was born on 28 May 1852. A stillborn child followed in December 1855. Auke, born about 1862, and Durkje, born about 1866, both married in Achtkarspelen in the 1880s and spent their lives in the district, Auke as a labourer at Drogeham.\n\nYtje died at Surhuizum on 26 September 1885. Tjeerd lived on for more than twenty years as a widower and died at Surhuizum on 12 April 1907, aged 85. His father had reached 93.","src":[["Marriage record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2abfce7c-7253-19f5-497d-4e2dbf94dd66"],["Birth record, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d61596b-4694-dd57-7806-32501db26aab"],["Birth of son Taeke, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5e9e5324-7761-5e3a-3057-023e3f53556b"],["Stillborn child, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:10826570-6c59-989d-6a53-29f3718a2c18"],["Marriage of son Auke, 1886","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3616580d-7d67-2052-ea8e-c4b01f283286"],["Marriage of daughter Durkje, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0039973b-2dbb-7f9f-9db9-c677ea108fe6"],["Death of Ytje Martens Tillema, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5860412e-9213-c24b-9c23-1da40705d6b3"],["Death record, Surhuizum 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:15995455-68f6-1f33-b6eb-5205433b558e"]],"x":1907},
ytjemt:{"n":"Ytje Martens Tillema","d":"1827–1885 · Surhuizum","b":1827,"x":1885,"s":"h","p":[null,"durkjen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Achtkarspelen on 26 August 1827, at Surhuizum. Her birth record names only her mother, Durkjen Simmes Tillema; no father was registered, so her patronymic, Martens, is the only clue to who he was.\n\nOn 18 May 1850 she married Tjeerd Taekes van der Meer of Surhuisterveen at Achtkarspelen. The marriage record gives her age as 22, which fits a birth in 1827 or 1828; her death record, which says 53, would point to about 1832, but the birth record settles it. They lived at Surhuizum, where Tjeerd worked as a labourer. Their son Taeke was born on 28 May 1852, a stillborn child in December 1855, Auke about 1862 and Durkje about 1866; her daughter carried her mother's name.\n\nShe died at Surhuizum on 26 September 1885, a few weeks after Durkje's wedding. Her death record again names her mother, as Durkje Tillema, and no father.","src":[["Marriage, Achtkarspelen, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2abfce7c-7253-19f5-497d-4e2dbf94dd66"],["Death record, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5860412e-9213-c24b-9c23-1da40705d6b3"],["Birth record, 26 Aug 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:84f47743-f711-84bf-6ca0-7c325de7effc"],["Birth of son Taeke, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5e9e5324-7761-5e3a-3057-023e3f53556b"],["Marriage of daughter Durkje, 16 Jul 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0039973b-2dbb-7f9f-9db9-c677ea108fe6"]]},
taekem:{"n":"Taeke Menderts van der Meer","d":"c. 1793–1886 · Rottevalle","s":"h","p":["hzmindertaekes","hztaapkejiskes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Rottevalle, the canal village on the border of Smallingerland and Achtkarspelen, about 1793, the son of Mindert Taekes and Taapke Jiskes. In 1811 his father registered the surname van der Meer, and Taeke, then 17, is on the list. His mother died that October, leaving five children.\n\nOn 12 October 1814, aged 20, he married Aukjen Tjeerds de Vries at Smallingerland; both lived at Rottevalle. She was 34 and had been married before, to Hendrik Jans van der Meer. A son, Meindert, had been born at Rottevalle in January 1814, before the wedding, and later records name Taeke and Aukjen as his parents. Their other children were Taapke (born 21 May 1818), named after Taeke's mother, and Tjeerd (born 11 June 1822), named after Aukjen's father.\n\nThe family moved to Surhuisterveen, the long peat village in Achtkarspelen, where Taeke worked as a labourer (arbeider). Aukjen died there on 14 April 1847, aged 66. Taeke outlived her by almost forty years and died at Surhuisterveen on 18 January 1886, a widower, aged 93.","b":1793,"x":1886,"src":[["Marriage, Smallingerland, 12 Oct 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06338d61-ba84-50a9-7edc-782e4581757d"],["Surname registration of Mindert Taekes van der Meer, Rottevalle 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0811530a-6983-4002-b05c-1b117cf46b4a"],["Birth of Taapke, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f7b123fe-8f47-1d50-acd7-a331fdc7fc4d"],["Birth of Tjeerd, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d61596b-4694-dd57-7806-32501db26aab"],["Marriage of son Meindert (born Rottevalle 18 Jan 1814), 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c1a039b1-2cda-c9d1-edab-813e8fb5ba21"],["Death of Aukje Tjeerds de Vries, Surhuisterveen 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0c79b5b8-f9b3-ec17-7045-40c4b8f434c3"],["Death record, Surhuisterveen 1886","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:442d3047-edd3-38ba-5ab7-ff47bfa2464a"]]},
auktjen:{"n":"Auktjen Tjeerds de Vries","d":"c. 1780–1847 · Surhuisterveen","s":"h","p":["hztjeerdhinnes","hzfolkjentjipkes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The daughter of Tjeerd Hinnes and Folkjen Tjipkes, who married at Veenwouden in 1774 and farmed at Hardegarijp in Tietjerksteradeel. She was born about 1780; her brother Tjipke, born about 1779, was named after their mother's father.\n\nHer first husband was Hendrik Jans van der Meer. Their son Tjeerd died at Rottevalle on 8 March 1817, aged seven; his mother is named in the record as Auktje Tjeerds de Vries, labourer. On 12 October 1814, aged 34, she married Taeke Minderts van der Meer, fourteen years her junior; both were then living at Rottevalle. A son, Meindert, had been born there in January 1814. Taapke followed in 1818 and Tjeerd in June 1822; Tjeerd was given her father's name, as her first son had been.\n\nThe family settled at Surhuisterveen, where she worked as a labourer (arbeidster). She died there on 14 April 1847, aged 66. Her brother Tjipke Tjeerds de Vries died at Rottevalle the next day, 15 April 1847, aged 68.","b":1780,"x":1847,"src":[["Marriage, Smallingerland, 12 Oct 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06338d61-ba84-50a9-7edc-782e4581757d"],["Death of son Tjeerd Hendriks van der Meer, Rottevalle 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2b98044e-e5c2-9f23-18e2-d8b609bd187e"],["Birth of Tjeerd, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d61596b-4694-dd57-7806-32501db26aab"],["Death record, Surhuisterveen 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0c79b5b8-f9b3-ec17-7045-40c4b8f434c3"],["Death of brother Tjipke Tjeerds de Vries, Rottevalle 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7eb04577-c1ec-9731-20e8-cb29514419cc"]]},
durkjen:{"n":"Durkjen Simmes Tillema","d":"Surhuizum","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Mother of Ytje Martens Tillema, who was born at Surhuizum in Achtkarspelen on 26 August 1827. Durkjen was unmarried when the child was registered, and only she is named as a parent, in the birth record, in Ytje's marriage record of 1850, and again, as Durkje Tillema, in Ytje's death record of 1885. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Simme.\n\nHer granddaughter, Ytje's daughter Durkje van der Meer, born about 1866, carried her name. In 1811 the surname Tillema was registered at Surhuizum by Hendrik Freerks Tillema, and at Surhuisterveen by Willemke Dirks Tillema; how Durkjen was related to either has not been found, and no record of her own marriage or death has turned up in the Frisian indexes.","src":[["Birth of Ytje Martens Tillema, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:84f47743-f711-84bf-6ca0-7c325de7effc"],["Marriage of Ytje, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2abfce7c-7253-19f5-497d-4e2dbf94dd66"],["Death of Ytje, 1885","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5860412e-9213-c24b-9c23-1da40705d6b3"],["Surname registration, Hendrik Freerks Tillema, Surhuizum 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d01dc7be-0fe5-4e87-aa3a-e6e76088ad51"],["Surname registration, Willemke Dirks Tillema, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ab50c0b3-7172-4d1d-a962-a7d689c87927"]]},
douwe:{"n":"Douwe Taekes IJsselstein","d":"1823–1900 · Wartena","b":1823,"s":"h","p":["taekeb","antjelk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wartena in Idaarderadeel on 11 October 1823, the third son of Taeke Beens IJsselstein, a labourer, and Antje Lieuwes Kooystra of Eernewoude. His father died in February 1842, when Douwe was 18, leaving four children and so little that the estate was declared without means.\n\nOn 19 May 1849, aged 25, he married Froukje Ulbes Visser, also of Wartena, the daughter of a ship's carpenter. He worked as a labourer (arbeider). Their children included Neeltje (born at Warga about 1851), Ulbe (5 September 1855), Rinske (30 September 1857), Grietje (17 April 1861) and Geertje (about 1864). Neeltje married Roelof Jochums Jansma in 1872, Ulbe married Korneliske Boorsma in 1879, Rinske married Taeke van der Meer in 1880 and Grietje married Sybren Bergsma in 1884; Rinske died in 1898, aged 41.\n\nIn 1877 he borrowed 600 guilders on a bond before the notary at Roordahuizum, and in October 1899 he sold a house at Wartena for 800 guilders to Roelof Johannes Jansma of Warga. He died at Wartena on 25 November 1900, aged 77, still married to Froukje.","src":[["Marriage record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2edc165c-9556-bab3-bbd8-cb5444960bb9"],["Birth record, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f148b4b8-3d0a-8c8c-b78b-c389d94806b8"],["Birth of Ulbe, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1ce4aa59-4919-027a-d4b2-ac96860d81d3"],["Birth of Rinske, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5de11e39-162a-fa49-cf11-81f6c641e3c2"],["Birth of Grietje, 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d2bbeed-3a14-ddad-94bf-e5c134f01647"],["Marriage of Neeltje, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1f01b433-1c90-82e4-9cd9-a49b380a79ce"],["Marriage of Ulbe, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6829b906-85a7-3773-97c7-5822ac9f804f"],["Marriage of Grietje, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3a500b1f-e61a-8abe-ef27-2c65815e86e1"],["Notarial deed, bond, 22 Aug 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0d4999a1-af7d-4f78-b41c-d4d99168050b"],["Notarial deed, sale of a house at Wartena, 17 Oct 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3be207bc-8eed-4976-9105-3f430a445619"],["Death record, Wartena 1900","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6d078228-3117-8040-7437-8f80a6065d07"],["Death duties file of his father, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7682c829-9476-4c74-a246-f58d044c9b2c"]],"x":1900},
froukje:{"n":"Froukje Ulbes Visser","d":"1823–1900+ · Wartena","b":1823,"s":"h","p":["ulbe","neeltje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wartena in Idaarderadeel on 30 March 1823, the daughter of Ulbe Berends Visser, a ship's carpenter's journeyman (scheepstimmerknecht), and Neeltje Durks de Jong. Her mother came from Eernewoude, where her grandfather had been village magistrate. Froukje had an elder sister Grietje, who married a skipper and lived at Oldeboorn, and a brother Durk; two other children of her parents died as babies.\n\nOn 19 May 1849, aged 26, she married Douwe Taekes IJsselstein, a labourer of Wartena, born seven months after her. Their children included Neeltje (about 1851), Ulbe (1855), Rinske (1857), Grietje (1861) and Geertje (about 1864). Four of them married in Idaarderadeel between 1872 and 1884, and Rinske, the mother of Yttje van der Meer, died in 1898.\n\nDouwe died at Wartena on 25 November 1900, when she was 77. Her own death has not been found in the Frisian indexes. Her daughters Neeltje and Geertje both lived into their eighties, at Warga.","src":[["Population register, Wartena (born 30 Mar 1823)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:541038af-fc72-674b-e2d6-5704194cad28"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2edc165c-9556-bab3-bbd8-cb5444960bb9"],["Birth of Rinske, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5de11e39-162a-fa49-cf11-81f6c641e3c2"],["Birth of Grietje, 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d2bbeed-3a14-ddad-94bf-e5c134f01647"],["Death of Douwe IJsselstein, 1900","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6d078228-3117-8040-7437-8f80a6065d07"],["Death of daughter Neeltje, Warga 1934","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:03b3dfde-5f83-5659-f2fa-8964b467f4d2"],["Death of daughter Geertje, 1952","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a5ecc14-0dae-11e5-b1d9-13b7ac6b886d"],["Death of sister Grietje Ulbes Visser, Oldeboorn 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:18d23351-4fdf-8e06-9701-a54c062e72b5"]]},
taekeb:{"n":"Taeke Berends IJsselstein","d":"1795–1842 · Wartena","b":1795,"x":1842,"s":"h","p":["beendouwes","gertjet"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 6 December 1795 at Wartena according to the death-duties file made after his death, the son of Been (Berend) Douwes IJsselstein, a labourer, and Gertje Taekes, whose father's name he carried. When his father registered the surname IJsselstein at Warga in 1811, Taeke was 17, with an elder brother Douwe and younger sisters Hinke and Sjoerdtje. The registers call him both Taeke Beens and Taeke Berends. The ages given for him in 1811 (17), at his marriage (23) and at his death (47) point rather to December 1794, and his marriage record gives his birthplace as Warga.\n\nOn 27 May 1818, aged 23, he married Antje Lieuwes Kooystra of Eernewoude in a double wedding: on the same day his brother Douwe married her sister Neeltje. Their first son, Berend, was born on 22 May 1819 at Hempens, near Leeuwarden; then the couple settled at Wartena, where Lieuwe (30 July 1821), Douwe (11 October 1823) and Gertje (23 July 1826) were born. In 1826 he was a labourer (arbeider) at Wartena, aged 31.\n\nHe died at Wartena on 18 February 1842, aged 47, leaving his wife and four children. The death-duties file states that he left no real estate and attaches a certificate of poverty (certificaat van onvermogen) from the municipality. His sons Berend and Douwe were labourers like him; Lieuwe became a farmer.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Death record, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3a019666-d715-560a-33a2-add45fefa56e"],["Death duties file, 1842 (born 6 Dec 1795)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7682c829-9476-4c74-a246-f58d044c9b2c"],["Surname registration, Berent Douwes IJsselstein, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50ebbb84-5af7-4c72-a968-97a74329c684"],["Marriage of brother Douwe, same day, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a87e78f8-185a-baaf-5047-f5415ea7b906"],["Birth of Berend, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69de8030-a226-7ea7-5643-dece95d6252e"],["Birth of Lieuwe, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06a04f1d-94a6-fecc-95a9-d8910ae10665"],["Birth of Douwe, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f148b4b8-3d0a-8c8c-b78b-c389d94806b8"],["Birth of Gertje, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f06687cd-460b-c764-f32b-80c992444d01"],["Death duties file of his widow, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:27889a09-af85-43b7-aa62-06db43b26a5d"]]},
antjelk:{"n":"Antje Lieuwes Kooystra","d":"1794–1857 · Eernewoude","s":"h","p":["lieuwek","akkea"],"r":["nl"],"b":1794,"t":"Born at Eernewoude in Tietjerksteradeel on 7 July 1794 and baptised at Garijp on 23 November, the third child of Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra, a labourer, and Akke Aukes. In 1811 she was living at Grouw with her elder sister Jetske.\n\nOn 27 May 1818, aged 23, she married Taeke Beens IJsselstein of Warga, on the same day that her sister Neeltje married his brother Douwe. Her children were Berend (born at Hempens in 1819), Lieuwe (1821), Douwe (1823) and Gertje (1826), the last three at Wartena. She was widowed at Wartena on 18 February 1842, when her husband left no property and the family was certified as being without means.\n\nShe lived on at Wartena for fifteen years and died there on 24 October 1857, aged 63. By then she owned some real estate, which made a death-duties file necessary; it names her heirs as Berend, a labourer, Lieuwe, a farmer (landbouwer), Douwe, a labourer, and Geertje, wife of Foppe Klazes Veenema of Workum. Her name is spelled both Kooystra and Kooistra.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Baptism, Garijp 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:642ee3d2-9a81-4aa5-a84e-7d61fdc4da6e"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3159663b-a535-49d9-a237-7fb8a6521a1d"],["Birth of Berend, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69de8030-a226-7ea7-5643-dece95d6252e"],["Birth of Gertje, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f06687cd-460b-c764-f32b-80c992444d01"],["Death record, Wartena 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a0952abd-3e23-7f66-e689-147881d06c1c"],["Death duties file, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:27889a09-af85-43b7-aa62-06db43b26a5d"],["Death duties file of Taeke Berends IJsselstein, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7682c829-9476-4c74-a246-f58d044c9b2c"]],"x":1857},
ulbe:{"n":"Ulbe Berends Visser","d":"1791–1879 · Wartena","b":1791,"x":1879,"s":"h","p":["berende","grietjes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wartena on 7 January 1791, the son of Berend (Been) Eeltjes Visser and Grietje Sipkes. His father, a ship's carpenter's journeyman, registered the surname Visser at Wartena in 1811, listing Ulbe (20), his elder brother Sipke and his sister Aaltje.\n\nOn 25 July 1812, aged 21, he married Neeltje Durks de Jong, daughter of the former village magistrate of Eernewoude; the clerk wrote his name Ulbe Beernts Visscher. Their first child, Antje, had been born on 31 May, before the wedding, and died at Wartena on 1 September 1812. He followed his father's trade and is called a ship's carpenter's journeyman (scheepstimmerknecht) in records of 1812, 1830 and 1858: a skilled hand in a boatyard, building and repairing the wooden barges and boats that carried the trade of the Frisian lake villages. The children who grew up were Grietje (about 1814), who married the skipper Rients Ages Hempenius, Durk (1816) and Froukje (1823). A son Sipke died in 1830 at three months.\n\nThe family lived at Wartena for good. Neeltje died there on 11 August 1858, and Ulbe on 30 March 1879, aged 88, outliving her by more than twenty years.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c5d26f9d-c8a8-509c-1dcb-d41330849fa9"],["Death record, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4d45842d-e307-b6c1-095a-8ea97f8e24b4"],["Population register, Wartena (born 7 Jan 1791)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:325324cb-b8bd-6968-70f9-c4baaa1dd316"],["Surname registration, Been Eeltjes Visser, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f4848cd0-55eb-44f1-a5c6-c79f8b1a3d66"],["Birth of Antje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:321d32e9-d8a7-3551-d7a2-f61709b8fdc0"],["Death of Antje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:83f4fe85-405b-4eb6-3c37-eb716b990388"],["Birth of Durk, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a6becd0-bba8-b7ce-7287-c2335e5cc67d"],["Death of Sipke, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:159ae3e6-676b-50b6-7658-68b12d176724"],["Death of Neeltje, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c877821-471a-adad-6992-6b640916b707"],["Death of daughter Grietje, Oldeboorn 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:18d23351-4fdf-8e06-9701-a54c062e72b5"]]},
neeltje:{"n":"Neeltje Durks de Jong","d":"1791–1858 · Eernewoude","s":"h","p":["durkfdj","antjero"],"r":["nl"],"b":1791,"x":1858,"t":"Born at Eernewoude on 27 May 1791, the daughter of Durk Folkerts de Jong, village magistrate (dorprechter) of Eernewoude and later a fisherman and peat master, and Antje Roelofs. She was one of nine children, three of whom died young.\n\nOn 25 July 1812, both aged 21, she married Ulbe Beernts Visscher (Ulbe Berends Visser), a ship's carpenter's journeyman of Wartena. Their first daughter, Antje, born that May, died in September 1812. Grietje followed about 1814, Durk (named after her father) in 1816 and Froukje in 1823; a son Sipke died in 1830 at three months. When her father died in 1838, the death-duties file named her among his heirs as the wife of Ulbe Berends Visser, ship's carpenter at Wartena.\n\nThey lived at Wartena for the rest of their lives, and she died there on 11 August 1858, aged 67. Her daughter Froukje's daughter Rinske was born the year before.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c5d26f9d-c8a8-509c-1dcb-d41330849fa9"],["Death record, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c877821-471a-adad-6992-6b640916b707"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Dirk Folkerts de Jong (her birth date)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:90585bdd-0384-4351-b34c-06d9462c2521"],["Birth of Antje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:321d32e9-d8a7-3551-d7a2-f61709b8fdc0"],["Birth of Durk, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a6becd0-bba8-b7ce-7287-c2335e5cc67d"],["Death duties file of Durk Folkerts de Jong, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d14ed81-35ae-4734-a61f-e0db0fe54d51"]]},
berende:{"n":"Berend Eeltjes Visser","d":"c. 1755–1818 · Wartena","s":"h","p":["hzeeltjebeens","hzhinkesjoerds"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1755, the son of Eeltje Beens (Beerents) and Hinke Sjoerds; his elder brother Sjoerd was a fisherman (visser) at Wartena. Berend, usually called Been or Beernt, married Grietje Sipkes at Wartena on 22 October 1780; both lived in the village.\n\nHe was a ship's carpenter's journeyman (scheepstimmerknecht). In 1811 he registered the surname Visser at Wartena, listing his children Sipke (25, at Warstiens), Aaltje (23) and Ulbe (20), and a granddaughter Grietje, aged five. Ulbe learned his father's trade. His son Eeltje, the father of that granddaughter, died young.\n\nGrietje died before him, and at his death he was married to Akke Ludzes, a widow born in 1748. He died at Wartena on 20 February 1818, aged 63. His brother Sjoerd died two days later. The death-duties file shows that he left no real estate and names his heirs: Sipke, a labourer at Warga; Ulbe, ship's carpenter's journeyman; Aaltje, a farm maid (boerenmeid), who married that December; and Grietje, daughter of the late Eeltje, a maid at Bergum.","b":1755,"x":1818,"src":[["Marriage, Wartena 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ca3f5578-c74d-4fb6-89bf-10278f8d32d9"],["Surname registration, Wartena 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f4848cd0-55eb-44f1-a5c6-c79f8b1a3d66"],["Death record, Wartena 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25bd7764-e82a-54e4-edf9-cd69dbaf31d4"],["Death duties file, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df4204b2-96d6-4dfb-ab9e-21e784e41087"],["Death of brother Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e57d70fc-79cb-d196-b0fb-72ff37787e76"],["Marriage of son Sipke, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3d047e2-9813-f14e-8c2b-ed99f451e0ec"],["Marriage of daughter Aaltje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e30bd87-8d93-816b-e1f4-0a3a6c5f9c4a"],["Church membership of his widow Akke Ludsers","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7652ce0a-cb93-465b-bf4e-0b7fbcbaa4b0"]]},
grietjes:{"n":"Grietje Sipkes","d":"m. 1780 · Wartena","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Wartena, in Idaarderadeel. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Sipke, and she gave that name to her eldest son. On 22 October 1780 she married Berend (Been) Eeltjes, a ship's carpenter's journeyman, at Wartena; both were living in the village.\n\nTheir children were Sipke (born about 1786), who became a labourer at Warga; Aaltje (about 1788), a farm maid who married Pieter Rintjes Terpstra in 1818; Ulbe (born 7 January 1791), a ship's carpenter like his father; and Eeltje, who died young, leaving a daughter Grietje, named after her. In 1811 the family took the surname Visser. Grietje died before February 1818, when her husband died married to a second wife; she is named as the mother at the weddings of Sipke and Ulbe in 1812 and of Aaltje in 1818.","src":[["Marriage, Wartena 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ca3f5578-c74d-4fb6-89bf-10278f8d32d9"],["Marriage of son Sipke, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3d047e2-9813-f14e-8c2b-ed99f451e0ec"],["Marriage of son Ulbe, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c5d26f9d-c8a8-509c-1dcb-d41330849fa9"],["Marriage of daughter Aaltje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e30bd87-8d93-816b-e1f4-0a3a6c5f9c4a"],["Death duties file of Been Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df4204b2-96d6-4dfb-ab9e-21e784e41087"]]},
johannesje:{"n":"Johannes Jans Elzinga","d":"b. 1852 · Irnsum","b":1852,"s":"h","p":["janje","jantje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Irnsum on 29 July 1852 (one population register says 24 July), the son of Jan Johannes Elzinga, a labourer, and Jantje Eelkes Roukema. He was given the name of a brother who had died six months earlier, on 28 January 1852. His mother died in 1865, when he was twelve, and his father married again the next year.\n\nOn 13 May 1875, aged 22, he married Theodora Joukes Stok of Grouw, who was 25. They had eight children: Jantje (1875) and Jan (1876) at Grouw, Berber (1878) at Sneek, Limke (1880) in Utingeradeel, and Bontje (1882), Joukje (1886), Trijntje (1888) and Wiemer (1892) at Irnsum. He was a labourer (arbeider) when Limke, a servant, married at Enschede in 1904.\n\nA Johannes Jans Elzinga, farmer (boer) at Irnsum, very probably he, made a will before a Leeuwarden notary in 1916 and is named in an Irnsum mortgage in 1929. A family card for Johannes and Theodora also survives in the population register of The Hague, where their eldest daughter Jantje, 23, married Frans Pina, a labourer, on 10 May 1899; Johannes was still described as a labourer. Jantje's own family card at The Hague shows that two of her daughters were born at Dortmund in Germany, so she too spent some years in the Ruhr, as her brother Jan did a few years later. Theodora died at Wieuwerd in 1936. His own death record has not been found.\n\nOf his daughters, Berber died at Grouw in 1965 aged 86, Limke at Dordrecht in 1968 aged 87, and Trijntje at Arnhem in 1972.","src":[["Marriage record, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e01687-b670-88ed-293d-2a333d60f076"],["Death of Bontje Elzinga, 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0019dc5b-6229-4449-9d20-1560ab55baaa"],["Marriage of Wiemer Elzinga, 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f300d9ca-25c8-eeb4-dd51-a3ecb18ffdb7"],["Population register, Irnsum (born 29 July 1852)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:10425c69-5de8-ac25-c93a-5b584fa8bc57"],["Estate file of his brother Johannes, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:62e98d93-eaf0-462b-b67f-7a717f86c3d1"],["Birth of Berber, Sneek, 1878","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8887e183-ab43-6b22-d880-58f2adaa6298"],["Birth of Limke, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:499305b1-cf87-3a10-2553-304955363103"],["Birth of Bontje, 1882","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a34896ad-5bc6-5064-e1de-8d0b4564684d"],["Birth of Wiemer, 1892","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:35969281-4fde-8f54-dfd5-6be441ec1652"],["Marriage of Limke, Enschede, 1904","https://www.openarchieven.nl/ens:006EBCFF-578E-5B8F-E054-002128F9EB56"],["Will, notary at Leeuwarden, 1916","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e9b575ad-20b1-4300-a5e6-98f8c96aa962"],["Mortgage, Grouw notary, 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8905bd82-80ea-24b8-3910-e0edd856966c"],["Family card, The Hague population register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:5BC89C35-C6A7-40AB-A808-B2E285D553F8"],["Marriage of daughter Jantje, The Hague, 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:35A92FAE-5790-4C9D-9525-4E7C5701908F"],["Family card of Frans Pina and Jantje Elzinga, The Hague","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:E69C243E-FBCD-4CEA-8AFD-B41E899848D6"],["Death of daughter Limke, Dordrecht, 1968","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:01CC60B7-0BC3-420E-B4A8-C100BE38FFA7"],["Death of daughter Trijntje, Arnhem, 1972","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:52D4EB72-493E-435E-A52F-6719B9F5CC46"],["Death of daughter Berber, Grouw, 1965","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:82e935f4-de5f-0c89-f57b-a88c06fbf5b0"]]},
theodora:{"n":"Theodora Joukes Stok","d":"1850–1936 · Grouw","b":1850,"s":"h","p":["jouke","berber"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Grouw on 12 April 1850, the eldest child of Jouke Klazes Stok, a skipper's mate, and Berber Hendriks Jonker, a seamstress. She was named after her father's mother, Theodora (Dora) Beyerman, who had died eight months before.\n\nThree of her five brothers and sisters died small. Her father died in October 1864, when she was 14, and her mother in July 1870, leaving her at 20 with a brother Klaas, 15, and a sister Limke, 9.\n\nOn 13 May 1875, aged 25, she married Johannes Jans Elzinga, a labourer from Irnsum three years her junior. Their first child, Jantje, was born on 6 June 1875, and seven more followed up to 1892: Jan, Berber, Limke, Bontje, Joukje, Trijntje and Wiemer. All eight grew up and married.\n\nShe died at Wieuwerd in Baarderadeel on 4 October 1936, aged 86. Her daughter Joukje also died in Baarderadeel, in 1946.","x":1936,"src":[["Birth record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d05379e7-35d2-4a99-6608-ad36814237d5"],["Marriage record, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e01687-b670-88ed-293d-2a333d60f076"],["Death record, Wieuwerd, 1936","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f8f1358-dc13-cbbd-e3ce-bf6116bf1868"],["Death of daughter Joukje, 1946","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:73896b80-c88d-4496-8cfa-bf4fc681f2d9"]]},
janje:{"n":"Jan Johannes Elzinga","d":"1820–1896 · Wirdum","b":1820,"x":1896,"s":"h","p":["johannesjans","trijntje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wirdum on 13 March 1820, the eldest known child of Johannes Jans Elzinga, a labourer, and Trijntje Oeges Tuinstra. He grew up at Idaard and was a farmhand (boerenknecht) at Grouw when, on 11 May 1845, he married Jantje Eelkes Roukema, a skipper's daughter from Gorredijk who was in service at Irnsum. Both were 25.\n\nThey settled at Irnsum, a village on the river Boorne between Grouw and Akkrum, where Jan worked as a labourer for the rest of his life. Both joined the Mennonite congregation of Irnsum and Poppingawier, being baptised as adults on confession of faith: Jantje on 18 April 1847 and Jan on 25 March 1849.\n\nTheir known children were Bontje (about 1848), Eelke, a first Johannes who died on 28 January 1852, a second Johannes (1852), Trijntje, who lived one week in September 1859, and Oege, a farmhand who died on 15 July 1881 aged 20. Bontje married a sailmaker's man at Grouw in 1871.\n\nJantje died on 12 June 1865. On 20 December 1866 Jan married Gepke Lolkes Ongeluk, 46, born at Sloten. He died at Irnsum on 20 December 1896, aged 76, thirty years to the day after that second wedding.","src":[["Marriage record, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51d72b4e-3811-7456-b5be-5e39e478fda7"],["Population register (born 13 March 1820)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:02f9d73e-895a-80e1-df5a-9c00d5accea5"],["Mennonite membership register, Irnsum (his baptism, 1849)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:695e8401-0699-4080-86ff-c4f03b7c7b5a"],["Mennonite membership register, Irnsum (her baptism, 1847)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:418931a0-7b51-4740-99a1-fdbc552f6312"],["Death of daughter Trijntje, 1859","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0171995b-9b6e-29f9-6f45-cb4e92dff255"],["Marriage of daughter Bontje, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:41e7830d-58be-74cb-eab1-133fef2f2704"],["Death of son Oege, 1881","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a0dad48d-f623-f4ac-88ca-610f082ba081"],["Second marriage, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f8606e9-ba5e-5dab-8334-c5db99ec4f30"],["Death record, 1896","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5ea0c06b-35b1-10ca-2ce3-33404385528d"]]},
jantje:{"n":"Jantje Eelkes Roukema","d":"c. 1820–1865 · Gorredijk","b":1820,"s":"h","p":["eelke","bontje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Gorredijk about 1820, the daughter of Eelke Wymers Roukema, a peat skipper, and Bontje Harkes Landmeter. Of the six children her parents had, four grew up: Grietje, Jantje, Wiemer and Kornelia.\n\nLike her sisters she went into service at Irnsum, some forty kilometres from home. Grietje married the Irnsum postman there in 1841, and Jantje was a maidservant (dienstmeid) in the village when she married Jan Johannes Elzinga, a farmhand, on 11 May 1845. On 18 April 1847 she was baptised on confession of faith in the Mennonite congregation of Irnsum and Poppingawier, the church her parents belonged to at Gorredijk.\n\nHer father died aboard his ship in January 1848, and her widowed mother later came to live at Irnsum. Jantje had at least six children, of whom the first Johannes (died 1852) and Trijntje (1859) died as babies; the second Johannes, born in 1852, carried the line on.\n\nShe died at Irnsum on 12 June 1865, aged 45, a year after her mother.","x":1865,"src":[["Marriage record, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51d72b4e-3811-7456-b5be-5e39e478fda7"],["Mennonite membership register, Irnsum, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:418931a0-7b51-4740-99a1-fdbc552f6312"],["Estate file of her father, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58462c57-e8cd-454d-863f-5ef8b15f9d42"],["Death record, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0e2b1ced-bdf0-e9ab-cb04-fa02b89f6420"]]},
johannesjans:{"n":"Johannes Jans Elzinga","d":"1791–1847 · Bozum","s":"h","p":["jansikkes","pietjel"],"r":["nl"],"b":1791,"t":"Born at Bozum on 12 December 1791 and baptised there on 8 January 1792, the son of Jan Sikkes and Pietje Lucas. His father died when he was about eighteen.\n\nIn 1811 he was a labourer living at Wytgaard, and was drawn (lot 14) in that year's conscription for Napoleon's army in the canton of Wirdum. Friesland had been part of the French Empire since 1810, and its young men were called up by lot. On 7 December 1812 he was enrolled as soldier no. 5093 in the 125th regiment of line infantry, one of the regiments formed from the old Dutch army, and on 12 March 1813 he passed to the 134th regiment.\n\nHe came home. On 20 May 1815, a farmhand (boerenknecht) at Roordahuizum, he married Trijntje Oeges Tuinstra. The family used the surname Elsinga or Elzinga. Jan was born at Wirdum in 1820; then, at Idaard, came Aukje (about 1823), Geertje (about 1826), Hylke (1827), Tjerk (about 1831), Pietje (1834) and Jeltje (1837).\n\nHe was a labourer at Idaard when Jan married in 1845, and died there on 11 March 1847, aged 55. The estate declaration notes that he left no property and was without means.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Baptism, Bozum, 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3c537225-2d22-4a59-84a2-aed6b0d8bbe1"],["Frisian soldiers under Napoleon, index","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:41a8b706-4d04-4f45-b0cf-e236eccc8642"],["Birth of son Hylke, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3df0dadf-9d95-c0dd-484e-7c6cda556936"],["Birth of daughter Pietje, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b2c78e35-d462-b0db-2b5a-758a2b63520c"],["Birth of daughter Jeltje, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:10c876af-b21a-bfd8-3fd6-e2dd034c6b24"],["Marriage of son Jan, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51d72b4e-3811-7456-b5be-5e39e478fda7"],["Estate declaration (memorie van successie), 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:46965e76-b40e-4f43-802d-9827e01d0163"]],"x":1847},
trijntje:{"n":"Trijntje Oeges Tuinstra","d":"1792–1863 · Roordahuizum","s":"h","p":["oege","aukjejac"],"r":["nl"],"b":1792,"x":1863,"t":"Born at Roordahuizum on 27 June 1792 and baptised there on 15 July, the second child of Oege Tjerks, a labourer, and Aukje Jacobs. When her father registered the surname Tuinstra in 1811 she was 19 and living at Roordahuizum.\n\nShe was a farm maid (boerenmeid) at Akkrum when she married Johannes Jans Elsinga on 20 May 1815, aged 22. They lived first at Wirdum and then at Idaard, where she too worked as a labourer (arbeidster). They had at least seven children.\n\nJohannes died in 1847. Three of her daughters married into the Dijkstra family of Deinum and settled at Huizum, by Leeuwarden. Aukje married Doede Klazes Dijkstra in 1850 and died in 1856, aged 33; later that year Doede married her youngest sister, Jeltje, who was 19. Pietje married his brother Wytze in 1858. Trijntje spent her last years at Huizum. Jeltje died there on 29 April 1863, and Trijntje herself a month later, on 31 May 1863, aged 70.\n\nHer sons lived long: Tjerk died at Tijnje in 1918 aged 87 and Hylke at Grouw in 1920 aged 92.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Death record, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9faa0fdc-ee20-25c9-95c9-429be4a73d5d"],["Baptism, Roordahuizum, 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3003f77e-2a6a-4590-9a53-6477bd68f032"],["Surname registration of her father, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4f457b39-f35f-45a0-839c-2b63f68cbeb3"],["Marriage of daughter Aukje, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2106f6be-0a1d-ba88-7952-8c7a767322de"],["Death of daughter Aukje, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8a11347a-eba4-653f-f805-68979f02b749"],["Marriage of daughter Jeltje, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c1bf115-59ba-5146-adfa-241ec1747d68"],["Marriage of daughter Pietje, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a8f64f7-9557-8c29-7a77-8825b52916cc"],["Death of daughter Jeltje, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c51810c3-5b5c-99e2-f6da-ca2fe9832ea9"],["Death of son Tjerk, 1918","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:38c2672a-4476-0e40-7693-2f534164ffcb"],["Death of son Hylke, 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:de76885d-3c3c-2e49-97e4-26601afd12a8"]]},
eelke:{"n":"Eelke Wymers Roukema","d":"c. 1786–1848 · Gorredijk","s":"h","p":["wymer","jantjepi"],"r":["nl"],"b":1786,"t":"Born at Gorredijk in Opsterland about 1786, the eldest known child of Wymer Eelkes Roukema and Jantje Pieters. When his father registered the surname Roukema in 1811, Eelke was 25 and still at home; his sisters were Lysbeth, Rinske and Jankjen (Joukjen).\n\nGorredijk grew up along a canal dug in the 1600s to carry peat out of the bogs of Opsterland, and the village lived by the turf trade. Eelke spent his life on that water as a skipper (schipper), carrying turf to the towns, and his death record calls him a peat skipper (turfschipper). On 10 May 1817, aged 30, he married Bontje Harkes Landmeter of Lippenhuizen. On 7 February 1818 the couple were baptised together on confession of faith in the Mennonite congregation of Gorredijk and Lippenhuizen.\n\nThey had six children: Grietje (about 1818), Jantje (about 1820), Wiemer (15 September 1821), Harke (who lived only 13 days in February 1823), Kornelia (11 November 1824) and Rinske (born 2 October 1827, died at six months). In 1827 he was a skipper's mate (schippersknecht), working another man's boat.\n\nHe died on 9 January 1848, aged 62, aboard his ship at Makkum on the Zuiderzee coast, far from home. The death-duty return lists four grown children and no real estate: Grietje, wife of the Irnsum postman Gerrit Aukes Boonstra; Jantje, wife of Jan Johannes Elzinga, a labourer at Irnsum; Wiemer, a skipper's hand; and Kornelia, a maidservant at Irnsum. Wiemer stayed a skipper all his life and died at Sneek in 1894.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Surname registration of his father, Gorredijk, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d10dc94e-e95f-4d9b-b4ea-cb08bd7008f7"],["Mennonite membership register, Opsterland, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7b529b94-5474-4841-80d5-aa72246b6edc"],["Birth of son Wiemer, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:adf0061d-7055-9e78-e405-f2c2ad806747"],["Death of son Harke, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:38d0d7e5-8445-1949-254f-0433d75891de"],["Birth of daughter Kornelia, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d2c5ded0-d0f9-ad47-d49c-5c55e770847d"],["Death of daughter Rinske, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:eaea7840-956c-3a06-1f86-86679a3d0483"],["Death record, Makkum, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1614136-d23f-68a9-1261-24c5524882cd"],["Death-duty file, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58462c57-e8cd-454d-863f-5ef8b15f9d42"],["Death of son Wiemer, Sneek, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:35dabf0c-a8c7-6695-9947-66bde6cc106f"]],"x":1848},
bontje:{"n":"Bontje Harkes Landmeter","d":"c. 1795–1864 · Lippenhuizen","s":"h","p":["harkel","grietjesy"],"r":["nl"],"b":1795,"x":1864,"t":"Born at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland about 1795, the daughter of Harke Kornelis Landmeter, a skipper's mate, and his first wife Grietje Sytzes. Her elder brother Kornelis was born about 1790. Her mother died while she was a child, and her father married again, to Antje Hessels van der Woude, so she grew up with half-brothers and half-sisters: Jan, Sytze, Sytske and Mettje.\n\nOn 10 May 1817, aged 22, she married Eelke Wymers Roukema, a skipper of Gorredijk. The next February they were baptised together in the Mennonite congregation of Gorredijk and Lippenhuizen, the church in which her parents had also been baptised. In 1845 she was called a skipper's wife (schipperse), which in the turf trade often meant a wife who lived and worked on the boat with her husband.\n\nShe had six children: Grietje (about 1818), Jantje (about 1820), Wiemer (1821), Harke (1823, who lived 13 days), Kornelia (1824) and Rinske (1827, who died at six months). Eelke died aboard his ship at Makkum in January 1848.\n\nHer daughters Grietje, Jantje and Kornelia all went into service at Irnsum, and as a widow Bontje followed them there. Grietje died in 1862 and Kornelia at Franeker in 1856. Bontje died at Irnsum on 18 March 1864, aged 69, a year before her daughter Jantje.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Death record, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d439f12-8ae0-9e41-7276-ba9284f07190"],["Marriage of daughter Jantje, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51d72b4e-3811-7456-b5be-5e39e478fda7"],["Mennonite membership register, Opsterland, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7b529b94-5474-4841-80d5-aa72246b6edc"],["Death of her father, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e80636e-922a-bc2e-6e3a-29d4e7b694a1"],["Marriage of daughter Grietje, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb4a17db-e2a5-fdcb-c104-48370a161a09"],["Death of daughter Grietje, Irnsum, 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:766085c3-3cb2-68c4-953c-0493ef86fa68"],["Death of daughter Kornelia, Franeker, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9eda046d-2bfc-eb49-39ab-256d8a1d4496"],["Death-duty file of her husband, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58462c57-e8cd-454d-863f-5ef8b15f9d42"]]},
jouke:{"n":"Jouke Klazes Stok","d":"1824–1864 · Grouw","b":1824,"x":1864,"s":"h","p":["klaass","dora"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Grouw on 14 May 1824, the son of Klaas Sjoerds Stok, a workman, and Dorothea (Dora) Beyerman. He was the sixth of their seven children; his elder brothers and sisters were Beitske, Sjoerd, Grietje, Abe and Andrieske, and Tjitske was born after him.\n\nThe late 1840s were hard years for the family. His brother Sjoerd, a skipper, died in 1846; his brother Abe, a baker's hand, and his sister Beitske died in 1848; his father died in January 1849 and his mother that August, and his sister Tjitske the following year.\n\nIn May 1849 he was a skipper's mate (schippersknecht) at Grouw. A notary drew up the consent for his marriage on 9 May, and on 31 May 1849, aged 25, he married Berber Hendriks Jonker, 24, a seamstress (naaister) of Grouw. They had six children: Theodora (12 April 1850), Limke (1852, who died aged four in 1857), Klaas (1855), Hendrik (born December 1857, died at three months), a second Limke (1858) and a third Limke (3 September 1860), who lived to 79. The name was for Berber's mother, Lemke Jans. By 1857 Jouke was working as a labourer (werkman).\n\nIn April 1861 he and Jetze Poppes van der Sluis of Langweer sold property together before a Leeuwarden notary. He died in Idaarderadeel, the district of Grouw, on 18 October 1864, aged 40, leaving Berber with Theodora, Klaas and the youngest Limke. Klaas became a miller's hand at Leeuwarden and died there in 1907.","src":[["Marriage record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5a083dc0-7882-a88e-2afa-6341ea982343"],["Birth record, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06faf589-9fdc-7ce4-83bb-4536baa6f373"],["Notarial consent to marry, 9 May 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:20cb242a-494c-420f-a347-d895717f66ad"],["Birth of daughter Theodora, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d05379e7-35d2-4a99-6608-ad36814237d5"],["Death of daughter Limke, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:080c75d3-23d6-dde6-8649-57ecce2765ef"],["Birth of son Klaas, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0ad1af80-2983-2360-b60a-34ed5d4aab34"],["Death of son Hendrik, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7d8c9d61-7fab-5b5d-224e-450b504c321d"],["Birth of daughter Limke, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f0e6b98a-c3fd-2df3-c078-d9d8268d575d"],["Notarial sale, Leeuwarden, 4 April 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0adb940d-d253-4b7d-8efc-150e71547378"],["Death record, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bf6d4472-4cf6-8718-4718-b25fdf4691cc"],["Death of son Klaas, Leeuwarden, 1907","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:95919dde-185b-947c-0473-904b24c06175"]]},
berber:{"n":"Berber Hendriks Jonker","d":"1824–1870 · Grouw","b":1824,"x":1870,"s":"h","p":["hendrikdj","lemke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Grouw on 26 September 1824, the daughter of Hendrik Durks Jonker, a labourer, and Lemke Jans. She was the sixth of their seven children: Durk (1811), Jan (1814), Lijsbert (1816), Wytske (1819), Sytze (1821), Berber and Tjerkje (1826). She also had an older half-brother, Klaas Klazes Postma, from her mother's first union. Her father died in 1841 at Hoogemeeden in Groningen, when she was 16.\n\nShe became a seamstress (naaister) at Grouw. On 31 May 1849, aged 24, she married Jouke Klazes Stok, a skipper's mate, after a notary had drawn up the consent to the marriage on 9 May.\n\nThey had six children: Theodora (1850), Limke (1852-1857), Klaas (1855), Hendrik (1857-1858), Limke (1858) and Limke (1860). Three were named after her mother; only the youngest of them grew up. Jouke died in October 1864, when the surviving children were 14, 9 and 4.\n\nBerber died in Idaarderadeel, the district of Grouw, on 7 July 1870, aged 45. Her mother outlived her by five years. Her daughter Theodora married Johannes Jans Elzinga in 1875, and her son Klaas became a miller's hand at Leeuwarden.","src":[["Birth record, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1cbdbe8-be1e-9f0a-a3f4-e0992d26879f"],["Notarial consent to marry, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:20cb242a-494c-420f-a347-d895717f66ad"],["Marriage record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5a083dc0-7882-a88e-2afa-6341ea982343"],["Death of her husband, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bf6d4472-4cf6-8718-4718-b25fdf4691cc"],["Death record, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0019b32c-c4bb-0722-9bd7-c16bbdcb7e26"],["Death of her mother, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:373592a0-a262-376a-874d-8b3c67faf5b3"],["Marriage of son Klaas, Leeuwarden, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:55606b7d-30f8-a9a5-5be0-c8593fcbf194"]]},
klaass:{"n":"Klaas Sjoerds Stok","d":"c. 1777–1849 · Grouw","b":1777,"x":1849,"s":"h","p":["sjoerdstok","tjitskev"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Grouw about 1777, the eldest known child of Sjoerd Abes Stok, a labourer, and Grietje Sjoerds. His death record of 1849 calls his mother Tjitske Sjoerds Visser, but every other record names her Grietje Sjoerds; Visser was the surname taken by her brother Jacob, a fisherman.\n\nOn 27 July 1806 he married Dorothea Tjitske Louisa Beyerman, a painter's daughter from Ritsumazijl by Deinum, west of Leeuwarden, in the church at Deinum, after the banns had been read at Grouw. When his father registered the surname Stok at Grouw in 1811, Klaas, 34, and his first two children, Beitske, 4, and Sjoerd, 1, were entered in his father's household.\n\nKlaas and Dora had seven children: Beitske (1808), Sjoerd (1810), Grietje (1813), Abe (1818), Andrieske (1821), Jouke (1824) and Tjitske (1826). Two daughters carried the names of Dora's mother Beitske Andries.\n\nHe was a workman (werkman) all his life. His last years brought one loss after another: Sjoerd, a skipper, died in 1846, and Abe, a baker's hand, and Beitske, a labourer at Warga, in 1848. Klaas died at Grouw on 30 January 1849, aged 71. Dora followed him in August, and their daughter Tjitske, a seamstress, in May 1850. Grietje married and lived until 1878, dying at Marssum, and Andrieske died at Harlingen in 1858.","src":[["Death record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3181a2c3-2316-1552-eb1a-01f340cf14ac"],["Marriage, Deinum, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c04e9df8-b6ff-450f-a6a4-114f6e9f3e9e"],["Banns, Grouw, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:473b7578-e1ec-f381-7d3e-0d8329c13282"],["Surname registration of his father, Grouw, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f6f1d8e1-cd17-48a5-aba9-23bd3e3d4a4c"],["Baptism of daughter Beitske, 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e20f0d75-2aef-4e98-84f5-21b3f3236892"],["Baptism of son Sjoerd, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7603a54e-14c0-4c92-b94b-14b8b240067c"],["Birth of daughter Grietje, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3255dcef-5cf2-b318-1a21-319a55b4d3ba"],["Birth of son Abe, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:088afccb-8b1a-a94c-0738-fbadd9a6ff1f"],["Birth of daughter Andrieske, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:32974984-12fc-ff47-26da-4b4edb6d7b73"],["Birth of daughter Tjitske, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8c073ead-b297-e19c-db87-b6f012b96dc4"],["Death of son Sjoerd, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:34c3fd4c-b522-9103-70af-94659866d60a"],["Death of son Abe, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5eaa1411-a937-f9ac-6f51-1e16a7b37c9b"],["Death of daughter Beitske, Warga, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:32919975-9f2e-0945-0dfd-887df7cc9538"],["Death of daughter Tjitske, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:817e54fd-db3e-8983-e08d-7b174f7b855d"],["Death of daughter Grietje, Marssum, 1878","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fce59807-7af6-d5fe-f902-32600c06c869"]]},
dora:{"n":"Dorothea Tjitske Louisa Beyerman","d":"1788–1849 · Leeuwarden","s":"h","p":["ezbeyerman","ezbeitskeandries"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Dorothea Tjitske Louisa Beyerman, called Dora, was born on 6 September 1788 and baptised at Leeuwarden on 5 October (the register writes Dorothea Tietske Louisa), the daughter of Georg Lodewijk Beyerman, a house painter and glazier (verver en glazenmaker) at Deinum near Leeuwarden, and Beitske Andries, who had married in 1786. Her long string of names, unusual in a Frisian village, matches her father's German-sounding ones. Her sisters Grietje (1791) and Beitske (1798) were also baptised at Leeuwarden, and Andrieske was born in 1795. Her father had an earlier wife, Grietje Jans, who died in 1785.\n\nOn 27 July 1806 she married Klaas Sjoerds of Grouw in the church at Deinum; she was then living at Ritsumazijl, a hamlet by Deinum. They settled at Grouw, where Klaas was a workman and his family took the name Stok in 1811. Their seven children were Beitske (1808), Sjoerd (1810), Grietje (1813), Abe (1818), Andrieske (1821), Jouke (1824) and Tjitske (1826); the names Beitske and Andrieske came from her mother and her mother's father.\n\nHer death record names her parents as Lodewijk Jacobs Beyermans and Beitske Tjitzes Zondervan. Her baptism leaves no doubt that her parents were Georg Lodewijk Beyerman and Beitske Andries; the mother's name in the death record is simply wrong.\n\nShe lost her son Sjoerd in 1846, Abe and Beitske in 1848 and her husband in January 1849. She died at Grouw on 24 August 1849, aged 61, a shopkeeper (winkelierster).","b":1788,"x":1849,"src":[["Death record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e4b4c569-3fd2-14e0-6998-af70577d976b"],["Marriage, Deinum, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c04e9df8-b6ff-450f-a6a4-114f6e9f3e9e"],["Banns, Grouw, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:473b7578-e1ec-f381-7d3e-0d8329c13282"],["Marriage of her parents, Deinum, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69e0106e-366c-4c6e-ac74-4fbd8a073e7e"],["Death of her father, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5cde8b1a-362b-0ddb-7940-b71c089fce1e"],["Death of sister Andrieske, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e51997c-98d3-d25a-5736-6a1ed4d2d6aa"],["Death of her husband, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3181a2c3-2316-1552-eb1a-01f340cf14ac"],["Baptism, Leeuwarden, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3c5c904e-41a0-4f54-abdd-6e7059baed4c"],["Baptism of sister Grietje, 1791","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4323514e-759f-45df-9cf0-0ac867e4894c"]]},
hendrikdj:{"n":"Hendrik Durks Jonker","d":"c. 1775–1841 · Grouw","s":"h","p":["durkh","lijsberts"],"r":["nl"],"b":1775,"x":1841,"t":"Born at Grouw, the son of Durk Hendriks Jonker, a farmer, and Lijsbert Sytzes. His brothers and sisters included Eeke, Sytze and Yke, all later called Jonker. His age is given inconsistently: 28 at his marriage in 1811 (so born about 1783) but 66 at his death in 1841 (so born about 1775).\n\nOn 14 August 1811 he married Lemke Jans of Wartena in Idaarderadeel; she already had a small son, Klaas. When he registered the surname Jonker at Grouw at the end of 1811, with his four-week-old son Durk, he declared that his father Durk Jonker was still alive and living at Tietjerk. That entry, his marriage record and his death record (which names his wife Limke) tie him firmly to these parents, and the earlier doubt about the link can be dropped.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider) at Grouw. He and Lemke had seven children: Durk (27 November 1811), Jan (1814), Lijsbert (1816), Wytske (1819), Sytze (1821), Berber (26 September 1824) and Tjerkje (1826). The names Durk, Lijsbert and Sytze came from his own parents' family, and Jan and Wytske from hers.\n\nHe died on 2 May 1841 at Hoogemeeden, a hamlet near Aduard in the province of Groningen, aged 66. The record notes that he still lived at Grouw. His son Durk became a sailor (matroos) and died at Harlingen in 1860; Sytze stayed a labourer at Grouw.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death record, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ac5854ca-7c0b-3152-9d6d-647be28ea432"],["Surname registration, Grouw, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:da2fc6bd-a6e9-4669-8a36-c9b9ae79375e"],["Birth of son Durk, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d43c8423-c9ca-e2c1-013d-f05c06d923ec"],["Birth of son Jan, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1074e78-8cc7-9261-8e61-d910ccbf780d"],["Birth of daughter Lijsbert, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:93cd6a8e-9a81-63a4-47b9-4e13dc1f92f0"],["Birth of daughter Wytske, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e1fff3a-a20b-15c3-2bb3-954902422806"],["Birth of son Sytze, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b443eb59-8152-810c-ba7b-2f51c8ea99a0"],["Birth of daughter Berber, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1cbdbe8-be1e-9f0a-a3f4-e0992d26879f"],["Birth of daughter Tjerkje, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b6765051-8fc9-65e0-ca82-cc871450e50a"],["Death of brother Sytze Durks Jonker, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b5019f3-abda-c4c4-e51a-13a6aa1a8cd0"],["Death of son Durk, Harlingen, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36b400ef-f662-70c2-9fa4-c4ce9c4cb0e3"]]},
lemke:{"n":"Lemke Jans","d":"c. 1784–1875 · Wartena","s":"h","p":["janjelkes","wytskew"],"r":["nl"],"b":1784,"t":"Born at Wartena, the daughter of Jan Jelkes and Wytske Wytzes. Her age is given as 27 in 1811 (born about 1784) but 94 at her death (born about 1781). Her brothers and sisters included Wytze, later a skipper at Grouw with the surname Zandberg, Bauke, a fisherman at Wartena who took the name de Jong, and Berbertje, who used van der Veen.\n\nHer first partner was Klaas Eeltjes, with whom she had a son, Klaas Klazes Postma, born about 1809 at Boornbergum; he became a ship's carpenter's hand at Grouw. On 14 August 1811 she married Hendrik Durks, soon to be Jonker, a labourer of Grouw. Her marriage record of 1811 names her parents as Jan Jelkes and Wytske Wytzes. Her death record of 1875 names her father Jan Jelkes and her mother only as Wytske, and lists both Klaas Eeltjes and Hendrik Jonker as her husbands.\n\nShe and Hendrik had seven children: Durk (1811), Jan (1814), Lijsbert (1816), Wytske (1819), Sytze (1821), Berber (1824) and Tjerkje (1826). Hendrik died in 1841 in Groningen. In 1860 she was working as a charwoman (werkster).\n\nShe lived to a great age and saw many of her children die before her: Durk in 1860, Sytze in 1866, Berber in 1870 and her eldest son Klaas on 4 June 1875. A month later, on 7 July 1875, she died at Grouw, aged 94.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death record, Grouw, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:373592a0-a262-376a-874d-8b3c67faf5b3"],["Death of son Klaas Klazes Postma, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:42de5b27-48a7-6539-9946-244b91044a2e"],["Death of son Durk, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36b400ef-f662-70c2-9fa4-c4ce9c4cb0e3"],["Death of son Sytze, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4da2db04-352f-6013-4e80-b313376701fe"],["Death of brother Wytze Jans Zandberg, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:907c6a62-8731-3b81-5ab7-fe5b3c385350"],["Death of brother Bauke Jans de Jong, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b62e5e52-08e2-553a-9e58-de358850418b"]],"x":1875},
jacobp:{"n":"Jacob Postma","d":"1855–1917 · Wijnjeterp","b":1855,"s":"h","p":["janrp","aaltjem"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 19 September 1855, the second son of Jan Roels Postma, a labourer, and Aaltje Jacobs Meinsma. He was named after his mother's father, Jacob Meines Meinsma. His brothers and sisters were Roel (1853), Fedde (1858) and Ibeltje (1861), and three little sisters called Fettje, born between 1860 and 1865, at least two of whom died as babies.\n\nOn 11 May 1877, aged 21, he married Aukjen Jilts Wijnstra, 20, of Lippenhuizen, at Opsterland. They had nine children: Jan (3 December 1877), Jilt (28 December 1879), Aaltje (8 July 1882), Aukje (11 May 1885), Ybeltje (12 June 1888), Feitze (3 November 1891), Neeltje (10 March 1894), a stillborn child (5 May 1898) and Roel (12 February 1901). Jan, the eldest, was born at Wijnjeterp, and by 1882 the family was living at Hemrik, a few kilometres to the south. Jacob worked as a labourer.\n\nLater he and Aukjen moved east to Ooststellingwerf, where several of their children settled. Their daughter Aaltje married Jan Elzinga in 1905. Ybeltje married Jan de Vries in Ooststellingwerf in 1914, and Feitze became a bicycle dealer (rijwielhandelaar) at Hoogeveen.\n\nJacob died in Ooststellingwerf on 17 October 1917, aged 62. Aukjen died there on 9 June 1921.","src":[["Marriage record, 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:38671897-c258-e072-c15b-c638680ef8a9"],["Birth record, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d9797d9-3ba1-7fb8-c3ef-e5659c6e02f8"],["Birth of son Jan, 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5306f3dc-7dd2-e1a9-e2fe-bf719790d915"],["Birth of son Jilt, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a268c47f-0fc9-fda4-8b5d-95066afda2a1"],["Birth of daughter Aaltje, 1882","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:dcca0cb5-7554-1bbd-e854-787c82e28471"],["Death of daughter Aukje, Oosterwolde, 1968","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f9133e9a-eec7-9af8-8118-08b7729da6bb"],["Marriage of daughter Ybeltje, 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:44ae8c9e-8e6e-5d4b-17d3-239559272c08"],["Birth of son Feitze, 1891","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8cb22911-6542-3196-b603-cf7311c9dff2"],["Death of son Feitze, Assen, 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:f3c574da-dc66-4521-98ce-a15d5a47e6ae"],["Stillborn child, 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b34ee0ce-d200-4d90-cbb2-0e1db1539d0c"],["Birth of son Roel, 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:934bfb6f-f593-733d-40ca-2be60cd3120b"],["Death record, 1917","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7f32bc86-78ab-271a-13ef-6aee86ce06ec"],["Death of his wife, 1921","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3f970dc1-3b30-fab3-dded-02161b322e59"]],"x":1917},
aukjenw:{"n":"Aukjen Jilts Wijnstra","d":"1856–1921 · Lippenhuizen","b":1856,"s":"h","p":["jilt","aukjenv"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland on 22 November 1856, the eldest child of Jilt Jans Wijnstra, a farmer, and Aukjen Feitzes de Vries. Her brothers and sisters included Neeltje (1858), Jantje (1860), Feitze (1862), Albertje (about 1865), Rinskje, Jan and Wietze. Her father died in October 1872, when she was 15.\n\nOn 11 May 1877, aged 20, she married Jacob Postma, 21, a labourer from Wijnjeterp, at Opsterland. They had nine children: Jan (1877), Jilt (1879), Aaltje (1882), Aukje (1885), Ybeltje (1888), Feitze (1891), Neeltje (1894), a stillborn child (1898) and Roel (1901). Jilt was named after her father, Feitze after her grandfather, and Aukje after herself. The family lived at Wijnjeterp and from about 1882 at Hemrik.\n\nIn February 1893 her widowed mother and the Wijnstra children sold property at a public sale before a notary. Among the sellers were the husbands of her married sisters, and a Jacob Jans Posthumus, labourer at Hemrik, very probably her own husband acting for her.\n\nLater she and Jacob moved to Ooststellingwerf, where several of their children lived. She was widowed in October 1917 and died in Ooststellingwerf on 9 June 1921, aged 64.","x":1921,"src":[["Birth record, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a42047f4-a51f-53e2-d039-be442f8beaa6"],["Marriage, Opsterland, 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:38671897-c258-e072-c15b-c638680ef8a9"],["Death of her father, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f67a6664-5de2-b4a1-5e25-620b05da8dc8"],["Public sale, 4 February 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c80c5da9-da7b-4f9b-814d-7473f847a397"],["Birth of daughter Aaltje, 1882","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:dcca0cb5-7554-1bbd-e854-787c82e28471"],["Death of her husband, 1917","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7f32bc86-78ab-271a-13ef-6aee86ce06ec"],["Death record, 1921","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3f970dc1-3b30-fab3-dded-02161b322e59"]]},
janrp:{"n":"Jan Roels Postma","d":"1828–1902 · Wijnjeterp","b":1828,"s":"h","p":["roel","fetje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 20 August 1828, the elder son of Roel Jans Postma, a labourer, and Fettje Alberts de Boer. He was named after his father's father, Jan Roels. His brother Albert was born in 1830. His mother died in February 1845, when he was 16, and before the year was out his father had married again, to Anna Maria de Vries, a woman of 25 born at Leiden in Holland.\n\nOn 4 September 1850, aged 22, he married Aaltje Jacobs Meinsma, 27, a farmer's daughter of Wijnjeterp, at Opsterland. They had eight children: Roel (29 January 1853), Jacob (19 September 1855), Fedde (25 June 1858), Fettje (17 August 1860), Ibeltje (10 December 1861), Fettje (24 December 1863), who died at eight weeks, and Fettje (3 June 1865), who died at nine weeks. Giving the name Fettje again and again was the Frisian way of keeping his mother's name alive in the family.\n\nHe worked for many years as a labourer (arbeider) at Wijnjeterp. His son Roel also stayed a labourer there and lived until 1935; Ibeltje married Durk Johannes de Jong in 1889.\n\nJan died at Jubbega-Schurega on 13 February 1902, aged 73; his death record describes him as living from farming. Aaltje died there fourteen months later.","src":[["Marriage record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fc8109de-3652-be82-0e5a-b6f5af031d9e"],["Birth record, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3271a3a9-b7f0-abf4-b71c-9fac0de3d158"],["Second marriage of his father, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df5d467c-1215-dbee-a787-8a171fe7e0f3"],["Birth of son Roel, 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50d2c574-0306-233a-b02c-c279a9e142a0"],["Birth of son Jacob, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d9797d9-3ba1-7fb8-c3ef-e5659c6e02f8"],["Birth of son Fedde, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0d775cc4-5b99-aa87-3cc4-4ee92e9e7dcf"],["Birth of daughter Fettje, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:376530ba-a62a-894e-9540-6c18bfeacc88"],["Birth of daughter Ibeltje, 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c24792e-b422-3329-2da1-a5a6da79c473"],["Death of daughter Fettje, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:82ca5af6-fa81-68f1-9478-45d9990a6f6a"],["Death of daughter Fettje, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ff40134-4da2-8b90-2c80-4a2b9033629e"],["Death of son Roel, 1935","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:767dd371-d00f-575c-87d9-969e6811b76b"],["Marriage of daughter Ibeltje, 1889","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:353ccd23-2194-5eb5-2990-ac3a2216a63d"],["Death record, 1902","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9a852aee-5b5a-49f0-03e5-a32edf252745"],["Death of his wife, 1903","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56da6228-d532-9681-b463-6e27e6f5f4ca"]],"x":1902},
aaltjem:{"n":"Aaltje Jacobs Meinsma","d":"1823–1903 · Wijnjeterp","b":1823,"s":"h","p":["jacobmm","ibeltje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 16 April 1823, the daughter of Jacob Meines Meinsma, a smallholder and later farmer, and Ibeltje Hendriks. She was one of at least eight children; her brothers and sisters included Meine (1810), Neeltje (about 1815), Binne (about 1818), Auke (about 1820), Jouke (1821), Froukjen (1825) and Ibele (1830). Her mother died on 13 January 1830, the day after Ibele was born, when Aaltje was six.\n\nIn January 1848 her brother Auke, a labourer, and her newly married sister Froukjen died within twelve days of each other at Wijnjeterp.\n\nOn 4 September 1850, aged 27, she married Jan Roels Postma, 22, a labourer of Wijnjeterp. They had eight children: Roel (1853), Jacob (1855), Fedde (1858), Fettje (1860), Ibeltje (1861), named after her mother, and two more Fettjes in 1863 and 1865 who both died as babies. Her father died at Wijnjeterp in 1856.\n\nShe was widowed in February 1902 and died at Jubbega-Schurega on 23 April 1903, aged 80.","x":1903,"src":[["Birth record, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d558cb1e-e7aa-f51b-11ce-48697182f8a0"],["Death of her mother, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa8cc44a-530f-7686-bd75-8d5f22062002"],["Birth of brother Ibele, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0289d139-4014-917c-1862-08c333114b6f"],["Death of brother Auke, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a3376bb-fd1a-140c-111d-3b9e30107a06"],["Death of sister Froukjen, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7f981ae7-232d-6099-5cc5-30944e2afdb9"],["Marriage, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fc8109de-3652-be82-0e5a-b6f5af031d9e"],["Death record, 1903","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56da6228-d532-9681-b463-6e27e6f5f4ca"]]},
roel:{"n":"Roel Jans Postma","d":"1802–1884 · Wijnjeterp","s":"h","p":["janrp0","aafked"],"r":["nl"],"b":1802,"x":1884,"t":"Born at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 7 April 1802 and baptised there on 2 May, the eldest son of Jan Roels Postma and Aafke Durks. His brothers were Durk, born at Hoornsterzwaag in 1807, and Hendrik, born about 1810.\n\nOn 14 June 1827, aged 25, he married Fettje Alberts de Boer, 22, of Hemrik, at Opsterland. They had two sons: Jan, born on 20 August 1828, and Albert, born on 2 March 1830. Roel was a labourer (arbeider) at Wijnjeterp all his working life.\n\nFettje died on 23 February 1845, aged 39. On 14 December 1845 Roel married again: his second wife, Anna Maria de Vries, was 25 and had been born at Leiden in Holland.\n\nHis parents both died in 1847-48. Roel himself lived to see his grandchildren grown: his grandson Jacob married in 1877. He died at Wijnjeterp on 13 December 1884, aged 82.","src":[["Death record, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8fa28629-8717-f478-1dd6-6a47522460bd"],["Baptism, Wijnjeterp, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ba68715a-0e77-444e-9383-45a5b1942bb2"],["First marriage, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a76b1a61-f8fc-3336-a52e-5102d7ced7eb"],["Birth of son Jan, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3271a3a9-b7f0-abf4-b71c-9fac0de3d158"],["Birth of son Albert, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:baccb1e2-7cd2-bc54-9f81-460509c9e4e0"],["Death of his first wife, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df461236-d9bd-6b88-c392-ede665c135ac"],["Second marriage, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df5d467c-1215-dbee-a787-8a171fe7e0f3"]]},
fetje:{"n":"Fetje Alberts de Boer","d":"1805–1845 · Hemrik","s":"h","p":["albertdb","aaltjee"],"r":["nl"],"b":1805,"x":1845,"t":"Born at Hemrik in Opsterland on 8 April 1805 and baptised on 21 April, the daughter of Albert Douwes and Aaltje Eiles, who had married the year before. Her father died in May 1807, when she was two, leaving two small daughters, Fettje and Hendrikje. Both later used the surname de Boer.\n\nIn 1809 her mother married Evert Jans Veenstra, a farmer at Hemrik, and Fettje grew up on his farm with six half-brothers and half-sisters: Eile, Catharina, Trijntje, Jan, Wybe and Hendrik.\n\nOn 14 June 1827, aged 22, she married Roel Jans Postma, a labourer of Wijnjeterp. Their sons Jan and Albert were born in 1828 and 1830. When her mother died in 1834, the death-duty file named Fettje as the wife of Roel Jans Postma, labourer at Wijnjeterp, and listed real estate in the estate.\n\nShe was herself a labouring woman (arbeidster). She died at Wijnjeterp on 23 February 1845, aged 39, when her sons were 16 and 14.","src":[["Death record, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df461236-d9bd-6b88-c392-ede665c135ac"],["Baptism, Hemrik, 1805","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:699ef323-c76e-4204-83a5-3ea9d92ca639"],["Burial of her father, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ff248827-9aa9-4289-ba1c-88df3f1cd296"],["Second marriage of her mother, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8818ee11-34d7-4a93-abad-9d81236608a3"],["Marriage, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a76b1a61-f8fc-3336-a52e-5102d7ced7eb"],["Death-duty file of her mother, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a90ec4a0-e15b-460d-90e0-39eb161b8bd1"],["Death of half-brother Jan Everts Veenstra, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ec4a8a91-c48d-d606-6bfb-a36e5d9b50b1"]]},
jacobmm:{"n":"Jacob Meines Meinsma","d":"c. 1783–1856 · Wijnjeterp","b":1783,"x":1856,"s":"h","p":["meine","aukjenm"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1783, the son of Meine and Aukjen, and a man of Wijnjeterp in Opsterland. On 16 April 1809 he married Ibeltje (also written Hiltje) Hendriks of Wijnjewoude in the church at Wijnjeterp. In 1811 he registered the surname Meinsma, made from his father's name, with his son Meine, then a year and a half old.\n\nFor most of his life he was a koemelker, a smallholder who kept a few cows and lived from their milk and butter. He and Ibeltje had at least eight children: Meine (1 September 1810), Neeltje (about 1815), Binne (about 1818), Auke (about 1820), Jouke (1821), Aaltje (16 April 1823), Froukjen (1825) and Ibele (12 January 1830). Ibeltje died the day after Ibele's birth, leaving Jacob with a houseful of children.\n\nIn January 1848 he lost two grown children in under a fortnight: Auke, a labourer of 28, and Froukjen, 22, who had married Beint Gerrits Bouma the year before. Neeltje married Barteld Rienks Nieuwland in 1836 and lived until 1879.\n\nBy the end of his life he was described as a farmer (boer). He died at Wijnjeterp on 13 October 1856, aged 73.","src":[["Death record, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e211ff9e-8fb2-6075-33da-64c534da20e9"],["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e01e90a-0b74-4e01-b9f7-00f527279fc3"],["Surname registration, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:825bc225-1156-4545-875a-b95aec02be78"],["Baptism of son Meine, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e570d0a0-1797-4986-9543-98210c8795f6"],["Birth of son Jouke, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:98717473-5ffe-64b6-3cc6-556210219988"],["Birth of daughter Froukjen, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:860c0efd-fcce-2fb9-5cd9-a16a67368023"],["Death of his wife, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa8cc44a-530f-7686-bd75-8d5f22062002"],["Death of son Auke, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a3376bb-fd1a-140c-111d-3b9e30107a06"],["Death of daughter Froukjen, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7f981ae7-232d-6099-5cc5-30944e2afdb9"],["Marriage of daughter Neeltje, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4aa2408d-b2f6-bdc9-edcf-d525d19dc5d6"]]},
ibeltje:{"n":"Ibeltje Hendriks","d":"c. 1782–1830","s":"h","p":["ezhendrikberends","ezneeltjemartens"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Ibeltje Hendriks, also written Hiltje or Ybeltje, was the daughter of Hendrik Berends and Neeltje Martens of Hemrik in Opsterland. Her brothers Berend (born 1788 at Hemrik, later Bloemsma) and Merten (born 1795 at Lippenhuizen) were baptised after her parents' marriage at Lippenhuizen on 13 May 1787. Her own age at death, 48, would put her birth about 1781, before that marriage; either the age is overstated or she was born before the wedding. No baptism for her has been found.\n\nShe was living at Wijnjewoude when she married Jacob Meines, later Meinsma, of Wijnjeterp, on 16 April 1809. Their children were Meine (1810), Neeltje (about 1815), named after her mother, Binne (about 1818), Auke (about 1820), Jouke (1821), Aaltje (1823), Froukjen (1825) and Ibele (12 January 1830). Her husband was a smallholder who kept cows (koemelker).\n\nShe died at Wijnjeterp on 13 January 1830, the day after her son Ibele was born. Her mother, a labourer, was still living. Aaltje, then six, became the grandmother of Aaltje Postma.","b":1782,"x":1830,"src":[["Death record, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa8cc44a-530f-7686-bd75-8d5f22062002"],["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e01e90a-0b74-4e01-b9f7-00f527279fc3"],["Marriage of her parents, Lippenhuizen, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:509e6ca4-b24c-49fc-8a16-fa268cd1c5ca"],["Baptism of son Meine, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e570d0a0-1797-4986-9543-98210c8795f6"],["Birth of son Ibele, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0289d139-4014-917c-1862-08c333114b6f"],["Marriage of brother Berend Hendriks Bloemsma, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a81f10bf-2274-5d73-43d0-6abca0ac2f39"]]},
jilt:{"n":"Jilt Jans Wijnstra","d":"1829–1872 · Hemrik","b":1829,"x":1872,"s":"h","p":["janwijnstra","albertjedv"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Hemrik in Opsterland on 12 March 1829, the eldest child of Jan Jilts Wijnstra, a labourer, and Albertje Hendriks de Vries, and named after his father's father, Jilt Geerts Wijnstra, who died that summer. His younger brothers and sister were Hendrik (1830), Neeltje (1832), Aize (1833) and Fokke (1836).\n\nOn 1 May 1856 he married Aukjen Feitzes de Vries, 18, of Lippenhuizen. They farmed at Lippenhuizen and had a large family: Aukjen (22 November 1856), Neeltje (1858), Jantje (1860), Feitze (1862), Albertje (about 1865), Rinskje, Jan and Wietze.\n\nHis father died in 1860 and his mother in 1871. Jilt himself died at Lippenhuizen on 17 October 1872, aged 43, a farmer (boer), leaving Aukjen with eight children.\n\nHis widow and children sold property at a public sale in February 1893. In 1894 his sons Jan and Wietze rented land at Lippenhuizen from their mother's brother Wytze Feitzes de Vries. His brother Aize became a cattle farmer at Hemrik and died there in 1902.","src":[["Marriage, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e5ed003c-34be-f6df-4c21-e6d5d4c808c9"],["Death-duty file, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:32243268-7574-4367-8518-250ba4bbecbf"],["Birth record, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:466834e3-9704-51ec-7153-e7d7213b649a"],["Marriage of his parents, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96f0b262-7ce0-75b0-a43b-23958184b581"],["Birth of daughter Aukjen, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a42047f4-a51f-53e2-d039-be442f8beaa6"],["Death record, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f67a6664-5de2-b4a1-5e25-620b05da8dc8"],["Public sale, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c80c5da9-da7b-4f9b-814d-7473f847a397"],["Lease, 4 July 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96cdc503-d314-467e-8a49-1ca68b6fc4e7"]]},
aukjenv:{"n":"Aukjen Feitzes de Vries","d":"1837–1908 · Lippenhuizen","b":1837,"s":"h","p":["feitzedv","aukjenjansma"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland on 28 May 1837, the daughter of Feitze Wytzes de Vries, a labourer, and Aukjen Jans Jansma, and named after her mother. Her father was 53 when she was born, her mother 30. Her brothers were Wytze (1834), Jan (1842), later a carpenter at Gorredijk, and Rinze (1 April 1850). Her mother died eight days after Rinze's birth, when Aukjen was 12.\n\nOn 1 May 1856, aged 18, she married Jilt Jans Wijnstra of Hemrik, and they farmed at Lippenhuizen. Their children were Aukjen (1856), Neeltje (1858), Jantje (1860), Feitze (1862), named after her father, Albertje (about 1865), Rinskje, Jan and Wietze.\n\nJilt died in October 1872, leaving her a widow at 35 with eight children. In February 1893 she and her children sold property at a public sale. Her brother Wytze, who died in 1895, rented land to two of her sons in 1894.\n\nShe died at Beetsterzwaag on 8 July 1908, aged 71. Her daughter Jantje also died at Beetsterzwaag, in 1920.","src":[["Marriage, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e5ed003c-34be-f6df-4c21-e6d5d4c808c9"],["Birth record, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:deaa8dcf-28da-8ae9-f28c-a47208a44107"],["Death of her mother, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8334eb66-eb89-af9b-8c72-7dd4c730d7c5"],["Death of her husband, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f67a6664-5de2-b4a1-5e25-620b05da8dc8"],["Public sale, 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c80c5da9-da7b-4f9b-814d-7473f847a397"],["Lease, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96cdc503-d314-467e-8a49-1ca68b6fc4e7"],["Death record, 1908","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:774807ed-e238-0013-576f-fddfa53f4e24"]],"x":1908},
janlewe:{"n":"Jan Jacobs Lewe","d":"1779–1826 · Wagenborgen","b":1779,"x":1826,"s":"b","p":["jakobkl","gepke"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wagenborgen on 3 November 1779 and baptised there four days later, the first child of Jakob Klaasens Leewe and his second wife, Gepke Stoffers. His father had first married in 1748 and had grown children from that marriage. A brother Jeje followed at the end of 1781 and a sister Engel in 1784.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider, dagloner). In February 1806 the banns were read at Oude Pekela for his marriage to Hilje Willems of that village, to be held at Wagenborgen, and the register notes that his father 'at first objected'. He later married Aaltje Okkes Eekstra from Noordbroek. Their children were Anje (10 January 1812), Gepke (19 November 1813), Derktje (11 May 1816), a child registered without a name on 13 April 1820, Engeltje (30 March 1821), who died at two, and Jacob (12 December 1824). The girls carried the names of his mother and his sister.\n\nHe signed his name Lewe, though the clerks usually wrote Leeuw or Leewe; all three mean lion. He died at Wagenborgen on 17 December 1826, aged 47, leaving four children. Jacob, the only son, was a farmhand when he died at twenty in 1845.","src":[["Baptism, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:66d44236-4602-4d4d-52a9-3a88ab5edc50"],["Banns, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5959de86-fa03-0031-e744-4f544adea0f8"],["Death record, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:80a20404-0bec-1afb-7aa3-daacc8c189d1"],["Death of Jacob Jans Lewe, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d873abb3-613e-d06c-afc8-0c77e9e3258d"],["Birth of Anje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f49b6b35-2c7a-c7d7-0d03-17fb1e65713a"],["Marriage of Derktje, Delfzijl 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d7267c62-bfbb-e953-9007-7367ee435792"],["Birth of an unnamed child, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:9916e0c8-b6e2-377f-1bc8-055eda760fcc"],["Birth of Engeltje, 1821 (father signs Lewe)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3e18f99f-9460-d55c-4aa9-a30deff05c71"],["Death of Engeltje, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4be890f7-794f-56b2-a56e-c03b1d497987"],["Birth of Jacob, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:00c77b5f-c84b-d94d-1498-9f9b8ce896b4"],["Marriage of Gepke, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e432d37-cbe8-40ad-0c5e-8f3c84a6eddf"]]},
aaltjeok:{"n":"Aaltje Okkes Eekstra","d":"1782–1864","b":1782,"x":1864,"s":"b","p":["okke","anjed"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Noordbroek on 24 March 1782, the year after her parents, Ocko Eijlderts and Anje Derks, moved there from Eexta. She was the youngest of at least eight children, and two older sisters had already been given the name Aaltjen, in 1767 and 1776.\n\nShe married Jan Jacobs Lewe, a labourer of Wagenborgen, and had Anje (1812), Gepke (1813) and Jacob (1824) among her children. Jan died in December 1826, leaving her a widow of 44 with four children. She supported them as a working woman (arbeidster, werkvrouw), saw her daughters marry between 1833 and 1840, and buried her son Jacob in 1845. She died at Wagenborgen on 28 February 1864, aged 82, still described as a labourer.\n\nThe civil registers add three more children to those named above: Derktje (11 May 1816), who married at Delfzijl in 1840, a child registered without a name in April 1820, and Engeltje (1821), who died in October 1823. Her father is now known to have been a shoemaker, baptised at Eexta in 1740, and her mother the daughter of Derk Hindriks of Noordbroek. A brother, Eildert, took the surname Eexterhof and was a shoemaker at Slochteren until his death in 1836.","src":[["Baptism, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:50fcb3cd-1074-2269-0b5f-3495b1f0824a"],["Death record, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:100c61e2-42f9-f5e1-1858-85cd746cfb12"],["Marriage of Gepke, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e432d37-cbe8-40ad-0c5e-8f3c84a6eddf"],["Marriage of Derktje, Delfzijl 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d7267c62-bfbb-e953-9007-7367ee435792"],["Death of Engeltje, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4be890f7-794f-56b2-a56e-c03b1d497987"],["Death of Eildert Okkes Eexterhof, Slochteren 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e486ccf-6f67-c33c-9a7e-62e5eab89c94"]]},
okke:{"n":"Okke (Ocko) Eijlderts Eekstra","sn":"Okke Eekstra","d":"b. 1740 · Eexta","s":"b","p":["greildertockes","grannekesijwerts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eexta, near Scheemda, on 26 March 1740 and baptised the next day, the son of Eildert Ockes and Anneke Sijwerts. He was a shoemaker (schoenmaker), the trade his son Eildert followed after him.\n\nOn 30 November 1764 he signed a marriage contract with Anje Derks of Noordbroek and married her at Eexta the same day. His father, Anke Sebes and Grietje Eilders stood on his side. Their children were baptised at Eexta: Anneke (1766), who had his mother's name, Aaltjen (1767), Rienje (1769), Reenje (1770), Eijldert (1775), a second Aaltjen (1776) and Derk (1780). In 1768 he and Anje stood by her father at his marriage contract with Claaske Cornellis.\n\nOn 11 June 1781 he and Anje were entered as members of the church at Noordbroek, 'come over with attestation', the certificate of good standing a Reformed family carried from one congregation to the next. Their last child, another Aaltje, was baptised there the following March. His children took surnames from the home village: Aaltje was called Eekstra, and Eildert, a shoemaker at Slochteren who died in 1836, Eexterhof. A Derktje Okkes Eekstra, born at Scheemda about 1771, died at Muntendam in 1839; the index does not name her parents.","src":[["Marriage, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:89c64a0d-54f1-3b7e-841c-b2368822202b"],["Church membership, Noordbroek, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1e09457d-2dae-44fa-bea6-ce0eee2bb660"],["Baptism of Eijldert, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0fd0cada-1688-a110-d59e-0db8ca5338af"],["Baptism, Eexta 1740","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6819a111-d25b-7a54-c2f0-bbed7d211cba"],["Marriage contract, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:430190a7-1a23-8a90-322b-a39099e417a4"],["Baptism of Derk, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bfc2d8a6-fd4a-535d-f0e7-8817ccb5ef28"],["Marriage contract of Derk Hindriks, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48cc8858-a296-7865-4a6e-9c00f089dee7"],["Death of Eildert Okkes Eexterhof, 1836 (father a shoemaker)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e486ccf-6f67-c33c-9a7e-62e5eab89c94"],["Death of Derktje Okkes Eekstra, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f4239a1-e681-082f-6ca3-2586b7e676c6"]],"b":1740},
anjed:{"n":"Anje Derks","d":"b. 1744 · Noordbroek","s":"b","p":["grderkhindriks","graeltjenjakobs"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Noordbroek on 28 November 1744, the daughter of Derk Hindriks and Aeltjen Jakobs, who had married there two years before. Her mother is not heard of again, and in 1751 Albertjen Jans was received into the Noordbroek church with Derk Hindriks's name beside hers.\n\nShe was just twenty when she married the shoemaker Ocko Eijlderts of Eexta on 30 November 1764. Her father and Albertjen Jans stood on her side of the contract, with Jacob Fockes, Aeltjen Gerrijts, Hindrick Jacobs and Focko Jacobs; two of them shared her mother's second name, Jakobs. She bore at least eight children over the next eighteen years: Anneke (1766), Aaltjen (1767), who had her mother's name, Rienje (1769), Reenje (1770), Eijldert (1775), Aaltjen (1776), Derk (1780), who had her father's, and Aaltje (1782).\n\nIn June 1781 the family moved back to her own village, and she and Ocko were received as members of the Noordbroek church with an attestation from Eexta. She is named as the mother of the shoemaker Eildert Eexterhof when he died in 1836 and of Aaltje Eekstra when Aaltje died in 1864.","src":[["Marriage, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:89c64a0d-54f1-3b7e-841c-b2368822202b"],["Church membership, Noordbroek, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1e09457d-2dae-44fa-bea6-ce0eee2bb660"],["Baptism, Noordbroek 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:be74ba11-1c01-ceff-c107-68d4efb6b7af"],["Parents' marriage, Noordbroek 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aaf62201-e10b-12be-87dc-44fe731303fe"],["Marriage contract, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:430190a7-1a23-8a90-322b-a39099e417a4"],["Baptism of Derk, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bfc2d8a6-fd4a-535d-f0e7-8817ccb5ef28"],["Death of Eildert Okkes Eexterhof, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4e486ccf-6f67-c33c-9a7e-62e5eab89c94"]],"b":1744},
harmh:{"n":"Harm Hulzebosch","d":"1816–1903 · Oudeschans","b":1816,"x":1903,"s":"b","p":["janjh","stijntje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Oudeschans on 15 January 1816, the youngest surviving son of the tailor Jan Joosten Hulzebos and Stijntje Jacobs IJzer. He was three when his mother died, eight days after the birth of her last child. Like his brothers Jacob and Jan he was brought up to his father's trade and worked as a tailor (kleermaker) all his life.\n\nHe married Helena Plat, a carter's daughter in service, at Finsterwolde on 16 December 1842, and they made their home at Noordbroek, where their daughter Epke was born the next year. He was still a tailor when Epke married Jakob Broesder in 1866. Helena died in 1880 and he lived on at Noordbroek as a widower for 22 years, dying there on 29 January 1903, aged 87.","src":[["Birth record, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:03080cb3-4655-d889-e764-b76fca87b529"],["Marriage, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:66387e03-ac44-a964-4c83-c4e007ded74b"],["Death record, 1903","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:56d12140-e497-776e-2dc9-821154d25e08"]]},
helena:{"n":"Helena Plat","d":"1813–1880 · Finsterwolde","b":1813,"x":1880,"s":"b","p":["aaldrikp","epkeh"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Finsterwolde on 8 August 1813, three months after her parents' wedding, the eldest child of the carter Aaldrik Wessels Plat and Epke Derks Hekman. She went into service as a maid (dienstmeid), and was 29 when she married the tailor Harm Hulzebosch of Oudeschans at Finsterwolde on 16 December 1842.\n\nThe couple settled at Noordbroek, where their daughter Epke, named for Helena's mother, was born in October 1843. When Epke married in 1866 Helena was described as a working woman (arbeidster). She died at Noordbroek on 14 August 1880, aged 67; Harm outlived her by more than twenty years.","src":[["Death record, 1880","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bf020af7-e1b2-0cd6-4141-177a3dabd290"],["Birth record, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:944003a1-7de1-55ad-96ac-6dd1f22f2df9"],["Marriage, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:66387e03-ac44-a964-4c83-c4e007ded74b"],["Marriage of Epke, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:39e4137b-4a92-a7fe-fe79-741828ffd1bb"]]},
janjh:{"n":"Jan Joosten Hulzebos","d":"c. 1776–1848 · Wymeer in East Frisia","b":1776,"x":1848,"s":"b","p":["joost","gepkejans"],"r":["de","nl"],"t":"A tailor, born about 1776 at Wymeer in East Frisia, just over the German border, the son of the day labourer Joost Hulzebos and Gepke Jans. He crossed into Groningen and settled at Oudeschans, a small star-shaped fortress village near Bellingwolde, where as Jan Joosten he married Stijntje Jacobs on 1 June 1800.\n\nTheir children were baptised or registered at Oudeschans: Gepke (born 6 October 1801), who had his mother's name, Jacob (19 December 1802), a second Gepke (18 September 1805), Jan (9 July 1808), who was buried in March 1809 at seven months, Janna (22 February 1810), who died aged two in 1812, Jan (1813), Harm (1816) and a second Janna, born on 18 April 1819. Stijntje died eight days after that birth and the baby ten days after her mother. The sons Jacob, Jan and Harm all became tailors like their father. He stayed at Oudeschans and died there on 8 February 1848, aged 72.\n\nHis wife is placed in the tree, without proof, as the daughter of Jacob Harms IJzer and Janna Filippus of Oudeschans; her own death record names Harm Jacobs IJzer and Antje Jans as her parents.","src":[["Death record, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:17cbb6b4-aaa6-e65e-48da-1b9fb6afbbb7"],["Marriage, Oudeschans, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:14bb3952-edf0-9dca-a524-f2ee12cd47e3"],["Birth of Harm, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:03080cb3-4655-d889-e764-b76fca87b529"],["Death of baby Janna, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:307840e8-04ee-6711-4188-cbb91b456487"],["Marriage of Jan Hulzebos, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1185a659-8680-82b0-b78e-2e82f28d4338"],["Burial of Jan, Oudeschans 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1a427deb-8eb8-2b2f-f1f4-d93628ee2230"],["Death of Janna, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:46d8b5a3-81f9-3d23-ea26-46a3b46a2eca"],["Baptism of Gepke, Oudeschans 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e75e9da3-325f-6885-a00c-bb4ff08aa128"],["Baptism of Jacob, Oudeschans 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2f756231-4581-b32d-6458-7757586e1776"],["Baptism of Gepke, Oudeschans 1805","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6848e5a3-c2c5-55bb-7fc7-cc0b0b12ad31"],["Baptism of Jan, Oudeschans 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bf0a532f-1ec2-5e33-f9c9-2031dc76e75a"],["Baptism of Janna, Oudeschans 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5095721a-5cfa-66ef-2d1a-120cb442001b"]]},
stijntje:{"n":"Stijntje Jacobs IJzer","d":"c. 1780–1819","b":1780,"x":1819,"s":"b","p":["grjacobharmsijzer","grjannafilippus"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Oudeschans, the fortress village near Bellingwolde, born about 1780. As Stijntje Jacobs she married Jan Joosten, a tailor from Wymeer in East Frisia, at Oudeschans on 1 June 1800, and later records call her a tailor's wife (kleermakersvrouw). Their children were baptised or registered at Oudeschans: Gepke (born 6 October 1801), Jacob (19 December 1802), a second Gepke (18 September 1805), Jan (9 July 1808), who was buried in March 1809 at seven months, Janna (22 February 1810), who died aged two in 1812, Jan (1813), Harm (1816) and another Janna, born on 18 April 1819. Stijntje died eight days after that last birth, on 26 April 1819, aged 39, and the baby died on 6 May.\n\nHer parents are uncertain. Her death record names them as the day labourer Harm Jacobs IJzer and Antje Jans, but that couple were having children baptised at Oudeschans in the 1750s, and her own second name, Jacobs, means daughter of Jacob. The tree places her instead as the daughter of their son Jacob Harms IJzer and his wife Janna Filippus, who had a daughter Antje baptised at Oudeschans in 1776. Janna's mother was called Stijntje Jans, and Stijntje named her first son Jacob and a daughter Janna twice. Her own baptism has not been found, so the link is marked unproven.","src":[["Death record, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:de197a07-af33-80b5-d0c4-c02a4d6ece07"],["Death of Jacob Harms IJzer, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec9b91e7-ef02-11dd-00aa-6a7c80860995"],["Birth of Jan, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d8674d54-c5ca-7f48-4991-737d70583517"],["Death of Janna Filippus, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"],["Baptism of Antje, daughter of Jacob Harms and Janna Filips, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f3e31962-2620-9856-d62d-4a7bfe4fe01b"],["Burial of her son Jan, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1a427deb-8eb8-2b2f-f1f4-d93628ee2230"],["Death of Janna, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:46d8b5a3-81f9-3d23-ea26-46a3b46a2eca"],["Marriage of Jan Joosten and Stijntje Jacobs, Oudeschans 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:14bb3952-edf0-9dca-a524-f2ee12cd47e3"],["Baptism of Gepke, Oudeschans 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e75e9da3-325f-6885-a00c-bb4ff08aa128"],["Baptism of Jacob, Oudeschans 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2f756231-4581-b32d-6458-7757586e1776"],["Baptism of Gepke, Oudeschans 1805","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6848e5a3-c2c5-55bb-7fc7-cc0b0b12ad31"],["Baptism of Jan, Oudeschans 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bf0a532f-1ec2-5e33-f9c9-2031dc76e75a"],["Baptism of Janna, Oudeschans 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5095721a-5cfa-66ef-2d1a-120cb442001b"]],"u":true},
joost:{"n":"Joost Hulzebos","d":"day labourer, Wymeer","s":"b","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"A day labourer (daglooner) at Wymeer, a village in the Rheiderland of East Frisia, a few kilometres over the border from Bellingwolde. The Rheiderland and the Groningen side were one countryside in his day: the same low marshland, the same dialect and the same Reformed church, and people crossed the border freely for work and marriage.\n\nHe and his wife, Gepke Jans, are known only from the death record of their son Jan Joosten, who was born at Wymeer about 1776, learned the tailor's trade and settled in the Dutch fortress village of Oudeschans.","src":[["Death record of Jan Joostens Hulzebos, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:17cbb6b4-aaa6-e65e-48da-1b9fb6afbbb7"]]},
gepke:{"n":"Gepke Stoffers Toornman","sn":"Gepke Stoffers","d":"c. 1742–1809","b":1742,"x":1809,"s":"b","p":["grstoffertoorman","grengelabrahams"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1742, by her age at burial. Her second name, Stoffers, means daughter of Stoffer, and the only Stoffer Toorman found in the registers of those years is the man who married Engel Abrahams at Termunten in 1730. That couple had a child baptised there on 7 April 1743 whose name the minister left out; the date fits her age. No record names her parents outright, so the link is marked as unproven, but two things support it: Wolter and Stoffer Toorman stood on her side when she married, and she called her only daughter Engel.\n\nShe was about 36 when she married Jakob Klaasens Leewe at Wagenborgen on 4 December 1778. Their children were baptised there: Jan in November 1779, Jeje on New Year's Day 1782 (born on 27 December 1781) and Engel in March 1784. In 1787 she and Jakob stood together at the marriage contract of the brewer's daughter Geertruid Freerks at Nieuwolda.\n\nJakob was buried in November 1808. She was buried at Wagenborgen on 24 December 1809, aged 67, and a file of death duties was opened on her estate.","src":[["Burial, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2ff5d2df-0a2d-ce63-1a74-e948bc8f86ad"],["Baptism of Jeje, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5840fe2c-c925-9aef-ea03-bc0f4fa23808"],["Baptism of Engel, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3eba7645-a6a8-cd36-83b9-a32788b89102"],["Marriage of Stoffer Toorma and Engel Abrahams, Termunten 1730","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6b2a439c-b4b6-86be-06b8-8a3e9554fd21"],["Baptism of an unnamed child of Stoffer Toormans, 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:086f11f3-8f74-cbaf-efca-c15c621ace21"],["Marriage, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b53e0e5d-4fb5-498c-6409-cca46971ecbd"],["Marriage contract, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3cf14616-8c41-6565-6832-95b5c4329958"],["Marriage contract of Geertruid Freerks, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:823b685c-9a67-ff54-648f-dbb953d25879"],["Death duties file, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5d78b30a-cae3-9cc4-3c28-fc1c64cb4410"]],"u":true},
aaldrikp:{"n":"Aaldrik Wessels Plat","d":"1790–1866 · Finsterwolde","b":1790,"x":1866,"s":"b","p":["wessel","jannaj"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Finsterwolde on 8 February 1790 and baptised six days later, the son of the carter Wessel Freerks Plat and Janna Jurjens. He was a farm servant (dienstknegt) of 23 when he married Epke Derks Hekman, a maid from Pekela four years older than himself, at Midwolda on 7 May 1813. Their first child, Helena, was born three months later.\n\nHe then took up his father's trade and was a carter (voerman) at Finsterwolde for the rest of his working life, hauling with horse and wagon. The children were Helena (1813), Derk (1815), Wessel (1820), Janna (who died at eighteen months in 1823), Lammert (1823) and Jurjen (1826). Derk and Wessel married within four days of each other in May 1844. Epke died in 1853 and he died at Finsterwolde on 18 January 1866, aged 75, leaving four children.\n\nHis grandparents are now known: on his father's side Freerk Freerks and Geeske Wessels of Nieuw-Beerta, and on his mother's Jurrien Harms and Leentje Harms of Finsterwolde. His father was buried at Finsterwolde in July 1811, when Aaldrik was 21, so the marriage record of 1813, which gives his father's trade as carter, describes a man already dead. His mother died in 1823.","src":[["Death record, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6066d2f9-0096-251f-7885-da5d17c4582f"],["Baptism, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f76433be-326a-fab2-37a9-cb0730faee1f"],["Marriage, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1bf7e209-2371-0998-5338-a71fa4a62084"],["Birth of Wessel, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6dd68c73-7e89-dfc4-ea32-51a98298632a"],["Burial of Wessel Freerks, Finsterwolde 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:639d0409-5050-8dc5-08b7-21a663b7f404"]]},
epkeh:{"n":"Epke Derks Hekman","d":"1785–1853 · Nieuwe Pekela","b":1785,"x":1853,"s":"b","p":["derkeh","jantjenegb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwe Pekela on 7 October 1785 and baptised two days later, the first child of the carpenter Derk Eppes and Jantje Kornelis, who had married there the previous November. The family later took the surname Hekman.\n\nShe went into service as a maid (dienstmaagd) in the Oldambt. Her mother died at Nieuwe Pekela on 6 April 1813, and a month later, on 7 May, Epke married Aaldrik Wessels Plat at Midwolda, aged 27. He was then a farm servant and later a carter. The Midwolda clerk wrote her mother's name as Jantjen Egberts, a slip that appears in no other record. Their daughter Helena was born that August.\n\nHer father was a master carpenter at Nieuwe Pekela, one of the two long canal villages of the Pekela peat colony, and her brothers Kornelius, Eppe and Lammert all became carpenters there. Her own children, born at Finsterwolde, were Helena (1813), Derk (1815), Wessel (1820), Janna (1821, died 1823), Lammert (1823) and Jurjen (1826). Four were living when she died at Finsterwolde on 10 February 1853, aged 67.","src":[["Marriage, Midwolda, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1bf7e209-2371-0998-5338-a71fa4a62084"],["Death record, 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:57e41f55-d073-3fb3-1fb9-38e79f4bda0e"],["Birth of Jurjen, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d9b7bac5-cca2-ba66-61dc-4d4bf279c808"],["Baptism, Nieuwe Pekela 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1632e8f9-3183-7915-6a09-7e9454408fc3"],["Parents' marriage, Nieuwe Pekela 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ceb4906-c45a-03f5-1338-00e031249b64"],["Death of her mother, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:965c1b4c-a615-4840-5b07-c3cc08006e15"]]},
wessel:{"n":"Wessel Freerks Plat","d":"1755–1811 · Nieuw-Beerta","s":"b","p":["grfreerkfreerks","grgeeskewessels"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Nieuw-Beerta on 5 October 1755, the son of Freerk Freerks and Geeske Wessels, with the name of his mother's father. An elder brother Wessel had been baptised in 1748, so the name was being given a second time. Nieuw-Beerta lies a few kilometres from Finsterwolde, and it was at Finsterwolde that he married Janna Jurjens on 10 October 1779.\n\nHe was a carter (voerman). A carter owned a horse and wagon and hauled for hire: grain from the big farms to the ships at the sluice, peat for fuel, and goods to and from Winschoten market. It put him a step above the day labourers around him.\n\nHe and Janna had Leentje (born 5 February 1782), Jurjen (27 October 1784), Frerik (5 July 1787) and Aaldrik (8 February 1790). Jurjen and Aaldrik became carters after him and Frerik a cooper (kuiper). He was buried at Finsterwolde on 18 July 1811, aged 55, shortly before surnames became compulsory; the name Plat was attached to him afterwards, when his sons married. The records of 1813 and 1827 still give his trade as carter, but he was dead by then. Janna lived until 1823.","src":[["Baptism of Leentje, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95926430-aed7-b57b-db9f-f07a9bafd1d8"],["Baptism of Aaldrik, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f76433be-326a-fab2-37a9-cb0730faee1f"],["Marriage of Freerk Wessels Plat, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b60df31b-04cf-89a6-9aa9-ccd0e490b1d9"],["Baptism, Nieuw-Beerta 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7c18e8d-45b8-a856-8ff4-0abf02eecf23"],["Baptism of the first Wessel, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bd5cfc62-be17-6d00-7def-2544ecc7d983"],["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ddc4039e-edaa-9460-7c7d-3035f22504a1"],["Baptism of Jurjen, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ed0c5a25-d600-7553-a3d6-221277174ed5"],["Baptism of Frerik, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7a8af6eb-7758-b8e0-cb84-20f628377d51"],["Burial, Finsterwolde 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:639d0409-5050-8dc5-08b7-21a663b7f404"],["Marriage of Jurjen Wessels Plat, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d69bb45f-c079-abc7-133c-47869fc7c307"]],"b":1755,"x":1811},
jannaj:{"n":"Janna Jurjens","d":"c. 1751–1823 · Finsterwolde","s":"b","p":["grjurrienharms","grleentjeharms"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Placed in the tree as the daughter of Jurrien Harms and Leentje Harms, who married at Finsterwolde in November 1750 and had two daughters named Janna baptised there, on 17 October 1751 and 12 August 1753, as well as a son Harm (1755) and a daughter Willemke (1758). The link is unproven. A second Janna Jurjens married at Finsterwolde in 1780, to Daniel Daniels van Barenburg, and no record names the parents of either woman. The reason for the choice is the naming of her children: her first daughter was Leentje and her first son Jurjen. Her age at death, 72 in December 1823, fits the baptism of 1751.\n\nShe married the carter Wessel Freerks at Finsterwolde on 10 October 1779, and the records call her a carter's wife (voermansche). Their children were Leentje (1782), Jurjen (1784), Frerik (1787) and Aaldrik (1790).\n\nWessel was buried in July 1811 and she was a widow for twelve years. She saw Aaldrik and Jurjen marry in 1813. She died at Finsterwolde on 15 December 1823, aged 72. Aaldrik's little daughter Janna, named for her, died at eighteen months in the same year.","b":1751,"x":1823,"src":[["Death record, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:61df34a2-7131-6cd7-511a-ca2b3b469412"],["Baptism of Aaldrik, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f76433be-326a-fab2-37a9-cb0730faee1f"],["Baptism of Janna, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95cb3a4e-d290-a3a1-e536-31e6c4e49455"],["Baptism of Janna, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:76cd7842-c27c-8d47-d47d-2eec5db3390a"],["Parents' marriage, Finsterwolde 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:469974a3-3b53-9760-585f-0ff4843deeeb"],["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ddc4039e-edaa-9460-7c7d-3035f22504a1"],["Burial of Wessel Freerks, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:639d0409-5050-8dc5-08b7-21a663b7f404"],["Marriage of Jurjen Wessels Plat, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d69bb45f-c079-abc7-133c-47869fc7c307"],["Marriage of the other Janna Jurjens, Finsterwolde 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5bcab68b-d6d1-79f8-2d3d-52e7aaa3601f"]],"u":true},
regina:{"n":"Regina Hendriks","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"The mother of Gerrit Willems Dijk, named in his death record of 1858; no father is given there. Gerrit was born about 1789 at Kielwindeweer, a peat-colony village on the Kieldiep canal south of Hoogezand, on the border of Groningen and Drenthe. His second name, Willems, shows that his father was called Willem.\n\nA Regina Hendriks living at the Kiel had three more children baptised at Zuidlaren in Drenthe, a few kilometres to the west: Grietje, born at De Kiel on 1 September 1791 and baptised on 6 May 1792; Hendrik, born at Zuidlaren on 6 February 1794; and Trijntje, baptised on 21 April 1799. The register names no father for any of them. Regina is a rare name in the district and the place and years match, but no record states that she is the same woman. Grietje was known as Grietje Ottes de Graaf and Hendrik as Hendrik Ottens, names that mean child of Otte.\n\nGrietje was a working woman at Windeweer when she died in July 1814, aged 23. Hendrik was a farmhand of 31 when he married at Hoogezand in May 1825, and Regina was then still alive and working as a labourer (arbeidster).","src":[["Death record of Gerrit Willems Dijk, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2a0a2dbf-8a37-352c-5912-c0116548ccde"],["Baptism of Grietje, Zuidlaren 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:6c681a8c-94aa-4e62-bd15-f911f47f9c3f"],["Baptism of Hendrik Ottens, Zuidlaren 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:5a6804a5-5cb1-1944-1d66-e12025745970"],["Baptism of Trijntje, Zuidlaren 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:ce2fda1e-706b-70b3-12ad-631e771600f9"],["Death of Grietje Ottes de Graaf, Windeweer 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:278be077-13a7-2240-c341-af1adbc4384e"],["Marriage of Hindrik Ottens, Hoogezand 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:97f0baae-bcfb-e688-9a60-28a9ed23f763"]]},
feibe:{"n":"Feibe Alberts Huisman","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Windeweer, one of the peat colonies of Hoogezand, where the bog was dug away for fuel and the cleared ground turned into farms. He and his wife Sjoukje Hindriks had at least three children there: Stijntje (about 1774), Roelfien (about 1784) and Hindrik (about 1788). The surname Huisman, which the children used after surnames became compulsory in 1811, means a man who farms a holding.\n\nSjoukje died at Windeweer on 25 January 1815, aged 72, and that November Stijntje, a working woman of 41, married the farmhand Freerk Harberts Boerma. Hindrik became a house carpenter (huistimmerman) at Windeweer and was later called a farmer; his sons were a seaman and a carpenter. Roelfien married the shipwright's man Gerrit Willems Dijk.\n\nHis first name came down both lines: Roelfien called a son Feibe in 1814, who became a shipbuilder at Hoogezand, and Hindrik called a son Feibe in 1823.","src":[["Death record of Roelfien, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dda1b8a6-044e-61b3-eeb2-31f5fcce49f2"],["Death of Sjouke Hindriks, Windeweer 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1ecc8329-a315-dc3b-05fc-23d58dd6c615"],["Marriage of Stijntje Fiebes Huisman, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0c5fe80f-c180-0643-d120-3bf6eedeafd7"],["Birth of Johannes Fredrikus Huisman, 1813 (father a house carpenter)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b0c75869-54fd-672f-2a4f-f94e20699b7d"],["Birth of Feibe Huisman, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1af98a8f-c043-66ff-d263-17d54f3622ae"],["Death of Stijntje Fiebes Huisman, 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b944901a-5451-9e02-03e6-becabca4b849"]]},
sjoukje:{"n":"Sjoukje Hindriks","d":"c. 1743–1815","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Feibe Alberts Huisman of Windeweer, in the Hoogezand peat colonies. She was born about 1743. Sjoukje is a Frisian name, uncommon in Groningen. The records write her name Sjouke as often as Sjoukje.\n\nHer known children were Stijntje (about 1774), Roelfien (about 1784) and Hindrik (about 1788), all born at Windeweer. Roelfien married a ship's carpenter's man, and Hindrik became a house carpenter in the village. She died at Windeweer on 25 January 1815, aged 72. Her daughter Stijntje married later that year, at 41, and lived at Windeweer until 1853; Roelfien died at Kalkwijk in 1847.","src":[["Death record of Roelfien, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dda1b8a6-044e-61b3-eeb2-31f5fcce49f2"],["Death record, Windeweer 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1ecc8329-a315-dc3b-05fc-23d58dd6c615"],["Marriage of Stijntje Fiebes Huisman, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0c5fe80f-c180-0643-d120-3bf6eedeafd7"]],"b":1743,"x":1815},
derkp:{"n":"Derk Pieters Hundt","d":"1760–1843 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":["grpeterderks","grgrietjejans"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Sappemeer on 2 November 1760, the same day his parents, Peter Derks and Grietje Jans, were married in the church there. He was the eldest of at least seven children.\n\nHe was a carpenter (timmerman) at Sappemeer, the long canal village east of Groningen where peat barges and seagoing wooden ships were built side by side. On 12 May 1793 he married Bouwina Aaldriks Hes, the daughter of a Sappemeer innkeeper and merchant. Their children were Grietje (1794), Aaldrik (about 1797), Pieter (1801), Albertje (about 1805), Jannes (1808), Klaassien (about 1811) and Remmert, born on 27 April 1816 when Derk was 55. Klaassien died at eleven in 1822. Aaldrik, Pieter, Jannes and Remmert all became carpenters, and Aaldrik's house was still a carpenter's house in the 1860s. Grietje married a Groningen painter and Albertje a smith's man from East Frisia.\n\nHe is entered in the first Sappemeer population register. Bouwina died in March 1835. He died at Sappemeer on 18 July 1843, aged 82; his death record names his parents as Pieter Derks and Grietje Jans. The name is written Hund as often as Hundt, and it later became fixed as Hundt.","src":[["Sappemeer population register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/mgr:98b717b6-89f5-45ee-a3ad-fc70f0ef1a46"],["Marriage of Jannes Hundt, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4bd80a50-012c-0a70-f2e9-b54d2a05dc5a"],["Marriage of Aaldrik, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb53da9b-f8e5-4ece-90dd-5a538665ff00"],["Baptism, Sappemeer 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b965e63b-9f52-0c44-9836-b934f8877586"],["Parents' marriage, Sappemeer 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666540c1-d7f7-dd60-64d7-39253f817ac0"],["Marriage, Sappemeer 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3dfce074-3fa6-daf0-9c0f-e4b63ba26b9b"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:644ddf22-0868-d258-c632-fc750af39f52"],["Baptism of Jannes, 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b3416a66-d6be-c42b-0c42-6bf182a10590"],["Birth of Remmert, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:de7978fd-98c8-3e87-6651-9dcdf86373ee"],["Death of Klaassien, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a4b717bf-e174-88fa-229a-5df52a32672e"],["Marriage of Grietje, Groningen 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:395832a5-5941-484a-8713-bcd975667663"],["Marriage of Albertje, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ef1ed0a-adda-924a-cf96-9a3f092fe887"],["Death record, Sappemeer 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:64740e5b-0bfe-ae68-0e2f-fbb6518f852f"]],"b":1760,"x":1843},
bouwina:{"n":"Bouwina Aaldriks Hes","d":"c. 1771–1835","b":1771,"x":1835,"s":"b","p":["aaldrikhes","albertjen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Sappemeer in the spring of 1771, the first child of the innkeeper and merchant Aaldrik Andries Hes and his second wife, Albertje Remmerts Niemeijer, who had married that January. Her given name was her grandmother's: Albertje's mother was Bouwke (Bouchien) Alberts. She had an older half-sister Rijkje and a half-brother Andries from her father's first marriage, a sister Klaaske (about 1776) and a brother Aaldrik Andries (1782), who went to sea as a sailor.\n\nOn 12 May 1793, aged 22, she married the Sappemeer carpenter Derk Pieters Hund. The burial of an Aaldrik Hes is entered at Sappemeer that August; both her father and her young brother bore that name. She had seven children over twenty-two years: Grietje (1794), Aaldrik (about 1797), Pieter (1801), Albertje (about 1805), Jannes (1808), Klaassien (about 1811) and Remmert (1816), who had the name of her mother's father, Remmert Tjarks. Klaassien died at eleven in 1822. Four sons became carpenters.\n\nHer sister Klaaske died in 1821 and her mother in 1823. She died at Sappemeer on 13 March 1835, aged 63 years and 11 months; Derk outlived her by eight years.","src":[["Death record, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:78b2f4f3-f875-e320-9884-21544141e092"],["Marriage of Jannes Hundt, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4bd80a50-012c-0a70-f2e9-b54d2a05dc5a"],["Marriage, Sappemeer 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3dfce074-3fa6-daf0-9c0f-e4b63ba26b9b"],["Birth of Remmert, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:de7978fd-98c8-3e87-6651-9dcdf86373ee"],["Burial of Aaldrik Hes, Sappemeer 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:489d88ac-5220-c3c1-04db-c2c1ad0e3864"],["Death of Derk Pieters Hund, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:64740e5b-0bfe-ae68-0e2f-fbb6518f852f"]]},
albert:{"n":"Albert Lues Gernaat","d":"1751–1818 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":["grluejocchums","graaltjealberts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Sappemeer on 14 February 1751, the son of Lue Jocchums and Aaltje Alberts, with the name of his mother's father. He worked all his life as a labourer in the peat colonies; one record calls him a farmer (landbouwer).\n\nIn December 1777, 'of Sappemeer', he married Antje Jacobs of Borgercompagnie. The banns were entered at Sappemeer on 23 December and the wedding was at Veendam on the 28th. Their first four children were baptised at Veendam: Luije (1778), Jakob (1780), Jan (1782) and Berent (1785). By 1787 the family had moved to Windeweer on the Kieldiep, where five more were baptised: Berend (1787), Aaltje (1789), another Berend (1792), Hemme (1795) and Emke (1798).\n\nBerend rose from farmhand to farmer at Kropswolde; Lue and Hemme stayed labourers at Windeweer, and Aaltje married a labourer there in 1817. Albert died at Windeweer on 12 November 1818, in his sixty-eighth year. The surname, taken in 1811, is written Gernaat and Garnaat, the Dutch word for a shrimp.","b":1751,"x":1818,"src":[["Death record, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:627550d3-8016-0e2a-5252-6b77928231bd"],["Baptism of Berend, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ae73b202-31bc-c426-056b-d9c679d294db"],["Death of Lue Alberts Gernaat, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e28c1184-0f16-fcf3-30d5-2aca33dd9aa8"],["Death of Berend, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1447e6c7-045e-3d74-ca54-af1190b2d150"],["Baptism, Sappemeer 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4126efe7-da67-8d9c-752b-2f7da59f3ceb"],["Banns, Sappemeer 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8940472c-c055-2072-c7e1-580d2c7e34b3"],["Marriage, Veendam 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2d3c4a90-dba2-7679-f236-a9db1af154d0"],["Baptism of Luije, Veendam 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4594bdad-a598-bb99-5592-0eaeb20dac20"],["Baptism of Jakob, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3ed479bb-224e-5911-cdce-2369cddeb7f8"],["Baptism of Jan, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:953b237c-2a48-01b0-54fc-d77f6bfa2ffa"],["Baptism of Berent, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:23f85209-80e5-cfb7-7444-d847f9d4f7a7"],["Baptism of Aaltje, Windeweer 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d9904d21-d01c-3c64-583e-d36b210168e6"],["Baptism of Emke (Hemke), 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0a47dc6c-4019-d576-dd08-95e73429400f"],["Marriage of Aaltje Alberts Garnaat, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0df728e8-69c1-66e0-005d-c9e79894bcba"]]},
antjej:{"n":"Antje Jacobs","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Borgercompagnie, a peat colony between Sappemeer and Veendam; the Veendam register calls her 'of Veendam'. She married Albert Luijes (Lues) of Sappemeer in December 1777: the banns were entered at Sappemeer on 23 December and they married at Veendam on the 28th.\n\nShe had nine children in twenty years. Luije (1778), Jakob (1780), Jan (1782) and Berent (1785) were baptised at Veendam, and Berend (1787), Aaltje (1789), another Berend (1792), Hemme (1795) and Emke (1798) at Windeweer, where the family had moved. Jakob carried her father's name.\n\nShe was a working woman (arbeidster) like most labourers' wives in the peat colonies. She was widowed in November 1818 and was still alive, and still working, when Emke married the Sappemeer carpenter Aaldrik Hundt in April 1822. Her son Berend's death record calls her a farmer's wife (landbouwersche).","src":[["Marriage of Emke, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb53da9b-f8e5-4ece-90dd-5a538665ff00"],["Death of Hemme Gernaat, 1869","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:980c3089-7260-81f0-f926-d56de5ce2bb4"],["Banns, Sappemeer 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8940472c-c055-2072-c7e1-580d2c7e34b3"],["Marriage, Veendam 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2d3c4a90-dba2-7679-f236-a9db1af154d0"],["Baptism of Luije, Veendam 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4594bdad-a598-bb99-5592-0eaeb20dac20"],["Baptism of Jakob, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3ed479bb-224e-5911-cdce-2369cddeb7f8"],["Baptism of Emke (Hemke), 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0a47dc6c-4019-d576-dd08-95e73429400f"],["Death of Berend, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1447e6c7-045e-3d74-ca54-af1190b2d150"]]},
laurensius:{"n":"Laurensius Holst","d":"Nordborg","s":"b","p":[],"r":["dk"],"t":"Father of the ship's mate Christen Holst. He is known only from his son's Amsterdam marriage record of 9 May 1832, where the clerk wrote his name as Laurensius, a Latin form of the Danish names Lauritz and Lars.\n\nChristen was born about 1793 at Nordborg, a small town in the north of the island of Als, beside Nordborg castle and its lake. A granddaughter born in Amsterdam about 1835 was named Bernardina Laurina.\n\nThe Nordborg church books of the 1790s survive, but only as page scans at the Danish National Archives; they have not been indexed by name. His son's baptism entry, and with it his trade and his own dates, still has to be looked up there by hand.\n\nHis name was passed on in Amsterdam: besides the granddaughter Bernardina Laurina (born 1834), a grandson born on 12 December 1836 was named Hendrikus Laurentius Holst.","src":[["Marriage of his son Christen, Amsterdam, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2A57B623-F124-49BC-BD2F-274F5B4FE795"],["Marriage of granddaughter Bernardina Laurina Holst, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:D48CDE4C-9F2A-4154-BFF5-825EA11C744A"],["Population register 1851-1853, Hendrikus Laurentius Holst","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:112c42f9-f6a4-4e2f-8ff4-814d78ac6114"]]},
metthe:{"n":"Metthe Christensdochter","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["dk"],"t":"Mother of the ship's mate Christen Holst, named in his Amsterdam marriage record of 1832 as Metthe Christensdochter. That is a Dutch clerk's version of the Danish Mette Christensdatter, 'Mette, daughter of Christen'. Most country people in Denmark at that time were known by their father's first name in this way instead of a fixed family name.\n\nHer son, born about 1793 at Nordborg on Als, was therefore named after her father. Her five granddaughters were born in Amsterdam between about 1833 and 1843, after Christen had settled there as a ship's mate; none of them was called Mette.\n\nNothing else about her has been found. The Nordborg church books of her time have not been indexed by name.","src":[["Marriage of her son Christen, Amsterdam, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2A57B623-F124-49BC-BD2F-274F5B4FE795"]]},
henricusv:{"n":"Henricus Verhoeckx","d":"warehouseman","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"A working man of Nijkerk and then Amsterdam. His daughter Hendrina was baptised in the Catholic church at Nijkerk on 25 February 1800. By March 1802 he and his wife, Joanna Antonia Gosseling, were in Amsterdam, where Bernardus (18 March 1802), Jacobus (4 April 1804) and Bernardina Sophia Geertruda (4 September 1806) were baptised at Het Haantje, the Catholic house church hidden in the attic of a canal house that is now the museum Our Lord in the Attic.\n\nThe records give him a different job each time one of his children married: labourer (arbeider) in 1831, warehouseman (pakhuisknecht) in 1832, an iron merchant's man (ijzerkopersknecht) in 1837 and servant or assistant (bediende) in 1846. His surname is written Verhoeck, Verhoeks, Verhoeckx and once Verkoekx.\n\nJacobus Verhoeck, who was godfather to Hendrina in 1800 and had married Catharina ten Brink in 1795, was probably his brother. A Gijsbert Verhoeks was godfather in 1806. His own baptism and marriage have not been found in the indexed Gelderland registers.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Hendrina, 1800 (Gelders Archief)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:980C7F68-15C2-4B57-B444-79FC5C045B11"],["Baptism of son Bernardus, Amsterdam, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:77e516a5-5ecb-4165-91b3-9f425e17da91"],["Baptism of son Jacobus, Amsterdam, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:1715c3f2-874f-4bcf-8b73-12bd71ec946a"],["Baptism of daughter Bernardina, Amsterdam, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:7f4d013f-cd85-43a5-9781-6b9caab4be81"],["Marriage of daughter Bernardina, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:DF89F799-F710-4436-B53E-E6B3659B6EF0"],["Marriage of daughter Hendrina, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2A57B623-F124-49BC-BD2F-274F5B4FE795"],["Marriage of son Bernardus, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:DD68177A-F908-42D5-9900-0C542D3A7BEC"],["Marriage of Jacobus Verhoeks and Catharina ten Brink, 1795","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:C67D04D9-F90E-46F5-9D6D-5F6760AF9BE9"]]},
joanna:{"n":"Joanna Antonia Gosseling","d":"","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Henricus Verhoeck. The priests wrote her name both ways round, Joanna Antonia and Antonia Joanna Gosseling.\n\nHer first known child, Hendrina, was baptised at Nijkerk on 25 February 1800. Three more were baptised in Amsterdam: Bernardus in 1802, Jacobus in 1804 and Bernardina Sophia Geertruda in 1806. Two people of her own surname stood as godparents there, Bernardus Gosseling in 1802 and Geertruij Gosseling in 1806, and two of her children carry their names.\n\nShe is named as the mother in the marriage records of Bernardina (1831), Hendrina (1832 and 1846) and Bernardus (1837). In those same years the surname Gosseling is found around Steenderen in the east of Gelderland, but her own baptism has not been identified.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Hendrina, 1800 (Gelders Archief)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:980C7F68-15C2-4B57-B444-79FC5C045B11"],["Baptism of son Bernardus, Amsterdam, 1802 (godfather Bernardus Gosseling)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:77e516a5-5ecb-4165-91b3-9f425e17da91"],["Baptism of daughter Bernardina, Amsterdam, 1806 (godmother Geertruij Gosseling)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:7f4d013f-cd85-43a5-9781-6b9caab4be81"],["Marriage of son Bernardus, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:DD68177A-F908-42D5-9900-0C542D3A7BEC"],["A Gosseling family at Steenderen, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:579A8B79-EDBF-4519-8ACC-789229FDD085"]]},
adriaan:{"n":"Adriaan de Kock","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tcwillemdekok",null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Adriaan de Kock was born at Tilburg. The register of his marriage calls him Adriaen Willem de Kok; the second name is a patronymic, the usual Brabant way of saying that his father was a Willem. The surname is spelled de Kok in the older records and de Kock in the later ones.\n\nHe married Johanna Span at Tilburg on 2 March 1783, after banns on 15 February. Five children are known: Anna Cornelia (baptised 6 February 1784), Maria (about 1786), Wilhelmus (9 September 1788), Adriana (19 August 1790) and Petrus (31 July 1793).\n\nWilhelmus married Johanna Verhoeven of Hilvarenbeek in 1817 and Petrus married Antonetta Peijnenborg in 1822; both records name Adriaan as the father. Maria, the wife of Peter van Gorp, died in 1827 aged 41. Cornelia, married to Cornelis de Rooij, died in 1841, and Adriana in 1847, aged 56.\n\nThe fuller copy of his marriage entry names the witnesses: Willem de Kok, the groom's father, and Adriaan Span, the bride's father. A Maria Willem de Kok (married 1785) and a Justinus Willem de Kok (married 1790), both born at Tilburg, were very likely his sister and brother; both had a stepfather, Johannes de Rooij, which suggests that his father died soon after 1783. His son Wilhelmus, named after the grandfather, died at Hilvarenbeek on 11 May 1870, aged 82.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:49e50d4b-152f-b051-2636-f9f6a4c91dc8"],["Baptism of daughter Anna Cornelia, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:30708f3e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Wilhelmus, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fa18de2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Adriana, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2f45e24e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Petrus, 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2eb940c8-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Wilhelmus, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:036eb30a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Maria, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9c06da52-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Cornelia, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a296c5b2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Adriana, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a51cb18e-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage with witnesses, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2169f9be-7d86-36ab-8706-4697f3f14680"],["Marriage of Maria Willem de Kok, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b0fdbb5a-c6dc-ccb6-b5c3-36b0b2526e59"],["Marriage of Justinus Willem de Kok, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b985ff30-d24c-597d-1703-8162ba1a0442"],["Death of son Wilhelmus, Hilvarenbeek, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:333aaaea-3865-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
johannas:{"n":"Johanna Span","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tcadriaanspan",null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Johanna Span was born at Tilburg and married Adriaan de Kock there on 2 March 1783; the banns had been entered on 15 February. In the Latin baptism register she appears as Joanna Span.\n\nShe had five known children in ten years: Anna Cornelia (1784), Maria (about 1786), Wilhelmus (1788), Adriana (1790) and Petrus (1793). Wilhelmus married in 1817 and Petrus in 1822. Her daughter Maria married Peter van Gorp and died in 1827, aged 41. Cornelia married Cornelis de Rooij and died in 1841. Adriana died in 1847 at 56; her death record names no husband.\n\nJohanna is named as the mother in each of these records, the last of them the death of her son Petrus in September 1860.\n\nThe fuller copy of her marriage entry names her father, Adriaan Span, as her witness. As Johanna Adriaan Span she made a will before a Tilburg notary in 1802 and signed acknowledgements of debt in 1806 and 1809, so she was still living in 1809. Her son Wilhelmus's death record of 1870 calls her Johanna van der Span.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:49e50d4b-152f-b051-2636-f9f6a4c91dc8"],["Baptism of son Petrus, 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2eb940c8-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Maria, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9c06da52-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Cornelia, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a296c5b2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Adriana, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a51cb18e-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Peter, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:abba49a2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage with witnesses, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2169f9be-7d86-36ab-8706-4697f3f14680"],["Notarial will, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2b1f58ae-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial acknowledgement of debt, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2b1f8144-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial acknowledgement of debt, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2b1faa70-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Wilhelmus, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:5f4b8987-c909-8372-92c1-72c1ed171735"]]},
janfp:{"n":"Jan Francis Peijnenborg","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Francis (Franciscus) Peijnenborg of Tilburg was the husband of Maria van den Hout and the father of Antonetta Peijnenborg. Five daughters of Franciscus Pijnenborg and Maria van den Hout were baptised at Tilburg: Joanna on 22 February 1787, Antonia on 2 December 1788, a second Antonia on 1 August 1798, Lucia on 18 April 1800 and Maria Elizabeth on 23 December 1802. The baptisms show that the couple had married about 1786. The priest wrote his name as Joannis Franciscus in 1798 and simply as Franciscus in 1800 and 1802. Antonetta's marriage and death records call him Jan Francis; her sisters' records call him Franciscus, and Joanna's marriage record of 1815, when she married Johannes Schapendonk, calls him Francus Pijnenborg.\n\nA Jan Francis Peijnenborg who left children was buried at Tilburg on 29 June 1798, and that burial was once attached to him. It does not fit: two more daughters were born to him and Maria in 1800 and 1802, and he was named as her husband when she was buried at Tilburg on 14 June 1809, aged 43. A second Joannis Franciscus Peijnenborg, married to Joanna de Kanter, was also having children baptised at Tilburg around 1800, which shows how easily the men are confused. His own death has not been identified.","src":[["Burial of a namesake, Tilburg, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fea21acc-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Antonia, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31f450b6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Lucia, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3226e512-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Maria Elizabeth, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3260923a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["The other J. F. Peijnenborg: baptism of Antonia Maria, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:32666caa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fe8b7b2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Antonia, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2f99a3ca-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:09c06e6a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of his wife, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:00fc5ad0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
mariavh:{"n":"Maria van den Hout","d":"c. 1766–1809 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria van den Hout was the wife of Jan Francis (Franciscus) Peijnenborg of Tilburg. Five daughters were baptised there: Joanna on 22 February 1787, Antonia on 2 December 1788, a second Antonia on 1 August 1798, Lucia on 18 April 1800 and Maria Elizabeth on 23 December 1802. The first Antonia evidently died young.\n\nThe four surviving daughters all married at Tilburg. Joanna married Johannes Schapendonk in 1815. Antonia, later called Antonetta, married Peter de Kock in 1822; Lucia married Petrus Jongbloets in 1827; and Maria Elizabeth married Peter de Brouwer in 1842. Maria is named as the mother in each marriage record and again when her daughters died, in 1861, 1876 and 1879. Van den Hout was a very common name in Tilburg, and her own baptism has not been singled out among the many women called Maria van den Hout.\n\nMaria van den Hout, wife of Francis Pijnenborg, was buried at Tilburg on 14 June 1809, aged 43, so she was born about 1766. Her daughter Joanna died in 1856, aged 69. The baptisms of 1787 and 1788 put her marriage about 1786. Her daughters were aged between 6 and 22 when she died.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Antonia, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31f450b6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Lucia, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3226e512-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Maria Elizabeth, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3260923a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Lucia, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:086fd474-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial, Tilburg, 14 Jun 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:00fc5ad0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fe8b7b2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Antonia, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2f99a3ca-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:09c06e6a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Johanna, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a9b406f2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"b":1766,"x":1809},
petervb0:{"n":"Peter van den Brekel","d":"1775–1855 · Tilburg","b":1775,"x":1855,"s":"b","p":["francis","catharinar"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Petrus at Tilburg on 10 December 1775, the sixth of seven known children of Francis van den Brekel and Maria Simons. About 1800 he married Elisabeth de Kort, who had been baptised at Tilburg three weeks after him. The two families were doubly linked: his sister Anna Cornelia married Elisabeth's brother Michiel de Kort.\n\nTheir first son, Franciscus Michael, was baptised at Tilburg on 29 September 1800. By 1805 they had moved to Goirle, where Joannes Baptista (1805), Peter (1807), Maria Catharina (1811) and Johanna Maria (1815) were born; the Goirle register also has a second baptism of a Joannes Baptista in 1810.\n\nHe outlived most of his family. Francis Michiel died in 1826 aged 25, his wife in 1831, Jan Baptist in 1837 aged 32 and Maria Catharina in 1838 aged 27. He died on 9 December 1855 at Loon op Zand, a village north of Tilburg where his granddaughter Elisabeth had married the year before.\n\nThe death record gives his age as 81 and his mother as Catharina Renders. Both are slips: he was just short of 80, and his baptism names Maria Simons. Renders was the surname of his daughter-in-law Huberta, Jan Baptist's widow.","src":[["Death record, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1099e6d2-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31d09662-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Franciscus Michael, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4d5840e3-da1c-f298-1ae5-d2ea810f4da9"],["Baptism of son Joannes Baptista, Goirle, 1805","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:428ad90e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of Joannes Baptista, Goirle, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:428b2c1a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Maria Catharina, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4a2785ee-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Johanna Maria, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fdbfa60-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Francis Michiel, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:7cf0682e-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Jan Baptist, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:116bb860-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Maria Catharina, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:116abc62-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of granddaughter Elisabeth, Loon op Zand, 1854","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:8c618ff0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Anna Cornelia, wife of Michiel de Kort, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9acfe7fa-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
elisabethk2:{"n":"Elisabeth de Kort","d":"1775–1831 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tbarnoldusdekort","tbcorneliasomers"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Elizabetha at Tilburg on 31 December 1775, the daughter of Arnoldus de Kort and Cornelia Somers. She had an elder sister Joanna Catharina (1770) and brother Michael (1773), and a younger brother Joannes (1778) and sister Petronilla (1784).\n\nAbout 1800 she married Peter van den Brekel, who was the same age and from the same town; her brother Michiel married Peter's sister. Her first son, Franciscus Michael, was baptised at Tilburg on 29 September 1800; the index to that entry gives the mother's first names as Maria Norberta. By 1805 the couple were at Goirle, where Joannes Baptista (1805), Peter (1807), Maria Catharina (1811) and Johanna Maria (1815) were born.\n\nHer father died at Tilburg in 1811 and her mother in 1818. Her eldest son died in January 1826, aged 25, a married man. Elisabeth died at Goirle on 10 May 1831, aged 55. Her husband lived another 24 years.","b":1775,"x":1831,"src":[["Baptism, Tilburg, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31ce2e7c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, Goirle, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1196c58c-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Franciscus Michael, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4d5840e3-da1c-f298-1ae5-d2ea810f4da9"],["Residents list: Michel de Kort and Cornelie van den Brekel","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1ed141a3-4b63-98c3-7687-23acc4f5141f"]]},
francis:{"n":"Francis Adriaan van den Brekel","d":"d. 1798 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Francis Adriaan van den Brekel married Maria Aart Sijmens at Oisterwijk on 3 February 1765. The middle names are patronymics: he was the son of an Adriaan and she the daughter of an Aart. In the Catholic south of the Dutch Republic the Reformed church was the only public church, so Catholic couples like them had to marry before the magistrates or the Reformed minister as well as before their own priest.\n\nTheir eldest known child, Maria, was born at Enschot about 1765. The next were baptised east of Tilburg: Adrianus at Berkel-Enschot on 26 January 1767 and Arnoldus at Oisterwijk in September 1769. By January 1772 the family was in Tilburg, where Petronilla (1772), Anna Cornelia (1773), Petrus (10 December 1775) and Cornelius (5 January 1779) were baptised.\n\nAnna Cornelia married Michiel de Kort and died at Tilburg in 1823; her death record names Francis as her father. Petrus married Michiel's sister Elisabeth de Kort and settled at Goirle. A Cornelius van den Brekel whose child was baptised at Waalwijk in 1812, with Michael de Kort and Cornelia van den Brekel as godparents, is probably the youngest son, since Michael de Kort was the husband of his sister Anna Cornelia.\n\nMaria married Gerardus Wouters and died at Tilburg on 7 January 1834, aged 69; her death record names her parents as Francis van den Brekel and Maria Simons. Francis made a will before a Tilburg notary in 1795 and another in 1798, and he was buried at Tilburg on 9 April 1798, leaving children. An inventory of his estate was drawn up that year, and his children divided the estate in notarial deeds of 1800 and 1802; his son Adriaan Francis van den Brekel appears in deeds of 1800 and 1804.","src":[["Marriage, Oisterwijk, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:71b25acc-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Adrianus, Berkel-Enschot, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:337594d6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Arnoldus, Oisterwijk, 1769","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:62be976a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Petronilla, Tilburg, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2bdaee74-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Anna Cornelia, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2c07e708-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Petrus, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31d09662-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:18643b9c-42e5-86b9-4755-f5858fff391e"],["Son Cornelius at Waalwijk, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:b672d79a-7d84-8206-7020-132a6e075c91"],["Death of daughter Maria, wife of Gerardus Wouters, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9f530604-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial will, 1795","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:23f46268-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial will, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:23f48a9a-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Estate inventory, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:23f4b614-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial, Tilburg, 9 Apr 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fec2d30c-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Division of the estate, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:27d86bb8-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Division of the estate, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:27d93cdc-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"x":1798},
catharinar:{"n":"Maria Simons","d":"Oisterwijk","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"The mother of Peter van den Brekel (1775–1855) was Maria Simons, not Catharina Renders. The name Catharina Renders comes only from Peter's death record, written at Loon op Zand in 1855 when he was about 80. His baptism at Tilburg on 10 December 1775 names his mother as Maria Simons, and Renders was the surname of his daughter-in-law.\n\nMaria married Francis Adriaan van den Brekel at Oisterwijk on 3 February 1765, where the register calls her Maria Aart Sijmens, the daughter of an Aart. Seven children are known. Maria was born at Enschot about 1765, soon after the wedding, and six were baptised: Adrianus (Berkel-Enschot, 1767), Arnoldus (Oisterwijk, 1769), and at Tilburg Petronilla (1772), Anna Cornelia (1773), Petrus (1775) and Cornelius (1779). The priests wrote her surname as Simons or Sijmens.\n\nShe is named again as the mother when Anna Cornelia, the wife of Michiel de Kort, died at Tilburg in September 1823 aged 49.\n\nHer eldest daughter Maria married Gerardus Wouters and died at Tilburg on 7 January 1834, aged 69; her death record names her mother as Maria Simons. Maria Simons was widowed when her husband was buried at Tilburg on 9 April 1798. Her own death has not been found.","src":[["Baptism of son Petrus, Tilburg, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31d09662-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Oisterwijk, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:71b25acc-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Anna Cornelia, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9acfe7fa-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record of son Peter, 1855 (names Catharina Renders)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1099e6d2-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of grandson Jan Baptist, husband of Huberta Renders, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:116bb860-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Maria, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9f530604-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of her husband, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fec2d30c-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
antonie:{"n":"Antonie de Rooij","d":"Goirle","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Antonie de Rooij was born at Goirle. The register of his marriage calls him Antonij Cornelis de Rooij, which shows that his father was a Cornelis. On 19 February 1798 he married Catharina van Gorp, also of Goirle; the civil ceremony was at Tilburg, because Tilburg and Goirle shared one bench of magistrates.\n\nFive children were born at Goirle over twenty years: Cornelia (baptised 15 September 1800), Aldegundis (13 February 1804), Johanna Cornelia (24 February 1808), Adrianus (19 August 1813) and Adriana Elisabeth (30 January 1821). Adriana Elisabeth died that November at ten months, and an Adriaan, son of the couple, died on 11 July 1822.\n\nHe is named as the father when his three daughters married at Goirle: Cornelia to Hendrik Snels in 1822, Aldegonda to Peter van den Brekel in 1829 and Johanna Cornelia to Johannes Woestenberg of Diessen in 1832. His wife died at Goirle in 1848.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5fb7412-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Cornelia, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:41e8c358-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Aldegundis, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:41e7f2ca-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Adrianus, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4d00f516-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Adriana Elisabeth, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:301addb6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of Adriana Elisabeth, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:e056da04-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Adriaan, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:e05eb6f2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Cornelia, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fd801f9c-3860-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna Cornelia, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fd80c906-3860-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
catharinavg:{"n":"Catharina van Gorp","d":"1776–1848 · Goirle","s":"b","p":["tbpetervangorp","tbaldegondaspaepen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Goirle on 4 June 1776, the daughter of Petrus van Gorp and Aldegundis Spaepen. Her godparents were Wilhelmus Spaepen and Joanna van Ravels. She had an elder sister Joanna (1773) and a younger brother Cornelius (1778). Her mother was buried at Goirle on 9 September 1794, when Catharina was 18.\n\nOn 19 February 1798, aged 21, she married Antonie de Rooij of Goirle. They had five children: Cornelia (1800), Aldegundis (1804), who carried the name of Catharina's mother, Johanna Cornelia (1808), Adrianus (1813) and Adriana Elisabeth (1821), born when Catharina was 44. The last died at ten months.\n\nHer three daughters married at Goirle in 1822, 1829 and 1832. Catharina died there on 15 January 1848, aged 71. Her sister Joanna, the wife of Peter van Dun, died at Goirle twelve weeks later, aged 74.","b":1776,"x":1848,"src":[["Baptism, Goirle, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42e9ddf0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism entry with godparents, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:34680d1d-44e2-930e-7077-db3af6dec8e5"],["Death record, Goirle, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:11d48188-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5fb7412-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Johanna, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:11d3d760-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
bernardus0:{"n":"Bernardus van den Berg","d":"c. 1784–1847 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tbantonievdberg","tbelisabethvloosbroek"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1784, the son of Antonie van den Berg and Maria Elisabeth van Loosbroek. His death record says he was born at Tilburg, but his baptism has not been found there: his parents had a daughter at Oss about 1779 and a son baptised at Hilvarenbeek in October 1781, and their first Tilburg baptism is from 1787. Tilburg in his lifetime was the main wool-cloth town of the Netherlands, and much of its spinning and weaving was done in workers' own homes.\n\nHe married Johanna Cornelia de Vries at Tilburg on 26 November 1810, after banns on 10 November. His father Antonij witnessed for him and Adriaan Heeren for the bride.\n\nThey had seven children: Maria Elisabeth (1811), Gerardina (1812), Antonius (about 1815), Johannes (1818), Johanna (1819) and two sons called Bernardus Hubertus (1822 and 1826). Johannes lived twenty days and the first Bernardus Hubertus died at two, on 25 December 1824. Maria Elisabeth married Hendricus Melis in 1833, Gerardina married Peter van Gerwen in 1838, and Johanna married Peter Arnoldus Ooms in April 1844. That August Antonius, a shoemaker (schoenmaker), married a Tilburg-born tradeswoman at Sas van Gent in Zeeland.\n\nBernardus died at Tilburg on 4 May 1847, aged 63.","b":1784,"x":1847,"src":[["Death record, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a54ca6fa-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:533d1b0b-3756-ff31-1d36-627c0d360f17"],["Marriage of daughter Maria Elisabeth, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:4ba52306-8eb1-21e1-94ed-62f3054c4ec9"],["Marriage of daughter Gerardina, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:04915530-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Johannes, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3757907e-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Johannes, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9911f282-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0491ac7e-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Antonius, Sas van Gent, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/zar:55367167-CB63-4629-8108-B0DB4FBCBB57"],["Baptism of brother Jacobus, Hilvarenbeek, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:49a4b246-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
johannac:{"n":"Johanna Cornelia de Vries","d":"1787–1860 · Tilburg","b":1787,"x":1860,"s":"b","p":["gerardusdv","idaj"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Joanna Cornelia at Tilburg on 15 June 1787, the third of six children of Gerardus de Vries and Ida Jonkers. Her father appears to have been buried in May 1797, when she was nine, and her mother did not marry again.\n\nOn 26 November 1810, aged 23, she married Bernardus van den Berg at Tilburg. Her sisters Johanna Maria and Adriana married within the next three years. She had seven children between 1811 and 1826. A son Johannes died at twenty days old in 1818 and a first Bernardus Hubertus at two in 1824; her last child, born when she was 38, was given the same names. Some of her children's records call her simply Cornelia.\n\nShe was widowed on 4 May 1847 and saw her youngest son marry in 1850. She died at Tilburg on 22 January 1860, aged 72. Her sisters also lived long: Johanna died in 1868 at 71, Johanna Maria in 1873 at 84 and Adriana in 1879 at 86.","src":[["Death record, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ab7d4fe8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fd7715a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:533d1b0b-3756-ff31-1d36-627c0d360f17"],["Death of sister Johanna, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b0e571ae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Johanna Maria, 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b4d15b7a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of sister Adriana, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:b86a3cf2-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
gerardus:{"n":"Gerardus Hamers","d":"1799–1849 · Tilburg","b":1799,"x":1849,"s":"b","p":["johanneshamers","geertruij"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Tilburg on 1 October 1799, the second child of Joannes Hamers and Geertrui Reijnen, who had married there in February 1797. His father died while he was small, and in July 1803 his mother married Augustinus Maurits. His sister Cornelia died in March 1815 aged 17.\n\nOn 25 November 1818, aged 19, he married Maria van Gestel, who was 33. Their first child, Johanna Cornelia, was born on 10 May 1819. Five more followed: Petronella (1821), Wouterus Hubertus (1824), Cornelia (1825), Johannes Hendrikus (1826) and Maria Gertruda (1829). His mother died in January 1829, shortly before the last was born.\n\nJohanna Cornelia married Hendricus Hoppenbrouwers in 1842. Petronella died in February 1843, aged 21. Gerardus died at Tilburg on 6 December 1849, aged 50. His two youngest children married soon afterwards, Maria Gertruda in 1850 and Johannes Hendrikus in 1851, and Johanna Cornelia married a second time in 1857.","src":[["Marriage, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d15bcae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a6497d94-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2dc38d40-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Johanna Cornelia, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:37aca87a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Petronella, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:39aaa6d6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Wouterus Hubertus, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:387fe848-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of daughter Cornelia, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:38e91c78-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Birth of son Johannes Hendrikus, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:3b2977bc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Petronella, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:382153be-d30f-58db-1100-1d2d45c6c87b"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna Cornelia, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a014f0c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Johannes Hendrikus, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:106086d8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Second marriage of daughter Johanna Cornelia, 1857","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a065bdc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
mariavg:{"n":"Maria van Gestel","d":"1785–1851 · Tilburg","b":1785,"s":"b","p":["petervg","johannacdl"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Tilburg on 13 September 1785, the eldest known child of Peter van Gestel and Johanna Catharina de Lepper, who had married there in November 1783. Twins, Adriana and Josephus, followed in January 1788, then Elisabeth (1789), Johanna (about 1794) and Maria Catharina (about 1801). A Tilburg residents list of about 1810 shows her still at home with her parents and three sisters.\n\nShe stayed single into her thirties. On 25 November 1818, aged 33, she married Gerardus Hamers, who was 19 and fourteen years her junior. Her father died in April 1820 and her mother in 1824. She and Gerardus had six children: Johanna Cornelia (1819), Petronella (1821), Wouterus Hubertus (1824), Cornelia (1825), Johannes Hendrikus (1826) and Maria Gertruda (1829), the last born when Maria was 43.\n\nHer daughter Petronella died in 1843, aged 21. Maria was widowed on 6 December 1849 and saw her two youngest marry in 1850 and 1851. She died at Tilburg on 28 December 1851, aged 66.","src":[["Marriage, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d15bcae-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:302c1a5c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1851","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:7c9d8a0f-74b0-018c-92a6-529573e0af08"],["Tilburg residents list, about 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c978c28d-7db4-fa29-6500-22d6271bf21e"],["Baptism of twin sister Adriana, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fbca622-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"x":1851},
herbert:{"n":"Herbert Anthony","d":"c. 1836–1897","b":1836,"x":1897,"s":"f","p":["davidanthony","annejones"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The son of David Anthony and Anne Jones, as his death registration names them. In the 1841 census a David Anthony, tailor, aged 50, and his wife Ann, 40, were living at Blanberach, in the Glynamman district of the parish of Llandeilo Fawr, Carmarthenshire, with their children William (14), Hannah (9), \"Harbert\" (7), Evan (5) and Margaret (2), all born in the county. It is the only household of a David and Ann Anthony with a son Herbert in the transcribed census, and very probably his family; the age of 7 puts his birth about 1834, a year or two earlier than his age at death suggests. His elder brother Thomas, born about 1829, was not at home that night. Glynamman lies in the Amman valley, on the edge of the South Wales coalfield. No record of his voyage out has been found.\n\nHe married Mara James in Victoria in 1867, and their two children were born at Sebastopol: Albert in 1869 and Matilda in 1873. Sebastopol, on the southern edge of Ballarat, was named during the Crimean War and lived on its deep leads, old river beds rich in gold that lay buried under basalt and were reached by company shafts. So many of its miners were Welsh that the town had Welsh chapels and held eisteddfods.\n\nBy June 1878 he was at Stawell, a quartz-reef gold town in western Victoria. In that month his brother Thomas, a coal miner who lived alone in a hut at Hamilton, near Newcastle, New South Wales, made a will in favour of \"his brother Herbert Anthony, of Stawell\". Thomas shot himself in February 1879, aged about 50, leaving a bank deposit receipt for £276 and some notes.\n\nIn February 1887 the Stawell Prospecting Board, which paid out government money to search for new gold, granted £100 to \"Herbert Anthony and party\" to prospect an old lead at Clifton Rock. In April 1888 the Ararat paper reported that he and his mates had struck a reef in the old Clifton Rock claim, stripped it for about ten feet and found a little gold in the drillings, and that July the board's list had £300 against his name.\n\nMara died at Stawell in 1893. Herbert died there on 26 December 1897, aged 61, and probate was granted on 5 July 1898. His will left everything to his two children, Albert and Matilda, but said the estate was not to be divided for eight years, and that if no tombstone had been put over his grave by then, his executors must spend between £10 and £30 on one. In 1901 a Melbourne judge ruled that once the tombstone was up, Albert and Matilda could have their shares straight away.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Stawell Prospecting Board, Age, 9 Feb 1887","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article197459719"],["Will case, Age, 20 Jul 1901","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190029801"],["Thomas Anthony, Sydney Morning Herald, 26 Feb 1879","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article13429904"],["Will and probate of Herbert Anthony, miner, Stawell, died 26 Dec 1897 (PROV, VPRS 7591/P2, 68/107)","https://prov.vic.gov.au/archive/6622B417-F536-11E9-AE98-4D14E6289853"],["Reef struck in the old Clifton Rock claim, Ararat Advertiser, 10 Apr 1888","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283861980"],["Ararat District Prospecting Board, Ararat Advertiser, 20 Jul 1888","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283866649"],["1841 census, Blanberach, Glynamman, Llandeilo Fawr, Carmarthenshire, HO107/1384 (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/67a90875b18f7c1bd0ecae11"]]},
mara:{"n":"Mara James","d":"c. 1840–1893","b":1840,"x":1893,"s":"f","p":["jamesevan","maryevans"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The daughter of James Evan James and Mary Evans, as her death registration names them. Mara is a Bible name: in the Book of Ruth, Naomi in her grief asks to be called Mara. She was born about 1840; no registration of her birth was found in the index for England and Wales.\n\nShe married Herbert Anthony, a gold miner, in Victoria in 1867. Their son Albert was born at Sebastopol, beside Ballarat, in 1869, and their daughter Matilda in 1873. By 1878 the family had moved about eighty miles north-west to Stawell, where Herbert went on mining and prospecting.\n\nShe died at Stawell in 1893, aged 53, when Albert was about 24 and Matilda 20. Herbert outlived her by four years.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
josephwil:{"n":"Joseph Wilson","d":"1836–1887 · Langley, Essex","b":1836,"x":1887,"s":"p","p":["johnwil","marthab"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Langley, a small Essex parish on the Hertfordshire border, on 17 March 1836 and baptised there on 22 May, the fifth child of John Wilson, then a dealer, and Martha Burgess. He was seven when the family landed at Port Phillip from the ship Royal Consort in February 1844, and he grew up on his father's farm above the Nillumbik lagoon at Diamond Creek, a small farming and gold-mining district north-east of Melbourne.\n\nAccording to the Nillumbik Historical Society, Joseph and his younger brother David made the first gold discovery at Diamond Creek in 1862, on land owned by Charles Orme and Dr Phipps. In 1866 he married Sarah Whatmough, daughter of the Plenty River orchardist Robert Whatmough, and the couple settled in a cottage on what is now Glen Park Road, Diamond Creek. They had eight children: John (1867), Martha (1869, Grandma Parky), Joseph (1871), Robert (1875), Rosalie (1877), Benjamin (1880), James Emmett (1883) and Charles Walter Burgess (1887).\n\nHe died at Diamond Creek in 1887, leaving Sarah with the family; the death entry gives his age as 48, though by his baptism he was 50. His youngest child, Charles, was registered the same year. In May 1893 his eldest son John, \"eldest son of the late Joseph Wilson, Diamond Creek\", married Sarah Jane Harrington of the same place at St Katherine's, St Helena.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Wilson–Harrington marriage, Age, 6 Jun 1893","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/193421412"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 22 May 1836 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64d90ca7f493fd2890b3b4f2"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Royal Consort, February 1844 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 2/3, pp. 163-166)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3AWILSON%20AND%20sams.description.arrival_date%3A1844*"],["Nillumbik Historical Society: Pioneer Families of Diamond Creek District, Wilson","https://www.nillumbikhistory.org.au/pioneer-families-of-diamond-creek-district-wilson/"],["A Pioneer Passes: Mrs Sarah Wilson, Advertiser (Hurstbridge), 3 August 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article56745828"]]},
sarahw:{"n":"Sarah Whatmough","d":"c. 1845–1934 · Greensborough","b":1845,"x":1934,"s":"p","p":["robertwh","maryhill"],"r":["au"],"t":"The daughter of Mary Hill and Robert Whatmough, the Rochdale-born fruit grower of the Plenty River whose apples won prizes in Melbourne in the 1850s and whose peach, Whatmough's Seedling, was grown across Victoria. Her obituary says she was born at her father's orchard on the bank of the Plenty at Greensborough in 1845, when Melbourne was ten years old, and that as a girl she went to the day school kept by a Mr Johnson at Greensborough. She grew up among his orchards near Greensborough and Diamond Creek.\n\nShe married Joseph Wilson in 1866, and the couple moved to a cottage on what is now Glen Park Road, Diamond Creek, where she lived for the rest of her life. They had eight children: John, known as Jack, the eldest son, Martha (Grandma Parky, born 1869), Joseph, Robert, Rose, Ben, James Emmett and Charles. Joseph died in 1887, leaving her with the family, the youngest born that year; in one bad snake season the local paper reported a snake killed at Mrs Joseph Wilson's at Diamond Creek.\n\nHer obituary remembered her physical strength, her kindness to neighbours in sickness or trouble, and a flower garden known for its snapdragons, which she dug herself until a few weeks before she died. She kept a box made from the timber of a heavily laden apple tree that was washed out of the Partington orchard in the great flood of 1878. She outlived her husband by 47 years and two of her sons, and died at Diamond Creek in 1934, aged 88.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Snakes at Diamond Creek, Evelyn Observer","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/64027537"],["A Pioneer Passes: Mrs Sarah Wilson, Advertiser (Hurstbridge), 3 August 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article56745828"]]},
johnwil:{"n":"John Wilson","d":"1798–1875 · Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":["pkjohnwilsonsr","pkmaryphilips"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Baptised at Langley, Essex, on 1 July 1798, the son of John Wilson and his wife Mary. Langley lies in the north-west corner of Essex, where the county meets Hertfordshire and Cambridgeshire. On 30 April 1825 he married Martha Burgess in Langley church; the entry gives no parish for her, but she was the Martha baptised at Furneux Pelham, just over the Hertfordshire border, in 1800. Both made their marks, and a James Wilson, probably his elder brother, was a witness. The baptism register follows his working life: he was a publican in 1835, a dealer in 1836 and a labourer in October 1843, when his two youngest children were baptised a few weeks before the family sailed.\n\nJohn, Martha and seven children, William, Elizabeth, John Burgess, Martha, Joseph, James and David, arrived at Port Phillip as bounty immigrants on the Royal Consort in February 1844. The Nillumbik Historical Society records that he opened a butcher's shop in Stephen Street, Melbourne, ran a carrying business to the Diamond Creek valley with his eldest son, and built a wattle-and-daub home above the Nillumbik lagoon. He bought the 86 acres there in October 1854 and another 104 acres later, and the place became known as Wilsons Crossing. He also took over Woodlands station in 1851 and leased the Christmas Hills run, renamed One Tree Hill, in 1853.\n\nMartha died in 1864. John died in 1875, a farmer of Diamond Creek. The death entry gives his age as 79 and his birthplace as Essex, though by his baptism he was 76 or 77 (the Royal Consort list in 1844 gave him as 44), and probate went to his sons William and John Burgess Wilson.","b":1798,"x":1875,"src":[["Baptism of John Wilson, Langley, 1 July 1798 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37e3f493fd767a9fa61e"],["Marriage of John Wilson and Martha Burgess, Langley, 30 April 1825 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64bc107bf493fd71b76d0981"],["Baptism of John Burgess Wilson (born 25 February 1833), Langley, 18 October 1835 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64d90ca6f493fd2890b3b4bf"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 22 May 1836 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64d90ca7f493fd2890b3b4f2"],["Baptism of David Wilson, Langley, 8 October 1843 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64d90cadf493fd2890b3b74d"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Royal Consort, February 1844 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 2/3, pp. 163-166)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3AWILSON%20AND%20sams.description.arrival_date%3A1844*"],["Nillumbik Historical Society: Pioneer Families of Diamond Creek District, Wilson","https://www.nillumbikhistory.org.au/pioneer-families-of-diamond-creek-district-wilson/"],["Victorian BDM index: death 1500/1875 (aged 79, born Essex, father John, spouse Martha Burgess)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Probate index: John Wilson, Diamond Creek, farmer, granted to W. Wilson and J. B. Wilson (PROV VPRS 28, 12/850)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A28%20AND%20family_name%3AWilson%20AND%20given_name%3AJohn%20AND%20text%3A%22Diamond%20Creek%22"]]},
marthab:{"n":"Martha Burgess","d":"1800–1864 · Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire","s":"p","p":["pkjohnburgess","pkmaryburgess"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Baptised at St Mary's, Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire, on 11 November 1800, the daughter of John and Mary Burgess of that village. She was the fifth of seven children baptised there between 1791 and 1805: John, Mary, William, a second John, Martha, Joseph and George. Her marriage entry gives no parish of origin, but hers is the only baptism of a Martha Burgess in the Hertfordshire and Essex transcripts between 1795 and 1804, and her Victorian death entry agrees on the county and on her mother's name, Mary.\n\nOn 30 April 1825 she married John Wilson at Langley, a few miles away over the Essex border. She signed the register with a mark, and the entry notes that the marriage was \"with consent of parents\". Her seven children were born at Langley between about 1826 and 1842; three of them, Elizabeth, John Burgess and Martha, were baptised together on 18 October 1835.\n\nShe was 43 when the family sailed for Port Phillip on the Royal Consort, with children aged from 17 down to one, and she made her home in the wattle-and-daub house above the Nillumbik lagoon at Diamond Creek. She died in 1864; her death entry gives her age as 65, though by her baptism she was 63 or 64, and her birthplace as Hertfordshire. The Nillumbik Historical Society records that she was buried on the family property and moved to the Nillumbik Cemetery in 1867. Her first name went to her granddaughter Martha Wilson, the family's Grandma Parky, and her surname to her son John Burgess Wilson and her grandson Charles Walter Burgess Wilson.","b":1800,"x":1864,"src":[["Baptism of Martha Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 11 November 1800 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379e8d86557439c20191fc"],["Marriage of John Wilson and Martha Burgess, Langley, 30 April 1825 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64bc107bf493fd71b76d0981"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Royal Consort, February 1844 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 2/3, pp. 163-166)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3AWILSON%20AND%20sams.description.arrival_date%3A1844*"],["Nillumbik Historical Society: Pioneer Families of Diamond Creek District, Wilson","https://www.nillumbikhistory.org.au/pioneer-families-of-diamond-creek-district-wilson/"],["Victorian BDM index: death 1059/1864 (aged 65, born Hertfordshire, father Burgess, mother Mary)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["FreeREG: Martha Burgess baptisms 1795-1804, Hertfordshire (one, Furneux Pelham 1800) and Essex (none)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"]]},
robertwh:{"n":"Robert Whatmough","d":"c. 1815–1887 · Rochdale","b":1815,"x":1887,"s":"p","p":["benjaminw","sarahbr"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"A Lancashire man from Rochdale, where his brother James was born, he was one of the early fruit growers of the Plenty River country north-east of Melbourne. By 1853 he was showing fruit at the Melbourne horticultural shows, taking a second prize for a general collection, and in February 1856 both the Age and the Argus singled out his apples, grown on the River Plenty and on show at Law and Gordon's in Melbourne, as proof of what the colony could grow: White Moss and Kentish Fill-basket apples averaging sixteen inches round.\n\nHe was a proud and plain-spoken grower. In March 1860, after showing twenty-five varieties of dessert, cooking and cider apples at the Victoria Agricultural Society, he wrote to the Age to complain that he should have had first prize, and that two of the judges were unfit for the job, having mistaken three of his russet apples for pears. The society later thanked him for accepting a certificate of merit instead of his prize money. In 1870 he showed four apples at the Horticultural Society, one a seedling of his own that the committee named the Greensborough Pippin, and a seedling plum named Whatmough's Prolific. A peach he raised, Whatmough's Seedling, was still on the recommended lists of peaches for Victoria in the 1880s.\n\nHe settled at Diamond Creek, beside Greensborough, where his daughter Sarah married Joseph Wilson in 1866, and he died there in 1887.\n\nHe was born in 1815 and, according to a local biography, went to work in a Rochdale cotton mill at the age of nine. He married Mary Hill at Manchester in the summer of 1839, and their first child, Jemima, was registered in the Bury district early in 1840. Later that year the three of them sailed on the Georgiana, which reached Port Phillip in February 1841; the immigration index enters the family as Wm Watmore, 24, Mary, 24, and Jemima, 1. The index gives his first name as William, but the wife's name, the child Jemima and the date of arrival all fit, and his death notice in 1887 called him a colonist of 47 years. In Melbourne he lived opposite John Batman's orchard on Batman's Hill and worked in the garden, and his death notice called him \"the first lamplighter in Victoria\". He then leased part of Willis Vale at Greensborough, on the Plenty, from his brother-in-law Charles Partington, the husband of his sister Ellen.\n\nThe Victorian Heritage Database describes him as a Chartist. He named a son Robert Emmett, the name of the Irish rebel executed in 1803, and it passed to his grandson James Emmett Wilson. Seven Whatmough and Partington children who died between 1848 and 1860 lie in the Plenty River Pioneer Children's Cemetery; his daughter Jessie died in 1858, aged seven. He served on the committee of the Horticultural Society of Victoria from 1859 to 1871. Mary died at Greensborough in 1881. Robert died at Diamond Creek on 25 September 1887, in his 72nd year, \"late of Willis Vale, Greensborough\", a colonist of 47 years and for 30 years a leading fruit grower. His brother James had died at Newbury in 1879 and his sister Ellen Partington lived at Greensborough until 1903.","src":[["Victorian BDM history search","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Apples from the Plenty, Age, 18 Feb 1856","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/154863675"],["Fruit from the Plenty district, Argus, 29 Feb 1856","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/4831954"],["Fair play: letter from Robert Whatmough, Age, 26 Mar 1860","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/154880205"],["Horticultural Society prizes, Argus, 5 Mar 1860","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/5678260"],["Greensborough Pippin and Whatmough's Prolific, Age, 3 Feb 1870","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/189326776"],["Whatmough's Seedling peach, Leader, 12 Jun 1880","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/196694398"],["FreeBMD: marriage of Robert Whatmough and Mary Hill, Manchester, Jul-Sep 1839 (vol. 20, p. 420); birth of Jemima Whatmough, Bury, Jan-Mar 1840 (vol. 21, p. 307)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Georgiana, February 1841 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 2, p. 365)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3AWATMORE"],["Death notice of Robert Whatmough, The Age, 8 October 1887","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article197159240"],["Plenty River Pioneer Children's Cemetery, Victorian Heritage Database","https://vhd.heritagecouncil.vic.gov.au/places/191836"],["Museums Victoria Collections: Willis Vale and the Batman apple tree","https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/articles/1631"],["Yarra Plenty Local History: Robert Whatmough, pioneering Victorian horticulturalist (book notice)","https://yplocalhistory.blogspot.com/2017/07/robert-whatmough-pioneering-victorian.html"],["Up the Creek Victoria: note on the book by Jacinta Crealy","https://www.upthecreekvictoria.com.au/1095-2/"]]},
maryhill:{"n":"Mary Hill","d":"c. 1813–1881","s":"p","p":["pkthomashill",null],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The wife of Robert Whatmough. Her death registration names her father as Thomas Hill. She married Robert at Manchester in the summer of 1839, and their daughter Jemima was registered in the Bury district early in 1840. Within the year she was at sea with a baby of nine months: the Georgiana reached Port Phillip in February 1841, and the immigration list gives her age as 24.\n\nShe spent forty years on the Plenty River at Greensborough, where Robert built up his orchards and nursery. Her children included Jemima (1840), Robert Emmett (about 1842), Sarah (1845, who married Joseph Wilson), Jessie (about 1851) and Rosalie (1856, registered at Collingwood). Jessie died in 1858, aged seven, and an infant grandson, Robert Emmett, in 1867. The Plenty River Pioneer Children's Cemetery at Greensborough holds seven Whatmough and Partington children who died between 1848 and 1860.\n\nShe died at Greensborough in 1881. The entry, indexed under the spelling \"Whatnough\", gives her age as 68, which puts her birth about 1813, three or four years earlier than the ship's list suggests.","b":1813,"x":1881,"src":[["FreeBMD: marriage of Robert Whatmough and Mary Hill, Manchester, Jul-Sep 1839 (vol. 20, p. 420); birth of Jemima Whatmough, Bury, Jan-Mar 1840 (vol. 21, p. 307)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Assisted immigrants, ship Georgiana, February 1841 (PROV VPRS 14, Book 2, p. 365)","https://api.prov.vic.gov.au/search/query?wt=json&q=series_id%3A14%20AND%20family_name%3AWATMORE"],["Plenty River Pioneer Children's Cemetery, Victorian Heritage Database","https://vhd.heritagecouncil.vic.gov.au/places/191836"],["Up the Creek Victoria: note on the book by Jacinta Crealy","https://www.upthecreekvictoria.com.au/1095-2/"],["Victorian BDM index: death 10633/1881 (Greensborough, aged 68, father Thomas Hill, spouse Robert); birth of Roseley Whatmough 10670/1856; deaths of Jessie 5391/1858, Robert Emmett (infant) 10475/1867, Robert Emmett 11850/1924, Rosalie Leach 3496/1925","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
benjaminw:{"n":"Benjamin Whatmough","d":"Rochdale, Lancashire","s":"p","p":["pkrobertwhatmough","pkjanewhatmough"],"r":["uk"],"t":"A weaver of Rochdale, Lancashire, and the father of Robert Whatmough. His name and that of his wife, Sarah Brierly, are given in the Victorian death registrations of three of their children: James (1879), Robert (1887) and Ellen Partington (1903). He married Sarah Brearley, as the clerk spelt it, at St Leonard's, Middleton, on 21 November 1813; he was a weaver, both were \"of this parish\", and both signed with a mark.\n\nThe couple lived at Brimrod Lane End in Castleton, on the south-west side of Rochdale, where Benjamin was still a weaver when their daughters Jane and Ann were baptised at St Chad's in 1821 and 1830. Their son Robert was born in 1815, James about 1822 and Ellen about 1829; no baptisms have been found for those three. Rochdale was a woollen and flannel town turning to cotton in these years, and weaving at home was giving way to the mills, where Robert was sent to work as a child.\n\nHe was probably the Benjamin baptised at St Chad's on 2 March 1789, son of the late Robert Whatmough, a dyer. A second Benjamin Whatmough, a tinner of Toad Lane, was raising a family in Rochdale at the same time, but he was 43 when he was buried in February 1842, so he was born about 1799 and cannot be the child baptised in 1789. That baptism is the only one of a Benjamin Whatmough found in Rochdale between 1775 and 1800, and it fits a weaver who married in 1813 at about 24. The link is still marked as uncertain because no record names the weaver's father. No burial of Benjamin the weaver, or of his wife, has been found in the Lancashire transcripts up to 1870, and no baptisms of Robert, James or Ellen appear in any of the Rochdale church or chapel registers transcribed so far.","u":true,"src":[["Marriage of Benjamin Watmough and Sarah Brearley, Middleton, 21 November 1813 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Benjamin&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=mar&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1810&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Baptisms of Jane (1821) and Ann (1830) Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=Benjamin&FatherSurname=&event=&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Sarah&MotherSurname=&year=1825&period=20&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Baptism of Benjamin Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale, 2 March 1789 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Benjamin&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1785&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Victorian BDM index: deaths 3824/1879, 9872/1887, 13561/1903 (father Benjamin Whatmough, mother Sarah Brierly)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Lancashire OPC: burial of Benjamin Whatmough, 43, Packer Meadow, St Chad, Rochdale, 25 February 1842; Whatmough baptisms 1805-1845","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"]]},
sarahbr:{"n":"Sarah Brierly","d":"","s":"p","p":["pkjamesbrearley","pkmarybrearley"],"r":["uk"],"t":"The wife of Benjamin Whatmough and mother of Robert. She married as Sarah Brearley, spinster, at St Leonard's, Middleton, on 21 November 1813, signing with a mark; the couple married by banns \"with consent of parents\", which suggests that she was not yet 21. Her children's Victorian death registrations spell the name Brierly or Brierley, and the Rochdale baptism register calls her Sarah in 1821 and Sally in 1830. She raised at least five children at Brimrod Lane End, Castleton: Robert (born 1815), Jane (1821), James (about 1822), Ellen (about 1829) and Ann (1830). Three of them, Robert, James and Ellen, emigrated to Victoria.\n\nShe was probably the Sarah Brearley born at Brimrod Lane End on 7 November 1794, daughter of James and Mary Brearley, and entered in the register of the West Street Baptist chapel in Rochdale on 21 January 1795. The same small hamlet, the right age (nineteen at marriage) and a dissenting family, which may explain the missing Church of England baptisms of three of her children, all point that way. The name was common, however: more than a dozen other girls named Sarah Brearley or Brierley were baptised in Rochdale and the neighbouring chapelries between 1790 and 1800, to fathers named James, John, William, David, Robert and others. No record states which was she, so the link to her parents is marked as uncertain and rests mainly on Brimrod Lane End.\n\nA different Sarah Brierley lived in Rochdale at the same time and should not be confused with her. That Sarah, daughter of Charles and Martha Brierley, married Edmund Whatmough, a whitesmith, and their children, including a Robert born on 25 February 1824, were baptised at the Union Street Wesleyan chapel from 1820. Our Sarah's husband was Benjamin and her son Robert was born in 1815.","u":true,"src":[["Marriage of Benjamin Watmough and Sarah Brearley, Middleton, 21 November 1813 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Benjamin&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=mar&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1810&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Baptisms of Jane (1821) and Ann (1830) Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=Benjamin&FatherSurname=&event=&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Sarah&MotherSurname=&year=1825&period=20&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Births of the children of James and Mary Brearley, West Street Baptist, Rochdale, 1791-1805 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Brearley&FatherFirst=James&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Mary&MotherSurname=&year=1795&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Lancashire OPC: baptisms at Union Street Wesleyan, Rochdale, 1820-1831, children of Edmund Whatmough and Sarah (formerly Brierley)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"]]},
howard:{"n":"Howard Baldwin","d":"1844–1928 · Stowupland, Suffolk","x":1928,"s":"y","p":["howard0","elizabethbr"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Stowupland, beside Stowmarket in Suffolk, in the last months of 1844, the eldest son of Howard Baldwin and Elizabeth Bristo. His father was a poulterer at Thorney Green, Stowupland, and later farmed a hundred acres at Forward Green, Earl Stonham.\n\nHe married Anna Haill, a carpenter's daughter from Earl Stonham, in the Bosmere district in the summer of 1864. Their first child, Ellen, was born at Earl Stonham that year, their son Howard Bristo at Islington in London late in 1866, and Bessie back at Earl Stonham in 1868. By 1871 he was farming forty acres at Old Newton, near Stowmarket, employing two men and a boy; Frank Herbert (1870), Albert (1872), Bruce (1873) and Kate Anna (1875) were born in that district.\n\nIn 1882 he brought the whole family out to Queensland: Howard, 37, Anna, 36, and their seven children arrived on the Mairi Bhan on 28 November 1882. By the mid-1880s he was in business in Fortitude Valley, Brisbane, as a poultry and egg merchant, and later a wholesale poulterer, in Wickham Street. He advertised as far away as the Warwick Argus for country farmers to send him their fowls and fresh eggs by rail to the Brunswick Street station, promising \"prompt returns, highest prices, no expenses deducted\", and he regularly sued customers in the Small Debts Court for unpaid poultry bills. The family lived in Alfred Street, in the Valley, where their daughter Bessie died in August 1889, aged 20.\n\nHe died on 9 March 1928 at his home, Harwich, in Bayview Terrace, Eagle Junction, aged 83, and was buried privately in Toowong Cemetery; the cemetery record gives the same date, 9 March 1928. One of his sons was named Howard Bristo Baldwin, after Howard's mother's family.","src":[["Advertisement, Warwick Argus, 25 Feb 1890","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/76983311"],["Advertisement, Warwick Argus, 22 Nov 1890","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/76985372"],["Funeral of Bessie Baldwin, Brisbane Courier, 10 Aug 1889","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/3499795"],["Death of Howard Bristo Baldwin, Courier-Mail, 15 Jun 1949","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/49686427"],["FreeBMD: birth of Howard Baldwin, Stow district, Dec quarter 1844, vol 12 p 424; marriage, Bosmere district, Sep quarter 1864, vol 4a p 815","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1871 census, Old Newton: farmer of 40 acres (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a942f0df4040b56892575f8"],["Passenger list, Mairi Bhan, 28 Nov 1882: Howard Baldwin, 37, wife and seven children (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR38800"],["Death, Qld 1928/B/3956 (parents Howard Baldwin and Elizabeth Bristo)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/ab77662258e90d7d86c47a57fc38f7b8b34d0710dcace4ec24fb29842b1c80d0"],["Death notice, Brisbane Courier, 10 Mar 1928","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article21231411"]],"b":1844},
annahaill:{"n":"Anna Haill","d":"1846–1939 · Earl Stonham, Suffolk","x":1939,"s":"y","p":["williamhaill","annapawsey"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Earl Stonham, Suffolk, early in 1846, the youngest known child of William Haill, a carpenter, and Anna Pawsey; her birth was registered as Anna Hail. In 1861 she was 15 and still at school, at home with her parents. Her mother died early in 1867.\n\nShe married Howard Baldwin, a farmer's son from Forward Green in the same parish, in the summer of 1864, and had seven children in eleven years: Ellen, Howard Bristo, Bessie Kate, Frank Herbert, Albert, Bruce and Kate Anna. In 1871 they were farming at Old Newton, and her widowed father, then 68, was staying with them. She was 36 when the family arrived in Queensland on the Mairi Bhan on 28 November 1882, and Howard became a poultry and egg merchant in Fortitude Valley, Brisbane.\n\nIn August 1889 they buried their daughter Bessie from their home in Alfred Street, in the Valley. Anna outlived Howard by eleven years. She died on 30 September 1939 at Bayview Terrace, Eagle Junction, \"in her 94th year\", and is buried with him in Toowong Cemetery. Bayview Terrace was also the street of Ryecroft, the Warbrick home, where her grandson Bruce Brazier's wedding reception had been held in 1929. Six of her seven children outlived her.","src":[["Death notice, Courier-Mail, 2 Oct 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/40898124"],["Funeral of Bessie Baldwin, Brisbane Courier, 10 Aug 1889","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/3499795"],["FreeBMD: birth of Anna Hail, Bosmere district, Mar quarter 1846, vol 12 p 335; marriage of Anna Haill, Bosmere district, Sep quarter 1864, vol 4a p 815","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1861 census, Earl Stonham: Anna Hail, 15, scholar (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/62836856f493fd5685d4ad20"],["1871 census, Old Newton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a942f0df4040b56892575f8"],["Passenger list, Mairi Bhan, 28 Nov 1882: Anna Baldwin, 36 (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR38800"],["Death, Qld 1939/B/45498 (parents William Haill and Anna Pawsey)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d76367cd93c8012954b7cb8dc263e525452f797b27b85fdf2bd4a899397c6167"]],"b":1846},
howard0:{"n":"Howard Baldwin","d":"c. 1810–1868 · Barham, Suffolk","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Barham, a village north of Ipswich in Suffolk, about 1810. In the census of June 1841 he was a poulterer, aged about 25, living at Thorney Green in Stowupland, beside Stowmarket, with a man servant and a woman servant, Elizabeth Bristo. He married her in the Ipswich district between April and June 1844. A poulterer dealt in fowls, eggs and game, and his son Howard followed the same trade in Brisbane forty years later.\n\nTheir children were all born at Stowupland: Howard (1844), Arthur (about 1847), Robert (about 1849), Kate (about 1851) and Herbert (about 1853). By 1861 he had turned to farming, with a hundred acres at Forward Green, Earl Stonham, where he employed four men and three boys and kept a housemaid.\n\nHe died late in 1868 in the Bosmere district, which covered Earl Stonham. The death index gives his age as 60; the 1861 census, which put him at 50, points to a birth two or three years later.","b":1810,"x":1868,"src":[["1841 census, Thorney Green, Stowupland: Howard Baldwin, poulterer, with Elizabeth Bristo, servant (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6890c8e6b18f7c93828cd82e"],["1861 census, Forward Green, Earl Stonham: farmer of 100 acres (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/62836855f493fd5685d4ac83"],["FreeBMD: marriage of Howard Baldwin and Elizabeth Bristo, Ipswich district, Jun quarter 1844, vol 12 p 512; death of Howard Baldwin, Bosmere district, Dec quarter 1868, aged 60, vol 4a p 348","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of his son Howard, Qld 1928/B/3956, naming both parents","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/ab77662258e90d7d86c47a57fc38f7b8b34d0710dcace4ec24fb29842b1c80d0"]]},
elizabethbr:{"n":"Elizabeth Bristo","d":"b. c. 1815 · Kirton, Suffolk","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1815 at Kirton, a village between Ipswich and Felixstowe in Suffolk. In June 1841 she was a servant, aged about 20, in the house of Howard Baldwin, a poulterer at Thorney Green, Stowupland. She married him in the Ipswich district between April and June 1844, and their first child, Howard, was born at Stowupland late that year. Four more followed there: Arthur, Robert, Kate and Herbert.\n\nBy 1861 she was a farmer's wife at Forward Green, Earl Stonham, aged 46, with the four younger children at home and a housemaid; her eldest, Howard, then 16, was away from home on census night. He married Anna Haill, from the same village, in 1864, and Elizabeth was widowed late in 1868.\n\nHer surname was kept in the family. Her grandson Howard Bristo Baldwin, born in London in 1866, carried it to Queensland, and he gave it in turn to his son Herbert Bristo Baldwin, born there in 1900.","b":1815,"src":[["1841 census, Thorney Green, Stowupland: Elizabeth Bristo, servant (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6890c8e6b18f7c93828cd82e"],["1861 census, Forward Green, Earl Stonham: Elizabeth Baldwin, 46, born Kirton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/62836855f493fd5685d4ac83"],["FreeBMD: marriage of Elizabeth Bristo and Howard Baldwin, Ipswich district, Jun quarter 1844, vol 12 p 512","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of her son Howard, Qld 1928/B/3956, naming both parents","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/ab77662258e90d7d86c47a57fc38f7b8b34d0710dcace4ec24fb29842b1c80d0"]]},
williamhaill:{"n":"William Haill","d":"b. c. 1804 · Earl Stonham, Suffolk","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"A carpenter of Earl Stonham, a village in mid Suffolk a few miles east of Stowmarket, where he was born about 1804. The 1841 census lists him there, aged about 35, with four children: Susanna, 13, Ellen, 11, Arthur, 9, and William, 3; his wife was not in the house that night. Another carpenter of the same surname, Stephen Haill, aged about 50, lived in the same village.\n\nHis wife was Anna Pawsey, from Stowupland, and their youngest known child, Anna, was born early in 1846. In 1861 William, 56, his wife, 57, and their daughter Anna, 15, were at home in Earl Stonham with a visiting niece, Catherine Parsey, 13, from Swilland. Anna married Howard Baldwin in 1864, his wife died early in 1867, and a William Haill aged 30, very likely their son, died in the district in 1868.\n\nIn April 1871 he was a widower of 68, still described as a carpenter, staying with his daughter Anna and her husband on their farm at Old Newton. No later record of him has been found. His granddaughter Ellen was six that year; eleven years later she sailed for Queensland with her parents.","b":1804,"src":[["1841 census, Earl Stonham: Wm Haill, 35, carpenter (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/687a54f6b18f7c3c71bbd3d1"],["1861 census, Earl Stonham: William Hail, 56, carpenter (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/62836856f493fd5685d4ad20"],["1871 census, Old Newton: William Hails, visitor, widower, 68, carpenter (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5a942f0df4040b56892575f8"],["Death of his daughter Anna Baldwin, Qld 1939/B/45498, naming both parents","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d76367cd93c8012954b7cb8dc263e525452f797b27b85fdf2bd4a899397c6167"]]},
annapawsey:{"n":"Anna Pawsey","d":"c. 1804–1867 · Stowupland, Suffolk","s":"y","p":["bzjohnpawsey","bzannquinting"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Stowupland, beside Stowmarket in Suffolk, about 1804. She married William Haill, a carpenter of Earl Stonham, a few miles to the east. Their children known from the censuses were Susanna (about 1828), Ellen (about 1830), Arthur (about 1832), William (about 1838) and Anna (1846). Her maiden name comes from the Queensland death registration of her daughter Anna Baldwin in 1939, which names her parents as William Haill and Anna Pawsey.\n\nHer own baptism has probably been found. On 15 September 1803 an Anna, daughter of John and Ann, was baptised at Stowupland; the register gives the mother's maiden name as Quinting, and the online transcription supplies the father's surname as Pawsey in square brackets, which means it is not clearly written in the register. The place and year fit her exactly, but because of that uncertain surname her parents are marked here as unproven.\n\nShe was not at home when the census was taken in June 1841, when her husband was alone with four children. In 1861 she was 57, living at Earl Stonham with William and their daughter Anna, a scholar of 15. A niece, Catherine Parsey, aged 13 and born at Swilland, was visiting; the niece's surname is a spelling close to Anna's own maiden name.\n\nHer daughter Anna married Howard Baldwin in the summer of 1864. Anna Haill died in the Bosmere district early in 1867, aged 63.","b":1804,"x":1867,"src":[["1861 census, Earl Stonham: Anna Hail, wife, 57, born Stowupland (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/62836856f493fd5685d4ad20"],["1841 census, Earl Stonham: husband and children (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/687a54f6b18f7c3c71bbd3d1"],["FreeBMD: death of Anna Haill, Bosmere district, Mar quarter 1867, aged 63, vol 4a p 389","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of her daughter Anna Baldwin, Qld 1939/B/45498, naming both parents","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d76367cd93c8012954b7cb8dc263e525452f797b27b85fdf2bd4a899397c6167"],["Baptism of Anna, daughter of John [Pawsey] and Ann (born Quinting), Stowupland (St Peter, Stowmarket register), 15 Sep 1803 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68572187865574b5db3f887a"]],"u":true},
thomasmoore:{"n":"Thomas Moore","d":"Oldham","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"A joiner of Oldham, Lancashire, named as Martha Moore's father when she married Henry Warbrick at Holy Trinity Church, Oldham, on 12 March 1865. A joiner made the finished woodwork of buildings, such as doors, window frames and stairs. Martha was then 22 and working as a cotton weaver in Coldhurst, on the north side of the town.\n\nHe is named again, as Thomas Moore, on the Queensland register entry for Martha's death at sea in August 1874. His three grandchildren, Lucy, Ernest and Frank Warbrick, landed in Brisbane a month later as orphans. No baptism for Martha naming him has been found in the Oldham registers transcribed online, and with so common a name he cannot yet be picked out in the census.","src":[["Marriage of his daughter, Holy Trinity, Oldham, 12 Mar 1865 (FreeREG record)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683f73bb865574024b6cfa73"],["Death of Martha Warbrick at sea, Qld 1874/M/1243 (father Thomas Moore)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/809d808b5c4e8c83e5624ce46f47cb38a4b667ba6bebeb3eae8c0b5926db7080"]]},
victor:{"n":"Victor White","d":"m. 1872 Queensland","s":"y","p":[],"r":["au"],"t":"Married Mary Stewart McGregor in Queensland on 14 September 1872; their daughter Adele was born the next August.\n\nThe only Victor White in Brisbane's newspapers of the 1870s was an ironmonger who lived in North Brisbane and kept a shop in Queen Street, opposite the Government Savings Bank, selling ironmongery and musical instruments. A wedding was held at his house at the end of 1874. In 1877 he was fined for setting out goods on the footpath, Mrs Victor White sailed for Sydney on the Tambaroora in March, and in November the Supreme Court declared him insolvent. No Queensland death has been found for him.\n\nThe Queensland registers give Victor and Mary three children in these years: Adele Janet (8 August 1873), Ernest Henry Victor (29 December 1875) and Ethel (27 October 1877). No Queensland birth registration has been found for the next three, Stuart Loftus, Eugenie and Muriel Stella. The family was in Queensland again by 19 November 1888, when a last daughter, Ilma Chloe, was born with Victor named as her father; she died on 18 September 1889. When his son Stuart died in Brisbane in 1928 the register called the father Victor Henry White. Mary's death notice in 1927 describes her as late of Sydney and does not mention him, and no record of his own death has been found.","src":[["Victor White, ironmonger, advertisement, The Telegraph, 25 Apr 1877","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22Victor%20White%22%20ironmonger"],["Insolvency notice, The Brisbane Courier, 3 Nov 1877","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1367585"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Marriage, Qld 1872/B/3682","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d08a6428841351a6b8404e2bd23ef52006e99428eca216975597634860e91b8a"],["Birth of Adele Janet White, Qld 1873/B/16292","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/aa2b3b8f8ab9ec392162321ae83bcf7505d9b98c26f4e2e8741250784c19ca9d"],["Birth of Ernest Henry Victor White, Qld 1876/B/20196","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/464844ba8587d923335b799c3fb78e6fcb68fcbe3671e232810be3e218b1055f"],["Birth of Ethel White, Qld 1877/B/22781","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/cb4daf6c8a19d7fc5b6a517744bc04b6b5b6f215458506dc6997d5d480f8f38d"],["Birth of Ilma Chloe White, Qld 1889/C/8940","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d43d34c001e33cdf5d5b5197a26b5c0a547984de4257ada4562408a77fa198dc"],["Death of Stuart Loftus White, Qld 1928/B/4738","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/cc2d51d70b64417aa66275b5fa062068270477998b04a8e8e4e262bea1ae39d7"],["Birth of Stewart Loftus White, NSW 1867/1880 (Sydney; father Victor, mother Mary S)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/lifelink/familyhistory/search"]]},
marys:{"n":"Mary Stewart McGregor","d":"c. 1851–1927 · Brisbane","x":1927,"s":"y","p":["alexmc","janetb"],"r":["au"],"src":[["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Death notice, Queensland Figaro, 19 Mar 1927","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article84900359"],["Marriage, Qld 1872/B/3682","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d08a6428841351a6b8404e2bd23ef52006e99428eca216975597634860e91b8a"],["Death, Qld 1927/B/584 (parents Alexander McGregor and Janet Buchanan)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/49ae127c197d6cc69c58b40c16ec7e31699e3c2034d11e897ac5fac03419e15f"],["Death of her brother John McGregor, Qld 1906/B/7096","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/13deb11912261de7e8db93e607b0db9424e416f6c5b9521315ab1a64ad9fcb4e"],["Death of her brother Stewart Young McGregor, Qld 1921/B/34731","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/da148b382c2ea7287b7fbd4bb8b4bf8092ca6713a5bc128565016a9da8a0fbac"],["Death of Ilma Chloe White, Qld 1889/C/4117","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7d926cbb097952e30b4552b97a9f9a997a1c4666a315bfd20fc17f920e45872a"],["Death of Muriel Stella White, Qld 1975/B/62881","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a5284f6feba0cabcab20f59a78360d2af0324833d4b6a038c67ebdb354432090"],["Birth of Stewart Loftus White, NSW 1867/1880 (Sydney; father Victor, mother Mary S)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/lifelink/familyhistory/search"],["Burial of Janet McGregor, aged 80, Toowong Cemetery, 10 Dec 1899 (grave Mon 12-42-33)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31790071"],["Funeral notice of Stewart Y. McGregor, Brisbane Courier, 13 Jun 1921","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article20459711"],["Burial of Stewart Young McGregor, aged 65, Toowong Cemetery, 13 Jun 1921","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31710824"]],"t":"Born about 1851, the daughter of Alexander McGregor and Janet Buchanan; her death notice in March 1927 put her in her 76th year. Two brothers also lived and died in Queensland: John McGregor (about 1850 to 1906) and Stewart Young McGregor (about 1855 to 1921). The family was in Queensland by 1868.\n\nShe married Victor White in Queensland on 14 September 1872. Their first three children were registered there: Adele Janet (1873), Ernest Henry Victor (1875) and Ethel (1877). The family then lived in Sydney, where Stewart (or Stuart) Loftus was born in 1880, Eugenie Mary in 1882 and Muriel Stella in 1885. A last daughter, Ilma Chloe, was born in Queensland on 19 November 1888 and died ten months later.\n\nShe later lived in Sydney, and died at St Martin's Hospital, Brisbane, on 10 March 1927, in her 76th year. Her death notice names four daughters: Mrs F. Warbrick (Adele), Mrs T. Latcham (Ethel, who had married George Arthur Thomas Latcham in 1901), and Eugenie and Muriel White. Her mother Janet had died in Brisbane in December 1899 and was buried at Toowong; her brother Stewart died at his home in Spring Hill in June 1921 and was buried at Toowong too. Her son Stuart died in Brisbane the year after her, and Muriel, who never married, in 1975.","b":1851},
alexmc:{"n":"Alexander McGregor","d":"","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"The father of Mary Stewart McGregor, named with his wife Janet Buchanan on Mary's Queensland death registration in 1927. The same couple are named as the parents of John McGregor, born about 1850, who died in Queensland on 26 July 1906, and of Stewart Young McGregor, born about 1855, who married Mary Jane Paine in 1883 and died on 11 June 1921. Mary herself was born about 1851.\n\nTwo more Queensland death registrations name Alexander McGregor and Janet Buchanan as parents, both for a son called Alexander: one who died on 20 November 1868 and one who died on 16 January 1873. The family was therefore in Queensland by 1868, four years before Mary married Victor White there. No McGregor family of this make-up appears in the index of assisted immigrants to Queensland before 1872; that index does not cover people who paid their own fares or came from another colony. Where Alexander was born, and when he died, has not been found.","src":[["Death of his daughter Mary Stewart White, Qld 1927/B/584","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/49ae127c197d6cc69c58b40c16ec7e31699e3c2034d11e897ac5fac03419e15f"],["Death of his son John McGregor, Qld 1906/B/7096","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/13deb11912261de7e8db93e607b0db9424e416f6c5b9521315ab1a64ad9fcb4e"],["Death of his son Stewart Young McGregor, Qld 1921/B/34731","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/da148b382c2ea7287b7fbd4bb8b4bf8092ca6713a5bc128565016a9da8a0fbac"],["Death of Alexander McGregor, Qld 1868/B/4728","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/bdd8770b4fedccaba2338de9e6aa423ed2d27feac84077d078afaadf121ef255"],["Death of Alexander McGregor, Qld 1873/C/629","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8621dc3b93818d91b2d333f363a16737ef682cff2134feebe9abfab466b1222f"]]},
janetb:{"n":"Janet Buchanan","d":"c. 1820–1899","s":"y","p":["bzjamesbuchanan","bzhellendawson"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Janet Buchanan was born about 1820, the daughter of James Buchanan and Hellen Dawson, as her Queensland death registration records. She married Alexander McGregor, and their children included John (about 1850), Mary Stewart (about 1851), Stewart Young (about 1855) and a son Alexander. She is named as their mother, as Janet Buchanan, on five Queensland death registrations: two entries for a son Alexander (1868 and 1873), and those of John (1906), Stewart Young (1921) and Mary (1927). The family was in Queensland by 1868.\n\nShe died in Brisbane on 9 December 1899 and was buried the next day at Toowong Cemetery, aged 80. Toowong had opened in 1875 and had become Brisbane's main burial ground. Her death registration names her parents but not her husband, so on its own it could belong to another Janet McGregor. The grave settles it: in May 1908 Mary Jane McGregor, born Paine, the wife of Janet's son Stewart Young McGregor, was buried in the same plot.\n\nJanet lived to see her granddaughter Adele White marry Frank Warbrick in Brisbane in March 1899, nine months before she died. Where she was born, and when she and Alexander married, has not been found.","src":[["Death of Janet McGregor, Qld 1899/B/32295 (parents James Buchanan and Hellen Dawson)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e69384e26508ecdf2bf03a1f166871e35dead48097165bded98102f9c12fcb39"],["Death of her daughter Mary Stewart White, Qld 1927/B/584","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/49ae127c197d6cc69c58b40c16ec7e31699e3c2034d11e897ac5fac03419e15f"],["Death of her son John McGregor, Qld 1906/B/7096","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/13deb11912261de7e8db93e607b0db9424e416f6c5b9521315ab1a64ad9fcb4e"],["Death of her son Stewart Young McGregor, Qld 1921/B/34731","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/da148b382c2ea7287b7fbd4bb8b4bf8092ca6713a5bc128565016a9da8a0fbac"],["Burial of Janet McGregor, aged 80, Toowong Cemetery, 10 Dec 1899 (grave Mon 12-42-33)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31790071"],["Burial of Mary Jane McGregor, aged 50, Toowong Cemetery, 12 May 1908 (same grave)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31909436"],["Death of Mary Jane McGregor, Qld 1908/B/9538 (parents John Paine and Elizabeth Stacey)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f52fe2eab6a4fa138d726453d9e3630254b4716a178814e65115d59b15796b23"]],"b":1820,"x":1899},
francish:{"n":"Francis Edward Hymus","d":"d. 1974","x":1974,"s":"y","p":["edwardch","frances"],"r":["au"],"t":"Doug's father, a career officer of the Bank of New South Wales in Queensland. In November 1916 he left Townsville on transfer to the bank's Atherton branch, and in 1917 he was in Cairns. He married Saddie Dorothy Napier on 7 September 1921. Their son Francis Neville was born in 1922, and their daughter Dorothy Melda in August 1924, when the family was at Normanton in the Gulf country. In September 1926 he came from Brisbane to be teller at the Cooroy branch, where Doug was born in January 1927; Saddie became secretary of the Cooroy Country Women's Association. In April 1935, when Saddie's father died at Charters Towers, the family were at Proserpine. In 1940 and 1941, at Millaa Millaa on the Atherton Tableland, he was president of its Chamber of Commerce, treasurer of the ambulance committee and organiser of wartime concerts. By 1945 the family lived at Bowen, where he represented the Bowen Chamber of Commerce in 1947, and by 1950 he was manager of the Bank of New South Wales at Charters Towers. His mother, Frances Harriet Hymus, died in Brisbane in December 1947. He died on 7 September 1974, the anniversary of his wedding.\n\nHis elder son, Francis Neville, born at Townsville on 23 November 1922, enlisted in the Army at Innisfail on 24 January 1942, No. QX46835, naming his father as next of kin; he served as a private with Headquarters 7th Brigade and was discharged on 2 July 1946.\n\nThe Queensland death index confirms his parents as Edward Charles Hymus and Frances Harriet Horsford. Between Proserpine and Millaa Millaa he served at Allora on the Darling Downs: in June 1936 his parents stayed with him and Saddie at the Bank of New South Wales there, and he played bowls at Allora in 1937 and 1938. His son Neville married in February 1947 and his daughter Melda in April 1948, while the family were at Bowen. At Charters Towers he bowled for the town against South Townsville in 1950, and by 1963 he and Saddie were living at Wynnum on Moreton Bay. The Bank of New South Wales, founded in Sydney in 1817, is the bank now called Westpac.\n\nHe was at Millaa Millaa from the start of 1939: in January that year he was welcomed as a new member of the town's ambulance committee, and in April he led the community singing at a local concert. A report from Allora in September 1957 recalled that he had been manager of the Bank of New South Wales branch there some twenty years before. His birth has not been found in the Queensland or New South Wales indexes, under Hymus or under his mother's maiden name.","src":[["Bank transfer to Atherton, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 7 Nov 1916","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/62261183"],["Birth of a daughter at Normanton, Brisbane Courier, 16 Aug 1924","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22F.%20E.%20Hymus%22%20Normanton"],["Teller at Cooroy, Daily Mail, 1 Sep 1926","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/218337251"],["Millaa Millaa Chamber of Commerce, Courier-Mail, 13 Feb 1941","https://trove.nla.gov.au/search/category/newspapers?keyword=%22F.%20E.%20Hymus%22%20Millaa"],["Engagement of Dorothy Melda Hymus, Courier-Mail, 9 Jul 1947","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/49328748"],["Funeral notice of Frances Harriet Hymus, Telegraph, 2 Dec 1947","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/187060125"],["Bank of NSW, Charters Towers, Northern Miner, 18 Jan 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/83135954"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["DVA WWII Nominal Roll: Hymus, Francis Neville, QX46835","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["NAA B883, QX46835, Hymus Francis Neville","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=4883336"],["Death, Qld 1974/B/54136 (parents named)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/71ea0906d2d2ebc5c06375e45537632258687dce1fd407ac175cfae901e13cb8"],["Bank of New South Wales, Allora, Toowoomba Chronicle, 11 Jun 1936","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285366493"],["Allora bowls, Warwick Daily News, 25 Apr 1938","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article190548982"],["Wedding of Melda Hymus of Bowen, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 6 Apr 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article63047478"],["Bowls, Charters Towers v. South Townsville, Northern Miner, 16 Oct 1950","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article81590436"],["Obituary of Mrs Hudson (names Mrs F. E. Hymus of Wynnum), Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"],["Millaa Millaa notes, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 20 Jan 1939","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article62813837"],["At Millaa Millaa, Cairns Post, 29 Apr 1939","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article42177179"],["Allora croquet visit, Toowoomba Chronicle, 26 Sep 1957","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article288666356"]]},
saddie:{"n":"Saddie Dorothy Napier","d":"1896–1990","b":1896,"x":1990,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Queensland on 2 June 1896, the third of the eight children of Robert Napier and Ethel Augusta Harvey: Gladys (1890), Robert Harvey (1894, who died at nine months), Saddie, William Sneddon (1899), Robert Barton (1901), Constance (1904), John (1907) and Ethel Octavia (1910). Her parents were remembered as old-time residents of Townsville who later settled at Charters Towers.\n\nShe married Francis Edward Hymus, an officer of the Bank of New South Wales, on 7 September 1921, and followed him from branch to branch across Queensland. Their children were Francis Neville, born at Townsville in 1922, Dorothy Melda, born in August 1924 when they were at Normanton, and Edward Douglas, born at Cooroy in January 1927.\n\nAt Cooroy she was honorary secretary of the Country Women's Association and read the branch's annual report in February 1930; in December 1927 her younger sisters Tavie and Constance came down from Charters Towers to stay with her there. Later postings took the family to Proserpine, Allora, Millaa Millaa, Bowen and, by 1950, back to Charters Towers, where her mother was still living.\n\nBy 1963 she and Frank were living at Wynnum. She was widowed on 7 September 1974, their 53rd wedding anniversary, and died on 13 August 1990, aged 94.\n\nHer father was born at Liverpool, New South Wales, in 1860, the son of a Scottish tailor from Largs. In September 1957 she came to Allora with visitors from the Warwick croquet club and told the members how pleased she was to be back after twenty years, since the time when her husband managed the bank there.","src":[["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Birth of sister Gladys, 1890","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/05dda93cc0b3445d06af7adb075259ed47d73d342dd2a151a07b0a42c22b0667"],["Birth, Qld 1896/C/10987","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Sisters visiting Cooroy, Brisbane Courier, 16 Dec 1927","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article21204999"],["Cooroy C.W.A. annual meeting, Nambour Chronicle, 14 Feb 1930","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article77301862"],["Obituary of her sister Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"],["Allora croquet visit, Toowoomba Chronicle, 26 Sep 1957","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article288666356"],["NSW birth index: Robert Napier, Liverpool, 7972/1860","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"]]},
edwardch:{"n":"Edward Charles Hymus","d":"1869–1939 · Linton, Cambridgeshire","b":1869,"x":1939,"s":"y","p":["charlesh2","rebecca"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born in the spring of 1869 at Linton, Cambridgeshire, a son of Charles Hymus, a farm labourer, and Rebecca Page. He grew up in the family's cottage at Bartlow, where the 1881 census lists him as a scholar of 12, the third of four boys at home, with four younger sisters.\n\nAt 18 he left for Queensland as an assisted immigrant. He sailed in the steamer Dorunda, which arrived on 27 June 1888, in a family party: his father's younger brothers Henry, 26, and William, 24, Henry's wife Harriet, a Sarah Hymus of 21, and four small children. Henry, who had been a blacksmith's apprentice at Linton, died in Queensland in November 1892.\n\nEdward married Frances Harriet Horsford on 6 April 1895 and settled in Townsville, where he worked as a carrier, delivering goods with a horse-drawn van; he sat on the council of a Townsville society in 1921, and in 1922 advertised his delivery van turnout for sale from South Townsville. By 1924 the couple had moved to Brisbane, where he was a Past Chief Ranger of the Ancient Order of Foresters, Court South Brisbane Hope, a friendly society that paid sick benefits to its members.\n\nIn retirement they lived at Wynnum and then at “Cambus”, Loudon Street, Sandgate; they took a holiday house at Southport in 1931, went north in 1935 to stay with their son Frank, a bank officer at Proserpine, and stayed with him again at Allora in June 1936. He died on 27 November 1939, aged 70, and is buried in Toowong Cemetery. His death registration names his parents as Charles Hymus and Rebecca Page.\n\nNo obituary for him has been found; the Brisbane Telegraph of 28 November 1939 printed only his funeral notice, “HYMUS, Edward Charles, of Loudon Street, Sandgate”.","src":[["Townsville Daily Bulletin, 10 Jun 1921","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/64067672"],["Advertisement, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 30 Jun 1922","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/62333361"],["A.O.F. Court South Brisbane Hope, Telegraph, 30 Apr 1924","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/178399453"],["Visit to Proserpine, Daily Mercury, 25 Jul 1935","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/173222497"],["Funeral notice, Courier-Mail, 28 Nov 1939","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/40888290"],["1881 census, Bartlow (Edward C. Hymus, 12)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0978b18f7c1745597da8"],["Queensland State Archives, assisted immigration index 1848–1912: Hymus, Dorunda, 27 Jun 1888","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset/assisted-immigration-1848-to-1912"],["Marriage, Qld 1895/C/1989; death, Qld 1939/B/46121 (parents Charles and Rebecca Page)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Visit to Allora, Toowoomba Chronicle, 11 Jun 1936","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285366493"],["FreeBMD: birth, Linton, Jun quarter 1869","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Funeral notice, Telegraph (Brisbane), 28 Nov 1939","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article184389436"]]},
frances:{"n":"Frances Harriet Horsford","d":"1869–1947 · Wimborne district, Dorset","b":1869,"x":1947,"s":"y","p":["georgeh","annap"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born late in 1869 in the Wimborne district of Dorset, the eldest child of George Horsford and Anna Pardy, and given the name of her grandmother Frances Pardy, the shoemaker's wife at Horton.\n\nHer parents sailed for Queensland with the three younger children in 1885, when she was 15, and she is not on that passenger list. A Harriet Horsford aged 18, the right age for her, arrived in Queensland on the steamer Chyebassa on 19 September 1888, so she seems to have followed them out three years later.\n\nShe married Edward Charles Hymus on 6 April 1895 and lived with him in Townsville, where he was a carrier, before they moved to Brisbane in the early 1920s; her mother lived nearby at Toowong. Their son Frank became a bank officer, and in the 1930s she and Edward travelled to stay with him at Proserpine and at Allora.\n\nWidowed in 1939, she stayed on at “Cambus” in Loudon Street, Sandgate. She died on 2 December 1947, aged 78; Frank, then manager of the Bank of New South Wales at Bowen, came down for the funeral, and she was buried with Edward in Toowong Cemetery. She had outlived her sister Edith; her sister Flora Mather lived until 1951 and her brother John William until 1953.","src":[["Funeral notice, Courier-Mail, 3 Dec 1947","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/49651659"],["Death of Anna Horsford, 1934","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/61864026"],["Queensland State Archives, assisted immigration index: Harriet Horsford, 18, Chyebassa, 19 Sep 1888","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset/assisted-immigration-1848-to-1912"],["Death, Qld 1947/B/14539 (parents George Horsford and Anna Pardy)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f63b7689cfb0bf941cb189932c893b0315e79eda5abb8891f0642ac512ddedee"],["Marriage, Qld 1895/C/1989","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Visit to Allora, Toowoomba Chronicle, 11 Jun 1936","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285366493"]]},
charlesh2:{"n":"Charles Hymus","d":"b. c. 1844 · Linton, Cambridgeshire","s":"y","p":["williamhymus","annehymus"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1844 at Linton, Cambridgeshire, the second son of Charles Hymus, a farm labourer, and Mary Kidman. In 1861, aged 17, he was already an agricultural labourer, living in his parents' cottage at Bartlow. Two doors away lived the family of William Page, whose daughter Rebecca, then 14, was also doing farm work.\n\nCharles married Rebecca Page in the Linton district in the spring of 1863. They lived at Bartlow, apart from a few years at Linton around 1869 to 1872, when Edward and Henry were born there, and he stayed a farm labourer. The 1881 census shows them with eight children at home: John (16), George W. (14), Edward Charles (12), Henry (9), Mary J. (7), Alice K. (5) and the baby twins Gertrude and Ethel. By 1891 they were in Camps Road, Bartlow, with the twins, Annie M. (9), Minnie (6) and Fred (4), and Rebecca was going out as a charwoman.\n\nFarm wages in eastern England were low through the agricultural depression of the 1870s and 1880s, while Queensland was offering assisted passages to farm workers. In 1888 their son Edward left for Queensland with two of Charles's younger brothers. Charles was still living at Bartlow in 1891; his death has not been found.\n\nAn earlier version of this tree made him the Charles Hymus baptised at Little Abington on 15 August 1841, but that child was buried there on 21 November 1841, aged two months. The censuses, and the death registrations of his son Edward and his brother Henry in Queensland, point to the Bartlow family instead.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Little Abington baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: household of Charles Hymus","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6437f493fd4d323d72fc"],["1881 census, Bartlow: Charles and Rebecca Hymus","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0978b18f7c1745597d9e"],["1891 census, Camps Road, Bartlow","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/66559424f493fd21ce73ef55"],["Baptism of Charles Hymus, Little Abington, 15 Aug 1841","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/682a150386557438f0aeb352"],["Burial of Charles Hymus, aged 2 months, Little Abington, 21 Nov 1841","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829c9eb86557468c3bf646b"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Linton, Jun quarter 1863","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of son Edward Charles Hymus, Qld 1939/B/46121 (parents Charles and Rebecca Page)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"]],"b":1844},
williamhymus:{"n":"Charles Hymus","d":"c. 1813–1880 · Bartlow","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1813 at Bartlow, a small parish on the Cambridgeshire–Essex border known for the Bartlow Hills, a group of Roman burial mounds. He was a farm labourer all his life. The Hymus name belongs to the chalk villages of this corner of south-east Cambridgeshire, around Horseheath, Balsham, West Wratting, Linton and the Abingtons.\n\nHe married Mary Kidman of Linton about 1842. Their elder children, James (about 1843), Charles (about 1844) and John (about 1847), were born at Linton, the small market town two miles away, and Mary Ann (about 1853) at Bartlow. In 1861 the family lived in a cottage at Bartlow; Charles was 48, and all three boys, aged 18, 17 and 14, were farm labourers like him. Two more sons followed, Henry in 1862 and William about 1864.\n\nHis death, aged 65, was registered in the Linton district in the last quarter of 1880. The next spring his widow was living in Bartlow Road, Linton. His son Charles stayed at Bartlow; his youngest sons Henry and William and his grandson Edward Charles all sailed for Queensland in 1888.\n\nThis place in the tree was earlier given to William Hymus, a labourer of Little Abington, whose son Charles, baptised in 1841, proved to have died as a baby.","src":[["Little Abington baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: Charles Hymus, 48, born Bartlow","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6437f493fd4d323d72fc"],["1881 census, Bartlow Road, Linton: Mary Hymus, widow","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0944b18f7c174559718e"],["FreeBMD: death of Charles Hymus, 65, Linton, Dec quarter 1880","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of son Henry Hymus, Qld 1892/C/4026 (parents Charles Hymus and Mary Kidman)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"]],"b":1813,"x":1880},
annehymus:{"n":"Mary Kidman","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1824 at Linton, Cambridgeshire, where Kidman was a common name. Her maiden name comes from the Queensland death registration of her son Henry in 1892, which names his parents as Charles Hymus and Mary Kidman.\n\nShe married Charles Hymus, a farm labourer of Bartlow, about 1842, when she was about 18. The censuses show six children: James (about 1843), Charles (about 1844), John (about 1847), Mary Ann (about 1853), Henry (1862) and William (about 1864). In 1861 she was 37 and keeping the cottage at Bartlow for her husband and three working sons.\n\nCharles died late in 1880. In 1881 Mary, a widow of 57, was the head of a household in Bartlow Road, Linton, with her sons John, 34, a farm labourer, Henry, 19, a blacksmith's apprentice, and William, 17, a farm labourer, and a five-year-old granddaughter, Alice Dockerell. Henry and William emigrated to Queensland in 1888 with her grandson Edward Charles. Her own death has not been found, and her parents are not known: no Mary Kidman of her age appears at Linton in the 1841 census.\n\nThis place in the tree was earlier given to Anne, wife of William Hymus of Little Abington, who is not an ancestor.","src":[["Little Abington baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: Mary Hymus, 37, born Linton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6437f493fd4d323d72fc"],["1881 census, Bartlow Road, Linton: Mary Hymus, widow, 57","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0944b18f7c174559718e"],["Death of son Henry Hymus, Qld 1892/C/4026 (mother Mary Kidman)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"]],"d":"b. c. 1824 · Linton, Cambridgeshire","b":1824},
rebecca:{"n":"Rebecca Page","d":"c. 1846–1905 · West Wickham","s":"y","p":["thomaspage","annpage"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1846 at West Wickham, a village on the chalk uplands of south-east Cambridgeshire near the Suffolk border, the daughter of William Page, a farm labourer, and his wife Ann. By 1861 the family lived in a cottage at Bartlow, and Rebecca, at 14, was working on the land like her father and her brother John.\n\nShe married Charles Hymus, the boy from two doors away, in the Linton district in the spring of 1863, when she was about 17. They lived at Bartlow for most of their married life; Edward and Henry were born at Linton. The censuses name eleven children: John, George W., Edward Charles (born 1869), Henry, Mary J., Alice K., the twins Gertrude and Ethel (born about 1880), Annie M., Minnie and Fred (born about 1887). In 1881 her widowed mother Ann, a laundress, lived next door in Camps Road, and by 1891 Rebecca herself was going out as a charwoman.\n\nHer son Edward left for Queensland in 1888. The death of a Rebecca Hymus, aged 60, was registered in the Linton district in the last quarter of 1905; no other Rebecca Hymus of that age appears in the district's censuses.\n\nAn earlier version of this tree identified her with Rebekah Page, baptised at Ickleton in 1844. A Rebecca Page was buried at Ickleton in September 1846, and the censuses give Rebecca Hymus's birthplace as West Wickham, so the Ickleton family has been set aside.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Ickleton baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: household of William Page (Rebecca, 14)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6438f493fd4d323d7316"],["1881 census, Bartlow: Rebecca Hymus, 35, born West Wickham","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0978b18f7c1745597d9e"],["1891 census, Camps Road, Bartlow: Rebecca Hymus, charwoman","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/66559424f493fd21ce73ef55"],["FreeBMD: death of Rebecca Hymus, 60, Linton, Dec quarter 1905","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]],"b":1846,"x":1905},
thomaspage:{"n":"William Page","d":"b. c. 1813 · West Wickham","s":"y","p":["hygeorgepage","hysarahpage"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1813 at West Wickham, Cambridgeshire, and a farm labourer all his life. In the 1841 census a William Page of about the right age was living at West Wickham in the household of George and Sarah Page. They may be his parents, but the 1841 census does not state relationships, so the link is not proven.\n\nWith his wife Ann he had at least six children, and their birthplaces trace the family's moves between neighbouring parishes in search of work: John (about 1840) and Rebecca (about 1846) were born at West Wickham, Jane (about 1850) and Mary (about 1852) at Horseheath, and Sarah Ann (about 1857) and Alice (1860) at Bartlow.\n\nIn 1861 William, 48, lived in a cottage at Bartlow with Ann and all six children; his son John, 21, and his daughter Rebecca, 14, were both farm labourers. Two doors away was the Hymus family, and in 1863 Rebecca married their son Charles. William died between 1861 and 1881, when Ann was a widow living next door to Rebecca.\n\nThis place in the tree was earlier given to Thomas Page, a labourer of Ickleton, who is not an ancestor.","src":[["Ickleton baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: William Page, 48, born West Wickham","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6438f493fd4d323d7316"],["1841 census, West Wickham: household of George Page","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683da859b18f7c2f690cb373"],["1881 census, Camps Road, Bartlow: Ann Page, widow","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0978b18f7c1745597dba"]],"b":1813,"u":true},
annpage:{"n":"Ann Page","s":"y","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"The wife of William Page and mother of Rebecca. Her maiden name is not known. The records disagree about her birthplace: the 1861 census, when she was 44, says Hadstock, the Essex village just across the county boundary from Linton, while the 1881 census, when she was 66, says Linton itself.\n\nShe and William, a farm labourer, had at least six children between about 1840 and 1860: John, Rebecca, Jane, Mary, Sarah Ann and Alice. The family lived at West Wickham, then Horseheath, and from the later 1850s at Bartlow, where the youngest, Alice, was seven months old at the 1861 census.\n\nBy 1881 Ann was a widow of 66, living alone in Camps Road, Bartlow, and earning her living as a laundress, taking in washing. Her daughter Rebecca Hymus, with a husband and eight children, lived in the next house. She is not in the Bartlow census of 1891.\n\nThis place in the tree was earlier given to Ann, wife of Thomas Page of Ickleton, who is not an ancestor.","src":[["Ickleton baptisms (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"],["1861 census, Bartlow: Ann Page, 44, born Hadstock","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/659d6438f493fd4d323d7316"],["1881 census, Camps Road, Bartlow: Ann Page, widow, 66, laundress, born Linton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/679a0978b18f7c1745597dba"]],"d":"b. c. 1816 · Hadstock or Linton","b":1816},
georgeh:{"n":"George Horsford","d":"c. 1847–1910 · Corscombe, Dorset","s":"y","p":["hyjohnhorsford","hymarthahorsford"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born about 1847 at Corscombe in west Dorset, the youngest of four sons of John Horsford, a farm labourer, and his wife Martha. By 1851 the family had moved across the county to Gussage All Saints, and by 1861 George, 14, was an apprentice, living with his widowed father, the village postman, in Horton Street, Horton, a few miles north of Wimborne. The census does not name his trade.\n\nAt the end of 1868 he married Anna Pardy, the daughter of Horton's shoemaker, in the Wimborne district. Their daughter Frances was born there the following year, followed by Flora Louisa (about 1876), John William (about 1879) and Albert (about 1884).\n\nThe family emigrated to Queensland as assisted immigrants on the steamer New Guinea, which arrived on 17 August 1885: George, 37, Anna, 36, Louisa, 9, John, 6, and Albert, 1. Frances seems to have followed in 1888. Two more daughters were born in Queensland, Anna in 1887 and Edith in 1890. They lived for a time in Townsville and later in Brisbane.\n\nHis children were Frances (Mrs E. C. Hymus), Flora (Mrs W. S. Mather), John William and Edith; one list of the children also names an Allie, who has not been identified for certain. He died at Toowong, Brisbane, on 6 October 1910, nursed at the end by Dr Culpin, and Anna and their daughter Edith thanked the neighbours in the paper for their kindness. His death registration names his father as John Horsford.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Deaths, Queensland Figaro, 13 Oct 1910","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/84445425"],["Bereavement notice, Telegraph, 10 Oct 1910","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/186551052"],["1851 census, Gussage All Saints: household of John Horsford (George, 4)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683791d6b18f7c1cdbe2f18a"],["1861 census, Horton Street, Horton: John Horsford, postman, and George, 14, apprentice","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/622e38aef493fd44f44f360c"],["Queensland State Archives, assisted immigration index: Horsford, New Guinea, 17 Aug 1885","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset/assisted-immigration-1848-to-1912"],["Death, Qld 1910/C/3381 (father John Horsford)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"]],"x":1910,"b":1847},
annap:{"n":"Anna Pardy","d":"c. 1849–1934 · Horton, Dorset","s":"y","p":["hygeorgepardy","hyfrancespardy"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born about 1849 at Horton, a village a few miles north of Wimborne in east Dorset, the youngest child of George Pardy, the village cordwainer (shoemaker), and his wife Frances. At the 1851 census she was a baby of one in her parents' house, with brothers George, 21, already a shoemaker, and Robert, 6.\n\nShe married George Horsford, who had grown up in the same village, in the Wimborne district at the end of 1868, and their daughter Frances was born there the next year. In 1885 George, Anna and three children sailed for Queensland on the steamer New Guinea, arriving on 17 August; Anna was 36.\n\nThe children grew up partly in Townsville: Frances (Mrs E. C. Hymus), Flora Louisa, who married William Samuel Mather in 1908 and lived in Townsville, John William, who married at Geraldton (now Innisfail) in 1908 and farmed at Mourilyan, Albert, a baby on the voyage, Anna, born in Queensland on 10 July 1887, who died on 24 December 1898 aged eleven, and Edith, born on 26 October 1890, who married Jack McAully in 1920. Another list of the children names an Allie, who has not been identified for certain.\n\nGeorge died at Toowong, Brisbane, in October 1910, and Anna stayed on in Sylvan Road, Toowong, with Edith. She outlived Edith, who died in January 1931, and died in Brisbane after a short illness in November 1934, in her 86th year. The Queensland index dates her death 13 November, while her death notice in the Townsville Daily Bulletin gives the 11th. She is buried in Toowong Cemetery. Her death registration names her father as George Pardy.\n\nHer older brothers and sisters, from the Horton baptism register and the 1841 census, were George (about 1830), Harriot (baptised 1832), David (1835), Ann (1838) and Robert (1842). Her own baptism, about 1849 or 1850, is not in the transcribed Horton register.","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Death notice, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 16 Nov 1934","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/61864026"],["Funeral notice, Courier-Mail, 14 Nov 1934","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/35634000"],["Funeral of Edith McAully, Brisbane Courier, 16 Jan 1931","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/21660696"],["1851 census, Horton: household of George Pardy, cordwainer (Anna, 1)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683792ccb18f7c1cdbe32ac9"],["Queensland State Archives, assisted immigration index: Horsford, New Guinea, 17 Aug 1885","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset/assisted-immigration-1848-to-1912"],["Death, Qld 1934/B/25927 (father George Pardy; dated 13 Nov 1934)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Horton baptisms 1801–1850 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/HortonBaps1801-1850.htm"],["1841 census, Horton (Dorset OPC transcript)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/1841Horton.htm"],["Bereavement notice of Mrs Horsford and daughter Edith, Telegraph, 10 Oct 1910","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/186551052"]],"b":1849,"x":1934},
robertnsr:{"n":"Robert Napier","sn":"Robert Napier (senior)","s":"y","p":["hxjohnnapier","hxmarthanapier"],"t":"Born about 1825 at Largs, on the Firth of Clyde in Ayrshire, Scotland, the son of John and Martha Napier, whose names are given in his New South Wales death registration. He was a tailor by trade.\n\nHe arrived in New South Wales in 1847 and was first appointed master tailor at the Government Orphanage, a post he held for about two years. Ill health then turned him to farming, and for about seventeen years he farmed at Moorebank, on the Georges River near Liverpool. His wife was Janet Sneddon; no marriage for the couple appears in the New South Wales index. Their children registered there include John (1850), William (1852), Isabella (1858, who died in 1859), Janet (1859), Robert (1860), a second Isabella (1862), Ann (1864) and Martha (1867).\n\nAbout 1867, the year the railway from Sydney reached the town, the family moved to Mittagong in the Southern Highlands. There he went back to his trade and built up a tailoring business, which he later handed on to his son John, who was also a captain in the Bowral volunteer company. William died in the Berrima district in 1887.\n\nRobert died at Mittagong on Thursday 8 September 1904, aged 79, of old age, and was buried in the Presbyterian part of the Fitzroy cemetery. He left his widow, two sons, John of Mittagong and Robert of Townsville, and three daughters, Mrs Backley of Sydney, Mrs Hobson of Carcoar and Mrs George of Adelaide.","src":[["Death of his son Robert Napier, Qld 1935/C/1808","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e5f89fc54ad5a42e9f21e21d6983424cbf684f75745ef0b6f58c8e1e80ca6efe"],["Obituary, Bowral Free Press, 14 Sep 1904","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112421815"],["Obituary, Robertson Advocate, 13 Sep 1904","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article114108579"],["NSW death index: Robert Napier, 1904, Bowral, reg. 8672/1904 (parents John and Martha)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["J. Napier's tailoring establishment, Mittagong, Robertson Advocate, 26 Nov 1907","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112098061"]],"d":"c. 1825–1904 · Largs, Ayrshire","b":1825,"x":1904,"r":["uk","au"]},
janetsneddon:{"n":"Janet Sneddon","s":"y","p":["hxjohnsneddon","hxannsneddon"],"t":"The wife of Robert Napier, a tailor from Largs on the Clyde, and the mother of Robert Napier of Townsville and Charters Towers. Her son's Queensland death registration of 1935 gives her maiden name as Sneddon, a surname of central Scotland. Her husband came to New South Wales in 1847, and no marriage for the couple is in the New South Wales index.\n\nHer children were registered in New South Wales from 1850: John (1850), William (1852), Isabella (1858, who died the next year), Janet (1859), Robert (1860), a second Isabella (1862), Ann (1864) and Martha (1867). The family farmed at Moorebank near Liverpool until about 1867 and then moved to Mittagong, where her husband was a tailor. Her maiden name lived on in Queensland in her grandson William Sneddon Napier, born in 1899.\n\nShe was widowed in September 1904. In July 1907 the Robertson Advocate reported the death of “Mrs. Napier, sen.”, widow of the late Robert Napier, at the home of her eldest daughter at Alexandria in Sydney; the print is damaged, but her age appears as 84. The New South Wales index has the death of a Janet Napier in the Waterloo district, which takes in Alexandria, in 1907, with parents named John and Ann. If that entry is hers, she was born about 1823, a daughter of John and Ann Sneddon; the link rests on the match of name, place and year only.","src":[["Death of her son Robert Napier, Qld 1935/C/1808","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e5f89fc54ad5a42e9f21e21d6983424cbf684f75745ef0b6f58c8e1e80ca6efe"],["Birth of grandson William Sneddon Napier, Qld 1899/C/10735","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/76038489bd655d579be932289169dc787f50b6e90ff2760d4e670d64d65622d8"],["Death of Mrs. Napier, sen., Robertson Advocate, 30 Jul 1907","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112092949"],["NSW death index: Janet Napier, 1907, Waterloo, reg. 11697/1907 (parents John and Ann)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["Obituary of Robert Napier (her husband), Bowral Free Press, 14 Sep 1904","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112421815"]],"d":"c. 1823–1907","b":1823,"x":1907,"u":true,"r":["au"]},
robertn:{"n":"Robert Napier","d":"1860–1935 · Liverpool, New South Wales","b":1860,"x":1935,"s":"y","p":["robertnsr","janetsneddon"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in 1860 at Liverpool, on the south-western edge of Sydney in New South Wales, the son of Robert Napier and Janet Sneddon. His birth was registered at Liverpool that year under his parents' names, and his Queensland death registration of 1935 names the same parents. His father, a tailor from Largs in Ayrshire, had come to Sydney in 1847 and was then farming at Moorebank, near Liverpool. Robert's older brothers were John (1850) and William (1852), and younger sisters included Isabella (1862), Ann (1864) and Martha (1867). About 1867 the family moved to Mittagong in the Southern Highlands, where his father went back to tailoring.\n\nHe married Ethel Augusta Harvey in Queensland on 3 October 1889, when he was about 29 and she was 21. The Queensland index holds a second marriage registration for the same couple dated 16 September 1892; the reason is not recorded. They had eight children: Gladys (1890), Robert Harvey (1894, who died as a baby), Saddie Dorothy (1896), William Sneddon (1899), Robert Barton (1901), Constance (1904), John (1907) and Ethel Octavia (1910).\n\nHis trade was very probably tailoring, like his father and his brother John. A Robert Napier, tailor, came by train from Ayr to Bowen in May 1902 and opened a shop in Herbert Street, and the Bowen paper welcomed him as a useful cricketer. In August 1903 he moved on to Brandon, a sugar town on the Burdekin near Ayr, and in April 1905 “Robert Napier, of Ayr, tailor” was declared insolvent. These reports do not name his wife, so the link is not proven; the dates fit, although his father's obituary of September 1904 placed him at Townsville.\n\nThe family lived for many years in Townsville, where John was born in 1907, and a daughter's obituary called Robert and Ethel “old-time residents of Townsville and latterly of Charters Towers”. Charters Towers, 85 miles inland, had been Queensland's richest goldfield in the 1890s, and its mines were closing by the time of the First World War. He died at home in Baker Street, Charters Towers, on 10 April 1935, aged 75. His death notice named his children as Mrs Barrie (Brisbane), Mrs Hymus (then at Proserpine), Constance (Charters Towers), Tavie (Townsville), Bill and Bob (Toowoomba) and Jack (Charters Towers).","src":[["Death notice, The Northern Miner, 27 Apr 1935","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/81246490"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Death, Qld 1935/C/1808 (parents Robert Napier and Janet Sneddon)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e5f89fc54ad5a42e9f21e21d6983424cbf684f75745ef0b6f58c8e1e80ca6efe"],["Marriage, Qld 1889/C/2130 (3 Oct 1889)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/cc461d56f455cf9353f4ef17343e8e8224b5c627d31205892a1d022c018229d5"],["Second marriage registration, Qld 1892/C/1950 (16 Sep 1892)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/49fbb7cccccf5bcb70b9a9b7cb59fb0a02b4312afdeda649e4b6b4ca158598e9"],["Obituary of daughter Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"],["NSW birth index: Robert Napier, 1860, Liverpool, reg. 7972/1860 (parents Robert and Janet)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["Obituary of Robert Napier senior, Bowral Free Press, 14 Sep 1904","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112421815"],["Robert Napier arrives at Bowen from Ayr to open a tailoring business, Port Denison Times, 10 May 1902","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article288871429"],["R. Napier, tailor, Herbert Street, Bowen (advertisement), Port Denison Times, 17 May 1902","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article288870109"],["Mr R. Napier opens a tailor's shop at Brandon, Port Denison Times, 29 Aug 1903","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article288866794"],["Robert Napier, of Ayr, tailor, adjudicated insolvent, North Queensland Register, 17 Apr 1905","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article85122050"],["Robert Napier, of Brandon, tailor, Northern Miner, 12 May 1905","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article79060230"]]},
ethel:{"n":"Ethel Augusta Harvey","d":"1868–1953","b":1868,"x":1953,"s":"y","p":["henryharvey","ellenalcock"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Queensland on 28 April 1868, the daughter of Henry Augustus Harvey and Ellen Elizabeth Alcock. An elder brother, Harold Augustus, was born and died in 1866, and her younger sisters were Eleanor Frances Hoskins (1870) and Thurza Anne (1874); her mother also registered a son, Sydney Edward, in 1872. Her father disappears from the records after 1874, and when Ethel was ten her mother married Arthur James Hosking, later of Townsville.\n\nShe married Robert Napier on 3 October 1889, aged 21, and the couple were registered as marrying a second time on 16 September 1892. Her first child, born in 1890, was named Gladys Elizabeth Hosking after her mother and stepfather. Seven more followed: Robert Harvey (1894, who died at nine months), Saddie Dorothy (1896), William Sneddon (1899), Robert Barton (1901), Constance (1904), John (1907) and Ethel Octavia (1910), whose second name marks her as the eighth child. Ethel's stepfather died in 1894 and her mother in 1904.\n\nThe Napiers lived for many years in Townsville and later at Charters Towers. Widowed in 1935, she died at Charters Towers on 12 August 1953, aged 85. Her death notice named her children as William (Oxley, Brisbane), Robert (Sydney), John (Townsville), and Mesdames Barrie (Brisbane), Hymus, Overs and Hudson (Charters Towers).","src":[["Death notice, The Northern Miner, 15 Aug 1953","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/81647028"],["Queensland BDM family history search","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Birth, Qld 1868/C/2865","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Marriages, Qld 1889/C/2130 and 1892/C/1950","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/cc461d56f455cf9353f4ef17343e8e8224b5c627d31205892a1d022c018229d5"],["Birth of Sydney Edward Wilmott, Qld 1872/C/3523","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a87450c5c3b8afdff0dceba44d288992c98af7d2c57c62f9eef17f3e1263c3be"],["Obituary of daughter Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
jesse:{"n":"Jesse Wetherall Frankish","d":"1890–1948 · Hull","b":1890,"x":1948,"s":"f","p":["thomas2","emily"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born in Hull in 1890, the eldest son. He lived at 13 Tate Street, West Leederville, with his wife Zelma, and their daughter Rena married Gerald Ford. He died suddenly at Subiaco on 24 December 1948, aged 58. The family’s death notices name Walter and Mona’s children as Ramona, Edward, Lawrence and Walter, and Jesse’s uncle as Walter Wetherall of Sutton Street, Mandurah.\n\nIn the Second World War, aged 51, he joined the Volunteer Defence Corps: he enlisted at Wembley on 25 March 1942, No. W69401, giving his birth as 12 April 1890 at Hull and his wife Zelma as next of kin, and served as a corporal in the 3rd Battalion, Swanbourne.\n\nHe was in England in the last year of the First World War. On 25 February 1918, as a Company Sergeant-Major in the Royal Engineers, he married Zelma Frankish at St John's Church, Newington. The notice his family placed in the West Australian that June called her the only daughter of Mr and Mrs R. H. Frankish of Hawthorn Avenue, Hull, so the bride already bore the family name; the first printing of the notice had wrongly given the church as St Mary's, West Perth, and was corrected three days later. Zelma was his first cousin: when her father, Robert Walter Frankish, late of Hull, died in Perth in 1923 aged 52, his death notice named him the father of Zelma and father-in-law of Jesse W. Frankish, and a Robert Walter Frankish, the youngest child of Jesse's grandparents Virgil and Jane, was born in Hull at the end of 1870.","src":[["Death notices, West Australian, 27 Dec 1948","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47633969"],["WWII nominal roll, W69401","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=831152&c=WW2"],["DVA WWII Nominal Roll: Frankish, Jesse Wetherall, W69401","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["NAA B884, W69401, Frankish Jesse Wetherall","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=5889041"],["Marriage notice, West Australian, 22 Jun 1918","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article27481506"],["Corrected marriage notice, West Australian, 25 Jun 1918","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article27481764"],["Death notices, Robert Walter Frankish, West Australian, 23 Feb 1923","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23888277"],["Funeral notice, Robert Walter Frankish, late of 13 Tate Street, West Australian, 24 Feb 1923","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23888390"],["WA death index: Robert Walter Frankish, Perth, 1923, aged 52, father Robert Virgil Frankish; Zelma Frankish, Mandurah, 1978, aged 80, parents Robert Walter Frankish and Mary Elizabeth Milner","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["Birth index, Robert Walter Frankish, Hull, Dec quarter 1870, vol. 9d p. 229 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Sub-branch notes, Victoria Park, Listening Post, 23 Mar 1928","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article257006350"],["Jail concert, Daily News, 13 Jun 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article84197226"],["General news (J. W. Frankish, baritone), West Australian, 14 Jul 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article32936899"],["Victoria Square Collegians, West Australian, 13 Jul 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article32938242"],["Death notices for Jesse W. Frankish, West Australian, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article47634121"]]},
richardf:{"n":"Richard Wetherall Frankish","d":"1895–1966 · Hull","b":1895,"x":1966,"s":"f","p":["thomas2","emily"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Richard Wetherall Frankish, known as Dick, was born in Hull in 1895, the third son of Thomas Frankish and Emily Wetherall, and was only a toddler when his mother brought the boys out to Western Australia in 1897. He became a boilermaker at the Government Railways' locomotive workshops at Midland Junction, and on 1 December 1915 he was one of 21 workshop men given parting gifts at a lunch-hour send-off after enlisting in the AIF.\n\nAt 20, single and living in St Leonards Avenue, West Leederville, he had enlisted in Perth that November as a gunner, No. 15109, naming his father, T. Frankish, as next of kin. He sailed from Melbourne on the Port Lincoln on 4 May 1916 with reinforcements for the 10th Field Artillery Brigade, served with the 8th Field Artillery Brigade, and came home on 23 July 1919.\n\nIn 1923 he married Sylvia Amy Kendrick at Geraldton. She was a daughter of Joseph Kendrick and Amy Dunn, and had sung in the Anglican choir and taught Sunday school there for eight years; when the parish farewelled her that August with a silver-mounted oak tray, Dick replied on her behalf. They settled at Mount Lawley, where they lived for decades. Their sons Kenneth Thomas and Ronald Richard were born in July 1924 and October 1925, and a daughter followed in 1931. Dick worked for the State as an inspector of machinery, one of the officers who inspected boilers and engines; in July 1947, on duty at Collie, his car was struck by a goods van being shunted across the Harvey Street level crossing, though he was unhurt.\n\nKen was an outstanding sportsman, an A-grade cricketer from 15, a baseball pitcher, and a member of the WA schoolboys' football team that toured Melbourne undefeated in 1938. He worked in the Lands Department before joining the RAAF at 18. On 23 October 1944, as an observer with No. 460 Squadron, he was killed when his Lancaster, hit by flak over Essen and iced up, crashed in England on the way home; he was 20 and is buried in Cambridge City Cemetery. In December 1948 Dick and Sylvia dedicated a plaque to him in the Honour Avenues at Kings Park, beside one for his cousin Robert. Sylvia died at Mount Lawley in 1964, aged 65, and Dick died there in 1966, aged 71.","src":[["West Australian, 5 Nov 1946","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article46182062"],["West Australian, 19 Jul 1947","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article46327063"],["Daily News, 2 Dec 1930","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article83827847"],["AIF Project (UNSW Canberra): Richard Wetherall Frankish, No. 15109","https://aif.adfa.edu.au/showPerson?pid=103266"],["NAA B2455, Frankish Richard Wetherall, service record","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=4019745"],["Send-off to soldiers at Midland locomotive shops, Swan Express, 10 Dec 1915","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article204771668"],["Presentation to Miss Sylvia Kendrick, Geraldton Guardian, 1 Sep 1923","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article66712892"],["WA marriage index: Richard Wetherall Frankish and Sylvia Amy Kendrick, Geraldton, 1923, reg. 2900028","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["Birth notice, Kenneth Thomas, West Australian, 9 Aug 1924","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article31246380"],["Birth notice, Ronald Richard, Western Mail, 5 Nov 1925","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article37641525"],["WA Flier Dies In 20,000ft Plunge, Daily News, 20 Dec 1944","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article78771392"],["CWGC: Flight Sergeant Kenneth Thomas Frankish, 427818","https://www.cwgc.org/find-records/find-war-dead/casualty-details/2651102/kenneth-thomas-frankish"],["Honour Avenues Kings Park: FSGT Ken Frankish, plaque L108","https://honouravenueskingspark.com.au/present/hap-database/1130-fsgt-ken-frankish"],["Kendrick death notices naming Sylvia and Dick Frankish, West Australian, 22 Sep 1950","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article47887971"]]},
alanb:{"n":"Alan Baldwin Brazier","d":"1895–1983 · Brisbane","b":1895,"s":"y","p":["george","ellen"],"r":["au"],"t":"The eldest son of George and Ellen Brazier, and elder brother of Bruce. He held a commission as a second lieutenant in the citizen forces until 1917, and in 1922 the Marine Board granted him a first-class engineer's certificate. On 10 November 1924 he married Phyllis May Yeo, only daughter of Mr and Mrs W. F. Yeo of Eagle Junction, at the Albert Street Methodist Church; his brother Felix was best man and his mother made the wedding cake. They lived at Wodonga, Garnett Street, Clayfield, and later in Junction Road, Clayfield, and in November 1929 he and his mother-in-law gave a pre-wedding afternoon for his brother Bruce's bride, Aileen Warbrick. In July 1946 their daughter Betty was one of the survivors when a small passenger plane crashed into flats in Peel Street, Mackay. Later he was a partner in Thomas Jack & Co., a store in Dalby, whose safe was stolen and blown open on the bank of the Condamine River in 1960.\n\nHe was born on 4 June 1895 and died on 13 May 1983, aged 87.","src":[["Wedding, Brisbane Courier, 15 Nov 1924","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article22890874"],["Marine Board, Daily Standard, 1 Aug 1922","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article179022237"],["Pre-wedding afternoon, 16 Nov 1929","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article21492424"],["Mackay plane crash, Daily Mercury, 22 Jul 1946","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article171148663"],["Dalby safe-breaking, 10 Mar 1961","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article289183528"],["Birth, Qld 1895/C/3647","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2a2f83cf8c7cda773179ae7f287af95609d6eab6ac286645f9990cd911c1ba13"],["Death, Qld 1983/3522","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/55824e0569088bad6fdbcc5e8ba3dbe9ec153f9110ef49149af2f89197a57ded"]],"x":1983},
felix:{"n":"Felix Howard Brazier","d":"1897–1989 · Brisbane","b":1897,"s":"y","p":["george","ellen"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Brisbane on 5 August 1897, the second of the three sons of George Herbert Brazier and Ellen Baldwin, and elder brother of Bruce. He grew up at Toowoomba and, from about 1908, at Warwick, and in 1910 he won a scholarship from Warwick West school to Toowoomba Grammar. His father died in January 1913, when Felix was 15. He went on to graduate in civil engineering from the University of Queensland.\n\nIn November 1924 he was best man when his elder brother Alan married in Brisbane. He married Dorothy Amy Dunstan on 11 March 1926, and they had a son, Douglas Alan. In 1930 he applied for a patent for an improved bottle stopper. He was engineer to the Townsville City Council for more than thirty years.\n\nIn the Second World War he served in the Australian Army (Q217608) with the Volunteer Defence Corps, the part-time home defence force: he enlisted at Townsville on 23 May 1942, living at Hermit Park, and was a lieutenant in the 16th Battalion, VDC, until his discharge on 22 October 1945.\n\nDorothy died on 30 May 1964, and their son Douglas Alan died in 1984. Felix died on 6 March 1989, aged 91.","src":[["Patent application, NAA A627 27661/1930","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/"],["WW2 Nominal Roll, DVA","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["Marriage, Qld 1926/B/39295","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/258944aef962e9fcd5541e70d9557d6b1a62d6027c06f5244278eb5f68d6ceae"],["Death of Dorothy Amy Brazier, 1964","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/ece081c4f142d8be35d00eb3fb848437201128817864c45e39591e022c2ed27d"],["Death, Qld 1989/51915","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/c6188937be9fa63e83d83ab1a623e0ad02546949747f3b5915f31a65d77875d9"],["NAA B884, Q217608, Brazier Felix Howard","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=4625668"],["Scholarship lists, The Week, 28 Jan 1910","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article187333210"],["Felix Brazier, City Engineer, Townsville Daily Bulletin","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article62991176"],["Wedding of Alan Brazier, Brisbane Courier, 15 Nov 1924","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article22890874"],["Obituary of his father, Toowoomba Chronicle, 17 Jan 1913","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article285533788"]],"x":1989},
everard:{"n":"Everard Hodgetts","d":"1906–1994 · Queensland","b":1906,"x":1994,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"img":"img/gingin1906.jpg","ch":"hazenberg","t":"Born in Queensland on 18 September 1906, the son of Joseph Hodgetts and Rosetta Maud Bastock. He married Amy Louisa Haken on 16 October 1934, and they raised their large family in the Bundaberg and Gin Gin district; in 1957 they lived at Perry Street, Bundaberg, when their second daughter Meg became engaged to Bill Hazenberg. Their sons included Barry Everard, Kevin Lawrence (Laurie) and Lewis Gordon (Aussie), who carried Everard's name and that of Amy's father, Gordon Haken. He died on 22 May 1994, a resident of Gin Gin, and is buried with Amy in the Bundaberg General Cemetery.\n\nHe was the fourth of thirteen children, and the Second World War nominal roll gives his birthplace as Rosedale. He enlisted at Bundaberg on 3 May 1942 (Q207652), when the family lived at Springfield, and served as a private in the 10th Battalion, Volunteer Defence Corps, until 21 October 1945; the roll spells his name Everand. Two of his brothers also served: Jack in the RAAF and George as an Army driver.\n\nHe was known as Sam. His birth was registered in 1906, when his parents were farming at Rosedale, between Bundaberg and Gladstone; by 1913, when his brother Jack was born, the family had moved in to Bundaberg. After Amy died in January 1961 he lived on for another thirty-three years.","src":[["Engagement notice, Bundaberg News-Mail, 31 Dec 1957","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283916690"],["Queensland birth, marriage and death indexes","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Bundaberg cemetery records","https://cemeterysearch.bundaberg.qld.gov.au/?q=Hodgetts"],["WWII nominal roll, Q207652","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=108374&c=WW2"],["Birth, Qld 1906/C/4836","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/3a6c9eae3e70e83b9cc5ffeb5f59b996b996ba9bfed71f28c71864d6be10ae7b"]]},
amyh:{"n":"Amy Louisa Haken","d":"1910–1961","b":1910,"x":1961,"s":"h","p":["gordonh","marymay"],"r":["au"],"ch":"hazenberg","t":"Born on 19 March 1910, the eldest of the four daughters of Gordon Haken and Mary May Tudman. Her sisters Edna May (1911–1956, Mrs Jennings), Esme Maud (1913–1994, Mrs Roberts) and Doris Marion (1915–1993, Mrs Hansen) followed her. The family lived at Childers, the sugar town south of Bundaberg, where Edna was born, and moved in to Bundaberg about 1919. Her father worked there as a linesman for the Bundaberg Electric Supply Company.\n\nShe married Everard Hodgetts on 16 October 1934 and they raised a large family in the Bundaberg and Gin Gin district. Her father was killed in a fall from an electric light pole in February 1938, and her sister Edna died in 1956.\n\nShe died on 9 January 1961, aged 50, when she and Everard, known as Sam, lived at North Bundaberg. Her funeral was held at the Methodist Church in Barolin Street; her son-in-law Bill Hazenberg was among the pall-bearers. She was buried in the Bundaberg General Cemetery, where Everard later joined her.","src":[["Funeral of Mrs A. L. Hodgetts, Bundaberg News-Mail, 11 Jan 1961","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284134810"],["Queensland birth, marriage and death indexes","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Bundaberg cemetery records","https://cemeterysearch.bundaberg.qld.gov.au/?q=Hodgetts"],["Birth of Edna May Haken, 1911","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/3eac50ecd0873b193db19b2152995655a59184df07b94483e1ae04a49c785b76"],["Birth, Qld 1910/C/8343","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e94481ffd416a9bf8386396287ee52f3fb903978c765260b3311be63d97e576e"],["Marriage, Qld 1934/C/3526","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/507cf46e2ceef2a31d838c5375322efb9bf2b41776626e1a8d54cf29ca9d3976"],["Death, Qld 1961/C/39","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a67ef04c6d9475c91f77086776c87724ff420e21e822a80597df891e09270cf6"],["Obituary of her sister, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"]]},
rosetta:{"n":"Rosetta Maud Bastock","d":"1878–1970 · Aston","b":1878,"x":1970,"s":"h","p":["robertbastock","marythornton"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Aston, Birmingham, early in 1878, the daughter of Robert Edwin Bastock and Mary Thornton. Her father died between April and June 1889, when she was eleven; her six-year-old brother Robert had died a few months earlier. In the 1891 census she was 13 and already at work as a spoon buffer, polishing plated spoons on a wheel. She was living in St Clement's parish, Aston, with her widowed mother, who worked as a laundress, her sisters Ellen and Jane, and two half-brothers and a half-sister from her mother's first marriage.\n\nIn 1900, aged 22, she came out on the steamer Oruba to marry Joseph Hodgetts, a moulder from Aston who was already in Queensland. The immigration register records her arrival in October 1900, with no other Bastock beside her, and they were married on the 17th of that month. They settled on a property in the Rosedale district before moving to the strawberry gardens at Newtown, Bundaberg, which became a well-known spot for parties and outings. They had seven sons and six daughters between 1901 and 1923, among them Joseph Bernard (1903), Everard (1906) and Leslie Frank (1909).\n\nJoseph died in December 1948. For her 80th birthday in February 1958, when she lived in Water Street, her family gave a party at the Coronation Hall; by then she had 44 grandchildren and 19 great-grandchildren. She died on 15 July 1970, aged 92, and was buried with Joseph in the Bundaberg General Cemetery, in the grave where their son Leslie Frank was later laid.","src":[["80th birthday report, Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Feb 1958","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284352215"],["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["Queensland marriage and death indexes","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["1891 census, Aston (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/590629cfe9379091b1c4315d/rosetta-bastock-1891-warwickshire-aston-1878-"],["Queensland assisted immigration index: Rosetta Bastock, 22, per Oruba, Oct 1900","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset?q=assisted+immigration+1848+to+1912"],["Marriage, Qld 1900/C/179","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/bfb819f5682b852d708ffd03520c71cf4ddfc0b8ceebe3eb746738ae9a74ca9a"],["FreeBMD: birth, Aston, Mar qtr 1878 (6d 455)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
josephh0:{"n":"Joseph Hodgetts","s":"h","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Married Sarah Ann Harris in the Aston district of Birmingham in the last quarter of 1869; the civil registration index lists them on the same page. Aston was then the fast-growing industrial side of Birmingham, all brass foundries, gun works and terraced streets, and much of it lay outside the city boundary until 1911.\n\nTheir son Joseph was registered at Aston in the first quarter of 1870. He became a moulder, emigrated to Queensland, married at Bundaberg in 1900 and named his own eldest son Joseph Bernard. When he died in 1948 his death registration gave his parents as Joseph Hodgetts and Sarah Harris, which is how this couple is known.\n\nHodgetts was a very common name in Aston, and several men called Joseph Hodgetts appear in its registers, so his own birth, trade and death have not been picked out with certainty. The 1871 and 1881 census returns for Aston, which list each household, are not in the free indexes that were searched.","d":"m. 1869 · Aston, Birmingham","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Aston, Dec qtr 1869 (6d 358)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Son's death, Qld 1948/C/4214","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f76ebfa9b15e022072f04edff27ddf928891f579781d741b0667472fac8f2471"]]},
sarahharris:{"n":"Sarah Ann Harris","s":"h","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Sarah Ann Harris married Joseph Hodgetts in the Aston district of Birmingham in the last three months of 1869. Their son Joseph's birth was registered at Aston between January and March 1870.\n\nNothing in the indexes searched shows her age, her parents or where she was born, and Harris is too common a name in Birmingham to pick her out without the marriage certificate, which names both fathers. That certificate (Aston, December quarter 1869, volume 6d, page 358) can be ordered from the General Register Office.\n\nHer son Joseph grew up to be a moulder, went out to Queensland and ran the strawberry gardens at Newtown, Bundaberg. On his death registration in 1948 she appears simply as Sarah Harris. Through him she had thirteen Queensland grandchildren, born between 1901 and 1923.","d":"m. 1869 · Aston, Birmingham","src":[["FreeBMD (England and Wales index)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Aston, Dec qtr 1869 (6d 358)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Son's death, Qld 1948/C/4214","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f76ebfa9b15e022072f04edff27ddf928891f579781d741b0667472fac8f2471"]]},
robertbastock:{"n":"Robert Edwin Bastock","s":"h","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Robert Bastock was born about 1841; his death registration in 1889 gives his age as 48. In the summer of 1875 he married Mary Thornton Layland, a widow with three young children, in the Aston district of Birmingham. The marriage index calls him Robert Francis E. V. Bastock; his death entry and his daughter's Queensland records call him Robert Edwin.\n\nThe 1891 census shows three daughters, Ellen, Rosetta and Jane, and the Aston birth registers have matching entries for Helen Alice (1876), Rosetta Maud (1878) and Jane Amelia (1880). A Robert Edwin Bastock registered at Aston in 1882 was evidently their son, and an Edith May born in 1884 was probably the youngest.\n\nThe first months of 1889 were terrible for the family. Robert Edwin, aged six, and Edith May both died in the March quarter, their deaths entered on consecutive pages of the register, and Robert himself died in the following quarter. Mary was left with six children at home, the three Bastock girls aged 13, 11 and 9. Rosetta, the middle one, emigrated to Queensland in 1900.\n\nHis trade and birthplace are not in the indexes searched; the 1881 census of Aston, which records both, was not available.","d":"c. 1841–1889","b":1841,"x":1889,"src":[["FreeBMD: marriage, Aston, Sep qtr 1875 (6d 450)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1891 census of his widow and children (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/590629cfe9379091b1c4315d/rosetta-bastock-1891-warwickshire-aston-1878-"]]},
marythornton:{"n":"Mary Thornton","s":"h","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1839 or 1840 at Wednesbury, in the Staffordshire Black Country; a Mary Thornton was registered in the West Bromwich district, which took in Wednesbury, in the spring of 1839. By her early twenties she was in Aston, Birmingham, where she married William Layland in the first quarter of 1862. Their children William (1866), Myra (1869) and Charles (1872) were born at Aston. A William Layland died at Aston in the summer of 1873, aged 36, and he was evidently her husband.\n\nTwo years later, in the summer of 1875, she married Robert Bastock. They had Helen Alice, called Ellen (1876), Rosetta Maud (1878), Jane Amelia (1880) and Robert Edwin (1882), and probably Edith May (1884). In early 1889 she lost the two youngest children and then her husband within a few months.\n\nThe 1891 census finds her at 255 Long Acre, Aston, a widow of 51 working as a laundress. All six children at home were earning or at school: William a guard, Myra a press girl, Charles an edge-tool stamper, Ellen a warehouse girl, Rosetta, 13, a spoon buffer, and Jane, 11, a scholar. The older children's surname is written Leyland in that return. A Mary Bastock who died at Aston early in 1921, aged 81, is probably her.","d":"b. c. 1839 · Wednesbury","b":1839,"src":[["1891 census, 255 Long Acre, Aston (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/590629cfe9379091b1c4315d/rosetta-bastock-1891-warwickshire-aston-1878-"],["FreeBMD: marriages, Aston, Mar qtr 1862 (6d 269) and Sep qtr 1875 (6d 450)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
gordonh:{"n":"Gordon Haken","d":"1883–1938 · Queensland","b":1883,"x":1938,"s":"h","p":["georgehaken","louisaredmund"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Queensland on 5 August 1883, the son of George Charles Haken and Louisa Grace Mitton; his birth registration gives his mother as Louisa Grace Redmund. He married Mary May Tudman on 28 July 1909, and they made their home in South Bundaberg; their daughters Edna May and Esme Maud were married at the Methodist churches there in 1933 and 1935.\n\nGordon worked for the Bundaberg Electric Supply Company as a linesman, and was well known to its customers. Just after midday on Tuesday 22 February 1938 he climbed a ladder to replace a fuse at the top of an electric light pole in Bourbon Street, opposite the School of Arts. A bus driver heard him cry out and saw him fall 25 or 30 feet to the road; a telephonist at the exchange across the street saw him lying at the foot of the ladder and called the ambulance. He had finished the job, and the inquest concluded that he had either touched a live wire or missed his footing on the ladder. He died of his injuries that day, aged 54. The company's manager said they had lost a valuable servant.\n\nHe is buried in the Bundaberg General Cemetery, where Mary May was laid beside him in 1986. His brother Edward John, a railway guard, collapsed and died while shunting a goods train at Caboolture in 1941.\n\nHis mother appears as Grace Redman, Redmond or Redmund in most of the family's registrations. He was one of eight children; a brother, John William, died in November 1883, three months after Gordon was born. When his second daughter Edna was born in 1911 the family was living at Childers, and her obituary says they moved to Bundaberg about 1919. His eldest brother George Henry and their father died within four days of each other in December 1934.","src":[["Sad fatality, Isis Recorder, 25 Feb 1938","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/287463023"],["Called away, Bundaberg Daily News and Mail, 24 Feb 1938","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284820621"],["Fatal fall: inquest, Bundaberg Daily News and Mail, 5 Mar 1938","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284825181"],["Jennings–Haken wedding, 1933","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284543915"],["Roberts–Haken wedding, 1936","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284563038"],["Death of Edward John Haken, Truth, 26 Oct 1941","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/203209535"],["Birth, Qld 1883/C/4005","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d1a7c38e4f1ca07d2a209b65cf90acdf5e88e034ee1a221600a58b6766217db5"],["Marriage, Qld 1909/C/2136","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a97a2b9fe8192bb510289c272b46a1ff7896a9059d19322b73befbefce5ef0f8"],["Death, Qld 1938/C/82","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8bf62bc406c88a0453001b995f7ab8c508ee0a9808dd654bac5cef3d4c05ebb5"],["Obituary of his daughter, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"]]},
marymay:{"n":"Mary May Tudman","d":"1891–1986 · Queensland","b":1891,"x":1986,"s":"h","p":["johntudman","emmapearson"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born in Queensland on 31 May 1891, the youngest of the eleven children of John Tudman and Emma Pearson, who had farmed on the Logan River before moving north. She married Gordon Haken on 28 July 1909, aged 18. They lived first at Childers, in the Isis sugar district, where their second daughter was born in 1911, and moved to South Bundaberg about 1919. They had four daughters: Amy Louisa (1910), Edna May (1911), Esme Maud (1913) and Doris Marion (1915); Edna married in 1933 and Esme at the end of 1935. Her widowed mother Emma died at their home in March 1926.\n\nIn February 1938 Gordon was killed in a fall from an electric light pole while working for the Bundaberg Electric Supply Company, leaving her a widow at 46. She outlived him by almost fifty years. In 1952 she was Mrs M. Haken of Elliott, near Bundaberg, when her brother John Brown Tudman died at Maryborough. She was back in Bundaberg by 1956, when her daughter Edna died aged 44, and she travelled to Rockhampton in 1963 for the funeral of her brother Arthur. Her eldest daughter Amy died in 1961.\n\nShe died on 31 March 1986, aged 94, and is buried with Gordon at Bundaberg. The death index gives her birth year as 1892; her birth registration says 1891.","src":[["Mrs M. Haken, News-Mail, 25 Mar 1963","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/283029739"],["Fatal fall, 1938","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/284825181"],["Obituary of J. B. Tudman, Maryborough Chronicle, 22 Mar 1952","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article150189554"],["Birth, Qld 1891/C/7706","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/513b79cee229e433b425c516d2fb0ec805e46ab6584112d29d8d61e530c822d1"],["Marriage, Qld 1909/C/2136","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a97a2b9fe8192bb510289c272b46a1ff7896a9059d19322b73befbefce5ef0f8"],["Death, Qld 1986/52327","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/4f80b53b0aef8245e90df9eccacafc685a48121f62f77910ead3e7c5b5076c36"],["Obituary of her daughter, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"]]},
georgehaken:{"n":"George Charles Haken","sn":"George Haken","d":"d. 1934","x":1934,"s":"h","p":["georgehaken0","mariah"],"r":["au"],"t":"Son of George Haken and Maria Haken; his death registration gives both parents that surname. Where and when he was born is not recorded in the Queensland indexes. One possible trace is a Charles Haken, aged 18, who landed in Queensland from the immigrant ship Ophelia on 25 August 1875, but nothing yet proves they were the same man.\n\nHe married Grace Redman, who the evidence suggests was born Louisa Mitton, in Queensland on 7 April 1877. Their children, registered in country districts, were George Henry (1878), Winifred Margaret (1880), Gordon (1883), John William (died November 1883), Edward John (1885), Roger (1888), Thomas (born January 1896, died that July) and Ethel May (1898). Louisa was stationmistress at Eudlo when she died in October 1911.\n\nOn 20 September 1913 he married Annie Mills, known as Nancy, and in later life they lived at Southport. His daughter Winifred, Mrs Mills, died in 1928. December 1934 was a hard month for the family: his eldest son George Henry died at Bulimba on 23 December, and George Charles died at his home in Lather Street, Southport, four days later, on 27 December. He was buried in Southport Cemetery. His funeral notice named sons in Bundaberg, Auchenflower and Bowen Hills and a married daughter at Bulimba. Annie lived until 1957.","src":[["Queensland birth, marriage and death indexes","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Funeral notice, Courier-Mail, 28 Dec 1934","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article35627780"],["In memoriam notice","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article35914477"],["Marriage, Qld 1877/C/164","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/31a7d19c584fa38d75d0176b2d39b849874e1adc589bae4eda253893a6ec5a16"],["Death, Qld 1935/B/26562","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8057e4980c9290150d49e72b000a0b88ffa76118f78317691862664798716f81"],["Second marriage, Qld 1913/C/1062","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/da81da731e1c50bca546ba424fee6343cee427809f6820c846b382c6e49e87c8"],["Death of Annie Haken, Qld 1957/B/20549","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/224041eeabed7b2a10252474832b6e859f7fff9c796969458dff614d40d2ec1e"],["Death of Winifred Margaret Mills, Qld 1928/B/5521","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/41e092c0c6153e5c53bc55c73e6cee63c4fdaf4bfdf030a78e84890740692164"],["Passenger list of the Ophelia, 1875 (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/api/download_file/DR38991"]]},
louisaredmund:{"n":"Louisa Grace Mitton","sn":"Grace Redman","d":"1855–1911 · Lincoln","s":"h","p":["hdwilliammitton","hdhannahbarnsley"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Known as Grace. She married as Grace Redman, and her children's births give her maiden name as Redman, Redmond or Redmund, but her death registration in 1911 names her parents as William Mitton and Ann and her birth year as 1856. That points to Louisa Mitton, born at Lincoln, England, late in 1855, the daughter of William Mitton, a wireworker, and Hannah Barnsley. In the 1861 census she was five, living in Melville Street, Lincoln, with her parents and five brothers and sisters. The family emigrated to Brisbane in the next few years, and her father died there in 1869, when she was thirteen. Why she used the name Redman is not known, so the link to her parents is marked as unproven.\n\nShe married George Charles Haken on 7 April 1877 and had eight children: George Henry (1878), Winifred Margaret (1880), Gordon (1883), John William, who died in November 1883, Edward John (1885), Roger (1888), Thomas, who lived six months in 1896, and Ethel May (1898).\n\nIn later life she was the stationmistress at Eudlo, a small railway stop in the hills behind the Sunshine Coast. In October 1911 she fell very ill and was taken to the General Hospital in Brisbane, where she died ten days later, on Wednesday 18 October, aged about 55. The local paper said she would be hard to replace: she was so obliging that she often looked after other people's needs at great inconvenience to herself. She left her husband, four sons and two daughters; three of the sons and one daughter were already married. George married again two years later.","src":[["Death of stationmistress, Chronicle and North Coast Advertiser, 28 Oct 1911","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article81936373"],["Queensland death index 1911/B/14837 and 1883/C/1721","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Death, Qld 1911/B/14837","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a26eb9fc53380c4acc40486bc3244910515778d50d54e71363e781f106443c38"],["Marriage, Qld 1877/C/164","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/31a7d19c584fa38d75d0176b2d39b849874e1adc589bae4eda253893a6ec5a16"],["1861 census, Melville Street, Lincoln (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/59038d5fe9379091b12e508e/louisa-mitton-1861-lincolnshire-lincoln-home-1856-"],["FreeBMD: birth of Louisa Mitton, Lincoln, Dec qtr 1855 (7a 425)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of her father William Mitton, Qld 1869/B/5091","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/67a6776616261a7c62cffbda3f03c578d3c9aa1d12f519dbc0194494ed4fc765"]],"b":1855,"x":1911,"u":true},
johntudman:{"n":"John Tudman","d":"1841–1913 · Colby, Norfolk","x":1913,"s":"h","p":["thomastudman","sarahtudman"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"A Norfolk man, he was born on 19 June 1841 and baptised on 25 July at St Giles, Colby, a farming parish near Aylsham, the son of Thomas Tudman, a labourer, and his wife Sarah, who had been Sarah Brown; he later gave his mother's maiden name to his son John Brown Tudman. He landed in Queensland from the immigrant ship General Caulfield on 3 September 1864, aged 22, with Stephen Tudman, 27, and Stephen's wife Ann. Stephen, later the licensee of the Norfolk Hotel, was the son of a John Tudman and Maria Lacey from the same corner of Norfolk, so probably a kinsman, though not a brother.\n\nHe married Emma Pearson in Brisbane on 22 December 1869, and they took up land on the Logan River near Jimboomba, south of Brisbane, where Stephen Tudman, who had sailed out with him, also selected land and kept the Norfolk Hotel. John selected 100 acres of second-class pastoral land on the Logan in 1873, another 40 acres in 1876 and 60 more in 1880; by 1883 he had met the conditions on all three blocks and was granted the deeds. Their home there was called Eton Vale, where their baby son Thomas William died in April 1875, aged nine months.\n\nTheir other children, born in Queensland, included Sarah Hannah (1870), Stephen (1872), William Pearson (1876), Robert Richard (1878), Thomas (1880), Emma Caroline Ann (1882), John Brown (1883), Arthur, Margaret Amy (1889) and Mary May (1891). The family later moved north to the Bundaberg district. He died on 30 May 1913.\n\nIn 1888 their eldest daughter, Sarah Hannah, married Francis John Cavell at Woodhill, near Beaudesert; sixty years later the Cavells were still there to mark their diamond wedding. Some time after that the family left the Logan for Gladstone, where, as one of the sons later put it, they worked on railway construction with their horses and drays. The sons stayed in the Gladstone and Rockhampton district: John Brown worked at Skyring's sawmill at Toolooa, outside Gladstone, before cutting cane at Childers, and Robert Richard became a Gladstone alderman.\n\nJohn and Emma had eleven children in all. Besides Thomas William, they lost William Pearson at six months in 1877, Margaret Amy just before her second birthday in 1891 and Emma Caroline Ann at twenty in 1902. Arthur Overton was born on 22 December 1886.","src":[["Selection, Brisbane Courier, 5 Nov 1873","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1331445"],["Death of Thomas William Tudman, Brisbane Courier, 12 May 1875","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1398643"],["Selections granted, Queenslander, 9 Jun 1883","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/19791625"],["Stephen Tudman, Norfolk Hotel, Jimboomba, Logan Witness, 1882","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/163898308"],["Obituary of J. B. Tudman, Maryborough Chronicle, 22 Mar 1952","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article150189554"],["Cavell–Tudman diamond wedding, Beaudesert Times, 29 Oct 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article216187561"],["Tabragalba Divisional Board, Logan Witness, 8 Mar 1890","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article163894735"],["Baptism, Colby, Norfolk, 25 Jul 1841 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68458d4586557454052c0c6e/john-tudman-baptism-norfolk-colby-1841-07-25"],["Passenger list of the General Caulfield, 1864 (Queensland State Archives)","https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/items/ITM18474"],["Marriage, Qld 1869/B/2837","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d7236ace35eb0f05b65477d7faedef888adaa5f5edb0d1340a82bbab5d651aa2"],["Death, Qld 1913/C/3337","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b45b9930baafb0fc90c6c20a02599e4198838e357d336126d9c00e3c0474265f"],["Death of Stephen Tudman, Qld 1897/C/78","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/be0aa9678bce495cbbf04f0086cfa5d0bd55f4be0414285919e2505bc92af1f5"],["Death of William Pearson Tudman, Qld 1877/C/1312","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7f7b4afec339ad9891c20b047f95173573fa7284f6f5e3e630dafd5a07bf669b"],["Death of Margaret Amy Tudman, Qld 1891/C/2495","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6b545b6e0274fc226d4baf97e2a13aff34ec7f84e8917558c166cd539fe773b2"],["Death of Emma Caroline Ann Tudman, Qld 1902/C/1945","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/0403a523606b5e48846e2dc1c4aa05ec9609e8da6af9ab86ff6c282103f59171"],["Birth of Arthur Overton Tudman, Qld 1887/C/5666","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d334b6caa5b9c798eb89fef48a70a61902c9bcc13d0efc679e035c1c88d34f78"]],"b":1841},
emmapearson:{"n":"Emma Pearson","d":"d. 1926","x":1926,"s":"h","p":["williampearson","hannahp"],"u":true,"r":["au"],"t":"Daughter of William and Hannah Pearson; one of her sons was christened William Pearson Tudman. She married John Tudman in Brisbane in 1869 and began married life on his selection on the Logan River near Jimboomba, at a place they called Eton Vale. There she lost her baby son Thomas William in 1875, at nine months. She raised a family of at least eleven, the youngest, Mary May, born in 1891, and the family later settled in the Bundaberg district.\n\nShe died on Monday 1 March 1926 at the South Bundaberg home of her son-in-law, Gordon Haken, and the local paper called her a very old resident of the town. She was buried in the General Cemetery the next afternoon; among the wreaths were ones from Skyring's Ltd and from Skyring's employees at Rockhampton; her son John Brown Tudman had worked at Skyring's sawmill near Gladstone.\n\nHer origins are not settled. Her death registration names her parents as William and Hannah Pearson. The Queensland immigration index has only one Emma Pearson who arrived before her marriage: a girl of 13 who landed from the David McIvor on 7 September 1863 with William Pearson, 39, Elizabeth, 39, and sisters Mary, 11, and Sarah, 9. That girl was about 19 in 1869 and 76 in 1926, which fits, but the mother's name does not match, so the link is not proved. The name she gave her son Arthur Overton Tudman in 1886 echoes a William Overton Pearson, son of William Pearson and Elizabeth Overton, who had died in Queensland in 1884.\n\nShe had eleven children. Three died in infancy, in 1875, 1877 and 1891, and her daughter Emma Caroline Ann died in 1902, aged 20. John died in May 1913, leaving her a widow for almost thirteen years.","src":[["Death of Thomas William Tudman, Brisbane Courier, 12 May 1875","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/1398643"],["Laid to rest, Bundaberg Daily News and Mail, 3 Mar 1926","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283333951"],["Death, Qld 1926/C/97","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/06a54b7ba9f652af8e8baf1252a5ca159d03b0304ac0cb9938f82ac627132eff"],["Marriage, Qld 1869/B/2837","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d7236ace35eb0f05b65477d7faedef888adaa5f5edb0d1340a82bbab5d651aa2"],["Queensland assisted immigration index: Pearson family per David McIvor, 1863","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset?q=assisted+immigration+1848+to+1912"],["Death of William Overton Pearson, Qld 1884/C/4528","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2de24a7dbd7bcaf2132be09159440b890999d2a08be2b151d7faa4834d28e04a"]]},
lesliefh:{"n":"Leslie Frank Hodgetts","d":"1909–1996","b":1909,"x":1996,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 22 September 1909, the fourth son and sixth child of Joseph Hodgetts and Rosetta Bastock, and known as Les. He was one of thirteen children raised at Rosedale and Bundaberg. He married Maria Catherine Poulsen on 17 June 1939, and in 1948, when his father died, lived at South Kalkie, the farming district on the eastern edge of Bundaberg where his sister Gwendoline also lived. He does not appear on the Second World War nominal roll, unlike his brothers Everard, Jack and George. He died on 2 December 1996, aged 87, and was laid in his parents' grave in the Bundaberg General Cemetery.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1909/C/928","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/0bd612d056584594e16122fd83449f57cc60a460bf42fdcadcf14bc943dfe9d9"],["Marriage, Qld 1939/C/2705","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/153d023aeb64a029c6b4d865b9e15410db3a2fcaa874215b0ebfd566ef6dfa65"],["Death, Qld 1997/50198","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d652feb4dcd612e6862f0fd2581d41e69c7e2c8eb440d03c94493325c7124d37"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
hindrikslik:{"n":"Hindrik Jans Slik","d":"d. by 1761","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"A man of Nieuw-Scheemda, a small village on the edge of the Oldambt in east Groningen. On 25 January 1755 he and Lupke Ides signed a marriage contract, with Hindrikje Jans standing on his side and Jan Ides and Roelf Jans Schudde on hers, and they married in the village church the next day. Their daughter Anna was baptised there on 27 July 1755 and a second girl, Stijntje, on 28 May 1758.\n\nHe died before November 1761, when Lupke married again as a widow; Anna was then six. Stijntje grew up to be a farmer's wife at Westerlee and lived until 1833. The surname Slik means mud or silt.","src":[["Marriage, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:126b07d6-a9cd-45b1-e4a5-5336b22c252e"],["Marriage contract, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29518a51-a98a-95fe-da04-72fa121878db"],["Baptism of Stijntje, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7024b8d1-1163-024b-78be-d3f36c0663ad"],["Death of Stijntje Hindriks Slik, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:79ddfcfc-9028-4822-1dd6-1334e3bd7c00"]]},
tjaartp:{"n":"Tjaart Pieters","d":"1746–1812 · Oostwold","b":1746,"x":1812,"s":"b","p":["grpietertjaarts","greisethijes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Oostwold on 4 December 1746, the son of Pieter Tjaarts and his second wife, Eise Thijes, who had married at Oostwold on 24 November 1743. He had a sister Hindrikje, baptised in April 1745, and older half-sisters from his father's first marriage; one of them, Geesje, lived at Oostwold until 1829. A half-brother, also Tjaart, was baptised in 1736, so the name had already been given once.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider). On 25 May 1783, aged 36, he married Wupke Roelfs at Finsterwolde, her village, a little to the east. Their daughter Eisse was born there on 17 April 1784 and named for his mother. A second daughter, Grietje, followed in 1789 and carried the name of Wupke's mother. Eisse married Hindrik Harms Broesder in 1807, and her eldest son was named Tjaard after him, the first of many Tjaards in the Broesder family.\n\nHe spent his last years back at Oostwold and died there on 16 November 1812, aged 66. Wupke outlived him by seventeen years. When she died in 1830 only Eisse was still living.","src":[["Marriage, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4b359f41-21b0-0b0d-bcbc-b4cbc9ea635c"],["Death record, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3cd5a700-fa2e-a573-aea6-44359aa3313f"],["Baptism, Oostwold 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83c221f4-283f-ad04-9bc3-123c78f80bc7"],["Baptism of Hindrikje, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4db8c085-83a8-c357-6812-4ef6b9ca3c08"],["Baptism of the first Tjaart, 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8c6bc504-3571-58ed-7ec0-f0cd97879283"],["Baptism of Eisse, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483118de-9d0a-1a95-84e3-0411ad06a678"],["Death of Geesje Pieters, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:70e6a4c4-3069-0ff4-0bb0-ae6bae749adf"],["Second marriage, to Eijse Thijes, Oostwold 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b0f420bf-31c6-068c-b9ff-ebf0c93d151a"]]},
wupke:{"n":"Wupke Roelfs","d":"1758–1830 · Finsterwolde","s":"b","p":["grroelfeernst","grgrietjeeilderts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Finsterwolde on 4 May 1758, the first child of Roelf Eernst and Grietje Eilderts, who had married there on 29 May 1757. Her death record makes her two years older, giving 1756 as her year of birth, but hers is the only baptism of a Wupke at Finsterwolde in those years and her parents were not yet married in 1756. She had the name of her father's mother, Wibke. A sister Jantje was baptised in 1759 and another of the same name in 1764, when the family lived 'by the Oosterdijk', the eastern dyke. In later records Wupke carries the surname Dijk, the Dutch word for dyke.\n\nShe married Tjaart Pieters, a labourer from Oostwold, at Finsterwolde on 25 May 1783. Their daughter Eisse was born the next April and Grietje, who had the name of Wupke's mother, in 1789.\n\nTjaart died at Oostwold in November 1812 and she lived on as a widow for seventeen years, still described as a working woman (arbeidster). She died at Midwolda on 28 May 1830, leaving one daughter. The record gives her age as 74; by the baptism register she was 72. Her name was passed on: Eisse called a daughter Wupke in 1815, and that Wupke lived to 89.","b":1758,"x":1830,"src":[["Death record, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:22a21b22-df64-85c4-8f06-78b800013b16"],["Marriage, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4b359f41-21b0-0b0d-bcbc-b4cbc9ea635c"],["Baptism, Finsterwolde 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aa5dd05f-e70c-79e8-ca5d-2db06cc5c5cb"],["Parents' marriage, Finsterwolde 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:35970d09-4f9c-f0a1-0c38-8490fae37d04"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1764 (by the Oosterdijk)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29021dfb-218b-6b2e-f2ad-090034637876"],["Baptism of Eisse, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483118de-9d0a-1a95-84e3-0411ad06a678"]]},
jakobkl:{"n":"Jakob Klaasens Leewe","d":"d. 1808 · Wagenborgen","s":"b","p":["gsklaaspeters","gsjeijejacobs"],"r":["nl"],"t":"A man of Wagenborgen who married twice, thirty years apart. On 23 May 1748, as Jacob Klaassen, he married Grietje Berents in the village church. They had six children baptised there: Jeije (1749), Klaas (1751), Berent (1752), a second Berent (1756), Peter (1758) and Jan (1760). Jeije, the first Berent and Jan all died within months and were buried at Wagenborgen. In 1751 he and Grietje stood beside Antje Klaassen, by her name his sister, when she contracted to marry Frerik Berents, who became a beer brewer at Nieuwolda. He was received as a member of the Wagenborgen church on 9 June 1763 and is still on its roll of members in 1794.\n\nNo burial of Grietje has been found. On 4 December 1778 he married Gepke Stoffers. His son Klaas Jakobs stood on his side of the contract, with Klaas, Pieter, Geert and Geertruid Freerks, by their names the brewer's children. With Gepke he had Jan (1779), Jeje (1781), who was given the name of his lost first child, and Engel (1784). From 1778 the records give him the surname Leew or Leewe, meaning lion, and Klaas used it too.\n\nIn 1806 he at first objected to his son Jan's marriage, as the banns record. No record states outright that the bridegroom of 1748 and the bridegroom of 1778 were one man; the tree joins them because of his son Klaas and the brewer's children at the 1778 contract and the reuse of the name Jeije. He was buried at Wagenborgen on 10 November 1808.\n\nHe is placed in the tree as the son of Klaas Peters and Jeije Jacobs of Wagenborgen, who married there in 1724 and had sons named Jacob baptised in 1726 and 1727 and a daughter Antje in 1731. That Antje would be the Antje Klaassen he stood beside in 1751, and his first son was named Jeije. The link is marked unproven because another boy, Jacob Leew, born to an unmarried mother, was baptised at Wagenborgen in March 1727, and Leew is the surname Jacob used from 1778.","src":[["Marriage, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b53e0e5d-4fb5-498c-6409-cca46971ecbd"],["Marriage contract, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3cf14616-8c41-6565-6832-95b5c4329958"],["First marriage, Wagenborgen 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f390fdc7-55d4-07ca-6bef-6932dc415aea"],["Baptism of Jeije, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2d891d5a-e23b-3fa4-f4a1-db27ba035515"],["Baptism of Klaas, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b937e9e0-31fa-00be-44ef-679aad1cc087"],["Marriage contract of Antje Klaassen, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6ccd06de-583e-5150-87bc-e5ffc485100b"],["Church membership, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dd81b61b-16f1-4df2-95c7-8fa27708797b"],["List of members, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4fcbe7bc-7146-4858-8a63-35bbe7709f49"],["Baptism of Jan, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:66d44236-4602-4d4d-52a9-3a88ab5edc50"],["Klaas Jakobs Leewe, church member 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7b5a70e0-5ab5-4852-9d83-c102ec9705eb"],["Death of Geertruit Freerks Brouwer, 1824 (brewer's daughter)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b469848c-b154-86de-9292-b088e800d69d"],["Banns of Jan, 1806","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5959de86-fa03-0031-e744-4f544adea0f8"],["Burial, Wagenborgen 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:58b8523a-aa7c-b26c-daaa-fb54ba342c91"],["Marriage contract of Klaas Peters and Jeije Jacobs, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8c6b5b32-b9c8-231c-41de-d9866c4f696c"],["Marriage, Wagenborgen 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8654c377-dc63-3ecd-84ad-e6fd2b1f8a55"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1726","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:619dddd5-f705-4594-769f-e59987431021"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bda4e04b-07cb-eb69-dd1d-f6773453e49e"],["Baptism of Antje, Wagenborgen 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a2e21639-512d-71b9-be86-373c6da258c9"],["Baptism of Jacob Leew, son of Geeske Ekkes, Wagenborgen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dd06167b-5d7a-f326-eba0-7cc49883ffe3"]],"x":1808,"u":true},
leendertb:{"n":"Leendert de Bruijn","d":"1868–1939 · Hoornaar","b":1868,"x":1939,"s":"b","p":["willembr","jannigjev"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Leendert de Bruijn was born at Hoornaar, in the Alblasserwaard, on 29 September 1868, the eldest son of Willem de Bruijn and Jannigje van Veen. He was seven when his father died in October 1875, leaving his mother with five young children; his little sister Metje died the following summer. In 1887 his mother married again, to Kornelis den Burger, and she died at Leerdam in 1895.\n\nOn 10 May 1889, aged 20, he married Gijsbertje de Kreij at Hoornaar. He was then a workman (werkman) who had done his militia service, and his widowed mother, by then a seamstress, came to give her consent. Gijsbertje had been born at Gorinchem and was the daughter of Jan Rokus de Kreij, a workman at Schelluinen. The couple settled at Schelluinen, where he later kept the village inn; at Grietje's wedding in 1929 he was described as a café-keeper (cafehouder). Eight children were born there: Lijntje (1889), Jannigje (1891), Jan Willem (1892), Willem (1895, who died at seven months), Willemina (1896), Cornelia Metje (1903), Leendert (1906) and Grietje Cornelia (1908). Jannigje and Willem carried his parents' names and Lijntje his wife's mother's. He registered Grietje's birth himself in 1908.\n\nFour of the children married at Schelluinen between 1909 and 1918: Lijntje to Jan den Braven in 1909, Jan Willem to Sophia Johanna van Vught in 1914, Jannigje to Gerrit Maas in 1916 and Willemina to Pieter Kamsteeg in 1918. Widowed on 15 February 1932, he died at Schelluinen on 5 August 1939, aged 70. His son Leendert died in Rotterdam in 1951 and his daughter Cornelia Metje at Gouda in 1965.","src":[["Birth record, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:008db361-3523-a91e-0834-c10a557d4fef"],["Birth register, Schelluinen 1908, no. 9","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6891711"],["Death record, 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:2c35d3e9-2cf3-4ea3-10ff-75a00d4af425"],["Death of Cornelia Metje de Bruijn, Gouda 1965","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:0456b2fe-1c9e-fe77-237c-215d9b7a992d"],["Death of Leendert de Bruijn, Rotterdam 1951","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:93219a49-9687-b41b-00fe-6db4cd5f3ce2"],["Birth register, Hoornaar 1868, no. 20","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6682919"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 10 May 1889","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20987089"],["Marriage of daughter Lijntje, 1909","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21091095"],["Marriage of son Jan Willem, 1914","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21092055"],["Marriage of daughter Jannigje, 1916","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21092773"],["Marriage of daughter Willemina, 1918","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21093200"],["Death of son Willem, 1895","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6911011"]]},
gijsbertje:{"n":"Gijsbertje de Kreij","d":"1869–1932 · Gorinchem","b":1869,"x":1932,"s":"b","p":["janrokus","lijntjeb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gijsbertje de Kreij was born at Gorinchem on 23 April 1869, the daughter of the labourer Jan Rokus de Kreij of Schelluinen and Lijntje Broeren of Hoornaar. Three of her mother's earlier babies had died within weeks of birth, and her mother herself died before Gijsbertje turned six. In December 1874 her father married Wilhelmina Slagboom, a widow from Hardinxveld, at Schelluinen. Gijsbertje gained a half-brother, Arie, born there in 1879; a half-sister, Neeltje, lived only three weeks in 1876.\n\nOn 10 May 1889, aged 20, she married Leendert de Bruijn, a workman, at Hoornaar; she had lived at Hoornaar but for the previous six months at Schelluinen, and her father came to consent. They settled at Schelluinen, where Leendert later kept the inn. She had eight children there between 1889 and 1908: Lijntje, named after her mother, Jannigje, Jan Willem, Willem (who died as a baby in 1895), Willemina, Cornelia Metje, Leendert and Grietje Cornelia. She saw four of them married at Schelluinen between 1909 and 1918, and Cornelia Metje in 1925, Leendert in 1928 and Grietje in 1929.\n\nShe died at Schelluinen on 15 February 1932, aged 62. Grietje named her own second daughter, born at Zwijndrecht in 1930, Gijsbertje after her.","src":[["Death register, Schelluinen 1932, no. 2","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6913813"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 10 May 1889 (gives her birth at Gorinchem)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20987089"],["Father's second marriage, Schelluinen 1874","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21084628"]]},
galeh:{"n":"Gale Harms","d":"m. 1769 Niekerk","s":"h","p":["hzharmjacobs","hztrijntjegales"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Niekerk in the Westerkwartier of Groningen, a carpenter (timmerman) who also kept a shop. He was probably the Gale baptised at Niekerk on 7 January 1748, son of Harm Jacobs and Trijntje Gales: it is the only such baptism there in those years, and he named children Harm and Trijntje. His marriage entry does not name his parents, so this link is likely rather than proven.\n\nHe married Eenje Jans at Niekerk on 27 August 1769. Their children were Aafke (baptised 14 January 1770), Harm (1772), Jan (1774), Gauke (1777), Trijntje (1779) and Geertje, born at Nuis about 1784. After Eenje died he married Janna Jans on 28 October 1787 and had Jan (1788), Grietje (1791), Trijntje (1792), another Jan (1794) and Antje (1798).\n\nThe family took the surname Staal. His son Harm Gales Staal was a carpenter at Feerwerd, his son Jan a labourer who died at Groningen in 1821, and his youngest daughter Antje Staal lived until 1881. Geertje's marriage record of 1814 calls him a shopkeeper (winkelier); those of two younger daughters in 1820 and 1821 call him a carpenter. His death has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, 1769","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83cb21cd-0d35-12fb-ca13-e25807e8bfc7"],["Baptism of Gale, son of Harm Jacobs, Niekerk, 7 Jan 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8a2fa03a-4aa2-b70b-8fb8-45528316cb3e"],["Baptism of Aafke, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f7203cee-b324-2ff1-d950-d1b84a778af5"],["Baptism of Harm, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0eb03b0c-8370-805d-6540-ceba9ed7d5b5"],["Baptism of Jan, 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb8f535c-8e5c-22bd-78b1-9ee53c37d8cc"],["Baptism of Gauke, 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95d4271b-0103-ecbb-cd4f-4b586e7ab78d"],["Baptism of Trijntje, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a193ca6f-4c27-a579-d77d-fefc6801c7dc"],["Second marriage, to Janna Jans, 28 Oct 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1f2c30c9-b18c-aa83-44ba-3f524f9a2219"],["Marriage of daughter Geertje, Marum 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d936761c-4db3-a565-63be-4a352a4c4da9"],["Marriage of daughter Trijntje, Zuidhorn 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:170ddfd1-7f96-054b-563c-2fcd9668c7c4"],["Marriage of daughter Antje, Grijpskerk 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:30d1acc7-0454-ec88-6493-d234386d31a0"],["Death of son Jan Gales, Groningen 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7394c12-1b6c-88ed-944e-09b08322ddbe"],["Death of daughter Antje Staal, 1881","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d3793f25-285f-340e-a48f-1f8b6f345ed5"],["Marriage of grandson Gale Harms Staal, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8a0258db-b224-766d-da32-5d6a8e71a3ea"]],"u":true},
eenje:{"n":"Eenje Jans","d":"of Niekerk","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Niekerk in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. She married the carpenter Gale Harms there on 27 August 1769. Their first child, Aafke, was baptised on 14 January 1770, followed by Harm (1772), Jan (1774), Gauke (1777) and Trijntje (1779), all while the couple lived at Niekerk, and by Geertje, born at Nuis about 1784.\n\nShe died before 28 October 1787, when Gale married again; no burial entry has been found. Her name, also written Enje and IJnje, was passed on: her son Harm called his first daughter Enje in 1795, and her daughter Aafke called hers IJnje in 1801. Geertje's marriage record of 1814 still names her as mother, alongside Gale as a shopkeeper.","src":[["Marriage, Niekerk, 27 Aug 1769","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83cb21cd-0d35-12fb-ca13-e25807e8bfc7"],["Baptism of Aafke, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f7203cee-b324-2ff1-d950-d1b84a778af5"],["Marriage of daughter Geertje, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d936761c-4db3-a565-63be-4a352a4c4da9"],["Baptism of granddaughter Enje, 1795","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7fcb8533-5aae-8017-f8d3-4d67a9470e2e"],["Remarriage of Gale Harms, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1f2c30c9-b18c-aa83-44ba-3f524f9a2219"]]},
willemharms:{"n":"Willem Harms","d":"m. 1772 Grootegast","s":"h","p":["hzharmabels","hzhilljealles"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Grootegast in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. He married Lootske Wijbes of Tolbert at Grootegast on 16 August 1772. Four children were baptised there: Harm on 27 June 1773, Wibe on 10 March 1776, Hiltje on 21 March 1779 and Trijntje, born 20 May 1790, on 13 June 1790.\n\nHis own baptism is not certain. The only Willem with a father called Harm in the Grootegast register of those years was baptised on 28 April 1743, the son of Harm Abels and Hillje Alles. Willem named his first son Harm and his first daughter Hiltje, which fits those parents, but the marriage entry of 1772 does not name them, so the link is shown as likely rather than proven. His children did not use a fixed surname in the church books; his grandchildren took the name Hazenberg from his wife's family.","src":[["Marriage, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:23cdd98c-66fc-5eab-a252-76cd8fdfbffe"],["Baptism of Wibe, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a4769de2-798f-7765-7b3e-ca96075a306f"],["Baptism of Harm, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483aac34-95d3-08cf-8f34-5c9010a86988"],["Baptism of Hiltje, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4a49c5ee-c957-7f2c-4720-8ddb6323eec6"],["Baptism of Trijntje, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:136dc5b5-740f-0478-341c-a9b5bcfbe377"],["Baptism of Willem, son of Harm Abels, Grootegast, 28 Apr 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0e39c0ca-d46b-7a7c-4298-fa65bd812709"]],"u":true},
lootske:{"n":"Lootske Wijbes","d":"b. 1745 · Tolbert","b":1745,"s":"h","p":["wijbewiggels","trijntjej"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Tolbert on 5 December 1745 as Loetske, the daughter of Wijbe Wiggels, who had married Trijntie Jinties there in October 1744. A sister, Joukje, was baptised in January 1750, and she had older half-brothers from her father's first marriage. The marriage register of 1772 calls her Lootske Wijbes \"of Tolbert\" and names no parents, but hers is the only baptism of a Loetske, Lootske or Lutske with a father called Wijbe in the indexed Groningen registers between 1738 and 1756. The baptism names only her father. Her mother is taken to be his second wife, Trijntie Jinties, whom he had married fourteen months earlier, and Lootske later named a daughter Trijntje.\n\nShe married Willem Harms at Grootegast on 16 August 1772, aged 26, and had Harm (1773), Wibe (1776), Hiltje (1779) and Trijntje (1790); the names Wibe and Trijntje match her own parents. Her father is called Wiebe Wiggels Hasenberg in a later record, and it was through her that the surname Hazenberg came to her son Wiebe's children. Her granddaughter Lutske, born at Grootegast in 1806, was named after her.","src":[["Baptism, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0308f785-ec7f-6a01-244f-403528751411"],["Marriage, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:23cdd98c-66fc-5eab-a252-76cd8fdfbffe"],["Baptism of sister Joukje, Tolbert 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7310725f-3b1f-0354-3520-b3e077760a03"],["Baptism of son Wibe, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a4769de2-798f-7765-7b3e-ca96075a306f"],["Baptism of daughter Trijntje, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:136dc5b5-740f-0478-341c-a9b5bcfbe377"],["Death of Wietse Wiebes Hasenberg, naming his father Wiebe Wiggels Hasenberg, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f44044f-23f6-00d0-7034-f831736708c3"]]},
wijbewiggels:{"n":"Wijbe Wiggels","d":"m. 1744 Tolbert","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Tolbert, beside Leek in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. On 25 October 1744 he married Trijntie Jinties of Leek at Tolbert. Their daughters Loetske and Joukje were baptised there on 5 December 1745 and 11 January 1750.\n\nThe register of neighbouring Midwolde and Leek shows a Wijbe Wiggels marrying Grietje Beerents on 1 July 1731, with sons Willem (baptised 2 December 1731) and Beerent (13 January 1737) born at Leek. The name is rare and this is taken to be his first marriage, though the entry of 1744 does not call him a widower. A third son of that marriage, Wietse, died at Grootegast on 29 July 1822, aged 81, a day labourer.\n\nWietse's death record names his father as Wiebe Wiggels Hasenberg and his mother as Grietje Berends, which ties him to the marriage of 1731. It is the earliest sign of the family surname, and it shows that the name Hazenberg comes from Wijbe himself. Hazenbergs of the next generations at Grootegast, such as the farmer Wiebe Willems Hazenberg (baptised 1768, son of a Willem Wijbes) and the day labourer Wiebe Wiggels Hazenberg (died 1827, son of a Wiggel Wiebes), were very probably his grandsons, though that has not been traced step by step.","src":[["Banns, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83786803-a7a0-7e05-fab1-0662b5ec8817"],["Marriage to Grietje Beerents, Midwolde en Leek, 1 Jul 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:06470f49-5151-103b-b887-fc4c77dec2ab"],["Baptism of Willem, 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c516ff53-c3ac-0bee-0ea3-4614794a1d7f"],["Baptism of Beerent, 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:210f8f52-8270-431c-0e9d-408f2d151f0a"],["Baptism of Loetske, Tolbert 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0308f785-ec7f-6a01-244f-403528751411"],["Baptism of Joukje, Tolbert 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7310725f-3b1f-0354-3520-b3e077760a03"],["Death of son Wietse Wiebes Hasenberg, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f44044f-23f6-00d0-7034-f831736708c3"],["Baptism of grandson Wijbe, son of Willem Wijbes, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ee81db4-dee1-848e-3ace-7f12e139e546"],["Death of Wiebe Wiggels Hazenberg the younger, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d259e826-bf5c-1762-ed3f-7b921a0713ab"]]},
trijntjej:{"n":"Trijntje Jentjes","d":"m. 1744","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Wijbe Wiggels of Tolbert on 25 October 1744; the Tolbert register writes her name Trijntie Jinties and says she came from Leek. Earlier notes placed her at Zevenhuizen, a village just south of Leek; the marriage entry itself says Leek.\n\nHer daughters Loetske and Joukje were baptised at Tolbert on 5 December 1745 and 11 January 1750; the baptism entries name only the father. Loetske named her own youngest daughter Trijntje in 1790. Tolbert, Leek and Midwolde lie within a few kilometres of one another, and the three churches shared the families of this corner of the Westerkwartier. Her parents and her burial have not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Tolbert, 25 Oct 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83786803-a7a0-7e05-fab1-0662b5ec8817"],["Baptism of Loetske, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0308f785-ec7f-6a01-244f-403528751411"],["Baptism of Joukje, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7310725f-3b1f-0354-3520-b3e077760a03"],["Baptism of granddaughter Trijntje, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:136dc5b5-740f-0478-341c-a9b5bcfbe377"]]},
georgehaken0:{"n":"George Haken","s":"h","p":[],"t":"Named as the father of George Charles Haken on the son's Queensland death registration of 1934, which gives the mother as Maria Haken, so both parents bore the same surname. Neither of them appears in the Queensland death index, and no Haken couple is on the Queensland immigration lists, so there is no sign that they followed their son to the colony.\n\nThe English marriage index has one entry that fits: a George Haken and a Maria Haken were married in the St Saviour district of Southwark, on the south bank of the Thames in London, in the last quarter of 1855. Whether this couple were the Queensland man's parents has not been proved; the certificate (St Saviour, December quarter 1855, volume 1d, page 2) names both their fathers and is the next record to check.","r":["uk"],"src":[["Son's death, Qld 1935/B/26562","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8057e4980c9290150d49e72b000a0b88ffa76118f78317691862664798716f81"],["FreeBMD: marriage, St Saviour, Dec qtr 1855 (1d 2)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
mariah:{"n":"Maria Haken","s":"h","p":[],"t":"Named as the mother of George Charles Haken on his death registration in Queensland in 1934. The registration gives her surname as Haken, the same as her husband's, which suggests she was born a Haken as well as marrying one.\n\nThat fits an entry in the English marriage index: a Maria Haken and a George Haken, listed on the same page, married in the St Saviour registration district, Southwark, London, between October and December 1855. No other marriage of a George Haken to a Maria has been found in the index for those years. The link to the Queensland family rests on the names alone and is not proved.\n\nNo death for her is in the Queensland index, although her son settled there. He married there in 1877 and had two daughters, Winifred Margaret and Ethel May; neither was given her name.","r":["uk"],"src":[["Son's death, Qld 1935/B/26562","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8057e4980c9290150d49e72b000a0b88ffa76118f78317691862664798716f81"],["FreeBMD: marriage, St Saviour, Dec qtr 1855 (1d 2)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
thomastudman:{"n":"Thomas Tudman","s":"h","p":["hdroberttudman","hdmaryhewitt"],"t":"Born on 15 February 1810 and baptised on 26 April at St Botolph's, Hevingham, in north-east Norfolk, the son of Robert Tudman and Mary Hewitt, who had married there the previous November. He worked as a farm labourer.\n\nOn 8 November 1835 he married Sarah Brown at St Giles, Colby, a few miles north of Aylsham; neither could write, and both signed the register with a cross. Their son John was born at Colby on 19 June 1841. By 1871 Thomas and Sarah were living in the next parish, Banningham, where the census lists him as an agricultural labourer of 60, with their twelve-year-old granddaughter Caroline in the house.\n\nJohn left for Queensland in 1864 and took up land on the Logan River; when he died in 1913 his death registration named Thomas and Sarah as his parents. Sarah died late in 1879. Thomas lived on in the same district for almost twenty years and died in the last quarter of 1898, aged 88.","d":"1810–1898 · Hevingham, Norfolk","b":1810,"x":1898,"r":["uk"],"src":[["Baptism, Hevingham, 26 Apr 1810 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68476c178655745405c55054/thomas-tudman-thomas-hewitt-baptism-norfolk-hevingham-1810-04-26"],["Marriage, Colby, 8 Nov 1835 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684590a986557454052d253d/thomas-tudman-sarah-brown-marriage-norfolk-colby-1835-11-08"],["Baptism of son John, Colby, 1841 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68458d4586557454052c0c6e/john-tudman-baptism-norfolk-colby-1841-07-25"],["1871 census, Banningham (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5903f506e9379091b150a313/thomas-tudman-1871-norfolk-buxton-1811-"],["FreeBMD: death, Aylsham, Dec qtr 1898 (4b 60)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
sarahtudman:{"n":"Sarah Brown","s":"h","p":[],"t":"Born about 1816 at Edingthorpe, a small parish near North Walsham in north-east Norfolk; the 1871 census gives her birthplace. She married Thomas Tudman, a farm labourer, at St Giles, Colby, on 8 November 1835. She signed the register with a cross, and one of the witnesses was a Daniel Brown, who shared her surname.\n\nHer son John was born at Colby on 19 June 1841 and baptised there five weeks later. He emigrated to Queensland in 1864 and carried her name with him: his son born in 1883 was christened John Brown Tudman, and his eldest daughter, born in 1870, was Sarah Hannah.\n\nIn 1871 Sarah, then 55, was living at Banningham with Thomas and a granddaughter, Caroline, aged 12. Her death was registered in the Aylsham district in the last quarter of 1879; one copy of the index gives her age as 63 and another as 65. No baptism for her was found at Edingthorpe in the transcripts searched, so her parents are not known.","d":"c. 1816–1879 · Edingthorpe, Norfolk","b":1816,"x":1879,"r":["uk"],"src":[["Marriage, Colby, 8 Nov 1835 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684590a986557454052d253d/thomas-tudman-sarah-brown-marriage-norfolk-colby-1835-11-08"],["Baptism of son John, Colby, 1841 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68458d4586557454052c0c6e/john-tudman-baptism-norfolk-colby-1841-07-25"],["1871 census, Banningham (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/5903f506e9379091b150a313/thomas-tudman-1871-norfolk-buxton-1811-"],["FreeBMD: death, Aylsham, Dec qtr 1879 (4b 55)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
williampearson:{"n":"William Pearson","s":"h","p":[],"t":"Named as the father of Emma Pearson on her death registration in 1926; she had also given his name to a son, William Pearson Tudman, born on the Logan River in 1876. Beyond that he has not been identified for certain.\n\nThe best candidate is the William Pearson, aged 39, who arrived in Queensland on the David McIvor on 7 September 1863 with his wife Elizabeth, 39, and daughters Emma, 13, Mary, 11, and Sarah, 9. That Emma was the right age to be the bride of 1869, but the wife's name is Elizabeth, not the Hannah of the death registration. A William Pearson and Elizabeth Overton were also the parents of William Overton Pearson, who married in Queensland in 1868 and died in 1884; Emma called a son Arthur Overton two years later. The parent link stays marked as unproven until Emma's 1869 marriage certificate is checked.","src":[["Daughter's death, Qld 1926/C/97","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/06a54b7ba9f652af8e8baf1252a5ca159d03b0304ac0cb9938f82ac627132eff"],["Queensland assisted immigration index: Pearson family per David McIvor, 1863","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset?q=assisted+immigration+1848+to+1912"],["Death of William Overton Pearson, Qld 1884/C/4528","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2de24a7dbd7bcaf2132be09159440b890999d2a08be2b151d7faa4834d28e04a"],["Marriage of William Overton Pearson, Qld 1868/B/2392","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/9567e6e53841959531ebe3621aa785e83bda562792561c7d80d7f65a07578b8f"],["Birth of William Pearson Tudman, Qld 1876/C/3826","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a176a1b8b985b5d4bb786873291f30e9cd843c76cc920cc601f157938e987e41"]]},
hannahp:{"n":"Hannah Pearson","s":"h","p":[],"t":"Named as Hannah Pearson, the mother of Emma, on Emma's death registration at Bundaberg in 1926. Emma's first child, born in 1870, was christened Sarah Hannah, which supports the name.\n\nNo other record of her has been found. The only Emma Pearson of the right age on the Queensland immigration lists came in 1863 on the David McIvor with a father William and a mother called Elizabeth, both 39, and a William Pearson and Elizabeth Overton had a son in Queensland whose middle name Emma later used for one of her own boys. Either the informant in 1926 gave the grandmother's name wrongly, or that family was not Emma's. Emma's marriage certificate of 22 December 1869 (Brisbane registry, 1869/B/2837) is the record most likely to settle it.","src":[["Daughter's death, Qld 1926/C/97","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/06a54b7ba9f652af8e8baf1252a5ca159d03b0304ac0cb9938f82ac627132eff"],["Daughter's marriage, Qld 1869/B/2837","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d7236ace35eb0f05b65477d7faedef888adaa5f5edb0d1340a82bbab5d651aa2"],["Birth of Sarah Hannah Tudman, Qld 1870/B/11822","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/1bbb7558db5ab88b100ff12f8114047a52dbc4696b67033d4f6e3d2d943e3eb9"],["Queensland assisted immigration index: Pearson family per David McIvor, 1863","https://www.data.qld.gov.au/dataset?q=assisted+immigration+1848+to+1912"]]},
janrokus:{"n":"Jan Rokus de Kreij","d":"1838–1920 · Schelluinen","b":1838,"x":1920,"s":"b","p":["ariekr","gijsbertjeboer"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Rokus de Kreij was born at Schelluinen on 12 July 1838, the son of the labourer Arie de Kreij and Gijsbertje de Rover; he was born at ten in the morning and his father, then 42, registered the birth at three that afternoon. He was named after his mother's father, Jan Rokusse de Rover of Hardinxveld. His elder sister Jannigje married Cornelis Kroon at Schelluinen in 1860.\n\nOn 17 April 1863, aged 24, he married Lijntje Broeren, a farmer's daughter of seventeen, at Hoornaar. He was then a workman (werkman), living at Schelluinen, and had done his militia service; both sets of parents were present. Their children were Arie (born October 1863, who died at seven weeks), a second Arie (1864), Lijntje (1865, who died at three months), another Lijntje (1867, who died at two months) and Gijsbertje, born at Gorinchem on 23 April 1869 and named after his mother.\n\nLijntje died before the end of 1874, probably at Gorinchem, and on 19 December 1874, aged 36 and now described as a farmer (bouwman), he married Wilhelmina Slagboom, 29, of Hardinxveld at Schelluinen; she was the widow of Willem Dubbeldam. Their daughter Neeltje lived three weeks in 1876; their son Arie, born in 1879, married Jenneke Roeland in 1899 and died at The Hague in 1965. By 1889 he was again a workman at Schelluinen, when he gave his consent to Gijsbertje's marriage to Leendert de Bruijn. He died at Schelluinen on 17 June 1920, aged 81, a widower for the second time.","src":[["Birth record, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:0c4946fc-c527-4adc-6e28-24c06d12e90c"],["Death record, 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:47a39f9b-8971-98ae-60b3-2adc51d4024c"],["Birth register, Schelluinen 1838","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6876679"],["First marriage, Hoornaar, 17 April 1863","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20980789"],["Second marriage, Schelluinen, 19 December 1874","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21084628"],["Marriage of sister Jannigje, 1860","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21082097"],["Death of son Arie, The Hague 1965","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hga:A6573104-0CFF-40E1-8EDC-002E033D2E2B"]]},
lijntjeb:{"n":"Lijntje Broere","d":"1845–by 1874 · Hoornaar","s":"b","p":["pieterbroeren","lijntjevogel"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Lijntje Broeren (also written Broere) was born at Hoornaar on 7 May 1845, the ninth of the fifteen children of the farmer Pieter Broeren and Lijntje Vogel.\n\nOn 17 April 1863, aged 17, she married Jan Rokus de Kreij, a 24-year-old workman from Schelluinen, at Hoornaar; her parents were present and consented. Her mother died a year later, in April 1864. Her children were Arie (4 October 1863, who died at seven weeks), a second Arie (29 October 1864), Lijntje (9 December 1865, who died in March 1866), another Lijntje (14 June 1867, who died that August), and Gijsbertje, born at Gorinchem on 23 April 1869. In 1867 her sister Adriana married Tijmen van Veen, whose sister Jannigje was the mother of Leendert de Bruijn, Gijsbertje's future husband.\n\nShe died between April 1869 and December 1874, when her husband remarried at Schelluinen as \"widower of Lijntje Broere\". The family had moved to Gorinchem by 1869, and she probably died there, but the town's civil records for those years are not indexed and her death record has not been found.","src":[["Birth of Arie, 4 Oct 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Kreij+%26+Broeren"],["Death of Arie, Peursum, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:09ba3920-502b-f002-c6f4-016154fd3065"],["Birth of her daughter Lijntje, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:8e348ba2-6e18-df42-b6f9-4b7799c883d0"],["Birth of Lijntje Broeren, Hoornaar, 7 May 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Lijntje+Vogel+%26+Broeren"],["Birth register, Hoornaar 1845, no. 7","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6673206"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 17 April 1863","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20980789"],["Husband's second marriage, 1874","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21084628"],["Birth of son Arie, 1863","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6880963"]],"b":1845},
pieterbroeren:{"n":"Pieter Broere (Broeren)","d":"1803–1890 · Noordeloos","s":"b","p":["zacornelisbroere","zaannigjenieuwpoort"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Pieter Broere, whose name is also written Broeren, was born on 13 August 1803, the son of Cornelis Broere and Annigje Nieuwpoort, farmers (bouwlieden) at Hoornaar. His 1830 marriage record gives his birthplace as Noordeloos, his death record as Hoornaar.\n\nHis first wife, Trijntje Gelderblom, died at Hoornaar, and their son Kornelis died there in February 1830. On 5 December 1830, aged 27 and a farmer (landbouwer), he married Lijntje Vogel, a seventeen-year-old daughter of day labourers, at Hoornaar, with both his parents present. They had fifteen children at Hoornaar between 1832 and 1856: Annigje, Kornelis, Jannigje, Jan (who lived a month), a second Jan, Pieter, Marigje, Adriana, Lijntje, Bart, Geertje, Dirk, Teuntje, Grietje and Huibert (who died aged two). Jannigje died at 31 and Marigje at 23.\n\nHe was still farming at Hoornaar in 1863, when his daughter Lijntje married Jan Rokus de Kreij. His wife died in April 1864. His children married across the Alblasserwaard and beyond, at Giessen-Nieuwkerk, Noordeloos, Peursum, Bleskensgraaf, Ottoland and Giessendam. He died at Giessen-Nieuwkerk on 11 September 1890, aged 86, without occupation, a widower; his son Kornelis had married there in 1865.","src":[["Hoornaar births (Regional Archives Gorinchem)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Lijntje+Vogel+%26+Broeren"],["Second marriage, Hoornaar, 5 December 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"],["Death register, Giessen-Nieuwkerk 1890, no. 17","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6600036"],["Death of son Kornelis, 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7463700"],["Marriage of daughter Lijntje, 1863","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20980789"]],"b":1803,"x":1890},
lijntjevogel:{"n":"Lijntje Vogel","s":"b","p":["zajanvogel","zajannigjevdheijden"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Lijntje Vogel was born on 2 July 1813 and registered at Hoogblokland, which then also administered Hoornaar, the daughter of Jan Vogel and Jannigje van der Heijden, day labourers (daglooners).\n\nOn 5 December 1830, aged 17, she married Pieter Broere, a widowed farmer of 27, at Hoornaar. Over the next twenty-four years she had fifteen children, from Annigje in December 1832 to Huibert in September 1856. Her son Jan died at a month old in 1837 and Huibert at two in 1858; her daughter Marigje died in 1865 at 23 and Jannigje in 1867 at 31. Lijntje, the ninth child, was born in 1845.\n\nShe saw her daughter Annigje married to Arie Verspui in 1857 and Lijntje to Jan Rokus de Kreij in April 1863. She died at Hoornaar at five in the afternoon of Friday 29 April 1864, aged 50, the wife of the farmer (landbouwer) Pieter Broere; both her parents were then dead. (The archive's index gives the date as 29 May; the register itself says April.) Her brother Huibert Vogel died there eight months later.","src":[["Hoornaar births (Regional Archives Gorinchem)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Lijntje+Vogel+%26+Broeren"],["Birth register, Hoogblokland 1813","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6624658"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 5 December 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"],["Death register, Hoornaar 1864, no. 5 (scan read)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7471739"],["Death of brother Huibert Vogel, 1864","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7471859"]],"d":"1813–1864 · Hoornaar","b":1813,"x":1864},
willembr:{"n":"Willem de Bruijn","d":"1838–1875 · Hoornaar","b":1838,"x":1875,"s":"b","p":["leendertb0","metjeh"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Willem de Bruijn was born at Hoornaar on 22 February 1838, the son of the farmer Leendert de Bruijn and Metje Hagen, and one of four of their eight children to grow up. His mother died when he was five, nine months after the birth of her last baby, and his father did not remarry. His father died in 1863.\n\nOn 4 October 1867, aged 29, he married Jannigje van Veen at Meerkerk, in the Vijfheerenlanden. The very next day her brother Tijmen van Veen married Adriana Broeren at Hoornaar; Adriana's younger sister Lijntje was the mother of the girl who would one day marry Willem's eldest son. A month after the wedding Jannigje's father died.\n\nTheir children were all born at Hoornaar: Leendert (1868), Katharina (1869), Kornelis (1870), Maarten (1872), Metje (1873) and Willempje (1875). By then Willem was a dealer (koopman), buying and selling goods in the district. He died at Hoornaar on 25 October 1875, aged 37, seventeen days after Willempje was born; the schoolmaster Antonie Arie in 't Veld was one of the two neighbours who reported the death. Little Metje died the following June, aged two. His widow married Kornelis den Burger in 1887 and died at Leerdam in 1895; her death record still names her as the widow of Willem de Bruin.","src":[["Birth record, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:00693f0e-7a19-bb9f-69f2-599493956883"],["Marriage, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:d01513da-21dd-4b3d-bf4f-0fa27a135c98"],["Death record, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:d5b64e90-bb22-deca-f734-1b88a6871b36"],["Birth register, Hoornaar 1838, no. 6","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6677695"],["Death register, Hoornaar 1875, no. 18","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7465912"],["Death of daughter Metje, 1876","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7466197"],["Marriage of Tijmen van Veen and Adriana Broeren, 1867","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20982089"]]},
jannigjev:{"n":"Jannigje van Veen","d":"1842–1895 · Meerkerk","b":1842,"s":"b","p":["cornelisvv","catharinaw"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jannigje van Veen was born at Meerkerk, in the Vijfheerenlanden, in 1842, the thirteenth of the sixteen children of Cornelis van Veen and Catharina Wink. Her birth record gives 13 March 1842, while her second marriage record gives 13 February. Six of her brothers and sisters died as babies or young adults.\n\nOn 4 October 1867, aged 25, she married Willem de Bruijn of Hoornaar at Meerkerk; her father died a month later. They had six children at Hoornaar: Leendert (1868), Katharina (1869), Kornelis (1870), Maarten (1872), Metje (1873) and Willempje (1875). Kornelis and Katharina carried her parents' names. Willem died on 25 October 1875, when Willempje was seventeen days old, and two-year-old Metje died the following June, leaving her a widow with five children under eight.\n\nShe supported herself as a seamstress (naaister). On 5 May 1887, at Hoornaar, aged 45, she married Kornelis den Burger, a 45-year-old workman born at Schoonrewoerd, the son of Joost den Burger and Maria Verschoor, and himself a widower. Two years later she gave her consent at the wedding of her eldest son Leendert. She died at Leerdam on 11 July 1895, aged 53; the record names her as the wife of Cornelis den Burger and the earlier widow of Willem de Bruin.","src":[["Marriage, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:d01513da-21dd-4b3d-bf4f-0fa27a135c98"],["Birth record, Meerkerk 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:d6359231-6536-4f17-a709-18ab28daf7a5"],["Second marriage, Hoornaar, 5 May 1887","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20986751"],["Death record, Leerdam 1895","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:eed9ccb2-d8c9-4d28-baa3-2e3c9868aa34"],["Death register of husband Willem, Hoornaar 1875","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7465912"]],"x":1895},
leendertb0:{"n":"Leendert de Bruijn","d":"1799–1863 · Hoornaar","x":1863,"s":"b","p":["jakobbr","susannav"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Leendert de Bruijn was born at Hoornaar on 31 August 1799, the youngest known child of Jakob de Bruijn and Susanna de Vaal. His mother died when he was nineteen.\n\nOn 6 July 1827, aged 27, he married Metje Hagen at Hoornaar. He was a farmer (bouwman); his father, then living on his own means at Hoornaar, was present and consented. Metje was 19 and had lost both parents, so her consent came from a family council held before the cantonal court at Gorinchem. Five years earlier Leendert's elder brother Jakob had married Metje's sister Cornelia: two brothers married two sisters.\n\nEight children were born at Hoornaar: Susanna (1828, who died at two weeks), a second Susanna (1829), Marigje (1830, who died at three weeks), Frans (1831), Jakob (1835, who died aged six), Willem (1838), a second Marigje (1840) and Jakob (1843, who lived three weeks). Metje died in February 1844, leaving him with four children under fifteen. He did not remarry; his death record calls him the widower of Metje Hagen.\n\nHis daughters Susanna and Marigje married two brothers, Teunis and Jan Bogaard, in 1854 and 1865, and Frans married Marigje Molenaar in 1858. His work changed over the years: a farmer in 1827, he was a workman (werkman) when Metje died in 1844, and a skipper (schipper) when he died at Hoornaar on 14 June 1863, aged 63. Much of the traffic of the Alblasserwaard went by water, along its rivers, canals and wide drainage ditches.","src":[["Death record, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:783681a5-2f2f-eda0-1a35-ebea4af84841"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 6 July 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"],["Death register, Hoornaar 1863, no. 7","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7471515"],["Marriage of daughter Susanna, 1854","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20978763"],["Marriage of son Frans, 1858","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20979775"],["Marriage of daughter Marigje, 1865","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20981366"]],"b":1799},
metjeh:{"n":"Metje Hagen","d":"1807–1844 · Arkel","x":1844,"s":"b","p":["franshagen","marigjeb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Metje Hagen was born at Arkel, on the river Linge just north of Gorinchem, on 8 November 1807, the youngest known child of Frans Hagen and Marigje Bronkhorst. Her father was dead before she was twenty, and her mother died in September 1826.\n\nWhen she married Leendert de Bruijn at Hoornaar on 6 July 1827 she was 19, still a minor, and worked on a farm (\"boerenbedrijf uitoefenende\"); she had lived at Hoornaar but for the previous six months at Arkel. Because her parents and all four grandparents were dead, her consent came from a family council held before the cantonal court of Gorinchem on 19 June 1827. Under the Dutch civil law of the time, an orphaned minor could only marry with the approval of such a council of relatives, presided over by the cantonal judge. Her sister Cornelia had married Leendert's brother Jakob in 1822.\n\nShe had eight children at Hoornaar between 1828 and 1843: Susanna, a second Susanna, Marigje, Frans, Jakob, Willem, a second Marigje and a last Jakob. Four died young, three of them as babies. Her husband was by then working as a labourer (werkman). She died at Hoornaar on 16 February 1844, nine months after her last baby, leaving four children, the youngest three years old. Her death record gives her age as 37, though she was 36, and was signed by her brother-in-law Jakob de Bruijn, then a shopkeeper (winkelier) at Hoornaar; it also notes that both her parents had died at Arkel.","src":[["Death record, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:cc28d411-3acf-f6a4-8341-aa445d4685fe"],["Marriage, Hoornaar, 6 July 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"],["Death register, Hoornaar 1844, no. 1","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7467283"]],"b":1807},
jakobbr:{"n":"Jakob de Bruijn","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jakob (also written Jacob) de Bruijn, or de Bruin, was born at Ottoland, a village in the heart of the Alblasserwaard, about 1761, and lived most of his life at nearby Hoornaar. With his wife Susanna de Vaal he had at least six children there: Cornelis (8 September 1787), Eigje (1790), Aaltje (1792), Jakob (1794), Barbera (1796) and Leendert (31 August 1799). Susanna died in September 1819; her death record says she left seven children, so one more child has still to be identified.\n\nAll six children married: Aaltje to Dirk Verhoef and Eigje to Teunis Vink at Hoogblokland in 1814 and 1815, Cornelis to Maaike Verwoerd at Hoornaar in 1817, Barbera to Arie Gelderblom in 1821, Jakob to Cornelia Hagen at Arkel in 1822 and Leendert to Cornelia's sister Metje in 1827.\n\nAt Leendert's wedding in July 1827 he was described as \"particulier\", a man living on his own means rather than working a farm himself, and he came to the town hall to give his consent. He died at Hoornaar on Sunday 20 August 1848 at eight in the evening, aged 87 and without occupation. His death record names him as the widower of Susanna de Vaal and gives his birthplace as Ottoland; his parents' names were not known to the two farmers who reported the death.","d":"c. 1761–1848 · Ottoland","b":1761,"x":1848,"src":[["Marriage of son Leendert, Hoornaar 1827 (names him as father)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"],["Marriage of son Cornelis, 1817","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20972560"],["Marriage of daughter Eigje, 1815","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20935341"],["Marriage of daughter Aaltje, 1814","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20935108"],["Marriage of son Jakob, Arkel 1822","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20830988"],["Marriage of daughter Barbera, 1821","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20973077"],["Death register, Hoornaar 1848, no. 8","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7468137"]]},
susannav:{"n":"Susanna de Vaal","s":"b","p":["zacornelisvaal","zaeigjevdpol"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Susanna de Vaal was born at Laag-Blokland, a small village beside Hoornaar, about 1764, the daughter of Cornelis de Vaal and Eigje, whose surname is partly lost in the binding of the register and appears to be van der Pol. She married Jakob de Bruijn of Hoornaar and was the mother of at least six children born there between 1787 and 1799: Cornelis (1787), Eigje (1790), Aaltje (1792), Jakob (1794), Barbera (1796) and Leendert (1799). Her name is written \"de Vaal\" in every record of her children, and her two eldest, Cornelis and Eigje, carried her parents' names.\n\nShe died at Hoornaar early on the morning of 5 September 1819, aged 55, leaving seven children; the neighbours who reported the death were a farmer and a carpenter. By then her daughters Aaltje and Eigje had married at Hoogblokland and her son Cornelis at Hoornaar; Barbera, Jakob and Leendert married in the years after her death, and at Leendert's wedding in 1827 she is named as deceased. Her parents are named only in her death record, so the link to them rests on that one source and the surname of her mother is uncertain. Her name was carried on by her granddaughters: Leendert named his first two daughters Susanna, and her son Jakob had a daughter Susanna too.","d":"c. 1764–1819 · Laag-Blokland","b":1764,"x":1819,"u":true,"src":[["Death register, Hoornaar 1819","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7461783"],["Marriage of son Leendert, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"]]},
franshagen:{"n":"Frans Hagen","s":"b","p":["zadirkhagen","zacorneliaboon"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Frans Hagen, whose name is also written Hage, lived at Arkel, a village on the Linge just north of Gorinchem that gave its name to the medieval lords of Arkel. He was the son of Dirk Hagen and Cornelia Boon, named as his daughter Metje's grandparents at her marriage in 1827.\n\nWith his wife Marigje Bronkhorst he had a family born at Arkel over twenty years: Dirk (5 February 1787), Maaike (1794), Cornelia (1801), Marigje (1804) and Metje (8 November 1807), as well as Cornelis, who died at Arkel in 1836, and Arie, who died there in 1875. Maaike married Jan Muilwijk at Kedichem in 1814 and, as a widow, Peter Blokland in 1824; Cornelia married Jakob de Bruijn of Hoornaar in 1822; Marigje married Arie van Arkel in 1826; and Dirk married Maaike van der Voort in March 1827.\n\nA Frans Hage died at Arkel on 11 March 1815, aged 57, three months after Maaike's wedding at Kedichem. The index of that death record names no wife or parents, so it is not proven to be him, but no other Frans Hage(n) appears at Arkel, the age fits a father of children born from 1787, and his wife was a widow by the time of her own death in 1826.","d":"c. 1758–1815 · Arkel","b":1758,"x":1815,"src":[["Marriage of daughter Metje, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"],["Marriage of daughter Maaike, Kedichem 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:7cc7a3f8-cefb-4c55-9571-ed99ee1d7d50"],["Marriage of daughter Cornelia, Arkel 1822","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20830988"],["Marriage of daughter Maaike, Arkel 1824","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20831344"],["Marriage of daughter Marigje, Arkel 1826","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20831824"],["Marriage of son Dirk, Arkel 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20831969"],["Death of wife Marigje Bronkhorst, 1826 (calls her his widow)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6479146"],["Death of Frans Hage, Arkel, 11 March 1815 (probably him)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:d960c8a1-3219-4359-ad37-e4e313a4fcfe"]]},
marigjeb:{"n":"Marigje Bronkhorst","s":"b","p":["zaariebronkhorst","zamaaikevervoorn"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Marigje Bronkhorst was born at Arkel about 1765, the daughter of Arie Bronkhorst and Maaike Vervoorn. She married Frans Hagen and lived at Arkel, where their children were born between 1787 and 1807: Dirk, Maaike, Cornelia, Marigje and Metje, and also Cornelis and Arie.\n\nShe saw three of her daughters married: Maaike to Jan Muilwijk at Kedichem in 1814, Cornelia to Jakob de Bruijn in 1822 and Marigje to Arie van Arkel in August 1826. By then she was a widow. She died at Arkel, in house number 23, on 18 September 1826, aged 61, a month after the last of these weddings; two labourers who lived nearby reported the death. Her youngest, Metje, was then eighteen, and married Leendert de Bruijn of Hoornaar the following summer with the consent of a family council.","d":"c. 1765–1826 · Arkel","b":1765,"x":1826,"src":[["Death register, Arkel 1826, no. 7 (scan read)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6479146"],["Death record, 1826 (Open Archives)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:9eb728cf-3878-bfc8-c529-92be4a44974f"],["Marriage of daughter Metje, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"]]},
cornelisvv:{"n":"Cornelis van Veen","d":"c. 1793–1867 · Meerkerk","s":"b","p":["zadirkvveen","zajannigjedonk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis van Veen was born at Meerkerk about 1793, the son of Dirk van Veen and Jannigje Donk. Meerkerk is a village in the Vijfheerenlanden, the strip of polder country between the Lek and the Linge east of the Alblasserwaard.\n\nOn 22 September 1821, aged 29, he married Catrijntje (Catharina) Wink, who was 17 and also from Meerkerk, at Willige Langerak on the Utrecht side of the Lek. They had sixteen children between 1822 and 1846: Johanna (1822, who died at seven weeks), Dirk (1823), a second Johanna (1825, who died in 1851 aged 25), Andries (1827), Jan (1829), the twins Jannigje and Geertrui (1830, both of whom died as infants), Geertrui (1832), Gerrit (1835), a stillborn son (1836), Peter (1837), Cornelis (1839), Jannigje (1842), Tijmen (1844) and Willem (1846, who lived five months).\n\nHis sons married in the villages round about: Dirk and Jan at Hei- en Boeicop, Gerrit at Noordeloos in 1860, Peter at Meerkerk in 1865, and Tijmen at Hoornaar in 1867, to Adriana Broeren. His daughter Jannigje married Willem de Bruijn at Meerkerk on 4 October 1867. Cornelis died a month later, on 7 November 1867, aged 74.","b":1793,"x":1867,"src":[["Marriage, Willige Langerak, 22 September 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D8A37F05-F6EE-4A3B-A66A-7DABD0715BD9"],["Death record, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:48344196-2b31-432a-9cef-497a964c310a"],["Births and deaths of his children (search)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Cornelis+van+Veen+%26+Catharina+Wink"]]},
catharinaw:{"n":"Catharina (Catrijntje) Wink","d":"c. 1804–1878 · Meerkerk","s":"b","p":["zaandrieswink","zajohannadonk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Catharina Wink, called Catrijntje at her marriage, was born at Meerkerk about 1804, the daughter of Andries Wink and Johanna Donk. Her father died in April 1817, when she was about twelve. Several of her brothers moved away: Andries to Ameide, and Cornelis and Johannes north to the Zuid- and Noord-Scharwoude area of North Holland, where Cornelis worked as a baker's man and later as a farmer, and Johannes as a wheelwright (wagenmaker).\n\nOn 22 September 1821, aged 17, she married Cornelis van Veen of Meerkerk at Willige Langerak. Over the next twenty-four years she gave birth sixteen times, including twins in 1830 and a stillborn son in 1836. Five of the children died as babies and her daughter Johanna died in 1851 at the age of 25. Among those who grew up were Dirk, Andries, Jan, Geertrui, Gerrit, Peter, Cornelis, Jannigje and Tijmen.\n\nShe was widowed on 7 November 1867, a month after her daughter Jannigje married Willem de Bruijn. She died on 5 June 1878, aged 73; her death record names her parents and calls her the widow of Cornelis van Veen.","b":1804,"x":1878,"src":[["Marriage, Willige Langerak, 22 September 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D8A37F05-F6EE-4A3B-A66A-7DABD0715BD9"],["Death record, 1878","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:1c2adf7d-8d77-49a3-bc46-e89223ea9237"],["Death of father Andries Wink, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:42cc4abc-8262-4afc-8e81-7b2c4c827f0f"]]},
ariekr:{"n":"Arie de Kreij","d":"c. 1795–1887 · Schelluinen","b":1795,"s":"b","p":["zageerlofkreij","zagrietjekreij"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Arie de Kreij was born at Schelluinen about 1795, the son of Geerlof (Geelof) de Kreij and Grietje de Kreij, farmers (bouwlieden) who lived and died there. His brothers Kornelis, Jan and Huig Rokusse and his sister Martijntje also married in Schelluinen and Hoornaar.\n\nHis first wife was Grietje Boonstoppel, and their children were born at Schelluinen: Adriana (6 March 1828), Grietje (8 September 1829), who married Cornelis van Houwelingen and died at Hoornaar in 1898, and Geelof (2 August 1832), named after Arie's father, who died in April 1834. Grietje Boonstoppel died at Schelluinen before 1836. On 27 August 1836, aged 41, he married Gijsbertje de Rover, a 26-year-old servant from Hardinxveld, at Schelluinen. He was a labourer (arbeider) living in the Banne of Gorinchem, the countryside outside the town walls that came under the town's jurisdiction. Their children included Jannigje, born at Gorinchem, who married Cornelis Kroon in 1860, and Jan Rokus, born at Schelluinen in 1838. When Jan Rokus was born he registered the birth himself, aged 42. (An earlier version of this tree read his age in that record as 32; the register says forty-two.)\n\nHe was still a workman when he was widowed on 26 March 1885. He died at Schelluinen at eleven at night on 20 January 1887, aged 91; his death record names his parents and calls him the widower of Gijsbertje de Rover. One of the two men who reported it was Bart de Kreij, a workman of 54.","x":1887,"src":[["Second marriage, Schelluinen, 27 August 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death register, Schelluinen 1887, no. 1 (scan read)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6909400"],["Birth of son Jan Rokus, 1838 (gives his age as 42)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6876679"],["Death of son Geelof, 1834","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6900642"],["Birth of daughter Adriana, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:adbf895a-c1a5-9411-dcb1-c660be9a1edf"],["Birth of daughter Grietje, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:2f1b760d-ac40-c00b-1c0b-9a5dc912954d"],["Death of daughter Grietje, Hoornaar 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rag:af7b462b-85e7-ec2e-8980-cfb2addcb9eb"]]},
gijsbertjeboer:{"n":"Gijsbertje de Rover","d":"c. 1810–1885 · Hardinxveld","s":"b","p":["zajanrover","zageertruivandel"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gijsbertje de Rover was born at Hardinxveld, a long dyke village on the Merwede, about 1810, the daughter of Jan Rokusse de Rover and Geertrui van Andel, labourers there. Earlier versions of this tree called her Gijsbertje de Boer; every marriage and death record that names her has de Rover.\n\nAs a young woman she went into service at Schelluinen, and on 27 August 1836, aged 26, she married the widowed labourer Arie de Kreij there; both her parents came to the wedding. Their children included Jannigje, born at Gorinchem, and Jan Rokus, born at Schelluinen in 1838 and named after her father. Her mother died at Schelluinen in 1873.\n\nShe died at Schelluinen at eight in the evening of 26 March 1885, aged 74, the wife of the workman Arie de Kreij, who died two years later. The two neighbours who reported her death did not know her parents' names. Her granddaughter Gijsbertje de Kreij, born in 1869, carried her name.","b":1810,"x":1885,"src":[["Marriage, Schelluinen, 27 August 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death register, Schelluinen 1885, no. 5","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6909088"],["Marriage of son Jan Rokus, 1863 (names her as de Rover)","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20980789"]]},
henryharvey:{"n":"Henry Augustus Harvey","s":"y","p":[],"r":["au"],"t":"The father of Ethel Augusta Harvey. He appears in the Queensland records only as the father named in four birth registrations, and nothing is known of his birthplace, his work or his parents.\n\nWith Ellen Elizabeth Alcock, a young widow whose first husband, Henry James Wilmott, had died in September 1864, he registered four children: Harold Augustus (born 20 January 1866, died 15 December 1866), Ethel Augusta (28 April 1868), Eleanor Frances Hoskins (17 October 1870) and Thurza Anne (24 June 1874). He was entered as Henry Harvey for the first two and as Henry Augustus Harvey for the last two, and both his eldest children were given a form of his middle name. No marriage for the couple appears in the Queensland index.\n\nIn July 1872, between Eleanor and Thurza, Ellen registered a son, Sydney Edward, under her own surnames of Alcock and Wilmott with no father named. Nothing more is found of Henry after 1874. No death in the Queensland index can be tied to him, and on 31 December 1878 Ellen, calling herself Ellen Elizabeth Harvey, married Arthur James Hosking. His surname lived on in his grandson Robert Harvey Napier, born in 1894.","src":[["Queensland BDM historical index","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Births of Harold Augustus (Qld 1866/C/138), Ethel Augusta (1868/C/2865), Eleanor Frances Hoskins (1870/C/3200) and Thurza Anne (1874/C/4438) Harvey","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Birth of Sydney Edward Wilmott, Qld 1872/C/3523","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a87450c5c3b8afdff0dceba44d288992c98af7d2c57c62f9eef17f3e1263c3be"],["Marriage of Ellen Elizabeth Harvey and Arthur James Hosking, Qld 1878/C/941","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/72455900a9d945a178add21a742e2a83e112d448a7e6aea421ddb36a65fb0543"]]},
ellenalcock:{"n":"Ellen Elizabeth Alcock","s":"y","p":["hythomasalcock","hyannturner"],"r":["au"],"t":"The mother of Ethel Augusta Harvey, and the daughter of Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner, whose names are given in her death registration. Where she was born is not known; she was in Queensland by 1864, five years after it became a separate colony.\n\nAs Ellen Elizabeth Alcock she married Henry James Wilmott on 4 June 1864, but he died on 20 September, three and a half months after the wedding. With Henry Augustus Harvey she then registered four children: Harold Augustus (born January 1866, died that December), Ethel Augusta (1868), Eleanor Frances Hoskins (1870) and Thurza Anne (1874). In July 1872 she also registered a son, Sydney Edward, as Ellen Alcock Wilmott, with no father named.\n\nOn 31 December 1878, as Ellen Elizabeth Harvey, she married Arthur James Hosking; Hosking, or Hoskins, was the middle name she had given her daughter Eleanor eight years before. The couple lived at Townsville. Arthur died on 16 March 1894, aged 47, in one of the colony's hospitals for the insane, of cirrhosis of the liver; the weekly report described him as late of Townsville.\n\nHer daughter Ethel named her first child Gladys Elizabeth Hosking Napier in 1890. Ellen died on 21 March 1904, registered as Ellen Hosking.","src":[["Queensland BDM historical index","https://familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["Death of Ellen Hosking, Qld 1904/C/3849 (parents Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/0f8dbe9761b6d6bbb73bbe0746d544c49be2deb01449095eef9b86a96e2dfc98"],["Marriage to Henry James Wilmott, Qld 1864/C/291","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/1cf35b17eea37c34eb92fedd619c90ff57f3d9ee731f5eb3129f5a3501f27256"],["Death of Henry Wilmott, Qld 1864/C/448","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/63b281f8efbf21a9355b6fd8871f0769d01c792a2a00152ead07ea407e13ef2f"],["Birth of Sydney Edward Wilmott, Qld 1872/C/3523","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a87450c5c3b8afdff0dceba44d288992c98af7d2c57c62f9eef17f3e1263c3be"],["Marriage to Arthur James Hosking, Qld 1878/C/941","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/72455900a9d945a178add21a742e2a83e112d448a7e6aea421ddb36a65fb0543"],["Death of Arthur James Hosking, Qld 1894/C/3488","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/294923f3e3ef768bdef88cbb98ca34c135b169b7709f2356fec26771966bd3e1"],["Hospitals for the Insane, weekly report, Telegraph, 20 Mar 1894","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article172551535"]],"d":"d. 1904","x":1904},
hendricusd:{"n":"Hendricus Degenhart","d":"Kampen","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"The earliest Degenhart traced so far. In 1795, when the new Batavian Republic counted its people, Hendricus Degenhart was head of a household of eight living behind the Nieuwe Muur in the upper quarter (Boven Wijk) of Kampen; his wife gave the details to the census taker. When his son Johannes married in 1815 he was a labourer (arbeider).\n\nWith Elisabeth van den Berg he had Johannes (baptised 1790 or 1791), Catharina (baptised 6 March 1792), Hendricus (born about 1793) and Hendrika (baptised 7 June 1804). Johannes Jacobus Degenhardt, a tailor who died at Kampen in 1816 aged 30, was probably their eldest son, though his death record names no parents. Johannes and Hendricus both became tailors. Catharina married Hendrik Bombam, a weaver of trijp (a woollen velvet), in 1818, and Hendrika married Cornelis de Weijs, a sergeant from Breda, in 1833.\n\nElisabeth died on 25 June 1812, aged 47. His own death has not been found in the civil registers of Kampen or the rest of Overijssel. The Catholic baptism registers of Kampen before 1811 have not been indexed, so his birthplace and parents are still unknown. Degenhart is a German surname.\n\nHis youngest daughter Hendrika later lived in Utrecht with her husband, Cornelis de Wijs; the Utrecht population register gives her birth as 17 June 1804, ten days after the baptism date in her marriage record. A second Degenhard family also lived in Kampen in the 1840s: Christiaan Hendrik Degenhard, a soldier, whose wife Anna Rhenius, born at Zutphen, died there in 1853 aged 89. No record links the two men. Dutch records of the 1700s and early 1800s show Degenhards in Amsterdam, Delft and Groningen as well, but none has been tied to Hendricus, and his own birthplace is still unknown.","src":[["Kampen census of 1795 (Volkstelling), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=Degenhart"],["Marriage of son Johannes, 1815 (father a labourer)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:FE3C50C6-80DB-479F-B7C0-112E5E541F98"],["Marriage of daughter Catharina, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:6198B6CE-8BB9-46B2-91C5-6FECF4E01346"],["Marriage of son Hendricus, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:7360A016-B6EE-4D74-8817-FCCDD0E6DBBD"],["Marriage of daughter Hendrika, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:285E42D6-6B44-4DAB-8926-DCB09116A6F9"],["Death of Johannes Jacobus Degenhardt, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:1B19417F-1705-4008-910C-20D1EE836850"],["Death of son Hendrikus, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5D4BCF14-B445-4D9A-AE2B-BF20B8CFAA25"],["Utrecht population register, Hendrica Degenhardt (b. Kampen 17 June 1804)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:E5CA42A0-4F7D-788E-E053-8F04000A542C"],["Death of Anna Rhenius, wife of Christiaan Hendrik Degenhard, Kampen 1853","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:719C0060-A91E-4F24-9B03-4A7FCA95CEC7"]]},
elisabethvdb:{"n":"Elisabeth van den Berg","d":"c. 1765–1812","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Hendricus Degenhart of Kampen. She died there on 25 June 1812, aged 47, which puts her birth about 1765. The record does not name her parents or her birthplace.\n\nIn the census of 1795 it was she who answered for the household of eight behind the Nieuwe Muur. The census taker wrote her name as Barta van den Berg, while the civil records of her children all call her Elisabeth or Elizabeth. A granddaughter born in 1827 was named Bertha.\n\nHer known children were Johannes (baptised 1790 or 1791), Catharina (1792), Hendricus (about 1793) and Hendrika (1804), and probably Johannes Jacobus, a tailor who died in 1816 aged 30. Hendrika was only eight when her mother died.\n\nA month before her death, on 24 May 1812, an unmarried Anna Catharina Willemina Degenhard, very probably her daughter Catharina, gave birth to a girl who was named Elizabeth. That child grew up to marry the tailor Dirk Vreenegoor, and through her Elisabeth van den Berg is an ancestor of this family twice over.","b":1765,"x":1812,"src":[["Death record, 25 June 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:84E368F0-0B84-4BC4-B073-34685D1E44E1"],["Kampen census of 1795 (Volkstelling), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=Degenhart"],["Marriage of daughter Hendrika, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:285E42D6-6B44-4DAB-8926-DCB09116A6F9"],["Birth of granddaughter Bertha, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:97118AC1-AF99-4809-88ED-1E2F0318DB9F"]]},
joannesdorr:{"n":"Joannes Dorr","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Father of Maria Elisabeth Dorr, named with his wife Margaritha Tellemans in her marriage record of 15 November 1832. That record says Maria Elisabeth was baptised at Kampen on 1 October 1799.\n\nThe Kampen census of 1795 lists a Jan Dörr on the Vloeddijk, in the upper quarter of the town, in a household of six that shared a house with Reijndert Morra. He is very probably the same man. Forty years later his daughter and her husband, the tailor Johannes Degenhart, owned a house on the same street.\n\nOther people named Dörr appear in the Kampen records of the next generation and may have been his children: Elizabeth, who died in 1813 aged 19; Barbera, wife of Frederik Hendrik Wils, who died in 1824 aged 34; and Alegonda, who died in 1841 aged 44. None of their death records names parents, and no death record for Joannes himself has been found after 1811, when civil registration began. Dörr is a German surname.","d":"Kampen","src":[["Marriage of his daughter, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6402D82-E48E-4CD2-ABD8-1F94ED92D2B3"],["Kampen census of 1795 (Jan Dörr, Vloeddijk), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=D*rr"],["Death of Elizabeth Dörr, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:57B02F09-F5E9-4E9D-9D20-015F5190F181"],["Death of Barbera Dörr, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:21AD6FB1-8CCF-42C5-9673-C7BE02A8A891"],["Death of Alegonda Dorr, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D7CB8073-99F1-4196-A6F3-718FC58E30C6"]]},
margaritha:{"n":"Margaritha Tellemans","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Mother of Maria Elisabeth Dorr. She is known from one record only: when her daughter married the tailor Johannes Degenhart at Kampen on 15 November 1832, the bride's parents were written down as Joannes Dorr and Margaritha Tellemans. The same record says the daughter was baptised at Kampen on 1 October 1799.\n\nIn the census of 1795 the household of Jan Dörr on the Vloeddijk counted six people. No other Tellemans has been found in the Kampen civil registers between 1811 and 1850, and her own death is not recorded there under that name. The Catholic baptism and marriage registers of Kampen from before 1811, where her marriage and her children's baptisms should be, have not yet been indexed by name.","d":"Kampen","src":[["Marriage of her daughter, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6402D82-E48E-4CD2-ABD8-1F94ED92D2B3"],["Kampen census of 1795 (Jan Dörr, Vloeddijk), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=D*rr"]]},
janwijnstra:{"n":"Jan Jilts Wijnstra","s":"h","p":["ezjiltgeerts","ezjankehepkes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 30 September 1794 and baptised there on 2 November, the son of Jilt Geerts Wijnstra and Janke Hepkes. He grew up in a large family: Ebeltje, Hepke, Geert, Roelke, Douwe and Hendrik are all known. His father was later called a farmer, but left no real estate when he died in 1829.\n\nOn 22 May 1828, aged 33, he married Albertje Hendriks de Vries, 30, of Hemrik, at Opsterland. Their children were Jilt (12 March 1829), named after Jan's father, Hendrik (13 June 1830), named after hers, Neeltje (2 June 1832), Aize (29 September 1833) and Fokke (9 May 1836).\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider) at Hemrik. His son Jilt became a farmer at Lippenhuizen; Hendrik stayed a labourer there; Neeltje married Willem Berends Huberts in 1856; and Aize became a cattle farmer at Hemrik.\n\nHe died at Hemrik on 27 June 1860, aged 66. Albertje outlived him by eleven years.","src":[["Marriage of Jilt Wijnstra, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e5ed003c-34be-f6df-4c21-e6d5d4c808c9"],["Baptism, Wijnjeterp, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cbede935-91ca-4aaf-bf89-84019a313717"],["Marriage, Opsterland, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96f0b262-7ce0-75b0-a43b-23958184b581"],["Birth of son Jilt, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:466834e3-9704-51ec-7153-e7d7213b649a"],["Death-duty file of his father, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6dbb23e5-92aa-47b8-a287-f40cb5027dd7"],["Death record, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a69f61d8-8698-18ea-193d-321783b4822d"]],"d":"1794–1860 · Wijnjeterp","b":1794,"x":1860},
albertjedv:{"n":"Albertje Hendriks de Vries","s":"h","p":["ezhendrikklazes","ezneeltjefokkes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Hemrik in Opsterland about 1798, one of the many children of Hendrik Klazes de Vries, a labourer, and Neeltje Fokkes. When her father registered the surname de Vries in 1811, she was 12 and the household list ran to twelve children: Klaas, Fokke, Loltje, Tjerk, Jan, Sijger, Maaike, Evert, Anne, Albertje, Reinder and Engbert.\n\nOn 22 May 1828, aged 30, she married Jan Jilts Wijnstra, a labourer of Wijnjeterp. Their sons and daughter were Jilt (1829), Hendrik (1830), Neeltje (1832), named after her mother, Aize (1833) and Fokke (1836), named after her mother's father. They lived at Hemrik.\n\nHer father died in 1841 and her mother, then a shopkeeper at Hemrik, in 1842. Jan died in June 1860.\n\nAlbertje worked as a labourer (arbeidster) and died at Lippenhuizen on 23 March 1871, aged 73, a year and a half before her eldest son Jilt.","src":[["Death of Jilt Wijnstra, 1872","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f67a6664-5de2-b4a1-5e25-620b05da8dc8"],["Marriage, Opsterland, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96f0b262-7ce0-75b0-a43b-23958184b581"],["Surname registration of her father, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:07d1324e-35f7-4074-8635-e6879a23fbe3"],["Death of her father, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ec9ebe8-292e-f0a2-aacb-a0fbb2e54442"],["Death of her mother, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56d06609-5917-9b7d-0d5e-e4c0b072729d"],["Death of her husband, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a69f61d8-8698-18ea-193d-321783b4822d"],["Death record, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8493eb99-3ee1-8981-8ab4-b6a25b1ae5b4"]],"d":"c. 1798–1871 · Hemrik","b":1798,"x":1871},
feitzedv:{"n":"Feitze Wytzes de Vries","s":"h","p":["ezwytzeberends","ezlolkjenaukes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland on 29 February 1784 and baptised there on 21 March, the son of Wytze Berends de Vries and Lolkjen Aukes, who had married in December 1782. His sister Wimke (or Minke) was baptised on 4 December 1785. His mother very probably also had children from an earlier marriage, with Pieter Feikes, and she died in April 1807, when Feitze was 23.\n\nHe and his father did business together: in January 1824 they bought property from Ibeltje Sjoerds, and in 1835 Wytze, Feitze and Wimke sold property to Ruurd Brugts Stobbe and Rinskje Pieters de Boer. In February 1836 they made a formal division of property (boedelscheiding) among themselves.\n\nOn 18 October 1832, aged 48, he married Aukjen Jans Jansma, 25, of Lippenhuizen. They had four children: Wytze (31 December 1834), named after Feitze's father, Aukjen (28 May 1837), Jan (30 June 1842) and Rinze (1 April 1850). His father died in 1839, a man living on his own means (rentenier).\n\nAukjen died on 9 April 1850, eight days after Rinze's birth, and Feitze, then a labourer, was left with four children aged 15 down to a newborn. That July he and his sister stood surety for succession duties. He sold property again in 1843 and died in Opsterland on 8 May 1859, aged 75. His son Wytze stayed at Lippenhuizen and in 1894 rented land to his nephews; Jan became a carpenter at Gorredijk.","src":[["Marriage of Aukjen de Vries, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e5ed003c-34be-f6df-4c21-e6d5d4c808c9"],["Marriage, Opsterland, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Notarial purchase, 28 January 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b0ce36f1-1c00-4a2e-be16-8f5a0c9bcffc"],["Notarial sale, 12 August 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6caef0ea-84f9-47d4-bba5-ba3913c0686b"],["Division of property, 10 February 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:30db6dbe-9c3b-486d-8fcd-1246bd55c34a"],["Notarial sale, 5 July 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:40907b1c-f91e-4502-a991-9f01ebdc6698"],["Surety for death duties, 5 July 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:599050ee-cc09-40f3-a7d7-a30b712ec091"],["Birth of son Wytze, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25af9978-1b31-ab45-48e8-62ffe63d7317"],["Birth of son Rinze, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ef5aeab5-8944-5e32-6606-7cdc34b674cc"],["Death of his wife, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8334eb66-eb89-af9b-8c72-7dd4c730d7c5"],["Death record, 1859","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58efff19-229e-8448-4d63-58ad81e6e1c2"],["Baptism, Lippenhuizen, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58e848aa-9075-48c5-a4b9-b91e735e5d17"],["Baptism of sister Wimke, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5fc08264-c301-498d-9f22-db142cfc02ad"],["Death of son Jan, Gorredijk, 1915","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:624485af-029f-8b03-dcde-55fa0511335f"]],"d":"1784–1859 · Lippenhuizen","b":1784,"x":1859},
aukjenjansma:{"n":"Aukjen Jans Jansma","s":"h","p":["ezjanaizes","ezaukjenmartens"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland and baptised there on 26 April 1807, the daughter of Jan Aizes (Jan Ayses) Jansma and his second wife Aukjen Martens. Her full brother Marten was born about 1804, and she had an older half-sister, Trijntje, from her father's first marriage to Jeltje Klazes; Trijntje became a farmer's wife at Hemrik.\n\nOn 18 October 1832, aged 25, she married Feitze Wytzes de Vries of Lippenhuizen, who was 48. They had four children: Wytze (31 December 1834), Aukjen (28 May 1837), Jan (30 June 1842) and Rinze (1 April 1850). The family lived at Lippenhuizen, and she worked as a labourer (arbeidster).\n\nShe died at Lippenhuizen on 9 April 1850, aged 43, eight days after the birth of her son Rinze. Her death record names her parents. Her daughter Aukjen, then 12, was the ancestor of Aaltje Postma.","src":[["Marriage of Aukjen de Vries, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e5ed003c-34be-f6df-4c21-e6d5d4c808c9"],["Death record, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8334eb66-eb89-af9b-8c72-7dd4c730d7c5"],["Baptism, Lippenhuizen, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1e2a3d02-a663-491a-9fea-0bc0d7af8367"],["Marriage, Opsterland, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Marriage of brother Marten Jans Jansma, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:161cc45c-3402-41c6-6171-7b198388f8cd"],["Death of half-sister Trijntje Jans Jansma, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4883b6af-33f6-7f7a-a1e3-e68ce2d9f312"],["Birth of daughter Aukjen, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:deaa8dcf-28da-8ae9-f28c-a47208a44107"],["Birth of son Rinze, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ef5aeab5-8944-5e32-6606-7cdc34b674cc"]],"d":"1807–1850 · Lippenhuizen","b":1807,"x":1850},
gerardusdv:{"n":"Gerardus de Vries","s":"b","p":["tbjoannesdevries","tbmariahuijben"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gerardus (Gerit) de Vries married Ida Jonkers at Tilburg on 18 February 1781, after banns on 16 December 1780. The register says he was born at Tilburg and gives his residence as both Namur (Namen, now in Belgium) and Tilburg. Namur was then a fortress town in the Austrian Netherlands where the Dutch Republic kept a garrison. His brother-in-law Michiel de Wilde witnessed for him, and Peeter Jonkers for the bride.\n\nSix children were baptised at Tilburg: Joannes (1782), Petrus (1784), Joanna Cornelia (1787), Joanna Maria (1789), Adriana (1792) and Joanna (1796). A Gerardus de Vries who left children was buried at Tilburg on 15 May 1797. This is almost certainly him: no child of the couple was baptised after July 1796, and Ida did not marry again.\n\nHis parents are probably Joannes de Vries and Maria Huijben, whose son Gerardus was baptised in the Goirke parish at Tilburg on 15 February 1750. That couple also had a daughter Adriana, and Michiel de Wilde's wife was an Adriana de Vries. The link is not proven, because a second Adriana de Vries was baptised at Tilburg in 1747 to other parents.\n\nA search of the Tilburg baptism registers for 1735 to 1760 found no other Gerardus de Vries, which strengthens the link, but no record states his parents directly. His mother's family name explains his own: she was Maria Adriaan Gerit Huijben, the granddaughter of a Gerit.","d":"d. 1797 · Tilburg","x":1797,"u":true,"src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2e77040e-6905-cca0-c348-c795eb7a44b0"],["Burial, Tilburg, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fe8a3d12-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of Gerardus de Vries, Tilburg, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:26dadf6a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Joannes, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:30d83418-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Petrus, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:305a08ea-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of Michiel de Wilde and Adriana de Vries, Oisterwijk, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:71393200-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Borgbrief of his probable parents, 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fab3a9d5-9b2d-9084-711b-08f4f8d0024a"]]},
idaj:{"n":"Ida Jonkers","s":"b","p":["tbpeterjonkers","tbmariavanloon"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Tilburg on 1 June 1759, the daughter of Petrus (Peter) Jonkers and Maria van Loon. Her mother was buried four days later, on 5 June. Her father, who had already been widowed twice before, married for the fourth time in June 1766, when Ida was seven, so she grew up with a stepmother, Maria Elisabet Deris.\n\nShe married Gerardus de Vries at Tilburg on 18 February 1781, aged 21, with her father as her witness. They had six children between 1782 and 1796: Joannes, Petrus, Joanna Cornelia, Joanna Maria, Adriana and Joanna. Her husband appears to have been buried in May 1797, leaving her with young children; the youngest was ten months old.\n\nShe did not remarry. Her daughter Joanna Maria married Johannes Baptist Melis in December 1812 and Adriana married Cornelis Claessen in February 1813; Joanna Cornelia had married Bernardus van den Berg in 1810. Ida died at Tilburg on 17 December 1839, aged 80, after 42 years as a widow.","d":"1759–1839 · Tilburg","b":1759,"x":1839,"src":[["Baptism, Tilburg, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:29cad8a6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9fd4c5cc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2e77040e-6905-cca0-c348-c795eb7a44b0"],["Marriage of daughter Joanna Maria, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a84cada-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Adriana, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0a85266a-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2e3bd43a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
johanneshamers:{"n":"Johannes Hamers","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Johannes (Jan) Hamers was born at Tilburg, according to the register of his marriage. He married Geertruijda Reijnen there on 27 February 1797. Two children were baptised at Tilburg: Cornelia on 20 December 1797 and Gerardus on 1 October 1799.\n\nHe died young. By 17 July 1803 his widow was marrying again, and the register of that marriage names Jan Hamers as her former husband. His daughter Cornelia died in 1815 at 17, so his line continued only through Gerardus. Hamers was a common surname in Tilburg at the time, and his own baptism cannot be picked out with certainty among the men of that name.","d":"d. before 1803 · Tilburg","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5e9ac96-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Cornelia, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2dfc5c56-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2dc38d40-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Widow's remarriage, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f6731f1c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
geertruij:{"n":"Geertruij Reijnen","s":"b","p":["tbgerardusreijnen","tbmariacdebeer"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Gertrudis at Tilburg on 16 November 1775, the daughter of Gerardus Reijnen and Maria Catharina de Beer. Her name is written Geertruij, Geertruijda, Gertrudis and Geertrui in different records.\n\nShe married Johannes Hamers at Tilburg on 27 February 1797 and had two children, Cornelia (baptised 20 December 1797) and Gerardus (1 October 1799). Her husband died within a few years. On 17 July 1803, after banns on 2 July, she married Augustinus Maurits, also of Tilburg.\n\nHer daughter Cornelia died on 23 March 1815, aged 17. Her son Gerardus married in November 1818 at 19 and gave her five grandchildren in her lifetime. She died at Tilburg on 14 January 1829, aged 50. Less than three weeks later Gerardus's youngest daughter was born and named Maria Gertruda.\n\nHer death record of 1829, which gave her age as 50, made her about three years younger than she was. She had brothers Norbertus (1773), Michael (1778) and Jan Baptist (1780) and a sister Petronilla (1784). Her mother was buried in May 1796, nine months before Geertrui's first marriage, and her father very probably in June 1799.","d":"1775–1829 · Tilburg","b":1775,"x":1829,"src":[["Death record, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9c5fee30-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage to Johannes Hamers, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5e9ac96-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage to Augustinus Maurits, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:09e114c6-2f3b-1ada-4370-b8a7eafeec44"],["Death of daughter Cornelia, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:98314fde-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 16 Nov 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31d4ba44-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of her mother, 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fe786d80-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
petervg:{"n":"Peter van Gestel","s":"b","p":["tbmathiasvgestel","tbelisabethlinsen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised Petrus Matheus at Valkenswaard on 21 September 1758, the son of Mathias van Gestel and Elisabeth Linsen. Valkenswaard is a village south of Eindhoven that was known across Europe for its falconers. Both his parents were dead by the autumn of 1779, when he was 21 and the children were settling the estate; an aldermen's deed of 10 November 1779 at Valkenswaard names him with Pietronella, Francis and Anna Maria van Gestel.\n\nHe moved some 35 kilometres north-west to Tilburg, where on 16 November 1783 he married Johanna Catharina de Lepper, a Tilburg woman. The register calls him Peeter Matthijs van Gestel. Their known children were Maria (1785), the twins Adriana and Josephus (1788), Elisabeth (1789), Johanna (about 1794) and Maria Catharina (about 1801).\n\nA residents list of about 1810 shows him at Tilburg with his wife, four daughters and two other young people in the household. Elisabeth married Johannes Snellen in 1811 and Maria married Gerardus Hamers in 1818. Peter died at Tilburg on 14 April 1820, aged 61. A copy of the death entry was sent to his birthplace and filed in the Valkenswaard register.","d":"1758–1820 · Valkenswaard","b":1758,"x":1820,"src":[["Baptism, Valkenswaard, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:0D76C6C9-48F3-4176-8D8F-4CA9F54B6BC6"],["Death record, Tilburg, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:99b63c98-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death extract filed at Valkenswaard, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:12ed14dd-1844-aa9e-99b3-d7946206f713"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:03766f45-79e4-08ed-2de8-a9a4a93ed401"],["Aldermen's deed, Valkenswaard, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:37E4D433-7401-4FDA-846D-D6FFFA2114FC"],["Tilburg residents list, about 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c978c28d-7db4-fa29-6500-22d6271bf21e"],["Utrecht notarial deed for the heirs of his mother, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:609C5C31-B951-4642-E053-4701000A17FD"],["Marriage of daughter Elisabeth, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1a6c9d38-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
johannacdl:{"n":"Johanna Catharina de Lepper","s":"b","p":["tbadriaandelepper","tbadrianamvermeer"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg about 1762, the daughter of Adriaan de Lepper and Adriana Maria Vermeer. The records call her Johanna Catharina, Joanna Cathrina or Anna Catharina.\n\nOn 16 November 1783 she married Peter van Gestel, who had come to Tilburg from Valkenswaard. Their first child, Maria, was baptised on 13 September 1785. Twins, Adriana and Josephus, were baptised together on 31 January 1788, and Elisabeth on 19 June 1789; Johanna was born about 1794 and Maria Catharina about 1801. Neither twin appears in the family's entry in the residents list of about 1810, which names four daughters.\n\nElisabeth married Johannes Snellen in 1811 and Maria married Gerardus Hamers in 1818. Johanna Catharina was widowed on 14 April 1820 and died at Tilburg on 30 November 1824, aged 62. Her two youngest daughters married after her death, Maria Catharina to Laurentius van Oirschot in 1827 and Johanna to Johannes Donders in 1829.","d":"c. 1762–1824 · Tilburg","b":1762,"x":1824,"src":[["Death record, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9b1d94e6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:03766f45-79e4-08ed-2de8-a9a4a93ed401"],["Baptism of twins Adriana and Josephus, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fbc7c60-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Elisabeth, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1ed21f93-b705-87a7-e69b-f01b5d6bfc28"],["Marriage of daughter Maria Catharina, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d1927e0-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1d19cfc4-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
nicolaasv:{"n":"Nicolaas Vreenegoor","s":"b","p":["dgjanvreenegoor","dgmariakroese"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The gravedigger (doodgraver) of Kampen. He is given that trade at the births of his children in 1813, when he was 27, and in 1815, when he was 30. His wife, Maria Alberts, was about ten years older than he was.\n\nThey had three known children: Dirk, baptised on 28 October 1810, who became a tailor; Albertus Bartholomeus, born on 14 February 1813, later a shoemaker at Arnhem; and Johanna Geertruida Maria, born on 16 May 1815. He died at Kampen on 15 June 1822, aged 37, when the children were eleven, nine and seven.\n\nHis death record names no parents. He was probably a son of the Kampen carpenter Jan Vreenegoor and Maria Kroese. His age fits among their children, and the names of that family (Johanna Geertruida, Catharina Maria, Clara, Hendrikus, Gerhardus) come back among his own children and grandchildren. But a second Vreenegoor, Martinus, also headed a household in Kampen in 1795, and the Catholic baptism registers of the town are not indexed, so the link is shown as unproven.\n\nMore records of the carpenter's family have since been opened, and they make the fit tighter rather than settling it. Jan Vreenegoor and Maria Kroese's son Gerardus was baptised on 17 December 1785, and their daughter Anna Christina Wilhelmina on 2 January 1788. Nicolaas's three recorded ages (27 in February 1813, 30 in May 1815, 37 in June 1822) put his birth between about the middle of 1784 and May 1785, so he would have been born only seven to eighteen months before Gerardus. That is possible but close. A Kampen notarial deed of 1826 shows that Catharina Maria Vrenegoor, the wife of the linen weaver Karreman, belonged to the other household, that of Martinus Vreenegoor of the Keizerstraat, so that family was also bringing up children in these years. The link to Jan and Maria therefore stays unproven.","d":"c. 1785–1822 · Kampen","b":1785,"x":1822,"u":true,"src":[["Death record, 15 June 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:79BF27E7-8061-41A3-8E29-BC7BDC1CB44A"],["Birth of son Albertus Bartholomeus, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D9B4063B-DFDE-43AB-ADD1-52CDC88B5D70"],["Birth of daughter Johanna Geertruida Maria, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5028D9B5-4C44-474B-B786-52178CE256D0"],["Marriage of son Theodorus (Dirk), 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:29173F07-E25F-48AC-9BF2-BD6CCFB9EADB"],["Son Albertus Bartholomeus, shoemaker at Arnhem, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:F9D2C277-3502-4D6D-B9C8-EFCCD55EED25"],["Marriage of Gerardus Vrenegoor at Zwolle (baptised 17 December 1785)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6AD101B-290A-45C9-8B2A-84ADA81734E1"],["Marriage of Anna Christina Wilhelmina Vrenegoor at Zwolle (baptised 2 January 1788)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5918367C-A019-4EA4-94CF-606ECABF6AAE"],["Notarial deed, Kampen, 8 June 1826 (Martinus and Catharina Maria Vrenegoor), Notarieel Archief Kampen 00124 inv. I-541b akte 0255","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mires=100&mip1=Vr*negoor"]]},
mariaalberts:{"n":"Maria Alberts","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Nicolaas Vreenegoor, the gravedigger of Kampen. She died there on 11 November 1836, aged 61, so she was born about 1775; the record names neither her parents nor her birthplace. Alberts began as a patronymic, 'daughter of Albert', and her second son was named Albertus.\n\nShe was about ten years older than her husband and in her late thirties when her children were born: Dirk (baptised 28 October 1810), Albertus Bartholomeus (14 February 1813) and Johanna Geertruida Maria (16 May 1815). Nicolaas died in June 1822, leaving her a widow of about 47 with three children under twelve.\n\nShe lived to see Dirk set up as a tailor and marry Elizabeth Degenhard in 1831, and to see the first three of his children born, the last of them Maria Klara in December 1835. Albertus became a shoemaker and moved to Arnhem.","d":"c. 1775–1836","b":1775,"x":1836,"src":[["Death record, 11 November 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:FF34FB80-BC92-48ED-9E3D-2EC60F4C50B2"],["Birth of son Albertus Bartholomeus, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:D9B4063B-DFDE-43AB-ADD1-52CDC88B5D70"],["Birth of daughter Johanna Geertruida Maria, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5028D9B5-4C44-474B-B786-52178CE256D0"],["Death of her husband, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:79BF27E7-8061-41A3-8E29-BC7BDC1CB44A"]]},
annacwd:{"n":"Anna Catharina Willemina Degenhard","s":"b","p":["hendricusd","elisabethvdb"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Mother of Elizabeth Degenhard, who was born at Kampen on 24 May 1812. Anna Catharina Willemina was unmarried. A midwife reported the birth and no father was named, but the baby was registered with the extra name Bakker, which may point to him. She is named as the mother again when Elizabeth married in 1831 and when Elizabeth died in 1893.\n\nShe was very probably the daughter of Hendricus Degenhart and Elisabeth van den Berg who was baptised at Kampen on 6 March 1792. That daughter married Hendrik Bombam, a weaver, on 5 November 1818 under the name Catharina Degenhart, and died on 12 February 1829, aged 39 by the record, as Johanna Catharina Willemina Degenhardt. Only one Degenhart household is listed in the Kampen census of 1795, and the baby of 1812 was given the name of Elisabeth van den Berg, who died a month after the birth.\n\nThe names match closely but not exactly (Anna in one record, Johanna in the other) and no record states the link outright, so her parents are shown as unproven. If it is right, Elizabeth was a niece of the tailor Johannes Degenhart, and the Degenhart and Vreenegoor cousins who married in 1863 shared the same great-grandparents.\n\nA further search found no other record under any form of her name, Anna Catharina Willemina, Catharina or Johanna Catharina Willemina, and no child of Hendrik Bombam and Catharina Degenhart in the Kampen registers. The dates also do not line up neatly: the marriage of 1818 gives a baptism on 6 March 1792, while the death in 1829 at 39 points to about 1790. Only the Catholic baptism register of Kampen for 1792 or a marriage file of 1831 could settle whether the two women were one.","d":"Kampen","u":true,"src":[["Birth of her daughter Elizabeth, 24 May 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:48509D0D-DB31-4E27-88C6-024248409040"],["Marriage of her daughter, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:29173F07-E25F-48AC-9BF2-BD6CCFB9EADB"],["Marriage of Catharina Degenhart and Hendrik Bombam, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:6198B6CE-8BB9-46B2-91C5-6FECF4E01346"],["Death of Johanna Catharina Willemina Degenhardt, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:2ED474C6-72D0-4D93-95D1-ECC99677691D"],["Kampen census of 1795 (Volkstelling), Stadsarchief Kampen","https://www.stadsarchiefkampen.nl/doorzoek-genealogische-bronnen?mivast=69&miadt=69&mizig=100&miview=tbl&milang=nl&micols=1&mip1=Degenhart"]]},
aaldrikhes:{"n":"Aaldrik Andries Hes","d":"innkeeper and merchant, Sappemeer","s":"b","p":["gsandriesaaldriks","gsrijktienlukens"],"r":["nl"],"t":"An innkeeper and merchant (herbergier, koopman) at Sappemeer. An inn on the Winschoterdiep was a place of business as much as a tavern: skippers, peat bosses and shipwrights made their bargains there, and the innkeeper commonly traded on his own account as well.\n\nOn 24 January 1771 he signed a marriage contract with Albertje Remmerts Niemeijer, a farmer's daughter from Scheemda, with Lucas Andries Hes, by his name a brother, standing on his side, and they married at Sappemeer three days later. Their children were Bouwina (1771), Klaaske (about 1776) and Aaldrik Andries, baptised on 24 November 1782. The son went to sea as a sailor (matroos) and in 1812 married Jacoba van der Waals, a maid born in Leiden.\n\nThe church books show that Albertje was his second wife. As Aaldrik Andries Hesze he married Tietje Hillebrands at Sappemeer on 21 October 1759; their daughter Rijkje was baptised in January 1760 and a son Andries, who had the name of Aaldrik's own father, in November 1763. Klaasjen Andries Hes, by her name a sister, stood beside his brother Lucas at the 1771 contract. The burial of an Aaldrik Hes is entered at Sappemeer on 1 August 1793. His son of that name was then a boy of ten who lived to marry in 1812.\n\nThe two at his side in 1771, Lucas and Klaasjen Andries Hes, were baptised at Sappemeer in 1721 and 1724, the children of Andries Aaldrix and Rijktien Lukens, who also had a son Aaldrik baptised there on 27 February 1718. Those records make Andries Aaldrix his father, and the tree places him as that boy of 1718. The link is marked unproven because a man of the same name had another son Aaldrik baptised at Groningen in 1727, with a wife called Anna Hindriks; Aaldrik's first daughter, Rijkje, carried the name of Rijktien.","src":[["Marriage contract, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:411cb5c4-d2cb-79c4-5600-acc515b873e3"],["Marriage at Sappemeer, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ad948f45-f6b6-0df5-b6d7-3d6fe005e0c7"],["Baptism of Aaldrik Andries, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3105f588-8049-8262-68cd-78d7eb45c0d2"],["Marriage of Aaldrik Andries Hes, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7b25b37b-43a1-6ec1-44c9-5258278fb44c"],["First marriage, Sappemeer 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ff61b7e9-3898-370b-b7e1-4e41f952b36f"],["Baptism of Rijkje, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e8d72d71-17eb-bec3-9e4b-570d2c527364"],["Baptism of Andries, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:badb832f-a9bf-f0d3-4ed0-940c0b9c9969"],["Burial of Aaldrik Hes, 1793","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:489d88ac-5220-c3c1-04db-c2c1ad0e3864"],["Marriage of Lucas Andries Hes, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:59a5c0b5-5608-1745-d2da-33874343df2a"],["Marriage of Andries Aaldrix and Rijktien Lukens, Sappemeer 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ce698b27-ca11-2fd0-dccb-933912529f9b"],["Baptism of Aaldrik, Sappemeer 1718","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a7b79a18-fbde-dfbd-b832-e3ba4059b63f"],["Baptism of Luiken (Lucas), Sappemeer 1721","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:59078c2b-42a0-7048-7c54-1a17649ca18d"],["Baptism of Klaasjen, Sappemeer 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:724854cf-f931-515c-b994-43be00bc69a7"],["Baptism of another Aaldrik, son of Andries Aaldrix and Anna Hindriks, Groningen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0ed26f5d-c0de-5432-c624-0c547f42ec5b"]],"u":true},
albertjen:{"n":"Albertje Remmerts Niemeijer","s":"b","p":["remmertn","bouchien"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Scheemda on 11 March 1742, the daughter of the farmer Remmert Tjarks and Bouwke (Bouchien) Alberts. She had an elder brother Tjark (1738) and younger sisters Foctina (1745) and Geesijn (1752). The family later used the surname Niemeijer.\n\nShe was 28 when she married the widower Aaldrik Andries Hes at Sappemeer on 27 January 1771, with a contract signed three days earlier; Bouke Alberts and Remke Tonkes stood on her side. Aaldrik had two children from his first marriage.\n\nShe kept the inn at Sappemeer with her husband and had Bouwina (1771), Klaaske (about 1776) and Aaldrik Andries (1782), the last when she was 40. She outlived her daughter Klaaske, who died in 1821, and spent her last years in the city of Groningen, where she died on 4 May 1823. The record gives her age as 85, but by the baptism register she was 81. Her sister Geesijn lived at Scheemda until 1836.","d":"1742–1823 · Scheemda","b":1742,"x":1823,"src":[["Death record, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6a3ec028-23af-e663-42d6-6bf6b82696e1"],["Marriage contract, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:411cb5c4-d2cb-79c4-5600-acc515b873e3"],["Death of Klaaske Aaldriks Hes, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:66ae49d6-8f57-f71d-a690-1fb294da5608"],["Baptism, Scheemda 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c93af34d-f83e-bc9a-aa17-1f88d348d93c"],["Parents' marriage, Scheemda 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0f7fbce5-c579-2ac4-e9b3-a50ac076d647"],["Marriage, Sappemeer 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ad948f45-f6b6-0df5-b6d7-3d6fe005e0c7"],["Death of Geesijn Remmers Niemeijer, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2a5d2739-b402-5684-560f-938ea863f73c"]]},
harmijzer:{"n":"Harm Jacobs IJzer","d":"day labourer","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"A day labourer (dagloner) at Oudeschans, the little fortress built in 1593 to guard the road into Groningen from the east. By his time the garrison was small and the village lived from farm work on the surrounding polders.\n\nWith his wife Antje Jans he had children baptised in the fortress church: Dorothea in September 1754, Boele in March 1757 and Jochem in November 1759. An older son, Jacob Harms IJzer, born about 1750, married Janna Filippus from Bunde and died at Oudeschans in 1818; his death record names Harm as his father. The surname IJzer means iron.\n\nStijntje Jacobs IJzer, who died in 1819, is recorded in her death record as his daughter. Her second name, Jacobs, and the dates do not fit that, and the tree places her, unproven, as his granddaughter, Jacob's child.","src":[["Baptism of Dorothea, 1754","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fb207b7a-aa09-cc27-928d-a3052dc169c8"],["Baptism of Boele, 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:69afa3e9-120e-47bd-795e-3f3abaca69cc"],["Baptism of Jochem, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d24e5123-13b8-a0af-f341-5b54cea467fe"],["Death of Jacob Harms IJzer, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec9b91e7-ef02-11dd-00aa-6a7c80860995"],["Death of Janna Filippus, wife of Jacob Harms IJzer, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"]]},
antjejans:{"n":"Antje Jans","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of the day labourer Harm Jacobs IJzer of Oudeschans. Their children Dorothea (1754), Boele (1757) and Jochem (1759) were baptised in the church inside the fortress, and an older son, Jacob, was born about 1750. Jacob's death record of 1818 calls her Antje Boeles, and one of her sons was named Boele. Jacob in turn named a daughter Antje in 1776.\n\nShe is named as the mother of Stijntje Jacobs IJzer in Stijntje's death record of 1819, but Stijntje was born about 1780, some thirty years after Antje's eldest child, and is placed in the tree, unproven, as Jacob's daughter and so Antje's granddaughter.","src":[["Baptism of Boele, 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:69afa3e9-120e-47bd-795e-3f3abaca69cc"],["Death record of Stijntje, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:de197a07-af33-80b5-d0c4-c02a4d6ece07"],["Death of Jacob Harms IJzer, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec9b91e7-ef02-11dd-00aa-6a7c80860995"],["Baptism of her granddaughter Antje, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f3e31962-2620-9856-d62d-4a7bfe4fe01b"]]},
meine:{"n":"Meine","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Meine was the father of Jacob Meines Meinsma, a smallholder and farmer at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland who was born about 1783 and died in 1856. He is known only from Jacob's death record, which gives the parents simply as \"Meine\" and \"Aukjen\", without second names.\n\nHis name was handed down twice. Jacob named his eldest son Meine, born on 1 September 1810, and in 1811, when every Frisian family had to choose a fixed surname, Jacob formed the name Meinsma from his father's first name, as many families did.\n\nNo baptism, marriage or death record that can be tied to him has been found.","src":[["Death of son Jacob, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e211ff9e-8fb2-6075-33da-64c534da20e9"],["Surname registration of son Jacob, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:825bc225-1156-4545-875a-b95aec02be78"],["Baptism of grandson Meine, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e570d0a0-1797-4986-9543-98210c8795f6"]]},
aukjenm:{"n":"Aukjen","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aukjen was the mother of Jacob Meines Meinsma, born about 1783, who spent his life at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland as a smallholder and farmer. She is known only from her son's death record of 1856, which names his parents simply as \"Meine\" and \"Aukjen\".\n\nHer son married Ibeltje Hendriks in 1809 and had at least eight children, among them Aaltje (1823), who married Jan Roels Postma in 1850 and became the grandmother of Aaltje Postma.\n\nNo baptism, marriage or death record that can be tied to her has been found.","src":[["Death of son Jacob, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e211ff9e-8fb2-6075-33da-64c534da20e9"],["Marriage of son Jacob, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e01e90a-0b74-4e01-b9f7-00f527279fc3"]]},
sjoerdstok:{"n":"Sjoerd Abes Stok","s":"h","p":["ezabestok","ezklaaskehyltjes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1755, the son of Abe Hendriks Stok and Klaaske Hyltjes of Grouw. Grouw lies on the Pikmeer, a lake on the main waterway between Leeuwarden and the southern Frisian lakes, and many of its people earned their living on the water as skippers, fishermen and boatmen.\n\nOn 25 April 1773 he married Grietje Sjoerds, a fisherman's daughter, in the church at Grouw; both were of the village. Their children included Klaas (about 1777), Pietje (about 1783), Hendrik (about 1793) and Klaaske (about 1794).\n\nIn 1811 he registered the surname Stok at Grouw. The entry lists Klaas, 34, Pietje, 28, Hendrik, 18, and Klaaske, 16, with Klaas's children Beitske, 4, and Sjoerd, 1.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider), and so was his wife. He died at Grouw on 11 May 1816, aged 61, and Grietje only six months later. Of their children, Pietje married Jurjen Sipkes Spinstra and died in Ferwerderadeel in 1817; Hendrik, a labourer, married Aaltje Pieters de Jong in 1818 and died at Grouw in 1826, aged 33; and Klaaske married twice, the second time to Jentje Rinzes Elzinga, and died at Grouw in 1875 aged 81.","src":[["Death of Klaas Stok, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3181a2c3-2316-1552-eb1a-01f340cf14ac"],["Marriage, Grouw, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e69d3432-90be-4ea0-9388-391154c23af3"],["Surname registration, Grouw, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f6f1d8e1-cd17-48a5-aba9-23bd3e3d4a4c"],["Death record, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d8baf2af-f9aa-e7c0-047f-b170e910e135"],["Death of daughter Pietje, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9069b710-f5a9-0627-186f-6b9fafd1ea0f"],["Marriage of son Hendrik, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5cd8702e-b89a-d85e-338f-50139be36c67"],["Death of son Hendrik, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6d99a563-4e57-d75d-3def-e034ceef0d04"],["Second marriage of daughter Klaaske, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b4e40358-23d2-c6fd-e5b1-4fe6ec9d6b80"],["Death of daughter Klaaske, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:36f14710-5a48-1662-677e-cce30d527dbb"]],"d":"c. 1755–1816 · Grouw","b":1755,"x":1816},
tjitskev:{"n":"Grietje Sjoerds","s":"h","p":["ezsjoerdjacobs","ezpietjegerrits"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Grouw on 30 August 1750 as Grijtje, the daughter of Sjoerd Jacobs; the baptism entry does not name her mother. Sjoerd Jacobs of Grouw had married Pijttrik (Pietje) Gerrits of Makkum in February 1748, and the death records of Grietje's sister Okje and brother Jacob name Pietje Gerrits as their mother. Her brothers and sisters included Okje (about 1749), Rinske (1752), Jacob (1756), the twins Gerryt and Rinske (1760) and Jouke (1763). Her brother Jacob became a fisherman at Grouw and took the surname Visser (fisherman) in 1811.\n\nThe tree used to call her Tjitske Sjoerds Visser. That name comes only from her son Klaas's death record of 1849. Her marriage record, her husband's and her own death records, and the records of her other children all call her Grietje Sjoerds. Her own death record names her mother as Pietje Joukes, while her brother's and sister's records give Pietje Gerrits.\n\nOn 25 April 1773 she married Sjoerd Abes, later Stok, at Grouw. They had at least four children who grew up: Klaas (about 1777), Pietje (about 1783), Hendrik (about 1793) and Klaaske (about 1794). In 1811 she and Sjoerd were sharing their home with Klaas, his wife and two small grandchildren.\n\nShe worked as a labourer (arbeidster). Sjoerd died on 11 May 1816, and Grietje died at Grouw six months later, on 17 November 1816, aged 65.","src":[["Death of Klaas Stok, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3181a2c3-2316-1552-eb1a-01f340cf14ac"],["Baptism, Grouw, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d1f1698-9006-4817-bc7e-4b957841e362"],["Marriage, Grouw, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e69d3432-90be-4ea0-9388-391154c23af3"],["Death record, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9094480d-109b-5c7a-a28e-33fa51814564"],["Death of her husband, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d8baf2af-f9aa-e7c0-047f-b170e910e135"],["Death of sister Okje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c2fce4c3-52f6-593e-de80-55121c124242"],["Death of brother Jacob Sjoerds Visser, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:99956b62-fefe-bb4d-5d39-5073b2c23679"],["Surname registration, Grouw, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f6f1d8e1-cd17-48a5-aba9-23bd3e3d4a4c"],["Marriage of her parents, Makkum, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:52d7f0f3-1f6c-4195-92e6-cf77319c948d"]],"d":"1750–1816 · Grouw","b":1750,"x":1816},
derkeh:{"n":"Derk Eppes Hekman","d":"c. 1756–1817 · Nieuwe Pekela","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"A master carpenter (meester timmerman) at Nieuwe Pekela. The Pekela villages were peat colonies, strung for kilometres along a canal dug to carry the turf away, and by his day they were turning to shipping and shipbuilding, so there was steady work for a carpenter with his own business.\n\nHis children included Epke (1785), Kornelius (1787), Wija (about 1791), Eppe (1795), Bijke (about 1798) and Lammert (1801). Three sons followed him into the trade; Kornelius was already a \"carpenter-boss\" (timmerbaas) at 24. Nearly every record names his wife as Jantje Kornelis Bakker, though Epke's marriage record calls her mother Jantjen Egberts. He died at Nieuwe Pekela on 20 May 1817, aged 61. His son Eppe was found drowned in the canal at Winschoten, under the English Bridge, in October 1840.\n\nThe church registers settle the question of his wife. He married Jantje Cornellis at Nieuwe Pekela on 21 November 1784, and their first child, Epke, was born on 7 October 1785 with Jantje Kornellis named as her mother. There was no earlier wife; 'Jantjen Egberts' in Epke's marriage record of 1813 was a clerk's slip. Jantje died at Nieuwe Pekela on 6 April 1813, aged 52, and he outlived her by four years. No baptism for him has been found at either Pekela, and no Eppe appears there as a father in the 1750s, so his birthplace is not known.","src":[["Marriage, Midwolda, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1bf7e209-2371-0998-5338-a71fa4a62084"],["Death record, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f302ea09-cf73-24d1-8269-e35260f2b515"],["Marriage of Kornelius, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:714ab780-2aff-4981-affc-d01002d16372"],["Marriage of Eppe, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dc75eae5-7bf8-c6d7-166f-695f585ad6ea"],["Death of Eppe, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:93a47251-106a-b0b5-f094-17429f348b4f"],["Marriage, Nieuwe Pekela 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ceb4906-c45a-03f5-1338-00e031249b64"],["Baptism of Epke, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1632e8f9-3183-7915-6a09-7e9454408fc3"],["Death of Jantje Kornelis Bakker, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:965c1b4c-a615-4840-5b07-c3cc08006e15"]],"b":1756,"x":1817},
jantjenegb:{"n":"Jantje Kornelis Bakker","s":"b","p":["grcornelistonnis","grwijkelammerts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwe Pekela on 28 September 1760 and baptised a week later, the daughter of Cornelis Tonnis and Wijke Lammerts. She married the carpenter Derk Eppes there on 21 November 1784, as Jantje Cornellis. Their first child, Epke, was born on 7 October 1785, followed by Kornelius (5 November 1787), Wija (about 1791), Eppe (25 March 1795), Bijke (about 1798) and Lammert (21 January 1801). Kornelius, Wija and Lammert carried the names of her father, her mother and her mother's father. Three sons became carpenters like their father, and the daughters went out to service in the Oldambt.\n\nShe died at Nieuwe Pekela on 6 April 1813, aged 52, and is called a carpenter's wife (timmermanse) in the record. A month later her daughter Epke married at Midwolda, and the clerk there entered the bride's mother as 'Jantjen Egberts'. For that reason she stood in this tree under that name. Every other record, from the baptism of 1785 to the deaths of her children, calls her Jantje Kornelis, with the surname Bakker (baker) after 1811.","src":[["Marriage, Midwolda, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1bf7e209-2371-0998-5338-a71fa4a62084"],["Marriage of Kornelius, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:714ab780-2aff-4981-affc-d01002d16372"],["Baptism, Nieuwe Pekela 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:90892207-57cb-33b6-b40a-35e944a9a477"],["Marriage, Nieuwe Pekela 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5ceb4906-c45a-03f5-1338-00e031249b64"],["Baptism of Epke, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1632e8f9-3183-7915-6a09-7e9454408fc3"],["Death record, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:965c1b4c-a615-4840-5b07-c3cc08006e15"],["Marriage of Wija, Beerta 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:643784c9-230d-8366-e21b-1a0330f76ef2"],["Marriage of Lammert, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b2ae61ae-d8e2-497c-b8a0-a723821b3aca"],["Death of Kornelis Derks Hekman, 1877","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8420126d-98d1-2d7b-1ae9-f141a0285b5c"]],"d":"1760–1813 · Nieuwe Pekela","b":1760,"x":1813},
harkel:{"n":"Harke Kornelis Landmeter","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Harke Kornelis was born about 1763; his second name shows that his father was called Kornelis. He was living at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland when he married Grietje Sytzes, also of Lippenhuizen, on 16 March 1788. On 31 January 1789 the two were baptised together in the Mennonite congregation of Gorredijk and Lippenhuizen. Mennonites (doopsgezinden) did not baptise babies: members were baptised as adults on their own confession of faith, usually shortly before or after marriage, so the church books give no infant baptisms for this family.\n\nTwo children of the first marriage are known: Kornelis, born about 1790, and Bontje, born about 1795. Grietje died young, and Harke married Antje Hessels van der Woude. Their children were Jan (about 1806), Sytze (about 1810, later a farmer), Sytske (born 16 September 1814) and Mettje (born 28 February 1819).\n\nHis family name means \"land surveyor\". In April 1810, then living at Gorredijk, he sold property to four brothers named Sietzes: Jan, Harke, Foeke and Jelle. In 1813, back at Lippenhuizen as Harke Cornelis Landmeter, he took part in public sales of land before a notary together with Fokke Freerks Landmeter of Terwispel.\n\nHe worked on the peat boats as a skipper's mate (schippersknecht). He died at Lippenhuizen on 15 March 1825, aged 62, leaving his second wife, a labourer, with young children. His son Kornelis died at Gorredijk in 1871, aged 80.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e33e6f09-1b69-4594-ab1d-283349ee218d"],["Mennonite membership register, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:09765423-901e-4f47-83ba-bb768bd7c5ec"],["Notarial deed, Gorredijk, 2 April 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f28fed01-bcd8-493f-b318-7afcfdc0027c"],["Notarial sale, 10 February 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a9725c58-fef1-4243-8871-94eb3803c89a"],["Birth of daughter Sytske, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:550f94fa-422c-2be8-7a89-03b04317720c"],["Birth of daughter Mettje, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:239b43a0-5bd1-aa64-4a9d-0389a5f69376"],["Death record, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e80636e-922a-bc2e-6e3a-29d4e7b694a1"],["Marriage of son Kornelis, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6f6dc47c-cd0b-8b89-07b8-f09b6ace9df2"],["Death of son Kornelis, Gorredijk, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7736b50d-11d5-3d87-335b-5f31156f5a92"]],"d":"c. 1763–1825 · Lippenhuizen","b":1763,"x":1825},
grietjesy:{"n":"Grietje Sytzes","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Grietje Sytzes (also written Sytses or Sietzes) lived at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland. Her second name shows that her father was called Sytze, and her husband later gave the name Sytze to a son. She married Harke Kornelis, a young man of the same village, on 16 March 1788.\n\nOn 31 January 1789 she and Harke were baptised together on confession of faith in the Mennonite congregation of Gorredijk and Lippenhuizen. Because Mennonites baptised only adults, there is no baptism record for her as a baby and none for her children.\n\nTwo of her children are known: Kornelis, born about 1790, who lived to 80 and died at Gorredijk in 1871, and Bontje, born about 1795, the ancestor of this family. Grietje died young, some time between about 1795 and about 1805; Harke had married again by the time his son Jan was born about 1806. Her death falls before the civil registers began in 1811, and no burial entry for her has been found.\n\nShe is named as Bontje's mother in her daughter's marriage record of 1817 and death record of 1864.","src":[["Death of Bontje Landmeter, 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d439f12-8ae0-9e41-7276-ba9284f07190"],["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e33e6f09-1b69-4594-ab1d-283349ee218d"],["Mennonite membership register, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:09765423-901e-4f47-83ba-bb768bd7c5ec"],["Marriage of daughter Bontje, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Death of son Kornelis, 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7736b50d-11d5-3d87-335b-5f31156f5a92"]],"d":"d. c. 1795–1805 · Lippenhuizen"},
wymer:{"n":"Wymer Eelkes Roukema","s":"h","p":["ezeelkewymers","ezrinskewybes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1752, the son of Eelke Wymers and Rinske Wybes, and a man of Gorredijk all his known life. His name is written Wymer, Wiemer or Weimer, and the family name Roukema or Raukema. He married Jantje Pieters.\n\nFour of their children are known: Eelke (about 1786), Lysbeth (about 1788), Rinske (about 1791) and Jankjen or Joukjen (about 1794). In 1811, when Napoleon's government required every Frisian family to take a fixed surname, he registered at Gorredijk as Wymer Eelkes Roukema. The register lists Eelke, 25, at home; Lysbeth, 23, living at Oldeboorn; Rinske, 20, at Lippenhuizen; and Jankjen, 18.\n\nIn February 1815 Joukjen married Lomme Jelmers de Jong; she and her father both signed the record with the new name Roukema, so he could write. Eelke, a skipper, married in 1817. Rinske worked as a labourer at Lippenhuizen and died there in March 1827, aged 36.\n\nWymer died at Gorredijk on 9 February 1823, aged 70. His death record names his parents and his wife Jantje Pieters, who outlived him. The death-duty file lists no real estate and names his children: Eelke, a skipper's mate; Joukje, wife of Lomme Jelmers de Jong; Rinske, a maidservant at Lippenhuizen; and Lijsbert, who was absent, \"wandering, whereabouts unknown\". His grandson Wiemer Eelkes Roukema, born in 1821, was named after him.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Death record, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:46d10024-63b4-aa6a-b2a4-f1315317bd0e"],["Surname registration, Gorredijk, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d10dc94e-e95f-4d9b-b4ea-cb08bd7008f7"],["Marriage of daughter Joukjen, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f70f3e07-0314-960f-1aab-d7b72cf97b28"],["Death of daughter Rinske, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c343385a-526a-7270-e387-88a950fb8130"],["Death-duty file, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:48fb932a-b0fd-49f4-99ed-772e6a0975df"]],"d":"c. 1752–1823 · Gorredijk","b":1752,"x":1823},
jantjepi:{"n":"Jantje Pieters","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jantje Pieters was the wife of Wymer Eelkes Roukema of Gorredijk and the mother of Eelke (about 1786), Lysbeth (about 1788), Rinske (about 1791) and Jankjen or Joukjen (about 1794). Her own parents are not named in any record found; her second name shows only that her father was called Pieter. Her eldest son named his daughter Jantje after her.\n\nShe was alive at her daughter Joukjen's wedding in 1815 and her son Eelke's in 1817, and she was named as Wymer's widow when he died at Gorredijk in February 1823. Her daughter Rinske died at Lippenhuizen in 1827.\n\nA Jantje Pieters, a widow, died at Gorredijk on 12 February 1844, aged 89, so born about 1755. The death-duty office recorded that she left nothing and had a certificate of poverty. The index does not name her husband, so it is not proved that she was Wymer's widow, though no other woman of that name is known at Gorredijk. If she was, she outlived her husband by 21 years.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f5bb7c9-bf13-8b99-362b-b6f2cdca6945"],["Marriage of daughter Joukjen, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f70f3e07-0314-960f-1aab-d7b72cf97b28"],["Death of her husband, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:46d10024-63b4-aa6a-b2a4-f1315317bd0e"],["Death of a Jantje Pieters, Opsterland, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0fcef74f-9673-d47b-8e78-63b40a82a656"],["Death-duty file of Jantje Pieters, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:87056c12-2093-4733-8f56-a44608b53dc0"]],"d":"c. 1755–1844 · Gorredijk","b":1755,"x":1844},
oege:{"n":"Oege Tjerks Tuinstra","d":"1763–1827 · Idaard","s":"h","p":["eztjerkoeges","eztrijntjereins"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Idaard on 11 October 1763 and baptised five days later, the eldest child of Tjerk Oeges, a farmer, and Trijntje Reins. On 25 November 1787 he married Aukje Jacobs, also of Idaard, in the church there.\n\nHe worked as a labourer and the young family moved between neighbouring villages: Tjerk was born at Warga on 29 October 1789, Trijntje at Roordahuizum in 1792, and Jacob at Idaard on 29 December 1794. Geertje (1800) and Hylkje (1804) followed, and from 1796 the household stayed at Idaard.\n\nIn 1811 he registered the surname Tuinstra. The entry lists Tjerk, 22, \"in service\"; Trijntje, 19, at Roordahuizum; Jakob, 16, at Grouw; Gertje, 11; and Hijlkje, 7. Tjerk had been conscripted in 1809 and was serving in a French naval crew, the 17th flotilla company. The compilers of the soldiers' index thought he never came back, but a Tjerk Oeges Tuinstra, son of this couple, died at Wartena in 1860 aged 71.\n\nOege was still a labourer at Idaard when Trijntje married in 1815. He died there on 30 August 1827, aged 63.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Baptism, Idaard, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cca82e52-3c6a-4f22-a407-78ec5838fbc2"],["Marriage, Idaard, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9987bca7-28fc-4571-909e-910b4ed36ab4"],["Baptism of Tjerk, Warga, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a0dff1f1-d32a-4305-9ea3-c73927c50a60"],["Baptism of Jacob, Idaard, 1795","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:034b7cf7-824e-4e5f-a3da-1517244efe48"],["Surname registration, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4f457b39-f35f-45a0-839c-2b63f68cbeb3"],["Family index card (Tietjerksteradeel e.o.)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:303c10f2-bc6f-44d7-9035-0daf3af96dd7"],["Son Tjerk in the soldiers' index","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69ee157e-d1f6-4b4a-aeb6-6aba5a164d99"],["Death of son Tjerk, Wartena, 1860","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:27a8f991-a4f1-4996-03cc-b10e79896d2f"],["Death record, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c274043b-7f24-e0c7-ecd2-a626e6c6f83c"]],"b":1763,"x":1827},
aukjejac:{"n":"Aukje Jacobs","d":"Idaard","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aukje (Aukjen) Jacobs was living at Idaard when she married Oege Tjerks there on 25 November 1787. Her parents have not been found; her second name shows only that her father was called Jacob, and her second son was named Jacob after him.\n\nShe had five known children: Tjerk (born at Warga in 1789), Trijntje (Roordahuizum, 1792), Jacob (Idaard, 1794), Geertje (1800) and Hylkje (1804). Her husband took the surname Tuinstra in 1811. That year her eldest son was away in Napoleon's navy.\n\nShe was living at Idaard when Trijntje married in 1815, and was a labourer (arbeidster) there when Oege died in August 1827. She is not found after that. Her son Jacob, a labourer who married Hyke Ydes Oevering in 1815, named a daughter Aukje after her in 1831; he died at Wirdum in 1865 aged 70.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Marriage, Idaard, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9987bca7-28fc-4571-909e-910b4ed36ab4"],["Death of her husband, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c274043b-7f24-e0c7-ecd2-a626e6c6f83c"],["Death of son Jacob, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:298f1fac-6d13-95cb-4e3b-521d9b069d63"],["Granddaughter Aukje Jacobs Tuinstra, born 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:49d40bea-f0cb-46ee-ac36-5bc07eb51ef9"]]},
jansikkes:{"n":"Jan Sikkes","d":"d. 1809 or 1810 · Rauwerd","x":1809,"s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Sikkes was living at Wirdum, south of Leeuwarden, when he married Pijtje (Pietje) Lucas of the neighbouring village of Swichum in the church at Wirdum on 19 May 1782. His second name is a patronymic: his father was called Sikke.\n\nFour of their children are known. Marijke was born about 1783 and Lucas about 1786, at Wirdum. Johannes was born on 12 December 1791 at Bozum, so the family was living there by then. Geertje followed about 1795. Lucas became a shoemaker at Wirdum.\n\nJan died at Rauwerd. His son's marriage record of 1815 gives the date as 12 February 1809; the church burial register of Rauwerd enters him under 12 February 1810. He died just before Napoleon's decree of 1811 made every family in Friesland choose a fixed surname. His widow and children took the name Elzinga or Elsinga, and later records call him Jan Sikkes Elzinga, a name he never used himself.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Marriage, Wirdum, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cdbd5db2-96ce-4086-9d54-3c561b5910e1"],["Baptism of son Johannes, Bozum, 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3c537225-2d22-4a59-84a2-aed6b0d8bbe1"],["Burial register, Rauwerd","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7c260c05-1e20-4389-a284-5970df2adcd4"],["Death of daughter Marijke, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cad92957-6184-cdd4-fc4d-62f2477240c6"],["Death of son Lucas, shoemaker, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:10b111a8-9b1c-239a-866c-f7cfe5611458"],["Marriage of daughter Geertje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b4421935-db82-6c46-dcef-84028b2bd94f"]]},
pietjel:{"n":"Pietje Lucas","d":"1753–1840 · Wirdum","s":"h","p":["ezlucasjans","ezgeertjewillems"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Wirdum on 4 February 1753 as Pyttje, the daughter of Lucas Jans and Geertje Willems. She was living at Swichum when she married Jan Sikkes at Wirdum on 19 May 1782. They had at least four children: Marijke, Lucas, Johannes (born at Bozum in 1791) and Geertje.\n\nJan died at Rauwerd in 1809 or 1810 and Pietje went back to Wirdum, where she lived as a widow for some thirty years. She was there when Johannes married in 1815 and when Geertje married in 1818. On 7 May 1821, aged 68, she made her confession of faith and became a full member of the Reformed church at Wirdum. The census of 1829 lists her there as Pietje Lucas Elzinga, aged 77, and that of 1839 as 88.\n\nHer daughter Geertje died in 1824, aged 28. Pietje died on 26 April 1840; the civil register gives her age as 89, though by her baptism she was 87. The church's membership book puts her death in 1838.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6cf369ac-e86f-e02c-8906-f1d707c1a474"],["Baptism, Wirdum, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7967885f-4efd-49c5-be57-d70a86dd3248"],["Marriage, Wirdum, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cdbd5db2-96ce-4086-9d54-3c561b5910e1"],["Confession of faith, Wirdum, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96dac20d-9f12-4382-b31a-3a57045f4982"],["Census of 1829, Wirdum","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2466483c-ca77-637a-7ecc-404e93f57ec7"],["Census of 1839, Wirdum","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:15dce7ae-b0e5-4ae5-4909-a646b7b7889a"],["Membership book (death noted under 1838)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3756f5a0-e56f-40b1-991a-aa82d922258e"],["Death of daughter Geertje, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2f66669a-c4c0-40a4-f885-4241651db1f7"],["Death record, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9fa6eea1-a4d9-e5d7-052e-63312fad5baa"]],"b":1753,"x":1840},
beendouwes:{"n":"Been Douwes IJsselstein","d":"Warga","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Been (Berend) Douwes lived at Warga in Idaarderadeel, a village on the waterways south of Leeuwarden. Been is a Frisian short form of Berend. He married Gertje Taekes, and in 1811 he registered the surname IJsselstein at Warga, listing four children: Douwe (22), Taeke (17), Hinke (11) and Sjoerdtje (7). His sons carried his father's name, Douwe, and his wife's father's name, Taeke.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider). The family lived at Wartena by 1816, when his daughter Sjoerdtje died there on 17 November, aged 12. On 27 May 1818 both his sons married on the same day, Douwe to Neeltje and Taeke to Antje, daughters of Lieuwe Piebes Kooystra of Eernewoude. Douwe named his first son Been in 1823, but the boy died at Warga the next year. Been's own death has not been found in the indexes.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Surname registration, Berent Douwes IJsselstein, Warga 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50ebbb84-5af7-4c72-a968-97a74329c684"],["Death of daughter Sjoerdtje, Wartena 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d439042-7515-da35-84bf-569e29055478"],["Marriage of son Douwe, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a87e78f8-185a-baaf-5047-f5415ea7b906"],["Death of grandson Been Douwes IJsselstein, Warga 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb38f9a7-27ac-79a2-c8d8-d9a250e28cb0"]]},
gertjet:{"n":"Gertje Taekes","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Been (Berend) Douwes IJsselstein of Warga, in Idaarderadeel. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Taeke, and her second son, born at Wartena on 6 December 1795, was named Taeke after him. Her other children were Douwe (born about 1789), Hinke (about 1800) and Sjoerdtje (about 1804), all listed when her husband registered the surname IJsselstein in 1811.\n\nSjoerdtje died at Wartena on 17 November 1816, aged 12. On 27 May 1818 Gertje is named as the groom's mother at both of her sons' weddings, held on the same day, when Douwe and Taeke married the Kooystra sisters Neeltje and Antje of Eernewoude. Taeke named his only daughter Gertje in 1826. Her death has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Death of daughter Sjoerdtje, Wartena 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d439042-7515-da35-84bf-569e29055478"],["Marriage of son Douwe, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a87e78f8-185a-baaf-5047-f5415ea7b906"],["Surname registration, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50ebbb84-5af7-4c72-a968-97a74329c684"],["Birth of granddaughter Gertje, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f06687cd-460b-c764-f32b-80c992444d01"],["Death duties file of Taeke Berends IJsselstein (born 6 Dec 1795)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7682c829-9476-4c74-a246-f58d044c9b2c"]],"d":"Warga"},
lieuwek:{"n":"Lieuwe Piebes Kooystra","d":"1755–1826 · Eernewoude","s":"h","p":["hzpiebelieuwes","hzantjerutgers"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eernewoude on 25 December 1755 according to the French civil register, and baptised there on 18 January 1757, the youngest child of Piebe Lieuwes and Antje Rutgers. His grandfather Lieuwe Piebes had been a farmer, peat master and schoolmaster in the village. Eernewoude stood in wet peat and lake country, and peat digging was its main work.\n\nHe was a labourer (arbeider), called \"ouvrier\" in the French register of 1811. On 4 May 1788 he married Akke Aukes, also of Eernewoude, and their children were baptised at Garijp: Piebe (1789), Jetske (1791), Antje (1794), Neeltje (1796), Jacob (1798, died 1808), Maaike (1801) and Age (1804). The family name Kooistra (also written Kooystra) came into use for them after 1811.\n\nHis daughters Antje and Neeltje married the brothers Taeke and Douwe IJsselstein of Warga on the same day in May 1818; Maaike married Aan Jans Bogemaker that October and Piebe married Tetje Baukes de Jong in 1819. Lieuwe died in Tietjerksteradeel on 27 May 1826, aged 70, a married man.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3159663b-a535-49d9-a237-7fb8a6521a1d"],["Marriage, Eernewoude 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1bb825c-c986-4b9c-8e29-8f4f67d882cc"],["Baptism of Piebe, Garijp 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e1cbaab-ab87-4a0a-950a-954907bd9624"],["Baptism of Neeltje, Garijp 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:33885b59-9b0e-4c5b-baeb-9f1b1c0dbecd"],["Marriage of Maaike, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7981e212-ce5f-d4cc-50d7-97cb1ecf8579"],["Marriage of Piebe, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6e07f903-9eac-6fd1-0a09-b78af98bd6b7"],["Death record, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:30134fc2-07a2-dd48-fff5-26bdf07686de"]],"b":1755,"x":1826},
akkea:{"n":"Akke Aukes","s":"h","p":["hzaukejacobus","hzjetskefolkerts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Eernewoude on 27 October 1765, the daughter of Auke Jacobus and Jetske Folkerts, who had married there in 1762. Her younger brother Jacob was baptised in 1769. By 1797 her parents were living near Wartena.\n\nOn 4 May 1788 she married Lieuwe Piebes, a labourer of Eernewoude; the registers write her name as Akke or Aukje Aukes. Seven children were born at Eernewoude and baptised at Garijp: Piebe (1789), Jetske (1791), Antje (1794), Neeltje (1796), Jacob (1798), Maaike (1801) and Age (1804). Jacob, named after her father, died in March 1808, aged nine.\n\nHer daughters scattered round the lakes: Jetske married Jan Freerks Terpstra, a farmer at Workum; Antje and Neeltje married the IJsselstein brothers of Warga in 1818; Maaike stayed at Garijp. Lieuwe died in 1826, still married to her. She is named as deceased when her daughter Neeltje died at Warga in 1863, but her own death has not been found in the indexes.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea475521-511e-87e9-8ee6-62fa571cb710"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Akke Aukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d57930bc-1a64-4fa0-9368-6793f204392f"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Auke Jacobus","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51dffd0b-df63-4e2f-9e87-b0bbd3b83b60"],["Marriage, Eernewoude 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1bb825c-c986-4b9c-8e29-8f4f67d882cc"],["Baptism of Age, Garijp 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:edb1292e-0e39-46cc-9162-20af4e300bfb"],["Death of daughter Jitske, Workum 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bccc0f30-ed81-5ae2-4a8d-ec909ea13aba"],["Death of daughter Neeltje, Warga 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a8418cfb-071b-f992-55e9-5fbf74471723"]],"d":"b. 1765 · Eernewoude","b":1765},
durkfdj:{"n":"Durk Folkerts de Jong","s":"h","p":["hzfolkertgjolts","hzneeltjedirks"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eernewoude in Tietjerksteradeel and baptised there on 27 September 1750 (the French civil register later gave 14 October 1749), the son of Folkert Gjolts and Neeltje Dirks. Durk and Dirk are the same name, and both spellings are used for him. He grew up in the house his father headed in Eernewoude from 1753.\n\nOn 30 August 1778 he married Antje Roelofs of Eernewoude. In 1796 he was the village magistrate (dorprechter), a local office of trust; later he is called a fisherman (visser), in 1807 a peat master (veenbaas), the man who organised and paid the gangs digging peat, and in 1811 and 1812 a labourer. In 1811 he took the surname de Jong. Their children, all born at Eernewoude, were Folkert (1781, died), Froukje (1783), Roelof (1787), Folkert (1790, died), Neeltje (1791), Wemeltje (1794), Folkertje (1796), Folkert (1798) and Jan (1802).\n\nFroukje died in 1816 and Folkje in 1817, aged 20. In 1827 he sold a house plot at Eernewoude to his son Roelof for 50 guilders. He died at Eernewoude on 4 April 1838, leaving no real estate. His heirs were his son Jan, a skipper; Neeltje, wife of Ulbe Visser at Wartena; and the children of his late sons Roelof and Folkert and of his daughters.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c5d26f9d-c8a8-509c-1dcb-d41330849fa9"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Dirk Folkerts de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:90585bdd-0384-4351-b34c-06d9462c2521"],["Baptism, Eernewoude 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:008e5353-905e-4ed8-aceb-2c88c88cc9ff"],["Marriage of daughter Wemeltje, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25c4ccf0-d41b-aa22-aa03-05338d2c7fff"],["Death of daughter Folkje, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1b11508c-1059-5166-5262-1705a4056195"],["Notarial deed, sale to his son Roelof, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4be89d1c-a871-4a3b-83ae-3736b90f9816"],["Death duties file, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d14ed81-35ae-4734-a61f-e0db0fe54d51"]],"d":"1750–1838 · Eernewoude","b":1750,"x":1838},
antjero:{"n":"Antje Roelofs","s":"h","p":["hzroelpieters","hzfroukjegeukes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Eernewoude on 3 May 1758, the daughter of Roel Pieters, who came from Oudwoude, and Froukje Geukes of Eernewoude, who had married there in 1755. Her eldest daughter and eldest surviving son were named Froukje and Roelof after them.\n\nOn 30 August 1778, aged 20, she married Durk (Dirk) Folkerts of Eernewoude, later village magistrate, fisherman and peat master, who took the surname de Jong in 1811. She had nine children between 1781 and 1802: Folkert, Froukje, Roelof, Folkert, Neeltje, Wemeltje, Folkertje, Folkert and Jan. The first two Folkerts died as babies. Froukje married Jan Pieters Zandburg in 1807 and died in 1816; Folkje died unmarried in 1817; Wemeltje married Pieter Sjoerds Raven of Drachten in 1815; Neeltje married Ulbe Visser of Wartena in 1812.\n\nShe is named as the mother of the bride in 1812 and 1815, and as mother in Neeltje's death record of 1858. Her husband died at Eernewoude in 1838; her own death has not been found.","src":[["Death of Neeltje de Jong, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c877821-471a-adad-6992-6b640916b707"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Antje Roelofs","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08229ba0-18ff-498f-aa97-d9a004a2909b"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Froukje Dirks de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:124460e2-2f19-4a7a-8f8a-85d6bdfb2395"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Dirk Folkerts de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:90585bdd-0384-4351-b34c-06d9462c2521"],["Marriage of Wemeltje, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25c4ccf0-d41b-aa22-aa03-05338d2c7fff"],["Marriage of Neeltje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c5d26f9d-c8a8-509c-1dcb-d41330849fa9"]],"d":"b. 1758 · Eernewoude","b":1758},
janrp0:{"n":"Jan Roels Postma","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Roels was born about 1775; his second name shows that his father was called Roel. He was living at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland when he married Aafke Durks, also of Wijnjeterp, in the church there on 17 May 1801. In the notarial records the family name is written Posthuma as well as Postma.\n\nThree sons are known: Roel (born 7 April 1802 at Wijnjeterp), Durk (born 24 February 1807 at Hoornsterzwaag, another village in Opsterland) and Hendrik (about 1810, Wijnjeterp). On 11 May 1820 Jan and Aafke made their confession of faith together and became members of the Reformed church in Opsterland.\n\nOn 3 April 1816 Jan Roels Posthuma and Aafke Durks of Wijnjeterp signed a bond for a loan from Freerk Jans Oosterbaan and Kornelis Hartmans of Gorredijk. It was paid off only in 1844. In November 1843 he borrowed again, and on 2 December 1843 property of his was sold at a public auction before a notary.\n\nHe died in Opsterland on 12 March 1847, aged 71. Aafke died ten months later. His son Durk lived until 1889, dying in Utingeradeel aged 82.","src":[["Death of Roel Postma, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8fa28629-8717-f478-1dd6-6a47522460bd"],["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:259c7c91-0207-4b92-adac-67ff7981aeb4"],["Baptism of son Roel, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ba68715a-0e77-444e-9383-45a5b1942bb2"],["Birth of son Durk, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:967e09f2-dadc-4556-9595-298b538d8451"],["Confession of faith, Opsterland, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0bdbd3e0-e358-4a81-ba4f-35a3aab8ee0e"],["Bond, 3 April 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b336660d-d41f-45f9-b52c-1bc5d49c7585"],["Discharge of the bond, 1844","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:710cc328-fef9-45c5-aec5-e191e5e36354"],["Bond, 16 November 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:53f115f1-ff44-485b-b927-c4596b6a2195"],["Public sale, 2 December 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1632459b-7602-44bb-8fda-e010e239dab0"],["Death record, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9160d831-57d5-020c-2287-fca71d3fe9e2"],["Death of son Durk, 1889","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a42464cc-da0f-5f9a-3a7d-4f693c95093d"],["Marriage of son Hendrik, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5c658960-65d8-bd18-6e5e-8efa0de69303"]],"d":"c. 1775–1847","b":1775,"x":1847},
aafked:{"n":"Aafke Durks","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aafke Durks was born about 1776, the daughter of Durk and Froukjen Berends; her father's own second name is not given. She was living at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland when she married Jan Roels, also of Wijnjeterp, on 17 May 1801.\n\nTheir sons were Roel (1802), Durk (1807, born at Hoornsterzwaag) and Hendrik (about 1810). In 1816 she and her husband together signed a bond for a loan from two men of Gorredijk, and on 11 May 1820 they made their confession of faith together in the Reformed church.\n\nJan died in March 1847. Aafke died at Wijnjeterp on 28 January 1848, aged 71, under the name Aafke Durks Posthuma. Her death record calls her a koemelkster, a woman who kept a few cows and lived from their milk, and names her parents, Durk and Froukjen Berends. No record of them has yet been found.","src":[["Death of Roel Postma, 1884","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8fa28629-8717-f478-1dd6-6a47522460bd"],["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:259c7c91-0207-4b92-adac-67ff7981aeb4"],["Confession of faith, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0bdbd3e0-e358-4a81-ba4f-35a3aab8ee0e"],["Bond, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b336660d-d41f-45f9-b52c-1bc5d49c7585"],["Death record, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:21983448-e97a-4b83-8652-ea44b28ae43d"]],"d":"c. 1776–1848","b":1776,"x":1848},
albertdb:{"n":"Albert Douwes (de Boer)","s":"h","p":["ezdouwelourens","ezhendrikjenalberts"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Albert Douwes was born about 1765; his second name shows that his father was called Douwe. He was very probably the Albert, son of Douwe Lourens and Hendrikjen Alberts, who was baptised in the church of Lippenhuizen, Terwispel and Hemrik on 9 December 1764. The age at his burial (42 in 1807) fits that baptism, and he named his second daughter Hendrikje, which matches that mother's name; but no record states his parents outright, so the link is not proved. He was living at Hemrik in Opsterland when he married Aaltje Eiles of Haule, a village to the east in Ooststellingwerf, at Lippenhuizen on 2 September 1804.\n\nTheir daughter Fettje was born at Hemrik on 8 April 1805, and a second daughter, Hendrikje, followed.\n\nHe died at Hemrik in May 1807; the entry of 30 May records that he was 42, married, and left two children. He died before 1811, so he never had a surname, but his daughters later called themselves de Boer, and the death-duty file of his widow in 1834 calls him Albert de Boer. Aaltje married Evert Jans Veenstra, a farmer of Hemrik, in 1809.","src":[["Death of Fetje de Boer, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df461236-d9bd-6b88-c392-ede665c135ac"],["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5be2068b-f725-4240-90a8-0148c0a38434"],["Baptism of daughter Fettje, 1805","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:699ef323-c76e-4204-83a5-3ea9d92ca639"],["Burial entry, Hemrik, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ff248827-9aa9-4289-ba1c-88df3f1cd296"],["Death-duty file of his widow, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a90ec4a0-e15b-460d-90e0-39eb161b8bd1"],["Probable baptism, Lippenhuizen, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e2fac35b-0fc0-44f0-924c-54f6f4ef5737"]],"d":"1764–1807 · Hemrik","b":1764,"x":1807,"u":true},
aaltjee:{"n":"Aaltje Eiles Akkerman","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aaltje Eiles came from Haule in Ooststellingwerf; her second name shows that her father was called Eile, and she later used the surname Akkerman. On 2 September 1804 she married Albert Douwes of Hemrik at Lippenhuizen. Their daughters Fettje (1805) and Hendrikje were born at Hemrik, and Albert died there in May 1807.\n\nOn 15 January 1809 she married again, to Evert Jans Veenstra, a farmer (boer) of Hemrik. They had six children: Eile (born 14 August 1809), Catharina (24 December 1811), Trijntje, Jan, Wybe (about 1817) and Hendrik (about 1818). In 1815 she and Geertje Oedzes of Hemrik sold property to Wiebe Eyles Akkerman and Corneliske Wiebes; Wiebe shared her father's name and her surname. In 1828 a notary drew up a division of an estate (boedelscheiding) in which she took part.\n\nShe died at Hemrik on 7 February 1834. The death-duty file names her as widow of Albert de Boer and wife of Evert Jans Feenstra, farmer, and lists her children from both marriages: Fetje, wife of Roel Jans Postma, and Hendrikje Alberts de Boer, a farmer's daughter; and Eile, Trijntje, the wife of Ate Jurrits Bijstra, and the young Jan, Wybe and Hendrik Everts Veenstra. Real estate belonged to the estate.","src":[["Death of Fetje de Boer, 1845","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:df461236-d9bd-6b88-c392-ede665c135ac"],["First marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5be2068b-f725-4240-90a8-0148c0a38434"],["Second marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1809","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8818ee11-34d7-4a93-abad-9d81236608a3"],["Notarial sale, 8 November 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:561fb950-3528-4519-8ee8-56ffe59f8665"],["Division of estate, 30 July 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0c3c5b88-1669-4114-93a4-fdccd18f9123"],["Death-duty file, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a90ec4a0-e15b-460d-90e0-39eb161b8bd1"],["Death of daughter Catharina, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e4ef5496-ed33-663a-67cb-822c225798ea"],["Evert Jans Veenstra, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d2cdd0f1-2793-3e56-dad0-a0e724d227d8"]],"d":"d. 1834 · Hemrik","x":1834},
durkh:{"n":"Durk Hendriks Jonker","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Durk Hendriks was born about 1726; his second name shows that his father was called Hendrik. He was living at Drachten when he married Lijsbert (Lysbet) Sytzes of Grouw in the church at Grouw on 26 August 1764. Their children were born over a long span: Eeke about 1764, Tjerkje (baptised at Grouw 14 April 1771), Pytterkjen (28 February 1773), Hendrik between about 1775 and 1783, Sytze about 1779 and Yke about 1780, the last at Wartena. Grouw, Wartena and Tietjerk, the places linked with the family, all lie within a few hours' walk of one another, east and south of Leeuwarden.\n\nHe was a farmer (boer). In 1811, when Frisians had to register fixed surnames, his sons took the name Jonker, and his son Hendrik declared at Grouw that his father Durk Jonker was still alive and living at Tietjerk.\n\nHis daughter Eeke married Uilke Bouwes Schaap, a smallholder at Huizum, on the southern edge of Leeuwarden, and Durk died there on 2 February 1816, aged 90. His death record names his wife as Elisabet Zytzes, a formal spelling of Lijsbert Sytzes. Eeke died at Huizum in 1832; her death record, which gives her age as 68, calls her Eeke Sytzes Jonker, though it names Durk and Lijsbert as her parents. Sytze died at Grouw in 1852, and Yke married Hayte Sjoerds van der Meer in 1813.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death record, Huizum, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9f82e0cb-d09b-0218-c72a-7ff8b31e2704"],["Surname registration of son Hendrik, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:da2fc6bd-a6e9-4669-8a36-c9b9ae79375e"],["Death of daughter Eeke, Huizum, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:277c0276-9763-7267-3c73-21449b01c831"],["Death of son Sytze, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b5019f3-abda-c4c4-e51a-13a6aa1a8cd0"],["Marriage of daughter Yke, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e776b74c-96fc-0a46-71af-bb16cf0473bf"],["Marriage, Grouw, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fdf1ba00-ef2e-48ae-a183-480880d3e7e3"],["Baptism of daughter Tjerkje, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ad74bc5f-64ca-4205-ac1d-27ae648c00f0"],["Baptism of daughter Pytterkjen, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3430b0c6-dc47-4caa-9282-0b2ebad65ede"]],"d":"c. 1726–1816","b":1726,"x":1816},
lijsberts:{"n":"Lijsbert Sytzes","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Lijsbert Sytzes, also written Lysbet Sytses or Elisabet Zytzes, was living at Grouw when she married Durk Hendriks of Drachten, later a farmer and called Jonker, in the church at Grouw on 26 August 1764. Her second name shows that her father was called Sytze, and she gave that name to her son Sytze, born about 1779.\n\nHer children were Eeke (about 1764), Tjerkje (baptised at Grouw 1771), Pytterkjen (1773), Hendrik (born between about 1775 and 1783 at Grouw), Sytze (about 1779) and Yke (about 1780, born at Wartena). She is named as their mother in her son Hendrik's marriage record of 1811 and in the death records of Eeke (1832) and Sytze (1852), and as Durk's wife in his death record of 1816.\n\nHer name lived on in her grandchildren: Hendrik named a daughter Lijsbert in 1816, a son Sytze in 1821 and a daughter Tjerkje in 1826. Her own death has not been found, and nothing shows whether she was alive when Durk died at Huizum.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death of her husband, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9f82e0cb-d09b-0218-c72a-7ff8b31e2704"],["Death of daughter Eeke, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:277c0276-9763-7267-3c73-21449b01c831"],["Death of son Sytze, 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0b5019f3-abda-c4c4-e51a-13a6aa1a8cd0"],["Birth of granddaughter Lijsbert, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:93cd6a8e-9a81-63a4-47b9-4e13dc1f92f0"],["Marriage, Grouw, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fdf1ba00-ef2e-48ae-a183-480880d3e7e3"],["Baptism of daughter Tjerkje, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ad74bc5f-64ca-4205-ac1d-27ae648c00f0"]]},
janjelkes:{"n":"Jan Jelkes","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Jelkes was the father of Lemke Jans; his second name shows that his own father was called Jelke. He was living at Oudega when he married Wytske (Wijtske) Wytzes, also of Oudega, in the church there on 3 April 1774. They had at least four children, born in several villages east and south-east of Leeuwarden: Wytze about 1778, Lemke about 1781-1784 at Wartena, Bauke about 1787 at Bergum, and Berbertje about 1791 at Oudega.\n\nWhen Frisians had to take fixed surnames in 1811, his grown children registered separately and chose different names. Wytze, a skipper at Grouw, became Zandberg; Bauke, a fisherman at Wartena, became de Jong; Berbertje used van der Veen, and when she married Sybren Jakobs Valk in 1835 her father was called Jan Jelkes van der Veen. Lemke married Hendrik Durks Jonker of Grouw.\n\nBauke died at Wartena in 1820, aged 33, and Wytze at Grouw in 1833. No record of Jan's own death has been found.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death of daughter Lemke, 1875","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:373592a0-a262-376a-874d-8b3c67faf5b3"],["Death of son Wytze Jans Zandberg, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:907c6a62-8731-3b81-5ab7-fe5b3c385350"],["Death of son Bauke Jans de Jong, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b62e5e52-08e2-553a-9e58-de358850418b"],["Marriage of daughter Berbertje, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:43132163-b3bd-4816-518d-37ce96d1930b"],["Marriage, Oudega, 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d1e2b04-c774-41ea-8d51-b7eb79d38a72"]]},
wytskew:{"n":"Wytske Wytzes","s":"h","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wytske Wytzes was living at Oudega when she married Jan Jelkes, also of Oudega, on 3 April 1774. She was the mother of Lemke Jans, born at Wartena. Her second name shows that her father was called Wytze, and her eldest known son, Wytze Jans Zandberg, a skipper at Grouw, was named after him. Her other children were Bauke (about 1787), a fisherman at Wartena, and Berbertje (about 1791).\n\nWhen her son Bauke died at Wartena in 1820, she was alive and working as a labourer (arbeidster). The census of 1839 lists a Wytske Wytzes Draaistra, aged 86 and born at Wartena, living at Wartena; she is very probably the same woman, which would put her birth about 1753, but the census entry does not name her husband or children.\n\nRecords of her children disagree about her: Lemke's death record of 1875 gives only \"Wytske\", and Berbertje's of 1836 calls her mother Wytske Pieters, while Lemke's marriage record and Bauke's death record give Wytske Wytzes.","src":[["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:160e38d1-7bdc-24f9-ecf3-a2c46f6fd9e2"],["Death of son Bauke, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b62e5e52-08e2-553a-9e58-de358850418b"],["Census of Wartena, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ec50831-f681-ebc1-ba95-3845717c49a5"],["Death of daughter Berbertje, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9cfee3d0-6538-8cfe-6d96-d24bd4a5e385"],["Marriage, Oudega, 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d1e2b04-c774-41ea-8d51-b7eb79d38a72"]]},
davidanthony:{"n":"David Anthony","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Father of Herbert Anthony, the Stawell gold miner, and of Thomas Anthony, a coal miner. The Victorian registration of Herbert's death in 1897 gives his father's names as \"Anthony David\", with the surname left blank, and his mother as Anne Jones.\n\nHe was very probably the David Anthony, tailor, who was living with his wife Ann at Blanberach, in the Glynamman district of Llandeilo Fawr, Carmarthenshire, at the 1841 census. He was entered as 50 (adults' ages that year were usually rounded down to the nearest five, so he was born about 1786 to 1791) and, like all the household, as born in Carmarthenshire. The children at home were William (14), Hannah (9), Herbert, written \"Harbert\" (7), Evan (5) and Margaret (2). Thomas, then about 12, was not there. This is the only David and Ann Anthony with a son Herbert in the transcribed 1841 census; no baptisms for the family have been found to confirm it.\n\nLlandeilo Fawr was a very large parish, stretching from the market town of Llandeilo south-east into the Amman valley, at the western end of the South Wales coalfield. A village tailor of the time made and mended clothes by hand. Both his sons who went to Australia worked underground.","src":[["Death registration of Herbert Anthony, 14761/1897, naming his parents (Victorian BDM index)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Inquest on Thomas Anthony, Sydney Morning Herald, 26 Feb 1879","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article13429904"],["1841 census, Blanberach, Glynamman, Llandeilo Fawr, Carmarthenshire, HO107/1384 (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/67a90875b18f7c1bd0ecae11"]],"d":"b. c. 1790 · Carmarthenshire","b":1790},
annejones:{"n":"Anne Jones","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mother of Herbert Anthony and his elder brother Thomas. Her maiden name, Jones, is recorded on the registration of Herbert's death at Stawell, Victoria, in 1897.\n\nShe was very probably the Ann Anthony, aged 40 and born in Carmarthenshire, who was living with her husband David, a tailor, at Blanberach, in the Glynamman district of Llandeilo Fawr, at the 1841 census. That makes her born about 1796 to 1801. Five children were at home: William (14), Hannah (9), Herbert (7), Evan (5) and the baby of the house, Margaret (2). Thomas, her eldest known son, born about 1829, was living elsewhere. Thomas and Herbert both went to Australia as miners, Thomas to the coal pits near Newcastle, New South Wales, and Herbert to the Victorian goldfields.\n\nIn February 1879, the day before he died in his hut at Hamilton, near Newcastle, Thomas burst into tears in front of a neighbour and said he had \"a good mother somewhere\". The Sydney Morning Herald reported it from the inquest. His will left what he had to Herbert.","src":[["Death registration of Herbert Anthony, 14761/1897, naming his parents (Victorian BDM index)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Inquest on Thomas Anthony, Sydney Morning Herald, 26 Feb 1879","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article13429904"],["1841 census, Blanberach, Glynamman, Llandeilo Fawr, Carmarthenshire, HO107/1384 (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/67a90875b18f7c1bd0ecae11"]],"d":"b. c. 1800 · Carmarthenshire","b":1800},
jamesevan:{"n":"James Evan James","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Father of Mara James, who married Herbert Anthony in Victoria in 1867. The registration of her death at Stawell in 1893 gives her mother as Mary Evans and her father's names as \"James Evan\", with the surname column left blank. In the tree he is called James Evan James, taking James from Mara's maiden name. The same index gives Herbert's father, David Anthony, as \"Anthony David\", with surname and first name turned round, so the man may equally have been Evan James. Mara was born about 1840.\n\nWelsh surnames grew out of fathers' first names, which is why a few of them, such as James, Evans and Jones, are shared by so many families. It also makes one James family hard to tell from another. No birth of a Mara James was found in the index for England and Wales, no Mara James appears in the transcribed 1841 census, and none of the Evan James households in the 1841 census of Carmarthenshire, Herbert's home county, has a wife Mary and a baby daughter. The parish he lived in has not been identified.","src":[["Death registration of Mara Anthony, 16094/1893, naming her parents (Victorian BDM index)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["GRO birth index search, James, 1837 to 1843 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["1841 census search, James, Carmarthenshire (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_queries/new"]]},
maryevans:{"n":"Mary Evans","s":"f","p":[],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mother of Mara James, named with her maiden name, Evans, on the registration of Mara's death at Stawell, Victoria, in 1893. Her husband is given there as James Evan. Mara was born about 1840 and married Herbert Anthony, a miner, in Victoria in 1867; Mary's grandchildren Albert and Matilda Anthony were born at Sebastopol in 1869 and 1873.\n\nNo brother or sister of Mara's with the same parents appears in the Victorian death index, so Mara is the only child of hers known so far.","src":[["Death registration of Mara Anthony, 16094/1893, naming her parents (Victorian BDM index)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
/* brothers, sisters and cousins: not on the direct line, shown in their parents' profiles */
diana:{"n":"Diana Broesder","s":"b","p":["jerry","carla"],"r":["au"],"t":"One of the five children of Jerry Broesder and Carla Degenhart, with Greg, Monique, Robert and Yolanda. Their parents were both Dutch migrants who had come out alone, Jerry from Dordrecht by ship in 1952 and Carla from the Netherlands by air in 1958. The children were raised in Adelaide's southern suburbs; the family home was at Mitchell Park in 1968, when both parents became Australian citizens on the same day, 18 March."},
monique:{"n":"Monique Broesder","s":"b","p":["jerry","carla"],"r":["au"],"t":"A daughter of Jerry and Carla Broesder, and sister of Greg, Diana, Robert and Yolanda. She grew up in Adelaide's southern suburbs, in a family where both parents had been born in the Netherlands. Her mother's mother, Maria van den Berg, came out on the Groote Beer and stayed in Adelaide from November 1962 to November 1963, and her mother's brother, Johannes Degenhart, had settled in Adelaide as well."},
robertb:{"n":"Robert Broesder","s":"b","p":["jerry","carla"],"r":["au"],"t":"A son of Jerry and Carla Broesder, and brother of Greg, Diana, Monique and Yolanda, raised in Adelaide's southern suburbs. His great-grandfather Tjaard Broesder was a baker at Nieuwolda in Groningen, and his grandfather, Jakob Tjaart, moved south to the river country around Dordrecht. His father reached Sydney on the Fairsea on 10 October 1952, aged 20, and his parents were naturalised together on 18 March 1968."},
yolanda:{"n":"Yolanda Broesder","s":"b","p":["jerry","carla"],"r":["au"],"t":"A daughter of Jerry and Carla Broesder, and sister of Greg, Diana, Monique and Robert, raised in Adelaide's southern suburbs. Her father was born in Dordrecht in 1932 and her mother in 1935; her mother's father, a ship's engineer in the Dutch merchant navy, was lost with the steamer Groenlo in November 1941. Both parents came to Australia in the 1950s as young single migrants, and they married here."},
alena:{n:'Alena Broesder',d:'',s:'r',p:['greg','merrilee'],r:['au'],t:'Rory\'s sister, daughter of Greg and Merrilee Broesder.'},
alexanderh:{n:'Alexander Hazenberg',d:'',s:'r',p:['trevor','sue'],r:['au'],t:'Claire\'s brother, son of Trevor and Sue Hazenberg.'},
georgina:{n:'Georgina Hazenberg',d:'',s:'r',p:['trevor','sue'],r:['au'],t:'Claire\'s sister, daughter of Trevor and Sue Hazenberg.'},
anita:{"n":"Anita Frankish","d":"Kerang","s":"f","p":["lawrie","phyllis"],"r":["au"],"t":"One of the three younger children of Lawrie and Phyllis Frankish, born at Kerang, on the Murray River plains of northern Victoria, between 1963 and 1965; her sister Merrilee had been born at Yarra Junction in 1961. Her first years were spent on the family dairy at Murrabit, out towards Gonn Crossing north of Kerang, where her parents milked up to about 100 cows. In January 1974 the family moved to Queensland, to a 3,000-acre grazing property at Tara on the Western Downs that they called Marston."},
anthony:{"n":"Anthony Frankish","d":"Kerang","s":"f","p":["lawrie","phyllis"],"r":["au"],"t":"The only son of Lawrie and Phyllis Frankish, born at Kerang between 1963 and 1965, with sisters Merrilee, Anita and Cathrine. He grew up on the Murrabit dairy and, from January 1974, at Marston, the family's grazing property at Tara, where his parents also owned Tara Independent Tyre Service. The Marston name runs through all the family farms; it comes from his great-grandmother Emily Wetherall, born in 1863 at Marston, beside Marston Moor in Yorkshire."},
cathrine:{"n":"Cathrine Frankish","d":"Kerang","s":"f","p":["lawrie","phyllis"],"r":["au"],"t":"A daughter of Lawrie and Phyllis Frankish, born at Kerang between 1963 and 1965, and sister of Merrilee, Anita and Anthony. The family were dairy farmers at Murrabit, near Kerang, until January 1974, when they moved north to Tara on Queensland's Western Downs. Her father had grown up in Perth and entered the Naval College as a boy of 13 in 1951; her mother came from a timber-milling family at Gladysdale in the Upper Yarra."},
joan:{"n":"Joan Mabel Parkinson","d":"b. 1937 · Yarra Junction","b":1937,"s":"p","p":["ben","alma"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Yarra Junction on 10 September 1937, her mother's thirtieth birthday, the second daughter of Ben Parkinson and Alma Smith. Her second name, Mabel, was that of her mother's mother, Mabel Webb. She grew up at Gladysdale with her sisters Phyllis, a year older, and Pam, born in 1946, next door to her uncle Don Parkinson and aunt Elsie, who was her mother's sister. Her father and Don worked together in the timber mills, and in the 1950s her father played cricket for Gladysdale in the Yarra Valley Cricket Association. Her grandmother Martha Parkinson died at Yarra Junction in 1949, when Joan was twelve.","src":[["District cricket results (Gladysdale: Ben Parkinson 19), Healesville Guardian, 27 Jan 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/65384658"],["Cricket results and notes (Ben Parkinson, Gladysdale), Lilydale Express, 20 Oct 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/267640744"]]},
pam:{"n":"Pamela Elsie Wagner","d":"b. 1946 · Yarra Junction","b":1946,"s":"p","p":["ben","alma"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Yarra Junction on 5 February 1946, the youngest of Ben and Alma Parkinson's three daughters, some nine years after her sisters Phyllis and Joan. Her second name is that of her aunt Elsie, her mother's sister, who had married her father's brother Don and lived next door at Gladysdale. She was twelve when Phyllis married Lawrie Frankish at Gladysdale in September 1958. In 1968 she married John David Wagner, born in Mason City, Iowa, in the United States."},
antjeb:{"n":"Antje Broesder","d":"b. 1929 · Maurik","b":1929,"s":"b","p":["jakobt","grietje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest child of Jakob Tjaart Broesder and Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn, born at Maurik in 1929 and named after her father's mother, Antje Dijk. Maurik is a village on the Rhine in the Betuwe, in Gelderland, and the Maurik population register of 1910–1939 lists her there with her parents. The family soon moved west to Zwijndrecht, where her sister Gijsbertje was born in 1930, and then across the river to Dordrecht, where her brother Tjaard Jacob was born in 1932. The Dordrecht family card lists all five. She was ten or eleven when Germany invaded in May 1940. Her brother left for Australia in 1952.","src":[["Dordrecht family card (parents and three children)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:AD7A4373-C760-4C8D-8AD1-9DA06DF32109"],["Maurik population register 1910-1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rar:AFE4A852-699A-4FC2-9BA1-0C6BCE08618C"]]},
gijs:{"n":"Gijsbertje Broesder","d":"b. 1930 · Zwijndrecht","b":1930,"s":"b","p":["jakobt","grietje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The second daughter of Jakob Tjaart Broesder and Grietje Cornelia de Bruijn, born at Zwijndrecht in 1930 and named after her mother's mother, Gijsbertje de Kreij, the innkeeper's wife at Schelluinen. Zwijndrecht faces Dordrecht across the Oude Maas, and the Zwijndrecht population register for 1921–1935 lists her there. The family then crossed to Dordrecht, where her brother Tjaard Jacob was born in 1932; the Dordrecht family card lists her with her parents, her elder sister Antje and her brother. She was nine or ten when Germany invaded in May 1940.","src":[["Zwijndrecht population register 1921-1935","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:83a3ac45-cde7-1300-d73f-0cbe7200208c"],["Dordrecht family card (parents and three children)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rad:AD7A4373-C760-4C8D-8AD1-9DA06DF32109"]]},
johannesc:{"n":"Johannes Degenhart","d":"b. 1930","b":1930,"s":"b","p":["hendricus","mariavdb"],"r":["nl","au"],"t":"Carla's elder brother, born in 1930, the son of the merchant-navy engineer Hendricus Degenhart and Maria van den Berg, who had married in Amsterdam in 1929. He was eleven when his father was lost with the steamer Groenlo in November 1941, and his mother was left to bring up the two children in occupied Amsterdam.\n\nWith his wife Maria Agatha, née Schuijt, he came out under the Netherlands Australia Migration Agreement; they flew to Sydney on the Dutch airliner PH-LKX, landing on 30 January 1957, and settled in Adelaide. His sister Carla followed by air in November 1958, and their mother came out on the Groote Beer for a year's visit, from November 1962 to November 1963. He was naturalised on 23 April 1965, when he lived at Brighton on the Adelaide coast.\n\nAmong Adelaide's Dutch he was Hans, the everyday form of Johannes, and his wife was Ria. In December 1960 a Hans Degenhart was among the visitors, led by the president of the Netherlands Society of South Australia, who travelled from Adelaide to Whyalla for the Dutch association's Saint Nicholas evening, where he played one of the saint's helpers. In October 1967 Hans Degenhart presented the cup at the Dutch clubs' Inter-Cities soccer tournament in Adelaide. Neither report gives more than the name. Ria died on 22 January 2001, aged 67.","src":[["NAA D4881","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7208037"],["Migrant selection documents, Johannes and Maria A. Degenhart (NAA A2478)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=1228119"],["Alien registration, Maria Agatha Degenhart (NAA D4881)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7208038"],["Immigration file, NAA D400 SA1964/5778","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=201020745"],["Alien registration of his mother, Groote Beer 1962 (NAA D4881)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7197308"],["Certificates of Naturalization, Commonwealth of Australia Gazette, 16 Sep 1965","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article241034600"],["Saint Nicholas Receives Enthusiastic Welcome, Whyalla News, 6 Dec 1960","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/296285764"],["Soccer: Inter-Cities Tournament, Dutch Australian Weekly, 20 Oct 1967","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/225329276"],["Ryerson Index: death notice of Maria Agatha (Ria) Degenhart, Adelaide Advertiser, 24 Jan 2001","https://ryersonindex.org/search.php"]]},
harmb:{"n":"Harm Broesder","d":"1914–1949 · Nieuwolda","b":1914,"x":1949,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 13 February 1914, the youngest of the nine children of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk. His father was 42 and his mother 48, and his eldest brother Epke was already sixteen.\n\nHe was still a boy when the family left Nieuwolda for Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where his father kept Café Dennenbosch by 1922. He became a shopkeeper (winkelier). His mother died in 1941 and his father on 25 June 1948.\n\nHe was engaged to be married when he died at Zuidlaren on New Year's Day 1949, aged 34, six months after his father. The death record names no wife.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 13 Feb 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0a128e00-2a59-dfba-c887-213e063b73f0"],["Death, Zuidlaren, 1 Jan 1949","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:0603de5b-875d-49e9-8524-7b543d38d0a0"],["Death of his father, Zuidlaren 1948","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:97c98bb0-eff6-4c20-9230-cc40ff672bbc"]]},
augb:{"n":"Augustinus Broesder","d":"1904– · Nieuwolda","s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"b":1904,"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 2 February 1904, the fifth child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk. He had the name of his mother's father, the blacksmith Augustinus Dijk, and he learned his own father's trade.\n\nAs a young man he worked away from home. He is entered in a servants' register (dienstbodenregister) kept at Rolde in Drenthe for the years 1920-1939, and on a family card at Utrecht, where he and his brother Jakob Tjaart lodged in the household of their cousin Epke Apotheker and her husband Albert Oosterheerd. Their aunt Elena Broesder lived there too.\n\nHe was a baker of 26 when he married Aaltje Steringa, 27, at Eelde on 24 April 1930. She was the daughter of the Eelde blacksmith Kornelis Steringa and Jantje Nijdam. His father by then kept the café at Zuidlaren.","src":[["Open Archives: Augustinus Broesder","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Augustinus+Broesder"],["Utrecht population register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:373975D4-3D73-69A2-E063-8F04000A801D"],["Birth, Nieuwolda, 2 Feb 1904","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e25411c7-2e0e-3e7a-38c3-6c3668a51f66"],["Marriage, Eelde, 24 Apr 1930","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:fc8666be-cfc3-4190-9149-129f77b0405f"],["Servants' register, Rolde 1920-1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:419a249b-e1e0-411b-bbed-6a84ac5a10b4"],["Marriage of Epke Apotheker and Albert Oosterheerd, 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b3d665b4-1755-3704-d0f6-5359e241ce5f"]]},
roelfienb:{"n":"Roelfien Antje Broesder","d":"1899– · Nieuwolda","s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"b":1899,"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 2 April 1899, the second child and eldest daughter of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk. Her names came from her mother's side: Roelfien was the name of her great-grandmother Roelfien Huisman and, as Roelfie, of her aunt, and Antje that of her mother and grandmother.\n\nShe grew up in the bakery at Nieuwolda and moved with her parents to Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where her father became a café keeper. She was 29, with no occupation of her own recorded, when she married Jacobus Mennen there on 22 August 1928. He was 28, a gardener (tuinman) born at Oude Pekela, the son of the carpenter Derk Mennen and Fennechien Korvemaker. Her sister Elena married at Zuidlaren the following spring.\n\nJacobus died at Zuidlaren on 4 March 1969, aged 69, and the record names her as his wife.","src":[["Open Archives: Roelfien Antje Broesder","https://www.openarchieven.nl/search.php?lang=en&name=Roelfien+Antje+Broesder"],["Birth, Nieuwolda, 2 Apr 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dd19cd7f-f8c3-3b01-632b-ad7fb453f157"],["Marriage, Zuidlaren, 22 Aug 1928","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:ce971a23-258e-4b81-a46f-067cee594d42"],["Death of Jacobus Mennen, Zuidlaren 1969","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:7ed15d95-22de-fb82-ad79-2e70342c7814"]]},
edwardaf:{"n":"Edward Anthony Frankish","sn":"Ted Frankish","d":"1935–1974 · Narembeen","b":1935,"x":1974,"s":"f","p":["walter","mona"],"r":["au"],"t":"Known as Ted. Born at Narembeen Private Hospital, in the WA wheatbelt, on 17 January 1935, while the family home was at Gallipoli Street, Victoria Park; his middle name, Anthony, was his grandmother Matilda's maiden name. He was the second child and eldest son of Walter Frankish, a Perth civil servant and bass-baritone singer, and Mona Fisk. Narembeen is a wheat and sheep town about 280 kilometres east of Perth.\n\nHe grew up in Victoria Park through the war years with an elder sister and two younger brothers. The children went to Rivervale School, where their father was a vice-president of the Parents and Citizens' Association in 1941. In December 1948, aged thirteen, he was named with his sister and brothers in the family's death notices for his uncle Jesse Frankish.\n\nHe moved to Melbourne with his parents in 1952 or 1953, when he was about eighteen, and died in 1974, not yet forty, ten years after his father.","src":[["Birth notice, West Australian, 19 Jan 1935","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/32847485"],["Death notices for Jesse Frankish, West Australian, 27 Dec 1948","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47633969"]]},
walterwf:{"n":"Walter Wetherall Frankish","d":"m. 1962","s":"f","p":["walter","mona"],"r":["au"],"t":"The youngest of the four children of Walter Frankish and Mona Fisk, born after his brother Lawrie, whose birth was in January 1937; Ramona and Ted were the eldest. He was given his father's first name and the Wetherall name of his grandmother Emily, which his father and Lawrie also carried. His first years were spent at the family home in Victoria Park, Perth, where his mother ran a wartime play centre for neighbourhood children in the house and his father sang with the Perth City Band, at St George's Cathedral and in the ABC opera seasons. In 1952 or 1953 he moved to Melbourne with his parents, Ted and Ramona; Lawrie was already in Victoria with the Navy. He married in 1962."},
romona:{"n":"Ramona Frankish","sn":"Ramona (Romona) Frankish","d":"b. 1932 · Scarborough, WA","b":1932,"s":"f","p":["walter","mona"],"r":["au"],"ch":"frankish","t":"Walter and Mona's only daughter and eldest child, born at the family home at Scarborough Beach on 6 December 1932. Known in the family as Romona; the Perth papers spell her Ramona. In May 1941, when the family lived at Victoria Park, her uncle Private E. A. Fisk, Mona's brother, wrote to her from the Middle East with the AIF about his leave in Jerusalem and Bethlehem, and the Daily News printed his letter.\n\nShe was 15 when, in September 1948, the Western Mail named her among the Perth mannequins at a fashion parade held for the Wooroloo colony appeal. She modelled again at Boans' parade of evening gowns in September 1949 and at another parade in March 1950. At Christmas 1951 she was in Cairns, where the Sunday paper noted Miss Ramona Frankish of Perth as the guest of her uncle Ted Fisk, the writer of the wartime letter, and his wife. She moved to Melbourne with her parents in 1952 or 1953.","src":[["Letter from Private E. A. Fisk, Daily News (Perth), 27 May 1941","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/83021295"],["Fashion parade, West Australian, 24 Mar 1950","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47837258"],["Death notices for Jesse Frankish, West Australian, 27 Dec 1948","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47633969"],["These People (fashion parade for Wooroloo appeal), Western Mail, 23 Sep 1948","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/39090870"],["Smart Evening Gowns At Boans Parade, West Australian, 23 Sep 1949","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/47672338"],["Cairns Chronicles, Sunday Australian (Cairns), 30 Dec 1951","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/290862730"]]},
robertwf:{"n":"Robert Wetherall Frankish","d":"1892–1973 · Hull","b":1892,"x":1973,"s":"f","p":["thomas2","emily"],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Robert Wetherall Frankish was born in Hull in 1892, the second son of Thomas Frankish and Emily Wetherall, and was brought to Western Australia as a small boy, landing at Albany with his mother and brothers in 1897. He grew up at Northam, where his father worked for the railways.\n\nIn 1915 he married Nora McDermott at Maylands, Perth; her death record names her parents as William James McDermott and Helen Wetherall. In 1916, aged 23 and living at Maylands, he applied to enlist. Their first son, Robert Walter Wetherall, was born at Maylands in April 1916 and their second, Cyril James Wetherall, at Kalgoorlie in 1918. By 1920 they were back in Northam for good, and Thomas Jesse Wetherall (1920), William Noel Wetherall (1922) and Mervyn Roy Wetherall (1925) were born there. Every son carried the Wetherall name. Two little boys died: Mervyn in Northam Hospital in 1927, aged 18 months, and Brian Keith Wetherall on 5 October 1928, aged ten months; Brian was buried at Karrakatta.\n\nThe family lived in West Northam and later in Lockyer Avenue. Robert was active in the Labor Party's Avon Valley district council by 1930, and from 1945 sat on Northam Municipal Council for the West Ward, retiring for health reasons at the end of 1948 and then being returned unopposed for another vacancy a week later. In September 1943 he and Nora were told that their eldest son, Flight Sergeant Robert Walter Frankish, had been killed in an aircraft accident in England. In the Second World War two more sons named him as next of kin: Thomas Jesse in the RAAF and William Noel in the Army.\n\nRobert died at Northam in 1973, aged 80, and Nora died there in 1978, aged 84. Their son Cyril died at Melville in 1982, and William Noel at Nedlands in 1994.","src":[["Northam Advertiser, 25 Sep 1943","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article260330520"],["Northam Advertiser, 3 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article211929962"],["Northam Advertiser, 10 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article211930318"],["Northam Advertiser, 12 Feb 1930","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article260227226"],["NAA MT1486/1, Frankish, Robert [W]etherall; age 23; address Maylands (application to enlist, 1916)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=9549473"],["DVA WWII Nominal Roll: Frankish entries","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["WA marriage index: Robert Wetherall Frankish and Nora McDermott, Maylands, 1915, reg. 100739","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"],["Death notice, Brian Keith Wetherall Frankish, Northam Advertiser, 6 Oct 1928","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article260121412"]]},
raaf:{"n":"Robert Walter Wetherall Frankish","d":"1916–1943","b":1916,"x":1943,"s":"f","p":["robertwf",null],"r":["au","uk"],"t":"Lawrie's first cousin, the eldest son of Robert and Nora Frankish of Northam. A Flight Sergeant in the RAAF, he had been in England about a year when he was killed in an aircraft accident on 18 September 1943, aged 27. He is buried at Harrogate in Yorkshire, not far from the Marston farm country his grandmother Emily came from.\n\nHe was born at Maylands, Perth, on 16 April 1916 and enlisted in the RAAF in Perth on 21 July 1941, No. 415130, with his father Robert as next of kin. He was serving with No. 1658 Conversion Unit when his Halifax, JB904, was lost in Yorkshire on 18 September 1943. His name is on the Roll of Honour for Northam.\n\nOther accounts of the crash give the aircraft as Halifax JB905 rather than JB904. He was the pilot on a night training flight from RAF Riccall, near Selby, when the starboard engines caught fire at 17,000 feet; part of the crew baled out, and the bomber came down at Mount Pleasant Farm, Armthorpe, near Doncaster, at five minutes to midnight. Three of the crew were killed. He lies in Harrogate (Stonefall) Cemetery, and his name is on panel 122 at the Australian War Memorial in Canberra and at the State War Memorial in Kings Park, Perth. The Aviation Heritage Museum of Western Australia gives his birthday as 15 April 1916, a day earlier than the date above.","src":[["Northam Advertiser, 25 Sep 1943","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article260330520"],["DVA WWII Nominal Roll: Frankish, Robert Walter Wetherall, 415130","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/ww2"],["NAA A705, 166/14/98, casualty file, Halifax JB904, 18 September 1943","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=1068333"],["NAA A9301, 415130, service record","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=5538619"],["Halifax JB905 near Armthorpe, 18 September 1943 (Yorkshire Aircraft)","https://www.yorkshire-aircraft.co.uk/aircraft/yorkshire/york43/jb905b.html"],["Lest We Forget: Frankish, R. W. W. (Aviation Heritage Museum of WA)","https://aviationmuseumwa.org.au/?p=7436"],["Honour Avenues Kings Park: FSGT Ken Frankish, plaque L108, with L108A for FSGT Robert Frankish","https://honouravenueskingspark.com.au/present/hap-database/1130-fsgt-ken-frankish"],["WA birth index: Robert Walter Wetherall Frankish, Maylands, 1916, reg. 100679","https://www.wa.gov.au/organisation/department-of-justice/online-index-search-tool"]]},
margaret:{"n":"Margaret Elaine Brazier","d":"b. 1932","b":1932,"s":"y","p":["bruce","aileen"],"r":["png","au"],"img":"img/debutantes-1951.jpg","ch":"guinea","t":"Born in 1932, the elder daughter of Bruce Brazier and Aileen Warbrick, while the family lived in the Territory of New Guinea. She was about two when she sailed from Rabaul to Brisbane on the Montoro in 1934 with her parents and her baby sister Beverley. In June 1940, when the family's home was at Kavieng, the two girls came to Brisbane with their mother, and after the evacuation of December 1941 their mother and the three children were listed care of her grandmother at Hendra. Later in the war the family home was at Rose Bay in Sydney.\n\nOn 2 January 1951 she landed at Brisbane from New Guinea on the Bulolo; her passenger card is in the National Archives. In November 1951 she made her debut at the Michaelmas Ball in Port Moresby, one of five debutantes photographed for Pacific Islands Monthly. In February 1956 she was visiting her grandmother at Hendra with her mother before they went back to Port Moresby.","src":[["Port Moresby Debutantes, PIM, 1 Nov 1951","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-331027255"],["Port Moresby Has City Comforts, Telegraph, 11 Feb 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/293831648"],["Passenger card, Bulolo, 1951 (NAA BP26/1)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=9106592"],["The Social Round, Telegraph, 20 Jun 1940","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/188198567"],["Evacuees from NG and Papua, PIM, 17 Apr 1942","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-329097613"],["Passenger list, Montoro, 1934 (NAA A907)","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=7829294"]]},
brucew:{"n":"Bruce Warwick Brazier","d":"1941–2006","b":1941,"x":2006,"s":"y","p":["bruce","aileen"],"r":["png","au"],"t":"Bruce Warwick Brazier was born in 1941, the youngest of the three children of Bruce Wilton Brazier, an officer of the New Guinea administration, and Aileen Lucy Warbrick, and their only son. He was still a baby in December 1941, when European women and children were sent south from New Guinea after Japan entered the war. In April 1942 Pacific Islands Monthly listed his mother \"and three children\" among the evacuees, care of his grandmother Adele Warbrick at Hendra, Brisbane. Later in the war the family home was at Rose Bay in Sydney, where his father enlisted in November 1943 before serving in New Guinea with ANGAU.\n\nAfter the war his father went back to the Treasury Department at Port Moresby. Bruce landed at Brisbane from New Guinea on the Bulolo on 2 January 1951, aged nine. By February 1956, aged 14, he was a boarder at the Church of England Grammar School in East Brisbane, known as Churchie, while his parents lived in Port Moresby.\n\nHe died on 24 April 2006, aged 64, late of Indooroopilly, Brisbane.","src":[["Evacuees from NG and Papua, Pacific Islands Monthly, 17 Apr 1942","https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-329097613"],["Port Moresby Has City Comforts, The Telegraph (Brisbane), 11 Feb 1956","https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/293831648"],["WWII service record of his father, NAA B884 N465736","https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/SearchNRetrieve/Interface/DetailsReports/ItemDetail.aspx?Barcode=6163296"]]},
charlesw:{"n":"Charles Elliott Webb","d":"c. 1878–1943 · Adelaide","s":"p","p":["elliott","marthah"],"r":["au"],"t":"Charles Elliott Webb was born in Adelaide in about 1878, the eldest child of Elliott Agar Webb and Martha Hicks, who had married at Laura in January of that year. His sisters were Mabel Ann (1882), Lucy May (about 1886) and Gladys (1889). The family moved to Melbourne in 1887 and settled in Richmond.\n\nHe became a jeweller and watchmaker with a shop in Greville Street, Prahran, and made a crescent-moon brooch for his sister Mabel, which is still in the family. On 20 April 1907 a horse and buggy left unattended in Greville Street by a traveller for Sargood Brothers took fright, mounted the pavement and put a hoof through his shop window, smashing it and damaging jewellery on display. He sued in the County Court for £49; Judge Chomley found the firm negligent, thought the losses somewhat overstated, and awarded £12 with costs.\n\nBy 1928, when his father died, he was living at Queenscliff. He died at Prahran in 1943, aged 65, the same year as his mother.","src":[["The Herald, 18 Jun 1907: Damages for a Street Accident","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article243289078"],["The Argus, 24 Mar 1928: death notice of Elliott Agar Webb","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article3920437"],["Death index, 1943 (reg. 8969/1943), Prahran","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]],"b":1878,"x":1943},
elsie:{"n":"Elsie Alice Smith","d":"b. 1905 · Armadale, Prahran","b":1905,"s":"p","p":["ern","mabel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Elsie Alice Smith was born on 20 June 1905 at Armadale, in the City of Prahran, Melbourne, the third child of Ern Smith, a painter and paperhanger, and Mabel Ann Webb. (The birth index misreads her father's name as John Ernest Martin.) Her elder sister Mabel Ruth had died as a baby in 1902; her brother Ernest Joseph was born in 1903, her sister Alma in 1907 and her youngest sister Doris in 1910. Doris died in 1913, aged three.\n\nAbout 1911 the family moved to Hoddles Creek in the Upper Yarra, where her grandparents Joseph Henry and Eliza Smith had farmed. On 27 January 1934 Elsie married Donald Frederick George (Don) Parkinson at Hoddles Creek. The next year her sister Alma married Don's brother Ben, and the two couples lived side by side at Gladysdale, south of Yarra Junction.","src":[["Birth index, 1905 (reg. 14919/1905), Armadale","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
epkeb:{"n":"Epke Broesder","d":"1897–1971 · Nieuwolda","b":1897,"x":1971,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk, born at Nieuwolda on 3 December 1897, the year after their wedding, and given the name of his father's mother, Epke Hulzebos. Epke was used for boys and girls alike in this family; his cousin Epke Apotheker, born at Loppersum in 1901, was a girl.\n\nEight brothers and sisters followed him at Nieuwolda between 1899 and 1914. He was seven when his baby sister Elena died in 1905, and sixteen when the youngest, Harm, was born. The family moved to Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where his father kept Café Dennenbosch by 1922 and his grandparents Jakob and Epke spent their last years.\n\nHe died at Zuidlaren on 23 July 1971, aged 73, in the village where his mother had died in 1941, his father in 1948 and his brother Harm in 1949. The death entry names no wife.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 3 Dec 1897","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cbe58500-c34c-87d6-2f7d-0707edc4be8c"],["Death, Zuidlaren, 23 Jul 1971","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:03746377-fb5a-21d4-900b-60e6292ecb6a"],["Birth of Epke Apotheker, Loppersum 1901","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f69a448-91fb-09e0-bcea-7883e9ab090c"]]},
antjebroesder:{"n":"Antje Broesder","d":"b. 1900 · Nieuwolda","b":1900,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 4 July 1900, the third child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk, and named after her mother. Her father was then 28. She had an elder brother Epke and sister Roelfien Antje, and six more children followed, of whom one, Elena, died as a baby in 1905.\n\nThe family left Nieuwolda for Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where her father kept a café by 1922. Her sisters Roelfien and Elena both married there, in 1928 and 1929. No marriage or death record for Antje herself has been found in the Groningen or Drenthe registers that are open to the public. Several other women named Antje Broesder appear in those years, daughters of other fathers, and are not to be confused with her.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 4 Jul 1900","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2cd88064-2ba0-b169-8c37-721507fc3cdc"]]},
elenab1905:{"n":"Elena Broesder","d":"1905–1905 · Nieuwolda","b":1905,"x":1905,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 8 March 1905, the sixth child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk. Her mother was two days short of her fortieth birthday. She had the same name as her father's sister, Elena Broesder, a butcher's wife at Loppersum.\n\nShe died at Nieuwolda on 14 July 1905, four months old. She left five older brothers and sisters, aged from seven down to one. The next daughter, born on 5 November 1906, was given the same name.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 8 Mar 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d646df9a-97a4-7db2-b6b1-43ea2f8c79c1"],["Death, Nieuwolda, 14 Jul 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a1186e0f-754b-3ffe-e1d5-4ad9a5fea60c"],["Marriage of her aunt Elena Broesder, Appingedam 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:50c71284-258c-df40-e9c2-c454a51393d0"]]},
elenab:{"n":"Elena Broesder","d":"1906–1966 · Nieuwolda","b":1906,"x":1966,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 5 November 1906, the seventh child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk, and named for the baby sister who had died the year before. The name was also that of her father's sister.\n\nShe moved with her parents to Zuidlaren in Drenthe and was 22 when she married Jan Staal there on 26 April 1929; her father then kept the café. Jan was 25, an office clerk (kantoorbediende) born in the city of Groningen, the son of Riene Lambert Staal and Jantje Sans. A son, also Jan, died in Groningen on 30 January 1934, a year old; his father was then working as a bank messenger (wissellooper).\n\nJan Staal died in Groningen on 11 February 1941, aged 37, leaving her a widow at 34, two months before her own mother died. She died at Zuidlaren on 30 April 1966, aged 59.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 5 Nov 1906","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2218f2cc-3d93-3d1d-84cb-b067a2fa3e57"],["Marriage, Zuidlaren, 26 Apr 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:84d8d23d-cc4d-4579-84a6-78f8b5a0e7cf"],["Death of Jan Staal, Groningen, 11 Feb 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6ff8cf00-cdc6-2b4a-ebc4-77d54dab1b35"],["Death, Zuidlaren, 30 Apr 1966","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:59468db3-156c-e1ed-1ca9-016586cd99a1"],["Death of her son Jan Staal, Groningen 1934","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:af33a660-669f-f6eb-7700-e13391547326"]]},
emkelenus:{"n":"Emkelenus Hendrik Broesder","d":"b. 1909 · Nieuwolda","b":1909,"x":null,"s":"b","p":["tjaardj","antje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Nieuwolda on 8 June 1909, the eighth child of the baker Tjaard Jakob Broesder and Antje Dijk. His unusual names came from his mother's family: her sister Emkelina Dijk had married the Nieuwolda shoemaker Hindrik Harhuis in 1898, and Emkelina in turn had the name of her grandmother Emke Gernaat.\n\nHe grew up at Nieuwolda and then at Zuidlaren in Drenthe, where his father kept a café and later a hotel. He became a driver (chauffeur) and was living at Wildervank, in the Groningen peat colonies, when he married Egberdina Eitens at Zuidlaren on 15 May 1935. Both were 25. She was born at Haren and lived at Zuidlaren, the daughter of Jan Eitens and Harmtje Nijdam. His father is called a hotel keeper (hotelhouder) in the marriage record.","src":[["Birth, Nieuwolda, 8 Jun 1909","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4067b9cb-1032-e849-75d0-7b675247ca96"],["Marriage, Zuidlaren, 15 May 1935","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:4aff0cbd-ea70-72bf-6e4c-d31e33df0ebf"],["Marriage of Emkelina Dijk and Hindrik Harhuis, Nieuwolda 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cb587ed6-e60e-58e0-fcb2-a146a764f81c"]]},
georgedeg:{"n":"George Jacobus Hendricus Degenhart","d":"b. 1899 · Amsterdam","b":1899,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest known child of the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart and Catharina Bruckmann, who married in Amsterdam on 27 July 1898. He was born there in 1899. His first name came from his mother's father, the German seaman Georg Bruckmann, and his other two from his father's father, Hendrikus Johannes Jacobus Degenhart.\n\nHe became a schoolteacher (onderwijzer), as his uncle Hendrikus Degenhart had done before him, and his name is in the Amsterdam militia register. On 29 January 1925, aged 25, he married Agatha Maria Grooff in Amsterdam. She was 24 and had been born in Johannesburg, South Africa, the daughter of Jacobus Philippus Grooff, a loading master (ladingmeester) on the Dutch railways, and Aaltje Kuijper.\n\nHis brothers became a window dresser, a ship's engineer and a printer. The engineer, Hendricus, was lost at sea in November 1941.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 29 Jan 1925","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:7ACA777E-FD70-4329-9D9D-8104B98016BB"],["Parents' marriage, Amsterdam, 27 Jul 1898","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:79F960AD-15A8-46A1-8169-5CC4C0FB825C"],["Amsterdam militia register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9853345c-b3aa-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Marriage of his uncle Hendrikus, teacher, 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:BA863A53-B41B-4B23-83EF-1DC771493D06"]]},
johannesmhd:{"n":"Johannes Maria Hendricus Degenhart","d":"1900–2000 · Amsterdam","b":1900,"x":2000,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 25 September 1900, the second son of the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart and Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann. His name is in the Amsterdam militia register.\n\nHe was a window dresser (etaleur) when he married Petronella Anna Catharina Pieterse in Amsterdam on 1 June 1927. He was 26 and she was 27, born in the city, the daughter of Johannes Jacobus Pieterse, a lithographer (steendrukker), and Anna Catharina Klementkow.\n\nThe national register of honours lists him with the silver medal of the Order of Orange-Nassau. His younger brother Hendricus, a ship's engineer, was lost at sea in November 1941.\n\nHe died in Amsterdam on 9 April 2000, aged 99, and was cremated at De Nieuwe Ooster five days later. The Amsterdam city archive also holds a personal card for him.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 1 Jun 1927","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:F35DE39E-72C4-427C-8A56-C0F7451A10BB"],["Amsterdam personal card (born 25 Sep 1900)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:0d192dec-0039-4e1e-bf7b-46e5b9a32170"],["Register of honours, Order of Orange-Nassau (silver)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/ghn:1728d35c-d2d8-11e8-828a-00505693001d"],["Amsterdam militia register","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:98533459-f66c-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Amsterdam cremation and burial register (De Nieuwe Ooster), 2000","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Degenhart%20Begraafregisters%22%7D"]]},
elizabethdeg:{"n":"Elizabeth Bernardina Jacoba Degenhart","d":"b. 1905 · Amsterdam","b":1905,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam about 1906, the fourth of the six known children of the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart and Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann, and their elder daughter. She came after three brothers, George, Johannes and Hendricus; her sister Carolina was born at the end of 1914 and her brother Henricus in 1916.\n\nOn 29 August 1928, aged 22, she married Wilhelmus Johannes Schuijt in Amsterdam. He was 29, a shop assistant (winkelbediende) born at Zaandam, the son of Gerrit Schuijt and Maria Agtha Bergman. Her father was still working as a diamond polisher.\n\nHer brother Hendricus, a ship's engineer in the merchant navy, married the following year and was lost at sea in November 1941.\n\nThe Amsterdam family card of her parents' household gives her birth date as 13 November 1905, which fits her age of 22 at her marriage in August 1928.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 29 Aug 1928","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:172CF9C1-3335-448E-906A-EFC80F82FE0F"],["Family card (gezinskaart) of J. Degenhart, Stadsarchief Amsterdam archive 5422 inv. 303","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Degenhart%20Gezinskaarten%22%7D"]]},
carolinahmd:{"n":"Carolina Hermina Maria Degenhart","d":"1914–2004 · Amsterdam","b":1914,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 26 December 1914, a daughter of the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart and Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann, and a younger sister of Hendricus. Her first name was that of her mother's sister, Carolina Maria Bruckmann.\n\nOn 5 June 1940, three weeks after the German occupation began, she married Cornelis Dorotheus de Beurs in Amsterdam. Both were 25. He was a ship's carpenter (scheepstimmerman) born in the city, the son of Johannes de Beurs, a smith, and Hendrica Anna Maria Bleeker.\n\nHendricus's daughter, born in 1935, was also given the name Carolina. Carolina Hermina Maria died in Amsterdam on 13 April 2004, aged 89, and was cremated at De Nieuwe Ooster; the register still names Cornelis Dorotheus de Beurs as her husband. The Amsterdam city archive also holds a personal card for her.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 5 Jun 1940","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:AA7F8373-E088-4610-AD93-89D0615AD6BE"],["Amsterdam personal card (born 26 December 1914)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:35689468-4fbb-4972-81a2-8e39365a245c"],["Amsterdam family card","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:98533444-a4a4-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"],["Amsterdam cremation and burial register (De Nieuwe Ooster), 2004","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Degenhart%20Begraafregisters%22%7D"]],"x":2004},
henricusmd:{"n":"Henricus Maria Degenhart","d":"b. 1916 · Amsterdam","b":1916,"x":null,"s":"b","p":["johannesd","catharinab"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born in Amsterdam on 11 April 1916, the youngest known child of the diamond polisher Johannes Degenhart and Catharina Elisabeth Bruckmann. He was twelve years younger than his brother Hendricus, whose first name he shared in its Latin form.\n\nHe was a printer (boekdrukker) aged 24 when he married Catharina Gerarda de Bree in Amsterdam on 2 April 1941, in the first year of the German occupation. She was 21, born in the city, the daughter of Petrus Theodorus de Bree, a carrier's man (expeditieknecht), and Cornelia Maria Gruter.\n\nHis brother Hendricus was lost at sea that November, when the SS Groenlo was sunk off the English coast.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 2 Apr 1941","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:357A5825-F58F-489C-846F-79D92A40A711"],["Amsterdam militia register (born 11 April 1916)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9853345d-0e4a-56a3-e053-b784100ade19"]]},
bernardusjvdb:{"n":"Bernardus Josephus van den Berg","d":"b. 1892 · Tilburg","b":1892,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest child, born at Tilburg on 28 January 1892, nine months after his parents married there. His childhood followed his father's work: the family was at Waalwijk in 1893, at Besoijen from 1894 to 1896, at Meppel in Drenthe from 1899 to 1902 and at Zaandam by 1905, and the population registers of Waalwijk, Zaandam and Rotterdam all list him with his parents.\n\nHe followed his father's trade as a shoemaker (schoenmaker). On 11 April 1923, aged 31, he married Petronella Helena Heselaars, 28, in Amsterdam. She was born in Amsterdam, the daughter of Gerardus Heselaars and Klazina van Doorn, and worked as a charwoman (werkster). His father was still working as a shoemaker at the time. His sister Johanna married in Amsterdam five weeks later.\n\nThe Stadsarchief Amsterdam holds an archived person card for Bernardus Josephus, marked as deceased, in the series of cards withdrawn between 1964 and 1994, so the death took place at Amsterdam in those years. The scan of the card is not yet open to the public, so the exact date could not be read.","src":[["Birth, Tilburg, 28 Jan 1892","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:6fd18df6-0df1-0979-068c-64b255ad652d"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 11 Apr 1923","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:315F6CA3-8349-4C7F-A773-84B2205C0AE4"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth record, Tilburg (Regionaal Archief Tilburg)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:75ac7114-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Waalwijk population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:815a5693-cbeb-1b2d-5c2e-b8f4d0760628"],["Amsterdam person card index (Archiefkaarten, inv. 1291)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/1291"]]},
franciscusvdb:{"n":"Franciscus van den Berg","d":"b. 1893 · Waalwijk","b":1893,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Waalwijk on 5 July 1893 and named after his mother's father, Franciscus de Kock. His father, then 22, registered the birth, with two Waalwijk shoemakers as witnesses. Waalwijk was the centre of the Langstraat, the main shoe and leather district of the Netherlands.\n\nA shoemaker (schoenmaker) like his father and elder brother. He married Gerardina Maria Anna Sindorf, a policeman's daughter, in Amsterdam on 5 August 1914, in the first week of the First World War; he was 21 and she was 19.\n\nTheir daughter Cornelia Catharina Gerardina was born in Amsterdam about 1915. She married there on 25 November 1936, and her husband, Joannes Cornelis de Kok, was a Tilburg-born shoemaker too. Franciscus was still working as a shoemaker then. His own marriage ended in divorce on 20 June 1955, after almost 41 years.","src":[["Birth, Waalwijk, 5 Jul 1893","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:5e739a25-5a09-df91-862a-93029a8342c1"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 5 Aug 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:0E2C8EC9-F187-4D84-A6E4-FAADC648B8E6"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth record, Waalwijk (Streekarchief Langstraat)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:a3b485b4-e14b-ab5d-c4fc-cd128ed3aff3"],["Marriage of daughter Cornelia, Amsterdam, 1936","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:516A6020-C6C1-4DA5-9399-75814790300F"]]},
mariageertruida:{"n":"Maria Geertruida van den Berg","d":"b. 1894 · Besoijen","b":1894,"x":null,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Besoijen on 14 October 1894 and shares her names with her father's mother, Maria Gertruda Hamers. Her father, a shoemaker of 23, registered the birth. The two witnesses were her uncles by marriage, Laurens Antonius van der Wiel and Floris Poulus van Eekert, both Besoijen shoemakers married to her mother's sisters.\n\nShe was the third child and eldest daughter. She was a year old when her sister Maria Elisabeth was born at Besoijen, and about four when the family left Brabant for Meppel in Drenthe. She is listed with her parents in the Rotterdam population register, and also in the registers of Waalwijk, Zaandam and a Drenthe municipality, which trace the family's moves.\n\nHer mother's Amsterdam person card records that she left the family household for Sloten, then a separate municipality just west of Amsterdam, on 21 June 1915, aged 20. No marriage or death has been found for her.","src":[["Birth, Besoijen, 14 Oct 1894","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:d82f4732-881b-d290-f286-7df162eca7a4"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth record, Besoijen (Streekarchief Langstraat)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:9c565094-4811-e56b-952a-39667a25dca6"],["Besoijen population register entry","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:68eb2a06-3866-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Drenthe population register entry","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:0b5ba5d8-9213-10d1-3080-c5fd43e0c08a"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Mother's Amsterdam person card (Archiefkaarten, inv. 438)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/438"]]},
mariaelisabethvdb:{"n":"Maria Elisabeth van den Berg","d":"b. 1895 · Besoijen","b":1895,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Besoijen on 19 December 1895, the fourth child. Her father, a shoemaker of 24, registered the birth, and the witnesses were the same two shoemaker uncles who had stood for her sister Maria Geertruida the year before, Lourens Antonius van der Wiel and Floris Poulus van Eekert.\n\nShe moved with the family to Meppel, Zaandam and Amsterdam, and is listed with her parents in the population registers of Waalwijk, Zaandam and Rotterdam.\n\nOn 6 October 1926, aged 30, she married Leonard Hessels, a 35-year-old Amsterdam shoemaker, the same trade as her father and brothers. He was born in Amsterdam, the son of Bernardus Hessels and Cornelia Martina Johanna van der Poll. Her father was still described as a shoemaker in the marriage record.\n\nHer mother's Amsterdam person card records that she left the family household for Sloten, just west of Amsterdam, on 20 August 1917, where her sister Maria Geertruida had gone two years earlier.","src":[["Birth, Besoijen, 19 Dec 1895","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:1a511c34-7647-aca9-9930-15eddf135a94"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 6 Oct 1926","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:A8BE2446-8DFD-4ABC-876A-825A07F60762"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth record, Besoijen (Streekarchief Langstraat)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:2d2e8452-833e-481b-fc0b-eaf8393d0fd2"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Mother's Amsterdam person card (Archiefkaarten, inv. 438)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/438"]]},
johannamcvdb:{"n":"Johanna Maria Catharina van den Berg","d":"b. 1899 · Meppel","b":1899,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Meppel in Drenthe on 30 September 1899, twenty months before her sister Maria Catharina Louiza was born in the same town. Her father, a shoemaker of 28, registered the birth. The Waalwijk population register enters her birth date as 30 December 1899, but the Meppel birth record and the Rotterdam register both give 30 September.\n\nShe was the fifth child and the first born outside Brabant. The family moved on to Zaandam by 1905 and then to Amsterdam.\n\nOn 16 May 1923, aged 23, she married Johannes Hendricus Maria Veerkamp, a 27-year-old railway worker (spoorwegarbeider), in Amsterdam. He was born in Amsterdam, the son of Jacobus Joannes Veerkamp and Christina Maria Faanhof. Her brother Bernardus had married there five weeks earlier.\n\nThe Stadsarchief Amsterdam holds an archived person card for Johanna Maria Catharina, marked as deceased, in the series of cards withdrawn between 1964 and 1994, so the death took place at Amsterdam in those years. The scan of the card is not yet open to the public, so the exact date could not be read.","src":[["Birth, Meppel, 30 Sep 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:6392eb59-30d5-4095-8fbd-c38ee10a1dac"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 16 May 1923","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:F74DC247-2223-490B-9983-746BF403C9DD"],["Waalwijk population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:815a5693-cbeb-1b2d-5c2e-b8f4d0760628"],["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Amsterdam person card index (Archiefkaarten, inv. 1289)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/1289"]]},
petrusavdb:{"n":"Petrus Anthonius van den Berg","d":"b. 1905 · Zaandam","b":1905,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Zaandam on 3 October 1905, after the family had moved from Drenthe to North Holland. He was the eighth child, and the Zaandam population register lists him with his parents and seven older brothers and sisters. The family later lived in Amsterdam.\n\nUnlike his father and brothers he did not go into shoemaking: he became a house painter (huisschilder). On 7 September 1938, aged 32, he married Hermanna Wilhelmina Visscher, 24, born at Zwolle, the daughter of a dock worker (bootwerker), Hendrik Visscher, and Luitje Stork. The marriage record names both his parents but gives no occupation for his father.\n\nThe Stadsarchief Amsterdam holds an archived person card for Petrus Anthonius, marked as deceased, in the series of cards withdrawn between 1964 and 1994, so the death took place at Amsterdam in those years. The scan of the card is not yet open to the public, so the exact date could not be read.","src":[["Birth, Zaandam, 3 Oct 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:FBCBAE7C-4617-460A-A568-B08C0E8BBADA"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 7 Sep 1938","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:97B4FACC-4CD6-4D34-A6FD-BF57E4F9F91E"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"],["Amsterdam person card index (Archiefkaarten, inv. 1289)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/1289"]]},
josephusjvdb:{"n":"Josephus Johannes van den Berg","d":"b. 1913 · Amsterdam","b":1913,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The youngest known child, born in Amsterdam on 27 June 1913 and given his father's full name. He was the only one of the nine children born in Amsterdam, some eight years after his brother Petrus, and more than twenty years after the eldest, Bernardus.\n\nHe too was a shoemaker (schoenmaker), the fourth in the family after his father and his brothers Bernardus and Franciscus. On 10 July 1940, aged 27, he married Marie Hiemerda, 21, a farm labourer's daughter from Menaldumadeel in Friesland. Her parents were Klaas Hiemerda and Akke van der Weijde. The wedding took place in Amsterdam two months after the German invasion of the Netherlands. The marriage record names both his parents but gives no occupation for his father.\n\nThe Stadsarchief Amsterdam holds an archived person card for him, which gives his birth date and marks him as deceased, in the series of cards withdrawn between 1939 and 1960, which places his death at Amsterdam in those years. The scan is not open to the public, so the exact date could not be read.","src":[["Marriage, Amsterdam, 10 Jul 1940","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:8DF67C6A-3923-49A2-BA9D-F06AE1CD8C02"],["Amsterdam person card index (Archiefkaarten, inv. 57)","https://archief.amsterdam/archief/30238/57"],["Stadsarchief Amsterdam index search","https://archief.amsterdam/indexen/persons?ss=%7B%22q%22:%22Josephus%20Johannes%20van%20den%20Berg%22%7D"]]},
rinskeh:{"n":"Rinske Hazenberg","d":"b. 1908 · Grouw","b":1908,"x":null,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest child of Willem Hazenberg and Yttje van der Meer, born at Grouw on 28 June 1908 and registered at Idaarderadeel. She has the same first name as her mother's mother, Rinske IJsselstein, who had died ten years before. The family moved to Warga, where her father farmed, when she was a small girl.\n\nOn 15 May 1930, aged 21, she married Uiltje Kalsbeek, 26, born at Swichum, the son of Klaas Kalsbeek and Joukje Hornstra, at Idaarderadeel. Their daughter Yttje, named after Rinske's mother, was born on 29 September 1932; she married Hendrik van der Meer and died in Ferwerderadeel on 15 February 1974, aged 41. Her father's death notice of 1958 lists \"R. Kalsbeek-Hazenberg, U. Kalsbeek\" at Warga.","src":[["Birth, Idaarderadeel, 28 Jun 1908","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b5a1bee-09ce-11e5-a87c-131354e96ba8"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 15 May 1930","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e136aef0-09d0-11e5-b230-ab8365150100"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"],["Population register, Grouw (born 28 Jun 1908)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:292969ee-b823-0ae1-2e6b-d6699f55882d"],["Death of daughter Yttje Kalsbeek, 1974","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a56e4e0-0dae-11e5-bfa3-2758a17421bc"]]},
hendrikh:{"n":"Hendrik Hazenberg","d":"b. 1909 · Grouw","b":1909,"x":null,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born on 29 June 1909 and shares a name with his father's father, Hendrik Hazenberg. He married Anna Barbara van Schagen of Leeuwarden at Idaarderadeel on 13 May 1933, the same day his younger brother Taeke married Aukje Elzinga. He was 23 and she was 20. The public prosecutor at Leeuwarden had waived the usual waiting period for the marriage on 9 May. In 1958 the couple are listed at Rotstergaast in his father's death notice.\n\nHis bride was born at Leeuwarden on 14 April 1913, the daughter of Silvester Adrianus van Schagen and Trijntje Zijlstra. By 1939 the couple lived at Rottum, near Heerenveen, and the family register there lists four children born between 1934 and 1939, the first two at Grouw and the others at Rottum.","src":[["Birth, Idaarderadeel, 29 Jun 1909","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b5a4c18-09ce-11e5-a8e9-934362ce51f4"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 13 May 1933","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e136784a-09d0-11e5-b1c7-f7908df37256"],["Population register, Rottum, 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9c13d899-f1fc-520a-86fd-8ec0797d0615"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"]]},
aaltjehaz:{"n":"Aaltje Hazenberg","d":"b. 1916 · Warga","b":1916,"x":null,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Warga on 13 August 1916, when her father was a cattle farmer there aged 39. She has the same first name as his mother, Aaltje Iedema. She was the fourth of six children; her brothers Hendrik and Taeke married on the same day in 1933.\n\nOn 17 October 1942, aged 26, she married Willem Martens, 33, born at IJsbrechtum, the son of Ate Martens and Sipkje Agema, at Rauwerderhem, the municipality that included Irnsum, where her parents were living by the following year. Her father's death notice of 1958 lists \"A. Martens-Hazenberg, W. Martens\" at Dokkum, the town where he spent his last years.","src":[["Birth, Warga, 13 Aug 1916","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cd83dc47-22a3-4812-85f5-0d4e4d9d51ce"],["Marriage, Rauwerderhem, 17 Oct 1942","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e150ebe4-09d0-11e5-ba1b-4334672d70a7"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"]]},
tjeerdhaz:{"n":"Tjeerd Hazenberg","d":"b. 1919 · Warga","b":1919,"x":null,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Warga on 26 July 1919, the fifth of the six children of Willem Hendriks Hazenberg and Yttje van der Meer. His father, then 42, is called a cattle farmer (veehouder) at Warga in the birth record. He shares a first name with his mother's grandfather, Tjeerd Taekes van der Meer of Surhuizum, and with his mother's brother, who died as a baby in 1880.\n\nHe grew up at Warga with his younger sister Sipkje, while the older children married in the 1930s. He later left Friesland: his father's death notice of December 1958 lists \"T. Hazenberg, A. Hazenberg-Flim\" at Hengelo, the textile town in Overijssel, among the children who mourned him.","src":[["Birth, Warga, 26 Jul 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3a6cc4d-0f18-44e2-88f0-854d551fdf2a"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"]]},
sipkjeh:{"n":"Sipkje Hazenberg","d":"b. 1922 · Warga","b":1922,"x":null,"s":"h","p":["willemh","yttje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The youngest child, born at Warga on 17 July 1922, when her father was 45 and a cattle farmer there. She was a maid (dienstbode) of 21 when she married Gabe Hornstra, a farmhand born at Aegum and the son of Hotze Hornstra, a shopkeeper at Warga, at Idaarderadeel on 23 September 1943. Her father, by then a farm labourer at Irnsum, and her mother are named in the record.\n\nLike her brother Taeke, she emigrated to Australia. On 12 December 1950 the Leeuwarder Courant carried a notice in Frisian from \"G. Hornstra, S. Hornstra-Hazenberg\" announcing the birth of a son on 6 December 1950 at Drouin, in Gippsland, Victoria. Her father's death notice of 1958 lists the couple under Australia.","src":[["Birth, Warga, 17 Jul 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5e2f98d1-be2b-4aa1-94d4-4baf48d4d217"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 23 Sep 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3988c2d6-8565-8264-7452-1d334dd1042b"],["Birth notice Hornstra-Hazenberg, Drouin (Vic.), Leeuwarder Courant, 12 Dec 1950","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19501212-6033"],["Death notice of Willem H. Hazenberg, Leeuwarder Courant, 8 Dec 1958","https://www.dekrantvantoen.nl/vw/article.do?id=LC-19581208-12019"]]},
corneliametje:{"n":"Cornelia Metje de Bruijn","d":"1903–1965 · Schelluinen","b":1903,"x":1965,"s":"b","p":["leendertb","gijsbertje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelia Metje de Bruijn was born at Schelluinen on 13 April 1903, the sixth of the eight children of the innkeeper Leendert de Bruijn and Gijsbertje de Kreij, and an elder sister of Grietje. Her second name, Metje, came from her father's grandmother, Metje Hagen.\n\nOn 18 September 1925, aged 22, she married Adriaan de Groot at Schelluinen. He was born there on 24 September 1900, the son of Arie de Groot and Maria Smits, and at the time of the wedding he was the skipper of a motor barge (motorschipper). He later worked as a life-insurance agent, and the family were registered at Schoonhoven, on the Lek.\n\nShe died at Gouda on 5 August 1965, aged 62. Adriaan died at Schoonhoven on 2 July 1967.","src":[["Family card, Schoonhoven","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:7b7e3956-d72e-46ce-b3fe-ac9c0cbe00d0"],["Death, Gouda, 5 Aug 1965","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:0456b2fe-1c9e-fe77-237c-215d9b7a992d"],["Death of Adriaan de Groot, Schoonhoven, 2 Jul 1967","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:264afa0a-cbbc-923f-1321-6ff78ada77a8"],["Birth register, Schelluinen 1903","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6890775"],["Marriage, Schelluinen, 18 September 1925","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21094947"]]},
leendertdb:{"n":"Leendert de Bruijn","d":"1906–1951 · Schelluinen","b":1906,"x":1951,"s":"b","p":["leendertb","gijsbertje"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Leendert de Bruijn was born at Schelluinen on 27 February 1906, the seventh of the eight children of the innkeeper Leendert de Bruijn and Gijsbertje de Kreij, and named after his father. His younger sister Grietje was born two years later.\n\nOn 7 June 1928, aged 22, he married Antonia Vonk at Schelluinen. He became a cable fitter (kabelmonteur), a man who laid and joined underground electricity and telephone cables, and later lived at Schoonhoven on the Lek, where his sister Cornelia Metje also lived.\n\nHe died in Rotterdam on 12 September 1951, aged 45, while still living at Schoonhoven; his death record names him as the husband of Antonia Vonk.","src":[["Death, Rotterdam, 12 Sep 1951","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:93219a49-9687-b41b-00fe-6db4cd5f3ce2"],["Death (occupation), 12 Sep 1951","https://www.openarchieven.nl/smh:4de6ad89-5d1a-3b8e-f95c-40dfb2b47911"],["Birth register, Schelluinen 1906","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6891252"],["Marriage, Schelluinen, 7 June 1928","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21095458"],["Death record, Rotterdam 1951 (current id)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:3864B8CD-D5A3-42F6-A11C-2748806B105C"]]},
johanneselz:{"n":"Johannes Elzinga","d":"b. c. 1906 · Gevelsberg, Germany","b":1906,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest child of Jan Elzinga and Aaltje Postma, who married at Rauwerderhem on 25 May 1905. His marriage record gives his birthplace as Gevelsberg, a small industrial town in Westphalia, Germany, on the southern edge of the Ruhr, which shows that his parents spent time there soon after their wedding. In the decades before the First World War many thousands of Dutch workers went to the mines and factories of the Ruhr. By 1909 the family was back in Friesland, where his sister Aukje was born at Akkrum. He was named after his father's father, Johannes Jans Elzinga.\n\nHe grew up at Irnsum and Terband with his brothers Jacob and Wiemer and his sisters Aukje, Theodora, Ybeltje and Jantje. On 1 December 1927, aged 21, he married Grietje Lageveen, 26, born at Beets, at Opsterland.\n\nThey settled at Irnsum, where his father's family had lived since the 1840s. A child of theirs was stillborn there on 30 May 1944. Their son Geert, who lived at Irnsum, died at Leeuwarden on 11 August 1958, aged 21.","src":[["Marriage, Opsterland, 1 Dec 1927","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e14d4fc0-09d0-11e5-b381-e3b2125bdde9"],["Parents' marriage, Rauwerderhem, 25 May 1905","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9a9ce2a9-0b80-5c58-fd8b-edf9aa0c710f"],["Stillborn child, Rauwerderhem, 1944","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a0ad7d7b-44e5-4204-84f9-a656a1f61dc0"],["Death of son Geert, Leeuwarden, 1958","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b3259f18-8578-778c-af79-b521780d9fce"]]},
jacobelz:{"n":"Jacob Elzinga","d":"b. 1912 · Irnsum","b":1912,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Irnsum on 8 April 1912, the third child of Jan Elzinga, then a labourer, and Aaltje Postma, and named after his mother's father, Jacob Postma. His elder brother Johannes had been born in Germany and his sister Aukje at Akkrum; after him came Theodora (1916), Wiemer (1919), Ybeltje (1922) and Jantje (about 1923). He moved with the family to Terband, near Heerenveen, when he was a small boy, and his father became a cattle farmer there.\n\nOn 12 May 1934, both aged 22, he married Aukje Kroes, born at Terhorne on 20 May 1911, at Rauwerderhem. Their first child was born at Rottum, in Schoterland south-east of Heerenveen, in November 1935, and a population register card of 1939 shows the family living at Rottum.","src":[["Birth, Rauwerderhem, 8 Apr 1912","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9b7ce37c-09ce-11e5-be9a-2f3690e2f6e5"],["Marriage, Rauwerderhem, 12 May 1934","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1506c50-09d0-11e5-b935-17a446f4c774"],["Population register, Rottum, 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1a382a35-eb3d-5d1b-93e0-105f440d63e2"]]},
theodoraelz:{"n":"Theodora Elzinga","d":"b. 1916 · Irnsum","b":1916,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Irnsum on 12 October 1916, when her father Jan Elzinga was a labourer there, the fourth child of Jan and Aaltje Postma. She was named after her father's mother, Theodora Joukes Stok, who lived until 1936. Within three years of her birth the family moved to Terband, near Heerenveen, where her father kept cattle and later collected milk by boat.\n\nShe married Gooitzen Peenstra, 23, born at Grouw, at Idaarderadeel on 13 May 1939, six years to the day after her sister Aukje's wedding. A child of theirs was stillborn in Idaarderadeel on 21 June 1946. Her brother Wiemer also married in Idaarderadeel, in 1942.","src":[["Birth, Irnsum, 12 Oct 1916","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:32c495fd-8407-4415-917b-f77cc06fcf5a"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 13 May 1939","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1378492-09d0-11e5-b3b6-1ba2b159807b"],["Stillborn child, Idaarderadeel, 1946","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:54a096dc-5cfb-4b28-84f5-fb0c2ebfa138"],["Death of her grandmother Theodora Stok, 1936","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8f8f1358-dc13-cbbd-e3ce-bf6116bf1868"]]},
wiemerelz:{"n":"Wiemer Elzinga","d":"b. 1919 · Terband","b":1919,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Terband, near Heerenveen, on 29 April 1919, by which time his father Jan Elzinga had become a cattle farmer (veehouder). He was the fifth child of Jan and Aaltje Postma, and the first born at Terband; his elder brothers and sisters Johannes, Aukje, Jacob and Theodora had been born in Germany, at Akkrum and at Irnsum. He bore the name of his father's brother Wiemer, a labourer at Irnsum. Two sisters, Ybeltje and Jantje, followed.\n\nDuring the German occupation, on 14 November 1942, he married Rigtje Visser, 28, born at Roordahuizum, at Idaarderadeel. Roordahuizum, north of Grouw, was also the birthplace of his great-great-grandmother Trijntje Oeges Tuinstra.","src":[["Birth, Terband, 29 Apr 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25a143e3-ec50-996a-b834-bf62ca0c6e90"],["Marriage, Idaarderadeel, 14 Nov 1942","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e1387a1e-09d0-11e5-b56e-d7c7eb798431"],["Marriage of his uncle Wiemer Elzinga, 1919","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f300d9ca-25c8-eeb4-dd51-a3ecb18ffdb7"]]},
ybeltjeelz:{"n":"Ybeltje Elzinga","d":"b. 1922 · Terband","b":1922,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Terband, near Heerenveen, on 4 June 1922, the sixth child of Jan Elzinga and Aaltje Postma. At her birth her father's work was given as milk boatman (melkvaarder). She was named after her mother's sister Ybeltje Postma, who had married in Ooststellingwerf in 1914. Her elder brothers and sisters were Johannes, Aukje, Jacob, Theodora and Wiemer, and her younger sister Jantje was born about a year after her.\n\nShe went into service, and in 1943 she was a domestic servant at Haskerdijken, where her parents were then also living. On 16 September 1943, during the German occupation, she married Sierd Eize Eyzenga, a 20-year-old farmhand from Akkrum, at Haskerland.","src":[["Birth, Terband, 4 Jun 1922","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ebc5fc9-98ea-b660-8a1c-30d78c525d56"],["Marriage, Haskerland, 16 Sep 1943","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2e9f115e-dab5-2ef0-b3dc-b0ba9dccdf40"],["Marriage of her aunt Ybeltje Postma, 1914","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:44ae8c9e-8e6e-5d4b-17d3-239559272c08"]]},
jantjeelz:{"n":"Jantje Elzinga","d":"b. c. 1923 · Terband","b":1923,"s":"h","p":["janelz","aaltjep"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The youngest known child of Jan Elzinga and Aaltje Postma, born at Terband, near Heerenveen, about 1923, when her father was farming cattle and collecting milk by boat. She bore the name of her father's grandmother, Jantje Eelkes Roukema. Her eldest brother Johannes was some seventeen years older than she was, and her sister Aukje fourteen.\n\nShe married Sies de Boer, a 24-year-old farmhand born at Oudeschoot, at Heerenveen on 9 May 1946, a year after the liberation of the Netherlands. Her parents, aged 69 and 63, were still farming at Terband and were named in the record. Four years later her sister Aukje left with her family for Australia.","src":[["Marriage, Heerenveen, 9 May 1946","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9db3270f-5ffd-9c24-acfd-00daca162d5f"]]},
edithh:{"n":"Edith Alice Hodgetts","d":"1901–1982 · Queensland","b":1901,"x":1982,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"The eldest of the thirteen children of Joseph Hodgetts and Rosetta Bastock, born on 19 September 1901, eleven months after her mother stepped off the ship from England and married. She married Alfred Richard Bedford on 22 August 1919, aged 17, when her three youngest sisters were either babies or not yet born, and was Mrs A. Bedford of Bundaberg when her father died in 1948. Alfred died on 17 August 1950. She married again, and it was as Edith Alice Jeude that she died on 8 September 1982, aged 80. Her second marriage has not been found in the public marriage index.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1901/C/4684","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/fc8af4c6a39bfab7a7b14278446efce1dec18486a61e1d68e8a4ad3022736228"],["Marriage, Qld 1919/C/1735","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/5be2d9d779493b072708abb80a3e23cc7efe55e4d34d06a2254f49b7abb7b1e5"],["Death, Qld 1982/56429","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f7ae8b742b9ce4e10d89694ba6d4e0eb75edaac4f498f3f97819377cac1c9644"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"],["Death of Alfred Richard Bedford, Qld 1950/C/2661","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/900a6fbe0e4dec5561c0010f018636d2966ddd0733f1b94bb4b1b9ee493457b1"]]},
josephbh:{"n":"Joseph Bernard Hodgetts","d":"1903–1983 · Queensland","b":1903,"x":1983,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"The eldest son, born on 14 May 1903 and known as Bernard; he carried the name Joseph into a third generation, after his father and his Aston grandfather. He married Hilda Matilda Frances Gorlick on 14 July 1926 and lived at Bundaberg, where he was named first among the seven sons in his father's obituary in 1948. Their son Brian Keith married in December 1951 and died in 1988. Bernard died on 22 May 1983, aged 80, and Hilda on 1 August 1989. When his brother Everard died in 1994 the death index gave their father's name as Joseph Bernard, mixing up the two generations.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1903/C/4072","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/fb6ba6e4b62f3102cda0f1456094a8ea1cde224b463c2d677362b771253ad2f4"],["Marriage, Qld 1926/C/2037","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b1521b2c9370a82ba061db430c8febc92ab7ca126b0c3b8e1b90cd406fc57f1c"],["Death, Qld 1983/53351","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/530dcb76f72e31e4ac356d4eca81143340223b8aa4d06207bd144f3035580bd5"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
dorothyh:{"n":"Dorothy Ada Hodgetts","d":"1904–1985 · Queensland","b":1904,"x":1985,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 23 October 1904, the third child and second daughter of Joseph Hodgetts and Rosetta Bastock. She married Robert Clive Rasmussen, the son of Hans Jorgen Rasmussen and Nellie Maud Scarfe, on 25 August 1928, and in 1948 lived at Tegege, north of Bundaberg. In February 1958 it was Mrs R. Rasmussen who made the cake for her mother's 80th birthday party at the Coronation Hall, a gathering of a family that by then counted 44 grandchildren. Robert died on 18 May 1978. Dorothy died on 8 February 1985, aged 80.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1904/C/4794","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/71b383964f51c502485ceb48e841f9c737fcae90559108120764cf8cb7d41128"],["Marriage, Qld 1928/C/2055","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f3ddcd610e0282959cc0f1af6d9b98116d0748842cbd175b4968c3bd958736dc"],["Death, Qld 1985/51148","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/4c7062c92e7d9f6ac69bbf968b48dcbb066a877c10aade426d3e4994274dfe81"],["Mother's 80th birthday, Bundaberg News-Mail, 15 Feb 1958","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article284352215"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"],["Death of Robert Clive Rasmussen, Qld 1978/C/1850","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/09d4e0c26b9c0592998c5a477ad07a6294c62b4670756702d9e287f62b2d7bef"]]},
christopherh:{"n":"Christopher Harry Hodgetts","d":"1908–1989 · Queensland","b":1908,"x":1989,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 30 March 1908, the third son and fifth of the thirteen children, and known as Chris. His birth came two years after the family was recorded at Rosedale and five years before they are found in Bundaberg. He married Violet Cantrell on 12 October 1935. Unlike most of his brothers and sisters he left the district: he was living in Brisbane when his father died in 1948, as was his younger brother George. He died on 23 July 1989, aged 81.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1908/C/615","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e5c20c487b9c4b7615da1b210ef0fd5724c2d92db9fef2debaeb2c89a0b1081d"],["Marriage, Qld 1935/C/3768","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8673b6e9874bf85d27058952250f696334afb578b7557826b06d96489ad1284a"],["Death, Qld 1989/55606","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2c0c6e53eb31e0a274c25549ea0a9005e689e822cb1b0b8cc4b9f12b5a1f59d1"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
joyceh:{"n":"Joyce Rosetta Hodgetts","d":"1911–1962 · Queensland","b":1911,"x":1962,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 3 July 1911, the seventh of the thirteen children, and given her mother's name as a middle name; the birth index spells the surname Hodgettes. She married Cyril Stanley Svensson on 19 January 1929, aged 17, and lived at Bundaberg, where she was Mrs C. Svenson in her father's obituary in 1948. She died on 22 July 1962, aged 51, eight years before her mother, and is the first of the thirteen known to have died. Cyril, born on 11 January 1911, outlived her by thirty-four years and died on 18 July 1996.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1911/C/820","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f45c75f57ff7afbd5f929cc8b7c1ecd7a7f00334cd5b0ebc68f648bd16df5652"],["Marriage, Qld 1929/C/59","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a3fbfdcc81e8f7778a07064a634b9a9c44f46bb493e0c046e8ef2d31b8ad62a3"],["Death, Qld 1962/C/3091","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/57c11c184bd05241ef07b2626c5274ce9ce4c49c0486e50fac38467f4b64754d"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"],["Death of Cyril Stanley Svensson, Qld 1996/56204","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/3ef4b7d8295c04925ae7bc6a9069fd3e53cc8629f49df321c94cf5b6d4870e17"]]},
johnch:{"n":"John Charles Hodgetts","d":"1913–1974 · Bundaberg","b":1913,"x":1974,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Bundaberg on 12 April 1913, the eighth child, and known as Jack. He married Betty Pyke on 2 October 1937. He enlisted in the Royal Australian Air Force in Brisbane on 16 December 1942 (service number 79959) and was discharged on 8 February 1946 as a corporal with 15 Aircraft Repair Depot, an air force unit that overhauled and repaired aircraft. He was back in Bundaberg by 1948, when his father died. He died on 28 July 1974, aged 61.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1913/C/778","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b16d6f2fc6eb82e8261f099ae705e1006778b4efca7c1dfa134047240c60b383"],["Marriage, Qld 1937/C/3684","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/48ee5c7e3fabec0c9e7f3807658da55fda2112a3fd57328df0cacfa9555d8066"],["WWII nominal roll, RAAF 79959","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=925297&c=WW2"],["Death, Qld 1974/C/4033","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8a8ddf6c417bc935163414d444c528abdefe55e9a8adb1db57ec02af9fae092d"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
royh:{"n":"Roy Edwards Hodgetts","d":"b. 1915 · Queensland","b":1915,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 5 May 1915, the ninth of the thirteen children; his middle name is spelled Edwards in the birth register. He married Helen Josephine Fanning on 29 January 1938, and in 1948 lived at Bucca, in the farming country north-west of Bundaberg. He is not on the Second World War nominal roll. Helen died on 24 July 1983. A Roy Edward Hodgetts, son of Joseph and Rosetta Maude, died on 11 February 1996; the index gives the mother's surname differently, so the match is likely but not certain.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1915/C/4251","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/1d6530d6a95b06894038dce82163acf3aaade4af689cb40a6430ecb6a85796e5"],["Marriage, Qld 1938/C/86","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/53cc526392161ef5b507a3d86e3d496a5034824f835ef541b639061c9143108a"],["Death, Qld 1996/51540","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/862a5170a7667e4f4a830fc985ef441ba5b5314a6e4d7ad456d3254f1a50d18c"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
robertgh:{"n":"Robert George Hodgetts","d":"1917–1978 · Bundaberg","b":1917,"x":1978,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Bundaberg on 22 April 1917, the tenth child, and called George in his father's obituary. He enlisted in the Army at Maryborough on 20 May 1941 (QX21236), giving his home as South Bundaberg, and served as a driver with the 1st Australian Corps Troops Supply Column until his discharge on 23 October 1945. He married Ruby Florence MacNeil on 21 December 1944, and Ruby is the next of kin on his service record. He was living in Brisbane in 1948 and died on 13 April 1978, aged 60.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1917/C/4082","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2d4e92d3e67791413d6a96d17a28858b32257aec243a3c51a479ed7a1c7191f1"],["WWII nominal roll, QX21236","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=20426&c=WW2"],["Marriage, Qld 1944/B/63540","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/d70b8649a6d401fad9008b6cd1b2948f4de726f83065e37c86640cd340d58614"],["Death, Qld 1978/B/86024","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6d56c2666570cbbead6ee2bec31960988ce1c149a185caa61a60f51d8691d2b7"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
gwendolineh:{"n":"Gwendoline Mary Hodgetts","d":"1919–1979 · Queensland","b":1919,"x":1979,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 28 June 1919, the eleventh child, two months before her eldest sister Edith's wedding. She married Alan Frederick Courtice on 9 May 1936, aged 16, and in 1948 was Mrs Alan Courtice of South Kalkie, on the eastern edge of Bundaberg, where her brother Les also lived. She later married again: when she died on 30 November 1979, aged 60, her surname was Douglas. Neither that second marriage nor Alan Courtice's death was found in the index.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1919/C/8055","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/42d8d3a31ce7330f9988382cbe6737d7035ae6e0e1dcbc775fc029d0a3638938"],["Marriage, Qld 1936/C/1317","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e4abcbdac5094fb1f7fc1d627fb5285e3ea65c67c4231da7c73fd8bb23238537"],["Death, Qld 1979/C/5922","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/2eb6d12ae3de48024ee875a75c78a29b72e39496e957911012efb83df8103229"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
winifredh:{"n":"Winifred Esther Hodgetts","d":"b. 1921 · Queensland","b":1921,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 11 February 1921, the twelfth child and fifth daughter. She married Clarence Edwin Beikoff on 28 September 1940, aged 19, five weeks before her younger sister Ruth's wedding. She was Mrs C. Beikoff in her father's obituary in 1948, when she lived at Maryborough, the river port south of Bundaberg. Clarence, born in 1912, the son of Herman Beikoff and Anna Marie Shelback, died on 28 April 1981. Her own death has not been found in the Queensland index.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1921/C/246","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b890cbb734d16ac245d63db276136e1bd936b157813751c276f2460d8e82ad77"],["Marriage, Qld 1940/C/4485","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6fda1b4ef76f955c4155b927a899dffa22033f2f72dc0b4e0ef520f96defbdcc"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"],["Death of Clarence Edwin Beikoff, Qld 1981/52694","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6117e827721bf25c944c980ec78c784d9e553ef1d1e2fb2d4f7fc30a251e5e5e"]]},
ruthh:{"n":"Ruth Isabel Hodgetts","d":"b. 1923 · Queensland","b":1923,"s":"h","p":["josephh","rosetta"],"r":["au"],"t":"The youngest of the thirteen, born on 6 January 1923, when her eldest sister was already married and her mother was nearly 45. She married Keith Henry William Kitching on 2 November 1940, aged 17, five weeks after her sister Winifred's wedding, and was Mrs K. Kitching of Bundaberg when her father died in 1948. Six of the thirteen married between 1936 and 1940, and Ruth was the last of them. Her death has not been found in the Queensland index.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1923/C/218","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6eebb49b2139be4843c3fdce63f2682909bdfac5d1bbde12022bb0120957d141"],["Marriage, Qld 1940/C/4499","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/5c7bcca4f1f843a3ceea1acae66c3a7660851010dfe5d20b9c5fee846bcc895d"],["Father's obituary, Bundaberg News-Mail, 28 Dec 1948","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283903767"]]},
ednah:{"n":"Edna May Haken","d":"1911–1956 · Queensland","b":1911,"x":1956,"s":"h","p":["gordonh","marymay"],"r":["au"],"t":"The second of Gordon and Mary May Haken's four daughters, born at Childers on 15 July 1911, sixteen months after Amy. She came to Bundaberg with her parents about 1919 and went to the South State School. She married John Philip Jennings on 28 October 1933 at a Methodist church in South Bundaberg, and they had three children. She died at St Vincent's Private Hospital, West Bundaberg, on 15 June 1956, aged 44, when the family lived in East Bundaberg; the News-Mail described her parents as well-known residents of the town. Her mother and her three sisters survived her. John died on 17 May 1978.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1911/C/8724","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/3eac50ecd0873b193db19b2152995655a59184df07b94483e1ae04a49c785b76"],["Marriage, Qld 1933/C/2994","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/169e3cadfc5482af9454bac425bbc836a603e407e3cb85a96f32feb1a286cea2"],["Death, Qld 1956/C/1414","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/fc36181b00722f1f5bd03b13469a9020d44c8d7169a5a7dda606ac0910f685c0"],["Obituary, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"],["Death of John Philip Jennings, Qld 1978/C/1849","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/c93d64914b834a034182366d7fb66aa09151586beb3ae42386d5cd1cff998c08"]]},
esmeh:{"n":"Esme Maud Haken","d":"1913–1994 · Queensland","b":1913,"x":1994,"s":"h","p":["gordonh","marymay"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 9 August 1913, the third of the four Haken sisters; the registers spell her name both Esme and Esma, and her wedding report calls her Esme Marie. Her family moved from Childers to Bundaberg about 1919, when she was six. She married Walter Frederick Roberts at a Bundaberg Methodist church on New Year's Eve 1935. She was Mrs Roberts of Bundaberg when her sister Edna died in 1956. Walter, born in 1909, died on 24 March 1991, and Esme died on 18 January 1994, aged 80, five months after her sister Doris.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1913/C/9947","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/036fb5a273cf4cdf05d3efac72f70b362170950cb3a57137f8276931b4ccfd54"],["Marriage, Qld 1936/C/39","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/248a40242e160207880f7e2da092537d0ba7cc12e23e3d5f9825a74990abebaa"],["Death, Qld 1994/50873","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/61d2e50d6c47f58e1a603e8cc13d5a6c83c9ef3539d32fc2fb3df218393d8d41"],["Obituary of her sister, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"],["Death of Walter Frederick Roberts, Qld 1991/52353","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f34164651d89e3648ee4f0cccc24a0ca43aa310c25712b5d762318fd85bc736d"]]},
dorish:{"n":"Doris Marion Haken","d":"1915–1993 · Queensland","b":1915,"x":1993,"s":"h","p":["gordonh","marymay"],"r":["au"],"t":"The youngest daughter, born on 20 November 1915; the birth index misspells her mother's maiden name as Fudman. She was about four when the family moved from Childers to Bundaberg. She married John William Hansen on 7 September 1935, aged 19, a few months before her sister Esme's wedding. She was the only one of the four sisters to leave the district: in 1956 she was Mrs Hansen of Rocklea, in Brisbane. She died on 23 August 1993, aged 77.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1915/C/14663","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/c8d45c497076a464e4da47384d664225e5b7b4521b82499a53d087fd850e66bd"],["Marriage, Qld 1935/C/2601","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/4d64eed1f1772742fa8b5903cad0d713a2950c1a3d903c91101db59bbe6d09c7"],["Death, Qld 1993/56347","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e7e2bd602d59a67c6561c9c971dd84c510bd8ad4ec9ce02560337eabb6e4f2a9"],["Obituary of her sister, Mrs J. P. Jennings, Bundaberg News-Mail, 22 Jun 1956","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article283636774"]]},
gladysn:{"n":"Gladys Elizabeth Hosking Napier","d":"1890–1976 · Queensland","b":1890,"x":1976,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"The eldest child of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey, born on 2 May 1890. Her names came from her mother's side: Elizabeth from her grandmother Ellen Elizabeth, and Hosking from her grandmother's husband, Arthur James Hosking of Townsville. She was the big sister of a family of eight, twenty years older than the youngest, Tavie.\n\nShe married John Alfred Barrie on 15 March 1915 and settled in Brisbane, where she was the Mrs Barrie named in her father's death notice in 1935, her mother's in 1953 and her sister Tavie's obituary in 1963. She died on 10 April 1976, aged 85, forty-one years to the day after her father.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1890/C/12141","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/05dda93cc0b3445d06af7adb075259ed47d73d342dd2a151a07b0a42c22b0667"],["Marriage, Qld 1915/C/926","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/f9653839b585404352fb97839755ff76843f270d1ac2ec3b00f01057157dc7a5"],["Death, Qld 1976/C/1881","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/6c47371075c161d220757bfd8d101d48859bfdb8ac49dfa9e66c477df9f2550a"],["Obituary of her sister Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
williamsn:{"n":"William Sneddon Napier","d":"1899–1971 · Queensland","b":1899,"x":1971,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 13 April 1899, the second son of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey and the eldest to grow up, and given the maiden name of his father's mother, Janet Sneddon. Known as Bill. He spent his childhood in Townsville, where his parents lived for many years.\n\nHe married Annie Dawkins Strelton on 19 December 1922. He and his brother Bob were both at Toowoomba when their father died in 1935; by 1953, when his mother died, he was at Oxley in Brisbane, and he was still in Brisbane when his sister Tavie died in 1963. He died on 11 December 1971, aged 72.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1899/C/10735","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/76038489bd655d579be932289169dc787f50b6e90ff2760d4e670d64d65622d8"],["Marriage, Qld 1922/C/3574","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/09d422166ddc181d0575a9d4cd966a8071ba6e586496b8b27e0486f89285ca44"],["Death, Qld 1971/B/28898","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b08c6557f5e0ad50abaf2eac9bc322021fe3b9effee6a09b8f0c8e709519dbe1"],["Obituary of his sister Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
constancen:{"n":"Constance Napier","d":"1904–1954 · Queensland","b":1904,"x":1954,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 11 July 1904, the sixth child of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey. In December 1927 she and her younger sister Tavie travelled south from Charters Towers to stay with their married sister Saddie Hymus at Cooroy.\n\nShe was still at home in Charters Towers when her father died in 1935. On 23 January 1940, aged 35, she married Albert Thomas Overs; the marriage index spells his surname Orero. She was the Mrs Overs of Charters Towers named in her mother's death notice in August 1953. She died on 20 April 1954, aged 49, eight months after her mother.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1904/C/11665","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/9d616ca259aa08746eb9249a5f0a3c16601d6fc812a683139d55d115b8f991dc"],["Marriage, Qld 1940/C/720","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e6884028aacc3c04f9ebc98a7b64c547196bd41b267695ec5b4d34d7c7c35b20"],["Death, Qld 1954/C/1899","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/9be9de124ba9d1a926b47f3a29de5a1207ac4c8f3f987ee67a016dc86a6c678c"],["Visit to Cooroy, Brisbane Courier, 16 Dec 1927","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article21204999"]]},
johnn:{"n":"John Napier","d":"1907–1986 · Townsville","b":1907,"x":1986,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born at Townsville on 9 February 1907, the youngest son of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey, and known as Jack. The family later moved inland to Charters Towers, and he was living there when his father died in 1935.\n\nHe married Clara Andrews on 25 May 1932. He enlisted in the Army at Townsville on 12 June 1941 (Q111053) and was discharged on 15 November 1945 as a corporal with the 30th Australian Salvage Section, a unit that recovered and sorted used equipment. He was living in Townsville when his mother died in 1953 and when his sister Tavie died in 1963. He died on 26 October 1986, aged 79.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1907/C/11248","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/b0637eac4929f0feef20673aee930521dd446af9b85d247f0c2b8045119ef482"],["Marriage, Qld 1932/C/1044","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7c0b2a61a5ff53e5f68f5a73d2d032c80cfd16e42be7337078e34ece85a5492e"],["WWII nominal roll, Q111053","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=76711&c=WW2"],["Death, Qld 1986/8571","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/8abcbac6dbd762b815239c4d33cf764e161726999437db855ac6b18b3e163892"],["Obituary of his sister Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
roberthn:{"n":"Robert Harvey Napier","d":"1894–1894 · Queensland","b":1894,"x":1894,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"The first son of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey, born on 31 January 1894 and given his father's first name and his mother's maiden name. He had one sister, Gladys, who was three.\n\nHe died on 5 November 1894, nine months old. In the 1890s about one Queensland baby in ten died before its first birthday. His sister Saddie was born nineteen months later, and in 1901 his parents gave the name Robert again to another son, Robert Barton.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1894/C/10867","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/563cc24133e1b316ae9267baf44c9e514996d8974fcce2faa1063e6c61ca998e"],["Death, Qld 1894/C/4187","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/41ca3eb5cd66ee5cdccbc7a587435864b95b6da2813967cd63d29d96195aa2a5"]]},
robertbn:{"n":"Robert Barton Napier","d":"b. 1901 · Queensland","b":1901,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"Born on 6 November 1901 and named Robert like his father, his grandfather and the baby brother who had died in 1894. Known as Bob. The family were living in Townsville when he was small, and the South Townsville State School prize list for December 1908 puts a Robert Napier first in the Upper First Class, the right age for him.\n\nHe married Edith Elizabeth Gelling on 31 August 1927. He and his brother Bill were at Toowoomba when their father died in 1935, and he was living in Sydney when his mother died in 1953. He is not among the brothers named as surviving in his sister Tavie's obituary of November 1963, so he seems to have died between 1953 and 1963. His death is not in the Queensland index.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1901/C/11717","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/192c438bdebf65c8f848f2f761b7cf9a71d0ea3848a21d80ff632e4a3f4fc713"],["Marriage, Qld 1927/C/2087","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/75c5a7ba9a554a2b70ca6c80f6560e1c6d3e1ae2b65bb9d6c0648cd7771b4648"],["South Townsville State School prize list, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 21 Dec 1908","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article58113594"],["Obituary of his sister Mrs Hudson, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
ethelon:{"n":"Ethel Octavia Napier","d":"1910–1963 · Queensland","b":1910,"x":1963,"s":"y","p":["robertn","ethel"],"r":["au"],"t":"The youngest child of Robert Napier and Ethel Harvey, born on 20 November 1910 and known as Tavie. Octavia means eighth, and she was the eighth child. After a good pass in the Senior university examination she trained as a nurse and worked at the Cloncurry and Townsville hospitals.\n\nShe married Keith Armstrong MacLeod, second son of Mr and Mrs W. F. MacLeod of Townsville, on 8 June 1935, two months after her father died, and they had a daughter and a son. Keith was killed in action at Tobruk. On 20 November 1941, her 31st birthday, she married Alfred George Hudson, who came from a western Queensland family; they lived at Cloncurry for nine years and then at Charters Towers, and had two sons and two daughters.\n\nShe died at Charters Towers on 12 November 1963 after a long illness, aged 52. Her obituary named her sisters Mrs F. E. Hymus of Wynnum and Mrs J. Barrie of Brisbane and her brothers William of Brisbane and John of Townsville.","src":[["Birth, Qld 1911/C/12822","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/aac62ae3d35b34de84750536715f778c4917504f6539e82b6572dddbca1245e8"],["Marriage, Qld 1935/C/1848","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/14fc006e2c9078c356d81d00196bb61c9d2bb414229b1d2d0ad240cc7b1409cb"],["Marriage, Qld 1941/C/4313","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/058b81eb4f777fa565d2788e1f756d5b5d9e75ce977d1a4bff6ff800defd40d1"],["Death, Qld 1963/C/5560","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/600256261760e5bb429afff2ad3dfa1218e42b9894588787e7f23b20b94076ce"],["Obituary, Townsville Daily Bulletin, 27 Nov 1963","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article296789017"]]},
tedfisk:{"n":"Edward Anthony Fisk","d":"b. 1909 · Kalgoorlie","b":1909,"s":"f","p":["edwardf","matilda"],"r":["au"],"t":"Mona's only brother, known as Ted, born at Kalgoorlie on 4 September 1909; his middle name was his mother's maiden name. As a young man he went into mining ventures with his father: both were subscribers to a prospecting company in 1931 and directors of a mining company registered in Perth in June 1935. His mother's death notice that August named him and Mona as her children.\n\nHe enlisted in the AIF at Paddington, Sydney, on 14 June 1940 (NX25982), when he was living at Bondi, and named his sister Mona Frankish as next of kin. He served with the 2/2nd Machine Gun Battalion, wrote home from the Middle East in 1941, rose from private to captain, and was discharged on 28 November 1945. In June 1945 the Cairns Post announced his engagement to Eileen, only daughter of Mr and Mrs T. J. Boland of Cairns.\n\nHe married Eileen Boland after the war. In December 1947 the Cairns Post noted that Mr and Mrs E. Fisk of Sydney were spending a holiday in Cairns with her parents, Mr and Mrs T. J. Boland.","src":[["WWII nominal roll, NX25982","https://nominal-rolls.dva.gov.au/veteran?id=144525&c=WW2"],["Mother's death notice, West Australian, 26 Aug 1935","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article32904449"],["Mining company directors, Kalgoorlie Miner, 24 Jun 1935","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article94898833"],["New companies, Daily Commercial News, 10 Jul 1931","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article272043611"],["Engagement, Cairns Post, 23 Jun 1945","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article42452113"],["Town and Country, Cairns Post, 27 Dec 1947","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article42562443"]]},
olivep:{"n":"Olive Amelia Martha Parkinson","d":"1900–1995 · Thornton","b":1900,"s":"p","p":["wgp","martha"],"r":["au"],"t":"The eldest of William George and Martha Parkinson's five children, born at Thornton and registered at Alexandra in 1900 as Olive Amelia Martha; she was known as Ollie. She was 17 when her mother moved the family to Hoddles Creek in May 1917.\n\nIn 1919 she married Walter Henry Wood, the \"Harry\" of the family notices. Their first child was registered at Lilydale in 1920, and the birthplaces of the next four trace the family's moves: Murrumbeena in 1922 and 1924, Highett in 1925 and Mildura in 1926. When Martha died in November 1949 one death notice was placed by \"Ollie and Harry\", and it named ten grandchildren from their family.\n\nHer father died at Highett in 1960. Olive outlived her sister Ivy and her brothers Ben and Don, and died in 1995, aged 95.\n\nHer death registration, made at Parkdale in 1995, names her parents as William George and Martha Parkinson, her mother's maiden name as Wilson, and her husband as Walter Henry Wood.","src":[["Mother's death notice, The Age, 7 Nov 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article189479189"],["Death notice from Ollie and Harry, Sun News-Pictorial, 8 Nov 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article279518976"],["Victorian BDM index: birth 15682/1900 (Alexandra); marriage 8599/1919 (Walter Henry Wood); births of Wood children 1920-1926 (mother Olive Amelia Martha Parkinson); death 17642/1995 (aged 95, born Thornton)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]],"x":1995},
dorisp:{"n":"Doris Maggie Rose Parkinson","d":"1902–1988 · Alexandra","b":1902,"s":"p","p":["wgp","martha"],"r":["au"],"t":"The second daughter of William George Parkinson and Martha Wilson, registered at Alexandra in 1902 as Doris Maggie Rose; her death entry gives Alexandra as her birthplace. Her early childhood was spent at Thornton, on the Goulburn River, where her brothers Ben and Don were born. She was thirteen when her father enlisted in July 1915 and fifteen when her mother moved the family to Hoddles Creek in May 1917.\n\nIn 1919, at about seventeen, she married Henry Berry; the index enters the couple as Doris Parkinson and Hy Berry. No children of that marriage appear in the Victorian birth index. In 1940, as Doris Maggie Rose Berry, she married Hayden Clark Down, who was born at Chelsea, the son of Arthur Clark Down and Florence Margaret Baker. The index does not show how her first marriage ended.\n\nShe was named with her sisters and brothers, 'Olive, Doris, Ivy, Ben and Don', in her mother's death notice in November 1949. Hayden died at Healesville in 1976, aged 62. Doris died at Healesville in 1988, aged 85; her death registration names her parents as William Parkinson and Martha Wilson, which confirms both marriages as hers.","src":[["Mother's death notice, The Age, 7 Nov 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article189479189"],["Victorian BDM index: birth 15022/1902 (Alexandra); marriage 4642/1919 (Doris Parkinson and Hy Berry); marriage 771/1940 (Doris Maggie Rose Berry and Hayden Clarke Down); death 10090/1988 (Doris Maggie Rose Down, aged 85, parents William Parkinson and Martha Wilson)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]],"x":1988},
ivyp:{"n":"Ivy Myrtle Parkinson","d":"1905–1967 · Thornton","b":1905,"s":"p","p":["wgp","martha"],"r":["au"],"t":"The third daughter of William George Parkinson and Martha Wilson, born at Thornton and registered at Alexandra in 1905 as Ivy Myrtle, three years before her brother Ben. She was eleven when the family moved to Hoddles Creek in May 1917.\n\nIn 1923, at seventeen or eighteen, she married Percival Francis Ockwell, who was born at Healesville. Their first child was registered at Lilydale that year and a second at Yarra Junction in 1925, in the Upper Yarra district where her mother and brothers lived. She was living when her mother died at the Yarra Junction Bush Nursing Hospital in November 1949.\n\nIvy died at Box Hill in 1967, aged 61. Her death entry gives her father's name as William Benjamin, her brother's names, rather than William George. Percival died at Lilydale in 1975.","src":[["Mother's death notice, The Age, 7 Nov 1949","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article189479189"],["Victorian BDM index: birth 22828/1905 (Alexandra); marriage 4155/1923 (Percival Francis Ockwell); births of Ockwell children 32513/1923 and 9011/1925; death 17154/1967 (Box Hill, aged 61, born Thornton); death of Percival Francis Ockwell 5847/1975","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]],"x":1967},
catharinabk:{"n":"Catharina Elisabeth Brückmann","d":"c. 1852–1920 · Oberkaufungen","b":1852,"x":1920,"s":"b","p":["jakobb","marie"],"r":["de","nl"],"t":"Born at Oberkaufungen near Kassel about 1852, a daughter of Jacob Brückmann and Maria Dietrich and so a much younger sister of the seaman Georg Bruckmann. She too settled in Holland, following her brothers Georg and Heinrich to Amsterdam as a girl of about sixteen. The city's register of foreigners has her on 11 May 1868 and again in 1869, 1870 and 1871. The clerks struggled with the name of her village ('Oberkaüfingen', 'Oberkausingen') and wrote 'Prussia' after it.\n\nOn 25 September 1873, aged 22 and working as a shop girl (winkeldochter), she married Johannes Stephanus Kieft, 30, an Amsterdam confectioner (dessertwerker, later suikerwerker) whose mother kept a shop. By 1904 he was a shop manager.\n\nAt least eight children were born in Amsterdam between about 1875 and 1895: Henriëtte Maria, Jan Jacob, Anna Maria, Jansje, Wilhelmina, Elisabeth, Friedrich Albert and Heinrich. Several of them later lived at Laren in the Gooi.\n\nShe died at Blaricum, the next village, on 21 September 1920, aged 68, the wife of Johannes Stephanus Kieft. Her niece in Amsterdam carried the same name.","src":[["Death, Blaricum 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:2FE10B7E-7DA4-4785-B041-8B55B317BD16"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 25 September 1873","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:FFB69EB2-40F2-4263-9B66-F6BB955E762A"],["Amsterdam foreigners register, 11 May 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:9f6e4379-2137-45e2-ac6e-6f1e98ff8aad"],["Amsterdam foreigners register, 12 September 1871","https://www.openarchieven.nl/saa:1eb40c9f-5709-4635-8939-326b87db03b9"],["Marriage of daughter Henriëtte Maria, 1899","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:76A0F431-30E1-428F-ACC3-0FB358A927A6"],["Marriage of daughter Wilhelmina, Elst, 1904","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gld:AF52221A-377E-4E8D-9DDB-3D29F54D4B39"],["Marriage of son Friedrich Albert, Laren, 1920","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:76260D89-2838-4F47-B8AE-31C326B6BD9A"],["Death of son Heinrich, Enschede, 1961","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:44ABDE42-704A-4F74-AA9F-D4A76C6FCB56"],["Marriage of brother Heinrich Brückmann, 1865","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:EFEB6D0D-C1A6-438B-A5EF-4B014CD77AC4"]]},
wilhelminajvdb:{"n":"Wilhelmina Josephina van den Berg","d":"b. 1902 · Meppel","b":1902,"x":null,"s":"b","p":["josephus","mariadk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Meppel on 27 August 1902, fourteen months after her sister Maria. Her father, a shoemaker of 31, registered the birth. She was the seventh child and the last of three daughters born at Meppel. She is listed with her parents in the Rotterdam population register, and in the Zaandam register after the family moved there by 1905.\n\nOn 13 June 1929, aged 26, she married Johannes Petrus van Oijen in Amsterdam. He was also 26, an office clerk (kantoorbediende) born at Vlaardingen, the son of a cooper (kuiper), Cornelis van Oijen, and Maria Thoemen. Her sister Maria married in Amsterdam twelve weeks later.","src":[["Rotterdam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/srt:1214B9CE-CD12-4266-9EE0-71B7986BA9A5"],["Birth record, Meppel, 1902","https://www.openarchieven.nl/dar:5ff80400-c884-401e-b1a7-1f151d7c05e9"],["Marriage, Amsterdam, 13 Jun 1929","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:CFA15BCE-DE55-4433-A495-093485FCB315"],["Zaandam population register (family)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gaz:D524FFD1-DCA9-4221-A6D3-D2D76858E6DE"]]},
gepkejans:{"n":"Gepke Jans","d":"Wymeer, East Frisia","s":"b","p":[],"r":["de"],"t":"Wife of the day labourer Joost Hulzebos of Wymeer, in the Rheiderland of East Frisia, and mother of Jan Joosten Hulzebos, who was born there about 1776. She is named once, in her son's death record at Oudeschans in 1848. Jan Joosten became a tailor, married in the Dutch fortress village in 1800 and had at least eight children there. The first, born in October 1801, was named Gepke after her, and so was a daughter born in 1805; three sons grew up and followed his trade.","src":[["Death record of Jan Joostens Hulzebos, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:17cbb6b4-aaa6-e65e-48da-1b9fb6afbbb7"],["Baptism of Gepke, Oudeschans 1801","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e75e9da3-325f-6885-a00c-bb4ff08aa128"]]},
remmertn:{"n":"Remmert Tjarks Niemeijer","d":"farmer, Scheemda","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"A farmer (landbouwer) at Scheemda, in the Oldambt of east Groningen. The Oldambt clay was some of the richest grain land in the country, and a farmer there stood well above the labourers who worked for him, which fits his daughter's marriage to a Sappemeer innkeeper and merchant.\n\nHe married Bouwke Alberts at Scheemda on 30 December 1736. Four children were baptised there: Tjark on 5 December 1738, Albertje on 11 March 1742, Foctina on 31 January 1745 and Geesijn on 22 October 1752. His second name shows that his own father was called Tjark, and he gave the name to his eldest son. Albertje married at Sappemeer in 1771, and Geesijn married Hittje Egges Pijl and lived at Scheemda until 1836.\n\nHe is named as a farmer in Albertje's death record at Groningen in 1823, where he is given the surname Niemeijer. It means 'the new farmer', the man on a newly settled holding. A Remmert Tjarks also married at Beerta in 1721; whether that was an earlier marriage of the same man is not known.","src":[["Death record of Albertje Niemeijer, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6a3ec028-23af-e663-42d6-6bf6b82696e1"],["Marriage, Scheemda 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0f7fbce5-c579-2ac4-e9b3-a50ac076d647"],["Baptism of Tjark, 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:56ee08c6-b4d6-f591-bcf4-5b1b279465b9"],["Baptism of Albertje, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c93af34d-f83e-bc9a-aa17-1f88d348d93c"],["Baptism of Foctina, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:72f9da73-a562-4e24-036d-04c5379317ed"],["Baptism of Geesijn, 1752","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:12b8a105-08e6-8b45-3d1c-b6021eab4e43"],["Death of Geesijn Remmers Niemeijer, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2a5d2739-b402-5684-560f-938ea863f73c"],["Marriage of a Remmert Tjarks, Beerta 1721","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0596521e-ae73-37a3-2dd5-99167733eb3a"]]},
bouchien:{"n":"Bouchien Alberts","d":"Scheemda","s":"b","p":[],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of the Scheemda farmer Remmert Tjarks Niemeijer, whom she married in the church there on 30 December 1736. The registers write her name Bouwke, Bouke and Bouchijn. She had four children baptised at Scheemda: Tjark (1738), Albertje (1742), Foctina (1745) and Geesijn (1752).\n\nA 'Bouke Alberts' stood on the bride's side when Albertje signed her marriage contract in January 1771. Her name was handed down: Albertje called her first child Bouwina. She is named as the mother in the death records of two daughters, Albertje at Groningen in 1823 and Geesijn at Scheemda in 1836.","src":[["Death record of Albertje Niemeijer, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6a3ec028-23af-e663-42d6-6bf6b82696e1"],["Marriage contract, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:411cb5c4-d2cb-79c4-5600-acc515b873e3"],["Marriage, Scheemda 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0f7fbce5-c579-2ac4-e9b3-a50ac076d647"],["Baptism of Albertje, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c93af34d-f83e-bc9a-aa17-1f88d348d93c"],["Death of Geesijn Remmers Niemeijer, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2a5d2739-b402-5684-560f-938ea863f73c"]]},
hdwilliammitton:{"n":"William Mitton","d":"c. 1821–1869 · Lincoln","b":1821,"x":1869,"s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["uk","au"],"t":"Born at Lincoln about 1821; his Queensland death registration names his father as Samuel Mitton. He was a wireworker, a maker of wire goods such as sieves, screens and netting. He married Hannah Barnsley, who was from Sheffield, at Lincoln in the spring of 1842.\n\nThe 1861 census shows them at 7 Melville Street, Lincoln, with six children: Eliza (18), Joseph (16), apprenticed to his father's trade, Tom (12), Hannah (10), Louisa (5) and Sarah, eight months old. Within five years the family was in Brisbane. No assisted passage is recorded for them, which suggests they paid their own fares.\n\nJoseph died in Brisbane on 12 June 1866, and William died there on 2 September 1869, aged about 48. Tom and Eliza both married in Brisbane in 1873 and lived there until 1903 and 1929. Louisa, thirteen when her father died, is believed to be the Grace Redman who married George Charles Haken in 1877.","src":[["1861 census, 7 Melville Street, Lincoln (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/59038d5fe9379091b12e508e/louisa-mitton-1861-lincolnshire-lincoln-home-1856-"],["Death, Qld 1869/B/5091","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/67a6776616261a7c62cffbda3f03c578d3c9aa1d12f519dbc0194494ed4fc765"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Lincoln, Jun qtr 1842 (14 825)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of son Joseph, Qld 1866/B/3339","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/78febecca672d7935f6cba11f4839d0027c5c049c902ceae38940d14d16568cc"],["Death of son Tom, Qld 1903/B/4097","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a4bfa973b39d45d2d5bce7f7787edbfeafc91b58140bc1825990e9886f6f53cc"],["Death of daughter Eliza McRobbie, Qld 1929/B/8377","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7053cb67ab03c258601c27f5b66de9d4c5a5ff1d1e4d21b33bb0153395796051"]]},
hdhannahbarnsley:{"n":"Hannah Barnsley","d":"b. c. 1819 · Sheffield","b":1819,"s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Sheffield, Yorkshire, about 1819. She married William Mitton, a Lincoln wireworker, in the Lincoln district in the spring of 1842, and was 42 when the census was taken at their home in Melville Street, Lincoln, in 1861. Six children were at home then: Eliza, Joseph, Tom, Hannah, Louisa and the baby, Sarah.\n\nHer husband and children were in Brisbane by 1866, and the Queensland death registrations of her children Joseph (1866), Tom (1903) and Eliza McRobbie (1929) all name her as Hannah Barnsley. Her own death is not in the Queensland index. The death of a Hannah Mitton was registered at Lincoln in the summer of 1865; that could be her or her fifteen-year-old daughter of the same name, and the index does not say which. When her daughter Louisa died in 1911 the mother's name was given as Ann.","src":[["1861 census, 7 Melville Street, Lincoln (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/59038d5fe9379091b12e508e/louisa-mitton-1861-lincolnshire-lincoln-home-1856-"],["FreeBMD: marriage, Lincoln, Jun qtr 1842 (14 825)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Death of son Tom, Qld 1903/B/4097","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/a4bfa973b39d45d2d5bce7f7787edbfeafc91b58140bc1825990e9886f6f53cc"],["Death of daughter Eliza McRobbie, Qld 1929/B/8377","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/7053cb67ab03c258601c27f5b66de9d4c5a5ff1d1e4d21b33bb0153395796051"]]},
hdroberttudman:{"n":"Robert Tudman","d":"m. 1809 · Hevingham, Norfolk","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Robert Tudman married Mary Hewitt at St Botolph's church, Hevingham, Norfolk, on 10 November 1809. Their son Thomas was born on 15 February 1810 and baptised there on 26 April; the register names both parents. Hevingham is a farming parish a few miles south of Aylsham.\n\nA Mary Tudman was buried at Hevingham on 6 November 1815, and a Robert Tudman married Elizabeth Kemp at Colby, a few miles to the north, on 11 November 1817. These entries may show Robert losing his first wife and marrying again in the parish where his son Thomas later married and raised a family, but the registers indexed do not say so outright.\n\nThomas became a farm labourer at Colby and Banningham, and Thomas's son John, born in 1841, emigrated to Queensland in 1864.","src":[["Baptism of son Thomas, Hevingham, 26 Apr 1810 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68476c178655745405c55054/thomas-tudman-thomas-hewitt-baptism-norfolk-hevingham-1810-04-26"],["FreeREG index: marriage, Hevingham, 10 Nov 1809","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"]]},
hdmaryhewitt:{"n":"Mary Hewitt","d":"m. 1809 · Hevingham, Norfolk","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mary Hewitt, whose name is also written Hewett, married Robert Tudman at St Botolph's, Hevingham, Norfolk, on 10 November 1809. Their son Thomas was born three months later, on 15 February 1810, and baptised at Hevingham on 26 April. The baptism register gives her maiden name, which is how she can be traced.\n\nA Mary Tudman was buried at Hevingham on 6 November 1815. If that was her, she died when Thomas was five. A Robert Tudman married Elizabeth Kemp at nearby Colby two years later, which would fit a widower marrying again. The burial entry in the index gives no age or husband's name, so this is not certain.\n\nHer son Thomas lived to be 88, and her grandson John Tudman emigrated to Queensland in 1864.","src":[["Baptism of son Thomas, Hevingham, 26 Apr 1810 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68476c178655745405c55054/thomas-tudman-thomas-hewitt-baptism-norfolk-hevingham-1810-04-26"],["FreeREG index: marriage, Hevingham, 10 Nov 1809","https://www.freereg.org.uk/"]]},
tbantonievdberg:{"n":"Antonie van den Berg","d":"c. 1750–1826","b":1750,"x":1826,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Antonie (Antonius, Antonij) van den Berg was born about 1750. A Tilburg residents list of about 1810 gives his birthplace as 'Vervolde', a spelling that has not been matched to a known place, and records him as a Catholic and head of his household.\n\nHe married Maria Elisabeth van Loosbroek, who came from Macharen on the Maas. The family moved several times before settling: a daughter, Maria Anna, was born at Oss about 1779, a son Jacobus was baptised at Hilvarenbeek on 17 October 1781, and from 1787 the children were baptised at Tilburg. Seven are known: Maria Anna, Jacobus, Bernardus (about 1784), Joanna Henrica (1787), Johanna Maria (about 1791), Maria Theresia (about 1794) and Wilhelma (1799).\n\nIn November 1810 he stood witness when his son Bernardus married Johanna Cornelia de Vries. Three daughters married at Tilburg in 1812, 1815 and 1823. Jacobus became a wheelwright (radenmaker) at Sprang, north of Tilburg. Antonie died on 5 August 1826; the date comes from the death-duty file drawn up for his estate at Tilburg. His widow lived until 1842.","src":[["Tilburg residents list, about 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:747caed9-05ed-bf06-8cee-d9496b6eb52a"],["Death-duty file (memorie van successie), Tilburg, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:e7eadb3f-f60d-5e40-5b17-7d3d35547dd5"],["Baptism of son Jacobus, Hilvarenbeek, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:49a4b246-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna Henrica, Tilburg, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2fd9ec46-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Wilhelma, 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:32147daa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of son Bernardus, 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:533d1b0b-3756-ff31-1d36-627c0d360f17"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna Henrica, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:004aa224-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Johanna Maria, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:008b9ebe-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Wilhelma, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:59447b33-124b-364d-cefb-7a48efd3ea57"],["Death of son Jacobus, Baardwijk, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sha:8f030afa-5340-babf-369a-1e35c618e82d"],["Death of son Bernardus, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a54ca6fa-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbelisabethvloosbroek:{"n":"Maria Elisabeth van Loosbroek","d":"c. 1752–1842 · Macharen","b":1752,"x":1842,"s":"b","p":["tbjacobusvloosbroek","tbmariavgrinsven"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1752 at Macharen, a small village on the river Maas beside the little town of Megen, north of Oss. She was the daughter of Jacobus van Loosbroek and Maria van Grinsven, who had married at Oss in May 1751. Clerks struggled with her surname: it appears as van Loosbroek, Lozenbroek, Losenbroek and Loonbraek.\n\nShe married Antonie van den Berg and had seven known children between about 1779 and 1799, the first at Oss, the second baptised at Hilvarenbeek and the rest at Tilburg. A residents list of about 1810 shows her at Tilburg with her husband and five daughters still at home.\n\nShe was widowed on 5 August 1826. Her son Jacobus died in 1828 and her daughter Hendrika in 1840, both at Baardwijk. Maria Elisabeth died at Tilburg on 7 February 1842; the record gives her age as 89. Her daughter Maria Theresia died eight weeks later, aged 47. Her son Bernardus and three other daughters outlived her.","src":[["Death record, Tilburg, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a2e8d014-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Tilburg residents list, about 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:747caed9-05ed-bf06-8cee-d9496b6eb52a"],["Parents' marriage, Oss, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:6d58ec0b-06cf-caa7-9759-ea9da7da1c90"],["Death of daughter Hendrika, Baardwijk, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:170a955c-3b78-49e3-a84b-76dc126956ed"],["Death of daughter Maria Theresia, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a2f033cc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Maria Anna, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a6140f4c-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Wilhelmina, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a7141c16-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Johanna Maria, 1866","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:afaa67d6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbjacobusvloosbroek:{"n":"Jacobus van Loosbroek","d":"Macharen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jacobus van Loosbroek lived at Macharen, a farming village on the Maas in the north-east of Brabant. He was a widower when banns were entered for him on 8 May 1751; the banns entry names his late wife as Elisabet Peters and the marriage entry as Maria Peters. On 23 May 1751 he married Maria Peters van Grinsven of Oss, at Oss.\n\nTheir daughter Maria Elisabeth was born at Macharen about 1752. She later married Antonie van den Berg and settled at Tilburg, some 40 kilometres to the south-west. When she died there in 1842 her death record named Jacobus van Loosbroek and Maria van Grinsven as her parents, some ninety years after their wedding.","src":[["Marriage, Oss, 23 May 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:6d58ec0b-06cf-caa7-9759-ea9da7da1c90"],["Banns, Oss, 8 May 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:dfa7ca31-2b15-fec3-4fa1-e37a41ae9a14"],["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a2e8d014-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbmariavgrinsven:{"n":"Maria Peters van Grinsven","d":"Oss","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria van Grinsven was born at Oss and was living there when she married. The marriage register calls her Maria Peters van Grinsven; Peters is a patronymic and shows that her father was a Peter. Oss was then a small country town in the north-east of Brabant.\n\nBanns were entered on 8 May 1751, and on 23 May 1751 she married Jacobus van Loosbroek, a widower from the nearby river village of Macharen. Their daughter Maria Elisabeth was born at Macharen about 1752. Maria is named once more, as Maria van Grinsven, in the record of that daughter's death at Tilburg in February 1842.","src":[["Marriage, Oss, 23 May 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:6d58ec0b-06cf-caa7-9759-ea9da7da1c90"],["Banns, Oss, 8 May 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:dfa7ca31-2b15-fec3-4fa1-e37a41ae9a14"],["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:a2e8d014-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbjoannesdevries:{"n":"Joannes de Vries","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Joannes de Vries lived at Tilburg in the 1740s with his wife Maria Huijben. Four of their children were baptised there: Antonius on 30 November 1744, Adrianus on 9 January 1746, Adriana on 12 May 1747 and Gerardus on 15 February 1750. The last entry is marked as belonging to the Goirke parish, one of the two Catholic parishes of Tilburg; Catholics there worshipped in plain barn churches, because the old parish church was in Reformed hands.\n\nHe is placed here as the probable father of Gerardus de Vries, who married Ida Jonkers in 1781. The marriage register says that Gerardus was born at Tilburg, his eldest son was named Joannes, and his brother-in-law Michiel de Wilde was married to an Adriana de Vries. No record names Joannes outright as that Gerardus's father, so the link is likely but not proven.\n\nA Tilburg letter of surety (borgbrief) dated 26 July 1738 names Jan de Vries, born at Baardwijk, and his partner Maria Adriaan Gerit Huijben, born at Tilburg. Such letters were issued by the poor relief of a man's home parish, promising to take him back if he fell on hard times, so that another place would let him settle; this one shows that Jan came from Baardwijk in the Langstraat and settled at Tilburg about the time of his marriage. The full name of his wife shows that her father was an Adriaan and her grandfather a Gerit, which fits the names Adrianus, Adriana and Gerardus given to their children. A search of the Tilburg baptism registers from 1735 to 1760 found no other Gerardus de Vries.","src":[["Baptism of son Antonius, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:25bfbc4a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Adrianus, 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0ad45aa4-28be-1e86-e243-56c91c710be4"],["Baptism of daughter Adriana, 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:264d80d4-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:26dadf6a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of Gerardus (Goirke parish entry)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:72730360-d596-b3b1-d75f-11614e8cf736"],["Borgbrief (letter of surety), Tilburg, 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fab3a9d5-9b2d-9084-711b-08f4f8d0024a"]]},
tbmariahuijben:{"n":"Maria Huijben","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria Huijben was the wife of Joannes de Vries of Tilburg. The priests wrote her surname in several ways: Huijben in 1744 and 1747, Huijbers in 1746 and Huijbrechs or Huijbreghs in 1750. All are forms of the same name, 'child of Huibert'.\n\nShe had four children baptised at Tilburg in a little over five years: Antonius (30 November 1744), Adrianus (9 January 1746), Adriana (12 May 1747) and Gerardus (15 February 1750).\n\nShe is placed here as the probable mother of the Gerardus de Vries who married Ida Jonkers in 1781. He was born at Tilburg, and his sister's name and his own first son's name fit this couple, but no record states the relationship directly.\n\nA letter of surety issued at Tilburg on 26 July 1738 calls her Maria Adriaan Gerit Huijben, born at Tilburg, 'Maria, daughter of Adriaan, son of Gerit Huijben', and her partner Jan de Vries, born at Baardwijk. Her children's names, Adrianus, Adriana and Gerardus, repeat her father's and grandfather's names.","src":[["Baptism of son Antonius, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:25bfbc4a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Adrianus, 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0ad45aa4-28be-1e86-e243-56c91c710be4"],["Baptism of daughter Adriana, 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:264d80d4-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:26dadf6a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Borgbrief (letter of surety), Tilburg, 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fab3a9d5-9b2d-9084-711b-08f4f8d0024a"]]},
tbpeterjonkers:{"n":"Peter Jan Jonkers","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Peter Jonkers was born at Tilburg. The registers call him Petrus Jonckers in Latin and Peter Jan Jonkers in Dutch, the second name showing that his father was a Jan. His own baptism has not been identified.\n\nHe married four times at Tilburg. On 9 September 1742 he married Cornelia Norbert Donders, with his brother Embrecht (Imbrech) Jan Jonckers and the bride's father Norbert Cornelis Donders as witnesses. As her widower he married Jenne Maria Reijnier Jansen van Tongeren on 1 February 1745. A widower again, he married Maria Bartel van Loon on 26 July 1756, after banns on 10 July; she too had been widowed twice. Their daughter Ida was baptised on 1 June 1759, and Maria was buried four days later, leaving him with a newborn.\n\nHe stayed a widower for seven years. On 2 June 1766 he married Maria Elisabet Hermanus Deris, a Tilburg woman whose father was a Hermanus; Willem Reijnier Jan van Tongeren, a relative of his second wife, was witness for the bride. He appears in Tilburg court registers in 1744, 1745 and 1765.\n\nWhen Ida married Gerardus de Vries on 18 February 1781, Peter stood as witness for his daughter. He is named for the last time, as her father, in the record of Ida's death in December 1839.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Ida, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:29cad8a6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of first wife, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ded5ff59-dd05-7be2-469f-1bc7e5374a47"],["Fourth marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f38a6c6a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Ida, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2e77040e-6905-cca0-c348-c795eb7a44b0"],["Daughter's death record, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9fd4c5cc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["First marriage, Tilburg, 1742 (with witnesses)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:66780071-bd53-3129-76d1-3fc74e9512a7"],["Third marriage, to Maria Bartel van Loon, 1756","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f38a429e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Fourth marriage, 1766 (with witness)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:9f31bddf-599f-dfa5-6f62-2f5c052284f4"],["Court register entry, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:8bfe9fcc-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Second marriage, to Jenne Maria van Tongeren, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f38a1896-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbmariavanloon:{"n":"Maria van Loon","d":"d. 1759 · Tilburg","x":1759,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria van Loon was born at Tilburg, the daughter of a Bartel (Bartholomeus): her marriage entries call her Maria Bartel van Loon. Her first husband was Peter Elissen. As his widow she married Norbartus de Laat at Tilburg on 29 September 1749, and as the widow of Norbartus she married Peter Jan Jonckers on 26 July 1756, after banns on 10 July; she was his third wife. She appears as Maria Bartholomeus van Loon in a Tilburg court register of 1757.\n\nHer daughter Ida was baptised at Tilburg on 1 June 1759. Maria was buried there on 5 June 1759, four days after the baptism; the burial register describes her as the wife of Peeter Jonkers.\n\nIda never knew her. She was brought up by her father, who married again in 1766, and she lived to be 80. When Ida died in December 1839 the civil registrar still recorded Maria van Loon as her mother, eighty years after Maria's own death.","src":[["Burial, Tilburg, 5 June 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ded5ff59-dd05-7be2-469f-1bc7e5374a47"],["Baptism of daughter Ida, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:29cad8a6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Husband's remarriage, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f38a6c6a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's death record, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9fd4c5cc-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Second marriage, to Norbartus de Laat, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f518b58c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Third marriage, to Peter Jan Jonckers, 1756","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f38a429e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Court register entry, 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:bcb31094-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbgerardusreijnen:{"n":"Gerardus Reijnen","d":"1742–1799 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tcnorbertusreijnen","tcmechtildisvdbos"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gerardus Reijnen was baptised at Tilburg on 10 October 1742, the son of Norbertus Reijnen and Mechtildis van den Bos. As Geraard Norbart Rijnen, 'son of Norbert', he married Maria Catharina Jan Baptist de Beer at Tilburg on 20 April 1772, after banns on 4 April; his brother Martinus and the bride's father Jan Baptist Cornelis de Beer were the witnesses.\n\nFive children were baptised at Tilburg: Norbertus (28 June 1773), Gertrudis (16 November 1775), Michael (13 February 1778), Joannes Baptista (3 August 1780) and Petronilla (3 June 1784). Jan Baptist married Jacoba van Gestel and died in 1826, aged 46. Petronilla married Judocus Noijen and died in 1822, aged 37. Gertrudis, called Geertrui, married twice at Tilburg, in 1797 to Johannes Hamers and in 1803 to Augustinus Maurits; her death record of 1829 names Gerardus Reijnen and Maria Catharina de Beer as her parents. It is through her son Gerardus Hamers that the Reijnen line runs down to the van den Berg family.\n\nHis wife was buried at Tilburg on 9 May 1796. A Gerardus Reijnen who left children was buried at Tilburg on 29 June 1799. No other man of that name is known at Tilburg in these years, so this is very probably him; it was three months before his first Hamers grandson, Gerardus, was baptised.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9c5fee30-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's first marriage, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5e9ac96-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of grandson Gerardus Hamers, 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2dc38d40-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Own baptism, Tilburg, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2546ff62-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 20 Apr 1772 (with witnesses)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:15f9444a-7b50-4f0d-babc-6a3101616b23"],["Baptism of son Norbertus, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2c3b83d8-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Gertrudis, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31d4ba44-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Michael, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31705284-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Joannes Baptista, 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:311c8294-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Petronilla, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:306051b4-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Jan Baptist, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9bb33438-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Petronella, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9a5cd7f6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of his wife, 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fe786d80-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of Gerardus Reijnen, 29 Jun 1799","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fee49564-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"b":1742,"x":1799},
tbmariacdebeer:{"n":"Maria Catharina de Beer","d":"1741–1796 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tcjanbaptistdebeer","tcgertruidabrocken"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria Catharina de Beer was baptised at Tilburg on 28 December 1741, the daughter of Jan Baptist de Beer and Gertruij Brocken. She married Gerardus Reijnen at Tilburg on 20 April 1772, when the register called her Maria Catharina Jan Baptist de Beer, with her father as her witness.\n\nShe had five children baptised at Tilburg: Norbertus (1773), Gertrudis (16 November 1775), Michael (1778), Joannes Baptista (1780) and Petronilla (1784). She was buried at Tilburg on 9 May 1796, leaving children; she did not live to see her daughter Geertrui marry Johannes Hamers in February 1797.\n\nGeertrui was widowed early and married twice, in 1797 and 1803. When she died at Tilburg on 14 January 1829 the registrar named Maria Catharina de Beer as her mother. She had two children by her first husband: Cornelia, who died at 17, and Gerardus, who married in 1818. Through Gerardus Hamers, Maria Catharina was the great-grandmother of Maria Gertruda Hamers, born in 1829.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9c5fee30-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's second marriage, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f6731f1c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 1741","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:251f7406-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:15f9444a-7b50-4f0d-babc-6a3101616b23"],["Burial, Tilburg, 9 May 1796","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fe786d80-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"b":1741,"x":1796},
tbmathiasvgestel:{"n":"Mathias van Gestel","d":"1714–before 1779 · Valkenswaard","s":"b","p":["tcpetervgestel","tchenricavdijck"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Mathias (Matthijs) van Gestel was baptised at Valkenswaard on 19 February 1714, the son of Petrus van Gestel and Henrica van Dijck; he is the only Mathias van Gestel in the indexed Valkenswaard baptism register of those years. Valkenswaard lies in the sandy Kempen country south of Eindhoven.\n\nOn 8 April 1742 he married Elisabeth Linsen at Valkenswaard; the register notes that the bride lived at Lommel, a Kempen village just across what is now the Belgian border. Their children were baptised at Valkenswaard: Henricus (21 January 1743, with Gijsbertus Linsen and Maria van Gestel as godparents), Joanna Elisabeth (15 March 1745), Elisabeth (4 March 1750), Catharina (21 January 1754), Petronella (28 June 1756), Petrus Matheus (21 September 1758), who was given his father's name as a second name, Anna Maria (20 December 1760) and Franciscus (12 December 1762), whose godfather is noted as a physician (medicus).\n\nIn March 1763 an aldermen's deed names Matthijs van Gestel as an overseer of the poor (armenmeester) of Waalre and Valkenswaard, an unpaid office given to respected householders, who managed the parish poor fund and handed out relief. He was still alive in May 1775, when he and his wife appear together in another Valkenswaard deed.\n\nHe died before his wife. A Utrecht notarial deed of 26 October 1779 describes her as 'in her lifetime widow of Matthijs van Gestel', when their children and co-heirs appointed representatives to settle the estate, and a deed at Valkenswaard followed on 10 November 1779. His son Peter left Valkenswaard for Tilburg and married there in 1783.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Elisabeth, Valkenswaard, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:03BD6A65-17DB-4578-B16F-9F95C1859B71"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1754","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:467148A7-68CB-4D5D-9C02-7B73BF07F682"],["Baptism of son Petrus Matheus, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:0D76C6C9-48F3-4176-8D8F-4CA9F54B6BC6"],["Utrecht notarial deed for the heirs, 26 Oct 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:609C5C31-B951-4642-E053-4701000A17FD"],["Aldermen's deed, Waalre en Valkenswaard, 10 Nov 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:37E4D433-7401-4FDA-846D-D6FFFA2114FC"],["Own baptism, Valkenswaard, 1714","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:791F84BE-015C-43B6-8BDB-9B42BA8A315B"],["Marriage, Valkenswaard, 8 Apr 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:dea22fb1-a021-b4b6-b019-cc5568455858"],["Baptism of son Henricus, 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:d9c087f2-7574-2c15-2c24-724ebd84abe3"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna Elisabeth, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:29af22e0-85b3-a46b-360a-a0bf0a90841a"],["Baptism of daughter Petronella, 1756","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:5788429E-AEE9-4A6E-9289-CFFC898EF085"],["Baptism of daughter Anna Maria, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:73f22a60-6571-9392-80a8-9d6303e6bc68"],["Baptism of son Franciscus, 1762","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:de9a8bdc-2cc8-bfa6-08ee-41c3917718d9"],["Aldermen's deed naming him overseer of the poor, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:0661C0E8-1517-43AB-B7EC-C8D385FBF185"],["Aldermen's deed with his wife, 2 May 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:48401AD5-2511-4D59-A2F9-42B50B921DB0"]],"b":1714},
tbelisabethlinsen:{"n":"Elisabeth Linsen","d":"1717–1779 · Lommel","s":"b","p":["tcwilhelmuslinsen","tcelisabethbourlet"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Elisabeth Linsen was the wife of Mathias van Gestel of Valkenswaard. Her children Elisabeth (1750), Catharina (1754) and Petrus Matheus (1758) were baptised there. An Anna Margareta Linsen was godmother in 1754, and in 1758 the godmother was another Elisabeth Linsen, with Arnoldus Hoefnagels as godfather.\n\nShe outlived her husband and died before 26 October 1779. On that day a Utrecht notary drew up a deed in which her children and co-heirs, among them her daughter Catharina, who was living in Utrecht, appointed Willem van den Bosch and Arnoldus Hoefnagel to act for them. A deed followed at Valkenswaard on 10 November 1779, naming Pietronella, Pieter, Francis and Anna Maria van Gestel. Her son Pieter was then 21; four years later he married at Tilburg.\n\nElisabeth came from Lommel. She was baptised there on 21 August 1717, the youngest child of Wilhelmus Linsen and Elisabeth Bourlet, with Philippus Bourlet and Margareta Linsen as godparents. When she married Mathias van Gestel at Valkenswaard on 8 April 1742 the register noted that she lived at Lommel, and a Gijsbertus Linsen, very probably her brother, stood godfather to her first child, Henricus, in January 1743. A Jacobus Barlet was godfather in 1760, and Mathias himself was godfather at Lommel in 1759, so the family kept close ties with her home village. Besides the children named above she had Henricus (1743), Joanna Elisabeth (1745), Petronella (1756), Anna Maria (1760) and Franciscus (1762).\n\nIn May 1775 Elisabet van Linsen of Valkenswaard and her husband appear in an aldermen's deed with Anna Maria Linsen and Francis Boer of Utrecht, which explains why her daughter Catharina was living in Utrecht in 1779. Elisabeth was buried at Valkenswaard on 8 October 1779, entered as Elisabeth Lintse, wife of Mattijs van Gestel. In the same week the Valkenswaard register records the burials of an Anna Maria van Gestel (4 and 6 October, one entry noting that she left a mother) and an Elisabeth van Gestel (4 and 7 October). They may have been two of her daughters, but this is not certain: an Anna Maria van Gestel is also named among the family in the deed of 10 November 1779. The autumn of 1779 brought a severe epidemic of dysentery, the 'red flux' (rode loop), to North Brabant and neighbouring provinces; the cause of these deaths is not written in the register.","src":[["Utrecht notarial deed for her heirs, 26 Oct 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:609C5C31-B951-4642-E053-4701000A17FD"],["Aldermen's deed, Waalre en Valkenswaard, 10 Nov 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:37E4D433-7401-4FDA-846D-D6FFFA2114FC"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1754","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:467148A7-68CB-4D5D-9C02-7B73BF07F682"],["Baptism of son Petrus Matheus, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:0D76C6C9-48F3-4176-8D8F-4CA9F54B6BC6"],["Baptism, Lommel, 21 Aug 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:01790A99-F167-4652-A834-C0AFDD6C0271"],["Marriage, Valkenswaard, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:F5078C78-72E4-493A-8A56-3A5A211E0937"],["Baptism of son Henricus, 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:C98121F8-45B1-4976-953B-E8D5CE24FD29"],["Baptism at Lommel with Mathias van Gestel as godfather, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:B4E69509-6E9A-4E20-B1FA-D3B30C30C023"],["Aldermen's deed, 2 May 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:48401AD5-2511-4D59-A2F9-42B50B921DB0"],["Burial, Valkenswaard, 8 Oct 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:D7EFE0B4-E00D-48FD-B639-01745EB9F905"],["Burial of Anna Maria van Gestel, 6 Oct 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b6c3fe49-136e-4759-7299-fd2440b7dd13"],["Burial of Elisabeth van Gestel, 7 Oct 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:4507fd70-0345-f5c9-c129-5d72b5cb6887"],["Article on the rode loop epidemics (Open Universiteit, Locus)","https://locus.ou.nl/locus-actueel/de-rode-loop-corona-van-de-18de-eeuw"]],"b":1717,"x":1779},
tbadriaandelepper:{"n":"Adriaan de Lepper","d":"Enschot","s":"b","p":["tccornelisdelepper","tcjennekevdlinden"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Ariaan (Adriaan) de Lepper, born at Enschot, a hamlet between Tilburg and Oisterwijk, and living at Tilburg, married Jenne Marie Vermeer, born at Tilburg, on 8 July 1742 after banns on 23 June. His witness was Jan van Hest, his brother-in-law, and hers was her brother Peter Vermeer.\n\nThe couple had at least seven children baptised at Tilburg: Cornelius (22 May 1744), Franciscus (18 February 1746), a second Franciscus (1 May 1747), Joannes (2 December 1749, in the Goirke parish, where the name is written Lappers), Godefridus (9 February 1752), a second Joannes (1 March 1760) and, about 1762, Johanna Catharina. The baptisms of 1744 to 1752 call the mother Adriana Maria Vermeer, those of 1749 and 1760 Joanna Maria. Johanna Catharina's death record of 1824 names her parents as Adriaan de Lepper and Adriana Maria Vermeer, and her marriage entry of 1783 calls her Johanna Catharina Adriaen de Lepper, 'Adriaan's daughter'.\n\nHis parents are not proven. He may be the Adrianus de Lepper baptised at Tilburg on 28 September 1710, son of Cornelis Peters de Lepper and Jenneke Peters van der Linden: his witness Jan van Hest had married a Maria Cornelis de Lepper, and naming the first son Cornelius fits a father called Cornelis. But there were several men of this name nearby. Another Adrianus de Lepper, son of Joannes, was baptised at Oisterwijk in 1715; an Adrianus Cornelius de Lepper with a wife Helena van Es had a child baptised at Berkel-Enschot in 1754; and an Adrianus de Lepper with a wife Joanna Nauwens had children at Tilburg in 1742 and 1744. The 1710 baptism was at Tilburg, while the marriage entry gives Enschot as his birthplace. A Tilburg court register of 1761 names an Adriaan Cornelis de Lepper, and an Adriaan de Lepper was buried at Tilburg on 1 June 1765; with so many namesakes, neither can be tied to him with certainty.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9b1d94e6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:03766f45-79e4-08ed-2de8-a9a4a93ed401"],["Baptism of Joannes de Lepper, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:29f187bc-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 8 Jul 1742 (with witnesses)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:a5ac0dab-6c13-7b7a-e044-7c0a2ff3fba9"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:25a620aa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Franciscus, 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:260a6704-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Franciscus, 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:264c3ed6-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Godefridus, 1752","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2748cd4a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Possible own baptism, Tilburg, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1f3f250e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Court register entry for Adriaan Cornelis de Lepper, 1761","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:be1d3afe-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of an Adriaan de Lepper, Tilburg, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0f2658ea-ba49-6293-35de-085c99400da8"],["The other Adrianus de Lepper, Oisterwijk, 1715","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6cbba4ce-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Joannes (Lappers), Tilburg, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:26cd7aaa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of a namesake's son: Adrianus Cornelius de Lepper and Helena van Es, Berkel-Enschot, 1754","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:33573702-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of a namesake's son: Adrianus de Lepper and Joanna Nauwens, Tilburg, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:252d6660-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]],"u":true},
tbadrianamvermeer:{"n":"Adriana Maria Vermeer","d":"b. 1719 · Tilburg","s":"b","p":["tccornelisvermeer","tcmariaverbruggen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Adriana Maria Vermeer was the wife of Adriaan de Lepper of Tilburg. As Jenne Marie Vermeer, born at Tilburg, she married Ariaan de Lepper there on 8 July 1742, with her brother Peter Vermeer as her witness. The baptisms of her children from 1744 to 1752 call her Adriana Maria Vermeer, and those of 1749 and 1760 Joanna Maria. Her children baptised at Tilburg were Cornelius (1744), Franciscus (1746), a second Franciscus (1747), Joannes (1749), Godefridus (1752), a second Joannes (1760) and Johanna Catharina (about 1762).\n\nJohanna Catharina married Peter van Gestel in November 1783. When she died on 30 November 1824 the registrar recorded her mother as Adriana Maria Vermeer. Through Johanna Catharina she was the grandmother of six van Gestel children born between 1785 and about 1801, among them a pair of twins.\n\nHer parents are not proven. An Adriana Maria Vermeer was baptised at Tilburg on 23 August 1719, the daughter of Cornelis Vermeer and Maria Verbruggen, and she is the only girl of that name in the Tilburg baptism register of those years. But no son Peter of that couple has been found, while a Petrus Vermeer was baptised at Tilburg in 1721 to Joannes Vermeer and Joanna Leppers, and the marriage register calls the bride Jenne Marie, not Adriana. A Joanna Maria Vermeer was also baptised at Haaren in 1716, the daughter of Petrus Cornelis Vermeer. The link to Cornelis Vermeer and Maria Verbruggen therefore rests on her name in the baptisms and death record and remains uncertain.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9b1d94e6-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of Joannes de Lepper, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:29f187bc-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's marriage, Tilburg, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:03766f45-79e4-08ed-2de8-a9a4a93ed401"],["Baptism, Tilburg, 23 Aug 1719","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:20f5f9fe-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 8 Jul 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ef31f5f2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2ac3ab4c-71e2-7ec2-50fb-7d03d7061986"],["Baptism of son Godefridus, 1752","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2748cd4a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Joannes, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:26cd7aaa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage with witnesses, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:a5ac0dab-6c13-7b7a-e044-7c0a2ff3fba9"],["Baptism of Petrus Vermeer, son of Joannes Vermeer and Joanna Leppers, 1721","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2141b3da-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of Joanna Maria Vermeer, Haaren, 1716","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:4b8aae4a-4629-11e3-a747-d206bceb4d38"]],"b":1719,"u":true},
tbarnoldusdekort:{"n":"Arnoldus de Kort","d":"c. 1741–1811 · Heukelom","b":1741,"x":1811,"s":"b","p":["tbmichieldekort","tbmariejacobs"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born about 1741, the son of Michiel de Kort and Maria Jacobs. The register of his marriage says he was born at Heukelom, a hamlet between Tilburg and Oisterwijk; his death record says Oisterwijk, the nearest town.\n\nAs Arnoldus Michiels de Kort he married Cornelia Somers, a Tilburg woman, at Tilburg on 27 April 1766, after banns on 12 April. Five children were baptised there: Joanna Catharina (1770), Michael (1773), Elizabetha (31 December 1775), Joannes (1778) and Petronilla (1784). His son Michiel and his daughter Elisabeth married a sister and brother, Anna Cornelia and Peter van den Brekel.\n\nHe died at Tilburg on 26 August 1811, aged 70. Brabant had been annexed to the French Empire the year before, and the death record uses French forms of the names: it calls him Arnould Adrien de Kort and his widow Anne Corneille Somers. The second name Adrien does not agree with the Michiels of his marriage entry.\n\nAn Arnoldus de Kort was baptised at Oisterwijk on 22 September 1733, the son of Michael Art de Cort and Catharina Jacob Cornelisse. The father's name matches his death record; the death record calls the mother Marie Jacobs, which may be a garbled form of 'Catharina, daughter of Jacob'. No other Arnoldus de Kort appears in the Oisterwijk register of the 1730s and 1740s, so this is very probably his baptism. If so, he was about 77 when he died, not 70 as the death record says; the Tilburg residents list of about 1810 gives a third version, January 1737. At his marriage in 1766 his witnesses were his father, Michiel de Kort, and Cornelis Adriaen Beertens, a cousin of the bride. He appears in Tilburg court registers in 1783 and in a notarial acknowledgement of debt in 1789.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ada41c4e-ffa6-ec1a-2172-253c53e9694f"],["Marriage register entry with banns, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:efee4efa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death record, Tilburg, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:96f64db8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna Catharina, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2b89816a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Michael, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2c3773ce-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Elizabetha, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31ce2e7c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Joannes, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:315a59f2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Petronilla, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:30671bf2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Probable baptism, Oisterwijk, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a514072-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Residents list entry, c. 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a817c57-cc95-476f-a324-08775ab63588"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1766 (with witnesses)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b0298539-997f-dd85-96f2-4d9d73ab8855"],["Court register entry, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:c0543520-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial deed, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:25718fa8-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbcorneliasomers:{"n":"Cornelia Somers","d":"d. 1818 · Tilburg","x":1818,"s":"b","p":["tbjansomers","tbelisabethverschueren"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Born at Tilburg, the daughter of Jan Somers and Elisabeth Verschueren. Her surname is also written Zomers. She married Arnoldus de Kort at Tilburg on 27 April 1766.\n\nFive children of the marriage were baptised at Tilburg over fourteen years: Joanna Catharina (15 January 1770), Michael (23 May 1773), Elizabetha (31 December 1775), Joannes (21 July 1778) and Petronilla (9 April 1784). A residents list of about 1810 shows her son Michel living at Tilburg with his wife Cornelie van den Brekel. Her daughter Elisabeth had moved to Goirle with her husband by 1805.\n\nShe was widowed on 26 August 1811 and died at Tilburg on 25 December 1818. The death record gives her age as 87. That would make her 53 when her youngest child was baptised in 1784, so the age is probably overstated.","src":[["Death record, Tilburg, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:973ea694-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ada41c4e-ffa6-ec1a-2172-253c53e9694f"],["Baptism of daughter Elizabetha, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31ce2e7c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Petronilla, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:30671bf2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Residents list: Michel de Kort and Cornelie van den Brekel","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1ed141a3-4b63-98c3-7687-23acc4f5141f"]]},
tbmichieldekort:{"n":"Michiel de Kort","d":"Oisterwijk","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Michiel de Kort is known from the records of his son Arnoldus. When Arnoldus died at Tilburg on 26 August 1811, aged 70, the registrar entered him as born at Oisterwijk, the son of Michel de Kort and Marie Jacobs.\n\nArnoldus's marriage entry of 1766 gives his birthplace more exactly as Heukelom, a hamlet on the road between Oisterwijk and Tilburg, and calls him Arnoldus Michiels, 'Michiel's son'. Arnoldus named his own first son Michael in 1773, and the name passed on again to a great-grandson, Francis Michiel van den Brekel, baptised in 1800.\n\nThe Oisterwijk baptism register records three children of Michiel (Michael) de Kort and Catharina Jacob Cornelisse: Joanna on 20 January 1727, a second Joanna on 5 October 1731 and Arnoldus on 22 September 1733. In the last entry the father is written Michael Art de Cort, 'son of Art' (Arnold), which explains why his son was named Arnoldus. Two women with his patronymic, very probably his daughters, married later: Cornelia Michiel de Kort, born at Oisterwijk, married Hendrick Peter Reijnen at Oisterwijk on 3 February 1765, and Jenne Marie Michiel de Kort, born at Heukelom, married Willem Jan de Rooij there on 29 March 1778. Michiel was alive in April 1766, when he witnessed his son Arnoldus's marriage at Tilburg.","src":[["Son's death record, Tilburg, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:96f64db8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Son's marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ada41c4e-ffa6-ec1a-2172-253c53e9694f"],["Baptism of grandson Michael, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2c3773ce-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, Oisterwijk, 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a505d10-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a5114bc-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Arnoldus, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a514072-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Cornelia, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6fe31c72-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of daughter Jenne Marie, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:7000c402-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Son's marriage with him as witness, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b0298539-997f-dd85-96f2-4d9d73ab8855"]]},
tbmariejacobs:{"n":"Maria Jacobs","d":"Oisterwijk","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria Jacobs was the wife of Michiel de Kort. She is named in the death entry of her son Arnoldus de Kort, who died at Tilburg on 26 August 1811, aged 70; it was written under French rule and calls her Marie Jacobs.\n\nIn the Oisterwijk baptism register the wife of Michiel (Michael) de Kort is Catharina Jacob Cornelisse, 'Catharina, daughter of Jacob Cornelis'. She is very probably the same woman, and the 1811 record turned her name into Marie Jacobs, but the first names do not agree. That register records her children Joanna (20 January 1727), a second Joanna (5 October 1731) and Arnoldus (22 September 1733); women named Cornelia (married 1765) and Jenne Marie (married 1778) with the patronymic Michiel de Kort also appear in Oisterwijk marriage registers.\n\nHer son Arnoldus was born at Heukelom, a hamlet in the Oisterwijk district a few kilometres east of Tilburg. He married at Tilburg in 1766 and had five children baptised there between 1770 and 1784, her grandchildren. One of them, Elisabeth, married Peter van den Brekel.","src":[["Son's death record, Tilburg, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:96f64db8-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Son's marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ada41c4e-ffa6-ec1a-2172-253c53e9694f"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, Oisterwijk, 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a505d10-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Arnoldus, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:6a514072-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbjansomers:{"n":"Jan Somers","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Somers of Tilburg is named in the death record of his daughter Cornelia, who died there on 25 December 1818. The registrar wrote that she was born at Tilburg, the daughter of Jan Somers and Elisabeth Verschueren, and the widow of Arnoldus de Kort.\n\nCornelia's marriage entry of 27 April 1766 also gives Tilburg as her birthplace, so Jan was settled there by the time she was born, a generation before her marriage. Her death record gives her age as 87, which is probably too high. Through Cornelia he was the grandfather of five de Kort children baptised at Tilburg between 1770 and 1784; one of them, Joannes, baptised in 1778, bore his name.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:973ea694-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's marriage, Tilburg, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ada41c4e-ffa6-ec1a-2172-253c53e9694f"],["Baptism of grandson Joannes de Kort, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:315a59f2-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbelisabethverschueren:{"n":"Elisabeth Verschueren","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Elisabeth Verschueren was the wife of Jan Somers of Tilburg. She is named as the mother of Cornelia Somers in Cornelia's death record of 25 December 1818. Cornelia was born at Tilburg and married Arnoldus de Kort there in April 1766.\n\nThe surname Verschueren means 'from the barn' and is found all over Brabant. Elisabeth's own first name came down the family: Cornelia's third child, baptised at Tilburg on 31 December 1775, was called Elizabetha. That granddaughter, Elisabeth de Kort, married Peter van den Brekel and moved to Goirle, where she died in 1831.","src":[["Daughter's death record, Tilburg, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:973ea694-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of granddaughter Elizabetha de Kort, 1775","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:31ce2e7c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbpetervangorp:{"n":"Peter van Gorp","d":"Goirle","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Peter (Petrus) van Gorp lived at Goirle with his wife Aldegonda Spaepen. He married her in the Catholic church at Goirle on 11 May 1772, and their first child, Joanna, followed in June 1773.\n\nThree of their children were baptised at Goirle: Joanna on 24 June 1773, Catharina on 4 June 1776 and Cornelius on 23 May 1778. Catharina's godparents were Wilhelmus Spaepen and Joanna van Ravels. His wife was buried at Goirle on 9 September 1794.\n\nWhen Catharina married Antonie de Rooij in February 1798, the register called her Catharina Peter van Gorp, 'Peter's daughter'. Both daughters lived into their seventies and died at Goirle within twelve weeks of each other in 1848; each death record names Peter van Gorp and Aldegonda Spapens as the parents.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Joanna, Goirle, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42e9b456-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42e9ddf0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42ea05aa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of wife, Goirle, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:bf99db70-d251-dfa5-86f7-74c20ae75eb7"],["Marriage of daughter Catharina, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f5fb7412-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Catharina, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:11d48188-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of daughter Johanna, 1848","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:11d3d760-3864-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Goirle, 11 May 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:7a4ae6a4-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tbaldegondaspaepen:{"n":"Aldegonda Spaepen","d":"d. 1794 · Goirle","x":1794,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aldegonda Spaepen married Petrus van Gorp in the Catholic church at Goirle on 11 May 1772. The priest wrote her name in Latin as Aldegundis Spaepen; the burial register has Allegonda Spapen, and her daughters' death records Aldegonda Spapens.\n\nShe had three children baptised at Goirle: Joanna (24 June 1773), Catharina (4 June 1776) and Cornelius (23 May 1778). A Wilhelmus Spaepen was godfather to Catharina.\n\nShe was buried at Goirle on 9 September 1794, when her daughters were 21 and 18. Catharina married three and a half years later, and in 1804 gave her second daughter the name Aldegundis. That granddaughter, Aldegonda de Rooij, lived until 1886.\n\nOn 14 September 1760, twelve years before her own wedding, an Adriana Maria (Janne Mie) Spapen had married Cornelis van Gorp, also of Goirle, with a Willem Spapen as her brother and witness; the Wilhelmus Spaepen who was godfather to Aldegonda's daughter Catharina may be the same man, which would make the two brides sisters, but this is not proven.","src":[["Burial, Goirle, 9 Sep 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:bf99db70-d251-dfa5-86f7-74c20ae75eb7"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42e9ddf0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism entry with godparents, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:34680d1d-44e2-930e-7077-db3af6dec8e5"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42e9b456-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:42ea05aa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of granddaughter Aldegundis de Rooij, 1804","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:41e7f2ca-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage, Goirle, 11 May 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:7a4ae6a4-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of Cornelis van Gorp and Janne Mie Spapen, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:e58062ce-e157-05c6-4591-e1d57adafebc"]]},
dgjanvreenegoor:{"n":"Jan (Joannes) Vreenegoor","d":"c. 1761–1826 · Kampen","b":1761,"x":1826,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"A carpenter (timmerman) of Kampen; that is his trade in the record of his daughter Clara's marriage in 1821. He died at Kampen on 29 October 1826, aged 65.\n\nWith his wife, Maria Kroese, he had at least five children whose own records name them as parents: Gerhardus (about 1786-1870), a labourer; Joanna Geertruida (about 1791-1863); Clara, baptised on 18 February 1793; Catharina Maria, baptised on 21 October 1795; and Hendrikus (about 1800-1850), a weaver.\n\nHe is placed here as the probable father of Nicolaas Vreenegoor, the Kampen gravedigger who was born about 1785 and died in 1822. No record says so directly, because Nicolaas's death record names no parents. The case rests on the dates and on the family's names: Nicolaas called his daughter Johanna Geertruida, and his son Dirk named children Catharina Maria, Maria Klara, Johannes and Hendrikus Gerhardus. A second Vreenegoor household in Kampen, that of Martinus (about 1753-1835), cannot be ruled out, so the link is marked as unproven.\n\nMore of the children's records add dates. Gerardus was baptised on 17 December 1785 and was a linen weaver when he married Maria Bouwmeester at Zwolle; that record calls his father a carpenter. A sixth child, Anna Christina Wilhelmina, was baptised on 2 January 1788, worked as a maid, married Jannes Nijboer, a miller's man, at Zwolle, and died there on 5 August 1864 aged 76. Hendrikus was baptised on 1 September 1800. Clara died at Edam on 26 July 1862, aged 69, still the wife of Gerardus Johannes Huisink; her death record calls her mother Wilhelmina Kroese.","src":[["Death record, 29 October 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:B8554A14-7159-4F98-B045-047C2B07C74B"],["Marriage of daughter Clara, 1821 (father a carpenter)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:8255D6B9-2567-47DD-867F-51D32764F82B"],["Marriage of daughter Catharina Maria, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E0E79F0C-65B3-489E-92B2-E16DD2D4C35D"],["Death of son Gerhardus, 1870","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:BF30F0A6-F2D7-4DC6-B2A4-A566718C8F01"],["Death of daughter Joanna Geertruida, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:78F60137-E825-4D3C-811C-7DFB746F1C22"],["Death of son Hendrikus, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:459F48C2-7EB3-4D9D-99BC-23C8A90DA89A"],["Death of Nicolaas Vreenegoor, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:79BF27E7-8061-41A3-8E29-BC7BDC1CB44A"],["Death of Martinus Vreenegoor, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:48CA5462-2D45-4BC8-993C-7B422D428B99"],["Marriage of Gerardus Vrenegoor and Maria Bouwmeester, Zwolle","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E6AD101B-290A-45C9-8B2A-84ADA81734E1"],["Marriage of Anna Christina Wilhelmina Vrenegoor and Jannes Nijboer, Zwolle","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5918367C-A019-4EA4-94CF-606ECABF6AAE"],["Death of Anna Christina Wilhelmina Vreenegoor, Zwolle 1864","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:7F5306D4-CC20-4814-9AF4-51CD57BC536C"],["Marriage of Hendrikus Vreenegoor, 1834 (baptised 1 September 1800)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:F799CB20-63A0-4F7C-BC17-B3ABF24F8AEF"],["Death of Clara Vrenegoor, Edam 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:CC33C995-50E7-4CF0-B838-9BBE9236B048"]]},
dgmariakroese:{"n":"Maria Kroese","d":"c. 1755–1833","b":1755,"x":1833,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of the Kampen carpenter Jan Vreenegoor. Her surname is also written Kroeze and Croese. She died at Kampen on 23 October 1833, aged 78, seven years after her husband, and was about six years older than he was. Her death record names no parents.\n\nFive children are named as hers in their own records: Gerhardus (about 1786-1870); Joanna Geertruida (about 1791-1863), who married Albert Veldhuis; Clara (baptised 1793), who married Gerardus Johannes Huisink of Winterswijk in 1821; Catharina Maria (baptised 1795), who married the turner Henricus Sprokhout of Zwolle in 1819; and Hendrikus (about 1800-1850), a weaver. Clara and Catharina Maria were both in service (dienstbaar) before they married.\n\nShe is shown as the probable mother of the gravedigger Nicolaas Vreenegoor (about 1785-1822), whose granddaughter Maria Klara Vreenegoor carried two of this family's names. The link rests on dates and names only and is marked as unproven.\n\nA sixth child is now known: Anna Christina Wilhelmina, baptised at Kampen on 2 January 1788, who married the miller's man Jannes Nijboer and died at Zwolle in 1864. Her eldest known son, Gerardus, was baptised on 17 December 1785. When her daughter Clara died at Edam in 1862, the clerk wrote the mother's name as Wilhelmina Kroese.","src":[["Death record, 23 October 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:454FEDED-E675-4BB7-8F47-26ED92081603"],["Death of her husband, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:B8554A14-7159-4F98-B045-047C2B07C74B"],["Marriage of daughter Clara, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:8255D6B9-2567-47DD-867F-51D32764F82B"],["Marriage of daughter Catharina Maria, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:E0E79F0C-65B3-489E-92B2-E16DD2D4C35D"],["Death of daughter Joanna Geertruida, 1863","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:78F60137-E825-4D3C-811C-7DFB746F1C22"],["Marriage of Anna Christina Wilhelmina Vrenegoor, Zwolle","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hco:5918367C-A019-4EA4-94CF-606ECABF6AAE"],["Death of Clara Vrenegoor, Edam 1862","https://www.openarchieven.nl/nha:CC33C995-50E7-4CF0-B838-9BBE9236B048"]]},
bzjamesbuchanan:{"n":"James Buchanan","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"James Buchanan is named as the father of Janet McGregor, born Buchanan, on her Queensland death registration; her mother is given there as Hellen Dawson (the registration spells the name Hellen). Janet was born about 1820, married Alexander McGregor, and died in Brisbane on 9 December 1899, aged 80. Her children Mary Stewart, John and Stewart Young McGregor all lived and died in Queensland.\n\nThe registration does not name Janet's husband, but she was buried at Toowong in the same grave as Mary Jane McGregor, the wife of her son Stewart, which ties her to this family. Through Janet, he was the great-grandparent of Adele White, who married Frank Warbrick in Brisbane in 1899. Where James lived, and when he died, has not been found.","src":[["Death of Janet McGregor, Qld 1899/B/32295 (parents James Buchanan and Hellen Dawson)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e69384e26508ecdf2bf03a1f166871e35dead48097165bded98102f9c12fcb39"],["Burial of Janet McGregor, aged 80, Toowong Cemetery, 10 Dec 1899 (grave Mon 12-42-33)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31790071"],["Burial of Mary Jane McGregor, aged 50, Toowong Cemetery, 12 May 1908 (same grave)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31909436"]]},
bzhellendawson:{"n":"Hellen Dawson","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Hellen Dawson is named as the mother of Janet McGregor, born Buchanan, on her Queensland death registration; her father is given there as James Buchanan (the registration spells the name Hellen). Janet was born about 1820, married Alexander McGregor, and died in Brisbane on 9 December 1899, aged 80. Her children Mary Stewart, John and Stewart Young McGregor all lived and died in Queensland.\n\nThe registration does not name Janet's husband, but she was buried at Toowong in the same grave as Mary Jane McGregor, the wife of her son Stewart, which ties her to this family. Through Janet, she was the great-grandparent of Adele White, who married Frank Warbrick in Brisbane in 1899. No other record of Hellen has yet been found.","src":[["Death of Janet McGregor, Qld 1899/B/32295 (parents James Buchanan and Hellen Dawson)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/e69384e26508ecdf2bf03a1f166871e35dead48097165bded98102f9c12fcb39"],["Burial of Janet McGregor, aged 80, Toowong Cemetery, 10 Dec 1899 (grave Mon 12-42-33)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31790071"],["Burial of Mary Jane McGregor, aged 50, Toowong Cemetery, 12 May 1908 (same grave)","https://brisbane.discovereverafter.com/profile/31909436"]]},
fkwilliamfisksr:{"n":"William Fisk","d":"Framlingham, Suffolk","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A plumber of Framlingham, Suffolk, a market town best known for its castle. He is known from his son's marriage entry of 5 February 1845, which a Thurlow family researcher copied from the Framlingham register: the groom, William Fisk, was a glazier of that parish, and his father was William Fisk, plumber. Father and son were in the same line of work, and the son carried the trade to Adelaide, where his firm was W. Fisk & Son.\n\nA descendant's notes name his wife as Fanny Bridges and date their son's birth to 3 April 1822. The civil death index for the Plomesgate district, which covered Framlingham, has a Fanny Fisk in the last quarter of 1852 and a William Fisk, aged 84, in the spring of 1867. The index alone cannot show whether these are the couple.","src":[["Fisk and Thurlow marriage entry, Framlingham, 5 Feb 1845, and descendant's notes, on the Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0001/f0000107.html#I04907"],["GRO death index, Fisk, Plomesgate, 1852 and 1867 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
fkfannybridges:{"n":"Fanny Bridges","d":"Framlingham, Suffolk","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Named as the mother of William Fisk, the Adelaide plumber, in a descendant's notes published on a Thurlow family site. They give his birth as 3 April 1822 at Framlingham, Suffolk, to William Fisk and Fanny, formerly Bridges. No baptism or marriage register has been seen to confirm it, so she is shown as unproven.\n\nThe civil death index has a Fanny Fisk in the Plomesgate district, which covered Framlingham, in the last quarter of 1852, less than two years before her son, his wife and their two children sailed for South Australia.","src":[["Descendant's notes on William Fisk, Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0001/f0000107.html#I04907"],["GRO death index, Fanny Fisk, Plomesgate, December quarter 1852, vol. 4a p. 376 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
fkjohnthurlow:{"n":"John Thurlow","d":"c. 1785–1850 · Wingfield, Suffolk","b":1785,"x":1850,"s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A stonemason and builder of Saxmundham, Suffolk. A Thurlow family researcher places his birth at Wingfield, a village near the Norfolk border, and the register of St Andrew's there has a John Thurlow baptised on 27 March 1785, six weeks old, with only his mother, Elizabeth Thurlow, named.\n\nOn 29 December 1812, described as of Saxmundham, he married Rebecca Wells at Framlingham. Their children were Thomas (born 24 October 1813), Rebecca (baptised 1815), John (who died on 11 January 1824, aged six), Henry (born 20 August 1820), Hannah, William (1822) and David, who died as a baby in March 1824. He is a builder, aged 56, in the 1841 census, a cabinet maker when Hannah married in 1842 and a stone mason when Rebecca married William Fisk in 1845. He built the White House, now Holly Lodge, in Saxmundham High Street.\n\nThomas, the eldest, learned stone carving in London and came home to be the town's sculptor, gas works manager and surveyor. Henry was a builder employing nine men and two boys in 1861 and died in Brisbane in 1904. William was a bricklayer and plasterer who died in Adelaide in 1874.\n\nJohn died on 2 June 1850, and his death is in the civil index for the Plomesgate district that quarter. The family tomb in Saxmundham churchyard remembers him as \"many years builder of this town\", aged 65.","src":[["Family of John Thurlow and Rebecca Wells of Saxmundham, with register, census and gravestone transcripts (Thurlow family site)","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0000/f0000061.html"],["Baptism of John Thurlow, Wingfield St Andrew, 27 Mar 1785 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6b003e25bc1c596ab216d318/john-thurlow-baptism-suffolk-wingfield-1785-03-27"],["GRO death index, John Thurlow, Plomesgate, June quarter 1850, vol. 12 p. 273 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Thomas Thurlow, sculptor, son of John Thurlow, builder and stonemason (Wikipedia)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Thurlow_(sculptor)"]]},
fkrebeccawells:{"n":"Rebecca Wells","d":"c. 1789–1860 · Framlingham, Suffolk","b":1789,"x":1860,"s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born at Framlingham, Suffolk, about 1789, according to the census. She married John Thurlow, a mason from Saxmundham, at Framlingham on 29 December 1812, and brought up her family at Saxmundham, a small market town on the turnpike road from Ipswich to Yarmouth. Of her seven children, two boys, John and David, died within weeks of each other early in 1824.\n\nShe was widowed in June 1850. In the 1851 census she was living on independent means, with her son William, a plasterer of 28, in the house. Her elder daughter Rebecca, who bore her name, had married William Fisk at Framlingham in 1845 and sailed with him for South Australia in 1854; William Thurlow went out there too.\n\nShe died at Saxmundham on 23 November 1860, aged 71. Her death is in the civil index for the Plomesgate district that quarter, and she is named with John on the family tomb in the churchyard.\n\nA descendant's note on the Thurlow family site names her parents as Henry Wells and Hannah, whose maiden name was Paxman. No baptism or marriage entry to confirm that has been found in the transcribed Suffolk registers, so they are not added to the tree.","src":[["Family of John Thurlow and Rebecca Wells of Saxmundham, with register, census and gravestone transcripts (Thurlow family site)","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/d0000/f0000061.html"],["GRO death index, Rebecca Thurlow, Plomesgate, December quarter 1860, vol. 4a p. 374 (FreeBMD)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"],["Note on Rebecca Wells, Thurlow family site","https://freepages.rootsweb.com/~weston/genealogy/thurlow/notes/not0197.html"]]},
fkjohnrescorla:{"n":"John Rescorla","d":"m. 1761","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Of St Breock in north Cornwall when he married Mary Mureau at Egloshayle on 14 October 1761, after banns. Both signed the register, and William Hull and Jane Pearse were the witnesses. The two parishes face each other across the River Camel and share the town of Wadebridge, where the old bridge joins them. Their son Henry was baptised at St Breock on 29 April 1772. Two burials of a John Rescorla are entered at St Breock in 1781, on 14 January and 3 June.\n\nHe is in this tree as the probable father of Henry Roscorla, the schoolmaster of Lewes; that link is unproven, and the reasons for it are on Henry's page. The surname, spelt Roscorla, Rescorla and Rescorle by different clerks, is a Cornish place name, and there is a hamlet called Rescorla near St Austell.","src":[["Marriage of John Rescorla and Mary Mureau, Egloshayle, 14 Oct 1761 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/marriages/"],["Baptism of Henry Rescorla, St Breock, 29 Apr 1772 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/baptisms/"],["Burials of John Rescorla, St Breock, 1781 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/burials/"]]},
fkmarymureau:{"n":"Mary Mureau","d":"b. 1733 · Egloshayle, Cornwall","b":1733,"s":"f","p":["fkcharlesmurow","fkmarydingey"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Baptised at Egloshayle, Cornwall, on 20 January 1733, the daughter of Mr Charles Murow and Mary Dingey. The clerks never settled how to spell the family's name: it is Murow and Marow at her parents' marriage, Mureau at her brother's baptism, Muro and Maro at her own, and Mureau again when she married. It was the only family of the name in the Cornish parish transcripts.\n\nOn 14 October 1761 she married John Rescorla of St Breock at Egloshayle and signed her name. A Mary Mureau was buried at Egloshayle in March 1762; Mary herself was Mrs Rescorla by then, so this was most likely her mother. Her brother Charles was buried there in June 1769, aged 38. Her son Henry was baptised at St Breock on 29 April 1772, when she was in her late thirties.\n\nIf Henry was the Lewes schoolmaster, which is not proven, her maiden name survives in his son Harry Munro Roscorla.","src":[["Baptism of Mary Muro (Maro), Egloshayle, 20 Jan 1733 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/baptisms/"],["Marriage of John Rescorla and Mary Mureau, Egloshayle, 14 Oct 1761 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/marriages/"],["Burials of Mary Mureau, 1762, and Charles Mureau, 1769, Egloshayle (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/burials/"]]},
fkcharlesmurow:{"n":"Charles Murow","d":"m. 1728","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Styled \"Mr\" in the Egloshayle register, a title parish clerks kept for men of some standing, when he married Mary Dingey of that parish on 30 January 1728, after banns. His surname is spelt Murow, Marow, Mureau and Muro in different entries, and no other family of the name appears in the Cornish parish transcripts.\n\nTheir son Charles was baptised at Egloshayle on 5 July 1731 and their daughter Mary on 20 January 1733. The younger Charles was buried there on 28 June 1769, aged 38. Mary married John Rescorla of St Breock in 1761. Egloshayle lies on the east bank of the River Camel, and its church stands by the river a little above Wadebridge.","src":[["Marriage of Mr Charles Murow and Mary Dingey, Egloshayle, 30 Jan 1728 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/marriages/"],["Baptisms of Charles Mureau, 1731, and Mary Muro, 1733, Egloshayle (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/baptisms/"],["Burial of Charles Mureau, aged 38, Egloshayle, 28 Jun 1769 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/burials/"]]},
fkmarydingey:{"n":"Mary Dingey","d":"m. 1728","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Of Egloshayle, Cornwall, when she married Mr Charles Murow there on 30 January 1728, after banns. She had a son, Charles, baptised on 5 July 1731, and a daughter, Mary, baptised on 20 January 1733.\n\nA Mary Mureau, most likely this Mary, was buried at Egloshayle in March 1762, five months after her daughter's wedding to John Rescorla; the day of the month is hidden in the binding of the register. The entry notes that she was buried in woollen. From the 1660s until 1814 the law required a woollen shroud, to help the wool trade.","src":[["Marriage of Mr Charles Murow and Mary Dingey, Egloshayle, 30 Jan 1728 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/marriages/"],["Burial of Mary Mureau, Egloshayle, March 1762 (Cornwall Online Parish Clerks database)","https://www.cornwall-opc-database.org/search-database/burials/"]]},
hygeorgepage:{"n":"George Page","d":"b. c. 1786 · Cambridgeshire","b":1786,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The head of a household at West Wickham, Cambridgeshire, in the census of 1841, when his age was given as 55. Adults' ages in that census were rounded down to the nearest five years, so he was born between about 1782 and 1786, and the return says he was born in Cambridgeshire.\n\nLiving with him were Sarah, 60, and four younger adults, William, 25, and John, Mary and Ann, each put down as 20, with a ten-year-old girl, Mary Roof. He is shown here as the father of William Page, who was born at West Wickham about 1813 and later lived at Bartlow, because that William is the only one of the right age in the village. The 1841 census does not state how the people in a house were related, and no baptism has been seen, so the link is uncertain.","src":[["1841 census, West Wickham: household of George Page","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683da859b18f7c2f690cb373"]]},
hysarahpage:{"n":"Sarah Page","d":"b. c. 1780 · Cambridgeshire","b":1780,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The woman of 60 living in the house of George Page at West Wickham, Cambridgeshire, in the census of 1841, and presumably his wife. Ages of adults were rounded down to the nearest five years in that census, so she was born between about 1777 and 1781; the return says she was born in the county. Her maiden name is not known.\n\nThe household also held William, 25, John, 20, Mary, 20, Ann, 20, and Mary Roof, 10. She is shown here as the mother of William Page, later of Bartlow, who was born at West Wickham about 1813. The 1841 census does not record relationships and no baptism or marriage has been seen, so this link is uncertain.","src":[["1841 census, West Wickham: household of George Page","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683da859b18f7c2f690cb373"]]},
hyjohnhorsford:{"n":"John Horsford","d":"b. c. 1812 · West Milton, Dorset","b":1812,"s":"y","p":["hxjameshorsford","hxmarylegg"],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1812 at West Milton, a hamlet in the parish of Powerstock in the hills of west Dorset. He began as a farm labourer. He and his wife Martha, who came from Nettlecombe in the same parish, had four sons: John (about 1834), James (about 1836) and William (about 1840), all born at West Milton, and George, born about 1847 at Corscombe.\n\nBy 1851 the family had moved right across the county to The Street, Gussage All Saints, in east Dorset. John and his two eldest sons, 17 and 15, were farm labourers, and William, 11, was a plough boy.\n\nMartha died during the 1850s. In 1861 John was a widower in Horton Street, Horton, working as the village postman, with only George, 14 and an apprentice, still at home. Ten years later he was living alone in the same street, a messenger for the Post Office. The three censuses give his age as 39, 44 and 59. His death has not been traced. His son George named him as his father and took his own family to Queensland in 1885.\n\nHis sons were baptised at Powerstock, the family name written Horseford: John on 24 March 1834, when the family was entered at Milton, and James on 10 June 1835, with their father a labourer both times, and William on 9 December 1839. At the 1841 census the family lived at West Milton, John giving his age as 25, with two other Horsfords in the house: Mary, 55, and Jane, 16. They were probably his widowed mother and his younger sister. James Horsford of Weymouth had married Mary Legg at Powerstock in 1810; they lived at Milton, where James was a labourer, and had a daughter Jane baptised in December 1824, and a James Horsford of West Milton was buried at Powerstock in July 1837, aged 57. No baptism for John himself appears in the transcribed Powerstock registers, and the 1841 census gives no relationships, so his parentage is not proven.","src":[["1851 census, Gussage All Saints: John Horsford, 39, farm labourer","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683791d6b18f7c1cdbe2f18a"],["1861 census, Horton Street, Horton: John Horsford, widower, postman","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/622e38aef493fd44f44f360c"],["1871 census, Horton: John Horsford, 59, messenger (Post Office)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/67f702dab18f7c5870692eac"],["Death of son George Horsford, Qld 1910/C/3381 (father John Horsford)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["1841 census, West Milton, Powerstock, HO107/281B p. 12 (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6833df80b18f7c24ca87291c"],["Powerstock baptisms 1813–1836 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps1813-1836.htm"],["Powerstock Bishop's Transcripts baptisms 1818–1843 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps3.htm"],["Powerstock marriages 1720–1837 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockMarrs2.htm"],["Powerstock burials 1813–1851 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBurs1.htm"]],"u":true},
hymarthahorsford:{"n":"Martha Horsford","d":"b. c. 1809 · Nettlecombe, Dorset","b":1809,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1809 at Nettlecombe, a hamlet in the parish of Powerstock in west Dorset, and the wife of John Horsford, a farm labourer from the neighbouring hamlet of West Milton. Her maiden name is not known.\n\nShe had four sons: John, James and William, born at West Milton between about 1834 and 1840, and George, born about 1847 at Corscombe. At the 1851 census she was 42 and living in The Street at Gussage All Saints, in east Dorset, with her husband and all four boys, three of them already at work on the land. She died between 1851 and 1861, when John was a widower at Horton with George, 14, the only son left at home. George's Queensland death registration of 1910 leaves his mother's name blank.\n\nAt the 1841 census she was 30 and living at West Milton with John and their three eldest boys, whose baptisms at Powerstock in 1834, 1835 and 1839 name their parents as John and Martha Horseford. No marriage for John and Martha appears in the transcribed Powerstock registers up to 1837.","src":[["1851 census, Gussage All Saints: Martha Horsford, 42, born Nettlecombe","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683791d6b18f7c1cdbe2f18a"],["1861 census, Horton: John Horsford, widower","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/622e38aef493fd44f44f360c"],["1841 census, West Milton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6833df80b18f7c24ca87291c"],["Powerstock baptisms 1813–1836 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps1813-1836.htm"]]},
hygeorgepardy:{"n":"George Pardy","d":"b. c. 1802 · Bishopstoke, Hampshire","b":1802,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1802 at Bishopstoke, a village on the River Itchen in Hampshire. He was a cordwainer, the old name for a shoemaker who made new boots and shoes from new leather, as distinct from a cobbler, who mended them.\n\nHe had crossed into east Dorset by about 1830, when his son George was born at Chalbury, and he then settled in the next parish, Horton, where his son Robert (about 1845) and daughter Anna (about 1849) were born. His wife Frances came from Witchampton, a few miles away.\n\nIn 1851 he was 49 and working at Horton with his eldest son George, 21, who had followed him into the trade. In 1871, at 69, he was still entered as a cordwainer, living at Haythorne in Horton parish with Frances and a six-year-old grandson, Henry George Pardy.\n\nHis daughter Anna married George Horsford, a Horton boy, in 1868 and emigrated to Queensland in 1885. Her death registration in Brisbane in 1934 names him as her father.\n\nThe 1841 census at Haythorne, Horton, with the name spelled Purdy, lists George, 35, shoemaker, born outside Dorset, with Frances, 30, George, 11, Harriott, 9, David, 6, and Ann, 2. Horton's register records the baptisms of four of their children: Harriot on 3 June 1832, David on 13 April 1835 and Ann on 29 July 1838, when George was entered as a shoemaker, and Robert on 19 May 1842, when he was a cordwainer; the 1842 baptism does not fit Robert's age of 6 in 1851. No marriage for George and Frances has been found in Horton's register, which stops in 1837, or on FreeREG.","src":[["1851 census, Horton: George Pardy, 49, cordwainer, born Bishopstoke","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683792ccb18f7c1cdbe32ac9"],["1871 census, Horton (Dorset Online Parish Clerks transcript): George Pardy, 69, Haythorne","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/1871Horton.htm"],["Death of daughter Anna Horsford, Qld 1934/B/25927 (father George Pardy)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/"],["1841 census, Horton (Dorset OPC transcript), HO107/288 f25 p7","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/1841Horton.htm"],["Horton baptisms 1801–1850 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/HortonBaps1801-1850.htm"],["Baptism of Harriot Pardy, Horton, 1832 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6831bb62865574f09da7f94d"]]},
hyfrancespardy:{"n":"Frances Pardy","d":"b. c. 1807 · Witchampton, Dorset","b":1807,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born about 1807 at Witchampton, a village on the River Allen north of Wimborne in Dorset, and the wife of George Pardy, the cordwainer (shoemaker) at Horton. Her maiden name is not known.\n\nHer children in the 1851 census were George, 21, born at Chalbury and working as a shoemaker beside his father, Robert, 6, a scholar, and Anna, a baby of one, both born at Horton. She was 44 that year.\n\nIn 1871 she was 64 and living with George at Haythorne, in Horton parish, with their grandson Henry George Pardy, 6, who had been born at Damerham. Her daughter Anna had married George Horsford in 1868, and Anna's first child, born in 1869, was named Frances Harriet. Anna and her family left for Queensland in 1885.\n\nIn 1841 she was 30 and living at Haythorne with George and four children, George, Harriott, David and Ann. Horton's register records the baptisms of Harriot (1832), David (1835), Ann (1838) and Robert (1842), all children of George and Frances Pardy.","src":[["1851 census, Horton: Frances Pardy, 44, born Witchampton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/683792ccb18f7c1cdbe32ac9"],["1871 census, Horton (Dorset Online Parish Clerks transcript)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/1871Horton.htm"],["1841 census, Horton (Dorset OPC transcript)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/1841Horton.htm"],["Horton baptisms 1801–1850 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/HortonFiles/HortonBaps1801-1850.htm"]]},
hythomasalcock:{"n":"Thomas Alcock","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":[],"t":"The father of Ellen Elizabeth Alcock. He is known only from the Queensland registration of her death on 21 March 1904, under her last married name of Hosking, which gives her parents as Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner.\n\nEllen was already in Queensland when she first married, in June 1864. Where the family lived is not recorded in any index seen so far: Ellen's name is not in Queensland's index of assisted immigrants, and no Queensland death has been found for Thomas. Alcock is an English surname, commonest in the Midlands and the north.","src":[["Death of Ellen Hosking, Qld 1904/C/3849 (parents Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/0f8dbe9761b6d6bbb73bbe0746d544c49be2deb01449095eef9b86a96e2dfc98"]]},
hyannturner:{"n":"Ann Turner","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":[],"t":"The mother of Ellen Elizabeth Alcock. She is known only from the Queensland registration of Ellen's death on 21 March 1904, which gives the dead woman's parents as Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner; Turner is her maiden name.\n\nHer daughter Ellen was in Queensland by June 1864, when she married for the first time, and went on to marry twice more and to raise the Harvey children, among them Ethel Augusta, born in 1868. Whether Ann herself ever came to Australia is not known. No record seen so far gives her birthplace, her dates or her parents, and both her names are too common to trace without one.","src":[["Death of Ellen Hosking, Qld 1904/C/3849 (parents Thomas Alcock and Ann Turner)","https://www.familyhistory.bdm.qld.gov.au/details/0f8dbe9761b6d6bbb73bbe0746d544c49be2deb01449095eef9b86a96e2dfc98"]]},
ezlucasjans:{"n":"Lucas Jans","d":"Wirdum","t":"Lucas Jans lived at Wirdum, a village on its mound a few kilometres south of Leeuwarden. He and his wife Geertje Willems had three children baptised in the church there: Jan on 26 October 1749, Pyttje (Pietje) on 4 February 1753 and Willem on 1 June 1766.\n\nHe appears in the Frisian tax registers of 1749 (the quotisatiekohieren). After a tax revolt in 1748 the province drew up a list of every household, with its size and a rating of what it could pay. Lucas Jans at Wirdum was entered as a household of two adults, assessed at a little over 11 guilders, one of the lower ratings. He and Geertje were entered together as members of the Reformed church at Wirdum from 23 April 1755, and were still listed in 1772.\n\nHis grandson, Pietje's son, was named Lucas after him and became a shoemaker at Wirdum.","src":[["Tax register of 1749, Wirdum","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e733388-046b-4f41-8d30-004b10330e53"],["Baptism of Jan, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d3bfec3b-5564-4eaf-9708-7ec4884f5295"],["Baptism of Pyttje, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7967885f-4efd-49c5-be57-d70a86dd3248"],["Baptism of Willem, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b3f87ce5-f797-4311-acd9-0e50c91eddab"],["Death of Pietje Lucas, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9fa6eea1-a4d9-e5d7-052e-63312fad5baa"],["Membership book, Wirdum (members 1755 and 1772)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fe6f02e3-f930-40eb-9fcf-ac5b606798f5"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezgeertjewillems:{"n":"Geertje Willems","d":"d. 1803? · Wirdum","t":"Geertje Willems was the wife of Lucas Jans of Wirdum and the mother of Jan (baptised 1749), Pietje (1753) and Willem (1766). Her youngest son carried her father's name, Willem.\n\nThe membership book of the Reformed church at Wirdum enters her together with her husband: both were members from 23 April 1755 and again in 1772. The same entry notes a death on 10 February 1803, which is taken here as hers, although the index wording does not say plainly whether it refers to her or to Lucas. Her daughter Pietje made her own confession of faith in the same church in 1821, and Pietje's daughter Geertje, born about 1795, was named after her.","src":[["Membership book, Wirdum (member 1755, died 1803)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fe6f02e3-f930-40eb-9fcf-ac5b606798f5"],["Baptism of Pyttje, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7967885f-4efd-49c5-be57-d70a86dd3248"],["Death of Pietje Lucas, 1840","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9fa6eea1-a4d9-e5d7-052e-63312fad5baa"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
eztjerkoeges:{"n":"Tjerk Oeges","d":"c. 1738–1811 · Idaard","b":1738,"x":1811,"t":"Tjerk Oeges lived all his known life at Idaard, a small farming village between Leeuwarden and Grouw. On 7 November 1762 he married Trijntje Reins, also of Idaard, in the village church.\n\nTheir children were baptised there: Oege (1763), Rein (1765), Jeltje (1768), Gertje (1772), Geertje (1774), Jouke (1776) and Wijbren (1779), with a second Jeltje entered in 1787. On 5 December 1768 he and Trijntje made their confession of faith together and became full members of the Reformed church of Idaard.\n\nWhen his daughter Geertje died at Akkrum in 1846, the record called her parents farmers (landbouwer and landbouwster). Tjerk died in December 1811, aged 73; the new civil register, then only a few months old, enters him on the 13th without a surname. His sons Oege and Jouke had both just registered the family name Tuinstra. Jouke stayed at Idaard and died there in 1858 aged 81.","src":[["Marriage, Idaard, 1762","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a7ff8ff-77d9-453d-9ff0-b5ac88b4e210"],["Baptism of Oege, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cca82e52-3c6a-4f22-a407-78ec5838fbc2"],["Confession of faith, Idaard, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2af4e618-eb3b-4bf3-92c0-77dceccfb053"],["Baptism of Jouke, 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:811835c3-a7c0-41e7-9e41-44005c9d81f8"],["Death record, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:15faeec0-9bdf-d57b-927f-0a7148999645"],["Death of daughter Geertje, Akkrum, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:68ffd680-cae5-0750-d253-dad3c2fcb5ad"],["Death of son Jouke, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c7846d3d-7a74-cf6f-4807-870670c55c3f"],["Death of son Oege, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c274043b-7f24-e0c7-ecd2-a626e6c6f83c"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
eztrijntjereins:{"n":"Trijntje Reins","d":"Idaard","t":"Trijntje Reins, whose father was called Rein, was living at Idaard when she married Tjerk Oeges there on 7 November 1762. Their first child, Oege, was baptised eleven months later, and their second son was named Rein after her father.\n\nShe had at least seven children baptised at Idaard between 1763 and 1779. On 5 December 1768 she and her husband made their confession of faith together, and the membership list of 1773 names them both.\n\nThe death record of her daughter Geertje, who died at Akkrum in 1846 as the wife of a cooper, describes Trijntje as a farmer's wife (landbouwster). Her granddaughter Trijntje Oeges Tuinstra, born in 1792, was named after her. Her own death has not been found; she is not named beside Tjerk in the short civil entry of his death in 1811.","src":[["Marriage, Idaard, 1762","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a7ff8ff-77d9-453d-9ff0-b5ac88b4e210"],["Baptism of Rein, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a43227d-9acd-49b0-96a5-911e26c3dfbc"],["Confession of faith, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2af4e618-eb3b-4bf3-92c0-77dceccfb053"],["Membership list, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:54cad68b-d1ff-4c19-b77c-1c6d1d33d9d0"],["Death of daughter Geertje, 1846","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:68ffd680-cae5-0750-d253-dad3c2fcb5ad"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
fredwarddales:{"n":"Edward Dales","d":"fl. 1673–1682 · Caistor","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Edward Dales lived at Caistor, the small market town on the edge of the Lincolnshire Wolds, in the 1670s and 1680s. He and his wife Ruth appear in the parish register from 1673: their son John was baptised on 22 March 1673, a son Thomas was baptised and buried in December 1676, and a daughter Ruth was baptised on 20 January 1677. Another child was baptised on 28 February 1680, the name now unreadable in the transcript.\n\n1681 was a hard year for the household: little Ruth was buried on 17 May and a daughter Elizabeth on 18 September. The next year their last recorded child, Susanna, was baptised on 29 October 1682. She grew up to marry Virgil Frankish at Caistor in 1705.\n\nAn Edward Dales married Anne Skepper at Caistor on 18 July 1683. If that was the same man, Ruth had died within months of Susanna's birth, but no burial for her appears in the transcript, and the entry does not say whether the groom was a widower.","src":[["Baptism of son John, Caistor, 22 Mar 1673 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f03f493fda395f40a7d"],["Burial of son Thomas, Caistor, 23 Dec 1676 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b51865574f305a959ce"],["Baptism of daughter Ruth, Caistor, 20 Jan 1677 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f11f493fda395f40bf8"],["Baptism of a child, Caistor, 28 Feb 1680 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f19f493fda395f40d0b"],["Burial of daughter Ruth, Caistor, 17 May 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b57865574f305a95b9b"],["Burial of daughter Elizabeth, Caistor, 18 Sep 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b58865574f305a95be9"],["Baptism of daughter Susanna, Caistor, 29 Oct 1682 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1cf493fda395f40d9a"],["Marriage of Edward Dales and Anne Skepper, Caistor, 18 Jul 1683 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c5a865574f305a9b125"]]},
frruthdales:{"n":"Ruth Dales","d":"fl. 1673–1682 · Caistor","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Ruth was the wife of Edward Dales of Caistor; her maiden name and her marriage have not been found. She is named as the mother in the baptisms of five children there over ten years: John (22 March 1673), Ruth (20 January 1677), a child whose name is lost (28 February 1680) and Susanna (29 October 1682), with a son Thomas baptised and buried in December 1676.\n\nShe buried three of them as small children: Thomas in 1676, and her daughters Ruth, aged about four, and Elizabeth within four months of each other in the summer of 1681. Susanna, born the following year, was the child who lived to marry; she became the wife of Virgil Frankish in 1705.\n\nRuth is last named in the register at Susanna's baptism in October 1682. An Edward Dales married Anne Skepper at Caistor in July 1683, and a Ruth Dales married John Hudson there on 13 November 1693; the transcript does not show whether either entry concerns her or her husband.","src":[["Baptism of son John, Caistor, 22 Mar 1673 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f03f493fda395f40a7d"],["Baptism of daughter Ruth, Caistor, 20 Jan 1677 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f11f493fda395f40bf8"],["Burial of daughter Ruth, Caistor, 17 May 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b57865574f305a95b9b"],["Burial of daughter Elizabeth, Caistor, 18 Sep 1681 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411b58865574f305a95be9"],["Baptism of daughter Susanna, Caistor, 29 Oct 1682 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ee52f1cf493fda395f40d9a"],["Marriage of John Hudson and Ruth Dales, Caistor, 1693 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68411c5b865574f305a9b1a0"]]},
frjohnhickson:{"n":"John Hickson","d":"fl. 1746–1754 · Brocklesby","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"John Hickson lived at Brocklesby in north Lincolnshire in the middle of the eighteenth century. He and his wife Mary had Elizabeth (17 August 1746), John (1 April 1750) and Ann (10 September 1754) baptised there, and two girls named Mary Hickson were baptised in the same church in 1748 and 1753.\n\nBrocklesby was the estate village of the Pelham family, later Earls of Yarborough, whose hall and park took up much of the parish. On 24 December 1754, three months after Ann's baptism, the register records the burial of a Hickson described only as a cottager, with no first name given. A cottager held a cottage with little or no land and lived by labouring for others.\n\nHe is shown as the father of Ann Hickson, who married Virgil Frankish at Elsham in 1787, because his daughter is the only Ann Hickson of the right age found in the registers of the district. That link is unproven.\n\nA John Hickson was buried at Wrawby, three miles from Elsham, on 5 April 1776, and an Elizabeth and a Maria Hickson married there in 1781 and 1783; a Mary Hixon of Elsham married in 1780. These may be this family, but no record joins them.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Elizabeth, Brocklesby, 1746 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59bff493fd6830701a0b"],["Baptism of son John, Brocklesby, 1750 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59c0f493fd6830701a5b"],["Baptism of daughter Ann, Brocklesby, 10 Sep 1754 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59c0f493fd6830701aa6"],["Burial of a Hickson, cottager, Brocklesby, 24 Dec 1754 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68410f8e865574f305a4196c"],["Marriage of Robert Dee and Mary Hixon, both of Elsham, 28 Nov 1780 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68416363865574f305c7c672"],["Marriage of William Buley and Elizabeth Hickson, Wrawby, 22 May 1781 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edeb8d0f493fd400f3696e6"],["Marriage of William Wilkinson and Maria Hickson, Wrawby, 11 May 1783 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5edeb8d2f493fd400f369787"],["Burial of John Hickson, Wrawby, 5 Apr 1776 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ede915ff493fd400f35ad6c"],["Burial of Mary Hickson, Wrawby, 21 Oct 1778 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ede9168f493fd400f35af64"]]},
frmaryhickson:{"n":"Mary Hickson","d":"fl. 1746–1754 · Brocklesby","s":"f","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mary was the wife of John Hickson of Brocklesby, and is known only from the baptism register there, where she is named as the mother of Elizabeth in 1746, John in 1750 and Ann in 1754. Her maiden name and her marriage have not been found. Two girls called Mary Hickson were also baptised at Brocklesby, in 1748 and 1753, which points to a first daughter Mary who died young and a second given the same name.\n\nA John Hickson married a Mary Colbeck at Kirmington, the next parish, on 28 March 1758, but that was after these children were born, so it is not her wedding. She is shown as the mother of Ann Hickson, who married Virgil Frankish at Elsham in 1787; like her husband's, that link is unproven.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Elizabeth, Brocklesby, 1746 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59bff493fd6830701a0b"],["Baptism of daughter Ann, Brocklesby, 10 Sep 1754 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/643c59c0f493fd6830701aa6"],["Marriage of John Hickson and Mary Colbeck, Kirmington, 1758 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6a33d176bab85cc8e5c79bdb"]]},
pkjohnwilsonsr:{"n":"John Wilson","d":"c. 1757–1831 · Langley, Essex","b":1757,"x":1831,"s":"p","p":["pkedwardwilson","pkmariawilson"],"u":true,"r":["uk"],"t":"The father of the John Wilson who took his family to Port Phillip in 1844. He married Mary Philips at St John the Evangelist, Langley, Essex, on 19 November 1782. Both made their marks, and one witness was a Burgess Wilson, a forename used by the Wilsons of Langley and nearby Elmdon. The Langley register records four children of John and Mary: Sarah (born 2 April 1785), Mary (baptised 1788), James (born 14 March 1790) and John (baptised 1 July 1798).\n\nMary was buried at Langley on 1 March 1808, \"wife of John Wilson\". John was living when his son John married Martha Burgess in Langley church in 1825, and was buried there on 18 March 1831, aged 74.\n\nThat age puts his birth about 1757, and a John Wilson, son of Edward and Maria, was baptised at Langley on 8 May 1757. He is the only John Wilson baptised at Langley between 1745 and 1766, but none of the later entries names his parents. Two other John Wilsons were baptised at Elmdon, the next parish, in March 1754 and August 1759; the Langley baptism of 1757 fits his age at burial best, but the link is marked as uncertain.\n\nHis parents, Edward Wilson and Maria Smith, both of Langley, had married at Clavering on 5 January 1757, four months before his baptism. His brothers and sister Ann, Robert, Joseph and James were baptised at Langley between 1760 and 1770, and his mother was buried there in 1795. The identification rests on his age at burial and his parish.","src":[["Marriage of John Wilson and Mary Philips, Langley, 19 November 1782 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64e5cc70f493fd4b5159eb3e"],["Burial of John Wilson, aged 74, Langley, 18 March 1831 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64f385d1f493fd6bcf36787a"],["Burial of Mary, wife of John Wilson, Langley, 1 March 1808 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3869f493fd767a9fa952"],["Baptism of John Wilson, son of Edward and Maria, Langley, 8 May 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d1f493fd767a9fa01d"],["Baptism of John Wilson, Langley, 1 July 1798 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37e3f493fd767a9fa61e"],["Baptism of James Wilson (born 14 March 1790), Langley (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37e0f493fd767a9fa4f8"],["Marriage of Edward Wilson and Maria Smith, Clavering, 5 January 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77b1"],["FreeREG: John Wilson baptisms in Essex 1750-1764 (Langley 1757; Elmdon 1754 and 1759)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_queries/new"]]},
pkmaryphilips:{"n":"Mary Philips","d":"c. 1759–1808 · Langley, Essex","x":1808,"s":"p","p":["pmgeorgephillips","pmsarahsmith"],"r":["uk"],"t":"The wife of John Wilson of Langley, Essex, and mother of the emigrant John Wilson. She married at Langley on 19 November 1782 as Mary Philips, spinster, and made her mark, as her husband did. Four children were entered in the Langley register over the next sixteen years: Sarah in 1785, Mary in 1788, James in 1790 and John in 1798. She was buried at Langley on 1 March 1808, described as the wife of John Wilson, when her youngest son was nine.\n\nShe was probably the Mary Phillips baptised at Langley on 1 July 1759, daughter of George Phillips and his wife Sarah, which makes her 23 at her wedding. A second Mary Philips, daughter of William and Philippa, was baptised at neighbouring Clavering on 29 August 1756, but a Mary Phillips described as \"a child\" was buried at Clavering on 6 May 1759, which very likely ends that line. The link to George and Sarah is still marked as uncertain because no record joins the bride of 1782 to the baptism of 1759.\n\nHer mother, born Sarah Smith of Langley, married at Clavering in 1758, a year after Edward Wilson married Maria Smith there with the same witness, John Smith.","src":[["Marriage of John Wilson and Mary Philips, Langley, 19 November 1782 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64e5cc70f493fd4b5159eb3e"],["Burial of Mary, wife of John Wilson, Langley, 1 March 1808 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3869f493fd767a9fa952"],["Baptism of Mary Phillips, daughter of George and Sarah, Langley, 1 July 1759 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d2f493fd767a9fa054"],["Baptism of Mary Philips, Clavering, 29 August 1756 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68348f2a86557413f97f1323"],["Burial of Mary Phillips, a child, Clavering, 6 May 1759 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68348ff686557413f97f5c6c"],["Marriage of George Phillips and Sarah Smith, Clavering, 11 October 1758 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77da"]],"b":1759,"u":true},
pkedwardwilson:{"n":"Edward Wilson","d":"c. 1731–? · Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":["pmedwardwilsonsr","pmelizabethturner"],"r":["uk"],"t":"A labourer of Langley, in the north-west corner of Essex. On 5 January 1757 Edward Wilson, single, of Langley, married Maria Smith, single, also of Langley, at the neighbouring church of Clavering. He made his mark; she signed her name. Their son John was baptised at Langley four months later, on 8 May 1757, followed by Ann (15 June 1760), Robert (3 April 1763), Joseph (8 June 1766, when the clerk wrote the mother's name as Mary) and James (15 April 1770). Maria was buried at Langley on 5 October 1795, described as the wife of Edward Wilson and a labourer's wife.\n\nThe John of 1757 is very probably the John Wilson buried at Langley in 1831 aged 74, grandfather of Joseph Wilson of Diamond Creek. That link rests on age and is marked as uncertain.\n\nAn Edward Wilson was buried at Langley on 11 February 1783. Because Maria was still called Edward's wife in 1795, that burial may be of his father rather than of Edward himself. He was most likely the Edward baptised at Langley on 18 April 1731, son of Edward and Elizabeth Wilson, which would make him 25 at his marriage; another Edward Wilson, son of Edward and Bridget, was baptised at nearby Elmdon in 1720, so this is also marked as uncertain.\n\nThe Wilsons were an established family in this corner of Essex. A Burgess Wilson married at Elmdon in 1748 and another of that name at Langley in 1764; one of them witnessed John's wedding in 1782. Langley had no single village centre: its houses stood around two greens, Upper Green and Lower Green, in high open farmland where most men worked on the land.","src":[["Baptism of John Wilson, son of Edward and Maria, Langley, 8 May 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d1f493fd767a9fa01d"],["Burial of John Wilson, aged 74, Langley, 18 March 1831 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64f385d1f493fd6bcf36787a"],["Marriage of Burgess Wilson and Martha Gamgee, Elmdon, 10 November 1748 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/655a32a5f493fd0481b90689"],["Marriage of Burgess Wilson and Mary Gayler, Langley, 18 May 1764 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64e5cc6af493fd4b5159e983"],["Marriage of Edward Wilson and Maria Smith, Clavering, 5 January 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77b1"],["Baptism of Ann Wilson, daughter of Edward and Maria, Langley, 15 June 1760 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d3f493fd767a9fa078"],["Baptism of Robert Wilson, Langley, 3 April 1763 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d5f493fd767a9fa0f1"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 8 June 1766 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d6f493fd767a9fa18e"],["Baptism of James Wilson, Langley, 15 April 1770 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d7f493fd767a9fa1e6"],["Burial of Maria, wife of Edward Wilson, Langley, 5 October 1795 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de386cf493fd767a9faa1c"],["Burial of Edward Wilson, Langley, 11 February 1783 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de386ef493fd767a9faa9d"],["Baptism of Edward Wilson, son of Edward and Elizabeth, Langley, 18 April 1731 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3748f493fd767a9f9c56"],["Baptism of Edward Wilson, son of Edward and Bridget, Elmdon, 28 February 1719/20 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/655b21e7f493fd31b48f5410"]],"b":1731,"u":true},
pkmariawilson:{"n":"Maria Smith","d":"d. 1795 · Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Born Maria Smith, of Langley in north-west Essex. She married Edward Wilson, also of Langley, at the neighbouring church of Clavering on 5 January 1757. Both were single; Edward made his mark, but Maria signed her own name, and the witnesses were John Debnam and a John Smith (the transcript queries the surname). Her first child, John, was baptised at Langley four months later, on 8 May 1757, and four more followed: Ann (1760), Robert (1763), Joseph (1766) and James (1770). In the entry for Joseph the clerk wrote her name as Mary.\n\nShe was buried at Langley on 5 October 1795, entered as the wife of Edward Wilson and a labourer's wife. If her son John is the John Wilson buried at Langley in 1831 aged 74, as the dates suggest, she was the great-grandmother of Joseph Wilson, who was born in the same parish in 1836 and died at Diamond Creek, Victoria; that link is marked as uncertain.\n\nIn October 1758 a Sarah Smith of Langley married George Phillips at Clavering, again with a John Smith as witness. Their daughter Mary Phillips is probably the Mary who married Maria's son John in 1782. Whether Maria and Sarah were related has not been established. A Mary Wilson buried at Langley in 1824 aged 80 was born about 1744 and was a different woman.","src":[["Baptism of John Wilson, son of Edward and Maria, Langley, 8 May 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d1f493fd767a9fa01d"],["Burial of Mary Wilson, aged 80, Langley, 13 May 1824 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64f385cff493fd6bcf3677fb"],["Marriage of Edward Wilson and Maria Smith, Clavering, 5 January 1757 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77b1"],["Burial of Maria, wife of Edward Wilson, Langley, 5 October 1795 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de386cf493fd767a9faa1c"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, son of Edward and Mary, Langley, 8 June 1766 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d6f493fd767a9fa18e"],["Marriage of George Phillips and Sarah Smith, Clavering, 11 October 1758 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77da"]],"x":1795},
pkjohnburgess:{"n":"John Burgess","d":"Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The father of Martha Burgess, named in her baptism at St Mary's, Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire, on 11 November 1800. He and his wife Mary had seven children baptised there over fourteen years, each entry giving their home as Furneux Pelham: John (16 January 1791), Mary (17 March 1793), William (17 May 1795), a second John (29 October 1797), Martha (1800), Joseph (6 March 1803) and George (1 September 1805). The repeated name suggests that the first John died as a small child.\n\nFurneux Pelham is one of three villages called the Pelhams in the north-east corner of Hertfordshire, close to the Essex border, in arable country. A John Burgess was buried at Furneux Pelham on 6 June 1811; the entry gives no age, so it may be the father or his son. Martha's marriage at Langley in 1825 was made \"with consent of parents\".\n\nThe youngest child, George, baptised in September 1805, was probably the George Burgess of Furneux Pelham buried there on 10 January 1806. The transcribed Furneux Pelham registers show no Burgess entries before 1790, so John and Mary were probably newcomers to the parish when their first child was baptised in 1791.","src":[["Baptism of Martha Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 11 November 1800 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379e8d86557439c20191fc"],["Baptism of John Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 16 January 1791 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379e8886557439c2018eed"],["Baptism of George Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 1 September 1805 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379e9086557439c2019387"],["Burial of John Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 6 June 1811 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379eeb86557439c201a6f4"],["Marriage of John Wilson and Martha Burgess, Langley, 30 April 1825 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64bc107bf493fd71b76d0981"],["Burial of George Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 10 January 1806 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379ee886557439c201a67d"]]},
pkmaryburgess:{"n":"Mary Burgess","d":"Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The wife of John Burgess of Furneux Pelham, Hertfordshire, and mother of Martha Burgess. Her first name is given in the baptism entries of her children at St Mary's between 1791 and 1805, and again, more than sixty years later and on the other side of the world, in her daughter's death registration in Victoria in 1864, which records Martha's parents simply as \"Burgess\" and \"Mary\". Her maiden name is not given in either place, and no marriage of a John Burgess to a Mary has been found in the Hertfordshire and Essex registers indexed so far for the 1780s.\n\nHer daughter Mary was baptised in 1793 and her youngest child, George, in 1805. Martha left the village when she married at Langley, a few miles away in Essex, in 1825.\n\nA Mary Burgess of Bonhams, Furneux Pelham, was buried at St Mary's on 23 September 1852, aged 88, which puts her birth about 1764. She is the right age to be Martha's mother, who was bearing children between 1791 and 1805, but the entry names no husband, so the identification is not certain. If it is her, she outlived her husband by some forty years and died twelve years before her daughter Martha in Victoria.","src":[["Baptism of Martha Burgess, Furneux Pelham, 11 November 1800 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379e8d86557439c20191fc"],["Victorian BDM index: death of Martha Wilson 1059/1864 (father Burgess, mother Mary, born Hertfordshire)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Burial of Mary Burgess, aged 88, of Bonhams, Furneux Pelham, 23 September 1852 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68379eb086557439c2019b04"]]},
pkthomashill:{"n":"Thomas Hill","d":"","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The father of Mary Hill, wife of the orchardist Robert Whatmough. His name comes from Mary's death registration at Greensborough, Victoria, in 1881, which records her father as Thomas Hill and gives no mother. Mary married at Manchester in 1839 and her first child was born in the Bury district, which places her in the cotton towns of south-east Lancashire at that time.\n\nHill is a common name there and Mary's age varies between records, which makes her baptism hard to fix. One candidate is the Mary Hill baptised at St James's, Milnrow, beside Rochdale, on 30 April 1815, daughter of Betty and of Thomas Hill of Whiteley Dean, a slubber (a worker who drew out carded wool or cotton into a loose rope ready for spinning). That entry fits the father's name and Robert Whatmough's home town, but it has not been proved to be the same Mary.","src":[["Victorian BDM index: death of Mary Whatnough (Whatmough) 10633/1881 (father Thomas Hill)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Baptism of Mary Hill, Milnrow, 30 April 1815 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Mary&Surname=Hill&FatherFirst=Thomas&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1815&period=3&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["FreeBMD: marriage of Robert Whatmough and Mary Hill, Manchester, Jul-Sep 1839 (vol. 20, p. 420); birth of Jemima Whatmough, Bury, Jan-Mar 1840 (vol. 21, p. 307)","https://www.freebmd.org.uk/cgi/search.pl"]]},
pkjamesbrearley:{"n":"James Brearley","d":"Castleton, Rochdale","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A Rochdale man of Brimrod Lane End in Castleton township, and probably the father of Sarah Brierly, who married Benjamin Whatmough in 1813. He and his wife Mary were Baptists: the births of seven of their children were entered in the register of the West Street chapel in Rochdale by the minister, Thomas Littlewood, between 1791 and 1805. They were Abraham (born 28 July 1791), Mary (22 June 1793), Sarah (7 November 1794), a second Mary (16 December 1796), William (7 May 1799), Charles (11 December 1800) and Alice (9 February 1805). The family was living at Castleton in 1791, at New Barn in 1793 and at Brimrod Lane End from 1794.\n\nThe link to the Whatmoughs is uncertain. It rests on Sarah Whatmough living at Brimrod Lane End as a married woman in 1821 and 1830, on her age, and on the lack of Church of England baptisms for most of her children. Many other Sarah Brearleys were baptised in Rochdale in the 1790s, so the hamlet is the main link.","src":[["Births of the children of James and Mary Brearley, West Street Baptist, Rochdale, 1791-1805 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Brearley&FatherFirst=James&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Mary&MotherSurname=&year=1795&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Baptisms of Jane (1821) and Ann (1830) Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=Benjamin&FatherSurname=&event=&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Sarah&MotherSurname=&year=1825&period=20&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="]]},
pkmarybrearley:{"n":"Mary Brearley","d":"c. 1773–1809 · Castleton, Rochdale","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The wife of James Brearley of Brimrod Lane End, Castleton, on the edge of Rochdale, and probably the mother of Sarah Brierly. The West Street Baptist register in Rochdale names her as the mother of seven children born between 1791 and 1805. Her daughter Mary, born in June 1793, apparently died young, since another daughter was given the same name in December 1796; Sarah was born between the two, on 7 November 1794, and entered at the chapel on 21 January 1795.\n\nBaptists did not baptise infants, so the chapel register records the children's births, with the parents' names and their home. If Sarah was her daughter, Mary Brearley was the grandmother of Robert Whatmough, who took his family to Port Phillip in 1841. The connection is marked as uncertain because no record states it outright.\n\nA Mary Brearley, wife of James Brearley, weaver, of Brimrod Lane End, was buried at St Chad's, Rochdale, on 29 March 1809, aged 36. She is very probably this Mary: the hamlet, the husband's name and the trade all match, and an age of 36 fits a mother whose children were born between 1791 and 1805. If so, Sarah was fourteen when her mother died. Three marriages of a James Brearley to a Mary took place at St Chad's in the 1780s, to Mary Fletcher (1784), Mary Heap (1787) and Mary Clegg (1789), so her maiden name is still open.","src":[["Births of the children of James and Mary Brearley, West Street Baptist, Rochdale, 1791-1805 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=&Surname=Brearley&FatherFirst=James&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=Mary&MotherSurname=&year=1795&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Lancashire OPC: burial of Mary Brearley, wife of James, 36, Brimrod Lane End, St Chad, Rochdale, 29 March 1809; Brearley marriages 1783-1792","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"]],"x":1809},
pkrobertwhatmough:{"n":"Robert Whatmough","d":"d. 1789 · Rochdale","x":1789,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A dyer of Rochdale, buried at St Chad's on 2 March 1789. The burial register describes him as poor and gives his home as Barns o'th Lane. On the same day the baptism register enters Benjamin, son of the late Robert Whatmough, poor, and Jane, of Yorkshire Street: his son was christened on the day of his funeral.\n\nThat Benjamin is probably the weaver Benjamin Whatmough who married Sarah Brearley in 1813 and whose eldest son, born in 1815, was named Robert. If so, the fruit grower of the Plenty River carried his grandfather's name. The link is marked as uncertain because no record names the weaver's father. A second Benjamin Whatmough in Rochdale in the 1820s, a tinner, was born about 1799 and so cannot be the child baptised in 1789.\n\nDyers worked in the town's woollen trade, colouring the flannels and baizes for which Rochdale was known.","src":[["Burial of Robert Whatmough, dyer, St Chad, Rochdale, 2 March 1789 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Robert&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=bur&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1788&period=2&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Baptism of Benjamin Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale, 2 March 1789 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Benjamin&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1785&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="]]},
pkjanewhatmough:{"n":"Jane Whatmough","d":"Rochdale","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The widow of Robert Whatmough, a dyer of Rochdale. She is named once, in the St Chad's baptism register for 2 March 1789, as the mother of Benjamin, son of the late Robert Whatmough and Jane, of Yorkshire Street. Her husband was buried at the same church that day. She was left a widow with an infant, and both entries mark the family as poor.\n\nHer maiden name is not known; no marriage of a Robert Whatmough to a Jane has been found in the Lancashire parish transcripts for the 1770s and 1780s. Her place in this tree depends on her son being the Benjamin Whatmough who married Sarah Brearley in 1813, which is likely but unproven.\n\nMarriages of men named Robert Whatmough at St Chad's, Rochdale, in 1774 (to Mally Tweedale), 1782 (Betty Beet), 1785 (Peggy Howarth) and 1786 (Betty Chadwick) are all to women of other names, so her marriage probably took place elsewhere or has not been transcribed.","src":[["Baptism of Benjamin Whatmough, St Chad, Rochdale, 2 March 1789 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Benjamin&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=bap&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1785&period=10&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Burial of Robert Whatmough, dyer, St Chad, Rochdale, 2 March 1789 (Lancashire OPC)","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/cgi-bin/search.cgi?First=Robert&Surname=Whatmough&FatherFirst=&FatherSurname=&event=bur&location=%2C%2C&MotherFirst=&MotherSurname=&year=1788&period=2&SpouseFirst=&SpouseSurname=&perpage=200%2C1&WitnessFirst=&WitnessSurname=&distance=&units=miles&parish=&type=Parish&community=&token="],["Lancashire OPC: Robert Whatmough marriages, St Chad, Rochdale, 1774-1786","https://www.lan-opc.org.uk/Search/indexp.html"]]},
hzharmabels:{"n":"Harm Abels","d":"d. by 1745 · Grootegast","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Grootegast in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. With his first wife, Anke Jacobs, he had five children baptised at Grootegast: Jacob (1730), Femke (1731), a second Jacob (1734), Antje (1737) and Geertje (1739). With his second wife, Hillje Alles, he had a son Willem, baptised there on 28 April 1743.\n\nHe died before May 1745: when his daughter Nijne was baptised at Grootegast on 27 May that year, the register notes that the mother was a widow. His son Willem was then two.\n\nThat Willem is the only one with a father named Harm in the Grootegast baptism register between 1738 and 1752, and he is the likely Willem Harms who married Lootske Wijbes at Grootegast in 1772 and named his first son Harm and a daughter Hiltje. The marriage entry of 1772 names no parents, so Harm's place in the family is likely, not proven.","src":[["Baptism of Willem, Grootegast, 28 Apr 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0e39c0ca-d46b-7a7c-4298-fa65bd812709"],["Marriage of Willem Harms, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:23cdd98c-66fc-5eab-a252-76cd8fdfbffe"],["Baptism of Jacob, 1730","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:590aaad1-b058-49e1-393e-d61f619a4002"],["Baptism of Geertje, 1739","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:574db163-c51d-1c2b-73a3-d728407f242e"],["Baptism of Nijne, 1745 (\"mother is a widow\")","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c0dce2bc-e139-3ad1-3624-00f90af1ef9d"]]},
hzhilljealles:{"n":"Hillje Alles","d":"Grootegast","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"The second wife of Harm Abels of Grootegast, who already had five children by his first wife, Anke Jacobs. Their son Willem was baptised at Grootegast on 28 April 1743. Harm died before May 1745, and on 27 May 1745 she brought their daughter Nijne to be baptised as a widow; the clerk wrote her name as Hillje Abels.\n\nWillem is the likely Willem Harms who married Lootske Wijbes in 1772; that couple named a daughter Hiltje in 1779, the same name as Hillje in another spelling, which is the main reason for placing her here. Because the marriage entry of 1772 does not name the groom's parents, the link is likely rather than proven. Her own baptism and burial have not been found.","src":[["Baptism of Willem, Grootegast, 28 Apr 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0e39c0ca-d46b-7a7c-4298-fa65bd812709"],["Baptism of granddaughter Hiltje, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4a49c5ee-c957-7f2c-4720-8ddb6323eec6"],["Baptism of Nijne, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c0dce2bc-e139-3ad1-3624-00f90af1ef9d"]]},
hzharmjacobs:{"n":"Harm Jacobs","d":"m. 1747 · Niekerk","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Niekerk in the Westerkwartier of Groningen. On 8 October 1747, described as a young man of Niekerk, he married Trijntje Gales of Grootegast in the church of Niekerk, Oldekerk and Faan. Their son Gale was baptised there on 7 January 1748, three months after the wedding, followed by Antje (1750), Baiske (1752) and Jacob (1755), named after Harm's father; the family was living at Niekerk throughout.\n\nGale is the likely Gale Harms, carpenter and shopkeeper of Niekerk, who married Eenje Jans in 1769 and named a son Harm in 1772. It is the only baptism of a Gale with a father Harm in the Groningen registers of those years, but the marriage entry of 1769 names no parents, so the link is likely rather than proven. Gale's descendants took the surname Staal.","src":[["Baptism of Gale, Niekerk, 7 Jan 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8a2fa03a-4aa2-b70b-8fb8-45528316cb3e"],["Baptism of grandson Harm, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0eb03b0c-8370-805d-6540-ceba9ed7d5b5"],["Marriage, Niekerk 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5a487ba4-9d49-099c-9e09-2c05559179ea"],["Baptism of Antje, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8b75a8ea-8eb1-d872-3b98-47847a9cbd9e"],["Baptism of Jacob, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dd5345b6-5d72-0fca-ba18-0246db168be3"]]},
hztrijntjegales:{"n":"Trijntje Gales","d":"m. 1747 · Grootegast","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She was living at Grootegast when she married Harm Jacobs of Niekerk at Niekerk on 8 October 1747. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Gale. Their son Gale, named after him, was baptised at Niekerk on 7 January 1748, and Antje (1750), Baiske (1752) and Jacob (1755) followed.\n\nGale is the likely Gale Harms who married Eenje Jans at Niekerk in 1769. He named daughters Trijntje in both his marriages, in 1779 and 1792, which fits her as his mother. The marriage entry of 1769 does not name his parents, so the link is likely rather than proven.","src":[["Baptism of Gale, Niekerk, 7 Jan 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8a2fa03a-4aa2-b70b-8fb8-45528316cb3e"],["Baptism of granddaughter Trijntje, 1779","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a193ca6f-4c27-a579-d77d-fefc6801c7dc"],["Marriage, Niekerk 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5a487ba4-9d49-099c-9e09-2c05559179ea"],["Baptism of Baiske, 1752","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d7da8f26-8ace-e3db-9896-0da5666d1c3d"]]},
hzwybemichiels:{"n":"Wybe Michiels Bander","d":"m. 1760 Drachten","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Noorderdrachten, the northern half of what is now the town of Drachten in Friesland. He married Trijntje Gurbes, also of Noorderdrachten, in the church at Drachten on 1 June 1760. Their daughter Saakje was born there on 17 August 1762.\n\nThe other children known from later records are Michiel, who died childless in October 1807, Gurbe, who died unmarried at Smallingerland in 1820 aged 49, Alle, Anne and Jetske. In the death-duty file of 1807 only Trijntje, not Wybe, is named as Michiel's parent and heir. His children used the surname Bander.","src":[["Marriage, Drachten, 1 Jun 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d4c1ad7-f046-49ed-a883-2ef7ea1a6901"],["Death of daughter Saakje, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c21f5797-f445-5b4d-99ff-580ca39379ad"],["Death duties file of son Michiel Wybes, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:67694f8e-acae-4a24-8e7d-597050f2d852"],["Death of son Gurbe Wiebes Bander, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1a5a827c-14df-f578-17ff-bee59559e4d6"]]},
hztrijntjegurbes:{"n":"Trijntje Gurbes","d":"m. 1760 Drachten","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Noorderdrachten in Friesland. She married Wybe Michiels at Drachten on 1 June 1760 and had Saakje (born 17 August 1762), Michiel, Gurbe (about 1771), Alle, Anne and Jetske. Her son Gurbe carried the name of her father.\n\nShe was still alive in October 1807, when her son Michiel died without children. The death-duty file names her as his mother and as an heir in the direct line, alongside his brothers and sisters. Her daughter Saakje had by then long been married at Oostermeer.","src":[["Marriage, Drachten, 1 Jun 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d4c1ad7-f046-49ed-a883-2ef7ea1a6901"],["Death duties file of son Michiel Wybes, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:67694f8e-acae-4a24-8e7d-597050f2d852"],["Death of son Gurbe Wiebes Bander, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1a5a827c-14df-f578-17ff-bee59559e4d6"]]},
hzwybeannes:{"n":"Wybe Annes","d":"m. 1760 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"He married Grietje Beerns at Oostermeer on 27 April 1760; both were then living in the village. The reconstruction of the old Tietjerksteradeel population, compiled from the church books and court records, places the family at Oostermeer in 1761, \"bij de Wal\" in 1763, and at Snakkerburen in 1770. The court books record that he bought a house, and he is mentioned again in 1797.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Oostermeer: Anne (22 February 1761), named after Wybe's father; Beern (20 March 1763), named after Grietje's father; Neeltje (3 February 1765); the twins Wytske and Sjoerdje (9 August 1767); Janke (11 February 1770); and Rinse (born 8 December 1772). Beern, usually written Berend, lived at Opeinde before settling at Oostermeer in 1792, married Grietje Tobias and died at Witveen in 1808. Wybe's own parents have not been found.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybe Annes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3d847d3-0b22-4f9b-809b-75022049558f"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Beern Wybes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea247357-27a3-4762-b261-be208496cf58"]]},
hzgrietjebeerns:{"n":"Grietje Beerns","d":"m. 1760 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Oostermeer, where she married Wybe Annes on 27 April 1760. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Beern, the name she gave her second son. The family lived at Oostermeer, near \"de Wal\" in 1763 and at Snakkerburen in 1770.\n\nShe had seven children baptised at Oostermeer between 1761 and 1772: Anne, Beern (20 March 1763), Neeltje, the twins Wytske and Sjoerdje, Janke and Rinse. Her own first name passed to two of Beern's daughters, born in 1793 and 1796; the second, Grietje Berends van der Meer, married Wybe Idema in 1822.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybe Annes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a3d847d3-0b22-4f9b-809b-75022049558f"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Beern Wybes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea247357-27a3-4762-b261-be208496cf58"],["Baptism of granddaughter Grietje, 1797","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9f6a0328-572a-44a3-a962-756e1faa3df3"]]},
hztobiaspieters:{"n":"Tobias Pieters","d":"b. 1740 · Oostermeer","b":1740,"s":"h","p":["hzpietertobias","hzantjeklases"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 28 February 1740, the son of Pieter Tobias and Antje Klases. He married Stijntje Rienks at Oostermeer on 1 November 1761, when both lived in that village, and in 1762 they were at 't Hoogzand, where their daughter Grietje was baptised on 14 March.\n\nThe couple then moved to Kuikhorne and had children baptised in the church of Twijzel and Kooten: Pieter in 1763, a Grietje in 1767, and Rienk and another Grietje in 1771. By 1780 he was a farmer (boer) at Veenwouden. His son Pieter was later described as sailing on a ship of the state.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8bbcf8d7-7a2c-455a-8ece-89da0716d976"],["Baptism of Pieter, Twijzel/Kooten 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0ba3e6b0-b1bd-403e-ac3f-64fd956f200a"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8048cc73-4da2-4fc3-8d44-13bfea2ff508"],["Baptism of Rienk, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b5199ddb-c634-40d3-8101-1f88ce7f7286"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: son Pieter Tobias","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:89c4387e-c0bd-4a47-b3b5-db8f592f5ee4"]]},
hzstijntjerienks:{"n":"Stijntje Rienks","d":"b. 1738 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hzrienkwarners","hzgrietjewybrens"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 9 November 1738, the daughter of Rienk Warners and Grietje Wybrens. She married Tobias Pieters there on 1 November 1761. Their daughter Grietje was baptised at Oostermeer on 14 March 1762, and after the couple moved to Kuikhorne four more baptisms followed at Twijzel: Pieter (1763), Grietje (1767), and Rienk and Grietje together in May 1771, the boy ten weeks old and the girl about a year and a half.\n\nHer son Rienk and her daughters Grietje were named after her own parents. Her names were handed on again by the Grietje who married Berend Wybes in 1791 and called her children Stijntje and Rienk. The family later farmed at Veenwouden.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Stijntje Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:18a6693e-063a-4c70-b670-19f0d2a1e615"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8bbcf8d7-7a2c-455a-8ece-89da0716d976"],["Baptism of Rienk, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b5199ddb-c634-40d3-8101-1f88ce7f7286"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c94b4932-6868-4333-bb13-cdd0c74c3a12"],["Baptism of granddaughter Styntje, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:04a617b1-2959-42ce-8a8e-005ef683fae5"]],"b":1738},
hzcornelishayes:{"n":"Cornelis Hayes","d":"m. 1744 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hzhayecornelis","hzlijsbethtjebbes"],"r":["nl"],"t":"A labourer (arbeider) at Oostermeer, the son of Haye Cornelis and Lijsbeth Tjebbes. On 3 May 1744 he married Wytske Wytses of Drogeham in the church at Oostermeer. In the tax register of 1748 he is entered beside his father, between houses 115 and 116.\n\nThe provincial tax list of 1749, which graded every household by its means, describes him as \"a labourer who cannot earn his keep\", with two adults and two children, assessed at 11 guilders and 13 stuivers. His son Johannes was born at Oostermeer in 1763 and took the surname Idema in 1811. Johannes's death record of 1849 spells the father's name Kornelis Hayes. The reconstruction records that he married again as a widower, at Oostermeer on 3 February 1782, Baukje Severijns (or Syberijns) of 't Witveen.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Cornelis Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cec3afff-aa3b-46c9-b1bd-7ffcdcc049e4"],["Tax list (quotisatie) 1749, Oostermeer","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1f153c0c-7173-466f-94c7-cb3117eb249c"],["Death of son Johannes Kornelis Iedema, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b1ff9639-0fd5-b2b5-fefd-17fe83094df7"]]},
hzwytskewytses:{"n":"Wytske Wytses","d":"m. 1744 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Drogeham in Achtkarspelen. She married the labourer Cornelis Hayes at Oostermeer on 3 May 1744. The reconstruction of the village population made from the church books names her as the mother of Haye, who married at Oostermeer in 1774, and of Johannes, born on 11 March 1763.\n\nJohannes's death record of 1849, made 85 years after his birth, calls his mother Wytske Pieters instead; the two sources disagree on her patronymic and the church books are the older evidence. Johannes named his eldest daughter Wytske in 1789. The reconstruction records her husband marrying again as a widower in February 1782, so she had died by then.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Cornelis Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cec3afff-aa3b-46c9-b1bd-7ffcdcc049e4"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Johannes Cornelis Ydema","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cba1652e-6b20-4894-87ee-70c717d38835"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Haye Cornelis, married 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:420d024e-2dee-4b0c-8447-b1c565316d20"],["Death of son Johannes Kornelis Iedema, 1849","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b1ff9639-0fd5-b2b5-fefd-17fe83094df7"]]},
hzhayecornelis:{"n":"Haye Cornelis","d":"m. 1717 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hzcornelishayessr","hzgrietjefeikes"],"u":true,"r":["nl"],"t":"A labourer (arbeider) at Oostermeer, living in 1728 at the hamlet of Witveen. On 3 January 1717 he married Lijsbeth Tjebbes at Oostermeer, where both then lived. In 1728 he was party to the sale of a ferry (overzetterij), recorded in the district court books.\n\nHis sons were Tjebbe and Cornelis. In the tax register of 1748 and 1749 his household stood between houses 115 and 116, with Cornelis beside him. The population reconstruction gives his parents as Cornelis Hayes and Grietje Feikes, who married at Oostermeer in 1688, but under that couple it lists him only as a possible child, so the link is shown as likely rather than proven.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Haye Cornelis","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ae05ba8-0571-41ab-8688-f51048ae3c08"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: son Tjebbe Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2c1f73c7-bfd5-4ebf-a783-66e259ed3c60"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: son Cornelis Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cec3afff-aa3b-46c9-b1bd-7ffcdcc049e4"]]},
hzlijsbethtjebbes:{"n":"Lijsbeth Tjebbes","d":"m. 1717 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Oostermeer. She married the labourer Haye Cornelis there on 3 January 1717, and the couple lived at the hamlet of Witveen. Her sons were Tjebbe, named after her father, and Cornelis.\n\nTjebbe married Antje Roelofs at Oostermeer in 1751 and was baptised the next year in the Mennonite congregation of Rottevalle and Witveen; the tax list of 1749 calls him a bachelor who earned his keep. He died near Oostermeer on 10 November 1795 and named a daughter Lijsbeth after her. Cornelis married in 1744 and was the father of Johannes Cornelis Idema.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lijsbeth Tjebbes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25891d31-b16a-4423-bdf3-dc7e8d0eb004"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: son Tjebbe Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2c1f73c7-bfd5-4ebf-a783-66e259ed3c60"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: son Cornelis Hayes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cec3afff-aa3b-46c9-b1bd-7ffcdcc049e4"]]},
hzcornelishayessr:{"n":"Cornelis Hayes","d":"m. 1688 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Drogeham in Achtkarspelen. On 8 April 1688 he was baptised as an adult, on profession of faith, in the church at Oostermeer, and on the same day he married Grietje Feikes of 't Witveen there. In 1689 the couple were living at Witveen, a hamlet of Oostermeer.\n\nTheir daughter Trijntje was baptised at Oostermeer on 10 February 1689. The reconstruction of the village population lists Haye Cornelis, the labourer who married there in 1717, as possibly their son; his name repeats that of Cornelis's own father, Haye. The link is likely but not proven.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Cornelis Hayes, married 1688","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:07b396bb-7291-4245-971f-7c523c3cc725"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Haye Cornelis","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ae05ba8-0571-41ab-8688-f51048ae3c08"]]},
hzgrietjefeikes:{"n":"Grietje Feikes","d":"m. 1688 Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of 't Witveen, a hamlet of Oostermeer in Tietjerksteradeel. She married Cornelis Hayes of Drogeham at Oostermeer on 8 April 1688, the day he was baptised as an adult. They lived at Witveen in 1689, and their daughter Trijntje was baptised at Oostermeer on 10 February that year.\n\nThe reconstruction of the village population names her as the mother of Haye Cornelis, the labourer who married at Oostermeer in 1717 and stayed at Witveen, but lists him only as a possible child of the couple, so the link is likely rather than proven.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Cornelis Hayes, married 1688","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:07b396bb-7291-4245-971f-7c523c3cc725"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Haye Cornelis","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ae05ba8-0571-41ab-8688-f51048ae3c08"]]},
hzpietertobias:{"n":"Pieter Tobias","d":"1697–after 1779 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":["hztobiasaebes","hzmaaikepieters"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 10 October 1697, the son of Tobias Aebes and Maaike Pieters. On 5 November 1730 he married Antje Klases, also of Oostermeer, and he made his profession of faith there in 1743. He farmed at Oostermeer, in the village itself (\"de buyren\") and on the Hoogzand; the 1749 tax assessment calls him \"a small farmer\" (een gering boer), with four adults and three children in the house.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Oostermeer: Maaike (1731), named after his mother, Klaas (1733), Jan (1735), Tobias (28 February 1740), named after his father, Aebe (1743), Fokke (1746) and Marten (1755). Klaas became a merchant at Oostermeer and lived at 't Hoogzand; Aebe farmed at Suawoude and Fokke at Suameer; Tobias was a farmer at Veenwouden by 1780; Marten farmed at Oostermeer and Augustinusga and took the name van der Meer in 1811. Pieter was still alive in 1779. Tobias named his first son Pieter after him in 1763.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Pieter Tobias (b. 1697)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1f2ecac-37cf-4173-87bc-3e45a9f3a629"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Marten Pieters van der Meer","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8c39c2a0-2dfc-4ca6-a6cd-c5e7efe2582a"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8bbcf8d7-7a2c-455a-8ece-89da0716d976"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:22b30a3e-8122-42d4-bf49-0361591c3856"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Fokke Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d1440f7-6ba9-4745-82b5-8ce8eedb92b9"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Aebe Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:859f7ea0-d257-4932-be1f-bcedbbed123d"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Maaike Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5584f69a-1b68-4c45-932d-f8f32a290b1b"]],"b":1697},
hzantjeklases:{"n":"Antje Klases","d":"m. 1730 · Oostermeer","s":"h","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Oostermeer, where she married Pieter Tobias, a farmer, on 5 November 1730. Their children were baptised in the village church between 1731 and 1755: Maaike, Klaas, Jan, Tobias, Aebe, Fokke and Marten. Her eldest son Klaas carried her father's name.\n\nMaaike married Dirk Djoerds of Twijzel in 1757. Klaas, a merchant, married in 1768 and died at Oostermeer in 1811 aged 78; he and Fokke each named a daughter Antje after her. Tobias, baptised on 28 February 1740, married Stijntje Rienks in 1761 and was the father of Grietje Tobias. Marten, the youngest, farmed at Oostermeer and Augustinusga and died at Drogeham in 1819.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Pieter Tobias (b. 1697)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1f2ecac-37cf-4173-87bc-3e45a9f3a629"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:22b30a3e-8122-42d4-bf49-0361591c3856"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Maaike Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5584f69a-1b68-4c45-932d-f8f32a290b1b"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Fokke Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d1440f7-6ba9-4745-82b5-8ce8eedb92b9"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Pieters","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8bbcf8d7-7a2c-455a-8ece-89da0716d976"]]},
grpietertjaarts:{"n":"Pieter Tjaarts","d":"m. 1733 and 1743 · Oostwold","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"A man of Oostwold, a village on the edge of the Oldambt polders beside Midwolda. He married Antje Jurjens in the church there on 13 May 1733, and they had four children baptised at Oostwold: Geertje (1734), Tjaart (1736), Wieke (1737) and Geesje (1740). His second name shows that his own father was called Tjaart.\n\nOn 24 November 1743 he married again at Oostwold, to Eijse (Eise) Thijes. With her he had Hindrikje, baptised on 1 April 1745, and a second Tjaart, baptised on 4 December 1746. The name had already been given to his son of 1736; no burial of that first Tjaart has been found.\n\nHis daughter Geesje married Teije Meinderts and lived at Oostwold until January 1829, when she died aged 88. The younger Tjaart became a labourer, married Wupke Roelfs at Finsterwolde in 1783 and died at Oostwold in November 1812, aged 66, an age that fits the baptism of 1746. He named his first daughter Eisse, after his mother.","src":[["Marriage to Antje Jurjens, Oostwold 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6fc4c4d7-729f-7510-f387-ef54d9319570"],["Baptism of Geertje, 1734","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7bc11be8-f65b-39ca-e4bd-525ed04c6457"],["Baptism of the first Tjaart, 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8c6bc504-3571-58ed-7ec0-f0cd97879283"],["Baptism of Wieke, 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:26475ec5-edbf-ab2b-d767-563807b04b83"],["Baptism of Geesje, 1740","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:58f0d21f-f67a-876a-ab54-61846a51075e"],["Baptism of Hindrikje, 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4db8c085-83a8-c357-6812-4ef6b9ca3c08"],["Baptism of Tjaart, 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83c221f4-283f-ad04-9bc3-123c78f80bc7"],["Death of Geesje Pieters, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:70e6a4c4-3069-0ff4-0bb0-ae6bae749adf"],["Second marriage, to Eijse Thijes, Oostwold 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b0f420bf-31c6-068c-b9ff-ebf0c93d151a"]]},
greisethijes:{"n":"Eise Thijes","d":"m. 1743 · Oostwold","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Pieter Tjaarts of Oostwold in the church there on 24 November 1743; the marriage entry writes her name Eijse Thijes. Her second name means daughter of Thije. Pieter already had four children by his first wife, Antje Jurjens, the youngest of them, Geesje, then three years old.\n\nTwo children of her own are entered in the Oostwold baptism register: Hindrikje on 1 April 1745 and Tjaart on 4 December 1746. Her first name, written Eise, Eisse or Aijsse, was passed down: her son Tjaart called his first daughter Eisse in 1784, and that Eisse married Hindrik Harms Broesder and lived to 96. Through her son Tjaart, who carried the name of Pieter's father, the name Tjaard came into the Broesder family, which has used it in almost every generation since.","src":[["Baptism of Hindrikje, Oostwold 1745","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4db8c085-83a8-c357-6812-4ef6b9ca3c08"],["Baptism of Tjaart, Oostwold 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:83c221f4-283f-ad04-9bc3-123c78f80bc7"],["Second marriage, to Eijse Thijes, Oostwold 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b0f420bf-31c6-068c-b9ff-ebf0c93d151a"]]},
grroelfeernst:{"n":"Roelf Eernst","d":"b. 1729 or 1733 · Finsterwolde","b":1733,"s":"b","p":["grernstroelfs","grwibkeelsen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Finsterwolde in the Oldambt. He married Grietje Eilderts there on 29 May 1757. His second name means son of Eernst, and the only man of that name in the Finsterwolde registers of the time, Eernst (Ernst) Roelfs, had two sons baptised with his name: Roelf on 11 September 1729 and Roelof on 11 October 1733. The register does not show which of them grew up to marry. Roelf named his first daughter Wupke, a form of the name of Eernst's wife, Wibke.\n\nWupke was baptised on 4 May 1758, and daughters named Jantje followed in December 1759 and September 1764; at the second baptism the family was living 'by the Oosterdijk'. Finsterwolde lies on the old shore of the Dollard, and each new polder won from the sea left another dyke behind it. Wupke was later given the surname Dijk.\n\nA Roelf Eernst was still having children baptised at Finsterwolde in 1781 and 1784, Eike and Eernst, with a wife named Geeske Thomas. The registers do not show whether he was the same man.","src":[["Baptism of Roelf, Finsterwolde 1729","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ccf98760-933a-1293-c86e-be3965e6a9ab"],["Baptism of Roelof, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b13acd90-9b10-8f13-9fb3-3f53c90a493d"],["Burial of a child of Ernst Roelfs, 1732","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f95a1352-952c-578a-2bf5-28a0073d9da5"],["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:35970d09-4f9c-f0a1-0c38-8490fae37d04"],["Baptism of Wupke, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aa5dd05f-e70c-79e8-ca5d-2db06cc5c5cb"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5692c804-b155-272e-d26d-957c0928eea2"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29021dfb-218b-6b2e-f2ad-090034637876"],["Baptism of Eernst, son of Roelf Eernst and Geeske Thomas, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec53f7a8-67ee-4931-af0e-677c170224ff"]]},
grgrietjeeilderts:{"n":"Grietje Eilderts","d":"m. 1757 · Finsterwolde","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Roelf Eernst at Finsterwolde on 29 May 1757. Her second name means daughter of Eildert. Three baptisms of their children are entered in the Finsterwolde register: Wupke on 4 May 1758, Jantje on 23 December 1759, when the clerk wrote her name Grietje Eilders, and another Jantje on 23 September 1764, when the family lived by the Oosterdijk.\n\nHer daughter Wupke married the labourer Tjaart Pieters in 1783 and gave the name Grietje to her second daughter, born in 1789. No burial of Grietje Eilderts has been found. By 1781 a Roelf Eernst at Finsterwolde had a wife named Geeske Thomas; the registers do not show whether he was her husband.","src":[["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:35970d09-4f9c-f0a1-0c38-8490fae37d04"],["Baptism of Wupke, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aa5dd05f-e70c-79e8-ca5d-2db06cc5c5cb"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5692c804-b155-272e-d26d-957c0928eea2"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:29021dfb-218b-6b2e-f2ad-090034637876"],["Baptism of Eike, daughter of Roelf Eernst and Geeske Thomas, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f77db9fd-a899-d9ce-dc56-1c6086590887"]]},
grernstroelfs:{"n":"Eernst Roelfs","d":"Finsterwolde, 1728–1735","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"A man of Finsterwolde in the Oldambt; the register writes his name both Eernst and Ernst Roelfs. With his wife Wibke, called Wibke Elsen in 1735, he had five children baptised at Finsterwolde: Antje (22 August 1728), Roelf (11 September 1729), a second Antje (21 October 1731), Roelof (11 October 1733) and Else (7 August 1735). Two of their children were buried, in September 1728 and December 1732; the register does not give their names.\n\nAn Eernst Roelfs and his wife Frouwe buried three unnamed children at Finsterwolde, in February 1724, April 1725 and May 1726. No other man of that name appears in the village registers of those years, but no record shows whether this was the same man with an earlier wife.\n\nHis son Roelf, or Roelof, married Grietje Eilderts in 1757 and named his first daughter Wupke, a form of Wibke.","src":[["Burial of a child, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:387708d4-8f24-b374-6bf5-96d9ae6df341"],["Burial of a child, 1725","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8f5f2704-3d6d-20e8-7c12-a9163b2bd418"],["Burial of a child, 1726","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7099d348-1619-06a3-e466-b69374cc0cad"],["Baptism of Antje, 1728","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bb83d3df-49ac-e49f-628d-9a4e0bf9f9a9"],["Burial of a child, 1728","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:52c355cc-f28e-cb60-ea13-c6c0b643b2a3"],["Baptism of Roelf, 1729","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ccf98760-933a-1293-c86e-be3965e6a9ab"],["Baptism of Antje, 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e0063ae5-bd24-8d49-0f0b-597294b0148a"],["Burial of a child, 1732","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f95a1352-952c-578a-2bf5-28a0073d9da5"],["Baptism of Roelof, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b13acd90-9b10-8f13-9fb3-3f53c90a493d"],["Baptism of Else, 1735","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0383fc40-2bb0-3a08-2c68-5075cb2812e5"]]},
grwibkeelsen:{"n":"Wibke Elsen","d":"Finsterwolde, 1720s–1730s","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Eernst Roelfs of Finsterwolde. The register usually calls her simply Wibke; at the baptism of her last known child, in 1735, she is Wibke Elsen, daughter of Else, and the baby was named Else.\n\nShe had five children baptised at Finsterwolde between 1728 and 1735: Antje, Roelf, a second Antje, Roelof and Else. Two of her children were buried there as infants, in September 1728 and December 1732. Her son Roelf married Grietje Eilderts in 1757 and named his first daughter Wupke, a form of her name. That granddaughter, Wupke Roelfs Dijk, lived until 1830.","src":[["Baptism of Antje, 1728","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bb83d3df-49ac-e49f-628d-9a4e0bf9f9a9"],["Baptism of Roelf, 1729","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ccf98760-933a-1293-c86e-be3965e6a9ab"],["Baptism of Roelof, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b13acd90-9b10-8f13-9fb3-3f53c90a493d"],["Baptism of Else, 1735 (mother Wibke Elsen)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0383fc40-2bb0-3a08-2c68-5075cb2812e5"]]},
grstoffertoorman:{"n":"Stoffer Toorman","d":"m. 1730 · Termunten","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"He married Engel Abrahams at Termunten, on the Dollard coast of Groningen, on 10 December 1730. The clerk wrote his name Toorma; later entries have Toorman and Toormans, and his descendants Toornman. Their son Abraham was baptised on Christmas Day 1732 and their daughter Trijntje on 19 June 1735; both entries give the birthplace as 'de Holm' in the parish. Two more children were baptised on 7 April 1743 and 23 April 1747 without their names being entered.\n\nNo burial for him has been found; by September 1755 Engel was contracting to marry again. Gepke Stoffers, who married at Wagenborgen in 1778 with Wolter at her side, is placed in the tree as his daughter, and Wolter Stoffers Toorman, who married at Nieuwolda in 1765 and named a son Stoffer, fits as a son; no baptism names either of them. A Jan Stoffers Toorman of Hellum, whose own son Stoffer was born about 1749, is too old to be a child of the 1730 marriage; how he was related has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Termunten 1730","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6b2a439c-b4b6-86be-06b8-8a3e9554fd21"],["Baptism of Abraham, 1732","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cd15089e-f29a-2614-f00c-930d03a7a34b"],["Baptism of Trijntje, 1735","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7ad5d7c8-6649-5d5b-ca26-4feeece92528"],["Baptism of an unnamed child, 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:086f11f3-8f74-cbaf-efca-c15c621ace21"],["Baptism of an unnamed child, 1747","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:92b9def2-abe1-edc4-ec51-3f31e7ba8861"],["Marriage contract of Engel Abrahams, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:04c5a3cf-980a-97c9-49f2-b7b0f494082b"],["Marriage of Wolter Stoffers, Nieuwolda 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:893fe785-98eb-79bc-b64b-db9aca4c4d2a"],["Marriage contract of Gepke Stoffers, 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3cf14616-8c41-6565-6832-95b5c4329958"],["Death of Stoffer Jans Toorman, Hellum 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:74149852-8ce7-fadc-a544-38090ad10dd9"]]},
grengelabrahams:{"n":"Engel Abrahams","d":"m. 1730 · Termunten","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Stoffer Toorman at Termunten on 10 December 1730 and was received as a member of the Termunten church on 6 June 1732, where the entry mentions her husband. Engel is here a woman's name. Her children were baptised at Termunten: Abraham, who had her father's name, at Christmas 1732, Trijntje in June 1735, and two whose names were not written down, in April 1743 and April 1747.\n\nOn 26 September 1755 she signed a contract to marry Hendrik Jans; the deed is filed under Oostwold. Jan Toorman and Derk Abrahams stood on her side. Her name lived on: Gepke Stoffers, placed in the tree as her daughter, called her daughter Engel in 1784, and Gepke's son Jan called a daughter Engeltje in 1821.","src":[["Marriage, Termunten 1730","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6b2a439c-b4b6-86be-06b8-8a3e9554fd21"],["Church membership, Termunten 1732","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6601a451-c846-4364-9e78-53fff61314dd"],["Baptism of Abraham, 1732","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cd15089e-f29a-2614-f00c-930d03a7a34b"],["Baptism of Trijntje, 1735","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:7ad5d7c8-6649-5d5b-ca26-4feeece92528"],["Marriage contract with Hendrik Jans, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:04c5a3cf-980a-97c9-49f2-b7b0f494082b"],["Baptism of Engel, daughter of Gepke Stoffers, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3eba7645-a6a8-cd36-83b9-a32788b89102"]]},
greildertockes:{"n":"Eildert Ockes","d":"m. 1736 · Eexta","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Eexta, a hamlet beside Scheemda in the Oldambt. He married Anneke (Anke) Sijwerts in the church at Eexta on 18 November 1736. Their children were Sijwert, baptised at Scheemda in October 1737 with the name of her father, Occo (born 26 March 1740), with the name of his, Griete (1742), Rienje (1749) and Sijwertjen (1751), all baptised at Eexta.\n\nOn 30 November 1764 he stood on the bridegroom's side when his son Ocko signed a marriage contract with Anje Derks of Noordbroek; Anke Sebes and Grietje Eilders stood with him. Ocko became a shoemaker, and his descendants took the surnames Eekstra and Eexterhof from the village. The burial of an Eildert Okkes is entered at Scheemda on 7 April 1788.","src":[["Marriage, Eexta 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483fd4eb-3d4b-7da0-58f0-008fdbfbb04d"],["Baptism of Sijwert, Scheemda 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e9e986e-624d-3a3e-ec7e-1a90db7ad78b"],["Baptism of Occo, Eexta 1740","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6819a111-d25b-7a54-c2f0-bbed7d211cba"],["Baptism of Griete, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3f34a6f7-43f8-ae65-949e-d7bebd4c4ff6"],["Baptism of Rienje, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5160814a-bbeb-33e7-9224-0b1d11bafb3e"],["Baptism of Sijwertjen, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e037263d-bf31-cc05-f3e2-f98cbcaa5942"],["Marriage contract of Ocko, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:430190a7-1a23-8a90-322b-a39099e417a4"],["Burial of an Eildert Okkes, Scheemda 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8d89a3ab-12bb-e379-f89b-2f14bba4b361"]]},
grannekesijwerts:{"n":"Anneke Sijwerts","d":"m. 1736 · Eexta","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Eildert Ockes at Eexta, near Scheemda, on 18 November 1736; the marriage entry calls her Anke. Her second name means daughter of Sijwert, and her first son, baptised at Scheemda on 6 October 1737, was named Sijwert. Four more children were baptised at Eexta: Occo in March 1740, Griete in October 1742, Rienje in July 1749 and Sijwertjen in November 1751.\n\nHer son Occo, or Ocko, married in 1764 and called his first daughter Anneke in 1766. He also gave two of his daughters the name of his sister Rienje. Her granddaughter Aaltje Okkes Eekstra married into the Lewe family of Wagenborgen.","src":[["Marriage, Eexta 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:483fd4eb-3d4b-7da0-58f0-008fdbfbb04d"],["Baptism of Sijwert, Scheemda 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:2e9e986e-624d-3a3e-ec7e-1a90db7ad78b"],["Baptism of Occo, Eexta 1740","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6819a111-d25b-7a54-c2f0-bbed7d211cba"],["Baptism of Rienje, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5160814a-bbeb-33e7-9224-0b1d11bafb3e"],["Baptism of Anneke, daughter of Ocko, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:57426d37-8238-d7a7-4f79-035e8e379b12"]]},
grderkhindriks:{"n":"Derk Hindriks","d":"m. 1742 · Noordbroek","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Noordbroek, a village on the western edge of the Oldambt. He married Aeltjen Jakobs there on 2 December 1742, and their daughter Anje was baptised on 28 November 1744. A Derk Hindriks was received as a member of the Noordbroek church in June 1744.\n\nIn March 1751 Albertjen Jans was entered as a new church member with his name beside hers, and she stood with him on the bride's side in November 1764, when Anje married the shoemaker Ocko Eijlderts of Eexta. On 31 March 1768 he signed a contract to marry Claaske Cornellis, who came with an attestation from Warfhuizen. Ocko and Anje stood on his side of that contract, with Jacob Fockes and Aaltjen Gerrijts, who had also stood by Anje in 1764. In 1781 his daughter and son-in-law moved back to Noordbroek.","src":[["Marriage, Noordbroek 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aaf62201-e10b-12be-87dc-44fe731303fe"],["Baptism of Anje, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:be74ba11-1c01-ceff-c107-68d4efb6b7af"],["Church membership, Noordbroek 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a457aeb2-1ada-41b1-9691-bc1c00323b13"],["Church membership of Albertjen Jans, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:914c72ba-eec1-4ae5-b27e-8b5936ccb787"],["Marriage contract of Anje, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:430190a7-1a23-8a90-322b-a39099e417a4"],["Marriage contract with Claaske Cornellis, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48cc8858-a296-7865-4a6e-9c00f089dee7"],["Church membership of Claaske Cornellis, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d00a3e38-0229-4655-96e1-8e9e8f3341e2"]]},
graeltjenjakobs:{"n":"Aeltjen Jakobs","d":"m. 1742 · Noordbroek","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"The wife of Derk Hindriks of Noordbroek, whom she married there on 2 December 1742. Their daughter Anje was baptised at Noordbroek on 28 November 1744. No later record of Aeltjen has been found, and in March 1751 another woman, Albertjen Jans, is entered beside Derk's name in the church roll.\n\nWhen Anje married in 1764, Jacob Fockes, Aeltjen Gerrijts, Hindrick Jacobs and Focko Jacobs stood on the bride's side, and the same four stood by Derk Hindriks at his marriage contract of 1768. Two of them carried the second name Jacobs, as Aeltjen did, but no record states how they were related to her. Anje gave the name Aaltjen to three of her daughters; the third, baptised in 1782, lived until 1864.","src":[["Marriage, Noordbroek 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aaf62201-e10b-12be-87dc-44fe731303fe"],["Baptism of Anje, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:be74ba11-1c01-ceff-c107-68d4efb6b7af"],["Marriage contract of Anje, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:430190a7-1a23-8a90-322b-a39099e417a4"],["Marriage contract of Derk Hindriks, 1768","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48cc8858-a296-7865-4a6e-9c00f089dee7"]]},
grfreerkfreerks:{"n":"Freerk Freerks","d":"Nieuw-Beerta, 1740s–1750s","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Nieuw-Beerta, a village on the polders near Finsterwolde, on land won from the Dollard. With his wife Geeske Wessels he had five children baptised at Nieuw-Beerta: Wessel (22 March 1748), Freerk (15 November 1750), a second Freerk (1 October 1752), a second Wessel (5 October 1755) and Antje (4 March 1759). The entry of 1752 carries the note 'Freerk Freerks de jonge' (the younger), and an Aaldrik, son of a Freerk Freerks, was baptised there in 1746 without his mother's name, so more than one man of that name lived in the village.\n\nThe Wessel baptised in 1755 married Janna Jurjens at Finsterwolde in 1779 and became a carter there; he was buried in 1811 aged 55, which fits that baptism. His sons took the surname Plat.","src":[["Baptism of Wessel, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bd5cfc62-be17-6d00-7def-2544ecc7d983"],["Baptism of Freerk, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f6d689c8-3e1e-3f52-f227-578b915a392a"],["Baptism of Freerk, 1752 (Freerk Freerks de jonge)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:eeda9d71-1983-b316-eccc-69d3fd3621b0"],["Baptism of Wessel, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7c18e8d-45b8-a856-8ff4-0abf02eecf23"],["Baptism of Antje, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:05129ea4-3868-9b50-7e9b-5c2c75d02f61"],["Baptism of an Aaldrik, son of Freerk Freerks, Nieuw-Beerta 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:82b72aa2-91ec-0874-7a39-2e16b5b69722"]]},
grgeeskewessels:{"n":"Geeske Wessels","d":"Nieuw-Beerta, 1740s–1750s","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Freerk Freerks of Nieuw-Beerta. Her second name means daughter of Wessel, and she gave her father's name to her first son in 1748 and again to a son in 1755.\n\nFive of her children were baptised at Nieuw-Beerta in eleven years: Wessel (1748), Freerk (1750), Freerk (1752), Wessel (1755) and Antje (1759). The Wessel born in 1755 lived to grow up. He married Janna Jurjens at Finsterwolde in 1779, worked as a carter and was buried there in 1811. Through him she was the great-grandmother of Helena Plat, who married the tailor Harm Hulzebosch.","src":[["Baptism of Wessel, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bd5cfc62-be17-6d00-7def-2544ecc7d983"],["Baptism of Wessel, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e7c18e8d-45b8-a856-8ff4-0abf02eecf23"],["Baptism of Antje, 1759","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:05129ea4-3868-9b50-7e9b-5c2c75d02f61"]]},
grjurrienharms:{"n":"Jurrien Harms","d":"m. 1750 · Finsterwolde","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"He married Leentje Harms at Finsterwolde on 15 November 1750. Four children were baptised there: Janna (17 October 1751), a second Janna (12 August 1753), Harm (5 October 1755) and Willemke (9 July 1758). His son had the name of his own father, as his second name Harms shows; his wife's father was a Harm too.\n\nTwo women named Janna Jurjens married at Finsterwolde within a year of each other: one married the carter Wessel Freerks in 1779, the other Daniel Daniels van Barenburg in 1780. The tree places the first as his daughter, because she named her first children Leentje (1782) and Jurjen (1784), the names of Jurrien and his wife; no record names her parents, so the link is marked unproven. Her son Jurjen Wessels Plat became a carter at Finsterwolde.","src":[["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:469974a3-3b53-9760-585f-0ff4843deeeb"],["Baptism of Janna, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95cb3a4e-d290-a3a1-e536-31e6c4e49455"],["Baptism of Janna, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:76cd7842-c27c-8d47-d47d-2eec5db3390a"],["Baptism of Harm, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:424b69a8-7691-10e9-367f-1cf92e852049"],["Baptism of Willemke, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:3b7f8acb-25dd-2caa-44a2-d0c5d927670e"],["Baptism of Jurjen, son of Janna, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ed0c5a25-d600-7553-a3d6-221277174ed5"],["Marriage of the other Janna Jurjens, Finsterwolde 1780","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5bcab68b-d6d1-79f8-2d3d-52e7aaa3601f"]]},
grleentjeharms:{"n":"Leentje Harms","d":"m. 1750 · Finsterwolde","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Jurrien Harms at Finsterwolde on 15 November 1750 and had four children baptised there: two daughters named Janna, in 1751 and 1753, a son Harm in 1755 and a daughter Willemke in 1758.\n\nLeentje is a pet form of Helena. The Janna Jurjens who married the carter Wessel Freerks, placed in the tree as her daughter though no record names Janna's parents, called her first child Leentje in February 1782. The name came back in full a generation later, when Janna's son Aaldrik Plat named his eldest daughter Helena in 1813. That Helena Plat married the tailor Harm Hulzebosch and was the grandmother of the baker Tjaard Broesder.","src":[["Marriage, Finsterwolde 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:469974a3-3b53-9760-585f-0ff4843deeeb"],["Baptism of Janna, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95cb3a4e-d290-a3a1-e536-31e6c4e49455"],["Baptism of Janna, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:76cd7842-c27c-8d47-d47d-2eec5db3390a"],["Baptism of Leentje, daughter of Janna, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:95926430-aed7-b57b-db9f-f07a9bafd1d8"]]},
grpeterderks:{"n":"Peter Derks","d":"m. 1760 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Sappemeer, the canal village in the Groningen peat colonies. He married Grietje Jans, also of Sappemeer, in the church there on 2 November 1760, and their first child, Derk, was baptised the same day.\n\nSix more children were baptised at Sappemeer: Jannes (1763), Harm (1765), Pieter (1767), a second Pieter (1769), Grietje (1773) and Sijger (1777). Derk, the eldest, had the name of Peter's own father, as his second name shows. He became a carpenter at Sappemeer and took the surname Hund, later Hundt, which the family has carried since. When Derk died in 1843, aged 82, the clerk recorded his parents as Pieter Derks and Grietje Jans.","src":[["Marriage, Sappemeer 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666540c1-d7f7-dd60-64d7-39253f817ac0"],["Baptism of Derk, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b965e63b-9f52-0c44-9836-b934f8877586"],["Baptism of Jannes, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:5b5e6aae-6dd8-188c-1dab-7f38afe7aa9d"],["Baptism of Harm, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:1d0f6501-9ca5-aba5-7810-944dc5034f01"],["Baptism of Pieter, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:26f44cb5-5241-f4d8-2f19-4e9019f7e4ee"],["Baptism of Pieter, 1769","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:c0314b3f-bd03-2f49-221e-d352e00703eb"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cce81643-df4f-fcf7-5121-7c02405a7676"],["Baptism of Sijger, 1777","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:06e8ac05-12b3-4bb9-7aa1-bd3e9d77a8f8"],["Death of Derk Pieters Hund, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:64740e5b-0bfe-ae68-0e2f-fbb6518f852f"]]},
grgrietjejans:{"n":"Grietje Jans","d":"m. 1760 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Sappemeer. She married Peter Derks there on 2 November 1760, and their son Derk was baptised in the same church on the same day. She had six more children baptised at Sappemeer over the next seventeen years: Jannes (October 1763), Harm (May 1765), Pieter (April 1767), another Pieter (May 1769), Grietje (October 1773), who was given her own name, and Sijger (October 1777).\n\nHer eldest son, Derk Pieters Hund, became a carpenter, married an innkeeper's daughter in 1793 and called his own first child Grietje the following year. She is named as his mother in his death record of 1843.","src":[["Marriage, Sappemeer 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:666540c1-d7f7-dd60-64d7-39253f817ac0"],["Baptism of Derk, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b965e63b-9f52-0c44-9836-b934f8877586"],["Baptism of Grietje, 1773","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:cce81643-df4f-fcf7-5121-7c02405a7676"],["Baptism of her granddaughter Grietje, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:644ddf22-0868-d258-c632-fc750af39f52"],["Death of Derk Pieters Hund, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:64740e5b-0bfe-ae68-0e2f-fbb6518f852f"]]},
grluejocchums:{"n":"Lue Jocchums","d":"m. 1743 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"He married Aaltje Alberts at Sappemeer on 2 December 1743. Sappemeer was then a peat colony, a long line of houses on a canal dug to carry the turf to the city of Groningen. Two baptisms of their children are entered there: Gepke on 1 November 1744 and Albert on 14 February 1751.\n\nAlbert married a woman from Borgercompagnie in 1777 and named his first son Luije the next year. That grandson, known as Lue Alberts Gernaat, was a labourer at Windeweer and died in 1835. The family took the surname Gernaat in 1811.","src":[["Marriage, Sappemeer 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:faee91a3-70ba-02c7-a57d-b4357dc15221"],["Baptism of Gepke, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b3e826a5-d273-3d49-547f-857681bda2df"],["Baptism of Albert, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4126efe7-da67-8d9c-752b-2f7da59f3ceb"],["Baptism of Luije, Veendam 1778","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4594bdad-a598-bb99-5592-0eaeb20dac20"],["Death of Lue Alberts Gernaat, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:e28c1184-0f16-fcf3-30d5-2aca33dd9aa8"]]},
graaltjealberts:{"n":"Aaltje Alberts","d":"m. 1743 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"She married Lue Jocchums at Sappemeer on 2 December 1743; the entry writes her name Aaltie Albert. Their daughter Gepke was baptised there on 1 November 1744 and their son Albert, who was given her father's name, on 14 February 1751.\n\nAlbert grew up to be a labourer in the peat colonies at Borgercompagnie and Windeweer. He married in 1777 and had nine children, and he passed her name to his eldest daughter, Aaltje, baptised at Windeweer in December 1789. That Aaltje Alberts Garnaat was a maid of 27 when she married a widowed labourer at Windeweer in 1817.","src":[["Marriage, Sappemeer 1743","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:faee91a3-70ba-02c7-a57d-b4357dc15221"],["Baptism of Gepke, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:b3e826a5-d273-3d49-547f-857681bda2df"],["Baptism of Albert, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:4126efe7-da67-8d9c-752b-2f7da59f3ceb"],["Baptism of Aaltje, Windeweer 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d9904d21-d01c-3c64-583e-d36b210168e6"],["Marriage of Aaltje Alberts Garnaat, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0df728e8-69c1-66e0-005d-c9e79894bcba"]]},
grjacobharmsijzer:{"n":"Jacob Harms IJzer","d":"c. 1750–1818 · Oudeschans","b":1750,"x":1818,"s":"b","p":["harmijzer","antjejans"],"r":["nl"],"t":"The eldest known child of the day labourer Harm Jacobs IJzer of Oudeschans, born about 1750; his death record names his parents as Harm Jacobs IJzer and Antje Boeles, the woman the baptism register calls Antje Jans. Three younger children of his parents, Dorothea, Boele and Jochem, were baptised at Oudeschans between 1754 and 1759.\n\nHe married Janna Filippus, who came from Bunde, just over the border in East Frisia, and was a few years older than he was. Their daughter Antje, who had his mother's name, was born on 12 April 1776 and baptised at Oudeschans nine days later; the register writes the parents as Jacob Harms and Janna Filips.\n\nStijntje Jacobs IJzer, born about 1780, who married the tailor Jan Joosten Hulzebos at Oudeschans in 1800, is placed in the tree as their daughter, but this is unproven: her own death record names Harm Jacobs IJzer and Antje Jans as her parents. Her second name, Jacobs, and the names she gave her children, Jacob and Janna, are the reasons for placing her here.\n\nJanna died at Oudeschans on 13 December 1817, aged 73. He died there three months later, on 22 March 1818, aged 68, and the clerk wrote his wife's name as Janna Boeles.","src":[["Death record, Oudeschans 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec9b91e7-ef02-11dd-00aa-6a7c80860995"],["Death of Janna Filippus, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"],["Baptism of Antje, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f3e31962-2620-9856-d62d-4a7bfe4fe01b"],["Death of Stijntje Jacobs IJzer, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:de197a07-af33-80b5-d0c4-c02a4d6ece07"]]},
grjannafilippus:{"n":"Janna Filippus","d":"c. 1744–1817 · Bunde","b":1744,"x":1817,"s":"b","p":["grfilippusbartelts","grstijntjejans"],"r":["de","nl"],"t":"Born at Bunde, a village in the Rheiderland of East Frisia, about 1744, the daughter of the day labourer Filippus Bartelts and Stijntje Jans. She crossed the border to Oudeschans and married Jacob Harms IJzer, a labourer's son of the fortress village.\n\nTheir daughter Antje was born on 12 April 1776 and baptised at Oudeschans, where the register writes the mother's name Janna Filips. Stijntje Jacobs IJzer, born about 1780 and placed in the tree as their second daughter, though her death record names other parents, had the name of Janna's mother. She married the tailor Jan Joosten Hulzebos in 1800 and gave the name Janna to daughters born in 1810 and 1819.\n\nJanna died at Oudeschans on 13 December 1817, aged 73, and her husband the following March. The Janna born in 1810 died at two in 1812, and the second baby of that name lived only eighteen days in 1819.","src":[["Death record, Oudeschans 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"],["Baptism of Antje, 1776","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:f3e31962-2620-9856-d62d-4a7bfe4fe01b"],["Death of Jacob Harms IJzer, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ec9b91e7-ef02-11dd-00aa-6a7c80860995"],["Death of Janna Jans Hulsebosch, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:46d8b5a3-81f9-3d23-ea26-46a3b46a2eca"],["Death of baby Janna, 1819","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:307840e8-04ee-6711-4188-cbb91b456487"]]},
grfilippusbartelts:{"n":"Filippus Bartelts","d":"day labourer, Bunde","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["de"],"t":"A day labourer (dagloner) at Bunde, in the Rheiderland of East Frisia, a few kilometres from the Dutch border. He and his wife Stijntje Jans are known from one Dutch record: the death entry of their daughter Janna Filippus, who was born at Bunde about 1744 and died at Oudeschans in December 1817, aged 73.\n\nJanna crossed the border to marry Jacob Harms IJzer, a labourer's son of the fortress village of Oudeschans, and had a daughter Antje there in 1776. Her husband died at Oudeschans in March 1818, three months after her.","src":[["Death of Janna Filippus, Oudeschans 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"]]},
grstijntjejans:{"n":"Stijntje Jans","d":"Bunde, East Frisia","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["de"],"t":"Wife of the day labourer Filippus Bartelts of Bunde, in the Rheiderland of East Frisia, and mother of Janna Filippus, who was born there about 1744. She is named in Janna's death record at Oudeschans in 1817.\n\nHer first name crossed the border with her daughter. Stijntje Jacobs IJzer, born at Oudeschans about 1780 and placed in the tree as Janna's daughter, carried it; she married the tailor Jan Joosten Hulzebos in 1800 and died in April 1819, aged 39. Grandmothers' names were commonly given to granddaughters in Groningen families of the time, and this name is one of the reasons the younger Stijntje is placed with Jacob and Janna, although her death record names other parents.","src":[["Death of Janna Filippus, Oudeschans 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:19c48a17-a607-6535-c685-c1a840b37a4e"]]},
grcornelistonnis:{"n":"Cornelis Tonnis","d":"Nieuwe Pekela, 1750s–1760s","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Of Nieuwe Pekela, one of the two canal villages of the Pekela peat colony. The register writes his first name Cornelis, Cornellis and Knellis. With his wife Wijke Lammerts he had nine children baptised there over sixteen years: Lammert (1751), Geesje (1752), Geessijn (1755), Tjaakje (1756), a second Lammert (1758), Jantje (born 28 September 1760), Elsijn (1763), Tonnis (1765), who had the name of his own father, and Abel (1767).\n\nJantje married the carpenter Derk Eppes at Nieuwe Pekela in 1784 and called her first son Kornelius in 1787. In the civil records after 1811 she is Jantje Kornelis Bakker.","src":[["Baptism of Lammert, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:87abb640-1a3d-8c94-deb9-aa1f8d355517"],["Baptism of Geesje, 1752","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d58ce90f-eb3a-bcd7-6d76-b4da2f9cdb70"],["Baptism of Geessijn, 1755","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:336483df-46b6-8132-9f15-b6abc72514a8"],["Baptism of Tjaakje, 1756","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d9ec1bce-ad6e-aaf4-9ab9-676f97d6f364"],["Baptism of Lammert, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fe376a7f-6072-8321-9984-f584e0108191"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:90892207-57cb-33b6-b40a-35e944a9a477"],["Baptism of Elsijn, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d82bf075-f1f2-4f7b-a9e3-40c6a136023a"],["Baptism of Tonnis, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:48e2cf5e-d51f-07e2-92b7-7bba68e20090"],["Baptism of Abel, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d348d189-6c63-fad0-4d14-537a4ea3fee3"]]},
grwijkelammerts:{"n":"Wijke Lammerts","d":"Nieuwe Pekela, 1750s–1760s","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wife of Cornelis Tonnis of Nieuwe Pekela. The register spells her name Wijcke, Wijke and Wija. She had nine children baptised at Nieuwe Pekela between April 1751 and April 1767. Two sons were named Lammert, the name of her father, in 1751 and 1758.\n\nHer daughter Jantje, born on 28 September 1760, married the carpenter Derk Eppes Hekman and kept her mother's family names alive: she called a daughter Wija, born about 1791, and her youngest son Lammert, born in 1801. Wija Derks Hekman married a farmhand at Beerta in 1814 and died there in 1855.","src":[["Baptism of Lammert, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:87abb640-1a3d-8c94-deb9-aa1f8d355517"],["Baptism of Lammert, 1758","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:fe376a7f-6072-8321-9984-f584e0108191"],["Baptism of Jantje, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:90892207-57cb-33b6-b40a-35e944a9a477"],["Baptism of Abel, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:d348d189-6c63-fad0-4d14-537a4ea3fee3"],["Marriage of Wija Derks Hekman, Beerta 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:643784c9-230d-8366-e21b-1a0330f76ef2"],["Death of Wija Derks Hekman, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:aa8cd3e9-a55b-1de9-6751-5d4707a0199a"]]},
smjosephsmith:{"n":"Joseph Smith","d":"m. 1811 · Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk","b":null,"x":null,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Joseph Smith was a farm labourer at Stoke by Nayland in Suffolk. On 22 January 1811 he married Anne Deaves in the parish church of St Mary there; both were single and of that parish, and Joseph signed the register with a mark. The witnesses were Edward Hammant and Daniel Mann.\n\nTheir son William was baptised at Stoke by Nayland on 4 August 1811, Elizabeth on 27 April 1823 and James on 9 October 1825, when Joseph was described as a labourer living at Stoke. Their son Joseph, born about 1816, emigrated to Victoria in 1857; his death registration there names his parents as Joseph Smith and Ann Deaves and gives his birthplace as Suffolk. An Ann Smith of Stoke, aged 39, was buried there on 2 August 1829; her age fits the baptism of Ann Deaves in 1790, so she was most probably his wife.\n\nStoke by Nayland is a farming village in the Stour valley on the Suffolk and Essex border; the tall tower of its church appears in several of John Constable's paintings. Joseph Smith's own baptism and burial have not been identified.\n\nA daughter, Caroline, was also baptised at Stoke by Nayland on 1 July 1821, the child of Joseph and Ann Smith, a labourer. The free transcripts of the parish show no other baptism for this couple between William's in August 1811 and Caroline's in 1821, so the baptism of their son Joseph, born about 1816, has still not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Stoke by Nayland, 1811","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/685720ee865574b5db3f4c22"],["Baptism of William, 1811","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68571ed7865574b5db3ecf28"],["Baptism of Elizabeth, 1823","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c4b562af493fd164e430364"],["Baptism of James, 1825","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c4b5637f493fd164e4305b9"],["Death index of his son Joseph Smith, Victoria, 1878 (reg. 2923/1878)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Baptism of Caroline Smith, Stoke by Nayland, 1 Jul 1821","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c4b5621f493fd164e4301ba"]]},
smanndeaves:{"n":"Ann Deaves","d":"1790–1829 · Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk","b":1790,"x":1829,"s":"p","p":["smwilliamdeaves","smanncousins"],"u":true,"r":["uk"],"t":"Ann Deaves was baptised at Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk, on 22 February 1790, the daughter of William Deaves and Ann Cousins. She grew up in a large family: her brothers and sisters William, John, Mary, a second William, Elizabeth, James and Joseph were baptised there between 1786 and 1806. Her mother died in 1809.\n\nOn 22 January 1811 Anne Deaves, spinster, married Joseph Smith, a bachelor, both of Stoke by Nayland. Their son William was baptised that August, and later children included Joseph (born about 1816, who emigrated to Victoria), Elizabeth (1823) and James (1825). Joseph junior's death registration in Victoria in 1878 names his mother as Ann Deaves.\n\nAn Ann Smith of Stoke by Nayland, aged 39, was buried there on 2 August 1829, which fits Ann's baptism in 1790. That the bride of 1811 is the Ann Deaves baptised in 1790 rests on her name, her parish and this age at burial; no record directly links them.\n\nHer daughter Caroline was baptised at Stoke by Nayland on 1 July 1821, the child of Joseph and Ann Smith.","src":[["Baptism, Stoke by Nayland, 1790","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68571d29865574b5db3e15b2"],["Marriage, Stoke by Nayland, 1811","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/685720ee865574b5db3f4c22"],["Baptism of son William, 1811 (mother Ann Deaves)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68571ed7865574b5db3ecf28"],["Burial of Ann Smith, aged 39, 1829","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68572023865574b5db3f0f71"],["Death index of her son Joseph Smith, Victoria, 1878 (reg. 2923/1878)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"],["Baptism of Caroline Smith, Stoke by Nayland, 1 Jul 1821","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c4b5621f493fd164e4301ba"]]},
smwilliamdeaves:{"n":"William Deaves","d":"Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk","b":null,"x":null,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"William Deaves lived at Stoke by Nayland in Suffolk, where he and his wife Ann Cousins had at least eight children baptised: William (20 January 1786), John (11 February 1788), Ann (22 February 1790), Mary (20 January 1793), a second William (6 May 1797), Elizabeth (1 September 1800), James (17 April 1803) and Joseph (23 March 1806). A William Deaves was buried in the parish in May 1792, most likely the first son, as the name was given again in 1797.\n\nAnn, \"wife of Wm Deaves\", was buried at Stoke by Nayland on 16 July 1809. Their daughter Ann married Joseph Smith there in 1811.\n\nWilliam's marriage to Ann Cousins, probably about 1785, has not been found in the Suffolk or Essex transcripts. Several later William Deaves burials at Stoke by Nayland (1811, 1816, 1825 and 1850) have not been sorted out, so his own death is not known.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Ann, 1790","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68571d29865574b5db3e15b2"],["Burial of his wife Ann, 1809","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c7c2908f493fd203f4a411f"]]},
smanncousins:{"n":"Ann Cousins","d":"d. 1809 · Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk","b":null,"x":1809,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Ann Cousins was the wife of William Deaves of Stoke by Nayland, Suffolk. Her maiden name is recorded in the baptism entries of her children there, eight of whom were baptised between 1786 and 1806: William, John, Ann (1790), Mary, a second William, Elizabeth, James and Joseph. The first William appears to have died in 1792.\n\nAnn, \"wife of Wm Deaves\", was buried at Stoke by Nayland on 16 July 1809. Two years later her daughter Ann married Joseph Smith in the same church; their son Joseph emigrated to Victoria in 1857.\n\nHer own baptism and marriage have not been found. She should not be confused with the Ann Death who married a William Cousins at Stoke by Nayland in 1780.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Ann, 1790 (mother Ann Cousins)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68571d29865574b5db3e15b2"],["Burial, Stoke by Nayland, 1809","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5c7c2908f493fd203f4a411f"]]},
smjohnwragg:{"n":"John Wragg","d":"m. 1785 · Arrington, Cambridgeshire","b":null,"x":null,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"John Wragg of Arrington, Cambridgeshire, married Mary Janson, also of Arrington, in the parish church on 6 December 1785, by licence. The witnesses were Lawrence Coxall and Lydia Day.\n\nTheir son James was born on 14 August 1787 and their daughter Sarah on 31 July 1789. Another daughter, Elizabeth, born in 1786, was probably theirs too, although the transcript of her baptism names her father as Robert. James was farming at Arrington by 1817. Elizabeth married William Fordham of Great Eversden in March 1812, and Sarah married John Jones in July 1812, with James as a witness.\n\nMary died in 1801 and was buried at Arrington on 2 July. On 28 March 1802 John, a widower of Arrington, married Mary White by licence at Croydon cum Clopton, the next parish to the west. An earlier John Wragg of Arrington, a widower buried there in 1783, whose wife Elizabeth had been buried in 1774, may have been his father, but this has not been proven.","src":[["Marriage, Arrington, 1785","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68298aa686557451b4feb4e7"],["Baptism of James, 1788","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd0865574c41a1bd48f"],["Baptism of Sarah, 1789","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd0865574c41a1bd49b"],["Baptism of Elizabeth, 1786","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd0865574c41a1bd46d"],["Burial of his wife Mary, 1801","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e847498f493fd2668d6c423"],["Second marriage, Croydon cum Clopton, 1802","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829948386557451b40266b4"],["Baptism of grandson John, son of James Wragg, farmer, 1817","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd8865574c41a1bd9b0"],["Burial of John Wragg, widower, 1783","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e847495f493fd2668d6c3cd"],["Burial of Elizabeth wife of John Wragg, 1774","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e49529bf493fd2b72b6ac04"]]},
smmaryjanson:{"n":"Mary Janson","d":"d. 1801 · Arrington, Cambridgeshire","b":null,"x":1801,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mary Janson of Arrington, Cambridgeshire, married John Wragg of the same parish on 6 December 1785, by licence. Their children included James, born on 14 August 1787, and Sarah, born on 31 July 1789, and probably Elizabeth, born in August 1786, although the transcript of Elizabeth's baptism names her father as Robert. James later farmed at Arrington, Elizabeth married William Fordham, and Sarah married John Jones in 1812.\n\nMary, \"wife of John Wragg\", was buried at Arrington on 2 July 1801, when her children were aged between about eleven and fifteen. Her husband married again the following year.\n\nHer own baptism and parents have not been found. Arrington was a small parish on the Wimpole estate, where most people worked on the land.","src":[["Marriage, Arrington, 1785","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68298aa686557451b4feb4e7"],["Baptism of Sarah, 1789","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6829edd0865574c41a1bd49b"],["Burial, Arrington, 1801","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e847498f493fd2668d6c423"]]},
smmarthawilson:{"n":"Martha Wilson","d":"Devon","b":null,"x":null,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Martha Wilson was the mother of Joseph Warren, who was born at Devonport, Devon, in about 1810. Her name and that of her husband, William Warren, are recorded in Joseph's death registration in Melbourne in 1892; he died in the Melbourne Benevolent Asylum aged 82.\n\nHer son became a mason and then a butcher in Devonport, married Mary Fox in 1839, and sailed with his family for Victoria on the Parsee in 1854. No other record of Martha has been found yet: there is no William and Martha Warren household in the 1841 or 1851 census transcripts for Devon, and the Devonport parish registers are not in the free transcripts searched.","src":[["Death index of her son Joseph Warren, 1892 (reg. 10577/1892)","https://www.bdm.vic.gov.au/research-and-family-history/search-your-family-history"]]},
smelizabethhelmes:{"n":"Elizabeth Helmes","d":"b. c. 1811 · Huntingdonshire","b":1811,"x":null,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Elizabeth Helmes was born in Huntingdonshire in about 1811. She was living at Water Newton when she married William Hicks of Yaxley in the parish church of St Remigius there on 12 October 1828; both signed with a mark. Water Newton is a small village on the River Nene, beside Ermine Street and the site of the Roman town of Durobrivae.\n\nThe couple made their home at Alwalton, a few miles downstream, where William was a farm labourer. Their children were baptised there: Charles in 1829, George in 1833, Mary in 1835, Hannah in 1839, Emma in 1840 and William in 1846. In 1841 the family lived in Alwalton High Street.\n\nHer eldest son, Charles, emigrated to South Australia in 1855 with his wife Ann and their children. Elizabeth's baptism and death have not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Water Newton, 1828","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68384a2b865574838ea08125"],["Census 1841, Alwalton","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/64f0b932f493fd6cd8db826e"],["Baptism of son Charles, 1829","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/683825dc865574838e90fd66"]]},
bzjohnpawsey:{"n":"John Pawsey","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A John, with his wife Ann, who was born Quinting, had a daughter Anna baptised at Stowupland, next to Stowmarket in Suffolk, on 15 September 1803. The online transcription of the register gives the family surname as Pawsey in square brackets, meaning the transcriber supplied it because it is not clearly written. Stowmarket was a market town on the River Gipping, and Stowupland was the farming parish on its northern side.\n\nThat Anna fits Anna Pawsey, the wife of William Haill, a carpenter of Earl Stonham: she was born at Stowupland about 1804 by the 1861 census and died early in 1867 aged 63. John is therefore very probably her father, but the link is marked as unproven because the surname on the baptism is uncertain and no other record yet ties them together. Through Anna he would be the great-grandfather of Ellen Baldwin, who married George Brazier in Brisbane in 1893.","src":[["Baptism of Anna, daughter of John [Pawsey] and Ann (born Quinting), Stowupland (St Peter, Stowmarket register), 15 Sep 1803 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68572187865574b5db3f887a"]]},
bzannquinting:{"n":"Ann Quinting","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Named as the mother, Ann, born Quinting, when her daughter Anna was baptised at Stowupland, Suffolk, on 15 September 1803; the father was John, whose surname the online transcription gives as Pawsey in square brackets because it is not clearly written in the register.\n\nThe Quinting family had been in Stowupland for at least a generation. The same register records the baptisms of several Quinting children between 1773 and 1786, among them two girls called Ann: one on 25 July 1773, the daughter of John and Elizabeth Quinting, and another on 8 June 1781. Either could be her, but nothing yet shows which.\n\nThe baptised Anna is very probably Anna Pawsey, who married William Haill of Earl Stonham, so Ann is marked as her mother with the link unproven. If it is right, she was the great-grandmother of Ellen Baldwin, who married George Brazier in Brisbane in 1893.","src":[["Baptism of Anna, daughter of John [Pawsey] and Ann (born Quinting), Stowupland (St Peter, Stowmarket register), 15 Sep 1803 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68572187865574b5db3f887a"],["Baptism of Ann Quinting, daughter of John and Elizabeth, Stowupland, 25 Jul 1773 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6857215b865574b5db3f7469"],["Baptism of Ann Quinting, Stowupland, 8 Jun 1781 (FreeREG search result)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68572164865574b5db3f77ce"]]},
hxjohnnapier:{"n":"John Napier","d":"Largs, Ayrshire","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The father of Robert Napier, the tailor who left Largs for New South Wales in 1847. He is known only from Robert's New South Wales death registration of 1904, which names Robert's parents as John and Martha. Robert was born at Largs about 1825, so the family was living there at that time. Largs is a small town on the Ayrshire coast of the Firth of Clyde, facing the island of Great Cumbrae. Robert's eldest son, born in New South Wales in 1850, was named John, and a daughter born in 1867 was named Martha.","src":[["NSW death index: Robert Napier, 1904, Bowral, reg. 8672/1904 (parents John and Martha)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["Obituary of Robert Napier, Bowral Free Press, 14 Sep 1904","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112421815"]]},
hxmarthanapier:{"n":"Martha Napier","d":"Largs, Ayrshire","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"The mother of Robert Napier, the Largs tailor who emigrated to New South Wales in 1847 and died at Mittagong in 1904. Her first name comes from his New South Wales death registration, which gives his parents as John and Martha; her maiden name is not recorded in the index. Robert was born at Largs, in Ayrshire, about 1825. His children in New South Wales included a daughter Martha, born in 1867, and a son John, born in 1850.","src":[["NSW death index: Robert Napier, 1904, Bowral, reg. 8672/1904 (parents John and Martha)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"]]},
hxjohnsneddon:{"n":"John Sneddon","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Probably the father of Janet Sneddon, wife of the Mittagong tailor Robert Napier. The New South Wales index records the death in 1907, in the Waterloo district of Sydney, of a Janet Napier whose parents were John and Ann. A local paper reported that July the death of “Mrs. Napier, sen.”, widow of Robert Napier, at her eldest daughter's home at Alexandria, which lies in that district. The link rests only on that match of name, place and year. Sneddon is a surname of central Scotland, and Janet was born about 1823.","src":[["NSW death index: Janet Napier, 1907, Waterloo, reg. 11697/1907 (parents John and Ann)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["Death of Mrs. Napier, sen., Robertson Advocate, 30 Jul 1907","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112092949"]]},
hxannsneddon:{"n":"Ann Sneddon","d":"","s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Probably the mother of Janet Sneddon, wife of the Mittagong tailor Robert Napier. She is known only as “Ann”, the mother's name in the New South Wales index entry for the death of a Janet Napier in the Waterloo district of Sydney in 1907; Sneddon is her married name, and her own maiden name is not given. That Janet is taken to be Robert Napier's widow because a local paper reported the death of “Mrs. Napier, sen.” at Alexandria, in the same district, that July. Janet was born about 1823, so Ann was probably born around 1790 to 1805.","src":[["NSW death index: Janet Napier, 1907, Waterloo, reg. 11697/1907 (parents John and Ann)","https://familyhistory.bdm.nsw.gov.au/"],["Death of Mrs. Napier, sen., Robertson Advocate, 30 Jul 1907","https://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article112092949"]]},
hxjameshorsford:{"n":"James Horsford","d":"c. 1780–1837 · West Milton, Dorset","b":1780,"x":1837,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Probably the father of John Horsford of West Milton (born about 1812). James Horsford, “of Weymouth”, married Mary Legg at Powerstock, in the hills north-east of Bridport, on 25 October 1810. The couple lived at Milton, a hamlet of the parish, where he was a labourer. Their daughters Mary and Sarah were baptised together on 12 June 1814, and Jane was baptised privately on 13 December 1824 and received into the church the next month. A James Horsford of West Milton was buried at Powerstock on 12 July 1837, aged 57.\n\nThe link to John is not proven. No baptism for John appears in the transcribed Powerstock registers. It rests on the 1841 census, in which John, his wife and sons shared a house at West Milton with a Mary Horsford, 55, and a Jane Horsford, 16, the ages of James's widow and youngest daughter. Another couple of the same parish, Robert and Mary Horseford (married 1799), also had children baptised there between 1802 and 1808.","src":[["Powerstock marriages 1720–1837 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockMarrs2.htm"],["Powerstock baptisms 1813–1836 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps1813-1836.htm"],["Powerstock Bishop's Transcripts baptisms 1778–1817 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps2.htm"],["Powerstock burials 1813–1851 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBurs1.htm"],["1841 census, West Milton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6833df80b18f7c24ca87291c"]]},
hxmarylegg:{"n":"Mary Legg","d":"b. c. 1786","b":1786,"s":"y","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Probably the mother of John Horsford of West Milton. Mary Legg married James Horsford of Weymouth at Powerstock, west Dorset, on 25 October 1810. They lived at Milton in Powerstock parish, where James was a labourer, and had daughters Mary and Sarah, both baptised on 12 June 1814, and Jane, baptised on 13 December 1824. James was probably the James Horsford of West Milton buried in July 1837, aged 57.\n\nIn 1841 a Mary Horsford, 55, was living at West Milton with Jane Horsford, 16, and with John Horsford, 25, his wife Martha and their three small sons. That census gives no relationships, and no baptism for John has been found, so she is taken to be his mother on that evidence only.","src":[["Powerstock marriages 1720–1837 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockMarrs2.htm"],["Powerstock baptisms 1813–1836 (Dorset OPC)","https://www.opcdorset.org/PowerstockFiles/PowerstockBaps1813-1836.htm"],["1841 census, West Milton (FreeCEN)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/6833df80b18f7c24ca87291c"]]},
zadirkvveen:{"n":"Dirk van Veen","d":"c. 1758–1832","s":"b","p":["zagerritvveen","zaaagtjevmaurick"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Dirk van Veen was born about 1758, the son of Gerrit Rijke van Veen and Aagtje van Maurick, and married Jannigje Donk. Their children included Barbera (born 20 May 1788), Eigje, who was born at Nieuwland and married Klaas Maaijen, Geertje, Gerrit, Cornelis (about 1793), Neeltje, Bastiaantje and Mergje, who died in 1816 aged eleven. Cornelis was born at Meerkerk, and the family belonged to the villages around Meerkerk, Nieuwland and Leerbroek in the Vijfheerenlanden.\n\nHis children married between 1819 and 1835: Barbera to Willem Bor at Leerbroek, Cornelis to Catrijntje Wink at Willige Langerak in 1821, Gerrit to Elizabeth Tukker at Meerkerk in 1822, Neeltje to Servaas Homburg at Vianen in 1824 and Bastiaantje to Florus Smits at Meerkerk in 1835. Dirk died on 21 August 1832, aged 74; his death record names his parents and his wife.","src":[["Death record, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:893c318e-57bf-4bbc-8704-666da7b9da23"],["Marriage of son Cornelis, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D8A37F05-F6EE-4A3B-A66A-7DABD0715BD9"],["Death of daughter Eigje, Benschop 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D5DF0637-E229-476F-B951-44413AE14E71"]],"b":1758,"x":1832},
zajannigjedonk:{"n":"Jannigje Donk","d":"c. 1764–1837","s":"b","p":["zacornelisdonk","zabarberaderuiter"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jannigje Donk was born about 1764, the daughter of Cornelis Donk and Barbera Janse de Ruiter. She married Dirk van Veen, and their children, born from the late 1780s to about 1805, included Barbera, named after her mother, Eigje, Geertje, Gerrit, Cornelis, Neeltje, Bastiaantje and Mergje. Cornelis was born at Meerkerk about 1793 and Eigje at Nieuwland.\n\nShe lost her daughter Mergje in 1816, aged eleven, Barbera in 1824 and Eigje, at Benschop, in 1828. Her son Cornelis married Catrijntje Wink, whose mother Johanna was also a Donk; whether the two women were related has not been established. Jannigje was widowed in August 1832 and died on 15 May 1837, aged 73.","src":[["Death record, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:89873927-5bae-47d4-b64c-4a20151bdd50"],["Death of husband Dirk van Veen, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:893c318e-57bf-4bbc-8704-666da7b9da23"]],"b":1764,"x":1837},
zagerritvveen:{"n":"Gerrit Rijke van Veen","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gerrit Rijke van Veen is known from the death record of his son Dirk van Veen, who died in 1832 aged 74 and so was born about 1758. That record names Dirk's parents as Gerrit Rijke van Veen and Aagtje van Maurick. \"Rijke\" is probably a patronymic or second name; in the eighteenth century many people in the rivers district still used a father's name alongside or instead of a fixed surname.\n\nHis son Dirk lived in the Vijfheerenlanden around Meerkerk, and his grandson Cornelis van Veen was born at Meerkerk about 1793. A daughter, Aantje van Veen, died on 31 August 1838; her death record also names Gerrit Rijke van Veen and Aagtje van Maurick as her parents. No record of Gerrit himself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of son Dirk van Veen, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:893c318e-57bf-4bbc-8704-666da7b9da23"],["Death of daughter Aantje van Veen, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:3f1b1ac6-79f4-41f8-be7d-f59d7855b2eb"]]},
zaaagtjevmaurick:{"n":"Aagtje van Maurick","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Aagtje van Maurick is named as the mother of Dirk van Veen in his death record of 1832; Dirk died aged 74, so he was born about 1758, and Aagtje was the wife of Gerrit Rijke van Veen. Her surname points to the village of Maurik, on the Rhine in the Betuwe in Gelderland.\n\nHer son Dirk raised his family in the Vijfheerenlanden around Meerkerk, and her daughter Aantje van Veen died on 31 August 1838. No record of Aagtje herself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of son Dirk van Veen, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:893c318e-57bf-4bbc-8704-666da7b9da23"]]},
zacornelisdonk:{"n":"Cornelis Donk","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis Donk is named as the father of Jannigje Donk in her death record of 1837. Jannigje died aged 73, so she was born about 1764, and Cornelis's wife was Barbera Janse de Ruiter. Jannigje married Dirk van Veen, and their son Cornelis van Veen, born at Meerkerk about 1793, was probably named after him, following the Dutch custom of naming children after their grandparents.\n\nThe Donk family belonged to the Vijfheerenlanden around Meerkerk. No record of Cornelis himself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Jannigje Donk, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:89873927-5bae-47d4-b64c-4a20151bdd50"]]},
zabarberaderuiter:{"n":"Barbera Janse de Ruiter","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Barbera Janse de Ruiter is named as the mother of Jannigje Donk in Jannigje's death record of 1837; Jannigje died aged 73, so she was born about 1764. \"Janse\" is a patronymic, meaning daughter of Jan. Barbera's husband was Cornelis Donk.\n\nHer name was carried on by her granddaughter Barbera van Veen, born on 20 May 1788, a daughter of Jannigje Donk and Dirk van Veen, who married Willem Bor at Leerbroek in 1819. No record of Barbera herself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Jannigje Donk, 1837","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:89873927-5bae-47d4-b64c-4a20151bdd50"]]},
zaandrieswink:{"n":"Andries Wink","d":"d. 1817 · Meerkerk","s":"b","p":["zaandrieswinck","zatrijntjeplemper"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Andries Wink was the son of Andries Winck and Trijntje Plemper. He married Johanna Donk, and their children were born at Meerkerk: Andries (10 May 1800), Geertruij and Peter (both recorded as born 15 April 1802), Catrijntje (about 1804), Pietertje (16 January 1808), Cornelis (about 1811), Johannes (about 1815) and a second Peter (18 September 1816).\n\nHe died on 23 April 1817, seven months after his youngest son was born, leaving his widow with a large young family. His children later scattered: Andries to Ameide, Geertruij and Peter to Lexmond, Pietertje to Nieuwland and Lexmond, and Cornelis and Johannes to Zuid-Scharwoude in North Holland, where Cornelis was a baker's man and later a farmer and Johannes a wheelwright. Catrijntje stayed at Meerkerk as the wife of Cornelis van Veen.","src":[["Death record, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:42cc4abc-8262-4afc-8e81-7b2c4c827f0f"],["Birth of son Peter, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:2d16facd-3dcd-474a-b31a-e47ae7134e79"],["Marriage of daughter Catrijntje, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D8A37F05-F6EE-4A3B-A66A-7DABD0715BD9"]],"x":1817},
zajohannadonk:{"n":"Johanna Donk","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Johanna Donk was the wife of Andries Wink of Meerkerk and the mother of at least eight children born there between 1800 and 1816: Andries, Geertruij, Peter, Catrijntje, Pietertje, Cornelis, Johannes and a second Peter. She was widowed in April 1817, when her youngest was seven months old.\n\nShe was still alive in 1821, when her daughter Catrijntje married Cornelis van Veen at Willige Langerak; Cornelis's mother was Jannigje Donk, but whether the two women were related has not been established. Johanna's own death has not been found in the indexes searched so far.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Catrijntje, 1821","https://www.openarchieven.nl/hua:D8A37F05-F6EE-4A3B-A66A-7DABD0715BD9"],["Death of husband Andries Wink, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:42cc4abc-8262-4afc-8e81-7b2c4c827f0f"],["Death of daughter Catharina Wink, 1878","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:1c2adf7d-8d77-49a3-bc46-e89223ea9237"]]},
zaandrieswinck:{"n":"Andries Winck","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Andries Winck is named as the father of Andries Wink in the younger man's death record of 1817; the spelling of the surname with \"ck\" was common in the eighteenth century and later settled as Wink. His wife was Trijntje Plemper.\n\nHis son Andries raised a family at Meerkerk in the Vijfheerenlanden from 1800, and named his own first son Andries after him. His granddaughter Catrijntje Wink, born about 1804, carried the name of her grandmother Trijntje. No record of Andries Winck himself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of son Andries Wink, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:42cc4abc-8262-4afc-8e81-7b2c4c827f0f"]]},
zatrijntjeplemper:{"n":"Trijntje Plemper","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Trijntje Plemper is named as the mother of Andries Wink in his death record of 1817, as the wife of Andries Winck. Her son Andries married Johanna Donk and lived at Meerkerk, where his children were born between 1800 and 1816.\n\nTrijntje is a short form of Catharina, and her granddaughter Catrijntje (Catharina) Wink, born at Meerkerk about 1804 and later the wife of Cornelis van Veen, carried her name. No record of Trijntje herself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of son Andries Wink, 1817","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rzh:42cc4abc-8262-4afc-8e81-7b2c4c827f0f"]]},
zadirkhagen:{"n":"Dirk Hagen","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Dirk Hagen is named, with his wife Cornelia Boon, as the paternal grandparents of Metje Hagen in her marriage record at Hoornaar in 1827, which states that both were then dead. His son Frans Hagen lived at Arkel, near Gorinchem, where Frans's children were born from 1787.\n\nFrans named his eldest known son, born on 5 February 1787, Dirk after him, and his daughter Cornelia, born in 1801, after his mother. No record of Dirk Hagen himself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of granddaughter Metje Hagen, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"]]},
zacorneliaboon:{"n":"Cornelia Boon","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelia Boon is named, with her husband Dirk Hagen, as the paternal grandparents of Metje Hagen in her marriage record at Hoornaar in 1827; both were then dead. Her son Frans Hagen raised his family at Arkel, on the Linge near Gorinchem, from the 1780s.\n\nHer granddaughter Cornelia Hagen, born at Arkel on 17 July 1801, carried her name; Cornelia Hagen married Jakob de Bruijn of Hoornaar in 1822 and died at Hoornaar in 1866. No record of Cornelia Boon herself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of granddaughter Metje Hagen, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"]]},
zaariebronkhorst:{"n":"Arie Bronkhorst","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Arie Bronkhorst is named, with his wife Maaike, as the maternal grandparents of Metje Hagen in her marriage record at Hoornaar in 1827, which states that both were dead. His daughter Marigje Bronkhorst married Frans Hagen and had her children at Arkel between 1787 and 1807, so Arie was probably born about the 1730s or 1740s.\n\nMarigje named one of her sons Arie after him; that Arie Hagen died at Arkel in 1875. No record of Arie Bronkhorst himself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of granddaughter Metje Hagen, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"]]},
zamaaikevervoorn:{"n":"Maaike Vervoorn","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maaike is named, with her husband Arie Bronkhorst, as the maternal grandparents of Metje Hagen in her marriage record at Hoornaar in 1827; both were then dead. Her surname is hard to read in that record, but her daughter Marigje's death record of 1826 writes it clearly as Vervoorn.\n\nHer daughter Marigje Bronkhorst married Frans Hagen and lived at Arkel, near Gorinchem, where she named a daughter Maaike, born on 9 February 1794, after her. No record of Maaike herself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of granddaughter Metje Hagen, Hoornaar 1827","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20974290"],["Death of daughter Marigje Bronkhorst, Arkel 1826","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6479146"]]},
zageerlofkreij:{"n":"Geerlof de Kreij","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":["zapieterkreij","zakeetjevdgraaf"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Geerlof (also written Geelof) de Kreij was a farmer (bouwman) at Schelluinen, the son of Pieter de Kreij and Keetje or Neeltje van de Graaf. He married Grietje de Kreij, who carried the same surname by birth, a common thing in a small village where a few families had intermarried for generations.\n\nTheir children included Arie (about 1795), Kornelis, who married Marrigje Vink at Hoornaar in 1818, Jan, who married Neeltje Rietveld in 1830 and Jannigje Versluis in 1845, Huig Rokusse, who married Flora den Brave in 1830, and Martijntje, the wife of Sander van Bruggen, who died in 1872. By the time of Arie's second marriage in 1836, Geerlof and Grietje had both died at Schelluinen; their deaths have not been found in the civil registers from 1811, so they may have died earlier. Arie named a son Geelof after him.","src":[["Marriage of son Arie, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death of son Arie, 1887","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6909400"],["Marriage of son Kornelis, Hoornaar 1818","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20972706"],["Marriage of son Jan, 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21078776"],["Marriage of son Huig Rokusse, 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21078824"],["Death of daughter Martijntje, 1872","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6906629"]]},
zagrietjekreij:{"n":"Grietje de Kreij","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":["zacorneliskreij","zajannigjevbruggen"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Grietje de Kreij was born into the de Kreij family of Schelluinen, the daughter of Cornelis de Kreij and Jannigje van Bruggen, and married Geerlof de Kreij, a farmer there. Their children included Arie (about 1795), Kornelis, Jan, Huig Rokusse and Martijntje, who all married in Schelluinen or neighbouring Hoornaar between 1818 and 1845.\n\nShe had died at Schelluinen by August 1836, when her son Arie's marriage record names her and her husband as farmers who lived and died there. Her son Arie's first wife was also called Grietje: Grietje Boonstoppel.","src":[["Marriage of son Arie, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death of son Arie, 1887","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6909400"]]},
zapieterkreij:{"n":"Pieter de Kreij","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Pieter de Kreij is named as the paternal grandfather of Arie de Kreij in Arie's marriage record at Schelluinen in 1836, which describes all four of Arie's grandparents as farmers who lived and died at Schelluinen. Pieter's wife was Keetje (or possibly Neeltje) van de Graaf, and their son was Geerlof de Kreij, also a farmer there.\n\nSchelluinen was a small farming village in the polders just north of Gorinchem, on the edge of the Alblasserwaard. Since his grandson was born about 1795, Pieter probably lived in the middle decades of the eighteenth century. No record of Pieter himself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of grandson Arie de Kreij, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"]]},
zakeetjevdgraaf:{"n":"Keetje van de Graaf","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Keetje van de Graaf is named as the paternal grandmother of Arie de Kreij in his marriage record at Schelluinen in 1836; the first letter of her name is unclear in the handwriting, so she may be Neeltje rather than Keetje. She was the wife of Pieter de Kreij and the mother of Geerlof de Kreij, and the record describes her as one of a family of farmers who lived and died at Schelluinen.\n\nHer grandson Arie was born about 1795, so she lived in the eighteenth century. No record of her own has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of grandson Arie de Kreij, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"]]},
zacorneliskreij:{"n":"Cornelis de Kreij","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis de Kreij is named as the maternal grandfather of Arie de Kreij in Arie's marriage record at Schelluinen in 1836. He and his wife Jannigje van Bruggen were the parents of Grietje de Kreij, who married Geerlof de Kreij; the record describes all of Arie's grandparents as farmers who lived and died at Schelluinen.\n\nGrietje named a son Kornelis after him; that Kornelis married Marrigje Vink at Hoornaar in 1818 and died there in 1874. No record of Cornelis himself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of grandson Arie de Kreij, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"]]},
zajannigjevbruggen:{"n":"Jannigje van Bruggen","d":"Schelluinen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jannigje van Bruggen is named as the maternal grandmother of Arie de Kreij in his marriage record at Schelluinen in 1836, as the wife of Cornelis de Kreij. She was the mother of Grietje de Kreij, and the record describes her as one of a family of farmers who lived and died at Schelluinen.\n\nThe van Bruggen name stayed in the family's circle: her granddaughter Martijntje de Kreij married Sander van Bruggen, and her grandson Arie de Kreij named a daughter Jannigje, born at Gorinchem. No record of Jannigje herself has yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of grandson Arie de Kreij, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death of Martijntje de Kreij, wife of Sander van Bruggen, 1872","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6906629"]]},
zajanrover:{"n":"Jan Rokusse de Rover","d":"c. 1775 · Hardinxveld","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Rokusse de Rover was a labourer at Hardinxveld, on the Merwede. \"Rokusse\" is a patronymic, meaning son of Rokus. He married Geertrui van Andel, and their daughter Gijsbertje was born at Hardinxveld about 1810.\n\nIn August 1836 he was 61, so born about 1775, when he and Geertrui, both still living at Hardinxveld, came to Schelluinen for Gijsbertje's marriage to the labourer Arie de Kreij. Two years later Gijsbertje named her son Jan Rokus after him. His wife was later living at Schelluinen, where she died in 1873 as his widow; his own death has not been found in the Gorinchem archive, and Hardinxveld's registers are kept at Dordrecht.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Gijsbertje, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death of wife Geertrui van Andel, 1873","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6906792"]],"b":1775},
zageertruivandel:{"n":"Geertrui van Andel","d":"c. 1779–1873","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Geertrui van Andel was born about 1779 and married Jan Rokusse de Rover, a labourer at Hardinxveld on the Merwede. Their daughter Gijsbertje was born there about 1810.\n\nIn August 1836, aged 57, Geertrui came with her husband to Schelluinen for Gijsbertje's marriage to Arie de Kreij. She died at Schelluinen, where her daughter lived, on 30 January 1873, aged about 93, the widow of Jan Rokus de Rover.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Gijsbertje, Schelluinen 1836","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=21079598"],["Death register, Schelluinen 1873","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6906792"]],"b":1779,"x":1873},
zacornelisbroere:{"n":"Cornelis Broere","d":"Hoornaar","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis Broere (or Broeren) was a farmer (bouwman) at Hoornaar in the Alblasserwaard. With his wife Annigje Nieuwpoort he had a son Pieter, born on 13 August 1803, at Noordeloos according to Pieter's marriage record.\n\nIn December 1830 Cornelis and Annigje, both farming at Hoornaar, were present when Pieter, a widower of 27, married Lijntje Vogel there. Pieter named his first son Kornelis after him. Cornelis's wife died at Hoornaar in 1850. He died between December 1830 and February 1850, when Annigje's death record calls her his widow; his own death record has not yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of son Pieter, Hoornaar 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"],["Death of son Pieter, 1890","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6600036"],["Death of wife Annigje Nieuwpoort, 1850","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7468472"]]},
zaannigjenieuwpoort:{"n":"Annigje Nieuwpoort","d":"c. 1775–1850 · Giessen-Nieuwkerk","s":"b","p":["zacornelisnieuwpoort","zajannigjebaardwijk"],"r":["nl"],"t":"Annigje Nieuwpoort was born at Giessen-Nieuwkerk, on the Giessen river in the Alblasserwaard, about 1775, the daughter of Cornelis Nieuwpoort and Jannigje Baardwijk. She married Cornelis Broere, a farmer at Hoornaar, and their son Pieter was born on 13 August 1803.\n\nIn December 1830 she and her husband, then farming at Hoornaar, attended Pieter's second marriage, to Lijntje Vogel. Pieter's eldest daughter, born in December 1832, was named Annigje after her, and she lived to see fifteen grandchildren born to Pieter and Lijntje. She died in house number 11 at Hoornaar on the morning of Sunday 17 February 1850, aged 75, the widow of Cornelis Broeren.","src":[["Death register, Hoornaar 1850","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7468472"],["Marriage of son Pieter, Hoornaar 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"]],"b":1775,"x":1850},
zacornelisnieuwpoort:{"n":"Cornelis Nieuwpoort","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis Nieuwpoort is named as the father of Annigje Nieuwpoort in her death record at Hoornaar in 1850; his wife was Jannigje Baardwijk. Annigje married Cornelis Broere, a farmer at Hoornaar, and their son Pieter was born in 1803.\n\nThe surname Nieuwpoort recalls the small walled town of Nieuwpoort on the Lek, on the northern edge of the Alblasserwaard. No record of Cornelis himself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Annigje Nieuwpoort, Hoornaar 1850","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7468472"]]},
zajannigjebaardwijk:{"n":"Jannigje Baardwijk","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jannigje Baardwijk is named as the mother of Annigje Nieuwpoort in Annigje's death record at Hoornaar in 1850, as the wife of Cornelis Nieuwpoort. Her daughter married Cornelis Broere, a farmer at Hoornaar, and her grandson Pieter Broere was born in 1803.\n\nThe name Jannigje came down through the family: Pieter Broere named a daughter Jannigje in 1836. No record of Jannigje Baardwijk herself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Annigje Nieuwpoort, Hoornaar 1850","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7468472"]]},
zajanvogel:{"n":"Jan Vogel","d":"Hoornaar","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Vogel was a day labourer (dagloner) at Hoornaar, a man hired by the day by local farmers for work such as haymaking, ditching and threshing. With his wife Jannigje van der Heijden he had a daughter Lijntje, born on 2 July 1813, and a son Huibert, who married Aaltje de Vos and died at Hoornaar on 30 December 1864.\n\nIn December 1830 Jan and Jannigje were the parents who consented to the marriage of their seventeen-year-old daughter Lijntje to the widowed farmer Pieter Broere. Lijntje named her first two sons Kornelis and Jan, the second after him. He had died by April 1864, when Lijntje's death record names both her parents as deceased; his own death has not yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Lijntje, Hoornaar 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"],["Birth of daughter Lijntje, 1813","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=6624658"],["Death of son Huibert Vogel, 1864","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7471859"]]},
zajannigjevdheijden:{"n":"Jannigje van der Heijden","d":"Hoornaar","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jannigje van der Heijden was the wife of Jan Vogel, a day labourer at Hoornaar. Their children included Lijntje, born on 2 July 1813, and Huibert, who married Aaltje de Vos and died at Hoornaar in December 1864.\n\nIn December 1830 she and her husband gave their consent to Lijntje's marriage, at seventeen, to Pieter Broere, a widowed farmer of 27. Lijntje named her third daughter, born in January 1836, Jannigje after her. She had died by April 1864, when Lijntje's death record names both her parents as deceased; her own death has not yet been found.","src":[["Marriage of daughter Lijntje, Hoornaar 1830","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=20975058"],["Death of son Huibert Vogel, 1864","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7471859"]]},
zacornelisvaal:{"n":"Cornelis de Vaal","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis de Vaal is named as the father of Susanna de Vaal in her death record at Hoornaar in 1819. Susanna was born at Laag-Blokland, a small village next to Hoornaar, about 1764, so Cornelis lived there or nearby in the middle of the eighteenth century. His wife was called Eigje.\n\nSusanna named her eldest son, born at Hoornaar in 1787, Cornelis after him, and her eldest daughter Eigje after her mother. His link to the family rests on that one death record, which was written by a village clerk from what the neighbours told him; no baptism or marriage record for Cornelis has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Susanna de Vaal, Hoornaar 1819","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7461783"]],"u":true},
zaeigjevdpol:{"n":"Eigje van der Pol","d":"","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Eigje is named as the mother of Susanna de Vaal in Susanna's death record at Hoornaar in 1819, as the wife of Cornelis de Vaal. The end of her surname runs into the binding of the register; it appears to read van der Pol, but this is not certain. Susanna was born at Laag-Blokland, next to Hoornaar, about 1764.\n\nSusanna named her eldest daughter, born at Hoornaar on 19 November 1790, Eigje after her; that Eigje married Teunis Vink at Hoogblokland in 1815 and died at Hoornaar in 1876. No other record of Eigje herself has yet been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Susanna de Vaal, Hoornaar 1819","https://studiezaal.regionaalarchiefgorinchem.nl/detail.php?id=7461783"]],"u":true},
pmjasperbrowell:{"n":"Jasper Browell","d":"b. c. 1790 · Northumberland","b":1790,"s":"p","p":["pmjasperbrowellsr","pmcatherinejackson"],"u":true,"r":["uk"],"t":"The grandfather of Emily Browell, who emigrated to Victoria in 1867. In the 1861 census he was living in Cave Street, in the parish of St Paul, Bristol, aged 70, an annuitant (a person living on a fixed yearly income), born in Northumberland at a place the transcript reads as \"Haydon (or Eaydon)\". With him were his wife Mary Ann, 71, born in London, two unmarried daughters, Rosa Eliza, 28, born at Landkey in Devon, and Ellen S., 26, born at Pilton in Devon, and his granddaughter Emily, 17, born in Shropshire.\n\nTwenty years earlier, in 1841, he was at Horfield, then a village on the northern edge of Bristol, described as of independent means, with Mary, 40, and daughters Emily, 25, Julia, 20, Rosa, 15, and Ellen, 9. The two censuses do not agree on the ages of his wife and of Rosa. The daughters' birthplaces in north Devon show the family had moved about the country before settling near Bristol.\n\nHe was very probably the Jasper Browell born on 12 April 1790 and baptised a year later at St Alban's, Earsdon, Northumberland, son of Mr Jasper Browell, gentleman, of Link House, and his wife Catherine; \"Eaydon\" may be a misreading of Earsdon, although Haydon, the first reading of the transcript, is itself a parish in the Tyne valley of Northumberland. The free transcripts hold no other baptism of a Jasper Browell in Northumberland or Durham between 1780 and 1800, but the Haydon registers may not be among them. That link, and his own link to Emily's father William, are marked as uncertain.","src":[["1861 census, Bristol St Paul, Cave Street (FreeCEN, RG9/1718)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/690e8c61b18f7c9de27d4e29/emily-browell-1861-gloucestershire-bristol-st-pauls-1844-"],["1841 census, Horfield, Gloucestershire (FreeCEN, HO107/346)","https://www.freecen.org.uk/search_records/68a13eadb18f7c42c506ecb9/jasper-browell-1841-gloucestershire-horfield-1791-"],["Baptism of Jasper Browell, Earsdon, 12 April 1791 (born 12 April 1790) (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ce4567df493fd2336c4605f"]]},
pmjasperbrowellsr:{"n":"Jasper Browell","d":"Earsdon, Northumberland","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A gentleman of Earsdon parish in Northumberland, a few miles north of Newcastle upon Tyne. He married Catherine Jackson by licence at St Alban's, Earsdon, on 8 February 1787; the register describes him as a gentleman. Their son Jasper was born on 12 April 1790 at Link House and baptised at Earsdon on 12 April 1791, the father entered as Mr Jasper Browell, gentleman. Marriage by licence, rather than by banns read in church, cost a fee and was usual among families of some standing.\n\nIf his son was the Jasper Browell later found living on an annuity near Bristol, he was the great-grandfather of Emily Browell, who emigrated to Victoria in 1867. Browell was a Tyneside name, and many Browells appear in the registers of Northumberland and Durham in these years.","src":[["Marriage of Jasper Browell, gent., and Catherine Jackson, Earsdon, 8 February 1787 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e9ea43ef493fd450c04b4ce"],["Baptism of Jasper Browell, Earsdon, 12 April 1791 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ce4567df493fd2336c4605f"]]},
pmcatherinejackson:{"n":"Catherine Jackson","d":"Earsdon, Northumberland","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"She married Jasper Browell, gentleman, by licence at St Alban's, Earsdon, Northumberland, on 8 February 1787. Her son Jasper was born at Link House on 12 April 1790 and baptised at Earsdon the following April, entered as the son of Jasper and Catherine Browell.\n\nIf that son was the Jasper Browell who later lived near Bristol, she was the great-grandmother of Emily Browell, who went out to Victoria in 1867 and married George William Parkinson. The link is marked as uncertain because it rests on the son's name, age and county of birth.","src":[["Marriage of Jasper Browell and Catherine Jackson, Earsdon, 8 February 1787 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5e9ea43ef493fd450c04b4ce"],["Baptism of Jasper Browell, Earsdon, 1791 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/5ce4567df493fd2336c4605f"]]},
pmgeorgephillips:{"n":"George Phillips","d":"Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A Langley man who married Sarah Smith, also of Langley, at the neighbouring church of Clavering on 11 October 1758. Both were single; he made his mark and she signed her name, and the witnesses were John Smith and Isaac Deadman. Four children were baptised at Langley: Mary (1 July 1759), George (7 June 1761), Sarah (15 May 1763) and Elizabeth (28 July 1765). Their son George died as an infant and was buried at Clavering on 23 February 1762, his home given as Langley. Sarah, wife of George Phillips, was buried at Clavering on 23 February 1770.\n\nHis daughter Mary is probably the Mary Philips who married John Wilson at Langley in 1782, which would make George the great-grandfather of Joseph Wilson of Diamond Creek. That link is marked as uncertain. His own baptism and burial have not been identified.","src":[["Marriage of George Phillips and Sarah Smith, Clavering, 11 October 1758 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77da"],["Baptism of Mary Phillips, Langley, 1 July 1759 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d2f493fd767a9fa054"],["Baptism of George Phillips, Langley, 7 June 1761 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d4f493fd767a9fa0b3"],["Burial of George Phillips, infant, of Langley, Clavering, 23 February 1762 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68348ff886557413f97f5cd1"],["Baptism of Sarah Phillips, Langley, 15 May 1763 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d5f493fd767a9fa100"],["Baptism of Elizabeth Phillips, Langley, 28 July 1765 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de37d6f493fd767a9fa164"]]},
pmsarahsmith:{"n":"Sarah Smith","d":"d. 1770 · Clavering, Essex","x":1770,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Of Langley, Essex. She married George Phillips at Clavering on 11 October 1758; she signed the register while he made his mark. Her children Mary (1759), George (1761), Sarah (1763) and Elizabeth (1765) were baptised at Langley; George died as an infant in 1762. She was buried at Clavering on 23 February 1770, entered as the wife of George Phillips, when her eldest daughter was ten.\n\nHer daughter Mary is probably the Mary Philips who married John Wilson at Langley in 1782; that link is marked as uncertain. Clavering and Langley are neighbouring parishes on the chalk uplands of north-west Essex, and couples from Langley often married in Clavering church.","src":[["Marriage of George Phillips and Sarah Smith, Clavering, 11 October 1758 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/6834905586557413f97f77da"],["Burial of Sarah, wife of George Phillips, Clavering, 23 February 1770 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68348ffd86557413f97f5e6d"]]},
pmedwardwilsonsr:{"n":"Edward Wilson","d":"Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"A Langley man who married Elizabeth Turner in Langley church on 4 March 1727/28. Dates written that way fall between 1 January and 24 March in the years before 1752, when the English year still began on 25 March, so the wedding was in March 1728 by modern reckoning. Eight children were baptised at Langley, all entered as the children of Edward and Elizabeth Wilson: Elizabeth (5 April 1730), Edward (18 April 1731), John (17 December 1732), Ann (23 December 1733), Robert (6 April 1735), Joseph (17 October 1736), a second Joseph (16 April 1738) and Thomas (31 October 1742). A Joseph Wilson was buried at Langley in June 1737, which explains the repeated name.\n\nHis son Edward is probably the Edward Wilson who married Maria Smith in 1757, and his son Robert may be the Robert Wilson whose children were baptised at Langley from 1757. An Edward Wilson buried at Langley on 11 February 1783 may be him. The link to the younger Edward is marked as uncertain.","src":[["Marriage of Edward Wilson and Elizabeth Turner, Langley, 4 March 1727/28 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de38faf493fd767a9fb0bc"],["Baptism of Elizabeth Wilson, Langley, 5 April 1730 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3748f493fd767a9f9c38"],["Baptism of Edward Wilson, Langley, 18 April 1731 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3748f493fd767a9f9c56"],["Baptism of John Wilson, Langley, 17 December 1732 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3749f493fd767a9f9c83"],["Baptism of Ann Wilson, Langley, 23 December 1733 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3749f493fd767a9f9c97"],["Baptism of Robert Wilson, Langley, 6 April 1735 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3749f493fd767a9f9cd0"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 17 October 1736 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de374af493fd767a9f9cfe"],["Burial of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 3 June 1737 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3875f493fd767a9faca6"],["Baptism of Joseph Wilson, Langley, 16 April 1738 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de374af493fd767a9f9d30"],["Baptism of Thomas Wilson, Langley, 31 October 1742 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de374df493fd767a9f9dad"],["Burial of Edward Wilson, Langley, 11 February 1783 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de386ef493fd767a9faa9d"]]},
pmelizabethturner:{"n":"Elizabeth Turner","d":"Langley, Essex","s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"She married Edward Wilson in Langley church, Essex, on 4 March 1727/28 (March 1728 by modern reckoning). Over the next fourteen years she had eight children baptised at Langley: Elizabeth (1730), Edward (1731), John (1732), Ann (1733), Robert (1735), Joseph (1736), who was probably the Joseph Wilson buried in June 1737, a second Joseph (1738) and Thomas (1742).\n\nHer son Edward is probably the Edward Wilson who married Maria Smith in 1757, and so the great-grandfather of Joseph Wilson of Diamond Creek, making her his great-great-grandmother; that link is marked as uncertain. Her own baptism and burial have not been identified.","src":[["Marriage of Edward Wilson and Elizabeth Turner, Langley, 4 March 1727/28 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de38faf493fd767a9fb0bc"],["Baptism of Edward Wilson, son of Edward and Elizabeth, Langley, 18 April 1731 (FreeREG)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/64de3748f493fd767a9f9c56"]]},
ezeelkewymers:{"n":"Eelke Wymers","d":"Gorredijk","t":"Eelke Wymers is named as the father of Wymer Eelkes Roukema in Wymer's death record of 1823. His wife was Rinske Wybes. Their son Wymer was born about 1752 and lived all his known life at Gorredijk. Eelke's own second name shows that his father was called Wymer, so the names Eelke and Wymer alternated from father to son for at least four generations, down to the skipper Eelke Wymers Roukema (1786-1848) and his son Wiemer (1821-1894).\n\nThe church burial registers of Opsterland enter a man called Eelke Wymers twice: on 17 August 1749 and on 12 September 1765, the second time in the register of Gorredijk. The second entry fits a father of a boy born about 1752, but the register gives no age or wife, so which of the two (if either) was Wymer's father is not proved.\n\nThe surname Roukema came only in 1811, long after his time.","src":[["Death of son Wymer, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:46d10024-63b4-aa6a-b2a4-f1315317bd0e"],["Burial of an Eelke Wymers, Opsterland, 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ac3fb94a-fe6f-4721-bdc7-0336a3199c94"],["Burial of an Eelke Wymers, Gorredijk, 1765","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:357132d9-18ff-4770-87ee-f05994b588cb"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezrinskewybes:{"n":"Rinske Wybes","d":"Gorredijk","t":"Rinske Wybes was the wife of Eelke Wymers and the mother of Wymer Eelkes Roukema, who was born about 1752 and lived at Gorredijk in Opsterland. She is known from her son's death record of 1823, which names both his parents. Her second name shows that her father was called Wybe.\n\nHer name was carried on in the family in the usual Frisian way: Wymer named his second daughter Rinske (about 1791-1827), and his son Eelke named a daughter Rinske in 1827, though that child lived only six months.\n\nNo marriage, baptism or burial record for her has been found.","src":[["Death of son Wymer, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:46d10024-63b4-aa6a-b2a4-f1315317bd0e"],["Surname registration of her son, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d10dc94e-e95f-4d9b-b4ea-cb08bd7008f7"],["Birth of great-granddaughter Rinske, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:38b32890-ce9d-b9ba-e868-9b979a34285d"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezabestok:{"n":"Abe Hendriks Stok","d":"Grouw","t":"Abe Hendriks lived at Grouw and is named, with the surname Stok, as the father of Sjoerd Abes Stok in Sjoerd's death record of 1816. His wife was Klaaske Hyltjes (Klaeske Hieltjes). His second name shows that his own father was called Hendrik.\n\nThree of their children are known, born over a long span: Auke on 24 May 1736, Baukje on 14 October 1752 and Sjoerd about 1755. Auke and Baukje were not baptised as babies; both were baptised as adults in the Reformed church at Grouw, Baukje in 1781 and Auke in 1789, at the age of 52. Baukje married Auke Jans at Grouw in 1774 and died there in 1833 as Baukje Abes de Boer. Her death record gives her age as 86, which would put her birth about 1747, five years earlier than the date written at her baptism.\n\nThe Frisian tax register of 1749 lists an Abe Hendriks at Grouw with a household of three adults and four children, assessed at nearly 35 guilders, about double the rating of Sjoerd Jacobs, also of Grouw. It is very probably this Abe, though the register does not name his wife.","src":[["Death of son Sjoerd, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d8baf2af-f9aa-e7c0-047f-b170e910e135"],["Adult baptism of son Auke, Grouw, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:49c0bb92-f39f-452d-b736-b2b29185aef2"],["Adult baptism of daughter Baukje, Grouw, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:013d2f42-5f43-4737-b43d-897d610ebead"],["Death of daughter Baukje, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:079bd0a4-7676-fbf6-f906-bc4b1f59d808"],["Tax register of 1749, Grouw","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fda35d2a-6687-42c0-a0eb-09827aef70b6"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezklaaskehyltjes:{"n":"Klaaske Hyltjes","d":"Grouw","t":"Klaaske Hyltjes (also written Klaeske Hieltjes) was the wife of Abe Hendriks Stok of Grouw and the mother of Sjoerd Abes Stok, born about 1755. Her second name shows that her father was called Hyltje. She is named in her son Sjoerd's death record of 1816 and in the adult baptism records of her children Baukje (born 1752, baptised 1781) and Auke (born 1736, baptised 1789) at Grouw.\n\nHer name came back among her grandchildren: Sjoerd's eldest son was Klaas, born about 1777, and his youngest daughter Klaaske, born about 1794, who lived until 1875. Her daughter Baukje died at Grouw in 1833; her death record gives her age as 86, although her baptism entry dates her birth to 14 October 1752.\n\nHer children were born over almost twenty years, from 1736 to about 1755; the baptism record of 1789 names both Abe Hendriks and Klaeske Hieltjes as the parents of the eldest, Auke.","src":[["Death of son Sjoerd, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d8baf2af-f9aa-e7c0-047f-b170e910e135"],["Adult baptism of daughter Baukje, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:013d2f42-5f43-4737-b43d-897d610ebead"],["Adult baptism of son Auke, 1789","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:49c0bb92-f39f-452d-b736-b2b29185aef2"],["Death of daughter Baukje, 1833","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:079bd0a4-7676-fbf6-f906-bc4b1f59d808"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezsjoerdjacobs:{"n":"Sjoerd Jacobs","d":"Grouw","t":"Sjoerd Jacobs lived at Grouw; his second name shows that his father was called Jacob. On 4 February 1748, already living at Grouw, he married Pijttrik (Pietje) Gerrits of Makkum, a port on the Zuiderzee coast, and she came with a church certificate to Grouw. They had at least seven children, baptised in the church at Grouw: Okje (born about 1749), Grijtje or Grietje (30 August 1750, the entry naming only the father), Rinske (1 October 1752), Jacob (8 August 1756), the twins Gerryt and Rinske (6 January 1760) and Jouke (7 August 1763). Giving a second daughter the name Rinske suggests that the first Rinske had died.\n\nThe Frisian tax register of 1749 lists him at Grouw with a household of two adults and one child, assessed at a little over 17 guilders.\n\nHis son Jacob was a fisherman at Grouw, and in 1811 he took the surname Visser (fisherman). Jacob died in 1826 and Okje in 1818, both at Grouw. Grietje married Sjoerd Abes Stok in 1773.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Grietje, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d1f1698-9006-4817-bc7e-4b957841e362"],["Baptism of twins Gerryt and Rinske, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:13844fed-0f14-4528-84bf-cf04a5ee4086"],["Tax register of 1749, Grouw","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b09e84b2-e2ce-455a-afa5-5fc08c0da0cf"],["Death of daughter Okje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c2fce4c3-52f6-593e-de80-55121c124242"],["Death of son Jacob Sjoerds Visser, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:99956b62-fefe-bb4d-5d39-5073b2c23679"],["Death of daughter Grietje, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9094480d-109b-5c7a-a28e-33fa51814564"],["Marriage, Makkum, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:52d7f0f3-1f6c-4195-92e6-cf77319c948d"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezpietjegerrits:{"n":"Pietje Gerrits","d":"Grouw","t":"Pietje Gerrits was the wife of Sjoerd Jacobs of Grouw and the mother of Okje (about 1749), Grietje (1750), Rinske (1752), Jacob (1756), the twins Gerryt and Rinske (1760) and Jouke (1763). Her second name shows that her father was called Gerrit, and her son Gerryt, one of the twins of 1760, carried his name.\n\nShe is named as Pietje Gerrits in the death records of her daughter Okje (1818) and her son Jacob Sjoerds Visser, a fisherman (1826), and the baptism of the twins gives the mother's name as \"... Gerryts\". Her daughter Grietje's death record of 1816, however, calls her Pietje Joukes; the records disagree, and the two from her other children agree with the baptism.\n\nShe was living at Makkum, on the Zuiderzee coast, when she married Sjoerd Jacobs of Grouw there on 4 February 1748; the register writes her name Pijttrik Gerrits and notes that she left with a church certificate for Grouw. No record of her own birth or death has been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Okje, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c2fce4c3-52f6-593e-de80-55121c124242"],["Death of son Jacob Sjoerds Visser, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:99956b62-fefe-bb4d-5d39-5073b2c23679"],["Baptism of twins Gerryt and Rinske, 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:13844fed-0f14-4528-84bf-cf04a5ee4086"],["Death of daughter Grietje, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:9094480d-109b-5c7a-a28e-33fa51814564"],["Marriage, Makkum, 1748","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:52d7f0f3-1f6c-4195-92e6-cf77319c948d"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezbeyerman:{"n":"Georg Lodewijk Beyerman","d":"c. 1737–1816","b":1737,"x":1816,"t":"Georg Lodewijk (Lodewijk) Beyerman was a house painter and glazier (verver en glazenmaker) at Leeuwarden and later at Deinum, a village just west of the city. He was born about 1737. His daughter Dora's death record calls him Lodewijk Jacobs Beyermans, which suggests that his father was called Jacob. His first names and surname are German rather than Frisian, and in 1813 he borrowed money from the Evangelical Lutheran congregation of Leeuwarden, a church many German incomers belonged to; but no record of his birth or arrival in Friesland has been found.\n\nHe first appears in 1770, a painter living in Leeuwarden, when he married Grietje Jans of Marssum in the Westerkerk at Leeuwarden on 2 September. By 1783 they had moved out to Deinum, where they lived \"at the lime works\" (aan 't Kalkwerk) and joined the Reformed church. Grietje died in July 1785.\n\nOn 28 May 1786 he married Beitske Andries of Leeuwarden at Deinum. He was then about 49. Their daughters were baptised at Leeuwarden: Dorothea Tjitske Louisa (Dora) on 5 October 1788, Grietje on 13 February 1791 and Beitske on 18 February 1798; Andrieske was born in 1795. Dora married Klaas Sjoerds of Grouw in 1806, Grietje married Bouwe Douwes de Jong in 1812, and Beitske and Andrieske both married in 1822.\n\nBack at Deinum, he bought and sold small properties in 1810 and 1811, and the civic register (registre civique) drawn up under French rule in 1811-12 enters him as a painter's journeyman (verversknecht). He sold property again in June 1815 and died at Deinum on 29 August 1816, aged 79.","src":[["Banns, Leeuwarden, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7ca1a28f-1bf5-40e8-abdd-423cb9fd997c"],["First marriage, Westerkerk Leeuwarden, 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:db50214a-3bfe-415d-b363-0b6b56e0ffc4"],["Church membership, Deinum, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cab06bdb-0ad0-4988-92a9-6494ee35b13e"],["Banns, Leeuwarden, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e08bbf24-c5fc-4b5a-8513-17158e91a627"],["Second marriage, Deinum, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69e0106e-366c-4c6e-ac74-4fbd8a073e7e"],["Baptism of daughter Dora, Leeuwarden, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3c5c904e-41a0-4f54-abdd-6e7059baed4c"],["Baptism of daughter Grietje, 1791","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4323514e-759f-45df-9cf0-0ac867e4894c"],["Baptism of daughter Beitske, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0107b8f8-daac-45b2-80cd-8fec4595ad14"],["Notarial sale, Deinum, 30 May 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6daa50dd-ce11-475d-ae35-70af7089d664"],["Notarial deed, Deinum, 30 November 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:074c1094-d96e-46e7-8909-53a70f553cca"],["Civic register (registres civiques), Menaldumadeel, 1811-12","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a932c57f-7ee5-4da5-8c81-b6de3f3b979c"],["Marriage of daughter Grietje, 1812","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:50f619cb-34bb-a88d-9ed7-a077c12ea64b"],["Bond to the Lutheran congregation of Leeuwarden, 1813","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:422f1ac7-9144-4b46-ad1a-47d752132485"],["Notarial sale, 16 June 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3df4d257-c108-459a-b17d-99b7427dae84"],["Death record, Deinum, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5cde8b1a-362b-0ddb-7940-b71c089fce1e"],["Marriage of daughter Andrieske, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25ee39b1-d680-8e99-8a1c-dea906fca890"],["Marriage of daughter Beitske, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e659ef3-0d01-8546-ce19-a9c3236e9b0a"],["Death of daughter Grietje, Marssum, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:85900051-b8e2-c2dd-bd8e-4afa8a84b616"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezbeitskeandries:{"n":"Beitske Andries","d":"Leeuwarden","t":"Beitske Andries came from Leeuwarden, the capital of Friesland. Her second name shows that her father was called Andries. On 11 March 1785 she was baptised on her own confession of faith in the Reformed church of Leeuwarden, which means she had not been baptised there as a child.\n\nThe banns for her marriage to Georg Lodewijk Beyerman, a widowed painter and glazier of Deinum, were registered at Leeuwarden on 28 April 1786, and they married at Deinum on 28 May. On 11 September 1786 she transferred her church membership to Deinum.\n\nThe family soon lived in Leeuwarden again, for their daughters were baptised there: Dorothea Tjitske Louisa (Dora) on 5 October 1788, Grietje on 13 February 1791 and Beitske on 18 February 1798, the last named after her. Andrieske, born in 1795, carried her father's name; she married a farmer and lived until 1879. By 1810 the family was back at Deinum.\n\nShe was widowed when Georg Lodewijk died at Deinum on 29 August 1816, and she was still named in her daughters' marriage records in 1822. Dora's death record of 1849 calls her mother Beitske Tjitzes Zondervan, but Dora's baptism and her sisters' records all give Beitske Andries. Her own death has not been found.","src":[["Confession of faith, Leeuwarden, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2dc7815f-e0f1-4865-b68f-2bc69a043b8a"],["Banns, Leeuwarden, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e08bbf24-c5fc-4b5a-8513-17158e91a627"],["Marriage, Deinum, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69e0106e-366c-4c6e-ac74-4fbd8a073e7e"],["Church membership, Deinum, 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:506bff7d-f059-4b77-a03d-52268ceb6993"],["Baptism of daughter Dora, 1788","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3c5c904e-41a0-4f54-abdd-6e7059baed4c"],["Baptism of daughter Beitske, 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0107b8f8-daac-45b2-80cd-8fec4595ad14"],["Death of her husband, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5cde8b1a-362b-0ddb-7940-b71c089fce1e"],["Marriage of daughter Beitske, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8e659ef3-0d01-8546-ce19-a9c3236e9b0a"],["Death of granddaughter Hieke van der Meer, Sint-Annaparochie, 1856","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08953727-22a5-d60d-333d-c244381f65c8"],["Death of daughter Andrieske, 1879","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e51997c-98d3-d25a-5736-6a1ed4d2d6aa"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezhendrikberends:{"n":"Hendrik Berends","d":"Hemrik","t":"Hendrik Berends lived at Hemrik in Opsterland; his second name shows that his father was called Berend. On 13 May 1787 he married Neeltje Martens, also of Hemrik, in the church at Lippenhuizen.\n\nTheir son Berend, who carried the name of Hendrik's father, was born at Hemrik on 8 October 1788; he later took the surname Bloemsma and married in Smallingerland in 1815. A son, Merten, was born at Lippenhuizen on 29 June 1795. Their daughter Ibeltje married Jacob Meines Meinsma of Wijnjeterp in 1809 and died in 1830; her death record names her parents as Hendrik Berends and Neeltje Martens.\n\nNo record of his own birth or death has been found.","src":[["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:509e6ca4-b24c-49fc-8a16-fa268cd1c5ca"],["Marriage of son Berend Hendriks Bloemsma, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a81f10bf-2274-5d73-43d0-6abca0ac2f39"],["Death of daughter Ibeltje, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa8cc44a-530f-7686-bd75-8d5f22062002"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezneeltjemartens:{"n":"Neeltje Martens","d":"Hemrik","t":"Neeltje Martens (Mertens) was living at Hemrik in Opsterland when she married Hendrik Berends, also of Hemrik, at Lippenhuizen on 13 May 1787. Her second name shows that her father was called Marten or Merten, and she gave that name to her son Merten, born at Lippenhuizen on 29 June 1795.\n\nHer other known children were Berend, born at Hemrik on 8 October 1788, later Berend Hendriks Bloemsma, and Ibeltje, who married Jacob Meines Meinsma in 1809. Ibeltje named her own daughter Neeltje, born about 1815.\n\nWhen Ibeltje died at Wijnjeterp in January 1830, Neeltje was still alive and working as a labourer (arbeidster). Her own death has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1787","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:509e6ca4-b24c-49fc-8a16-fa268cd1c5ca"],["Death of daughter Ibeltje, 1830","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa8cc44a-530f-7686-bd75-8d5f22062002"],["Marriage of son Berend, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a81f10bf-2274-5d73-43d0-6abca0ac2f39"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezjiltgeerts:{"n":"Jilt Geerts Wijnstra","d":"c. 1755–1829","b":1755,"x":1829,"t":"Jilt Geerts was born about 1755; his second name shows that his father was called Geert, a name he passed to his son Geert. A Jilt Geerts living at Sparjebird, \"or on the canal\", became a member of the Reformed church in Opsterland in 1772; that is very probably he. He married Janke Hepkes in the church at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 19 January 1781.\n\nTheir children included Hepke (about 1782), named after Janke's father, Geert (about 1786), Roelke (born at Sparjebird, a hamlet in Opsterland, on 30 October 1790 and baptised on 27 November 1791), Jan (30 September 1794, Wijnjeterp), Douwe (about 1797) and Hendrik (baptised 16 March 1800). Douwe married Fimke Jans de Boer in 1826. A daughter, Ebeltje or Abeltje, married Pieter Eelkes Meester of Hemrik; she died at Hemrik in 1868 at a stated age of 92, which would put her birth before her parents' marriage, so either her age is overstated or she came from an earlier marriage.\n\nBy 1828 the family used the surname Wijnstra, taken under the decree of 1811. His son Douwe's death record of 1861 calls him a farmer (boer). He died at Wijnjeterp in 1829, aged 74; the civil register gives 9 July and the death-duty file 9 June. That file lists his children Ebeltje, Geert, Hendrik, Jan and Douwe and records that he owned no real estate. His son Hepke had died in 1818.","src":[["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:83139a82-3a70-406b-8ad9-e26d797f4873"],["Baptism of son Roelke, 1791","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:02d4d6b2-a596-4569-9841-1cb12f714a49"],["Baptism of son Jan, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cbede935-91ca-4aaf-bf89-84019a313717"],["Death of son Hepke, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa2622c5-00aa-5227-8d94-1e6789fba451"],["Death of son Douwe, 1861","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:388dbb8a-9f9b-63c2-ce28-b1dc2fcb2c29"],["Death record, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b0c1c55f-f4ce-e83f-f450-a42e7d09e21b"],["Death-duty file, 1829","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6dbb23e5-92aa-47b8-a287-f40cb5027dd7"],["Church membership, Opsterland, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:834b5d5a-0ff8-4eb1-8e93-fe6fe4475db8"],["Baptism of son Hendrik, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d764c7e6-4aa6-411c-8cb5-bad8f833c709"],["Marriage of son Douwe, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8a6b5c63-b64a-7eae-66b2-6cee5fcb6fe0"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezjankehepkes:{"n":"Janke Hepkes","d":"c. 1760–1836","b":1760,"x":1836,"t":"Janke Hepkes was born about 1760, the daughter of a man called Hepke. She married Jilt Geerts, later Wijnstra, at Wijnjeterp in Opsterland on 19 January 1781. Their eldest known son, Hepke, born about 1782, was named after her father.\n\nHer children included Hepke, Geert (about 1786), Roelke (1791), Jan (1794), Douwe (about 1797) and Hendrik (1800), and the daughter Ebeltje who married Pieter Eelkes Meester of Hemrik. Hepke, a labourer, died at Wijnjeterp in 1818, aged 36.\n\nOn 25 November 1827, in their seventies and late sixties, she and Jilt made their confession of faith together in the Reformed church. Jilt died in the summer of 1829. Janke died at Hemrik on 12 November 1836, aged 76; her death record names her father as Hepke. Ebeltje's death record of 1868 calls her mother Janke Hendriks, which does not agree.","src":[["Marriage, Wijnjeterp, 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:83139a82-3a70-406b-8ad9-e26d797f4873"],["Baptism of son Jan, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cbede935-91ca-4aaf-bf89-84019a313717"],["Death of son Hepke, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aa2622c5-00aa-5227-8d94-1e6789fba451"],["Confession of faith, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:91b3be10-7db2-46e4-9ec1-02ddfa295bb3"],["Death record, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:76a42da6-09be-d781-2a4c-a6dcfa8224e1"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezhendrikklazes:{"n":"Hendrik Klazes de Vries","d":"c. 1756–1841","b":1756,"x":1841,"t":"Hendrik Klazes was born about 1756, the son of Klaas Siegers and Maaike Hendriks. With his wife Neeltje Fokkes he raised one of the largest families in this tree at Hemrik in Opsterland. The eldest sons were named after his father (Klaas) and hers (Fokke), and another son Sijger after his grandfather Sieger.\n\nIn 1811 he registered the surname de Vries at Hemrik. The list gives Klaas 30, Fokke 28, Loltje 25, Tjerk 23, Jan 22, Sijger 21, Maaike 19, Evert 17, Anne 14, Albertje 12, Reinder 11 and Engbert 9, with a grandchild, Sakjen, aged 2. Tjerk, born at Hemrik on 20 February 1788, was called up for military service under Napoleon in 1808 in the district of Lippenhuizen.\n\nHis son Fokke, a labourer, died at Wijnjeterp in January 1824, aged 40, when both his parents were still working as labourers. Evert married Rulke Ibes Veenstra in 1820 and Anne married Grietje Sierds in 1825. Maaike married in Smallingerland in 1822, Sieger died in Ooststellingwerf in 1861, and Jan lived at Hemrik until 1870, latterly as a man of independent means. Albertje married Jan Jilts Wijnstra in 1828.\n\nHendrik was a labourer (arbeider). He died at Hemrik on 29 June 1841, aged 85, and Neeltje the following spring.","src":[["Death record, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ec9ebe8-292e-f0a2-aacb-a0fbb2e54442"],["Surname registration, Hemrik, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:07d1324e-35f7-4074-8635-e6879a23fbe3"],["Conscription of son Tjerk, 1808","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:555e7718-1c2e-4d76-9263-94fb7c3646a1"],["Marriage of daughter Albertje, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96f0b262-7ce0-75b0-a43b-23958184b581"],["Death of his wife, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56d06609-5917-9b7d-0d5e-e4c0b072729d"],["Death of son Fokke, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:959e708e-7487-8b2e-aa8e-5ebe57815a73"],["Marriage of son Evert, 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1b172af6-d792-2f24-4a99-719259f7815e"],["Marriage of son Anne, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:256a47d3-9bc6-d3e4-e831-c3a88f82b33c"],["Marriage of daughter Maaike, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:27c8e53b-4f16-f7d5-5133-d9fbceb2b6cf"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezneeltjefokkes:{"n":"Neeltje Fokkes","d":"c. 1761–1842","b":1761,"x":1842,"t":"Neeltje Fokkes was born about 1761, the daughter of Fokke Everts and a mother called Loltje. She married Hendrik Klazes, later de Vries, and spent her married life at Hemrik in Opsterland. She named three of her children after her own family: Fokke (about 1783) and Evert (about 1794) after her father and grandfather, and Loltje (about 1786) after her mother.\n\nShe bore at least twelve children between about 1781 and about 1802: Klaas, Fokke, Loltje, Tjerk, Jan, Sijger, Maaike, Evert, Anne, Albertje, Reinder and Engbert, all named in the 1811 surname register of the family.\n\nShe was widowed on 29 June 1841. At her own death at Hemrik on 20 April 1842, aged 81, she was a shopkeeper (winkelierse), and the record gives her the surname de Vries and names her parents.","src":[["Death record, 1842","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:56d06609-5917-9b7d-0d5e-e4c0b072729d"],["Surname registration, Hemrik, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:07d1324e-35f7-4074-8635-e6879a23fbe3"],["Death of her husband, 1841","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4ec9ebe8-292e-f0a2-aacb-a0fbb2e54442"],["Marriage of daughter Albertje, 1828","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:96f0b262-7ce0-75b0-a43b-23958184b581"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezwytzeberends:{"n":"Wytze Berends de Vries","d":"c. 1753–1839","b":1753,"x":1839,"p":["ezberendfeitzes","ezaaltjejohannes"],"t":"Wytze Berends was born about 1753, the son of Berend Feitzes and Aaltje Johannes. His brother Auke, born about 1760, became a skipper and died at Terhorne in 1826. Wytze (written Wytse Beerns) was living at Terwispel when he married Lolkjen Aukes of Lippenhuizen in the church at Lippenhuizen on 22 December 1782, and they made their home at Lippenhuizen. Their son Feitze was baptised there on 21 March 1784 and their daughter Wimke (or Minke) on 4 December 1785. Lolkjen, who was some years older than he was and very probably had children from an earlier marriage, died in April 1807.\n\nHe was a man of some property. In January 1824 he and his son Feitze, together with some brothers and sisters de Boer, very probably his stepchildren, bought property from Ibeltje Sjoerds. In 1835 he, Feitze and Wimke sold property at Lippenhuizen to Ruurd Brugts Stobbe and Rinskje Pieters de Boer, and in February 1836 the three made a formal division of property. Wimke married Teye Meints de Boer.\n\nHe died at Lippenhuizen on 15 May 1839, aged 86, described as a rentenier, a man living on his own means. His daughter Wimke died there in 1858, also of independent means. His grandson Wytze, born in 1834, carried his name.","src":[["Death record, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:791b22fc-83ca-45a6-dd46-526e57be821e"],["Marriage of son Feitze, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Notarial purchase, 1824","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b0ce36f1-1c00-4a2e-be16-8f5a0c9bcffc"],["Notarial sale, 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6caef0ea-84f9-47d4-bba5-ba3913c0686b"],["Public sale, 10 December 1835","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8a4e68d0-89f9-4a1d-92d6-da5ba4405a98"],["Division of property, 1836","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:30db6dbe-9c3b-486d-8fcd-1246bd55c34a"],["Death of daughter Wimke, 1858","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:172bd291-df99-66c5-e8bd-73ee44f17895"],["Death of brother Auke Berends de Vries, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8ef7582f-c19f-ce4f-a13b-fcaeeee4ccb3"],["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:19f343eb-8ba9-42df-b762-0017fcec56b0"],["Baptism of son Feitse, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58e848aa-9075-48c5-a4b9-b91e735e5d17"],["Burial of his wife, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5dafecd8-d4bb-4889-aa81-836529fd396b"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"]},
ezlolkjenaukes:{"n":"Lolkjen Aukes","d":"c. 1739–1807 · Lippenhuizen","b":1739,"x":1807,"t":"Lolkjen Aukes was born about 1739; her second name shows that her father was called Auke. She was living at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland when she married Wytse Beerns (Wytze Berends) of Terwispel in the church at Lippenhuizen on 22 December 1782. Their son Feitse was baptised there on 21 March 1784 and their daughter Wimke on 4 December 1785, when Lolkjen was about 46.\n\nShe had very probably been married before. A Lolkjen Aukes married Pyter Feykes at Kortezwaag in 1767, and two farmers of Lippenhuizen, Kornelis Pieters de Boer (died 1827) and Rinske Pieters de Boer (died 1850), were children of Pieter Feikes and Lolkjen Aukes. Wytze Berends later did business with brothers and sisters de Boer, and in 1835 he, Feitze and Wimke sold property to Rinske's husband Ruurd Brugts Stobbe, which fits a family of half-brothers and half-sisters. No record states outright that the two Lolkjens were the same woman.\n\nShe was buried at Lippenhuizen on 13 April 1807, aged 68. The church book notes that she was married and left five children.","src":[["Marriage, Lippenhuizen, 1782","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:19f343eb-8ba9-42df-b762-0017fcec56b0"],["Baptism of son Feitse, 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:58e848aa-9075-48c5-a4b9-b91e735e5d17"],["Baptism of daughter Wimke, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5fc08264-c301-498d-9f22-db142cfc02ad"],["Burial, Lippenhuizen, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5dafecd8-d4bb-4889-aa81-836529fd396b"],["Marriage of a Lolkjen Aukes and Pyter Feykes, Kortezwaag, 1767","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:66d1cafe-f2c9-479b-8e38-ff70fec77701"],["Death of Kornelis Pieters de Boer, 1827","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3767d479-c183-2cbb-443b-4a62ad987431"],["Death of Rinske Pieters de Boer, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d98862c-b7ee-73f3-00af-86ee8149390a"],["Marriage of son Feitze, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Death of her husband, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:791b22fc-83ca-45a6-dd46-526e57be821e"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezberendfeitzes:{"n":"Berend Feitzes","d":"Opsterland","t":"Berend Feitzes lived in Opsterland in the 1700s; his second name shows that his father was called Feitze, and his grandson and great-grandson both carried that name. He and his wife Aaltje Johannes had at least two sons: Wytze, born about 1753, who lived at Lippenhuizen and took the surname de Vries, and Auke, born about 1760, a skipper who died at Terhorne in 1826 under the same surname. Both sons' death records name him as their father.\n\nHe died before September 1800, when the church accounts of Opsterland record the burial of his widow. No record of his own birth, marriage or death has been found.","src":[["Death of son Wytze, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:791b22fc-83ca-45a6-dd46-526e57be821e"],["Death of son Auke, Terhorne, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8ef7582f-c19f-ce4f-a13b-fcaeeee4ccb3"],["Burial of his widow, Opsterland, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69326ab1-44fc-4058-8204-e177ac9e97f2"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezaaltjejohannes:{"n":"Aaltje Johannes","d":"d. 1800 · Opsterland","x":1800,"t":"Aaltje Johannes was the wife of Berend Feitzes and the mother of Wytze Berends de Vries (about 1753-1839) of Lippenhuizen and of Auke Berends de Vries (about 1760-1826), a skipper at Terhorne. Auke's death record gives her full name, Aaltje Johannes; Wytze's gives only Aaltje.\n\nThe church accounts of Opsterland record the burial of the widow of Berend Feitzes on 2 September 1800, very probably Aaltje. A few days later, on 10 September, her oorijzer was bought by H. Halbersma. The oorijzer was the metal headband, often of silver or gold, that Frisian women wore under their lace cap.","src":[["Burial of the widow of Berend Feitzes, 1800","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:69326ab1-44fc-4058-8204-e177ac9e97f2"],["Death of son Auke, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8ef7582f-c19f-ce4f-a13b-fcaeeee4ccb3"],["Death of son Wytze, 1839","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:791b22fc-83ca-45a6-dd46-526e57be821e"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezjanaizes:{"n":"Jan Aizes Jansma","d":"Lippenhuizen","t":"Jan Aizes lived at Lippenhuizen in Opsterland; his second name shows that his father was called Aize, and his children used the surname Jansma, taken under the decree of 1811. He married twice. With his first wife, Jeltje Klazes, he had a daughter Trijntje, born about 1786, who became a farmer's wife (boerin) at Hemrik and died there in 1855.\n\nHis second wife was Aukjen Martens. Their son Marten, named after her father, was born at Lippenhuizen about 1804 and married in 1834; their daughter Aukjen, baptised at Lippenhuizen on 26 April 1807 (the father written Jan Ayses), married Feitze Wytzes de Vries in 1832. All three children's records name him as their father.\n\nHe should not be confused with a younger Jan Aizes Jansma (about 1813-1871), the son of Aize Jans Jansma and Aaltje Luitzens Brouwer. No record of the elder Jan's own birth or death has been found.","src":[["Death of daughter Aukjen, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8334eb66-eb89-af9b-8c72-7dd4c730d7c5"],["Death of daughter Trijntje, 1855","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4883b6af-33f6-7f7a-a1e3-e68ce2d9f312"],["Marriage of son Marten, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:161cc45c-3402-41c6-6171-7b198388f8cd"],["Marriage of daughter Aukjen, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Baptism of daughter Aukjen, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1e2a3d02-a663-491a-9fea-0bc0d7af8367"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezaukjenmartens:{"n":"Aukjen Martens","d":"c. 1770–1838 · Lippenhuizen","t":"Aukjen Martens was the second wife of Jan Aizes Jansma of Lippenhuizen in Opsterland. Her second name shows that her father was called Marten, and her son Marten, born about 1804 at Lippenhuizen, carried that name. Her daughter Aukjen, baptised at Lippenhuizen on 26 April 1807, was named after her.\n\nBoth children's records name her as their mother: Marten's marriage in 1834 and Aukjen's marriage to Feitze Wytzes de Vries in 1832 and her death in 1850. Her stepdaughter Trijntje, from Jan's first marriage, became a farmer's wife at Hemrik.\n\nAn Aukjen Martens, a labourer, died at Lippenhuizen on 19 March 1838, aged 68; her father is given only as Marten. She is very probably this Aukjen, which would put her birth about 1770, but the record does not name her husband or children.","src":[["Marriage of son Marten, 1834","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:161cc45c-3402-41c6-6171-7b198388f8cd"],["Marriage of daughter Aukjen, 1832","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25e96e7f-c8c6-5526-3a3d-0c483a050d95"],["Death of daughter Aukjen, 1850","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8334eb66-eb89-af9b-8c72-7dd4c730d7c5"],["Baptism of daughter Aukjen, 1807","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1e2a3d02-a663-491a-9fea-0bc0d7af8367"],["Death of an Aukjen Martens, Lippenhuizen, 1838","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0452fe88-0ce3-d07c-ade6-f9ca4be116b7"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null],"b":1770,"x":1838},
ezdouwelourens:{"n":"Douwe Lourens","d":"Hemrik","t":"Douwe Lourens lived in the parish of Lippenhuizen, Terwispel and Hemrik in Opsterland in the 1760s; his second name shows that his father was called Lourens. With his wife Hendrikjen Alberts he had at least three sons baptised there: Lourens (20 February 1763, two weeks old), Albert (9 December 1764) and a second Lourens (31 August 1766, three and a half weeks old). Reusing the name Lourens shows that the first boy had died.\n\nHe is in this tree as the probable father of Albert Douwes of Hemrik (about 1765-1807), the father of Fettje Alberts de Boer. The link rests on Albert's age at death and on the name of Albert's daughter Hendrikje, not on a record naming Albert's parents, so it is uncertain.\n\nA Douwe Lourens had children baptised at Beetsterzwaag in the 1770s with a wife Sjoerdje Harmens, and the widow of a Douwe Lourens was buried in Opsterland in 1803; whether either is this man is not known.","src":[["Baptism of son Lourens, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a949f921-e570-4e04-8e53-98399708992e"],["Baptism of son Albert, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e2fac35b-0fc0-44f0-924c-54f6f4ef5737"],["Baptism of son Lourens, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c3ecb436-9704-4c5b-bcbb-6562af0e04d4"],["Burial of the widow of a Douwe Lourens, Opsterland, 1803","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:89aac031-d7bb-c6dc-27de-2ee794210a78"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
ezhendrikjenalberts:{"n":"Hendrikjen Alberts","d":"Hemrik","t":"Hendrikjen Alberts was the wife of Douwe Lourens in the parish of Lippenhuizen, Terwispel and Hemrik in Opsterland. Her second name shows that her father was called Albert, and her son Albert, baptised on 9 December 1764, carried his name. Her other known children were two sons named Lourens, baptised in 1763 and 1766.\n\nShe is in this tree as the probable mother of Albert Douwes of Hemrik (about 1765-1807). Albert named his younger daughter Hendrikje, which fits, but no record names Albert's parents, so the link is not proved. Her own birth and death have not been found.","src":[["Baptism of son Albert, 1764","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e2fac35b-0fc0-44f0-924c-54f6f4ef5737"],["Baptism of son Lourens, 1763","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a949f921-e570-4e04-8e53-98399708992e"],["Baptism of son Lourens, 1766","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c3ecb436-9704-4c5b-bcbb-6562af0e04d4"]],"s":"h","r":["nl"],"p":[null,null]},
tcpetervgestel:{"n":"Petrus van Gestel","d":"Valkenswaard","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Petrus (Peter) van Gestel lived at Valkenswaard, in the Kempen heathland south of Eindhoven, in the early 1700s. His wife was Henrica van Dijck, written Henrica Janssen van Dijck in the first baptism, which shows that her father was a Jan.\n\nSeven of their children were baptised in the Catholic register of Valkenswaard: Digna on 8 July 1710, Joannes on 4 January 1712, Mathias on 19 February 1714, Martinus on 3 April 1717, Maria Catharina on 5 March 1719, Elisabeth on 24 November 1720 and Catharina on 22 October 1724. Several godparents came from his wife's family: Martina Janssen van Dijck in 1710, Joannes van Dijck in 1712, Gualterus Janssen van Dijck in 1714 and Martinus van Dijck in 1717. Elisabeth's godfather came from Liempde.\n\nTheir son Mathias stayed in Valkenswaard, married there in 1742 and became one of the village's overseers of the poor. Through Mathias's son Peter, who moved to Tilburg, Petrus is an ancestor of the van den Berg family.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Digna, Valkenswaard, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:13BC0FC7-A6CD-438F-ABFD-3707037D7651"],["Baptism of son Joannes, 1712","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:46B5DB5F-59EA-44A8-839A-74526EA5553E"],["Baptism of son Mathias, 1714","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:791F84BE-015C-43B6-8BDB-9B42BA8A315B"],["Baptism of son Martinus, 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:A70E08D9-D153-4A56-8252-20FCB656E8AB"],["Baptism of daughter Maria Catharina, 1719","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:0018A448-2E05-401F-A465-9D7537541A23"],["Baptism of daughter Elisabeth, 1720","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:5697f150-f598-936e-9cc8-7046f0af69e8"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:8B9AF844-3AED-45C2-8B0F-761825DC7C2A"]]},
tchenricavdijck:{"n":"Henrica van Dijck","d":"Valkenswaard","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Henrica van Dijck was the wife of Petrus van Gestel of Valkenswaard. The priest wrote her as Henrica Janssen van Dijck at the baptism of her first known child, a patronymic showing that her father was called Jan.\n\nShe had seven children baptised at Valkenswaard between 1710 and 1724: Digna (1710), Joannes (1712), Mathias (19 February 1714), Martinus (1717), Maria Catharina (1719), Elisabeth (1720) and Catharina (1724). Members of the van Dijck family stood as godparents to several of them, among them Martina Janssen van Dijck, very likely her sister, in 1710, and Gualterus Janssen van Dijck at Mathias's baptism in 1714.\n\nHer son Mathias remained at Valkenswaard, and his son Peter carried the van Gestel name to Tilburg in the 1780s.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Digna, Valkenswaard, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:02cf9187-bf46-24cc-960f-ebfbaf659b9c"],["Baptism of son Mathias, 1714","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rhe:791F84BE-015C-43B6-8BDB-9B42BA8A315B"],["Baptism of daughter Catharina, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:ca6b2145-efa6-d4b8-3ed9-745cdb11df58"]]},
tcwilhelmuslinsen:{"n":"Wilhelmus Linsen","d":"Lommel","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Wilhelmus (Guilhelmus, Willem) Linsen lived at Lommel, a Kempen village of heath and sand just south of Valkenswaard; it lies in today's Belgian province of Limburg. His wife was Elisabeth Bourlet, whom the priest once described as Elisabetha Jacobi Bourlet, 'Jacob's daughter'.\n\nNine of their children were baptised at Lommel over twenty years: Anna Maria (22 August 1697), Dymphna (14 August 1700), Gerardus (26 July 1702), a second Gerardus (20 April 1706), the twins Matthias and Gisbertus (2 July 1708), Maria (29 December 1710), a second Gisbertus (10 December 1712) and Elizabetha (21 August 1717). Giving a new baby the name of a child who had died was usual, so the first Gerardus and the first Gisbertus probably died young. Members of his wife's family were often godparents: Egidius Bourlet in 1700, Theodorus Bourlet in 1708 and Philippus Bourlet in 1717. Wilhelmus himself was godfather to children of neighbours in 1706, 1708 and 1726.\n\nHis youngest daughter Elisabeth married Mathias van Gestel at Valkenswaard in 1742, and her brother Gijsbertus stood godfather to her first son the next year.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Anna Maria, Lommel, 1697","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:885F7D06-8AF8-4951-9ECA-91E437E7049D"],["Baptism of daughter Dymphna, 1700","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:FA2D5968-7A84-4CAB-80F0-4C3BD778412A"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1702","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:7EBD89CD-17B0-46BA-BF1E-EFB9625043A4"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1706","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:A8430D65-4CE7-42AD-AD27-37185F177F53"],["Baptism of twin Matthias, 1708","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:411EC549-95B1-4C60-8DBA-389D237DBBAC"],["Baptism of twin Gisbertus, 1708","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:D2007579-BD3C-41D8-8315-771E2190828B"],["Baptism of daughter Maria, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:F778E0C8-1CA6-44F6-9E2A-C8C3DD963C51"],["Baptism of son Gisbertus, 1712","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:DFAEE66D-A321-4CFD-BC27-CD7251AD38A1"],["Baptism of daughter Elizabetha, 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:01790A99-F167-4652-A834-C0AFDD6C0271"],["Godfather at a Lommel baptism, 1726","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:55BBD5CE-F62F-4585-8779-A79B0D43BA38"]]},
tcelisabethbourlet:{"n":"Elisabeth Bourlet","d":"Lommel","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Elisabeth Bourlet (also written Boirlet and Bourleth) was the wife of Wilhelmus Linsen of Lommel. At the baptism of her son Gisbertus in December 1712 the priest called her Elisabetha Jacobi Bourlet, 'daughter of Jacob'.\n\nShe had nine children baptised at Lommel between 1697 and 1717: Anna Maria, Dymphna, two sons named Gerardus, the twins Matthias and Gisbertus, Maria, a second Gisbertus and, last, Elizabetha on 21 August 1717. Her own relatives appear as godparents: Egidius Bourlet, Theodorus Bourlet, Maria Bourlet and Philippus Bourlet.\n\nA Jacobus Bourlet and his wife Anna Timmermans had children baptised at Lommel between 1676 and 1681, among them Egidius (1676) and Theodorus (1681), the same names as two of Elisabeth's children's godfathers. That makes them very possibly her parents, but no baptism of Elisabeth has been found to prove it. Her youngest daughter, Elisabeth Linsen, married Mathias van Gestel of Valkenswaard in 1742.","src":[["Baptism of son Gisbertus naming her 'Jacobi', Lommel, 1712","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:DFAEE66D-A321-4CFD-BC27-CD7251AD38A1"],["Baptism of daughter Anna Maria, 1697","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:885F7D06-8AF8-4951-9ECA-91E437E7049D"],["Baptism of daughter Elizabetha, 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:01790A99-F167-4652-A834-C0AFDD6C0271"],["Baptism of Egidius Bourlet, son of Jacobus Bourlet and Anna Timmermans, 1676","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:F0425C57-2698-4E5B-8CE7-9BDE275A47A1"],["Baptism of Theodorus Bourlet, son of Jacobus Bourlet and Anna Timmermans, 1681","https://www.openarchieven.nl/sla:40A110D7-80AA-4041-90F0-5E33534FBFD3"]]},
tccornelisdelepper:{"n":"Cornelis Peters de Lepper","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis de Lepper, called Cornelis Petersse de Lepper ('son of Peter'), married Jenneke Peterse van der Linden before the aldermen of Oisterwijk on 24 June 1708, after banns on 9 June. Oisterwijk lies a few kilometres east of Tilburg, and the de Lepper family appears in the court and church records of both places in these years.\n\nTheir son Adrianus was baptised at Tilburg on 28 September 1710; the register calls the parents Cornelius Peter de Lepper and Joanna Peters van der Linden. A Maria Cornelis de Lepper, born at Tilburg, married Jan Teunis van Hest there in 1735, and when Adriaan de Lepper married in 1742 a Jan van Hest was his witness as brother-in-law, so Maria was probably Adriaan's sister.\n\nThe link between this Cornelis and the Adriaan de Lepper who married Adriana Maria Vermeer is a probability rather than a proven fact. A second Adrianus de Lepper, son of Joannes de Lepper and Cornelia van Gurcum, was baptised at Oisterwijk in 1715, other men called Adrianus de Lepper were having children around Tilburg in the 1740s and 1750s, among them an Adrianus Cornelius de Lepper married to Helena van Es, and Adriaan's marriage entry gives his birthplace as Enschot rather than Tilburg.","src":[["Marriage, Oisterwijk, 24 Jun 1708","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:744974f0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Adrianus, Tilburg, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1f3f250e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of Maria Cornelis de Lepper and Jan Teunis van Hest, 1735","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:f4f2ccaa-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tcjennekevdlinden:{"n":"Jenneke Peters van der Linden","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jenneke (Joanna) Peters van der Linden, 'Peter's daughter', married Cornelis Petersse de Lepper before the aldermen of Oisterwijk on 24 June 1708. Jenneke is the everyday Brabant form of Johanna, and the Latin baptism register calls her Joanna.\n\nHer son Adrianus was baptised at Tilburg on 28 September 1710. A daughter, Maria Cornelis de Lepper, born at Tilburg, married Jan Teunis van Hest in 1735. If Adrianus is the Adriaan de Lepper who married Adriana Maria Vermeer in 1742, he had at least seven children at Tilburg, among them Johanna Catharina, who married Peter van Gestel in 1783.\n\nAs with her husband, the link to Adriaan is probable but not proven: another Adrianus de Lepper was baptised at Oisterwijk in 1715, and several men of that name lived around Tilburg in the 1740s and 1750s.","src":[["Marriage, Oisterwijk, 24 Jun 1708","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:744974f0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Adrianus, Tilburg, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1f3f250e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tccornelisvermeer:{"n":"Cornelis Vermeer","d":"b. c. 1690 · Tilburg","b":1690,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Cornelis Vermeer was born at Tilburg about 1690; he was 21 when he married Marij (Maria) Verbruggen, 22, also of Tilburg, on 11 January 1711 after banns on 27 December 1710. Both bride and groom had a stepfather as witness, so each had lost a father and seen their mother remarry.\n\nFour of their children appear in the Tilburg baptism register: Cornelius Franciscus (7 February 1714), Adriana Maria (23 August 1719), Anna Maria (24 April 1725) and Gertrudis (8 March 1727). Their daughter Adriana Maria is probably the woman who married Adriaan de Lepper in 1742, though that link is not proven: the bride, entered as Jenne Marie, had a brother Peter as her witness, and no Peter has been found among this couple's children.\n\nIf so, her daughter Johanna Catharina de Lepper, who married Peter van Gestel, brought this old Tilburg family into the van den Berg line. His wife Maria was buried at Tilburg on 25 May 1753, as the wife of Cornelis Vermeer.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 11 Jan 1711","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ede4c8f0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage with witnesses","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:db471d2d-09f5-a18b-7962-fcda5a0878e8"],["Baptism of son Cornelius Franciscus, 1714","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1fd60af0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Adriana Maria, 1719","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:20f5f9fe-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Anna Maria, 1725","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2213dd92-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Gertrudis, 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2283a35c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Marriage of Adriaan de Lepper and Jenne Marie Vermeer, with her brother Peter as witness, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:a5ac0dab-6c13-7b7a-e044-7c0a2ff3fba9"],["Burial of his wife, Tilburg, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0c4af8cd-9445-c6c8-caf7-addac64677f6"]]},
tcmariaverbruggen:{"n":"Maria Verbruggen","d":"c. 1689–1753 · Tilburg","b":1689,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Maria (Marij) Verbruggen was born at Tilburg about 1689; she was 22 when she married Cornelis Vermeer, 21, there on 11 January 1711. Her witness was her stepfather, which shows that her own father had died and her mother had married again.\n\nFour of her children appear in the Tilburg baptism register: Cornelius Franciscus (baptised 7 February 1714), Adriana Maria (23 August 1719), Anna Maria (24 April 1725) and Gertrudis (8 March 1727). Adriana Maria is probably the woman of that name who married Adriaan de Lepper in July 1742, but the link is not proven, because that bride had a brother Peter and no son Peter of Maria's has been found.\n\nMaria Verbruggen was buried at Tilburg on 25 May 1753, entered as the wife of Cornelis Vermeer.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 11 Jan 1711","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:ede4c8f0-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Adriana Maria, 1719","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:20f5f9fe-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Gertrudis, 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2283a35c-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial, Tilburg, 1753","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:0c4af8cd-9445-c6c8-caf7-addac64677f6"]],"x":1753},
tcnorbertusreijnen:{"n":"Norbertus Reijnen","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Norbertus (Norbert) Reijnen, also written Rijnen, lived at Tilburg with his wife Mechtildis van den Bos, whom the priests also wrote as Mechelina or Mechtelina. Five of their children are in the Tilburg baptism register: Maria Elisabetha (26 April 1733), Petronilla (21 August 1734), Joanna (16 April 1736), Joanna Maria (10 October 1737) and Gerardus (10 October 1742). There was also a son Martinus, who was witness for his brother Gerardus at his wedding in 1772.\n\nGerardus married as Geraard Norbart Rijnen, 'son of Norbert', and his first son was named Norbertus in 1773. A Norbertus Rijnen was baptised at Tilburg on 19 August 1710, the son of Dionisius Denis Rijnen and Elizabeth Jan Geerts; he may be this man, but that has not been proven.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Maria Elisabetha, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2365f45a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Petronilla, 1734","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:23a30f2a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna, 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:23fd976a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna Maria, 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:244b0964-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2546ff62-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Son's marriage with witnesses, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:15f9444a-7b50-4f0d-babc-6a3101616b23"],["Possible own baptism, 1710","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1f3a3332-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
tcmechtildisvdbos:{"n":"Mechtildis van den Bos","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Mechtildis van den Bos was the wife of Norbertus Reijnen of Tilburg. The baptism register spells her first name Mechtildis, Mechelina, Mechtelina and Mechlina, all forms of the old saint's name Mechteld, and her surname van den Bos or van den Bosch.\n\nShe had at least six children: Maria Elisabetha (baptised 26 April 1733), Petronilla (21 August 1734), Joanna (16 April 1736), Joanna Maria (10 October 1737), Gerardus (10 October 1742) and Martinus. Her son Gerardus married Maria Catharina de Beer in 1772, and their daughter Geertrui married Johannes Hamers, the great-great-grandfather of Maria van den Berg.","src":[["Baptism of daughter Maria Elisabetha, 1733","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2365f45a-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Joanna Maria, 1737","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:13a17c50-4ba7-77d6-abe5-0b4843e24bbe"],["Baptism of son Gerardus, 1742","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:1ffa012a-af7a-4714-0c5b-0e962f176447"]]},
tcjanbaptistdebeer:{"n":"Jan Baptist de Beer","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Jan Baptist (Joannes Baptista) de Beer married Gertruij Brocken in the Catholic church at Tilburg on 8 September 1738. His full name in a later register, Jan Baptist Cornelis de Beer, shows that his father was a Cornelis, and he gave that name to his first son.\n\nTheir children baptised at Tilburg include Cornelius (3 August 1740), Maria Catharina (28 December 1741) and Cornelia Catharina (1 October 1746). A Cornelis de Beer who died at Tilburg in 1823, aged 68, the husband of Anna Maria van den Dries, was also their son, born about 1755; the reuse of the name suggests that the first Cornelius had died young.\n\nJan Baptist was alive in April 1772, when he was witness at the marriage of his daughter Maria Catharina to Gerardus Reijnen.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 8 Sep 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:982168de-0529-5a1a-5de4-b37b01f63cef"],["Baptism of son Cornelius, 1740","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:24dcae1e-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Maria Catharina, 1741","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:251f7406-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Baptism of daughter Cornelia Catharina, 1746","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:262961ea-3863-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Death of son Cornelis, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:9aa20164-3861-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Daughter's marriage, with him as witness, 1772","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:15f9444a-7b50-4f0d-babc-6a3101616b23"]]},
tcgertruidabrocken:{"n":"Gertruij Brocken","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Gertruij (Gertrudis, Geertrui) Brocken married Jan Baptist de Beer in the Catholic church of Tilburg on 8 September 1738. Brocken was, and still is, a Tilburg family name.\n\nHer children baptised at Tilburg include Cornelius (3 August 1740), Maria Catharina (28 December 1741) and Cornelia Catharina (1 October 1746), and a second Cornelis was born about 1755; he died at Tilburg in 1823, and his death record names his parents as Jan Baptist de Beer and Geertrui Brocken. Her daughter Maria Catharina married Gerardus Reijnen in 1772 and was the grandmother of Gerardus Hamers.","src":[["Marriage, Tilburg, 8 Sep 1738","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:982168de-0529-5a1a-5de4-b37b01f63cef"],["Baptism of daughter Maria Catharina, 1741","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:39ec7521-eb0d-f4b5-b945-a0e197b12d78"],["Death of son Cornelis, 1823","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:09e2564b-4b71-f920-5eec-67397253d8cd"]]},
tcwillemdekok:{"n":"Willem de Kok","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Willem de Kok of Tilburg was the father of Adriaan Willem de Kok. He was the witness for his son when Adriaan married Johanna Span at Tilburg on 2 March 1783, and the register names him as the groom's father.\n\nTwo other Tilburg-born children of a Willem de Kok married soon afterwards: Maria Willem de Kok married Peter Jan Peijnenburg of Haaren on 17 April 1785, and Justinus Willem de Kok married Adriana Maria Heijnssen on 7 February 1790. At both weddings the witness was Johannes de Rooij, described as the stepfather. If they were Adriaan's sister and brother, as seems likely, Willem died between March 1783 and April 1785 and his widow married Johannes de Rooij. His wife's name and his own baptism have not been found.","src":[["Son Adriaan's marriage, with him as witness, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2169f9be-7d86-36ab-8706-4697f3f14680"],["Marriage of Maria Willem de Kok, 1785","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b0fdbb5a-c6dc-ccb6-b5c3-36b0b2526e59"],["Marriage of Justinus Willem de Kok, 1790","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:b985ff30-d24c-597d-1703-8162ba1a0442"]]},
tcadriaanspan:{"n":"Adriaan Span","d":"Tilburg","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"t":"Adriaan Span of Tilburg was the father of Johanna Span. He was her witness when she married Adriaan Willem de Kok at Tilburg on 2 March 1783, and the register names him as the bride's father. In later notarial deeds she is called Johanna Adriaan Span, 'Adriaan's daughter'.\n\nThrough Johanna he was the grandfather of Anna Cornelia, Maria, Wilhelmus, Adriana and Petrus de Kock, and the great-great-great-grandfather of Maria van den Berg. An Adriaan Cornelis Steven Span appears in many Tilburg court and notarial records between 1752 and 1761, including a will in 1761, and a child of his, Cornelis Ariaan Span, was buried in 1757; he may be the same man, but the records seen do not prove it.","src":[["Daughter's marriage, with him as witness, 1783","https://www.openarchieven.nl/bhi:2169f9be-7d86-36ab-8706-4697f3f14680"],["Notarial will of Johanna Adriaan Span, 1802","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:2b1f58ae-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Notarial will of Adriaan Cornelis Steevens Span, 1761","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:223ee18c-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"],["Burial of Cornelis Ariaan Span, child, 1757","https://www.openarchieven.nl/rat:fbd65542-3862-11e0-bcd1-8edf61960649"]]},
gsandriesaaldriks:{"n":"Andries Aaldriks","d":"m. 1717 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"src":[["Marriage of Andries Aaldrix and Rijktien Lukens, Sappemeer 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ce698b27-ca11-2fd0-dccb-933912529f9b"],["Baptism of Aaldrik, Sappemeer 1718","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a7b79a18-fbde-dfbd-b832-e3ba4059b63f"],["Baptism of Luiken (Lucas), Sappemeer 1721","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:59078c2b-42a0-7048-7c54-1a17649ca18d"],["Baptism of Klaasjen, Sappemeer 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:724854cf-f931-515c-b994-43be00bc69a7"],["Marriage contract of Aaldrik Andries Hes, 1771","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:411cb5c4-d2cb-79c4-5600-acc515b873e3"],["Marriage of Lucas Andries Hes, Noordbroek 1760","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:59a5c0b5-5608-1745-d2da-33874343df2a"],["Baptism of another Aaldrik, son of Andries Aaldrix and Anna Hindriks, Groningen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:0ed26f5d-c0de-5432-c624-0c547f42ec5b"]],"t":"Of Sappemeer, in the Groningen peat colonies. As Andries Aaldrix he married Rijktien Lukens in the church there on 25 April 1717. Three children were baptised at Sappemeer: Aaldrik on 27 February 1718, who had the name of Andries's own father, as Andries's second name shows; Luiken (Lucas) on 25 May 1721; and Klaasjen on 2 January 1724.\n\nThose three names come together again in 1771, when Aaldrik Andries Hes, by then an innkeeper at Sappemeer, signed his second marriage contract with Lucas Andries Hes and Klaasjen Andries Hes standing on his side. Lucas had married Sara Tammen at Noordbroek in 1760. The family name, written Hes, Hesse and Hesze, was in use by 1759.\n\nA man of the same name, Andries Aaldrix, had a son Aaldrik baptised in the city of Groningen in January 1727, with a wife named Anna Hindriks. No record shows whether he was the same man."},
gsrijktienlukens:{"n":"Rijktien Lukens","d":"m. 1717 · Sappemeer","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"src":[["Marriage of Andries Aaldrix and Rijktien Lukens, Sappemeer 1717","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:ce698b27-ca11-2fd0-dccb-933912529f9b"],["Baptism of Aaldrik, Sappemeer 1718","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a7b79a18-fbde-dfbd-b832-e3ba4059b63f"],["Baptism of Luiken (Lucas), Sappemeer 1721","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:59078c2b-42a0-7048-7c54-1a17649ca18d"],["Baptism of Klaasjen, Sappemeer 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:724854cf-f931-515c-b994-43be00bc69a7"]],"t":"She married Andries Aaldrix at Sappemeer on 25 April 1717; the marriage entry calls her Rijktien Lukens and the baptism of her first child Rijktien Lukas, both meaning daughter of Luken or Lucas. Her son Aaldrik was baptised at Sappemeer on 27 February 1718. A second son of Andries Aaldrix, baptised there on 25 May 1721 (the entry names only the father), was given her father's name, Luiken, and grew up as Lucas Andries Hes. A daughter Klaasjen followed on 2 January 1724, when the register calls the mother Rijkjen.\n\nHer name was carried on by the innkeeper Aaldrik Andries Hes, if he is her son of 1718: he called his first daughter, baptised in January 1760, Rijkje."},
gsklaaspeters:{"n":"Klaas Peters","d":"m. 1724 · Wagenborgen","s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"src":[["Marriage contract of Klaas Peters and Jeije Jacobs, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8c6b5b32-b9c8-231c-41de-d9866c4f696c"],["Marriage, Wagenborgen 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8654c377-dc63-3ecd-84ad-e6fd2b1f8a55"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1726","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:619dddd5-f705-4594-769f-e59987431021"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bda4e04b-07cb-eb69-dd1d-f6773453e49e"],["Baptism of Antje, Wagenborgen 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a2e21639-512d-71b9-be86-373c6da258c9"],["Burial of Jeije Jacobs, Wagenborgen 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:675b1835-81eb-f46e-070b-b2e896f72939"],["Baptism of Jacob Leew, son of Geeske Ekkes, Wagenborgen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:dd06167b-5d7a-f326-eba0-7cc49883ffe3"],["Marriage contract of Antje Klaassen and Frerik Berents, 1751","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:6ccd06de-583e-5150-87bc-e5ffc485100b"]],"t":"Of Wagenborgen, a village between the Oldambt and the Dollard coast. On 13 June 1724 he signed a marriage contract with Jeije Jacobs, with Peter Klasen, Jan Peters and Martjen Jans standing on his side and Jacob Tiakkes on hers, and they married in the Wagenborgen church on 23 July. His second name shows that his father was called Peter. Three children were baptised at Wagenborgen: Jacob on 8 September 1726, a second Jacob on 16 November 1727 and Antje on 2 September 1731. Jeije was buried there on 23 November 1736.\n\nThe tree places the second Jacob as Jacob Klaassen, who married Grietje Berents at Wagenborgen in 1748. In 1751 Jacob and Grietje stood beside an Antje Klaassen when she contracted to marry Frerik Berents, and Jacob named his first son Jeije and later sons Klaas and Peter. A boy called Jacob Leew, born to an unmarried mother, Geeske Ekkes, was also baptised at Wagenborgen in March 1727, and Jacob Klaassen used the surname Leew in later life, so the link is not certain."},
gsjeijejacobs:{"n":"Jeije Jacobs","d":"d. 1736 · Wagenborgen","x":1736,"s":"b","p":[null,null],"r":["nl"],"src":[["Marriage contract of Klaas Peters and Jeije Jacobs, 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8c6b5b32-b9c8-231c-41de-d9866c4f696c"],["Marriage, Wagenborgen 1724","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:8654c377-dc63-3ecd-84ad-e6fd2b1f8a55"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1726","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:619dddd5-f705-4594-769f-e59987431021"],["Baptism of Jacob, Wagenborgen 1727","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:bda4e04b-07cb-eb69-dd1d-f6773453e49e"],["Baptism of Antje, Wagenborgen 1731","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:a2e21639-512d-71b9-be86-373c6da258c9"],["Burial of Jeije Jacobs, Wagenborgen 1736","https://www.openarchieven.nl/gra:675b1835-81eb-f46e-070b-b2e896f72939"]],"t":"Of Wagenborgen. She married Klaas Peters there on 23 July 1724, after a marriage contract signed on 13 June at which Jacob Tiakkes stood on her side; her second name means daughter of Jacob. Her children were baptised in the Wagenborgen church: Jacob in September 1726, another Jacob in November 1727 and Antje in September 1731. She was buried at Wagenborgen on 23 November 1736.\n\nHer first name is rare. Jacob Klaassen, placed in the tree as her son, gave it to his first child in 1749 and, as Jeje, to a son born in 1781, after the first had been buried as a baby."},
sncharleshicks:{"n":"Charles Hicks","d":"c. 1777–1860 · Warmington, Northamptonshire","b":1777,"x":1860,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Charles Hicks lived at Warmington, in the north-east corner of Northamptonshire, close to the River Nene and the Huntingdonshire border. On 11 October 1799 he married Mary Wright in St Mary's church there, after banns.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Warmington: John (12 October 1800), Catharine (7 March 1803), William (14 October 1804), Elizabeth (13 April 1806), Mary (29 August 1809) and Charles (21 November 1813), when Charles was described as a labourer. Baby Charles was buried on 12 June 1814, aged 30 weeks, and Mary was buried on 14 April 1816, aged 43. Charles married again: with his second wife Ann he had Joseph, baptised in 1818, and Harriet, in 1820.\n\nCharles Hicks was buried at Warmington on 19 January 1860, aged 83. A Charles Hicks witnessed the wedding of William Hicks and Elizabeth Helmes at Water Newton in 1828. That the William baptised at Warmington in 1804 is the William Hicks of Alwalton is likely but not proven.","src":[["Marriage, Warmington, 11 Oct 1799","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684ceced865574b95e4c02b1"],["Baptism of John, 1800","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec3f865574b95e4bc4e9"],["Baptism of Catharine, 1803","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec40865574b95e4bc5bf"],["Baptism of William, 1804","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec41865574b95e4bc664"],["Baptism of Elizabeth, 1806","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec44865574b95e4bc773"],["Baptism of Mary, 1809","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec47865574b95e4bc8d0"],["Baptism of Charles, 1813","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec4e865574b95e4bcb49"],["Burial of Charles (infant), 1814","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684ceccc865574b95e4bf87b"],["Baptism of Joseph (mother Ann), 1818","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec54865574b95e4bcde2"],["Baptism of Harriet (mother Ann), 1820","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec56865574b95e4bcf03"],["Burial of Charles Hicks, aged 83, 1860","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cecc3865574b95e4bf4f0"],["Marriage of William Hicks and Elizabeth Helmes, Water Newton, 1828 (witness Charles Hicks)","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/68384a2b865574838ea08125"]]},
snmarywright:{"n":"Mary Wright","d":"c. 1773–1816 · Warmington, Northamptonshire","b":1773,"x":1816,"s":"p","p":[null,null],"r":["uk"],"t":"Mary Wright married Charles Hicks at St Mary's church, Warmington, Northamptonshire, on 11 October 1799, after banns. Warmington is a farming village on the River Nene, a few miles west of Peterborough.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Warmington: John in 1800, Catharine in 1803, William on 14 October 1804, Elizabeth in 1806, Mary in 1809 and Charles in November 1813. Little Charles died at 30 weeks and was buried on 12 June 1814.\n\nMary was buried at Warmington on 14 April 1816, aged 43, so she was born in about 1773. Her husband married again and lived until 1860. Her son William is probably the William Hicks who married at Water Newton in 1828 and settled at Alwalton, but that link is not proven.","src":[["Marriage, Warmington, 11 Oct 1799","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684ceced865574b95e4c02b1"],["Baptism of William, 1804","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684cec41865574b95e4bc664"],["Burial of Charles (infant), 1814","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684ceccc865574b95e4bf87b"],["Burial of Mary Hicks, aged 43, 1816","https://www.freereg.org.uk/search_records/684ceccc865574b95e4bf8b3"]]},
hzmindertaekes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Mindert Taekes van der Meer","d":"m. 1784 · Rottevalle","p":[null,null],"t":"Of Rottevalle, a village on the canal between Drachten and Surhuisterveen. On 30 May 1784 he married Taapke Jiskes at Rottevalle; both were living there. His patronymic shows his father was called Taeke, the name he gave his son.\n\nIn 1811 he registered the surname van der Meer at Oudega (Smallingerland), giving his address as Rottevalle no. 93. He listed his children Wietske (25, then at Blija), Liske (21), Teeke (17), Jisk (13) and Jogchum (8), and three grandchildren. Taapke died that October, aged 45.\n\nTaeke married in 1814 and moved to Surhuisterveen; Jisk married at Smallingerland in 1826 and Jogchum, born at Rottevalle, at Achtkarspelen in 1843. Liske died in 1868 and Jisk in 1875, both aged 77. Mindert's own death has not been found.","src":[["Marriage, Rottevalle 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:414ee8df-c3ec-4b45-93b4-d0fe97833acf"],["Surname registration, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0811530a-6983-4002-b05c-1b117cf46b4a"],["Marriage of son Taeke, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06338d61-ba84-50a9-7edc-782e4581757d"],["Marriage of son Jisk, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5673d07e-0d3c-0bc6-1e1a-2f90dffa7525"],["Marriage of son Jogchum, 1843","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:dae9cbef-c8fc-d50e-9742-aa6987d9ea66"],["Death of daughter Liske, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:94588610-d9c0-162f-c304-8acb350089e3"]]},
hztaapkejiskes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Taapke Jiskes","d":"c. 1766–1811 · Rottevalle","b":1766,"x":1811,"p":["hzjiskhotzes","hzliskejochums"],"t":"Born about 1766, the daughter of Jisk Hotzes and Liske Jochums of Rottevalle, whose family later took the name van der Veen. On 30 May 1784 she married Mindert Taekes of Rottevalle. Their children included Wietske (about 1786), Liske (about 1790, named after Taapke's mother), Taeke (about 1794), Jisk (about 1798, named after her father) and Jogchum (about 1803).\n\nShe died on 18 October 1811, aged 45, and was buried at Opeinde; the burial register notes that she was married and left five children. Her husband had registered the family surname van der Meer only months before. Her son Taeke named his eldest daughter Taapke after her in 1818.","src":[["Death record, Smallingerland 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b71d3798-de5a-5c0c-e6ad-e25eb1f2765f"],["Burial, Opeinde 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c8ff8f4e-11ad-48e7-ade6-ca8c472c257d"],["Marriage, Rottevalle 1784","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:414ee8df-c3ec-4b45-93b4-d0fe97833acf"],["Surname registration of her husband, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0811530a-6983-4002-b05c-1b117cf46b4a"]]},
hzjiskhotzes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Jisk Hotzes","d":"Rottevalle","p":[null,null],"t":"A labourer (arbeider) at Rottevalle, in the peat country where Smallingerland, Achtkarspelen and Tietjerksteradeel meet. He married Liske Jochums, and their children, who later took the surname van der Veen, included Hotze (about 1750), Jogchum (about 1763), Jisk (about 1764), Taapke (about 1766) and Iebeltje (about 1776). Hotze, Jogchum and Jisk were labourers at Rottevalle all their lives, and all three died there between 1825 and 1831.\n\nHe had died by January 1822, when Liske died as his widow. His daughter Taapke married Mindert Taekes van der Meer in 1784.","src":[["Death of his widow Liske Jochums, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5d7e5b03-5c95-b9ae-b0c8-0d7ac506e744"],["Death of son Hotze Jiskes van der Veen, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:776b1400-a5a9-ef25-1c1c-d958f84d3dde"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Jogchum Jiskes van der Veen","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ca29a58f-906a-4dcb-82b5-d54eaf06f684"],["Death of son Jisk, 1831","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ed01269a-4889-41e8-d96f-3866baf3675a"],["Death of daughter Taapke, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b71d3798-de5a-5c0c-e6ad-e25eb1f2765f"],["Marriage of daughter Iebeltje, 1815","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4dcce0e6-55b7-186d-ffa4-bf275a9ed9ab"]]},
hzliskejochums:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Liske Jochums","d":"c. 1728–1822 · Rottevalle","b":1728,"x":1822,"p":[null,null],"t":"Born about 1728, she married Jisk Hotzes, a labourer at Rottevalle, and worked as a labourer (arbeidster) herself. Her children included Hotze (about 1750), Jogchum (about 1763, named after her father), Jisk (about 1764), Taapke (about 1766) and Iebeltje (about 1776). The family took the surname van der Veen after 1811.\n\nHer daughter Taapke died in 1811, aged 45, and Taapke's daughter Liske was named after her. Liske outlived her husband and died in Achtkarspelen on 18 January 1822, a widow aged 94, the oldest age reached by anyone on this side of the family in the records found so far.","src":[["Death record, 1822","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5d7e5b03-5c95-b9ae-b0c8-0d7ac506e744"],["Death of son Hotze, 1825","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:776b1400-a5a9-ef25-1c1c-d958f84d3dde"],["Death of daughter Taapke, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b71d3798-de5a-5c0c-e6ad-e25eb1f2765f"],["Marriage of son Jogchum, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:1bd34676-7e06-1d2f-f538-34d7cdf4ef82"]]},
hztjeerdhinnes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Tjeerd Hinnes","d":"c. 1747 · Hardegarijp","b":1747,"p":[null,null],"t":"Born about 1747; in December 1781 he gave his age as 34 or 35. He lived near Bergum in Tietjerksteradeel. In May 1770 a Tjeerd Hinnes of Bergum, by name and village the same man, registered at Leeuwarden his intention to marry Sjoerdtje Symens of that town. On 29 May 1774, both living near Bergum, he married Folkjen Tjepkes at Veenwouden.\n\nThe village tax registers (speciekohieren) list him as head of a household at Bergum in 1770, as a lodger at another Bergum address in 1771, and from 1772 as head of household at Hardegarijp, where he was still living in 1805. On 27 December 1781, as a farmer (huisman) at Gaastmaburen under Hardegarijp, he reported a burglary to the court of Tietjerksteradeel: shoe buckles had been stolen.\n\nHis children with Folkjen included Tjipke (about 1779), named after her father, and Aukjen (about 1780). Both later lived at Rottevalle and took the surname de Vries. His death has not been found.","src":[["Intention to marry, Leeuwarden 1770","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:68e87aa6-96a7-473f-8087-3c291e2c2cd1"],["Marriage, Bergum/Veenwouden 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:01ceab34-5284-44e5-b1cd-562b2e2f2254"],["Tax register, Bergum no. 181","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0d80523b-bbb1-414b-b300-536fb738825f"],["Tax register, Hardegarijp no. 6","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:54f17116-149a-45a3-942b-e5dc7b5533b3"],["Court information book, 27 Dec 1781","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:45bd54b4-7c46-4cbb-bb0c-0d15f941cac6"],["Marriage of daughter Aukjen, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06338d61-ba84-50a9-7edc-782e4581757d"],["Death of son Tjipke, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7eb04577-c1ec-9731-20e8-cb29514419cc"]]},
hzfolkjentjipkes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Folkjen Tjipkes","d":"m. 1774 · Bergum","p":[null,null],"t":"She lived near Bergum in Tietjerksteradeel when she married Tjeerd Hinnes on 29 May 1774; the wedding was held at Veenwouden. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Tjipke. From 1772 her husband headed a household at Hardegarijp, and in 1781 he was a farmer at Gaastmaburen there.\n\nHer son Tjipke, born about 1779, was named after her father, and her daughter Aukjen was born about 1780. She is named as Folkjen Tjipkes, the mother of the bride, when Aukjen married Taeke Minderts van der Meer in 1814. In Tjipke's death record of 1847 the mother is written as Folkje Douwes, a clerk's slip that the other records do not share.","src":[["Marriage, Bergum/Veenwouden 1774","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:01ceab34-5284-44e5-b1cd-562b2e2f2254"],["Marriage of daughter Aukjen, 1814","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:06338d61-ba84-50a9-7edc-782e4581757d"],["Death of son Tjipke, 1847","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7eb04577-c1ec-9731-20e8-cb29514419cc"]]},
hzpiebelieuwes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Piebe Lieuwes","d":"b. 1729 · Eernewoude","b":1729,"p":["hzlieuwepiebes","hzbieukeysses"],"t":"Baptised at Eernewoude on 6 February 1729, the eldest son of Lieuwe Piebes, farmer, peat master and schoolmaster of the village, and his first wife Bieuke Ysses Venema of Grouw. His mother died while he was a boy, and his father married twice more.\n\nOn 8 March 1750 he married Antje Rutgers, a widow from Suameer living at Eernewoude. Their children were baptised at Eernewoude: Baukje (1750; the reconstruction takes the name as a form of his mother's, Bieuke), Maaike (1752), Neeltje (1754) and Lieuwe (born on 25 December 1755, baptised in January 1757), who later took the name Kooistra.\n\nThe village tax register shows a Piebe Lieuwes heading a household in Eernewoude from 1754, moving to Wartena in 1757, and again from 1783, having come from Suawoude, until 1797; the reconstruction places him at Suawoude in 1782 and 1783. His son Lieuwe's death record of 1826 names his mother as \"Antje Bapes\", a clerk's mistake for Antje Rutgers.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1729)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a14df923-43eb-4d9c-9e5d-735efc02ed20"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3159663b-a535-49d9-a237-7fb8a6521a1d"],["Tax register, Eernewoude no. 27","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:287c567d-6d0e-4b88-8daa-c315bcbc63e8"],["Death of son Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra, 1826","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:30134fc2-07a2-dd48-fff5-26bdf07686de"]]},
hzantjerutgers:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Antje Rutgers","d":"Suameer · Eernewoude","p":[null,null],"t":"She came from Suameer in Tietjerksteradeel. On 23 August 1744 she married Rommert Gerbens of Eernewoude, and after his death she married Piebe Lieuwes, eldest son of the village schoolmaster, at Eernewoude on 8 March 1750. A Jeltje Rutgers, also of Eernewoude, had become the schoolmaster's third wife a few months earlier, in December 1749.\n\nWith Piebe she had Baukje (1750), Maaike (1752), Neeltje (1754) and Lieuwe (born 25 December 1755), all baptised at Eernewoude. The family later lived at Suawoude. Her son Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra, a labourer at Eernewoude, was the father of Antje Kooystra, who married into the IJsselstein family of Warga.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Antje Rutgers","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:10ea3e55-9a5a-4ee6-a27b-f8856a86236f"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1729)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a14df923-43eb-4d9c-9e5d-735efc02ed20"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes (b. 1708)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a767ac1d-be52-4471-aa62-dcb24f86b3ee"]]},
hzlieuwepiebes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Lieuwe Piebes","d":"1708–after 1775 · Eernewoude","b":1708,"p":["hzpiebelieuwessr","hzfeikjefokkes"],"t":"Baptised at Eernewoude on 14 May 1708, the son of Piebe Lieuwes and Feikje Fokkes. In 1727 he was still a minor, with his uncle Klaas Lieuwes, the village magistrate, as guardian; he inherited from Johannes Klases of Eernewoude, and in 1743 from his uncle Klaas.\n\nHe was a farmer (boer), a peat master (veenbaas) and the village schoolmaster at Eernewoude. In 1728 he farmed two of the holdings listed in the village voting register (stemkohier), numbers 7 and 13. The 1749 tax assessment (quotisatie) calls him a school servant \"who earns his keep and no more\", with a household of three adults and five children. By 1768 he was assessed on a fortune of 500 pounds, and in 1775 he declared on oath that he had nothing.\n\nHe married three times at Eernewoude: in 1727 Bieuke Ysses Venema of Grouw, mother of Piebe (1729), Tjitse (1730), Ysse (1731), Scholte (1733) and Tjalke (1736); in 1738 Sijtje Ages, mother of Feikje (1738), Klaas (1740), Trijntje (1742), Age (1745) and Baukje (1747); and in 1749 Jeltje Rutgers, mother of a second Tjalke (1751). His son Age Lieuwes Venema followed him as a peat master. Lieuwe died at Eernewoude in or after 1775.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes (b. 1708)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a767ac1d-be52-4471-aa62-dcb24f86b3ee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:543f4941-26bd-43e1-bf28-f4ac3aa82c53"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Age Lieuwes Venema","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f861500f-c364-44d8-9473-ba7ea69704ff"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Lieuwes (b. 1740)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:855f1324-6c43-4237-bb12-67d24fc9d44e"]]},
hzbieukeysses:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Bieuke Ysses Venema","d":"Grouw · m. 1727 Eernewoude","p":[null,null],"t":"She came from Grouw, the lake village in Idaarderadeel, and her patronymic shows her father was called Ysse. On 13 July 1727 she married Lieuwe Piebes at Eernewoude, a young farmer who became the village schoolmaster and peat master.\n\nTheir five sons were baptised at Eernewoude: Piebe on 6 February 1729, Tjitse on 12 November 1730, Ysse, named after her father, on 25 November 1731, Scholte in 1733 and Tjalke in 1736. She died before March 1738, when Lieuwe married again. The name Venema was used again by her husband's son Age Lieuwes Venema, and her eldest son Piebe named his first daughter Baukje, which the reconstruction reads as a form of Bieuke.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes (b. 1708)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a767ac1d-be52-4471-aa62-dcb24f86b3ee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1729)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a14df923-43eb-4d9c-9e5d-735efc02ed20"]]},
hzpiebelieuwessr:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Piebe Lieuwes","d":"b. 1683 · Eernewoude","b":1683,"p":["hzlieuweklases","hzjeltjepiebes"],"t":"Baptised at Eernewoude on 23 December 1683, the son of Lieuwe Klases and Jeltje Piebes; his elder brother Klaas, baptised in January 1682, became village magistrate (dorprechter) and tax receiver of Eernewoude, a deacon and churchwarden.\n\nIn February 1705 he registered his marriage at Garijp to Feikje Fokkes of Boornbergum, who brought her church certificate from Boornbergum on 8 March. Their son Lieuwe was baptised at Eernewoude on 14 May 1708. Piebe died before his son came of age: in 1727 Lieuwe was a minor under the guardianship of his uncle Klaas.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1683)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d85f5ef4-51ce-4734-9164-ffe194e2adee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Feikje Fokkes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:84fc81fe-6ec3-418e-b736-9229662b0738"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:543f4941-26bd-43e1-bf28-f4ac3aa82c53"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes (b. 1708)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a767ac1d-be52-4471-aa62-dcb24f86b3ee"]]},
hzfeikjefokkes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Feikje Fokkes","d":"Boornbergum · m. 1705","p":[null,null],"t":"She came from Boornbergum in Smallingerland, and her patronymic shows that her father was called Fokke. In February 1705 she and Piebe Lieuwes of Eernewoude registered their marriage at Garijp, and on 8 March 1705 her church at Boornbergum sent the certificate (attestatie) that allowed her to marry and settle in his parish.\n\nTheir son Lieuwe was baptised at Eernewoude on 14 May 1708. He grew up to be a farmer, peat master and the schoolmaster of Eernewoude, and was the great-great-grandfather of Antje Lieuwes Kooystra.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Feikje Fokkes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:84fc81fe-6ec3-418e-b736-9229662b0738"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1683)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d85f5ef4-51ce-4734-9164-ffe194e2adee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lieuwe Piebes (b. 1708)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a767ac1d-be52-4471-aa62-dcb24f86b3ee"]]},
hzlieuweklases:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Lieuwe Klases","d":"Eernewoude · fl. 1682–1686","p":[null,null],"t":"Of Eernewoude in Tietjerksteradeel, where three of his children by Jeltje Piebes were baptised: Klaas on 19 January 1682, Piebe on 23 December 1683 and Gerlske on 2 May 1686. These baptisms are the earliest dates found on the Kooistra line.\n\nHis son Klaas became the village magistrate (dorprechter) and tax receiver of Eernewoude, deacon in 1720 and churchwarden in 1734, and in 1727 was guardian of Piebe's orphaned son Lieuwe. His daughter Gerlske married Hendrik Fokkes about 1711 and in 1768 was assessed on 2,020 pounds, one of the larger fortunes in the village; she died in 1771 or 1772.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:543f4941-26bd-43e1-bf28-f4ac3aa82c53"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1683)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d85f5ef4-51ce-4734-9164-ffe194e2adee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gerlske Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:13a6d242-dd92-457d-ba6b-a84a22745441"]]},
hzjeltjepiebes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Jeltje Piebes","d":"Eernewoude · fl. 1682–1686","p":[null,null],"t":"Wife of Lieuwe Klases of Eernewoude. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Piebe, and she gave that name to her second son, baptised at Eernewoude on 23 December 1683. Her other children baptised there were Klaas (19 January 1682) and Gerlske (2 May 1686).\n\nKlaas became the village magistrate and receiver of Eernewoude; Gerlske married Hendrik Fokkes and was one of the wealthier villagers in 1768. Piebe's son Lieuwe became the village schoolmaster and peat master, and through him Jeltje is an ancestor of the Kooystra and IJsselstein families of Warga and Wartena.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:543f4941-26bd-43e1-bf28-f4ac3aa82c53"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Piebe Lieuwes (b. 1683)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d85f5ef4-51ce-4734-9164-ffe194e2adee"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gerlske Lieuwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:13a6d242-dd92-457d-ba6b-a84a22745441"]]},
hzaukejacobus:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Auke Jacobus","d":"m. 1762 · Eernewoude","p":[null,null],"t":"He married Jetske Folkerts, also of Eernewoude, in the village church there on 20 June 1762. Their children were baptised at Eernewoude: Akke on 27 October 1765 and Jacob, named after Auke's father, on 17 September 1769. By 1797 the family was living near Wartena in Idaarderadeel, a few kilometres across the lakes.\n\nHis daughter Akke married Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra in 1788 and named her son Jacob after him in 1798. Auke is mentioned in the court records of Tietjerksteradeel; his death has not been found.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Auke Jacobus","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51dffd0b-df63-4e2f-9e87-b0bbd3b83b60"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Akke Aukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d57930bc-1a64-4fa0-9368-6793f204392f"],["Baptism of grandson Jacob, Garijp 1798","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ebd34606-2470-4086-8905-3e1e6e1c2637"]]},
hzjetskefolkerts:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Jetske Folkerts","d":"m. 1762 · Eernewoude","p":[null,null],"t":"Of Eernewoude, where she married Auke Jacobus on 20 June 1762. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Folkert. Her daughter Akke was baptised at Eernewoude on 27 October 1765 and her son Jacob on 17 September 1769; by 1797 the family lived near Wartena.\n\nAkke married Lieuwe Piebes Kooistra in 1788 and named her second child, born in December 1791, Jetske after her. That granddaughter later married Jan Freerks Terpstra, a farmer at Workum, and died there in 1852.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Auke Jacobus","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:51dffd0b-df63-4e2f-9e87-b0bbd3b83b60"],["Baptism of granddaughter Jetske, Garijp 1792","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4c536880-dda7-4ffe-89a5-120e8f50aec9"],["Death of granddaughter Jitske, Workum 1852","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bccc0f30-ed81-5ae2-4a8d-ec909ea13aba"]]},
hzeeltjebeens:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Eeltje Beens","d":"Wartena","p":[null,null],"t":"Eeltje Beens, also written Eeltje Beerents, is known from the death records of two of his sons, both of Wartena in Idaarderadeel, who died within two days of each other in February 1818. Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, a fisherman (visser), died on 22 February aged 74, so was born about 1744; Been (Berend) Eeltjes Visser, a ship's carpenter's journeyman, died on 20 February aged 63, so was born about 1755. Both records name their mother as Hinke Sjoerds.\n\nSjoerd carried his mother's father's name and Been his father's father's, following the usual Frisian pattern. In 1811 both sons registered the surname Visser at Wartena, which fits the fishing that Sjoerd lived by. Been named his own eldest son Eeltje.","src":[["Death of son Been Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25bd7764-e82a-54e4-edf9-cd69dbaf31d4"],["Death of son Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e57d70fc-79cb-d196-b0fb-72ff37787e76"],["Surname registration, Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7eb5a7cc-d2c7-41c6-9bfa-08989d634d0d"],["Surname registration, Been Eeltjes Visser, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f4848cd0-55eb-44f1-a5c6-c79f8b1a3d66"]]},
hzhinkesjoerds:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Hinke Sjoerds","d":"Wartena","p":[null,null],"t":"Wife of Eeltje Beens, and mother of two Wartena men who registered the surname Visser in 1811: Sjoerd, a fisherman born about 1744, named after her father, and Been (Berend), a ship's carpenter's journeyman born about 1755. Both sons died at Wartena in February 1818, two days apart, and both death records name her.\n\nHer name was handed on: Sjoerd named his daughter Hinke, born about 1794, who lived at Wartena as Hinke Sjoerds Visser. A Hinke Sjoerds of Wirdum married Evert Martens at Wartena in 1755, the year Been was born; whether she was a different woman or this Hinke remarrying has not been established, and that record is not used here.","src":[["Death of son Been Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:25bd7764-e82a-54e4-edf9-cd69dbaf31d4"],["Death of son Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, 1818","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e57d70fc-79cb-d196-b0fb-72ff37787e76"],["Surname registration, Sjoerd Eeltjes Visser, 1811","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7eb5a7cc-d2c7-41c6-9bfa-08989d634d0d"],["Population register, Hinke Sjoerds Visser (born 1794)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:bb713f6a-9ddd-5cd5-7942-758f4f3864b9"]]},
hzfolkertgjolts:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Folkert Gjolts","d":"d. 1787 · Eernewoude","x":1787,"p":[null,null],"t":"He came from Oudega in Smallingerland and married Neeltje Dirks of Eernewoude at Oudega on 18 August 1743. In 1748 the couple were living in the house of her father, Dirk Jans, at Eernewoude. He moved to another house in the village as a lodger in 1752 and became its head in 1753, and he remained there until his death in 1787.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Eernewoude: Gjolt (1744), named after his father, Durk (Dirk) (27 September 1750), Lolke (1755), who became a farmer at Oudega, Oedske (baptised 1758; a second Oedske was born in 1764) and Jan (1765), a skipper at Birdaard. Durk named his eldest son Folkert in 1781. After Folkert's death the house passed to a son of the same name.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Neeltje Dirks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e44e9bda-6712-4162-97e8-b0822ced077e"],["Tax register, Eernewoude no. 37 (1748)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:167ff3cb-b627-402d-8176-ddcc5484864f"],["Tax register, Eernewoude no. 36","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2fd47a78-2f4b-4c2a-8d21-a49e98a0ca34"],["Baptism of Gjolt, 1744","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2f0ec198-6b69-47f0-846b-c1f881b552bb"],["Baptism of Durk, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:008e5353-905e-4ed8-aceb-2c88c88cc9ff"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lolke Folkerts","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:722ad183-2435-47e8-9552-027357ed262f"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Jan Folkerts","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:33c3170a-7fd1-4be8-9f2c-bbaf816f21e9"]]},
hzneeltjedirks:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Neeltje Dirks","d":"1725–1806 · Eernewoude","b":1725,"x":1806,"p":[null,null],"t":"Baptised at Eernewoude on 11 March 1725, the daughter of Dirk Jans and Jeltje Fridses; her father headed a household in the village until his death in 1752. On 18 August 1743, aged 18, she married Folkert Gjolts of Oudega at Oudega, and the couple lived with her father at first.\n\nHer children were baptised at Eernewoude between 1744 and 1765; among them were Gjolt, Durk (1750), Lolke (1755), Oedske and Jan (1765). Her son Durk, named after her father, became village magistrate of Eernewoude, and he named his daughter Neeltje after her in 1791. In 1772 she is recorded in the court books, and in 1792 she stood surety for her son Jan, a skipper.\n\nShe was widowed in 1787 and died at Eernewoude on 1 March 1806, aged 82, leaving five children and two grandchildren according to the reconstruction.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Neeltje Dirks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:e44e9bda-6712-4162-97e8-b0822ced077e"],["Baptism of Durk, 1750","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:008e5353-905e-4ed8-aceb-2c88c88cc9ff"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Dirk Folkerts de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:90585bdd-0384-4351-b34c-06d9462c2521"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Oedske Folkerts de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:aca1b66b-a973-4ae0-89b1-7cad6cda3fa5"],["Tax register, Eernewoude no. 37 (1748)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:167ff3cb-b627-402d-8176-ddcc5484864f"]]},
hzroelpieters:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Roel Pieters","d":"Oudwoude · m. 1755 Eernewoude","p":[null,null],"t":"He came from Oudwoude, a village in Kollumerland in the north-east of Friesland, and married Froukje Geukes at Eernewoude on 1 November 1755. Their daughter Antje was born at Eernewoude on 3 May 1758. The couple were still in Eernewoude in 1782, when they appear in the court books of Tietjerksteradeel.\n\nAntje married Durk Folkerts, later village magistrate, in 1778, and named her son Roelof after him in 1787. A different Roel Pieters, an assistant of the court of Lemsterland married to Jetske Hattums, also appears in the reconstruction and is not the same man.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Froukje Geukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ab59270-ed86-4d67-aa9e-6d43657e03e9"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Antje Roelofs","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08229ba0-18ff-498f-aa97-d9a004a2909b"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Roelof Dirks de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:70f07c88-44c8-43b9-b35b-58b4b4ae8e6a"]]},
hzfroukjegeukes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Froukje Geukes","d":"Eernewoude · m. 1755","p":[null,null],"t":"Of Eernewoude, where she married Roel Pieters of Oudwoude on 1 November 1755. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Geuke. Her daughter Antje was born at Eernewoude on 3 May 1758, and the family was still in the village in 1782.\n\nAntje married Durk Folkerts de Jong in 1778 and named her eldest daughter Froukje after her in 1783. That granddaughter married Jan Pieters Zandburg in 1807 and died at Eernewoude in 1816. An older Froukje Geukes, who died at Garijp in 1738 childless, was a different woman.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Froukje Geukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3ab59270-ed86-4d67-aa9e-6d43657e03e9"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Antje Roelofs","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:08229ba0-18ff-498f-aa97-d9a004a2909b"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Froukje Dirks de Jong","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:124460e2-2f19-4a7a-8f8a-85d6bdfb2395"]]},
hzrienkwarners:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Rienk Warners","d":"1710–1787 · Oostermeer","b":1710,"x":1787,"p":["hzwarnerritskes","hzhiltjejans"],"t":"Born at Oostermeer on 18 January 1710, the son of Warner Ritskes, a farmer and peat master at 't Witveen, and his first wife Hiltje Jans. In 1723 his father married the widow Jetske Minses, and on 3 April 1730 Rienk married her daughter, his stepsister Grietje Wybrens. Both made their profession of faith at Oostermeer in 1734.\n\nHe farmed at 't Witveen, a hamlet of Oostermeer on the edge of the peat. The 1749 tax assessment calls him \"a reasonably well-to-do farmer\", with five adults and four children in the house. In 1768 he was assessed on 275 pounds plus 666 pounds inherited from his father, raised to 2,000 pounds in 1774. With Grietje, who died between 1753 and 1756, he had many children, among them Hiltje (1733), Jetske (1736), Stijntje (baptised 9 November 1738), Wybren (1739), Tetje (1741) and Jan (1751). On 8 April 1759 he married Geeske Jans and had more sons: Johannes (1761), Heinse (1764) and Bareld (1768).\n\nHe died at 't Witveen on 9 May 1787; his gravestone is recorded. His children took the surname van Warners. His son Johannes, a farmer and merchant at 't Witveen, lost his left hand in 1797 when his gun burst while he was marching against the rising at Kollum, and petitioned the provincial government for compensation.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Rienk Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b7c5d343-9311-4ecd-934b-9cceeaaa0942"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Stijntje Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:18a6693e-063a-4c70-b670-19f0d2a1e615"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Johannes Rienks van Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8a57ae4a-b659-4630-9c93-779eaa7c9a2c"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Heinse Rienks van Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:972b6aae-9d7d-414c-9652-086875b0e6e7"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Jan Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b18dec77-5010-4971-b139-e59b038f4e1b"]]},
hzgrietjewybrens:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Grietje Wybrens","d":"1711–c. 1755 · Garijp","b":1711,"p":["hzwybrenklases","hzjetskeminses"],"t":"Baptised at Garijp on 27 December 1711, the daughter of Wybren Klases, village magistrate (dorprechter) and tax receiver of Garijp, and Jetske Minses. Her father died between 1719 and 1722, and in 1723 her mother married Warner Ritskes, a farmer at 't Witveen near Oostermeer.\n\nOn 3 April 1730, aged 18, she married her stepfather's son Rienk Warners at Oostermeer, and the couple farmed at 't Witveen. Both made their profession of faith in 1734. Their children, baptised at Oostermeer, included Hiltje (1733), Jetske (1735 and 1736), Stijntje (9 November 1738), Wybren (1739, named after her father), Tetje (1741) and Jan (1751). Hiltje married Roel Heres at Rottevalle in 1755, and Stijntje married Tobias Pieters in 1761.\n\nGrietje died at Oostermeer between 1753 and 1756, and Rienk married again in 1759.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Grietje Wybrens","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3d65fae6-c4fd-47e6-81d9-a2641765ff83"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Hiltje Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2d3d267f-8f59-4c8d-bf29-060cfe37d149"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybren Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ab408d5b-25e1-4c3e-bee0-b9d0b776c18b"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Stijntje Rienks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:18a6693e-063a-4c70-b670-19f0d2a1e615"]]},
hzwarnerritskes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Warner Ritskes","d":"1676–1763 · Oostermeer","b":1676,"x":1763,"p":[null,null],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 16 January 1676, the son of Ritske Harkes and Hiltje Warners of 't Witveen; his sister Tetje married Jacob Abes of Bergum in 1682 and died within the year. On 12 November 1693, apparently not yet 18, he married Hiltje Jans of Oostermeer, and he made his profession of faith in 1696.\n\nHe was a farmer and peat master (veenbaas) at 't Witveen, where he is on the church membership list of 1709. In 1717 he bought, with Oeds Hendriks, half of one of the voting farms of Suawoude, and he served several times as guardian of orphaned children. With Hiltje he had many children baptised at Oostermeer, among them Stijntje (1695), Ritske (1700), Jan (1706), Harke (1709), Rienk (born 18 January 1710), Johannes (1714) and Petrus (1716). After Hiltje's death he married Jetske Minses, widow of the magistrate of Garijp, on 17 January 1723; their son Willem was born about 1725.\n\nIn later life he lived at Rottevalle, where he died on 3 October 1763; he was buried at Oostermeer. His estate was assessed in 1768 at 4,000 pounds and divided among his children. His son Ritske became a farmer, peat master and distiller at Rottevalle. This baptism of 1676 is one of the earliest dates reached on the family's Frisian lines.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Warner Ritskes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2dbe6bf4-6338-4f16-9276-dd78c67a9ba1"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tetje Ritskes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:399168c4-c72c-4540-b446-76c06b6e23d0"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Ritske Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:938db406-e79b-4be9-b214-aa7b03a3fd5c"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Petrus Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:8bb66be4-00f6-4be2-b6ee-01df8b7cd59d"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Stijntje Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:706ff1d0-7204-4587-a246-0ee4173ba8e0"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Willem Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3b237b06-39b7-46ec-9b58-b1bcfeb3a7d9"]]},
hzhiltjejans:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Hiltje Jans","d":"m. 1693 · Oostermeer","p":[null,null],"t":"Of Oostermeer, where she married Warner Ritskes of 't Witveen on 12 November 1693. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Jan, a name she gave to her son Jan, born in 1706.\n\nHer children, baptised at Oostermeer over more than twenty years, included Stijntje (1695), Ritske (1700), Jan (1706), Harke (1709), Rienk (1710), Johannes (1714) and Petrus (1716); a son Douwe was a labourer at Oostermeer in the 1740s. Most of her sons farmed or dug peat around 't Witveen and Rottevalle. She died before January 1723, when Warner married again.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Warner Ritskes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2dbe6bf4-6338-4f16-9276-dd78c67a9ba1"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Rienk Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:b7c5d343-9311-4ecd-934b-9cceeaaa0942"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Johannes Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ae787d7d-d548-4c8f-93e4-cb6e55c0012a"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Douwe Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:c9443419-7cd4-4af4-a304-a8e1d2fe98eb"]]},
hzwybrenklases:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Wybren Klases","d":"1669–c. 1720 · Oostermeer","b":1669,"p":[null,null],"t":"Born at Oostermeer in 1669, the son of Klaas Wybrens and Baukje Wybes. On 5 June 1701 he married Jetske Minses of de Tieke, a hamlet under Suameer, and he made his profession of faith at Oostermeer in 1702. The couple lived first at de Tieke and later at Siegerswoude under Garijp.\n\nHe was the village magistrate (dorprechter) and tax receiver (ontvanger) of Garijp, a man of standing whose name appears often in the court books of Tietjerksteradeel, including as guardian of orphaned children in 1708. His children were Trijntje (1702), Baukje (1704), Klaas (1706), Grietje (baptised at Garijp 27 December 1711) and Minse (1715).\n\nHe died at Garijp between 1719 and 1722; his household goods were sold at auction in 1723, and in 1728 his widow and heirs owned one of the voting farms of Garijp. His widow married Warner Ritskes, and his daughter Grietje married Warner's son Rienk. His birth in 1669 is the earliest date reached on this branch.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybren Klases","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0363c635-39c5-4c97-8408-852e57861138"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Minse Wybrens","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ace22166-8b56-4550-8c90-7adc2c96646d"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Baukje Wybrens","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:f5f681d7-bfb6-4dee-89ee-0eb38c2819c9"]]},
hzjetskeminses:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Jetske Minses","d":"de Tieke · m. 1701","p":[null,null],"t":"From de Tieke, a hamlet under Suameer in Tietjerksteradeel. On 5 June 1701 she married Wybren Klases of Oostermeer, later village magistrate of Garijp, and she made her profession of faith at Oostermeer in 1702. Her children were Trijntje (1702), Baukje (1704), Klaas (1706), Grietje (1711) and Minse (1715), named after her father.\n\nWidowed by 1722, she married Warner Ritskes, farmer and peat master at 't Witveen, at Oostermeer on 17 January 1723, and moved to Oostermeer with a church certificate from Garijp in November 1724. Their son Willem was born about 1725. In 1730 her daughter Grietje married Warner's son Rienk, so that Jetske was both mother and stepmother to the couple.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Jetske Minses","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2bc6677f-c91a-4033-aea5-306b091a22c1"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybren Klases","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0363c635-39c5-4c97-8408-852e57861138"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Willem Warners","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3b237b06-39b7-46ec-9b58-b1bcfeb3a7d9"]]},
hzliebbeklazes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Liebbe Klazes Wiersma","d":"b. 1755 · Bergum","b":1755,"p":["hzklaasouwes","hzgepkeliebbes"],"t":"Born on 8 February 1755, near Bergum according to the population reconstruction, and baptised at Veenwouden on 27 April 1755, the youngest child of Klaas Ouwes, then a labourer, and Gepke Liebbes, whose father's name he carried. By 1780 the family had a farm at Eestrum, in Tietjerksteradeel.\n\nHe became a farmer (boer) at Eestrum himself. On 10 November 1786 he married Lutske Sjoukes of Eestrum, and both made their profession of faith there in 1790. In 1796 he acted as guardian (voorspraak) for the children of Thomas Willems and Wytske Sipkes. In 1811 he registered as a cultivator and took the surname Wiersma (written Wierdsma in the French register). His children, all born at Eestrum, were Klaas (1787, died), Sjouke (1789), Gepke (1792), Aaltje (2 January 1794), Ebe (1795) and Klaas (1797, died).\n\nBy 1818 his four surviving children were acting together as owners and borrowers at Eestrum, and in 1820 they jointly bought half a house and land there for 2,000 guilders. His death has not been found in the indexes.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Liebbe Klazes Wiersma","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ea568fdd-0ed7-4b79-897c-1013f690089d"],["Marriage, Eestrum 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:01733302-f61f-45f3-b096-500dba854cd5"],["Baptism of Aaltje, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:157c185d-816c-4465-addc-663fdcd53f1c"],["Notarial deed, purchase at Eestrum, 17 Apr 1820","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:764ddc1e-e0d3-42ce-a570-8dfebf39cd9c"],["Death of son Ebe, 1867","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:4e2b3c27-aa26-42ab-1002-cecce9b2ebd8"]]},
hzlutskesjoukes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Lutske Sjoukes Tadema","d":"b. 1757 · Eestrum","b":1757,"p":["hzsjoukehendriks","hzaaltjeklases"],"t":"Baptised at Eestrum on 28 August 1757, the daughter of Sjouke Hendriks and Aaltje Klases; she carried the name of her father's mother, Lutske Sjoukes Tadema, and her children's death records later gave her that surname too. She was the second of at least ten children. The reconstruction also considers a daughter Lyske baptised in 1760, but that child was buried in 1778, so Lutske is the girl of 1757. On 1 December 1778 three of her younger sisters, Lyske, Lamke and Anke, were buried at Eestrum on the same day.\n\nOn 10 November 1786 she married Liebbe Klazes of Eestrum, a farmer, and made her profession of faith there in 1790. Her children were Klaas (1787), Sjouke (1789), named after her father, Gepke (1792), Aaltje (1794), named after her mother, Ebe (1795) and Klaas (1797); both Klaases died young. In 1810 she appears in a notarial deed as the wife of Liebbe Klazes. She was still living in 1816, when she is named at Ebe's wedding.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lutske Sjoukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5edd2b31-55d7-4b50-8a62-e245729c9534"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Sjouke Hendriks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:22d27dec-5133-482e-815c-b07295d092fc"],["Marriage, Eestrum 1786","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:01733302-f61f-45f3-b096-500dba854cd5"],["Notarial deed, 25 Apr 1810","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6a2fa647-e033-47f4-b4be-fa4e64f617c5"],["Marriage of son Ebe, 1816","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:6282863a-a308-b606-76ba-86e4eb72bc7c"],["Death of daughter Gepke, 1868","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:258175a6-f4d6-8c8c-7334-24aa2aa8cd11"]]},
hzklaasouwes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Klaas Ouwes","d":"c. 1717 · Bergum","b":1717,"p":[null,null],"t":"The reconstruction of the Tietjerksteradeel population suggests, as a possibility only, that he was the Klaas baptised at Murmerwoude on 23 May 1717, son of Ouwe Luitjens and Aaltje Klases; his birth year rests on that baptism. He was a labourer (arbeider) at Bergum when he married Gepke Liebbes of Veenwouden at Veenwouden on 6 August 1747. The 1749 tax assessment of Bergum describes him as \"a labourer who earns his keep and no more\", and the village tax registers place him at Bergum house number 28 in 1748 and 1749.\n\nTheir children were baptised at Veenwouden: Sjoukje (1749), Ouwe (1751), Trijntje (1753) and Liebbe (1755). By 1773 the family lived at Eestrum, and by 1780 Klaas was a farmer there, a step up from his labouring years. Both his sons farmed at Eestrum and took the surname Wiersma in 1811.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Ouwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cf8cfdd9-d4a8-4182-95b7-1ca7fe89abe4"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Ouwe Klazes Wiersma","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a62bf2ff-632e-421d-8b5d-bc71440e2fb0"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gepke Liebbes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7bbc2baa-54ff-458c-89b6-accb49e8fede"]]},
hzgepkeliebbes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Gepke Liebbes","d":"1726–1819 · Veenwouden","b":1726,"x":1819,"p":[null,null],"t":"Baptised at Veenwouden on 7 July 1726, the daughter of Liebbe Diddes and Trijntje Willems. On 6 August 1747 she married Klaas Ouwes, a labourer of Bergum, at Veenwouden. Their children Sjoukje (1749), Ouwe (1751), Trijntje (1753) and Liebbe (1755) were baptised there; Liebbe carried her father's name.\n\nBy 1773 the family lived at Eestrum, where her husband became a farmer. She died at Eestrum on 2 March 1819, aged 92. Her granddaughter Gepke Liebbes Wiersma, born in 1792, was named after her.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gepke Liebbes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:7bbc2baa-54ff-458c-89b6-accb49e8fede"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Klaas Ouwes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:cf8cfdd9-d4a8-4182-95b7-1ca7fe89abe4"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gepke Liebbes Wiersma","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:a2973899-fd5a-4715-9a0f-97a7cf74f374"]]},
hzsjoukehendriks:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Sjouke Hendriks","d":"b. 1726 · Eestrum","b":1726,"p":[null,null],"t":"Baptised at Eestrum on 19 May 1726, the son of Hendrik Melles and Lutske Sjoukes Tadema. He married Aaltje Klases and made his profession of faith at Eestrum in 1767; he appears in the court books of Tietjerksteradeel in the 1770s and 1780s.\n\nHe and Aaltje had at least ten children baptised at Eestrum: Hendrik (1754), Lutske (1757), Lyske (1760), Antje (1763, died young), Lamke (1766), the twins Anke and Klaaske (1769), Klaas (1772) and Wybrichje (1775). On 1 December 1778 three of the children, Lyske, Lamke and Anke, were buried at Eestrum on the same day. Wybrichje married Pieter Alles Tjepkema in 1796, and Lutske married Liebbe Klazes Wiersma in 1786.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Sjouke Hendriks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:22d27dec-5133-482e-815c-b07295d092fc"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Wybrichje Sjoukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:ad7df073-aed4-44f8-bf8b-de27c89f5843"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lutske Sjoukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5edd2b31-55d7-4b50-8a62-e245729c9534"]]},
hzaaltjeklases:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Aaltje Klases","d":"Eestrum","p":[null,null],"t":"Wife of Sjouke Hendriks of Eestrum in Tietjerksteradeel. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Klaas, and she gave that name to her son born in 1772. She had at least ten children baptised at Eestrum between 1754 and 1775: Hendrik, Lutske, Lyske, Antje, Lamke, the twins Anke and Klaaske, Klaas and Wybrichje.\n\nAntje died as a small child, and on 1 December 1778 three more of her children, Lyske, Lamke and Anke, were buried at Eestrum on a single day. Her daughter Lutske married Liebbe Klazes in 1786 and named her daughter Aaltje after her in 1794.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Sjouke Hendriks","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:22d27dec-5133-482e-815c-b07295d092fc"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Lutske Sjoukes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:5edd2b31-55d7-4b50-8a62-e245729c9534"],["Baptism of granddaughter Aaltje, 1794","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:157c185d-816c-4465-addc-663fdcd53f1c"]]},
hztobiasaebes:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Tobias Aebes","d":"1675–before 1749 · Oostermeer","b":1675,"p":[null,null],"t":"Baptised at Oostermeer on 10 January 1675, the son of Aebe Gerbens and Aaltje Sjoerds. He was a master tailor (mr. kleermaker, in Frisian schroor). On 4 November 1694 he married Maaike Pieters of Suameer, and he made his profession of faith at Oostermeer in 1698.\n\nThe family moved about the village: Bergum in 1695, Oostermeer \"bij de Wal\" in 1697 and 1700, 't Witveen in 1708, and the Heidburen in 1703 and 1716. Their children were Gerben (baptised at Bergum 1695), who became a master baker at Oostermeer, Pieter (10 October 1697), Roelof (1700), a baker at Surhuisterveen, Jouke (1703), Aaltje (1707), named after his mother, Aebe (1708), named after his father, and Wybren (born 1711, baptised 1716). He also acted as guardian for the children of Edse Jochums and Dieuwke Aebes.\n\nAfter Maaike's death he married Hinke Cornelis at Oostermeer on 10 January 1734. The 1749 tax assessment has a one-person household under his name at Oostermeer, \"old and poor\"; the reconstruction reads the entry as his widow's, which places his death before 1749.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Aebes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3af42e7f-f2a2-4b56-8ea8-32dc6ab406a4"],["Second marriage, Oostermeer 1734","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:2bf19235-7875-4ce7-b9db-92caa8e92db5"],["Tax assessment (quotisatie), Oostermeer 1749","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:0a78a055-134e-4d15-a126-9bd58fead0b6"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Gerben Tobias","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:35ee2347-0d23-485b-86e7-455844f80bcc"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Pieter Tobias (b. 1697)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1f2ecac-37cf-4173-87bc-3e45a9f3a629"]]},
hzmaaikepieters:{"s":"h","r":["nl"],"n":"Maaike Pieters","d":"Suameer · m. 1694","p":[null,null],"t":"She came from Suameer in Tietjerksteradeel and married Tobias Aebes, a master tailor of Oostermeer, at Oostermeer on 4 November 1694. Her patronymic shows that her father was called Pieter, and she gave that name to her second son, baptised on 10 October 1697.\n\nHer children were Gerben (1695), Pieter (1697), Roelof (1700), Jouke (1703), Aaltje (1707), Aebe (1708) and Wybren (born 1711). Gerben and Roelof became bakers, at Oostermeer and Surhuisterveen; Pieter farmed at Oostermeer and named his eldest daughter Maaike after her in 1731. She died before January 1734, when Tobias married again.","src":[["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Tobias Aebes","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:3af42e7f-f2a2-4b56-8ea8-32dc6ab406a4"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Pieter Tobias (b. 1697)","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:d1f2ecac-37cf-4173-87bc-3e45a9f3a629"],["Tietjerksteradeel population reconstruction: Roel Tobias","https://www.openarchieven.nl/frl:fbd4d9c4-75c8-4c90-9d17-631135341e51"]]},
};

